window.CYCLE_BANK=[{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":1,"q":"Under the NEC definitions, ampacity is best described as:","o":["The maximum voltage a conductor insulation can withstand without breakdown","The maximum current, in amperes, a conductor can carry continuously under the conditions of use without exceeding its temperature rating","The current at which the overcurrent device protecting a conductor will open within one hour","The maximum current a conductor can carry for a short time without damage during a fault"],"a":1,"e":"Ampacity is the maximum current a conductor can carry continuously under the conditions of use without exceeding its temperature rating.","r":"NEC 100 (Ampacity)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":1,"q":"In the NEC, a branch circuit is defined as the circuit conductors between:","o":["The service point and the service disconnecting means","The final overcurrent device protecting the circuit and the outlet(s)","The service equipment and the first panelboard","The utility transformer and the service equipment"],"a":1,"e":"A branch circuit consists of the conductors between the final overcurrent device protecting the circuit and the outlet(s). Conductors ahead of that final device are feeders.","r":"NEC 100 (Branch Circuit)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":1,"q":"Which description matches the NEC definition of a feeder?","o":["The circuit conductors between the final branch-circuit overcurrent device and the outlets","All circuit conductors between the service equipment (or the source of a separately derived system) and the final branch-circuit overcurrent device","Any conductor that supplies only a single piece of utilization equipment","The conductors from the utility transformer to the service point"],"a":1,"e":"Feeder conductors run from the service equipment, a separately derived system, or other power source to the final branch-circuit overcurrent device.","r":"NEC 100 (Feeder)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":1,"q":"A continuous load is defined in the NEC as a load where the maximum current is expected to continue for:","o":["3 hours or more","8 hours or more","1 hour or more","2 hours or more"],"a":0,"e":"The Article 100 definition of continuous load is a load where the maximum current is expected to continue for 3 hours or more.","r":"NEC 100 (Continuous Load)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"Equipment that is 'readily accessible' is capable of being reached quickly for operation, renewal, or inspections without requiring those to whom ready access is requisite to:","o":["Climb over or remove obstacles or resort to portable ladders","Be accompanied by a qualified person","Obtain a key from the building owner","Use any tool at all"],"a":0,"e":"Readily accessible means reachable quickly without climbing over or removing obstacles or resorting to portable ladders, and so on. Equipment behind a locked door can still be accessible but is not readily accessible to those without a key.","r":"NEC 100 (Accessible, Readily)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":1,"q":"A ground-fault circuit interrupter (GFCI) is a device intended for:","o":["Limiting transient overvoltages on branch circuits","Protection of equipment only, by opening at 30 mA or more of ground-fault current","Protection of conductors from overload","Protection of personnel, by de-energizing a circuit when the current to ground exceeds the values established for a Class A device"],"a":3,"e":"A GFCI is a personnel-protection device that de-energizes the circuit when ground-fault current exceeds the Class A trip values. Equipment protection is the job of a ground-fault protection of equipment device.","r":"NEC 100 (Ground-Fault Circuit Interrupter)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is required in a unit for it to meet the NEC definition of a dwelling unit?","o":["A minimum floor area of 400 square feet","A separate utility service for the unit","Permanent provisions for sleeping and sanitation only","Permanent provisions for living, sleeping, cooking, and sanitation"],"a":3,"e":"A dwelling unit is a single unit providing complete and independent living facilities, including permanent provisions for living, sleeping, cooking, and sanitation.","r":"NEC 100 (Dwelling Unit)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"The service point is defined as:","o":["The point where the grounding electrode conductor connects to the grounded conductor","The first panelboard supplied by the service conductors","The point of connection between the facilities of the serving utility and the premises wiring","The location of the main disconnecting means in the building"],"a":2,"e":"The service point marks the demarcation between the serving utility and the premises wiring. It is not necessarily where the disconnect is located.","r":"NEC 100 (Service Point)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"Under the NEC definition, a qualified person is one who:","o":["Has skills and knowledge related to the construction and operation of electrical equipment and installations and has received safety training to recognize and avoid the hazards involved","Has worked at least four years for an electrical contractor","Has been named in writing by the authority having jurisdiction","Holds a master electrician license in the state of the installation"],"a":0,"e":"The definition is based on skills, knowledge, and safety training rather than a license or a set number of years.","r":"NEC 100 (Qualified Person)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":1,"q":"The main bonding jumper is the connection between:","o":["Two grounding electrodes of the same system","The grounded circuit conductor and the equipment grounding conductor at the service","The service disconnect and the meter enclosure","The grounding electrode and the grounding electrode conductor"],"a":1,"e":"The main bonding jumper joins the grounded conductor and the equipment grounding conductor (or supply-side bonding jumper) at the service.","r":"NEC 100 (Bonding Jumper, Main)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":3,"q":"An effective ground-fault current path is best described as:","o":["Any metal path to earth, including a driven ground rod","A temporary path established by a bonding jumper during testing","The path through the earth that returns fault current to the utility transformer","An intentionally constructed, permanent, low-impedance path designed to carry fault current from the point of a ground fault on a wiring system to the electrical supply source and to facilitate operation of the overcurrent device"],"a":3,"e":"Fault current must return to the source through a low-impedance metallic path so the overcurrent device operates. The earth is not an effective ground-fault current path.","r":"NEC 100 (Ground-Fault Current Path, Effective)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"Which condition is an overload as the NEC defines the term?","o":["A momentary motor starting inrush that clears in a few cycles","A bolted line-to-line short circuit at a panelboard","A conductor carrying current in excess of its rated ampacity for long enough to cause damage or dangerous overheating","A line-to-ground fault on a metal raceway"],"a":2,"e":"An overload persists long enough to cause damage or overheating. A short circuit or ground fault is a fault, which the definition says is not an overload.","r":"NEC 100 (Overload)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"Which of these is a wet location as defined in Article 100?","o":["A cold-storage warehouse interior with moderate moisture","A roofed open porch that is protected from direct weather","An installation underground or in concrete slabs or masonry in direct contact with the earth","A dry basement with occasional humidity"],"a":2,"e":"Wet locations include installations underground or in slabs or masonry in direct contact with earth, locations subject to saturation, and unprotected locations exposed to weather. A roofed open porch is a damp location.","r":"NEC 100 (Location, Wet)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is an example of a damp location per Article 100?","o":["A dry attic with no moisture exposure","A car wash bay washed down by hose","A buried underground conduit run","A partially protected location under a roofed open porch"],"a":3,"e":"Damp locations include partially protected places such as under canopies, marquees, and roofed open porches, and interior locations with moderate moisture.","r":"NEC 100 (Location, Damp)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"A multiwire branch circuit has:","o":["Only one ungrounded conductor and several equipment grounding conductors","Two or more grounded conductors sharing a single overcurrent device","Ungrounded conductors from different panelboards sharing a neutral","Two or more ungrounded conductors with a voltage between them, and a grounded (neutral) conductor having equal voltage between it and each ungrounded conductor"],"a":3,"e":"A multiwire branch circuit has two or more ungrounded conductors and a common grounded conductor that is connected to the neutral or grounded conductor of the system.","r":"NEC 100 (Branch Circuit, Multiwire)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":3,"q":"Which secondary is a separately derived system?","o":["The output of an autotransformer that shares a conductor with its input","The load side of a service disconnect","A 120/240 V feeder supplied by a panelboard main breaker","A 480 V delta to 208Y/120 V dry-type transformer secondary with no direct electrical connection to the supply conductors"],"a":3,"e":"A separately derived system gets its power from a source other than a service and has no direct connection between its circuit conductors and those of another system except through grounding and bonding. An autotransformer has a direct connection.","r":"NEC 100 (Separately Derived System)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":1,"q":"Dead front, as applied to equipment, means:","o":["Without live parts exposed to a person on the operating side of the equipment","Mounted flush with a wall surface","Not energized at any time","Having all enclosure openings sealed"],"a":0,"e":"Dead front equipment has no live parts exposed to a person on the operating side, though live parts remain inside.","r":"NEC 100 (Dead Front)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"Equipment is 'labeled' when it:","o":["Has been approved by the electrical contractor installing it","Is merely included in a published catalog of products","Carries a manufacturer nameplate showing voltage and current","Has a label, symbol, or identifying mark of an organization acceptable to the AHJ that is concerned with product evaluation and maintains periodic inspection of production"],"a":3,"e":"Labeled equipment bears the mark of an acceptable evaluation organization that also inspects production periodically. A manufacturer nameplate alone does not make equipment labeled.","r":"NEC 100 (Labeled)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":1,"q":"In the NEC, an outlet is:","o":["A point on the wiring system at which current is taken to supply utilization equipment","Only a receptacle into which a plug is inserted","The enclosure that houses a switch","Any junction box containing a splice"],"a":0,"e":"An outlet is any point where current is taken to supply utilization equipment, which includes lighting outlets and receptacle outlets.","r":"NEC 100 (Outlet)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"As defined in Article 100, the voltage of a circuit is:","o":["The peak value of the supply waveform","The lowest voltage between a conductor and the grounded conductor","The greatest root-mean-square (effective) difference of potential between any two conductors of the circuit","The voltage from any ungrounded conductor to ground only"],"a":2,"e":"Circuit voltage is the greatest rms difference of potential between any two conductors of the circuit, such as 208 V line-to-line on a 208Y/120 V system.","r":"NEC 100 (Voltage of a Circuit)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":1,"q":"A device, in the NEC definitions, is:","o":["A protective component that only interrupts fault current","A listed enclosure for splices","Any equipment that uses electric energy to produce light or heat","A unit of an electrical system, other than a conductor, that carries or controls electric energy as its principal function"],"a":3,"e":"Switches and receptacles are devices. A device carries or controls electric energy rather than utilizing it.","r":"NEC 100 (Device)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"A wiring method is 'accessible' when it is:","o":["Capable of being removed or exposed without damaging the building structure or finish, or not permanently closed in by the structure or finish of the building","Installed in a raceway that has pull boxes at every 100 ft","Located no more than 6.5 ft above the floor","Visible without removing a cover plate"],"a":0,"e":"Accessible (as applied to wiring methods) means it can be exposed without damaging the building finish, or is not permanently enclosed by the structure.","r":"NEC 100 (Accessible, Wiring Methods)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":1,"q":"Utilization equipment is equipment that:","o":["Only protects conductors from overcurrent","Only connects a grounding electrode to the system","Only distributes power to branch circuits","Uses electric energy for electronic, electromechanical, chemical, heating, lighting, or similar purposes"],"a":3,"e":"Utilization equipment turns electric energy into another form of useful work, such as heat or light.","r":"NEC 100 (Utilization Equipment)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"A cabinet is best described as an enclosure that is:","o":["Designed solely for outdoor service equipment","Designed for surface mounting only and fitted with a removable cover with no door","A sheet-metal box used only to enclose splices","Designed for surface or flush mounting and provided with a frame, mat, or trim in which a swinging door or doors are or can be hung"],"a":3,"e":"A cabinet has a frame or trim for swinging doors and can be flush or surface mounted. A cutout box is surface mounted with a swinging door but no frame, mat, or trim on a flush type.","r":"NEC 100 (Cabinet)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":1,"q":"A grounded conductor is:","o":["A conductor that carries current only during a ground fault","Any conductor that is bonded to a metal raceway","A system or circuit conductor that is intentionally grounded","The conductor that connects equipment to the grounding electrode"],"a":2,"e":"The grounded conductor is intentionally grounded. It normally carries current, unlike an equipment grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 100 (Conductor, Grounded)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":2,"q":"The continuity of a grounded conductor in a premises wiring system:","o":["May rely on a metal raceway when the raceway is listed","May rely on cable armor in dwelling units only","Shall not depend on a connection to a metallic enclosure, raceway, or cable armor","May rely on a locknut connection if it is tightened with a torque tool"],"a":2,"e":"The grounded conductor must be continuous on its own and cannot depend on a metal enclosure, raceway, or armor for continuity.","r":"NEC 200.2(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":2,"q":"Premises wiring is permitted to be electrically connected to a supply system only if the supply system:","o":["Contains, for any grounded conductor of the interior wiring system, a corresponding conductor that is grounded","Is supplied at 120 volts or less","Is derived from a utility-owned transformer","Has an ungrounded delta secondary"],"a":0,"e":"Section 200.3 requires the supply system to have a corresponding grounded conductor when the interior system has one. Connection through electromagnetic induction does not count.","r":"NEC 200.3","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":2,"q":"A neutral conductor of a branch circuit or feeder:","o":["Shall not be used for more than one branch circuit, multiwire branch circuit, or set of ungrounded feeder conductors unless specifically permitted elsewhere in the Code","May be shared by all circuits fed from the same panelboard","May be shared by circuits on different phases only if both are 20 A","May be shared by any two branch circuits in the same raceway"],"a":0,"e":"Each neutral serves only one circuit, multiwire circuit, or feeder set unless the Code specifically permits otherwise.","r":"NEC 200.4(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":3,"q":"Two multiwire branch circuits are installed in the same panelboard enclosure. What is required for their neutral conductors?","o":["Each circuit's grounded conductor must be identified or grouped with its associated ungrounded conductors by wire markers, cable ties, or similar means in at least one location within the enclosure","Both circuits must share the same neutral conductor","The two neutrals must be connected together on one terminal","No identification is required if the neutrals are the same size"],"a":0,"e":"Where more than one neutral from different circuits is in an enclosure, each must be identified or grouped with its associated ungrounded conductors.","r":"NEC 200.4(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":1,"q":"A 10 AWG insulated conductor used as a grounded conductor can be identified by:","o":["A continuous green outer finish","A continuous white or gray outer finish","A continuous red outer finish","A continuous blue outer finish"],"a":1,"e":"For 6 AWG or smaller, a grounded conductor is identified by a continuous white or gray outer finish, or by three continuous white or gray stripes on other than green insulation.","r":"NEC 200.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":2,"q":"Which method is permitted to identify a 12 AWG insulated grounded conductor?","o":["A single white marking at each termination only","A yellow stripe on gray insulation","Three continuous white stripes along the conductor's entire length on insulation that is not green","Three continuous white stripes on green insulation"],"a":2,"e":"Conductors 6 AWG or smaller may use a white or gray finish or three continuous white or gray stripes on other than green insulation. Marking at terminations only is allowed for larger sizes.","r":"NEC 200.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":2,"q":"An insulated grounded conductor of 250 kcmil is permitted to be identified by:","o":["A green marking at the terminations","A distinctive white marking at its terminations, made at the time of installation","A blue marking at the terminations","Black insulation with no marking"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors 4 AWG or larger may be identified by a white or gray finish, three white stripes, or a distinctive white marking at the terminations applied at installation. The marking must encircle the conductor.","r":"NEC 200.6(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":3,"q":"A 3/0 AWG black THHN conductor is installed as the neutral of a feeder. Which statement is correct?","o":["It must be marked with green tape at the terminations","It is permitted only if it is bare at the terminations","It is not permitted, because neutrals must be purchased with white insulation","It is acceptable if it is identified with a distinctive white marking encircling the conductor at each termination at the time of installation"],"a":3,"e":"For 4 AWG and larger, white marking at terminations, applied at installation, is an allowed identification of a grounded conductor.","r":"NEC 200.6(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":2,"q":"An insulated conductor intended for use as the grounded conductor in a flexible cord is identified by:","o":["A green outer finish with a yellow stripe","A solid red outer finish","A white or gray outer finish, or by other means permitted in the flexible cord provisions such as a ridge or a tracer","Any dark color of the installer's choice"],"a":2,"e":"Flexible cords may identify the grounded conductor with white or gray finish or other means allowed by the cord provisions. Green with yellow stripe is for equipment grounding.","r":"NEC 200.6(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":3,"q":"Grounded conductors of two different systems, such as 120/240 V and 277/480 V, are installed in the same raceway. What is required?","o":["The systems may not share a raceway under any circumstances","Both neutrals must be white with no further marking","Each grounded conductor must be identified by system, and the identification method must be documented where it is readily available or permanently posted at each branch-circuit panelboard","Both systems must share one neutral conductor"],"a":2,"e":"Where grounded conductors of different systems are in the same raceway, box, or enclosure, each must be identified by system, and the method must be documented or posted.","r":"NEC 200.6(D)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":2,"q":"The insulated grounded conductor of a multiconductor cable is identified by:","o":["A continuous green outer finish","A continuous white or gray outer finish, or three continuous white stripes on other than green insulation along its entire length","A purple marking at the cable ends only","Orange insulation"],"a":1,"e":"A grounded conductor in a multiconductor cable is identified by a white or gray finish or three continuous white stripes over its length.","r":"NEC 200.6(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":2,"q":"In circuits of 50 volts or more, white or gray insulation, or three continuous white or gray stripes, is generally permitted to identify:","o":["Only equipment grounding conductors","Any conductor, if the circuit is 120 V","Only conductors that are intentionally grounded","Only the grounding electrode conductor"],"a":2,"e":"In 50 V and greater circuits, white, gray, or three-striped insulation is reserved for grounded conductors, with limited exceptions such as switch loops permanently reidentified.","r":"NEC 200.7","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":3,"q":"A 2-wire nonmetallic-sheathed cable is used as a switch loop and its white conductor is used as an ungrounded conductor. What must be done?","o":["The white conductor must be permanently reidentified with a color other than white, gray, or green, at each location where it is visible and accessible","It must be spliced to a black conductor inside the cable","Nothing, as white conductors may always be used as ungrounded conductors","It must be reidentified with green tape"],"a":0,"e":"A white conductor in a cable can serve as an ungrounded conductor in a switch loop only if permanently reidentified (marking encircling the insulation) with a color other than white, gray, or green.","r":"NEC 200.7(C)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":2,"q":"A white conductor reidentified for use as an ungrounded conductor must be marked with a color that is:","o":["Green with a yellow stripe","Any shade of gray","Other than white, gray, or green","White with a black stripe"],"a":2,"e":"The reidentification must use a color other than white, gray, or green so it is not mistaken for a grounded or grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 200.7(C)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":1,"q":"The terminal of a device to which the grounded conductor is connected is identified by being:","o":["Substantially red in color","Substantially green in color","Substantially white in color","Substantially black in color"],"a":2,"e":"Terminals for grounded conductors are substantially white, while terminals for other conductors are a readily distinguishable different color.","r":"NEC 200.9","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":2,"q":"On a receptacle, the terminal intended for the grounded conductor is identified by:","o":["A red-colored screw","A metal or metal coating substantially white in color, or the word 'white' or letter 'W' near the terminal","A brass screw only","A green screw"],"a":1,"e":"Receptacle terminals for the grounded conductor are marked with white metal or the word white or the letter W. If the terminal is not visible, the conductor entrance hole is colored white.","r":"NEC 200.10(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":2,"q":"On a screw-shell lampholder, the grounded conductor is connected to:","o":["The terminal connected to the screw shell","The terminal connected to the center contact","The mounting strap only","Either terminal, as polarity is not important"],"a":0,"e":"The grounded conductor goes to the screw shell so the shell is not a shock hazard when changing lamps.","r":"NEC 200.10(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":2,"q":"Which statement about polarity is correct?","o":["Reversed polarity is permitted for receptacles in dry locations","No grounded conductor shall be attached to any terminal or lead so as to reverse the designated polarity","Reverse polarity is acceptable when GFCI protection is provided","Polarity applies only to dc circuits"],"a":1,"e":"Section 200.11 prohibits connecting the grounded conductor to a terminal or lead such that designated polarity is reversed.","r":"NEC 200.11","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":1,"q":"Which color of insulation is NOT permitted for identifying a grounded conductor of 8 AWG?","o":["Green","White","White stripes on non-green insulation","Gray"],"a":0,"e":"Green is reserved for equipment grounding conductors. White, gray, or three white stripes on other than green insulation identify a grounded conductor.","r":"NEC 200.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":2,"q":"Can gray insulation be used to identify a grounded conductor in a 120/240 V system?","o":["No, gray is reserved for equipment grounding","No, only white is permitted","Yes, but only in industrial occupancies","Yes, white or gray may be used for grounded conductors"],"a":3,"e":"Article 200 recognizes continuous white or gray outer finish for grounded conductors.","r":"NEC 200.6(A), 200.7","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":3,"q":"Where a grounded conductor is insulated on a solidly grounded circuit of 1000 V or less, its insulation must be:","o":["Rated at 300 V regardless of circuit voltage","Suitable, other than color, for any ungrounded conductor of the same circuit","Of lesser rating than the ungrounded conductors, as it carries no fault energy","Rated at least 1000 V in all cases"],"a":1,"e":"For circuits under 1000 V, an insulated grounded conductor needs insulation suitable, other than color, for any ungrounded conductor of the same circuit.","r":"NEC 200.2(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":1,"q":"Which of the following may identify a grounded conductor 1/0 AWG or larger?","o":["Three continuous white stripes along its entire length on other than green insulation","A single green stripe on black insulation","A blue band at the terminations","Red insulation"],"a":0,"e":"Conductors 4 AWG and larger may use a white or gray finish, three continuous white stripes on non-green insulation, or white marking at terminations.","r":"NEC 200.6(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":1,"q":"Article 200 covers which of the following?","o":["Identification of terminals, grounded conductors in premises wiring systems, and identification of grounded conductors","Sizing of equipment grounding conductors","Installation of grounding electrodes","Overcurrent protection of grounded conductors"],"a":0,"e":"Article 200 deals with identification of terminals and grounded conductors and their use in premises wiring. Grounding electrodes and equipment grounding conductor sizing are in Article 250.","r":"NEC 200.1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":3,"q":"An electrician proposes to share one neutral between two separate 20 A single-pole circuits that originate at different breakers and run in two separate nonmetallic-sheathed cables to different rooms. Is this permitted?","o":["No, a neutral conductor cannot be used for more than one branch circuit unless it is a properly arranged multiwire branch circuit or otherwise specifically permitted","Yes, as long as the neutral is 10 AWG","Yes, as long as the breakers are in the same panelboard","Yes, if the two breakers have a handle tie"],"a":0,"e":"Each branch circuit needs its own neutral unless the circuits are arranged as a multiwire branch circuit or sharing is specifically permitted elsewhere.","r":"NEC 200.4(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":1,"q":"The identification of terminals for conductors other than the grounded conductor shall be:","o":["Gray only","The same white color as the grounded terminal","Not required if the terminal is brass","A readily distinguishable color different from white"],"a":3,"e":"Terminals for the grounded conductor are substantially white. The other terminals must be a readily distinguishable different color.","r":"NEC 200.9","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"Listed or labeled equipment must be installed and used:","o":["Only as the installer judges best, since listing is voluntary","In accordance with any instructions included in the listing or labeling","Only in the locations the AHJ has previously inspected","In accordance with the manufacturer's catalog, ignoring the listing instructions"],"a":1,"e":"Section 110.3(B) requires listed or labeled equipment to be installed per the instructions in the listing or labeling.","r":"NEC 110.3(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"Unused openings in boxes, raceways, cabinets, and equipment cases must be:","o":["Left open for ventilation","Filled with any nonmetallic material","Covered with tape","Effectively closed to afford protection substantially equivalent to the wall of the equipment"],"a":3,"e":"Unused cable or raceway openings must be closed to provide protection substantially equivalent to the equipment wall, using approved methods such as knockout seals or plugs.","r":"NEC 110.12(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"An electrician finds paint and drywall dust on the busbars and terminals of a new panelboard. Which statement applies?","o":["Internal parts must not be damaged or contaminated by foreign materials such as paint, plaster, or cleaners, so the contamination must be addressed","Paint on busbars is acceptable if it is the same color as the enclosure","Dust is acceptable if the cover is installed afterward","Contamination matters only for outdoor equipment"],"a":0,"e":"Internal parts of equipment including busbars and terminals must not be damaged or contaminated by foreign materials. Equipment that is contaminated or damaged must be cleaned or replaced as needed.","r":"NEC 110.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"A terminal is permitted to receive more than one conductor only if the terminal is:","o":["Rated 75 degrees C","Made of brass","Located in a dry location","Identified for more than one conductor"],"a":3,"e":"Terminals for more than one conductor, and terminals used to connect aluminum, must be identified for that use.","r":"NEC 110.14(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Which statement about mixing conductor materials in a terminal or splicing connector is correct?","o":["Mixing is acceptable if antioxidant is used","Copper and aluminum may be mixed in any listed connector","Mixing is acceptable for conductors 6 AWG and smaller","Dissimilar metals such as copper and aluminum may not be intermixed where physical contact occurs unless the device is identified for the purpose"],"a":3,"e":"Dissimilar conductors in a terminal or splicing connector need a device identified for that purpose and use.","r":"NEC 110.14(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Unless otherwise marked, what temperature rating column of Table 310.16 applies to conductors on equipment terminals for circuits rated 100 A or less?","o":["90 degrees C","105 degrees C","75 degrees C","60 degrees C"],"a":3,"e":"For circuits rated 100 A or less, or marked for 14 AWG through 1 AWG conductors, termination ampacity is based on the 60 C column unless the equipment is marked for higher.","r":"NEC 110.14(C)(1)(a)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Equipment terminals for a 200 A circuit are not marked with a temperature rating. The ampacity of the conductors terminating there is based on:","o":["The 90 degrees C column","The 75 degrees C column","The 105 degrees C column","The 60 degrees C column"],"a":1,"e":"For circuits over 100 A, or conductors larger than 1 AWG, termination ampacity is based on the 75 C column unless the equipment is marked otherwise.","r":"NEC 110.14(C)(1)(b)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":3,"q":"Four 12 AWG THHN copper current-carrying conductors are in a conduit and terminate on a 20 A breaker with terminals rated 60 degrees C. The 90 C ampacity of 12 AWG is 30 A, and the adjustment factor for four conductors is 80%. What is the highest ampacity that may be used?","o":["24 A","20 A","30 A","25 A"],"a":1,"e":"The 90 C rating can be used for adjustment (30 x 0.8 = 24 A), but the final ampacity cannot exceed the 60 C ampacity of 12 AWG, which is 20 A.","r":"NEC 110.14(C)(1)(a)(3), Table 310.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":3,"q":"A 200 A feeder uses THHN copper conductors terminated on equipment with unmarked terminals. What is the minimum copper conductor size permitted based on the termination ratings?","o":["1/0 AWG","4 AWG","3/0 AWG","2/0 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Termination ampacity is based on the 75 C column for circuits over 100 A, where 3/0 AWG copper is 200 A. 2/0 AWG is 175 A at 75 C.","r":"NEC 110.14(C)(1)(b), Table 310.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"For a motor with NEMA design letter B, C, D, or E, termination ampacity of conductors rated 90 C may be based on which column, provided the rule's conditions are met?","o":["The 40 degrees C column","The 60 degrees C column only","The 90 degrees C column","The 75 degrees C column"],"a":3,"e":"Motors marked with design letters B, C, D, or E may use conductors rated 75 C or higher with ampacity based on the 75 C column even for circuits of 100 A or less.","r":"NEC 110.14(C)(1)(a)(4)","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Where a numeric tightening torque value is marked on equipment or given in the installation instructions, the installer must:","o":["Tighten to the feel of an experienced electrician","Use a torque tool only on terminals 1/0 AWG and larger","Use a calibrated torque tool to achieve the indicated torque, unless the manufacturer provides an alternative method","Tighten until the conductor will not move by hand"],"a":2,"e":"A calibrated torque tool is required where a numeric torque value is specified, unless the manufacturer gives an alternative method of achieving the torque.","r":"NEC 110.14(D)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"When making a soldered splice, the conductors must first be:","o":["Held with tape and soldered afterward","Spliced or joined so they are mechanically and electrically secure without solder, then soldered","Twisted loosely and held together by solder only","Placed in a box and soldered while energized"],"a":1,"e":"Soldered splices must be mechanically and electrically secure before solder is applied, then insulated equivalent to the conductors.","r":"NEC 110.14(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"Wire-binding screws or studs and nuts with upturned lugs are permitted to terminate conductors sized:","o":["4/0 AWG or smaller","1 AWG or smaller","10 AWG or smaller","6 AWG or smaller"],"a":2,"e":"Terminals using wire-binding screws or studs with nuts and upturned lugs are limited to 10 AWG and smaller conductors.","r":"NEC 110.14","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"On a 4-wire delta system where the midpoint of one phase winding is grounded, the conductor with the higher voltage to ground must be marked with:","o":["A red outer finish","A blue outer finish","A gray outer finish","An outer finish that is orange in color, or other effective means"],"a":3,"e":"The high leg is identified orange. On a 240 V delta with a grounded midpoint, the high leg measures 208 V to ground.","r":"NEC 110.15","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Which equipment requires an arc-flash hazard warning marking under 110.16(A)?","o":["A commercial panelboard likely to be examined, adjusted, or serviced while energized","A general-use snap switch","A 15 A receptacle","A dwelling unit panelboard"],"a":0,"e":"Switchboards, panelboards, industrial control panels, meter socket enclosures, and motor control centers in other than dwelling units likely to be worked energized require an arc-flash warning label.","r":"NEC 110.16(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"Each service, feeder, and branch-circuit disconnecting means must be legibly marked to indicate its purpose unless:","o":["It is installed in a dwelling","It is rated less than 60 A","It is located and arranged so the purpose is evident","It is a circuit breaker rather than a fused switch"],"a":2,"e":"Disconnects must be marked for purpose unless evident, and the marking must be durable enough for the environment.","r":"NEC 110.22(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum depth of working space in front of a 120/240 V panelboard with exposed live parts on one side only and no grounded parts on the other?","o":["3.5 ft","3 ft","2.5 ft","4 ft"],"a":1,"e":"For 0-150 V to ground, Table 110.26(A)(1) requires 3 ft for Conditions 1, 2, and 3.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(1), Table 110.26(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"A 480Y/277 V panelboard has exposed live parts on one side and no live or grounded parts on the opposite side (Condition 1). The minimum working space depth is:","o":["4.5 ft","3 ft","4 ft","3.5 ft"],"a":1,"e":"For 151-600 V to ground, Condition 1 requires 3 ft. Condition 2 requires 3.5 ft and Condition 3 requires 4 ft.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(1), Table 110.26(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"A 480Y/277 V switchboard faces a grounded masonry wall (Condition 2). The minimum clear working space depth is:","o":["3.5 ft","5 ft","4 ft","3 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Condition 2 is exposed live parts on one side and grounded parts on the other. Concrete, brick, or tile walls are considered grounded. For 151-600 V, that is 3.5 ft.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(1), Table 110.26(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":3,"q":"Two 480Y/277 V panelboards are installed on opposite walls of an electrical room facing each other. What minimum clear distance is required between them?","o":["6 ft","4 ft","3 ft","3.5 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Exposed live parts on both sides is Condition 3. For 151-600 V to ground, the minimum depth is 4 ft.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(1), Table 110.26(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"The width of working space in front of electrical equipment must be at least:","o":["The width of the equipment only","36 in. in all cases","24 in. or the width of the equipment, whichever is greater","30 in. or the width of the equipment, whichever is greater"],"a":3,"e":"Width is the greater of 30 in. or the equipment width, and it must allow a 90-degree opening of equipment doors or hinged panels.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"The working space around a panelboard must permit its doors or hinged panels to open at least:","o":["90 degrees","120 degrees","60 degrees","45 degrees"],"a":0,"e":"Working space must allow at least a 90-degree opening of equipment doors or hinged panels.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"The height of the working space in front of a panelboard must be clear to a height of at least:","o":["7 ft in all cases","6 ft","6.5 ft, or the height of the equipment, whichever is greater","8 ft"],"a":2,"e":"The working space extends from grade, floor, or platform to a height of 6.5 ft or the height of the equipment, whichever is greater.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Within the required working space, other equipment associated with the electrical installation and located above or below the panelboard may extend into the space by not more than:","o":["3 in.","12 in.","18 in.","6 in."],"a":3,"e":"Associated equipment above or below may extend no more than 6 in. beyond the front of the electrical equipment into the working space.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"A switchboard rated 1200 A and over 6 ft wide contains overcurrent and switching devices. Where an entrance is required to the working space, it must be at least:","o":["24 in. wide and 6.5 ft high at each end of the working space","30 in. wide and 6.5 ft high in the center","36 in. wide and 7 ft high","18 in. wide and 6 ft high at one end"],"a":0,"e":"Large equipment rated 1200 A or more and over 6 ft wide requires an entrance of at least 24 in. wide and 6.5 ft high at each end, with exceptions.","r":"NEC 110.26(C)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"A room contains equipment rated 800 A with overcurrent devices. A personnel door is 20 ft from the nearest edge of the working space. What is required of the door?","o":["It must be fire rated only","It must swing inward for security","It must be locked at all times","It must open in the direction of egress and be equipped with listed panic hardware"],"a":3,"e":"For equipment rated 800 A or more, doors less than 25 ft from the working space must open in the direction of egress and have listed panic hardware.","r":"NEC 110.26(C)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"The lighting for the working space around indoor panelboards:","o":["Is not required in rooms with windows","May be controlled by a motion sensor alone","Must not be controlled by automatic means only","Must be on the emergency system"],"a":2,"e":"Illumination is required for indoor service equipment, panelboards, and similar equipment working spaces and cannot rely on automatic means only, such as a motion sensor with no manual control.","r":"NEC 110.26(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":3,"q":"A panelboard that is 6 ft tall sits in a room with a 9 ft structural ceiling. How high does the dedicated equipment space extend above the floor?","o":["To 12 ft, above the structural ceiling","To 7.5 ft","To 6 ft, the top of the panelboard","To the structural ceiling at 9 ft, because that is lower than 6 ft above the equipment"],"a":3,"e":"The dedicated space extends from the floor to 6 ft above the equipment or to the structural ceiling, whichever is lower. Here the ceiling at 9 ft is lower than 12 ft.","r":"NEC 110.26(E)(1)(a)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Which is NOT permitted in the dedicated space above an indoor panelboard?","o":["A suspended ceiling with removable panels within the 6 ft zone","Fire sprinkler protection","Conduits and cables serving the panelboard","A water pipe or duct foreign to the electrical installation"],"a":3,"e":"Piping, ducts, and equipment foreign to the electrical installation are not allowed in the dedicated space. Systems that protect the electrical installation, such as sprinklers, are permitted with protection against leakage.","r":"NEC 110.26(E)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"The working space required in front of electrical equipment:","o":["May be used for storage with a safety tag","Shall not be used for storage","May be used for storage in dwelling units only","May be used for storage if items are portable"],"a":1,"e":"Section 110.26(B) prohibits using the required working space for storage.","r":"NEC 110.26(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Live parts operating at 50 V or more are considered guarded if they are elevated, and the minimum elevation above the floor or working surface is:","o":["10 ft","8 ft","6 ft","6.5 ft"],"a":1,"e":"One of the permitted means of guarding live parts of 50 V or more is an elevation of 8 ft or more above the floor or working surface.","r":"NEC 110.27(A)(4)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Entrances to rooms containing exposed live parts must be marked with:","o":["A sign stating the room's voltage only","No marking if the door is locked","Conspicuous warning signs forbidding unqualified persons to enter","A fire-rating label"],"a":2,"e":"Entrances to rooms and guarded locations with exposed live parts must have conspicuous warning signs forbidding unqualified persons to enter.","r":"NEC 110.27(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"A fence used to enclose an outdoor electrical installation over 1000 V must be at least:","o":["4 ft high","6 ft high with no extension","7 ft high, or 6 ft of fence plus 1 ft of three or more strands of barbed wire","5 ft high"],"a":2,"e":"Fences must be not less than 7 ft high, or a combination of at least 6 ft of fence fabric and a 1 ft extension with three or more strands of barbed wire or equivalent.","r":"NEC 110.31(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"Which method of mounting electrical equipment to masonry is prohibited?","o":["Wooden plugs driven into the masonry","Masonry screws","Expansion anchors","Toggle bolts in hollow block"],"a":0,"e":"Wooden plugs driven in masonry, concrete, plaster, or similar material shall not be used.","r":"NEC 110.13(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Equipment that relies on natural air circulation and convection for cooling must be installed so that:","o":["It is boxed in to reduce dust","Room airflow over its exposed surfaces is not prevented by walls or adjacent equipment","It is wrapped in insulation","It is installed with covers on all vents"],"a":1,"e":"Natural-convection equipment needs free air circulation, and floor-mounted equipment needs clearance above to dissipate warm air.","r":"NEC 110.13(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Equipment intended to interrupt fault current must have an interrupting rating at nominal circuit voltage that is:","o":["At least equal to the current available at the line terminals of the equipment","At least equal to the conductor ampacity","At least 10,000 A in all cases","At least equal to the load current"],"a":0,"e":"Interrupting rating must be not less than the available fault current at the line terminals.","r":"NEC 110.9","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":3,"q":"Available fault current at a panelboard is 22,000 A. Which main breaker may be installed (assuming no series rating)?","o":["A breaker rated 20,000 A interrupting","A breaker with a 25,000 A interrupting rating","A breaker with a 10,000 A interrupting rating","A breaker with a 14,000 A interrupting rating"],"a":1,"e":"The interrupting rating must equal or exceed the 22,000 A available fault current, so only the 25,000 A device qualifies.","r":"NEC 110.9","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Unless identified for the use, conductors and equipment shall not be located:","o":["In any indoor location","Where ambient temperature is below 40 degrees F only","In damp or wet locations, or where exposed to gases, fumes, vapors, liquids, or other deteriorating agents, or excessive temperatures","Within 3 ft of a wall"],"a":2,"e":"Equipment must be identified for the operating environment where deteriorating agents, moisture, or excessive temperatures are present.","r":"NEC 110.11","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"Completed wiring installations must be free from:","o":["All voltage drop","All grounded conductors","Any splices","Short circuits, ground faults, or any connections to ground other than as required or permitted"],"a":3,"e":"Insulation integrity requires wiring to be free of shorts and faults, and of unintended grounds.","r":"NEC 110.7","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"Unless otherwise provided, conductors normally used to carry current shall be of:","o":["Copper-clad aluminum","Copper","Aluminum","Any suitable metal"],"a":1,"e":"The Code assumes copper conductors unless otherwise specified. If other materials are used, size must be changed accordingly.","r":"NEC 110.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"Conductor sizes in the NEC are expressed in:","o":["Standard wire gauge (SWG)","American Wire Gage (AWG) or in circular mils (kcmil)","Square inches only","Metric millimeters only"],"a":1,"e":"Conductor sizes are in AWG or kcmil, and sizes 4/0 and larger are in kcmil.","r":"NEC 110.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"The manufacturer's name, trademark, or other descriptive marking by which the responsible organization can be identified:","o":["Is required only on conductors","Must be placed on all electrical equipment","Is required only on equipment over 600 V","Is optional on listed equipment"],"a":1,"e":"Electrical equipment must have the manufacturer's identification, along with voltage, current, wattage, or other ratings as specified elsewhere.","r":"NEC 110.21(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Which enclosure type is suitable for outdoor use and provides protection against falling rain, sleet, and external ice formation?","o":["Type 12","Type 3R","Type 1","Type 2"],"a":1,"e":"Type 3R protects against falling rain, sleet, and external ice formation. Types 1 and 12 are for indoor use.","r":"NEC 110.28, Table 110.28","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Which enclosure type is designed for outdoor use and protects against corrosion, windblown dust and rain, splashing water, and hose-directed water?","o":["Type 4X","Type 3R","Type 12","Type 1"],"a":0,"e":"Type 4X is watertight, hosedown protected, and corrosion resistant. Type 3R is not hose protected.","r":"NEC 110.28, Table 110.28","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":3,"q":"A control enclosure is installed indoors in a machine shop where dust, falling dirt, and dripping noncorrosive liquids are present. Which enclosure type is suitable?","o":["Type 1","Type 3R","Type 12","Type 6P"],"a":2,"e":"Type 12 is for indoor use against dust, falling dirt, and dripping noncorrosive liquids. Type 1 protects only against falling dirt and incidental contact.","r":"NEC 110.28, Table 110.28","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":3,"q":"An enclosure that may be occasionally and prolongedly submerged in water must be:","o":["Type 4","Type 12","Type 3R","Type 6P"],"a":3,"e":"Type 6P is intended for prolonged submersion. Type 6 is for temporary submersion.","r":"NEC 110.28, Table 110.28","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Service equipment in other than dwelling units must be field marked with:","o":["The maximum available fault current and the date the calculation was performed","The voltage drop at the service","The utility company name","Only the nominal system voltage"],"a":0,"e":"Section 110.24 requires service equipment in other than dwelling units to be marked with maximum available fault current and the date of the calculation, with durable marking.","r":"NEC 110.24(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"When a disconnecting means is required to be lockable in the open position, the means of locking:","o":["Must remain in place with or without the lock installed","May be a removable hasp supplied by the installer","Must be installed only when a lock is used","Is required only on disconnects over 600 V"],"a":0,"e":"The provisions for locking must remain in place with or without the lock installed.","r":"NEC 110.25","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"Electrical equipment must be installed in a manner described by the NEC as:","o":["Painted to match the surroundings","Concealed whenever possible","Neat and workmanlike","Accessible only to the utility"],"a":2,"e":"Section 110.12 requires neat and workmanlike installation, including cables and raceways.","r":"NEC 110.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"Wiring methods permitted by the Code must be used only where:","o":["Approved by the utility","Suitable for the environment in which they are installed","Rated for 600 V or more","Concealed in walls"],"a":1,"e":"Wiring methods are to be used only where suitable for the environment, as noted in 110.8.","r":"NEC 110.8","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"The voltage rating of electrical equipment shall not be less than:","o":["The voltage to ground only","125 V in all cases","Half of the circuit voltage","The nominal voltage of the circuit to which it is connected"],"a":3,"e":"Equipment voltage rating must be at least the nominal circuit voltage.","r":"NEC 110.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"For equipment with enclosed live parts, the working space depth is measured from:","o":["The floor","The center of the equipment","The enclosure front or opening","The wall behind the equipment"],"a":2,"e":"Distance is measured from live parts if exposed, or from the enclosure front or opening if live parts are enclosed.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":3,"q":"Overcurrent protective devices, circuit impedance, and short-circuit current ratings of components must be selected so that:","o":["The ratings are matched to the load only","The device will clear a fault without extensive damage to the electrical equipment of the circuit","Only the main device needs a short-circuit rating","The device clears faults only after 3 seconds"],"a":1,"e":"Section 110.10 requires protection selected to clear short circuits and ground faults without extensive equipment damage.","r":"NEC 110.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"At least one entrance of sufficient area must be provided for:","o":["Utility personnel only","Emergency lighting","Moving large equipment only","Access to and egress from the working space about electrical equipment"],"a":3,"e":"Entrance and egress rules ensure that workers can reach and leave the working space safely.","r":"NEC 110.26(C)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Splices must be:","o":["Made only with tape","Made only with wire nuts","Made by twisting the wires together","Made with splicing devices identified for the use, or by brazing, welding, or soldering with a fusible metal"],"a":3,"e":"Conductors must be spliced with identified splicing devices or by brazing, welding, or soldering.","r":"NEC 110.14(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"Arc-flash hazard warning labels are NOT required under 110.16(A) on:","o":["Motor control centers in industrial plants","Dwelling unit panelboards","Industrial control panels","Commercial switchboards"],"a":1,"e":"The rule applies to equipment in other than dwelling units that is likely to be worked on energized.","r":"NEC 110.16(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":1,"q":"When examining equipment for approval, which of the following must be evaluated?","o":["The price of the equipment","The color of the enclosure","Mechanical strength and durability, including the adequacy of protection for enclosed parts","The brand's market share"],"a":2,"e":"Section 110.3(A) lists considerations such as mechanical strength and durability, wire-bending and connection space, electrical insulation, heating effects, arcing effects, and classification by type, size, voltage, current capacity, and use.","r":"NEC 110.3(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Working space is not required at the back or sides of a dead-front switchboard when:","o":["The switchboard is installed outdoors","The back is against a wall","The switchboard is rated under 1200 A","There are no renewable or adjustable parts at the back or sides and all connections are accessible from other locations"],"a":3,"e":"An exception to 110.26(A) removes the back and side working space requirement for such assemblies, provided there are no renewable or adjustable parts there and all connections can be reached from other locations.","r":"NEC 110.26(A) Exception No. 1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"A 14 AWG through 1 AWG conductor is terminated on equipment that is listed and identified for use with 75 degrees C conductors. A circuit of 60 A is supplied. The conductor ampacity may be based on:","o":["The 60 degrees C column only","The 75 degrees C column of Table 310.16","The 105 degrees C column","The 30 degrees C column"],"a":1,"e":"Where equipment is listed or marked for use with conductors rated 75 C, the 75 C column may be used even though the circuit is 100 A or less.","r":"NEC 110.14(C)(1)(a)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"Which of these is a standard rating for a branch circuit other than an individual branch circuit?","o":["35 A","25 A","45 A","30 A"],"a":3,"e":"Branch circuits other than individual circuits are rated 15, 20, 30, 40, or 50 A.","r":"NEC 210.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"All conductors of a multiwire branch circuit must:","o":["Be protected only by a fuse in the neutral","Originate from the same panelboard or similar distribution equipment","Be supplied by separate feeders","Originate from different panelboards on the same service"],"a":1,"e":"A multiwire branch circuit is considered as multiple circuits, but all its conductors must originate in the same panelboard or similar distribution equipment.","r":"NEC 210.4(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Each multiwire branch circuit must have a means that will:","o":["Disconnect only the neutral conductor","Disconnect one ungrounded conductor at a time","Open the equipment grounding conductor","Simultaneously disconnect all ungrounded conductors at the point where the branch circuit originates"],"a":3,"e":"A multiwire circuit requires simultaneous disconnection of all ungrounded conductors, typically with a 2-pole breaker or identified handle-tie.","r":"NEC 210.4(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"A multiwire branch circuit is permitted to supply:","o":["Only 240 V loads","Only line-to-neutral loads, with limited exceptions","Any combination of loads without restriction","Only line-to-line loads"],"a":1,"e":"Multiwire circuits supply only line-to-neutral loads, except where the circuit supplies only one piece of utilization equipment or all ungrounded conductors are opened simultaneously by the overcurrent device.","r":"NEC 210.4(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"The ungrounded and grounded conductors of each multiwire branch circuit must be grouped by cable ties or similar means:","o":["Only at the outlets","At every junction box","Only where the circuit is over 20 A","In at least one location within the panelboard or other point of origination"],"a":3,"e":"Grouping at the origination point helps ensure the neutral is kept with its associated ungrounded conductors.","r":"NEC 210.4(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Where a premises has branch circuits supplied from more than one nominal voltage system, the ungrounded conductors must be:","o":["Identified by phase or line and system at all termination, connection, and splice points, with the method documented or posted at each panelboard","Black only","Identified at the service only","Left unmarked if in separate raceways"],"a":0,"e":"Identification by phase or line and system is required at terminations, connections, and splices, and the method must be readily available or posted.","r":"NEC 210.5(C)(1)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Which load is permitted on a circuit exceeding 120 V but not exceeding 277 V nominal to ground?","o":["Listed electric-discharge or LED luminaires","Cord-and-plug loads up to 15 A in a dwelling","Portable lamps in guest rooms","Receptacle outlets in a dwelling unit"],"a":0,"e":"Circuits above 120 V and up to 277 V to ground may supply listed luminaires and certain utilization equipment, but dwelling unit voltage is limited to 120 V nominal between conductors for most loads.","r":"NEC 210.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"In a dwelling unit, which luminaire or load supply is limited to 120 V nominal between conductors?","o":["A 240 V electric range","A 240 V central air conditioner","Luminaires and cord-and-plug connected loads of 1440 VA or less or under 1/4 hp","A fixed electric water heater"],"a":2,"e":"In dwelling units, terminals of luminaires and small cord-and-plug loads are limited to 120 V nominal between conductors. Fixed appliances may use 240 V.","r":"NEC 210.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"A duplex receptacle has its two receptacles fed from different branch circuits. What is required?","o":["A means to simultaneously disconnect the ungrounded conductors supplying the devices on that yoke at the point the circuits originate","A GFCI breaker for each circuit","A separate neutral for each circuit","Nothing special"],"a":0,"e":"Where two or more branch circuits supply devices on the same yoke, a simultaneous disconnecting means is required at the origin.","r":"NEC 210.7","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"In a dwelling unit, 125 V receptacles in a bathroom require:","o":["No special protection","AFCI protection only","A 15 A dedicated circuit only","GFCI protection"],"a":3,"e":"All 125 V through 250 V receptacles in bathrooms of dwelling units require GFCI protection.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"A 125 V, 15 A receptacle is installed in the garage of a dwelling. It requires:","o":["GFCI protection","AFCI protection instead of GFCI","GFCI only if the garage floor is below grade","No protection if the garage is detached"],"a":0,"e":"Receptacles in dwelling garages require GFCI protection.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"A 125 V receptacle installed outdoors at a dwelling is required to have:","o":["A GFCI only if above 6.5 ft","AFCI protection only","Nothing special if on a covered porch","GFCI protection"],"a":3,"e":"Outdoor receptacles at dwelling units require GFCI protection.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"A finished basement in a dwelling has a 125 V, 15 A general-use receptacle. Is GFCI protection required?","o":["No, only unfinished basements require it","Only if the basement has a floor drain","Only if within 6 ft of a sink","Yes, all basement receptacles require it, finished or not"],"a":3,"e":"Dwelling unit basement receptacles require GFCI protection whether or not the space is finished.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(5)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"In a dwelling unit kitchen, which receptacles require GFCI protection?","o":["Only those within 3 ft of the refrigerator","Those serving the countertop surfaces","Only those on the small-appliance circuits over 15 A","None, as kitchens are exempt"],"a":1,"e":"Kitchen receptacles that serve countertop surfaces require GFCI protection.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(6)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"A utility sink in a dwelling laundry room has a 125 V receptacle 4 ft from the top inside edge of the sink bowl. GFCI is:","o":["Required","Required only if the sink is metal","Not required unless within 3 ft","Not required because the room is dry"],"a":0,"e":"Receptacles within 6 ft of the top inside edge of a sink bowl, other than in kitchens covered separately, require GFCI. Laundry areas also require GFCI.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(7), 210.8(A)(10)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"In a dwelling, a receptacle is installed 5 ft measured horizontally from the outside edge of a bathtub in a bedroom. GFCI is:","o":["Not required","Required","Required only in bathrooms","Required only within 3 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Receptacles within 6 ft horizontally of the outside edge of a bathtub or shower stall require GFCI protection.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(9)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"Dwelling unit laundry area 125 V receptacles require:","o":["GFCI only above 20 A","No protection","AFCI protection only","GFCI protection"],"a":3,"e":"Receptacles in dwelling unit laundry areas must be GFCI protected.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(10)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Receptacles in indoor damp or wet locations of a dwelling unit:","o":["Are exempt if installed above 6 ft","Require AFCI only","Require GFCI protection","Are exempt if on 20 A circuits"],"a":2,"e":"Indoor damp and wet locations require GFCI protection for 125 V through 250 V receptacles.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(11)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"In a dwelling unit, outlets that supply dishwashers:","o":["Are exempt if the dishwasher is hardwired","Require AFCI only","Require neither GFCI nor AFCI","Require GFCI protection"],"a":3,"e":"The Code requires GFCI protection for outlets that supply dishwashers installed in dwelling unit locations.","r":"NEC 210.8(D)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"A 125 V receptacle in the kitchen of a commercial restaurant requires:","o":["GFCI protection","GFCI only within 6 ft of a sink","AFCI protection","Nothing"],"a":0,"e":"In other than dwelling units, 125 V through 250 V receptacles in kitchens require GFCI protection.","r":"NEC 210.8(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"A receptacle on the rooftop of a commercial building requires:","o":["No protection","AFCI protection","GFCI protection","A weatherproof cover only"],"a":2,"e":"Rooftop receptacles in other than dwelling units require GFCI protection.","r":"NEC 210.8(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"In a commercial building, a receptacle in a service bay of a garage requires:","o":["GFCI protection","Nothing","A 30 A circuit only","AFCI protection"],"a":0,"e":"Receptacles in garages, service bays, and similar areas other than vehicle exhibition halls and showrooms require GFCI protection.","r":"NEC 210.8(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"A dwelling unit requires how many small-appliance branch circuits for the kitchen, pantry, breakfast, and dining areas?","o":["One 15 A circuit","Two or more 20 A circuits","Three 15 A circuits","One 20 A circuit"],"a":1,"e":"At least two 20 A small-appliance branch circuits are required for the receptacle outlets in those areas.","r":"NEC 210.11(C)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"A dwelling unit requires at least one additional branch circuit for the laundry receptacle outlet(s). Its rating is:","o":["30 A","20 A","40 A","15 A"],"a":1,"e":"A separate 20 A laundry branch circuit is required, with no other outlets.","r":"NEC 210.11(C)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"At least one 120 V, 20 A branch circuit must be provided to supply bathroom receptacle outlets. Such circuits:","o":["Shall have no other outlets, with an exception for outlets for other equipment within the same single bathroom","May supply lighting in other rooms","Must be 15 A","May supply bedroom outlets"],"a":0,"e":"Bathroom branch circuits can serve only the bathroom, though outlets for other equipment in the same bathroom may be supplied in a single-bathroom arrangement.","r":"NEC 210.11(C)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"A 120 V, 20 A branch circuit for garage receptacle outlets in an attached garage may supply:","o":["Kitchen receptacles","The laundry receptacle","No other outlets, except readily accessible outdoor receptacle outlets","The garage lighting and bathroom receptacles"],"a":2,"e":"The garage branch circuit has no other outlets, with an exception for readily accessible outdoor receptacle outlets.","r":"NEC 210.11(C)(4)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"In a dwelling unit, which circuit requires AFCI protection?","o":["A 120 V, 15 A circuit supplying outlets in a bedroom","A 120 V, 20 A circuit supplying only a bathroom","A 240 V range circuit","A 120 V circuit supplying only a garage"],"a":0,"e":"AFCI protection applies to 120 V, single-phase, 15 and 20 A circuits supplying outlets or devices in the listed living areas, which include bedrooms.","r":"NEC 210.12(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Which dwelling unit room is NOT listed in 210.12(A) as requiring AFCI protection?","o":["Closet","Hallway","Bathroom","Laundry area"],"a":2,"e":"The listed areas include kitchens, family rooms, dining rooms, living rooms, bedrooms, closets, hallways, laundry areas, and similar areas, but not bathrooms.","r":"NEC 210.12(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Do guest rooms and guest suites of hotels and motels require AFCI protection on 120 V, 15 and 20 A branch circuits supplying outlets and devices?","o":["Yes","Only the bathrooms","Only if over 1000 sq ft","No, only dwelling units require it"],"a":0,"e":"AFCI protection also covers dormitory units and guest rooms and suites.","r":"NEC 210.12(B), (C)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"The minimum branch-circuit conductor ampacity for a circuit supplying both continuous and noncontinuous loads is:","o":["100% of both loads","125% of the continuous load plus 100% of the noncontinuous load","80% of both loads","125% of both loads"],"a":1,"e":"Before adjustment or correction factors, the minimum ampacity is the noncontinuous load plus 125% of the continuous load.","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"A branch circuit carries a 16 A continuous load and a 4 A noncontinuous load. What is the minimum conductor allowable ampacity before adjustment or correction factors?","o":["20 A","25 A","26 A","24 A"],"a":3,"e":"(16 x 1.25) + 4 = 24 A.","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"For a household range rated 8-3/4 kW or more, the minimum branch-circuit rating is:","o":["40 A","50 A","60 A","30 A"],"a":0,"e":"Ranges of 8-3/4 kW or more require a branch circuit rated at least 40 A.","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"A branch circuit supplies a continuous load of 16 A and a noncontinuous load of 4 A. Using a standard (not 100% rated) overcurrent device, what is the minimum calculated OCPD rating the load requires?","o":["20 A","30 A","16 A","24 A"],"a":3,"e":"The OCPD rating must be at least the noncontinuous load plus 125% of the continuous load: 4 + (16 x 1.25) = 24 A.","r":"NEC 210.20(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"An OCPD that is listed for operation at 100% of its rating may supply a continuous load up to:","o":["100% of the device rating","125% of the device rating","90% of the device rating","80% of the device rating"],"a":0,"e":"An assembly including the OCPD listed for 100% operation is an exception to the 125% rule.","r":"NEC 210.20(A) Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"On a branch circuit rated above 20 A, lampholders must be:","o":["Mogul type only","Any type","Heavy-duty type","Medium base type only"],"a":2,"e":"A heavy-duty lampholder is rated at least 660 W if admedium, or 750 W if any other type.","r":"NEC 210.21(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"A single receptacle on an individual branch circuit must have an ampere rating:","o":["Not more than that of the branch circuit","Of exactly 15 A","Of half that of the branch circuit","Not less than that of the branch circuit"],"a":3,"e":"A single receptacle on an individual circuit must be rated at least the circuit rating.","r":"NEC 210.21(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"A receptacle on a circuit with two or more receptacles has a 20 A rating. The total cord-and-plug connected load must not exceed:","o":["16 A","20 A","12 A","18 A"],"a":0,"e":"The total cord-and-plug load may not exceed 80% of the receptacle rating, which is 16 A for 20 A.","r":"NEC 210.21(B)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"On a 20 A branch circuit supplying two or more receptacles, which receptacle ratings are permitted?","o":["15 A only","30 A","20 A only","15 A or 20 A"],"a":3,"e":"Table 210.21(B)(3) allows 15 or 20 A receptacles on a 20 A circuit.","r":"NEC 210.21(B)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"A 30 A branch circuit supplying two or more receptacles requires receptacles rated:","o":["15 A","50 A","20 A","30 A"],"a":3,"e":"Table 210.21(B)(3) requires 30 A receptacles on 30 A circuits.","r":"NEC 210.21(B)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"On a 20 A branch circuit supplying lighting and cord-and-plug connected equipment, fastened-in-place utilization equipment other than luminaires must not exceed:","o":["100% of the rating","25% of the rating","80% of the rating","50% of the branch-circuit ampere rating"],"a":3,"e":"Where lighting or non-fastened equipment is also supplied, fastened-in-place equipment is limited to 50% of the circuit rating.","r":"NEC 210.23(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"On a 30 A branch circuit, the rating of any one cord-and-plug connected utilization equipment must not exceed:","o":["27 A","20 A","24 A","30 A"],"a":2,"e":"Cord-and-plug equipment on a 30 A circuit is limited to 80% of the rating, which is 24 A.","r":"NEC 210.23(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"40 A and 50 A branch circuits may supply which of the following in any occupancy?","o":["Cooking appliances","Heavy-duty lampholders in dwelling units","Receptacle outlets for general lighting","Portable lamps"],"a":0,"e":"Cooking appliances in any occupancy are permitted on 40 or 50 A circuits.","r":"NEC 210.23(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Branch circuits larger than 50 A may supply:","o":["Only non-lighting outlet loads","Lighting only","Both lighting and receptacles","Heavy-duty lampholders"],"a":0,"e":"Circuits larger than 50 A supply only non-lighting outlet loads.","r":"NEC 210.23(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum copper conductor size for a 30 A branch circuit per Table 210.24?","o":["14 AWG","10 AWG","8 AWG","12 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Table 210.24 lists 10 AWG for 30 A, 12 AWG for 20 A, and 14 AWG for 15 A.","r":"NEC 210.24, Table 210.24","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Branch circuits supplying a dwelling unit within a multifamily building shall supply:","o":["Only loads within that dwelling unit or loads associated only with that dwelling unit","The hallway lights","Adjacent dwelling units","Common area loads"],"a":0,"e":"Dwelling unit circuits may not be shared with other units or common areas.","r":"NEC 210.25(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Branch circuits for common-area lighting in a multifamily building:","o":["May be supplied from any dwelling unit panel","Must be 15 A","Shall not be supplied from equipment that supplies an individual dwelling unit","Must be supplied from the first unit's panel"],"a":2,"e":"Common area circuits must not originate in a dwelling unit panel.","r":"NEC 210.25(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"In dwelling unit living areas, receptacles must be installed so no point along the floor line of any wall space is more than:","o":["4 ft","8 ft","10 ft","6 ft from a receptacle outlet"],"a":3,"e":"The 6 ft rule results in receptacles spaced no more than 12 ft apart.","r":"NEC 210.52(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"In a dwelling unit living room, a wall space must have a receptacle if it is:","o":["1 ft or more","6 ft or more","2 ft or more in width and unbroken by doorways and similar openings","4 ft or more in width"],"a":2,"e":"Any wall space 2 ft or more wide, measured around corners, must have a receptacle.","r":"NEC 210.52(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"A floor receptacle may count toward the wall-space receptacle requirement only if it is within:","o":["3 ft of the wall","6 ft of the wall","18 in. of the wall","6 in. of the wall"],"a":2,"e":"Floor receptacles count only if located within 18 in. of the wall.","r":"NEC 210.52(A)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"The kitchen small-appliance branch circuits serve receptacles in which areas?","o":["Bathrooms","Bedrooms","Kitchen, pantry, breakfast room, and dining room","Garage"],"a":2,"e":"Small-appliance branch circuits serve those areas.","r":"NEC 210.52(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Which of these may be connected to a small-appliance branch circuit?","o":["A garage receptacle","A bathroom receptacle","A receptacle for an electric clock","A laundry receptacle"],"a":2,"e":"Small-appliance circuits can supply only specified loads, such as a clock receptacle or gas range ignition.","r":"NEC 210.52(B)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"The receptacle outlet for refrigeration equipment in a dwelling kitchen is permitted to be supplied from:","o":["A bathroom circuit","An individual 15 A or larger branch circuit","Only the 20 A small-appliance circuit","A 30 A circuit"],"a":1,"e":"An exception allows the refrigerator receptacle on an individual 15 A or larger branch circuit.","r":"NEC 210.52(B)(1) Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"A dwelling unit kitchen wall countertop space must have a receptacle outlet if it is at least:","o":["24 in. wide","6 in. wide","12 in. wide","18 in. wide"],"a":2,"e":"Each wall countertop space 12 in. or wider requires a receptacle.","r":"NEC 210.52(C)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Receptacles along a dwelling kitchen wall countertop must be placed so no point along the wall line is more than:","o":["24 in. from a receptacle","36 in.","48 in.","12 in."],"a":0,"e":"No point along the wall line may be more than 24 in. horizontally from a receptacle in that space.","r":"NEC 210.52(C)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Kitchen countertop receptacles must be installed on or above the countertop but not more than:","o":["30 in.","20 in. above the countertop","12 in.","18 in."],"a":1,"e":"Receptacle outlets are to be on or above, not more than 20 in. above, the countertop.","r":"NEC 210.52(C)(5)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"In a dwelling bathroom, a receptacle outlet must be placed within what distance of the outside edge of each basin?","o":["6 ft","3 ft","1 ft","5 ft"],"a":1,"e":"At least one receptacle must be within 3 ft of the outside edge of each basin.","r":"NEC 210.52(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"One-family dwellings must have outdoor receptacles at:","o":["The back only","The front and the back, accessible from grade and not more than 6.5 ft above grade","Any location above 8 ft","The front only"],"a":1,"e":"At least one front and one back receptacle, accessible at grade, not over 6-1/2 ft above grade.","r":"NEC 210.52(E)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"In dwelling units, a laundry area must have:","o":["At least one receptacle outlet","A receptacle only if a dryer is present","No receptacle","Two receptacles"],"a":0,"e":"At least one receptacle outlet is required in the laundry area.","r":"NEC 210.52(F)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Dwelling basements require:","o":["Two receptacles per wall","A receptacle only in finished areas","At least one receptacle in each separate unfinished portion","No receptacle"],"a":2,"e":"At least one receptacle is required in each basement, and in each unfinished portion.","r":"NEC 210.52(G)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"In an attached dwelling garage, each vehicle bay requires at least one receptacle located not more than:","o":["7 ft","5.5 ft above the floor","4 ft","6.5 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Garage receptacles are installed in each vehicle bay and not more than 5-1/2 ft above the floor.","r":"NEC 210.52(G)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"A dwelling hallway requires a receptacle if it is at least:","o":["15 ft long","20 ft long","10 ft long","6 ft long"],"a":2,"e":"Hallways 10 ft or more in length require at least one receptacle.","r":"NEC 210.52(H)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"A dwelling foyer requires receptacle outlets if its area is greater than:","o":["30 sq ft","120 sq ft","100 sq ft","60 sq ft"],"a":3,"e":"Foyers over 60 sq ft that are not part of a hallway need receptacles in wall spaces 3 ft or more wide.","r":"NEC 210.52(I)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"A receptacle outlet for servicing heating or air-conditioning equipment must be located:","o":["Within 50 ft","Within 6 ft","On the load side of the equipment disconnect","On the same level and within 25 ft of the equipment"],"a":3,"e":"The 125 V receptacle must be on the same level, within 25 ft, and not on the load side of the equipment disconnect.","r":"NEC 210.63(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"In a commercial building, indoor service equipment requires a 125 V receptacle located within:","o":["10 ft","100 ft","25 ft","50 ft, in the same room or area"],"a":3,"e":"A 125 V, 15 or 20 A receptacle must be within 50 ft of indoor electrical service equipment.","r":"NEC 210.64","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"In dwelling units, every habitable room and bathroom requires:","o":["Two lighting outlets","A pull-chain fixture","A fixture with no switch","At least one wall switch-controlled lighting outlet"],"a":3,"e":"Each habitable room and bathroom requires at least one wall switch-controlled lighting outlet.","r":"NEC 210.70(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"In a dwelling habitable room other than a kitchen or bathroom, the required lighting outlet may be replaced by:","o":["One or more wall switch-controlled receptacles","A night light","A pull-chain fixture","A lighted exit sign"],"a":0,"e":"An exception allows switched receptacles in lieu of lighting outlets in habitable rooms other than kitchens and bathrooms.","r":"NEC 210.70(A)(1) Exception 1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Hallways, stairways, and attached garages of dwellings require:","o":["Pull-chain luminaires","Emergency lighting","At least one wall switch-controlled lighting outlet","No lighting"],"a":2,"e":"A wall switch-controlled lighting outlet is required in these locations.","r":"NEC 210.70(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"An interior dwelling stairway has six risers between floor levels. The lighting must be controlled by:","o":["A single switch at the bottom","A switch in the attic","No switch needed","A wall switch at each floor level that has an entryway"],"a":3,"e":"Stairways with six risers or more require a switch at each floor level and landing level that includes an entryway.","r":"NEC 210.70(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Outdoor entrances or exits of a dwelling that provide access to grade level require:","o":["A receptacle only","A wall switch-controlled lighting outlet","No lighting","A timer only"],"a":1,"e":"A switched lighting outlet is required at outdoor entrances with grade-level access, excluding vehicle doors.","r":"NEC 210.70(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"A dwelling attic used for storage or containing equipment requiring servicing requires:","o":["At least one lighting outlet, with a switch at the usual point of entry","Emergency lighting","A receptacle only","No lighting"],"a":0,"e":"Attics, underfloor spaces, utility rooms, and basements used for storage or containing equipment requiring servicing need a lighting outlet with a switch at the usual point of entry.","r":"NEC 210.70(A)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"In other than dwelling units, attic and underfloor spaces, utility rooms, and basements that are used for storage or contain equipment requiring servicing need:","o":["Two luminaires","No lighting","At least one lighting outlet with a switch at the usual point of entry","A receptacle only"],"a":2,"e":"A lighting outlet is required with at least one point of control at the usual point of entry.","r":"NEC 210.70(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"GFCI protection in dwelling units applies to receptacles rated:","o":["Only 125 V","125 V through 250 V","Over 250 V","Only 240 V"],"a":1,"e":"Section 210.8(A) covers 125 V through 250 V receptacles in the listed locations.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"A 120/240 V multiwire circuit supplies a 240 V load and a 120 V load from the same two-pole breaker. Is this permitted?","o":["Only if the neutral is oversized","No, never","Only with a handle tie","Yes, because the OCPD opens all ungrounded conductors simultaneously"],"a":3,"e":"A multiwire circuit may supply line-to-line loads if all ungrounded conductors are opened simultaneously by the OCPD.","r":"NEC 210.4(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Which type of device can provide AFCI protection at the origin of a dwelling branch circuit?","o":["A standard thermal-magnetic breaker","A GFCI receptacle","A surge protective device","A listed combination-type AFCI circuit breaker"],"a":3,"e":"A listed combination-type AFCI circuit breaker is one accepted method.","r":"NEC 210.12(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Wall space for the 210.52(A) receptacle rule includes:","o":["Fixed room dividers such as freestanding bar-type counters","Only doorways","Windows only","Fireplaces"],"a":0,"e":"Wall space includes fixed panels and fixed room dividers like freestanding bar-type counters.","r":"NEC 210.52(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"How many 20 A small-appliance circuits does the kitchen require at a minimum?","o":["Four","Three","One","Two"],"a":3,"e":"At least two 20 A small-appliance branch circuits are required.","r":"NEC 210.52(B)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"Branch circuit loads must be:","o":["Placed on the service only","Concentrated on one circuit","Evenly proportioned among branch circuits","Always on 15 A"],"a":2,"e":"Loads are evenly proportioned among multiple branch circuits within the panelboard.","r":"NEC 210.11(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"In a dwelling unit, 125 V receptacles installed in crawl spaces at or below grade level require:","o":["GFCI only if the crawl space is damp","No protection","GFCI protection","AFCI protection only"],"a":2,"e":"Crawl spaces at or below grade level in dwelling units are listed locations that require GFCI protection.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(4)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"A 40 A branch circuit supplies two or more receptacles. Which receptacle ratings are permitted?","o":["15 A or 20 A","30 A only","40 A or 50 A","60 A"],"a":2,"e":"Table 210.21(B)(3) allows 40 or 50 A receptacles on a 40 A circuit.","r":"NEC 210.21(B)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":1,"q":"Article 518 applies to buildings or portions of buildings designed or intended for the gathering of at least how many persons?","o":["300","100","1000","50"],"a":1,"e":"Section 518.2 applies Article 518 to assembly occupancies intended for the gathering of 100 or more persons.","r":"NEC 518.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is listed in Article 518 as an example of an assembly occupancy?","o":["Single-family dwelling","Warehouse with 10 employees","Detached private garage","Place of religious worship"],"a":3,"e":"518.2(A) lists examples such as churches and other places of religious worship, dance halls, gymnasiums, museums, skating rinks, and similar spaces.","r":"NEC 518.2(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":2,"q":"A building has a 40-person office area and a separate banquet hall designed for 400 persons. How does Article 518 apply?","o":["It applies only if the banquet hall is on the ground floor","It applies to the entire building","It does not apply because the building is mixed use","It applies only to the portion of the building with the assembly use"],"a":3,"e":"Where a building has multiple occupancies, Article 518 applies only to the portion of the building that is an assembly occupancy.","r":"NEC 518.2(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":2,"q":"In an assembly occupancy required by the building code to be of fire-rated construction, which fixed wiring method is permitted under 518.4(A)?","o":["Nonmetallic raceway encased in at least 2 in. of concrete","Type UF cable in walls","Type NM cable in concealed spaces","Open wiring on insulators"],"a":0,"e":"518.4(A) permits metal raceways, Type MC or AC cable, and nonmetallic raceways encased in at least 50 mm (2 in.) of concrete. NM and UF cable are not permitted in rated construction.","r":"NEC 518.4(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":1,"q":"Which cable type is among the fixed wiring methods permitted in assembly occupancies of fire-rated construction?","o":["Type MC","Type NM","Type UF","Type NMC"],"a":0,"e":"Type MC (and AC) cable and metal raceways are permitted by 518.4(A); the nonmetallic-sheathed types are not.","r":"NEC 518.4(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":2,"q":"An assembly occupancy is in a building that the building code does not require to be of fire-rated construction. Which additional wiring method is permitted by 518.4(B)?","o":["Flat conductor cable in the audience area","Surface nonmetallic extensions in all areas","Knob-and-tube wiring","Nonmetallic-sheathed cable"],"a":3,"e":"In nonrated construction, 518.4(B) permits Types NM, NMC and NMS cable and ENT in addition to the 518.4(A) methods.","r":"NEC 518.4(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum time allowed for an emergency system to restore power to emergency loads after normal power fails?","o":["30 seconds","60 seconds","10 seconds","5 seconds"],"a":2,"e":"Emergency systems must have power available within 10 seconds. The 60-second figure applies to legally required standby systems.","r":"NEC 700.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":2,"q":"A legally required standby system serves a community center assembly hall. Within what time must it supply power to the load after loss of normal power?","o":["10 seconds","60 seconds","30 seconds","5 minutes"],"a":1,"e":"701.12 requires legally required standby power to be available within 60 seconds. The 10-second limit applies to emergency systems.","r":"NEC 701.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":2,"q":"Storage battery unit equipment is used for emergency egress lighting in an assembly occupancy. For what minimum duration must it supply the required load?","o":["90 minutes","60 minutes","30 minutes","4 hours"],"a":0,"e":"700.12(F) requires unit equipment batteries to supply the load for at least 1-1/2 hours (90 minutes) to not less than 87-1/2 percent of nominal battery voltage.","r":"NEC 700.12(F)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":3,"q":"An assembly occupancy is designed for 1200 persons and has an emergency system. What is required for emergency circuit feeder wiring under 700.10(D)?","o":["Installation in a fully sprinklered space or use of a listed electrical circuit protective system with at least a 1-hour fire rating","Installation only in surface raceway","Installation only in Schedule 40 PVC","No special protection is required"],"a":0,"e":"700.10(D)(1) applies to assembly occupancies for not less than 1000 persons; feeders must be in areas protected by automatic fire suppression or be a listed circuit protective system with a 1-hour rating.","r":"NEC 700.10(D)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":2,"q":"A 125-volt, 20-ampere receptacle is installed in the commercial kitchen of a banquet hall. What protection is required?","o":["A dedicated neutral only","AFCI protection only","GFCI protection for personnel","No special protection"],"a":2,"e":"210.8(B) requires GFCI protection for personnel for receptacles in kitchens of nondwelling occupancies.","r":"NEC 210.8(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":2,"q":"A 125-volt receptacle is installed in an outdoor patio area of a restaurant-banquet facility. Which statement is correct?","o":["It requires AFCI protection instead of GFCI","It needs no protection if it is above 6 ft","It requires GFCI protection","It requires only a weatherproof cover"],"a":2,"e":"210.8(B) requires GFCI protection for receptacles installed outdoors in other than dwelling occupancies.","r":"NEC 210.8(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum headroom for working space about an electrical panelboard in the electrical room of a large assembly hall?","o":["6-1/2 ft","7 ft","6 ft","8 ft"],"a":0,"e":"110.26(A)(3) requires working space headroom of at least 2.0 m (6-1/2 ft), or the height of the equipment if greater.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":2,"q":"A 480-volt, 2000-ampere switchboard in an arena is 7 ft wide and has working space containing the equipment. What must be provided at each end of the working space?","o":["A personnel door or doors with panic hardware opening in the direction of egress","Nothing, a single exit suffices","A locked access door only","A removable panel"],"a":0,"e":"110.26(C)(3) requires one entrance at each end of the working space for equipment rated 1200 A or more and over 6 ft wide, with doors that open in the direction of egress and have listed panic hardware.","r":"NEC 110.26(C)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":2,"q":"Which type of transfer equipment is required for an emergency system serving an assembly occupancy?","o":["A nonautomatic transfer switch only","Any general-use manual switch","A plug-and-cord connection","Automatic transfer equipment listed for emergency use"],"a":3,"e":"700.5 requires transfer equipment for emergency systems to be automatic, identified for emergency use and approved by the AHJ.","r":"NEC 700.5","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":2,"q":"Emergency circuit wiring must be kept entirely independent of other wiring. Which describes this requirement under 700.10(B)?","o":["Emergency circuits may share enclosures with any other circuit","Emergency circuits may share raceways with normal circuits","Emergency circuits must be 12 AWG minimum","Emergency circuit wiring is kept entirely independent of all other wiring and equipment, with limited exceptions"],"a":3,"e":"700.10(B) requires emergency circuit wiring to be kept entirely independent of all other wiring and equipment, with specific exceptions such as in transfer equipment enclosures and exit or emergency luminaires supplied from two sources.","r":"NEC 700.10(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":1,"q":"What must be provided at the service equipment of a building with an emergency system, indicating the type and location of the on-site emergency power sources?","o":["A tag with the installer name","Nothing is required","A sign","A warning bell"],"a":2,"e":"700.7(A) requires a sign at the service-entrance equipment indicating the type and location of on-site emergency power sources.","r":"NEC 700.7(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":3,"q":"A new church (assembly occupancy for 600 persons, Type I fire-rated construction) wants to run branch circuits above a ceiling. Which wiring is acceptable?","o":["Type UF cable","Type NM cable in the ceiling space","EMT with insulated conductors","Open conductors on cleats"],"a":2,"e":"In fire-rated construction, 518.4(A) allows metal raceways such as EMT; NM, UF and open wiring are not permitted.","r":"NEC 518.4(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":3,"q":"A gymnasium is designed to hold 90 persons. Under 518.2, how does Article 518 apply?","o":["It applies only if it has a stage","It applies because it is a gymnasium","It applies only if it has bleachers","It does not apply because the occupancy is under 100 persons"],"a":3,"e":"Article 518 applies to assembly occupancies designed for 100 or more persons; the general NEC rules apply to smaller occupancies.","r":"NEC 518.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":2,"q":"Where an assembly occupancy contains a theater stage, which additional article provides requirements for the stage and audience area?","o":["Article 520","Article 547","Article 530","Article 555"],"a":0,"e":"Article 520 covers theaters, audience areas and similar locations, and applies in addition to Article 518.","r":"NEC 518.3, 520.1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":2,"q":"In which type of building are wiring methods in 518.4(A) most strictly limited to metal raceways, MC and AC cable, and encased nonmetallic raceways?","o":["Temporary tents with fewer than 100 persons","Detached sheds","Buildings required to be of fire-rated construction by the building code","Single-family dwellings"],"a":2,"e":"The 518.4(A) list applies to fire-rated construction; 518.4(B) adds nonmetallic cable types only for nonrated construction.","r":"NEC 518.4","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":1,"q":"Article 520 covers electrical installations in which of the following?","o":["Agricultural barns","Marinas and boatyards","Theaters, audience areas of motion picture and television studios, and similar locations","Recreational vehicle parks"],"a":2,"e":"520.1 applies Article 520 to buildings or parts of buildings used as theaters, audience areas of motion picture and TV studios, performance areas, and similar locations, both indoors and outdoors.","r":"NEC 520.1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":1,"q":"Motion picture projectors and associated equipment installed in a theater are covered by which article?","o":["Article 600","Article 518","Article 540","Article 530"],"a":2,"e":"520.3 states that motion picture projectors and enclosures are covered by Article 540.","r":"NEC 520.3, 540","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":2,"q":"A 500-seat theater is built of construction required by the building code to be fire-rated. Which fixed wiring method is permitted by 520.5?","o":["Type NM cable in the walls","Type UF cable concealed in ceilings","Open wiring on insulators","Nonmetallic raceway encased in at least 2 in. of concrete"],"a":3,"e":"520.5 permits metal raceways, Type MC and AC cable, and nonmetallic raceways encased in at least 50 mm (2 in.) of concrete in fire-rated construction. NM, UF and open wiring are not permitted there.","r":"NEC 520.5","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":2,"q":"In a small theater building that the building code does not require to be of fire-rated construction, which additional fixed wiring method becomes permitted?","o":["Type NM cable and ENT","Surface metal raceway only","Flat conductor cable under carpet","Open knob-and-tube wiring"],"a":0,"e":"For nonrated construction 520.5 allows Types NM, NMC and NMS cable and electrical nonmetallic tubing in addition to the standard fire-rated methods.","r":"NEC 520.5","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":2,"q":"The emergency lighting for the audience area of a 900-seat theater is supplied by an emergency system generator. After a normal power failure, within what time must power be available to the emergency loads?","o":["2 minutes","60 seconds","10 seconds","30 seconds"],"a":2,"e":"700.12 requires emergency systems to supply power within 10 seconds. 60 seconds applies to legally required standby systems under 701.12.","r":"NEC 700.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":2,"q":"A dressing room in a theater has a 125-volt, 20-ampere receptacle 4 ft from a makeup sink. What protection does 210.8(B) require?","o":["GFCI protection for personnel","A tamper-resistant cover only","AFCI protection only","No protection because it is a dressing room"],"a":0,"e":"210.8(B) requires GFCI protection for receptacles within 6 ft of the inside edge of a sink in nondwelling occupancies other than kitchens.","r":"NEC 210.8(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":3,"q":"A theater is designed for 1500 persons and has an emergency system. How must the emergency feeder-circuit wiring be protected?","o":["It requires no special protection","It must be 2-hour rated MI cable only","It must be installed only in nonmetallic raceway","It must be in a fully fire-suppressed area or be a listed electrical circuit protective system with at least a 1-hour fire rating"],"a":3,"e":"700.10(D)(1) applies to assembly occupancies for not less than 1000 persons: feeders must be in spaces protected by automatic fire suppression or be a listed circuit protective system with a 1-hour rating.","r":"NEC 700.10(D)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":1,"q":"Article 525 applies to the installation of which of the following?","o":["Wiring in motion picture studios","Portable wiring and equipment for carnivals, circuses, fairs and similar events","Permanent wiring in a bowling alley","Wiring of fixed grandstands only"],"a":1,"e":"525.1 covers portable wiring and equipment for carnivals, fairs, circuses, traveling attractions and similar events, including the wiring of rides and concessions.","r":"NEC 525.1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":2,"q":"At a county fair, a ride is to be set up near overhead conductors operating at 480 volts that do not supply the ride. What is the minimum horizontal clearance to the ride?","o":["15 ft","25 ft","10 ft","5 ft"],"a":0,"e":"525.5(B) prohibits installing amusement rides or attractions under or within 4.5 m (15 ft) horizontally of overhead conductors operating at 600 volts or less, other than the conductors supplying the ride.","r":"NEC 525.5(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":1,"q":"Electrical equipment and wiring methods on rides and concessions that are subject to physical damage must be provided with what?","o":["Arc-fault protection","Mechanical protection","A red warning label only","Surge protective devices"],"a":1,"e":"525.6 requires mechanical protection where equipment or wiring methods on rides, concessions or other units are subject to physical damage.","r":"NEC 525.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":2,"q":"Service equipment installed at a carnival in a location accessible to unqualified persons must be what?","o":["Installed 12 ft above ground","Painted yellow","Lockable","Protected by a fence at least 8 ft high"],"a":2,"e":"525.10(A) says service equipment must not be installed in a location accessible to unqualified persons unless the equipment is lockable.","r":"NEC 525.10(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":3,"q":"A portable structure at a fair is supplied from two separate generators. The equipment grounding conductors of the sources must be bonded together at the structure. What is the minimum size of this copper bonding conductor?","o":["8 AWG","6 AWG, or larger per 250.122 for the largest overcurrent device","10 AWG","4 AWG always"],"a":1,"e":"525.11 requires a copper bonding conductor sized per 250.122 for the largest overcurrent device supplying the structure, but not smaller than 6 AWG.","r":"NEC 525.11","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":1,"q":"What is the smallest size single-conductor cable permitted for portable wiring at a carnival under 525.20?","o":["8 AWG","2 AWG","12 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"525.20(B) permits single-conductor cable only in sizes 2 AWG or larger.","r":"NEC 525.20(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":2,"q":"Which statement about open conductors at a carnival is correct?","o":["They are permitted if the circuit is GFCI protected","They are permitted only as part of listed festoon lighting","They are permitted if supported every 4 ft","They are permitted for rides below 120 volts"],"a":1,"e":"525.20(C) prohibits open conductors except as part of a listed assembly or festoon lighting installed per Article 225.","r":"NEC 525.20(C)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":2,"q":"Flexible cords and cables used to feed a ride at an outdoor fair must be continuous. Which statement is correct?","o":["Splices are allowed in the middle of a run if taped","Splices are allowed with wire nuts","Splices are allowed if over 10 ft above ground","Flexible cords and cables must be continuous without splices or taps between boxes or fittings"],"a":3,"e":"525.20(D) requires flexible cords and cables to be continuous without splices or taps between boxes or fittings.","r":"NEC 525.20(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":2,"q":"Where must the fused disconnect or circuit breaker for a ride be located?","o":["At the generator only","Inside the ride's control cabinet only","At least 50 ft from the ride","Within sight and within 6 ft of the operator's station"],"a":3,"e":"525.21(A) requires a fused disconnect or circuit breaker for rides and concessions located within sight and within 1.8 m (6 ft) of the operator's station and readily accessible to the operator.","r":"NEC 525.21(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":2,"q":"A 125-volt, 20-ampere receptacle at a fair midway is readily accessible to the general public. What protection does 525.23 require?","o":["A lockable cover only","None if the cord is extra-hard usage","GFCI protection for personnel","Arc-fault protection only"],"a":2,"e":"525.23 requires GFCI protection for personnel for 125-volt, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles in areas accessible to the general public and for equipment readily accessible to the public.","r":"NEC 525.23","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":2,"q":"When must the continuity of the equipment grounding conductor be verified for a portable carnival installation?","o":["Only when the AHJ requests","Once per year","Only after a ground fault","Each time the installation is set up or connected"],"a":3,"e":"525.32 requires the continuity of the equipment grounding conductor to be verified each time the portable installation is connected.","r":"NEC 525.32","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 530 Motion Picture and Television Studios","d":1,"q":"Article 530 covers wiring and equipment in which of the following?","o":["Mobile home parks","Motion picture and television studios and similar locations where film or video is produced","Boat docks","Theater audience seating only"],"a":1,"e":"530.1 applies to motion picture and television studios and similar locations employing either stationary or portable equipment.","r":"NEC 530.1","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 530 Motion Picture and Television Studios","d":2,"q":"Article 520 covers the audience areas of motion picture and television studios. Which article covers the production areas (stages and sets) of those studios?","o":["Article 525","Article 520 only","Article 540 only","Article 530"],"a":3,"e":"Article 530 covers the production and stage areas of motion picture and TV studios, while Article 520 covers the audience areas.","r":"NEC 530.1, 520.1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":1,"q":"A mobile home power-supply cord is rated at how many amperes?","o":["50 amperes","30 amperes","40 amperes","100 amperes"],"a":0,"e":"550.10(A) allows a feeder assembly consisting of not more than one listed 50-ampere mobile home power-supply cord with a molded attachment plug.","r":"NEC 550.10(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":2,"q":"What attachment plug configuration must be on a 50-ampere mobile home power-supply cord?","o":["NEMA 5-20P","NEMA 10-30P","NEMA 14-50P, 125/250-volt, 3-pole, 4-wire grounding type","NEMA TT-30P"],"a":2,"e":"550.10(B) requires a 50-ampere, 125/250-volt, 3-pole, 4-wire grounding-type attachment plug (14-50P).","r":"NEC 550.10(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":3,"q":"In a mobile home panelboard, what must be done with the main bonding jumper or neutral bonding screw?","o":["It must be replaced with a 6 AWG wire","It may be left in place","It must be installed","It must be removed because the grounded conductor is insulated from the equipment grounding conductors and enclosures"],"a":3,"e":"550.16(A)(2) requires the grounded circuit conductor to be insulated from the EGC and enclosures, and bonding screws, straps or buses in the panelboard and appliances to be removed and discarded.","r":"NEC 550.16(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":2,"q":"In a mobile home load calculation, the general lighting load is figured at how many volt-amperes per square foot, based on outside dimensions?","o":["3","2","5","1"],"a":0,"e":"550.18(B)(1) computes lighting at 3 VA per square foot using the outside dimensions of the mobile home, excluding hitches, porches and the like.","r":"NEC 550.18(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":3,"q":"A mobile home has 5 fixed appliances (not counting heating or air conditioning) with nameplate loads totaling 12,000 VA. What demand load applies to them under 550.18(B)?","o":["12,000 VA at 100 percent","8,400 VA, which is 70 percent","6,000 VA, which is 50 percent","9,000 VA, which is 75 percent"],"a":3,"e":"For four or more fixed appliances other than electric ranges, dryers, heating and air conditioning, a 75 percent demand factor applies.","r":"NEC 550.18(B)(6)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":2,"q":"If a mobile home has a laundry area, what branch circuit must be provided?","o":["No dedicated circuit","A 30-ampere 120-volt circuit","A 20-ampere branch circuit for the laundry receptacle outlet(s)","A 15-ampere general lighting circuit"],"a":2,"e":"550.12(B) requires a 20-ampere branch circuit for laundry receptacle outlets when a laundry area is provided.","r":"NEC 550.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":2,"q":"Where must service equipment for a mobile home be located relative to the home it serves?","o":["Inside the home near the kitchen","Adjacent to the home, in sight, and not more than 30 ft from the exterior wall","Mounted on the mobile home","At least 100 ft away"],"a":1,"e":"550.32(A) requires mobile home service equipment to be located adjacent to, not mounted in or on, the home and in sight from and not more than 30 ft from the exterior wall it serves.","r":"NEC 550.32(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":1,"q":"Where are receptacle outlets prohibited in a mobile home bathroom?","o":["On the wall next to the lavatory","Anywhere in the room","Within or directly over a bathtub or shower space","On any wall"],"a":2,"e":"550.13 prohibits receptacle outlets within or directly over a bathtub or shower space.","r":"NEC 550.13","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":1,"q":"Under Article 551, a recreational vehicle is a vehicular-type unit primarily designed for what purpose?","o":["Permanent residence on a foundation","Mobile retail sales only","Commercial cargo hauling","Temporary living quarters for recreational, camping, travel or seasonal use"],"a":3,"e":"The 551.2 definition describes an RV as a vehicular-type unit primarily designed as temporary living quarters for recreational, camping, travel or seasonal use, either self-propelled or towable.","r":"NEC 551.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":2,"q":"What attachment plug configuration is required on a 30-ampere RV power-supply cord?","o":["NEMA L5-30P","NEMA 5-15P","NEMA 14-50P","NEMA TT-30P, 125-volt, 3-wire"],"a":3,"e":"551.46 requires a 30-ampere RV power-supply cord to have a 125-volt, 2-pole, 3-wire grounding-type TT-30P attachment plug. The 14-50P is for 50-ampere cords.","r":"NEC 551.46","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":2,"q":"A 50-ampere RV power-supply cord uses which type of attachment plug?","o":["125-volt, 2-pole, 3-wire","125/250-volt, 3-pole, 4-wire grounding type (14-50P)","480-volt, 3-pole, 4-wire","250-volt, 2-pole, 3-wire"],"a":1,"e":"551.46 calls for a 50-ampere, 125/250-volt, 3-pole, 4-wire grounding-type plug (14-50P) on a 50-ampere cord.","r":"NEC 551.46","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":2,"q":"A 125-volt, 15-ampere receptacle is installed in an RV 4 ft from a kitchen sink. What does 551.41(C) require?","o":["Only AFCI protection","A 30-ampere circuit","GFCI protection for personnel","No special protection"],"a":2,"e":"551.41(C) requires GFCI protection for receptacles in bathrooms, on kitchen countertops, within 6 ft of a lavatory or sink, on the exterior, and in compartments accessible from outside the RV.","r":"NEC 551.41(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":1,"q":"Which receptacle in an RV must have GFCI protection under 551.41(C)?","o":["A receptacle in the hallway","An exterior receptacle outlet","A bedroom receptacle on the far wall","A receptacle for a built-in hair dryer in a closet"],"a":1,"e":"Exterior receptacle outlets, along with bathrooms, countertops, sink-adjacent and exterior-accessible compartments, require GFCI protection.","r":"NEC 551.41(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":3,"q":"In a 120/240-volt RV electrical system, how must the grounded (neutral) conductor be handled at the RV panelboard?","o":["Connected to the chassis at every receptacle","Insulated from the equipment grounding conductors, enclosures and other grounded parts","Omitted entirely","Bonded to the panel enclosure and frame"],"a":1,"e":"551.54 requires the grounded conductor to be insulated from the equipment grounding conductor, enclosures and other grounded parts, with no neutral-to-ground bond in the RV; bonding occurs at the source.","r":"NEC 551.54","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":3,"q":"An RV park computes its load per site. Which VA value is used for each RV site equipped with 50-ampere supply facilities?","o":["6000 VA","9600 VA","2400 VA","3600 VA"],"a":1,"e":"551.73(A) calculates 9600 VA per site with a 50-ampere supply, 3600 VA per site with both 20- and 30-ampere supply, and lower values for 20-ampere-only and tent sites.","r":"NEC 551.73(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum mounting height above ground for receptacles at an RV site?","o":["24 in. (2 ft)","18 in.","4 ft","12 in."],"a":0,"e":"RV site supply receptacles must be mounted not less than 600 mm (2 ft) above the ground.","r":"NEC 551.77","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":2,"q":"A 125-volt, 20-ampere receptacle is provided at an RV site in a park. What protection is required?","o":["AFCI protection","Overvoltage protection only","GFCI protection","None if the pedestal is grounded"],"a":2,"e":"551.77 requires GFCI protection for 125-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles in RV site supply equipment.","r":"NEC 551.77","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":1,"q":"Article 555 covers fixed or floating piers, wharves and docks used for which purposes?","o":["Repair, berthing, launching, storage or supply of marine vessels","Only boat manufacturing","Aircraft hangars","Swimming pools"],"a":0,"e":"555.1 covers fixed or floating piers, wharves, docks and other areas used for repair, berthing, launching, storage or fueling of small craft and for moorage and supply of vessels.","r":"NEC 555.1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"For land areas subject to tidal fluctuation, the electrical datum plane is a horizontal plane located how far above the highest high tide level?","o":["1 ft","4 ft","2 ft","6 in."],"a":2,"e":"555.2 defines the electrical datum plane for tidal land areas as 600 mm (2 ft) above the highest high tide for the area.","r":"NEC 555.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"Where must the service equipment for a floating dock be located?","o":["Adjacent to, but not on or in, the floating structure","On the floating structure","Below the datum plane","Under the deck"],"a":0,"e":"555.4 requires the service equipment to be located adjacent to, but not on or in, the floating structure.","r":"NEC 555.4","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":3,"q":"A transformer is installed on a pier. What is a restriction on the location of its enclosure?","o":["It may be installed anywhere","It must be submerged for cooling","It must be mounted at ground level","The bottom of the enclosure must not be located below the electrical datum plane"],"a":3,"e":"555.5 requires that the bottom of a transformer enclosure not be located below the electrical datum plane.","r":"NEC 555.5","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":3,"q":"Electrical equipment enclosures on a pier over water must be mounted with the bottom of the enclosure at least how high above the deck, and not below the datum plane?","o":["6 in.","12 in.","36 in.","24 in."],"a":1,"e":"555.30(B) requires enclosures on piers to be at least 305 mm (12 in.) above the deck surface and not below the electrical datum plane.","r":"NEC 555.30(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum ampere rating for a shore power receptacle that supplies boats in a marina?","o":["30 amperes","15 amperes","20 amperes","60 amperes"],"a":0,"e":"555.33(A) requires shore power receptacles to be rated not less than 30 amperes and to be of the locking and grounding type.","r":"NEC 555.33(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"Which wiring method is NOT permitted for wiring on marina piers under 555.34(A)?","o":["Liquidtight flexible metal conduit","Type NM cable","Rigid metal conduit","Rigid PVC conduit"],"a":1,"e":"555.34(A) lists wet-location-suitable methods such as RMC, PVC, LFMC and listed MC cable; Type NM is not among them.","r":"NEC 555.34(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"Outlets that supply boat hoists at a marina must have what protection?","o":["Only a disconnect","AFCI protection","No protection if the hoist is 240 volts","GFCI protection for personnel"],"a":3,"e":"555.9 requires GFCI protection for personnel for outlets that supply boat hoists.","r":"NEC 555.9","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":1,"q":"What must permanent safety signs required at marinas warn about?","o":["Fuel spills only","Potential shock hazard from electrical currents that may be present in the water","Dock capacity","Speed limits"],"a":1,"e":"555.10 requires permanent safety signs giving notice of the electrical shock hazard to persons using or swimming near the marina or dock.","r":"NEC 555.10","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":3,"q":"The main overcurrent device feeding shore power receptacles for boats must have equipment ground-fault protection set at no more than what level?","o":["6 mA","500 mA","30 mA","100 mA"],"a":3,"e":"Ground-fault protection of equipment for feeders and branch circuits supplying marina shore power is limited to 100 mA; 30 mA/6 mA values are for personnel GFCI.","r":"NEC 555.35","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":1,"q":"Temporary electric power and lighting for holiday decorative lighting is permitted for a period not to exceed how long?","o":["60 days","30 days","90 days","180 days"],"a":2,"e":"590.3(B) permits temporary installations for holiday decorative lighting and similar purposes for not more than 90 days.","r":"NEC 590.3(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":1,"q":"Temporary wiring installed for a construction project must be removed when?","o":["At the next inspection","Only at the owner's request","Immediately upon completion of the purpose or project","After 1 year"],"a":2,"e":"590.3(D) requires temporary wiring to be removed immediately upon completion of the project or purpose for which it was installed.","r":"NEC 590.3(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":2,"q":"During which of these situations is temporary wiring permitted without a specified time limit under 590.3?","o":["Year-round outdoor lighting","Emergencies and tests, experiments, and developmental work","Permanent retail displays","Permanent signs"],"a":1,"e":"590.3(C) allows temporary electric power during emergencies and for tests, experiments and developmental work; 590.3(A) covers construction, remodeling, maintenance, repair or demolition.","r":"NEC 590.3(A),(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":2,"q":"Temporary services must be installed in accordance with which article?","o":["Article 225 only","Article 250 only","Article 230, Parts I through VIII as applicable","Article 400"],"a":2,"e":"590.4(A) requires services to comply with Parts I through VIII of Article 230 as applicable.","r":"NEC 590.4(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":2,"q":"Where must temporary feeders originate?","o":["In any junction box","At a receptacle","At a lighting fixture","In an approved distribution center"],"a":3,"e":"590.4(B) requires temporary feeders to originate in an approved distribution center.","r":"NEC 590.4(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":2,"q":"Where must all temporary branch circuits originate?","o":["At the nearest receptacle","In an approved power outlet, switchgear, switchboard, panelboard or other approved distribution center","At the generator frame","At a junction box"],"a":1,"e":"590.4(C) requires all branch circuits to originate in an approved power outlet, switchgear, switchboard, panelboard or other approved distribution center.","r":"NEC 590.4(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":2,"q":"Which statement about receptacles on a construction site is correct?","o":["Receptacles may share a branch circuit with temporary lighting","Receptacles must be 15-ampere only","Receptacles on construction sites shall not be installed on branch circuits that supply temporary lighting","Receptacles are not allowed at construction sites"],"a":2,"e":"590.4 prohibits installing receptacles on construction sites on branch circuits that supply temporary lighting, so a tripped tool circuit does not darken the work area.","r":"NEC 590.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":2,"q":"For temporary branch circuits supplying receptacles, when is a separate equipment grounding conductor not required?","o":["When the circuit is 15 amperes","When the circuit is installed in a continuous grounded metal raceway or metal-covered cable that qualifies as an EGC","Never","When the circuit is under 90 days"],"a":1,"e":"590.4 requires a separate EGC unless the circuit is in a continuous metal raceway or metal-covered cable that qualifies as an equipment grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 590.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":1,"q":"General-illumination lamps in temporary lighting must be protected from accidental contact or breakage by what?","o":["A GFCI breaker","Nothing is required","A paper shade","A suitable luminaire or lampholder with a guard"],"a":3,"e":"590.4 requires lamps for general illumination to be protected from accidental contact or breakage by a suitable luminaire or lampholder with a guard.","r":"NEC 590.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":3,"q":"Temporary flexible cords on a job site pass through a doorway. Which requirement applies?","o":["They must be buried","They must be protected from accidental damage, including at sharp corners and pinch points such as doorways","They may be pinched by the door","They must be spliced at the door"],"a":1,"e":"590.4 requires flexible cords and cables to be protected from accidental damage, including from sharp corners, projections and pinch points such as doorways.","r":"NEC 590.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":3,"q":"Can vegetation, such as trees, be used to support overhead spans of temporary branch circuits or feeders?","o":["Yes, if 10 ft above ground","Yes, if under 50 volts","Yes, always","No, except for holiday decorative lighting"],"a":3,"e":"590.4 states vegetation shall not be used for support of overhead spans of branch circuits or feeders, with an exception for holiday decorative lighting.","r":"NEC 590.4","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":1,"q":"Decorative lighting used for holiday purposes under 590.3(B) must be what?","o":["Listed","Installed by the utility","Rated 240 volts","Home-made"],"a":0,"e":"590.5 requires decorative lighting used for holiday lighting and similar purposes under 590.3(B) to be listed.","r":"NEC 590.5","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":2,"q":"A 125-volt, 20-ampere receptacle outlet used by personnel on a construction site and not part of the permanent wiring requires what?","o":["GFCI protection for personnel","AFCI protection","A 30-ampere breaker","No protection"],"a":0,"e":"590.6(A) requires GFCI protection for personnel for receptacle outlets on temporary wiring used by personnel and not part of the permanent wiring.","r":"NEC 590.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":1,"q":"Under Article 600, which description best fits an electric sign?","o":["A fixed, stationary, or portable self-contained, electrically illuminated utilization equipment with words or symbols designed to convey information or attract attention","A transformer that supplies neon tubing","A raceway used to supply discharge lighting","Any luminaire mounted on the exterior of a building"],"a":0,"e":"Article 600 defines a sign as self-contained, electrically illuminated utilization equipment with words or symbols meant to convey information or attract attention.","r":"NEC 600.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":1,"q":"An arrangement of incandescent lamps, electric-discharge tubing, or other electrically powered light sources used to outline or call attention to features such as the shape of a building is called:","o":["A sign body","Outline lighting","Skeleton tubing","A section sign"],"a":1,"e":"Outline lighting is the arrangement of light sources that outlines or calls attention to features such as a building's shape or a window decoration.","r":"NEC 600.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":1,"q":"Neon tubing that is itself the sign or outline lighting and is not attached to an enclosure or sign body is defined as:","o":["Skeleton tubing","A retrofit kit","A sign body","A section sign"],"a":0,"e":"Skeleton tubing is neon tubing that is itself the sign or outline lighting and is not attached to an enclosure or sign body.","r":"NEC 600.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":1,"q":"A sign or outline lighting system that is shipped as subassemblies and requires field assembly is called a:","o":["Section sign","Skeleton sign","Retrofit sign","Portable sign"],"a":0,"e":"A section sign is shipped as subassemblies and assembled in the field, in contrast to a complete factory-assembled sign.","r":"NEC 600.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":1,"q":"Fixed, mobile, or portable electric signs and outline lighting systems are generally required to be:","o":["Inspected by the manufacturer only","Listed and labeled","Supplied by a Class 2 source","Installed only in dry locations"],"a":1,"e":"Section 600.3 requires signs and outline lighting systems to be listed and labeled, with limited exceptions for field-installed skeleton tubing.","r":"NEC 600.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"Field-installed skeleton tubing, outline lighting, and their secondary wiring are not required to be listed when:","o":["They are installed in conformance with the NEC","A licensed sign contractor performs the work","The system operates at less than 50 volts","The installation is inside a building only"],"a":0,"e":"The 600.3 exception allows field-installed skeleton tubing, outline lighting, and secondary wiring to be unlisted when installed in conformance with the Code.","r":"NEC 600.3 Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":1,"q":"Which information must be marked on a sign or outline lighting system?","o":["Only the lamp wattage","Only the UL file number","The installer's license number and date of installation","The manufacturer's name, trademark, or other identification, plus input voltage and current rating"],"a":3,"e":"Signs must be marked with the manufacturer's identification and the input voltage and current rating.","r":"NEC 600.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":1,"q":"Each commercial occupancy accessible to pedestrians must have at least one outlet for sign or outline lighting supplied by a branch circuit rated at least:","o":["25 amperes","15 amperes","20 amperes","30 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"Section 600.5(A) requires an accessible exterior sign outlet on a branch circuit rated at least 20 amperes.","r":"NEC 600.5(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"The required 20-ampere sign branch circuit for a commercial occupancy may also supply which of the following?","o":["The store's general lighting","Nearby receptacle outlets for cleaning equipment","No other lighting, receptacles, or equipment","The store's HVAC unit controls"],"a":2,"e":"The required sign branch circuit must supply only the sign or outline lighting and no other loads.","r":"NEC 600.5(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":3,"q":"A strip mall has four tenant spaces, each with its own pedestrian entrance. How many sign outlets are required under 600.5(A)?","o":["One for the entire building","Two per building","One at each tenant space entrance accessible to pedestrians","None if the tenants use LED signs"],"a":2,"e":"Each tenant space accessible to pedestrians needs its own accessible sign outlet on a dedicated 20-ampere circuit.","r":"NEC 600.5(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"The maximum rating of a branch circuit supplying a sign that does not use neon tubing is:","o":["40 amperes","15 amperes","20 amperes","30 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"Branch circuits supplying signs and outline lighting, other than neon tubing installations, may not exceed 20 amperes.","r":"NEC 600.5(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"The maximum rating of a branch circuit supplying neon tubing installations is:","o":["40 amperes","20 amperes","30 amperes","50 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"Neon tubing installations may be supplied by branch circuits rated up to 30 amperes; other signs are limited to 20 amperes.","r":"NEC 600.5(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"Branch circuits supplying signs are treated as continuous loads. What is the maximum sign load on a 20-ampere, 120-volt sign branch circuit?","o":["15 amperes","16 amperes","18 amperes","20 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"Continuous loads are limited to 80 percent of the circuit rating, so 20 A x 0.80 = 16 A.","r":"NEC 600.5(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":3,"q":"A non-neon sign has a continuous load of 14 amperes. What is the minimum standard branch circuit rating that may supply it?","o":["25 A","30 A","20 A","15 A"],"a":2,"e":"Continuous load at 125 percent is 17.5 A, so the next standard size is 20 A, which is also the maximum allowed for non-neon sign circuits. A 15 A circuit is too small.","r":"NEC 600.5(B), 210.20(A), 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":1,"q":"The disconnecting means for a sign must open:","o":["All ungrounded conductors of the supply","Only the grounded conductor","Only one ungrounded conductor","The equipment grounding conductor"],"a":0,"e":"Each sign must be controlled by an externally operable switch or circuit breaker that opens all ungrounded conductors.","r":"NEC 600.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"The disconnecting means for a sign must be located:","o":["In the main service room only","Within sight of the sign it controls","Behind a locked door","At least 25 feet from the sign"],"a":1,"e":"The sign disconnect must be within sight of the sign or outline lighting it controls, with provisions for a lockable disconnect where not in sight.","r":"NEC 600.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":3,"q":"A sign disconnect is installed where it is not within sight of the sign. What is required?","o":["A GFCI breaker replaces the disconnect","A second disconnect inside the sign is not allowed","It must be mounted at least 8 ft above grade","It must be capable of being locked in the open position"],"a":3,"e":"If the disconnect is not within sight of the sign, it must be capable of being locked in the open position.","r":"NEC 600.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"A disconnecting means is NOT required for:","o":["Signs on commercial buildings","Neon signs","Exit directional signs located within a building","Signs installed on the exterior of a building"],"a":2,"e":"The 600.6 exception does not require a sign disconnect for an exit directional sign located within a building.","r":"NEC 600.6 Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"Metal parts of a sign are grounded by connection to:","o":["The equipment grounding conductor of the supply branch circuit","A local ground rod only","The neutral at the sign","A water pipe within 5 feet"],"a":0,"e":"Signs and metal equipment of outline lighting are grounded through the equipment grounding conductor of the supply branch circuit.","r":"NEC 600.7(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"Sign parts located above areas accessible to vehicles must be at least how high above the area unless protected from physical damage?","o":["14 feet","10 feet","8 feet","12 feet"],"a":0,"e":"Section 600.9(A) requires 14 ft above areas accessible to vehicles unless protected from physical damage.","r":"NEC 600.9(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":3,"q":"The lowest edge of an unprotected electric sign is 11 feet above a delivery driveway used by trucks. What is the result?","o":["Compliant, since 8 feet is the minimum","Noncompliant unless it is protected from physical damage or raised to 14 feet","Compliant if the sign is 120 volts only","Compliant if marked wet location"],"a":1,"e":"Over areas accessible to vehicles the sign must be at least 14 ft above or protected from physical damage.","r":"NEC 600.9(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"Neon tubing that is readily accessible to pedestrians must be:","o":["Wired only in rigid conduit","Protected from physical damage","Painted","Operated below 50 volts"],"a":1,"e":"Neon tubing exposed to contact by pedestrians must be protected from physical damage.","r":"NEC 600.9(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"Signs and outline lighting must be installed so that adjacent combustible materials are not subjected to temperatures in excess of:","o":["75 degrees C (167 degrees F)","60 degrees C (140 degrees F)","105 degrees C (221 degrees F)","90 degrees C (194 degrees F)"],"a":3,"e":"Adjacent combustible materials may not be exposed to temperatures above 90 degrees C (194 degrees F).","r":"NEC 600.9(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"Portable or mobile signs must be provided with:","o":["GFCI protection for personnel","AFCI protection only","A disconnect at the sign base only","A cord longer than 25 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Portable and mobile signs require GFCI protection for personnel, either factory-installed or at the receptacle.","r":"NEC 600.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"The supply cord on a portable sign may not exceed:","o":["15 ft","25 ft","6 ft","10 ft"],"a":0,"e":"The flexible cord supplying a portable sign is limited to 4.5 m (15 ft) in length.","r":"NEC 600.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"Ballasts, transformers, and electronic power supplies for signs must be:","o":["Installed outdoors only","Located so they are accessible","Placed in the attic only","Buried in the wall"],"a":1,"e":"Ballasts, transformers, electronic power supplies, and similar equipment must be accessible.","r":"NEC 600.21(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"Ballasts, transformers, and electronic power supplies for neon tubing should be located:","o":["Only in a separate building","Behind the sign face in a sealed compartment","As close to the lamps or tubing as practicable to keep secondary conductors short","As far as practicable from the tubing"],"a":2,"e":"They are to be located so that secondary conductors are as short as practicable.","r":"NEC 600.21","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"The secondary-circuit voltage of a transformer or electronic power supply for neon tubing may not exceed what value between conductors?","o":["15,000 volts","20,000 volts","7,500 volts","10,000 volts"],"a":0,"e":"Secondary circuit voltage is limited to 15,000 volts nominal between conductors.","r":"NEC 600.23","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"The secondary-circuit voltage of a neon transformer may not exceed what value to ground?","o":["15,000 volts","1,000 volts","7,500 volts","5,000 volts"],"a":2,"e":"The limit is 7,500 volts nominal to ground.","r":"NEC 600.23","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 600 Electric Signs and Outline Lighting","d":2,"q":"Secondary-circuit conductors for neon tubing operating over 1,000 volts must be:","o":["Listed gas tube sign and oil burner ignition cable (GTO)","Type THHN","Type NM","Type SO cord"],"a":0,"e":"Secondary conductors over 1,000 V must be listed GTO cable of the appropriate kV rating.","r":"NEC 600.32","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":1,"q":"Article 610 covers the installation of electrical equipment and wiring used in connection with:","o":["Portable power tools on construction sites","Cranes, monorail hoists, hoists, and all runways","Elevators and dumbwaiters only","Conveyors and escalators"],"a":1,"e":"The scope of Article 610 is cranes, monorail hoists, hoists, and all runways. Elevators and escalators fall under Article 620.","r":"NEC 610.1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":2,"q":"A crane operates in a Class I, Division 1 location. Which statement is correct?","o":["General-purpose enclosures are acceptable if gasketed","The equipment and wiring must also comply with Article 501 for Class I locations","Only Article 610 applies","Article 610 supersedes all hazardous location rules"],"a":1,"e":"Section 610.3 requires cranes and hoists in hazardous (classified) locations to meet the applicable classified location article, here Article 501.","r":"NEC 610.3(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":2,"q":"A hoist installed in a Class II location where combustible dust is present must comply with the additional requirements of:","o":["Article 501","Article 511","Article 502","Article 517"],"a":2,"e":"Class II locations are covered by Article 502, which applies in addition to Article 610.","r":"NEC 610.3(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":2,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted for conductors on a crane under the general wiring method rule?","o":["Open knob-and-tube wiring","Electrical metallic tubing","Type NM cable","Type UF cable"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors on cranes must be in raceways such as rigid metal conduit, IMC, or EMT, or be Type AC, MC, or MI cable (with specific exceptions for flexing and contact conductors). Type NM and UF are not included.","r":"NEC 610.11","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":2,"q":"Ampacities of insulated conductors used with short-time-rated crane and hoist motors are found in:","o":["Table 430.250","Table 610.14(A)","Table 310.16","Table 400.5(A)(1)"],"a":1,"e":"Table 610.14(A) gives ampacities for conductors used with 15-, 30-, and 60-minute short-time rated crane and hoist motors.","r":"NEC 610.14(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":2,"q":"When a crane or hoist is operated by more than one motor, a common-return conductor:","o":["Is permitted if it has the proper ampacity","Must be the grounded conductor of the building","Must be bare copper","Is prohibited"],"a":0,"e":"A common-return conductor of proper ampacity is permitted where more than one motor operates the crane or hoist.","r":"NEC 610.15","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":2,"q":"A readily accessible disconnecting means must be provided between the runway contact conductors and the power supply. It must open all ungrounded conductors:","o":["One at a time","Simultaneously","Only on fault","Only through a contactor"],"a":1,"e":"The runway conductor disconnect must open all ungrounded conductors simultaneously and be arranged to be locked in the open position.","r":"NEC 610.31","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":2,"q":"The runway conductor disconnecting means must be placed:","o":["At least 50 ft from the runway","Above the bridge girders","In the crane cab only","Within view of the runway contact conductors"],"a":3,"e":"The disconnect must be within view of the runway contact conductors.","r":"NEC 610.31","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":2,"q":"On cranes and monorail hoists, the switch or circuit breaker in the leads from the runway contact conductors must be:","o":["Mounted outdoors","An unfused knife switch","Arranged to be locked in the open position","A plug fuse"],"a":2,"e":"A motor-circuit switch or circuit breaker arranged to be locked in the open position is required in the leads from the runway contact conductors.","r":"NEC 610.32","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":2,"q":"The continuous ampere rating of the disconnecting means for a crane with several motors must be at least what percentage of the combined short-time ampere rating of the motors?","o":["75 percent","50 percent","25 percent","100 percent"],"a":1,"e":"The rating must be not less than 50 percent of the combined short-time ampere rating of the motors, or not less than 75 percent of the sum of the short-time ratings of the motors for any single motion.","r":"NEC 610.33","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":3,"q":"The motors for a single motion on a crane have a sum of short-time ampere ratings of 80 amperes. The disconnect rating based on the single-motion criterion must be at least:","o":["75 A","40 A","80 A","60 A"],"a":3,"e":"75 percent of 80 A is 60 A under the single-motion criterion.","r":"NEC 610.33","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":3,"q":"A crane has motors whose combined short-time ratings total 120 amperes. Using the combined-rating criterion, the minimum continuous rating of the disconnect is:","o":["120 A","75 A","60 A","90 A"],"a":2,"e":"50 percent of the combined short-time rating of 120 A is 60 A.","r":"NEC 610.33","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":2,"q":"Branch circuits for crane and hoist motors must be protected against short circuit and ground fault in accordance with:","o":["Article 240 only","Article 725","Article 700","Part IV of Article 430"],"a":3,"e":"Section 610.42 requires protection per Part IV of Article 430.","r":"NEC 610.42","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":2,"q":"Overload protection of motors, motor controllers, and branch-circuit conductors on a crane or hoist must comply with:","o":["Article 422","Article 445","Article 690","Part III of Article 430"],"a":3,"e":"Section 610.43 points to Part III of Article 430 for overload protection.","r":"NEC 610.43","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":1,"q":"A limit switch or other device is required on a hoisting mechanism to:","o":["Limit inrush current to the motor","Provide ground-fault protection","Disconnect the pendant control","Prevent the load block from passing the safe upper limit of travel"],"a":3,"e":"Every hoisting mechanism requires a limit device to stop the load block at the safe upper limit of travel.","r":"NEC 610.55","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":2,"q":"The minimum clear working space in the direction of access to live parts on a crane that may require servicing while energized is:","o":["30 in.","36 in.","18 in.","24 in."],"a":0,"e":"Section 610.57 requires a minimum of 750 mm (2 1/2 ft, or 30 in.) of clear working space.","r":"NEC 610.57","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":2,"q":"Where crane controls are enclosed in cabinets, the doors must:","o":["Open at least 90 degrees or be removable","Open at least 45 degrees","Be gasketed and locked","Be welded shut"],"a":0,"e":"The cabinet doors must open at least 90 degrees or be removable to provide the required working space.","r":"NEC 610.57","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":1,"q":"All exposed non-current-carrying metal parts of cranes, monorail hoists, hoists, and accessories, including pendant controls, must be:","o":["Painted yellow","Metallically joined together into a continuous electrical conductor and grounded per Article 250","Insulated from each other","Left floating"],"a":1,"e":"Exposed metal parts must be bonded into a continuous conductor and grounded in accordance with Article 250.","r":"NEC 610.61","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":2,"q":"Can the bridge and trolley wheels and their tracks serve as the equipment grounding path for the trolley and bridge frames?","o":["Yes, if the crane is under 600 volts","No, a separate bonding conductor must be provided","Yes, if the track is galvanized","Yes, always"],"a":1,"e":"The trolley frame and bridge frame are not considered grounded through the wheels and tracks; a separate bonding conductor is required.","r":"NEC 610.61","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":3,"q":"On a hoist, moving parts with metal-to-metal bearing surfaces (other than removable accessories) are:","o":["Required to be bonded with a copper jumper at each bearing","Never allowed to carry fault current","Allowed only for voltages under 50 volts","Considered electrically connected through the bearings and permitted to serve as the equipment grounding conductor"],"a":3,"e":"Moving parts with metal-to-metal bearing surfaces are considered electrically connected through the bearings, but the wheels-and-tracks path is specifically excluded for bridge and trolley frames.","r":"NEC 610.61","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":3,"q":"A hoist motor is rated 15-minute duty. The conductors supplying it are selected using ampacities from the table for:","o":["Table 430.248 only","Continuous-duty motors","Table 310.16 at 60 degrees C only","Short-time rated motors in Table 610.14(A), using the 15-minute column"],"a":3,"e":"Short-time rated crane motors use Table 610.14(A), which has columns for 15-, 30-, and 60-minute ratings.","r":"NEC 610.14(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":3,"q":"A runway disconnect for an overhead crane is mounted behind a locked door in a remote electrical room, out of view of the runway contact conductors. Under 610.31, this installation is:","o":["Noncompliant, because the disconnect must be readily accessible and within view of the runway contact conductors","Compliant if labeled","Compliant, since it is locked","Compliant for cranes under 5 tons"],"a":0,"e":"The runway disconnect must be readily accessible and within view of the runway contact conductors.","r":"NEC 610.31","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":3,"q":"A bridge crane has a runway disconnect at the power supply, as required by 610.31. For the crane itself, 610.32 additionally requires:","o":["No further disconnect","A lockable-open motor-circuit switch or circuit breaker in the leads from the runway contact conductors","A fused plug on each motor","A GFCI in the cab"],"a":1,"e":"The crane requires its own lockable motor-circuit switch or circuit breaker in the leads from the contact conductors, subject to the exceptions in 610.32.","r":"NEC 610.32","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":2,"q":"A crane operates in a Class III location where easily ignitible fibers are present. Which article's requirements apply in addition to Article 610?","o":["Article 503","Article 502","Article 505","Article 501"],"a":0,"e":"Class III locations are covered by Article 503.","r":"NEC 610.3(A), 503","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 610 Cranes and Hoists","d":1,"q":"Pendant control stations on a crane must have their exposed non-current-carrying metal parts:","o":["Connected to the neutral only","Left isolated","Bonded and grounded as part of the crane's continuous grounding conductor","Painted with insulating paint"],"a":2,"e":"Pendant controls are included in the metal parts that must be metallically joined and grounded.","r":"NEC 610.61","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":1,"q":"Article 620 covers electrical equipment and wiring used in connection with which of the following?","o":["Portable hoists on construction sites","Elevators, dumbwaiters, escalators, moving walks, platform lifts, and stairway chairlifts","Overhead cranes and monorail hoists","Industrial conveyors and assembly lines"],"a":1,"e":"Article 620 covers elevators, dumbwaiters, escalators, moving walks, platform lifts, and stairway chairlifts. Cranes and hoists fall under Article 610.","r":"NEC 620.1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":1,"q":"A power-driven, inclined, continuous stairway used for raising or lowering passengers is defined as a(n):","o":["Escalator","Platform lift","Moving walk","Dumbwaiter"],"a":0,"e":"That is the definition of an escalator.","r":"NEC 620.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":1,"q":"A hoisting and lowering mechanism with a car of limited capacity and size that moves in guides in a substantially vertical direction and is used for carrying material only is a(n):","o":["Moving walk","Escalator","Dumbwaiter","Elevator"],"a":2,"e":"A dumbwaiter carries material only; an elevator carries passengers or freight.","r":"NEC 620.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":1,"q":"A passenger-carrying device on which passengers stand or walk, where the carrying surface remains parallel to its direction of motion and is uninterrupted, is a:","o":["Stairway chairlift","Escalator","Moving walk","Dumbwaiter"],"a":2,"e":"This describes a moving walk.","r":"NEC 620.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"What distinguishes a machine room from a machinery space in Article 620?","o":["A machine room is always inside the hoistway","A machinery space must be fire-rated","A machine room is intended for full-bodied entry; a machinery space is not","There is no difference"],"a":2,"e":"A machine room is an enclosed machinery space outside the hoistway intended for full-bodied entry. A machinery space is not intended for full-bodied entry.","r":"NEC 620.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":1,"q":"Insulated conductors used in connection with elevators must be:","o":["Lead-covered","Bare","Flame retardant","Rated for 1000 volts minimum"],"a":2,"e":"Section 620.11 requires insulated conductors to be flame retardant and suitable for at least 60 degrees C.","r":"NEC 620.11(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"The demand factor in Table 620.14 for a feeder supplying two elevators is:","o":["0.90","0.85","0.95","1.00"],"a":2,"e":"Table 620.14 lists 1.00 for one elevator and 0.95 for two.","r":"NEC 620.14","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"The demand factor in Table 620.14 for a feeder supplying four elevators is:","o":["0.79","0.95","0.85","0.90"],"a":2,"e":"Table 620.14 lists 0.85 for four elevators (three is 0.90, five is 0.82).","r":"NEC 620.14","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":3,"q":"A single feeder supplies three elevators, each with a motor nameplate current of 40 amperes. Applying the Table 620.14 demand factor, the current used for sizing the feeder conductors is:","o":["120 A","114 A","102 A","108 A"],"a":3,"e":"Three elevators use a 0.90 demand factor: 3 x 40 A x 0.90 = 108 A.","r":"NEC 620.14","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":1,"q":"Elevator motor controllers and control panels must be marked with their:","o":["Short-circuit current rating","Maximum car speed only","Date of installation","Hoistway travel distance"],"a":0,"e":"Section 620.16 requires marking of the short-circuit current rating.","r":"NEC 620.16(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"An elevator control panel is marked with a short-circuit current rating of 10,000 amperes. It may be installed where the available fault current is at most:","o":["15,000 A","22,000 A","10,000 A","5,000 A"],"a":2,"e":"The control panel may not be installed where the available short-circuit current exceeds its marked rating.","r":"NEC 620.16(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted in an elevator hoistway?","o":["Electrical metallic tubing","Type NM cable","Open wiring on insulators","Type UF cable"],"a":0,"e":"Hoistway wiring is limited to methods such as rigid metal conduit, IMC, and EMT (and wireways, with other listed exceptions); NM and UF are not permitted.","r":"NEC 620.21(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"The elevator car lights, receptacles, and ventilation must be supplied by:","o":["The elevator motor feeder","Any available 20 A circuit","A separate branch circuit","The hallway lighting circuit"],"a":2,"e":"A separate branch circuit supplies the car lighting, receptacles, auxiliary lighting power source, and ventilation.","r":"NEC 620.22","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"Elevator machine room lighting and receptacles must be supplied by:","o":["The same circuit as the car lights","A separate branch circuit","The elevator motor branch circuit","A Class 2 power supply"],"a":1,"e":"Machine room or machinery space lighting and receptacles require a separate branch circuit.","r":"NEC 620.23","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":1,"q":"The lighting switch for an elevator machine room should be located:","o":["In the hoistway","Inside the controller","At the point of entry to the room","Outside the building"],"a":2,"e":"The machine room lighting switch is required at the point of entry.","r":"NEC 620.23(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"Hoistway pit lighting and receptacles are supplied from:","o":["A separate branch circuit","The elevator feeder","Any convenient receptacle circuit","The car light circuit"],"a":0,"e":"A separate branch circuit must supply the hoistway pit lighting and receptacle(s).","r":"NEC 620.24","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"The switch for the pit lighting must be located so that it is:","o":["Behind the controller","Readily accessible from the pit access door","In the machine room only","Inside the hoistway on the car top"],"a":1,"e":"The pit lighting switch must be readily accessible from the pit access door.","r":"NEC 620.24(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"Which wiring is permitted inside an elevator hoistway or machine room?","o":["Feeders for other floors' lighting","Telecom backbone cabling for the building","Only wiring used directly in connection with the elevator, such as signals, car lighting, and pit sump pumps","Any building branch circuit passing through"],"a":2,"e":"Only wiring and raceways directly related to the elevator are permitted in the hoistway, machine rooms, and machinery spaces.","r":"NEC 620.37","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":3,"q":"An electrician proposes to run a feeder for the building's upper-floor panelboards up the elevator hoistway because it is the shortest path. This is:","o":["Permitted if over 600 volts","Permitted if the feeder is MC cable","Not permitted, since only elevator-related wiring may be in the hoistway","Permitted if in EMT"],"a":2,"e":"Section 620.37 prohibits unrelated wiring in hoistways regardless of wiring method.","r":"NEC 620.37","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":1,"q":"The main power disconnecting means for an elevator must be:","o":["An enclosed, externally operable, listed fused motor-circuit switch or circuit breaker capable of being locked open","A knife switch without enclosure","Operated only remotely","A plug-and-receptacle"],"a":0,"e":"The disconnect must be an enclosed, externally operable fused motor-circuit switch or circuit breaker, listed, and lockable in the open position.","r":"NEC 620.51(A)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"The elevator main disconnecting means must be located:","o":["On the roof only","In the lobby of each floor","Where readily accessible to qualified persons, such as in the machine room or control room","In the elevator car"],"a":2,"e":"The disconnect must be readily accessible to qualified persons, normally in the machine room, control room, or similar space.","r":"NEC 620.51(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"May provision be made to open or close the elevator main disconnecting means from other parts of the premises?","o":["Yes, from the fire alarm panel only","No, except automatic opening for sprinklers or similar permitted cases","Yes, always","Yes, from each floor"],"a":1,"e":"No provision may be made to operate the disconnect remotely, except where permitted such as automatic opening for water in sprinklered hoistways or machine rooms.","r":"NEC 620.51(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":3,"q":"Sprinklers are installed in an elevator machine room. Which is permitted for the elevator main disconnect?","o":["No automatic tripping of any kind","A shunt-trip that opens power automatically upon or prior to the application of water","Removal of the lockout feature","An unlisted disconnect"],"a":1,"e":"Where sprinklers are installed, the disconnect may automatically open the power supply to the affected elevator on or before water is applied.","r":"NEC 620.51(B) Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"Several elevator disconnects are installed in the same machine room. They must be:","o":["Locked in the closed position","Located in separate buildings","Identical and unmarked","Identified to correspond to the elevator they control"],"a":3,"e":"Where multiple disconnecting means are present, each must be numbered or identified to match its elevator.","r":"NEC 620.51(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"The disconnecting means for car light, receptacles, and ventilation supply must be:","o":["Inside the car","Part of the main elevator disconnect","An enclosed externally operable device lockable in the open position, located in the machine room or machinery space","A snap switch in the hoistway"],"a":2,"e":"A separate lockable, externally operable disconnect is required, located in the machine room or machinery space.","r":"NEC 620.53","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"Each branch circuit for other utilization equipment on an elevator, such as car heating or air conditioning, must have:","o":["A disconnect inside the car only","A single disconnecting means for all ungrounded conductors, capable of being locked open","A disconnect for the grounded conductor only","No disconnect"],"a":1,"e":"A single disconnecting means that opens all ungrounded conductors and can be locked open is required.","r":"NEC 620.55","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"Where more than one driving machine disconnecting means is supplied by a single feeder, the overcurrent protective devices must be:","o":["Identical in rating","Omitted","Selectively coordinated with other supply-side overcurrent devices","Installed in the hoistway"],"a":2,"e":"Section 620.62 requires selective coordination.","r":"NEC 620.62","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"Metal raceways, Type MC, MI, or AC cable attached to elevator cars must be:","o":["Grounded through the rails only","Insulated from the car","Bonded to metal parts of the car that are bonded to the equipment grounding conductor","Left ungrounded"],"a":2,"e":"They must be bonded to the car metal parts that are connected to the equipment grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 620.81","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"A 125-volt, single-phase, 20-ampere receptacle in an elevator pit must be:","o":["Standard grounding type","Isolated ground type","GFCI type","AFCI type"],"a":2,"e":"Receptacles of this rating in pits, hoistways, car tops, and escalator and moving walk wellways must be GFCI.","r":"NEC 620.85","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":1,"q":"A 125-volt, 15-ampere receptacle installed on top of an elevator car must be:","o":["Switched","Weather-resistant only","Tamper-resistant only","GFCI type"],"a":3,"e":"Receptacles on elevator car tops require GFCI protection.","r":"NEC 620.85","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":3,"q":"A single receptacle in an elevator pit supplies a permanently installed sump pump. GFCI protection is:","o":["Required","Required only above 15 A","Required only if the pit is wet","Not required for that single receptacle"],"a":3,"e":"A single receptacle supplying a permanently installed sump pump is exempt from the GFCI requirement.","r":"NEC 620.85 Exception","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"An escalator wellway contains a 125-volt, single-phase, 15-ampere receptacle. It must be:","o":["Isolated ground type","Unswitched only","GFCI type","Locking type"],"a":2,"e":"GFCI is required for these receptacles in escalator and moving walk wellways.","r":"NEC 620.85","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":1,"q":"Any shaftway, hatchway, well hole, or other vertical opening or space in which an elevator or dumbwaiter is designed to operate is called a:","o":["Hoistway","Machine room","Pit sump","Control space"],"a":0,"e":"That is the definition of a hoistway.","r":"NEC 620.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":1,"q":"An enclosed control space outside the hoistway, intended for full-bodied entry, that contains the motor controller is a:","o":["Hoistway","Control room","Wellway","Machinery space"],"a":1,"e":"That is the definition of a control room.","r":"NEC 620.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"The overcurrent device protecting the elevator car light branch circuit must be located:","o":["At the lobby panel on each floor","In the machine room or machinery space","In the hoistway pit","On the car top"],"a":1,"e":"The device is located in the machine room or machinery space (or control space where there is no machine room).","r":"NEC 620.22","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":1,"q":"The conductors, electric vehicle connectors, attachment plugs, and all other fittings and apparatus installed specifically to transfer energy between the premises wiring and the electric vehicle are collectively known as:","o":["A utility interactive inverter","An energy storage system","A cable management system","Electric vehicle supply equipment (EVSE)"],"a":3,"e":"That is the definition of electric vehicle supply equipment, or EVSE.","r":"NEC 625.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":1,"q":"A device that, by insertion into an electric vehicle inlet, establishes an electrical connection to the vehicle for charging and information exchange is called an:","o":["Electric vehicle inlet","Attachment plug","Cable management system","Electric vehicle connector"],"a":3,"e":"The electric vehicle connector is the part attached to the EVSE that is inserted into the vehicle inlet.","r":"NEC 625.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":1,"q":"The device on the electric vehicle into which the electric vehicle connector is inserted is the:","o":["Electric vehicle inlet","Electric vehicle connector","Receptacle outlet","Charging cable"],"a":0,"e":"The inlet is the vehicle-side device and is part of the electric vehicle coupler.","r":"NEC 625.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":1,"q":"An electric vehicle coupler consists of:","o":["A transformer and a rectifier","An EV connector paired with an EV inlet","An attachment plug and a receptacle only","A disconnect and a breaker"],"a":1,"e":"The coupler is the mating pair of an EV connector on the EVSE and an EV inlet on the vehicle.","r":"NEC 625.2","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":1,"q":"An apparatus designed to control and organize unused lengths of power supply cable at electric vehicle charging locations is a:","o":["Cable management system","Interlock","Raceway","Cable tray"],"a":0,"e":"This is the definition of a cable management system.","r":"NEC 625.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":1,"q":"Under Article 625, electrical materials, devices, fittings, and associated equipment must be:","o":["Installed by the utility","Rated at 600 volts","Approved by the vehicle maker only","Listed or labeled"],"a":3,"e":"Article 625 requires all electrical materials, devices, fittings, and associated equipment to be listed or labeled.","r":"NEC 625.5","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"The means of coupling an EVSE to the electric vehicle may be:","o":["Mechanical only","Conductive or inductive","Inductive only","Conductive only"],"a":1,"e":"Section 625.16 allows conductive or inductive coupling, using listed or labeled attachment plugs, connectors, and receptacles.","r":"NEC 625.16","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"Which flexible cable type is suited for the output cable of electric vehicle supply equipment?","o":["Type SE","Type EV","Type NM","Type UF"],"a":1,"e":"EV cable types (EV, EVJ, EVE, EVJE, EVT, EVJT) are the types recognized for EVSE output cables.","r":"NEC 625.17","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"Unless the EVSE has a cable management system, the overall usable length of the output cable may not exceed:","o":["50 ft","35 ft","15 ft","25 ft"],"a":3,"e":"The usable length of the output cable is limited to 7.5 m (25 ft) unless a listed cable management system is part of the EVSE.","r":"NEC 625.17","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"EVSE must have an interlock that de-energizes the EV connector and its cable:","o":["Whenever the connector is uncoupled from the electric vehicle","Only at night","Every 3 hours","Only when the vehicle is fully charged"],"a":0,"e":"The interlock de-energizes the connector and cable when uncoupled from the vehicle.","r":"NEC 625.18","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"For which EVSE is the 625.18 interlock not required?","o":["DC supplies of less than 60 volts dc","Units installed outdoors","Any unit rated under 30 A","All 240-volt ac units"],"a":0,"e":"An interlock is not required for dc supplies of less than 60 volts dc.","r":"NEC 625.18","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"EVSE must have a means to de-energize the cable and EV connector when the cable is exposed to:","o":["High ambient noise","Frost","Strain that could damage the cable or disconnect it from the connector","Sunlight"],"a":2,"e":"Automatic de-energization is required on exposure to strain that could damage the cable or pull it from the connector.","r":"NEC 625.19","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"EVSE must incorporate which protection against shock?","o":["A shielded cable only","A fused disconnect on the cord","A listed personnel protection system","Isolation from the equipment grounding conductor"],"a":2,"e":"EVSE must have a listed system of protection against electric shock of personnel, composed of listed personnel protection devices.","r":"NEC 625.22","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"Each outlet installed for EVSE must be supplied by:","o":["A multiwire branch circuit shared with a dryer","An individual branch circuit with no other outlets","The garage door opener circuit","A shared 20 A lighting circuit"],"a":1,"e":"EVSE outlets require an individual branch circuit that has no other outlets.","r":"NEC 625.40, 210.17","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A homeowner wants to run one 50 A, 240 V circuit to an EV charging receptacle and also tap a second garage receptacle from it for tools. This is:","o":["Permitted if both are 240 V","Not permitted, since the EV circuit must be an individual branch circuit","Permitted if the tool receptacle is on the same strap","Permitted if GFCI protected"],"a":1,"e":"The EVSE circuit must be individual with no other outlets.","r":"NEC 625.40, 210.17","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":1,"q":"Overcurrent protection for a feeder or branch circuit supplying EVSE must be rated not less than what percentage of the EVSE maximum load?","o":["115 percent","125 percent","150 percent","100 percent"],"a":1,"e":"EVSE is a continuous load, so overcurrent protection is sized at not less than 125 percent of the maximum load.","r":"NEC 625.41","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 40-ampere EVSE (maximum continuous output 40 A) is installed. The minimum standard overcurrent device is:","o":["50 A","40 A","45 A","60 A"],"a":0,"e":"40 A x 1.25 = 50 A, which is a standard size.","r":"NEC 625.41, 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 32-ampere EVSE requires an overcurrent device rated at least:","o":["32 A","50 A","40 A","35 A"],"a":2,"e":"32 A x 1.25 = 40 A.","r":"NEC 625.41","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 48-ampere EVSE requires an overcurrent device rated at least:","o":["70 A","50 A","60 A","48 A"],"a":2,"e":"48 A x 1.25 = 60 A, a standard size.","r":"NEC 625.41","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 16-ampere portable EVSE circuit needs a minimum overcurrent device of:","o":["20 A","15 A","25 A","16 A"],"a":0,"e":"16 A x 1.25 = 20 A. A 15 A device would be less than 125 percent of the load.","r":"NEC 625.41","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 24-ampere EVSE requires an overcurrent device rated at least:","o":["40 A","30 A","25 A","35 A"],"a":1,"e":"24 A x 1.25 = 30 A.","r":"NEC 625.41","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"An 80-ampere EVSE requires an overcurrent device rated at least:","o":["90 A","125 A","100 A","80 A"],"a":2,"e":"80 A x 1.25 = 100 A, a standard size.","r":"NEC 625.41, 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":1,"q":"For the purposes of Article 625, electric vehicle charging loads are considered:","o":["Noncontinuous loads","Intermittent loads","Emergency loads","Continuous loads"],"a":3,"e":"Section 625.42 treats EV charging loads as continuous.","r":"NEC 625.42","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"Where an automatic load management system limits the EVSE load on a service or feeder, the maximum EVSE load used in the calculation is:","o":["Half the largest EVSE rating","Zero","The maximum load permitted by the automatic load management system","The sum of all EVSE nameplate ratings"],"a":2,"e":"With an automatic load management system, the maximum EVSE load on a service or feeder is the maximum load permitted by that system.","r":"NEC 625.42","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A disconnecting means for EVSE is required when the equipment is rated more than:","o":["30 A or 120 V to ground","60 A or 150 V to ground","100 A or 250 V to ground","15 A or 50 V to ground"],"a":1,"e":"A readily accessible disconnecting means is required for EVSE rated more than 60 amperes or more than 150 volts to ground.","r":"NEC 625.43","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 240-volt single-phase, 48-ampere hardwired EVSE is installed. Is a separate disconnecting means required by Article 625?","o":["Yes, since it is over 30 A","Yes, since it is over 150 V line-to-line","No, since it is not over 60 A and 240 V single-phase is 120 V to ground","Yes, since it is hardwired"],"a":2,"e":"The threshold is more than 60 A or more than 150 V to ground. This unit is 48 A and 120 V to ground.","r":"NEC 625.43","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"An 80-ampere, 240-volt single-phase EVSE is installed. Article 625 requires:","o":["A disconnect only if outdoors","No disconnecting means","A readily accessible disconnecting means capable of being locked in the open position","A GFCI disconnect only"],"a":2,"e":"The unit is rated more than 60 A, so a readily accessible, lockable disconnecting means is required.","r":"NEC 625.43","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 40-ampere, 480Y/277-volt three-phase EVSE is installed. A disconnecting means is:","o":["Not required for hardwired units","Not required, since it is under 60 A","Required only above 100 A","Required, since it is more than 150 volts to ground"],"a":3,"e":"277 V to ground exceeds 150 V, so a disconnect is required even though the current is under 60 A.","r":"NEC 625.43","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"The required EVSE disconnecting means must be:","o":["Inside the vehicle","Capable of being locked in the open position","Permanently locked closed","Operated only by the utility"],"a":1,"e":"The disconnect must be lockable in the open position; portable locking means are permitted and the provision for locking must remain in place with or without the lock.","r":"NEC 625.43","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"Upon loss of voltage from the utility, EVSE must be arranged so that:","o":["The GFCI resets","The vehicle automatically powers the building","Energy cannot be back-fed through the EVSE to the premises wiring, unless permitted by 625.48 and 702.6","The disconnect closes"],"a":2,"e":"Section 625.46 requires means to prevent back-feed through the EVSE on loss of primary source, unless bidirectional operation is permitted.","r":"NEC 625.46","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"EVSE that incorporates a power export (bidirectional) function must be:","o":["Listed and marked as suitable for that purpose","Unlisted if under 240 V","Installed only outdoors","Limited to 15 A"],"a":0,"e":"Equipment with a power export function must be listed and marked as suitable for that purpose, and Articles 702 or 705 apply depending on the use.","r":"NEC 625.48","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"For indoor locations, the coupling means of EVSE (unless listed and marked otherwise) must be stored or located at a height of not less than:","o":["24 in.","6 in.","12 in.","18 in."],"a":3,"e":"Indoors, the coupling means must be at least 450 mm (18 in.) and not more than 1.2 m (4 ft) above floor level.","r":"NEC 625.50","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"For indoor locations, the maximum height of the EVSE coupling means above the floor (unless listed and marked otherwise) is:","o":["5 ft","6 ft","3 ft","4 ft"],"a":3,"e":"The upper limit indoors is 1.2 m (4 ft).","r":"NEC 625.50","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"For outdoor locations, the EVSE coupling means (unless listed and marked otherwise) must be at least what height above grade?","o":["12 in.","36 in.","24 in.","18 in."],"a":2,"e":"Outdoors the coupling means must be at least 600 mm (24 in.) above grade.","r":"NEC 625.50","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"An outdoor receptacle installed for EV charging must have an enclosure that is:","o":["Raintight only when covered by a tarp","Dust-tight only","Weatherproof only when the plug is inserted","Weatherproof with the attachment plug inserted or removed"],"a":3,"e":"Receptacles for EV charging in wet locations require an enclosure that is weatherproof with the attachment plug cap inserted or removed.","r":"NEC 625.56","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":1,"q":"Article 625 covers the electrical conductors and equipment external to an electric vehicle that connect the vehicle to a supply of electricity for which purpose?","o":["Charging, power export, or bidirectional current flow","Only vehicle engine starting","Only roadway lighting","Only battery manufacturing"],"a":0,"e":"Article 625 covers conductors and equipment external to the vehicle used for charging, power export, bidirectional current flow, and related installations.","r":"NEC 625.1","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A branch circuit supplying a 40-ampere EVSE is protected by a 50 A breaker. What is the maximum continuous EVSE load this circuit can supply?","o":["40 A","45 A","32 A","50 A"],"a":0,"e":"EV charging is a continuous load, so the load cannot exceed 80 percent of the 50 A rating: 40 A.","r":"NEC 625.42, 625.41","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum continuous EVSE load on a 20-ampere branch circuit?","o":["12 A","16 A","20 A","18 A"],"a":1,"e":"80 percent of 20 A is 16 A.","r":"NEC 625.42, 625.41","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum continuous EVSE load on a 30-ampere branch circuit?","o":["27 A","30 A","20 A","24 A"],"a":3,"e":"80 percent of 30 A is 24 A.","r":"NEC 625.42, 625.41","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum continuous EVSE load on a 60-ampere branch circuit?","o":["48 A","54 A","60 A","40 A"],"a":0,"e":"80 percent of 60 A is 48 A.","r":"NEC 625.42, 625.41","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 240-volt single-phase EVSE operates continuously at 40 amperes. What is the load in kilovolt-amperes?","o":["9.6 kVA","12.0 kVA","6.4 kVA","7.2 kVA"],"a":0,"e":"40 A x 240 V = 9,600 VA = 9.6 kVA.","r":"NEC 625.42, 220.57","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 20-ampere continuous EVSE load results in a calculated 25 A minimum overcurrent device. Which conductor may be used if the circuit is protected by a 25 A breaker? (copper, 75 degrees C terminations)","o":["10 AWG","No size, since 25 A is not standard","12 AWG","14 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"12 AWG copper is limited to 20 A overcurrent protection by 240.4(D), so a 25 A breaker requires 10 AWG. 25 A is a standard size.","r":"NEC 625.41, 240.4(D), 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 40-ampere EVSE is supplied through a 50 A circuit. Using copper conductors with 75 degrees C terminations, the minimum conductor size is:","o":["4 AWG","8 AWG","10 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"The conductor must carry 125 percent x 40 A = 50 A. 8 AWG at the 75 degree C column of Table 310.16 is 50 A. 10 AWG is only 35 A.","r":"NEC 625.41, 210.19(A)(1), Table 310.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 32-ampere EVSE requires conductors with ampacity of at least 40 A. Using copper with 75 degrees C terminations, the minimum size is:","o":["6 AWG","12 AWG","10 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"10 AWG is 35 A at 75 degrees C, which is too small. 8 AWG is 50 A.","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1), Table 310.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 48-ampere EVSE requires conductors with ampacity of at least 60 A. Using copper with 75 degrees C terminations, the minimum size is:","o":["8 AWG","6 AWG","4 AWG","3 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"6 AWG copper is 65 A at the 75 degree C column; 8 AWG is only 50 A.","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1), Table 310.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 24-ampere EVSE on a 30 A circuit uses copper conductors with 75 degrees C terminations. The minimum size is:","o":["8 AWG","12 AWG","14 AWG","10 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"The conductor must carry 30 A; 10 AWG is 35 A at 75 degrees C. 12 AWG is limited to 20 A overcurrent protection under 240.4(D).","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1), 240.4(D), Table 310.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 16-ampere EVSE on a 20 A circuit may use which minimum copper conductor size (75 degrees C terminations)?","o":["10 AWG","8 AWG","12 AWG","14 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"The conductor must carry 20 A and 12 AWG copper is rated 20 A (60 degrees C) or 25 A (75 degrees C). 14 AWG is limited to 15 A overcurrent protection.","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1), 240.4(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"An 80-ampere EVSE requires conductors with ampacity of at least 100 A. Using copper with 75 degrees C terminations, the minimum size is:","o":["4 AWG","2 AWG","3 AWG","1 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"3 AWG copper is rated 100 A at 75 degrees C in Table 310.16. 4 AWG is 85 A.","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1), Table 310.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 40-ampere EVSE is supplied through a 50 A circuit using aluminum conductors with 75 degrees C terminations. The minimum size is:","o":["10 AWG","8 AWG","6 AWG","4 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"The conductor must carry 50 A. 6 AWG aluminum is 50 A at 75 degrees C; 8 AWG aluminum is only 40 A.","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1), Table 310.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 48-ampere EVSE requires 60 A ampacity. Using aluminum conductors with 75 degrees C terminations, the minimum size is:","o":["4 AWG","3 AWG","6 AWG","2 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"4 AWG aluminum is 65 A at 75 degrees C; 6 AWG aluminum is 50 A.","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1), Table 310.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 40-ampere EVSE on a 50 A circuit has terminals rated only for 60 degrees C. The minimum copper conductor is:","o":["6 AWG","8 AWG","10 AWG","4 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"At 60 degrees C, 8 AWG is only 40 A. 6 AWG copper is 55 A, which meets the 50 A requirement.","r":"NEC 110.14(C), Table 310.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"May 12 AWG copper conductors be used on a 30 A branch circuit for a 24 A EVSE?","o":["Yes, since 24 A is less than 25 A","Yes, if THHN","Yes, if installed in conduit","No, 12 AWG copper is limited to 20 A overcurrent protection"],"a":3,"e":"Section 240.4(D) limits 12 AWG copper to 20 A overcurrent protection.","r":"NEC 240.4(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"The minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for an EVSE branch circuit protected by a 50 A breaker is:","o":["10 AWG","8 AWG","12 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 10 AWG copper for overcurrent devices up to 60 A.","r":"NEC 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"The minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for an EVSE branch circuit protected by a 100 A breaker is:","o":["4 AWG","10 AWG","6 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 8 AWG copper for overcurrent devices rated over 60 A up to 100 A.","r":"NEC 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"The minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for an EVSE circuit protected by a 20 A breaker is:","o":["8 AWG","10 AWG","14 AWG","12 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 12 AWG copper for a 20 A overcurrent device.","r":"NEC 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"The minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for an EVSE circuit protected by a 60 A breaker is:","o":["12 AWG","10 AWG","6 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 10 AWG copper for overcurrent devices up to 60 A.","r":"NEC 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A feeder supplies four 40-ampere EVSE units with no load management. The minimum feeder ampacity and overcurrent protection rating calculation is based on:","o":["120 A","160 A","200 A","240 A"],"a":2,"e":"4 x 40 A = 160 A of continuous load, times 125 percent = 200 A.","r":"NEC 625.41, 625.42","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A feeder supplies six 32-ampere EVSE units with no load management. What is the minimum standard overcurrent device?","o":["200 A","225 A","300 A","250 A"],"a":3,"e":"6 x 32 A = 192 A; x 1.25 = 240 A, which is not a standard size, so the next standard size, 250 A, is used.","r":"NEC 625.41, 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A feeder supplies 100 A of noncontinuous load and one 48-ampere EVSE. The minimum feeder ampacity is:","o":["160 A","185 A","148 A","200 A"],"a":0,"e":"Noncontinuous at 100 percent plus continuous at 125 percent: 100 + (48 x 1.25) = 160 A.","r":"NEC 215.2(A)(1), 625.42","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":1,"q":"A continuous load is one where the maximum current is expected to continue for how long?","o":["5 hours or more","1 hour or more","15 minutes or more","3 hours or more"],"a":3,"e":"The NEC defines a continuous load as one expected to continue for 3 hours or more.","r":"NEC Article 100","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A standard breaker may supply a continuous EVSE load at 100 percent of its rating only when:","o":["The EVSE is below 20 A","It is always allowed","The breaker is AFCI","The assembly including the overcurrent device is listed for continuous operation at 100 percent of its rating"],"a":3,"e":"Unless the assembly is listed for 100 percent continuous operation, overcurrent devices are limited to 80 percent continuous loading.","r":"NEC 210.20(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"In a dwelling unit load calculation, each EVSE load is calculated at:","o":["7,200 watts or the nameplate rating, whichever is larger","1,500 watts","The nameplate rating times 80 percent","3,600 watts always"],"a":0,"e":"Section 220.57 sets the EVSE load at 7,200 W or the nameplate rating, whichever is larger.","r":"NEC 220.57","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A dwelling has an EVSE with a nameplate rating of 40 A at 240 V (9,600 VA). The load used in the calculation is:","o":["12,000 VA","7,200 VA","9,600 VA","8,000 VA"],"a":2,"e":"9,600 VA is larger than 7,200 VA, so the nameplate value is used.","r":"NEC 220.57","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A dwelling has an EVSE with a nameplate rating of 20 A at 240 V (4,800 VA). The load used in the calculation is:","o":["7,200 VA","4,800 VA","3,840 VA","6,000 VA"],"a":0,"e":"The load is the larger of 7,200 W and the nameplate rating, so 7,200 VA applies.","r":"NEC 220.57","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A dwelling has two EVSE units: one with a 9,600 VA nameplate and one with a 3,840 VA nameplate. Without load management, the total EVSE load is:","o":["14,400 VA","13,440 VA","9,600 VA","16,800 VA"],"a":3,"e":"9,600 VA for the first and 7,200 VA (the minimum) for the second: 16,800 VA.","r":"NEC 220.57","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A dwelling EVSE has a 7,680 VA nameplate (32 A at 240 V). The load used is:","o":["9,600 VA","6,144 VA","7,680 VA","7,200 VA"],"a":2,"e":"7,680 VA exceeds 7,200 VA, so the nameplate value is used.","r":"NEC 220.57","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 240-volt, 50-ampere receptacle for EV charging is installed in an attached garage of a dwelling. GFCI protection is:","o":["Required","Not required","Required only outdoors","Required only for 15 A and 20 A"],"a":0,"e":"Section 210.8(A) requires GFCI protection for 125-volt through 250-volt receptacles in dwelling unit garages and accessory buildings.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(2)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 125-volt, 20-ampere receptacle in a commercial (nondwelling) parking garage used for plugging in a portable EVSE must have:","o":["AFCI protection","GFCI protection for personnel","A twist-lock configuration only","No special protection"],"a":1,"e":"Section 210.8(B) requires GFCI protection for 125-250 volt receptacles in garages, service bays, and similar areas other than vehicle exhibition halls and showrooms.","r":"NEC 210.8(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 125-volt, 15-ampere receptacle on the exterior wall of a dwelling for plugging in an EV charger requires:","o":["Isolated ground","GFCI protection","No protection","AFCI only"],"a":1,"e":"Outdoor receptacles at dwelling units require GFCI protection.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"Receptacles installed for the connection of electric vehicle charging are required to have:","o":["Surge protection only","GFCI protection for personnel","No additional protection","AFCI protection only"],"a":1,"e":"Section 625.54 requires GFCI protection for personnel on receptacles installed for EV charging.","r":"NEC 625.54","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"The minimum cover for direct-buried Type UF cable supplying an outdoor 240-volt EV charging pedestal (not under a building or roadway) is:","o":["18 in.","24 in.","6 in.","12 in."],"a":1,"e":"Table 300.5 requires 24 in. for direct-buried cables outside the reduced-cover cases, such as GFCI-protected residential circuits of 120 V or less, 20 A or less.","r":"NEC 300.5, Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"The minimum cover for rigid metal conduit buried to feed an EV charging station, in a location not covered by a reduced-cover exception, is:","o":["12 in.","18 in.","24 in.","6 in."],"a":3,"e":"Table 300.5 requires 6 in. cover for rigid metal conduit and IMC.","r":"NEC 300.5, Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"The minimum cover for buried Schedule 40 PVC conduit feeding an EV charging station, in a location not covered by a reduced-cover exception, is:","o":["24 in.","12 in.","18 in.","6 in."],"a":2,"e":"Table 300.5 requires 18 in. for nonmetallic raceways listed for direct burial.","r":"NEC 300.5, Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 60-ampere, 208Y/120-volt three-phase EVSE is installed. Under Article 625, a separate EVSE disconnecting means is:","o":["Required, since it exceeds 50 A","Not required, since it is not over 60 A and not over 150 V to ground","Required, since it is three-phase","Required, since it is over 120 V line-to-line"],"a":1,"e":"The thresholds are more than 60 A or more than 150 V to ground. This unit is 60 A (not more) and 120 V to ground.","r":"NEC 625.43","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 100-ampere, 208Y/120-volt three-phase EVSE is installed. Under Article 625, a readily accessible disconnecting means is:","o":["Not required, since it is under 150 V to ground","Required only outdoors","Required only for cord-and-plug units","Required, since it is over 60 A"],"a":3,"e":"A disconnect is required for EVSE rated more than 60 A.","r":"NEC 625.43","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 60-ampere, 240-volt single-phase EVSE is installed. Article 625 requires a separate disconnecting means:","o":["Yes","Only if indoors","No, since it is not more than 60 A and is 120 V to ground","Yes, since it is 240 volts"],"a":2,"e":"The requirement applies above 60 A or above 150 V to ground; this unit meets neither condition.","r":"NEC 625.43","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 12-ampere portable EVSE circuit requires a minimum overcurrent device of:","o":["25 A","15 A","20 A","12 A"],"a":1,"e":"12 A x 1.25 = 15 A, a standard size.","r":"NEC 625.41","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 30-ampere EVSE requires a minimum overcurrent device of:","o":["50 A","30 A","40 A","35 A"],"a":2,"e":"30 A x 1.25 = 37.5 A. That is not a standard size, so 40 A is the minimum standard rating.","r":"NEC 625.41, 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 36-ampere EVSE requires a minimum overcurrent device of:","o":["60 A","45 A","50 A","40 A"],"a":1,"e":"36 A x 1.25 = 45 A, a standard size.","r":"NEC 625.41, 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 60-ampere EVSE requires a minimum overcurrent device of:","o":["60 A","100 A","80 A","70 A"],"a":2,"e":"60 A x 1.25 = 75 A, which is not standard, so the next standard size is 80 A.","r":"NEC 625.41, 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 72-ampere EVSE requires a minimum overcurrent device of:","o":["90 A","80 A","110 A","100 A"],"a":0,"e":"72 A x 1.25 = 90 A, a standard size.","r":"NEC 625.41, 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 36-ampere EVSE is on a 45 A circuit. Using copper conductors and 75 degrees C terminations, the minimum size is:","o":["4 AWG","10 AWG","8 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"The conductor must carry 45 A; 8 AWG copper is 50 A at 75 degrees C, while 10 AWG is only 35 A.","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1), Table 310.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 72-ampere EVSE needs conductors rated at least 90 A. Using copper with 75 degrees C terminations, the minimum size is:","o":["2 AWG","4 AWG","3 AWG","1 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"3 AWG copper is 100 A at 75 degrees C. 4 AWG is 85 A, which is insufficient.","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1), Table 310.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 12-ampere EVSE on a 15 A circuit may use which minimum copper conductor?","o":["16 AWG","14 AWG","10 AWG","12 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"14 AWG copper is acceptable for 15 A overcurrent protection and carries 15 A (60 degrees C) or 20 A (75 degrees C).","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1), 240.4(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 30-ampere EVSE needs a 37.5 A minimum conductor ampacity. Using copper with 75 degrees C terminations, the minimum size is:","o":["6 AWG","8 AWG","10 AWG","12 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"10 AWG copper is only 35 A at 75 degrees C, which is below 37.5 A. 8 AWG is 50 A.","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1), Table 310.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"An 80-ampere EVSE requires a 100 A circuit. Using aluminum conductors with 75 degrees C terminations, the minimum size is:","o":["1/0 AWG","3 AWG","1 AWG","2 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"1 AWG aluminum is 100 A at 75 degrees C. 2 AWG aluminum is only 90 A.","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1), Table 310.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"The minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for an EVSE circuit protected by a 40 A breaker is:","o":["12 AWG","8 AWG","10 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 10 AWG copper for overcurrent devices rated over 30 A through 60 A.","r":"NEC 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"The minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for an EVSE circuit protected by a 90 A breaker is:","o":["8 AWG","4 AWG","10 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 8 AWG copper for overcurrent devices over 60 A up to 100 A.","r":"NEC 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum continuous EVSE load on a 100-ampere branch circuit?","o":["80 A","90 A","100 A","70 A"],"a":0,"e":"80 percent of 100 A is 80 A.","r":"NEC 625.42","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum continuous EVSE load on a 40-ampere branch circuit?","o":["36 A","40 A","30 A","32 A"],"a":3,"e":"80 percent of 40 A is 32 A.","r":"NEC 625.42","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A dwelling has five EVSE units, each with a nameplate of 30 A at 240 V (7,200 VA), and no load management. The total EVSE load is:","o":["30,000 VA","36,000 VA","28,800 VA","43,200 VA"],"a":1,"e":"Each EVSE is 7,200 VA (equal to the minimum and the nameplate): 5 x 7,200 = 36,000 VA.","r":"NEC 220.57","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"Three 48-ampere EVSE units are controlled by a listed energy management system that limits their combined load to 80 A. The minimum feeder overcurrent device rating is:","o":["100 A","144 A","200 A","180 A"],"a":0,"e":"With energy management, the load used is the system's maximum, 80 A. Continuous at 125 percent is 100 A.","r":"NEC 625.41, 625.42, 750.30","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is NOT considered an electric vehicle under Article 625?","o":["An electric motorcycle","A neighborhood electric vehicle","An off-road golf cart","A passenger car"],"a":2,"e":"Off-road, self-propelled electric vehicles such as industrial trucks, golf carts, and tractors are excluded from the definition.","r":"NEC 625.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":1,"q":"Which vehicle type is included in the Article 625 definition of electric vehicle?","o":["Industrial forklifts","Neighborhood electric vehicles for on-road use","Airline ground support equipment","Electric boats"],"a":1,"e":"On-road automotive-type vehicles, including neighborhood electric vehicles, are included. Industrial trucks, ground support equipment, and boats are not.","r":"NEC 625.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"An EV with a bidirectional charger is used to back up a dwelling during an outage through transfer equipment, isolated from the utility. Which article's rules apply to the optional standby arrangement?","o":["Article 702","Article 701","Article 708","Article 700"],"a":0,"e":"Optional standby systems are covered by Article 702. Where interconnected with the utility, Article 705 applies.","r":"NEC 625.48, 702","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"An EV with bidirectional EVSE that operates in parallel with the utility must also comply with:","o":["Article 800","Article 760","Article 695","Article 705"],"a":3,"e":"Interactive (parallel) operation brings in Article 705 for interconnected power sources.","r":"NEC 625.48, 705","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"The minimum working space depth in front of a 120/240-volt panelboard supplying EVSE, with no live parts or grounded parts opposite, is:","o":["3.5 ft","2 ft","3 ft","4 ft"],"a":2,"e":"For nominal voltage of 0-150 V to ground (Condition 1), the minimum depth is 3 ft.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(1), Table 110.26(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":1,"q":"The minimum width of working space in front of electrical equipment such as an EVSE panelboard is:","o":["30 in.","24 in.","36 in.","20 in."],"a":0,"e":"The working space width must be the width of the equipment or 30 in., whichever is greater.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":1,"q":"The minimum headroom of working spaces for electrical equipment rated 1000 volts or less is:","o":["7 ft","8 ft","6 ft","6.5 ft"],"a":3,"e":"Working space headroom must be at least 6 1/2 ft (or the height of the equipment if greater).","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"In a dwelling unit garage, tamper-resistant receptacles are required for which receptacles?","o":["All 250-volt 50-ampere receptacles","Only receptacles on GFCI breakers","Only outdoor receptacles","Nonlocking 125-volt, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles"],"a":3,"e":"Section 406.12 requires tamper-resistant protection for nonlocking-type 125 V, 15 and 20 A receptacles in dwelling units, including garages. A 250 V, 50 A EV receptacle is outside that requirement.","r":"NEC 406.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"EVSE is listed and marked for mounting with its coupling means lower than the 18-inch indoor minimum. Is this permitted?","o":["Yes, because the height requirement applies unless specifically listed and marked for the location","No, never","Yes, in all installations","Only for commercial occupancies"],"a":0,"e":"The height requirement applies unless the equipment is specifically listed and marked for the location.","r":"NEC 625.50","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"Under the general branch-circuit rules, outlets installed for the purpose of charging electric vehicles must be supplied by:","o":["A multiwire branch circuit shared with a dryer","A lighting circuit only","Any available general-use circuit","A separate (individual) branch circuit with no other outlets"],"a":3,"e":"Section 210.17 requires a separate branch circuit with no other outlets for EV charging outlets.","r":"NEC 210.17","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":1,"q":"An electric vehicle under Article 625 is primarily powered by an electric motor that draws current from:","o":["A utility overhead line only","A rechargeable storage battery, fuel cell, photovoltaic array, or other source of electric current","A diesel generator only","A gasoline engine only"],"a":1,"e":"The definition lists a rechargeable battery, fuel cell, PV array, or other source of electric current.","r":"NEC 625.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"A 100-ampere EVSE requires a minimum overcurrent device of:","o":["110 A","150 A","125 A","100 A"],"a":2,"e":"100 A x 1.25 = 125 A, a standard size.","r":"NEC 625.41, 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A 100-ampere EVSE requires 125 A conductor ampacity. Using copper with 75 degrees C terminations, the minimum size is:","o":["2/0 AWG","2 AWG","1 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"1 AWG copper is 130 A at 75 degrees C. 2 AWG is only 115 A.","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1), Table 310.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"The minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for an EVSE circuit protected by a 125 A breaker is:","o":["8 AWG","4 AWG","3 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 6 AWG copper for overcurrent devices over 100 A up to 200 A.","r":"NEC 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum continuous EVSE load on a 125-ampere branch circuit?","o":["110 A","100 A","125 A","90 A"],"a":1,"e":"80 percent of 125 A is 100 A.","r":"NEC 625.42","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A dwelling has a 120-volt, 12-ampere EVSE (1,440 VA nameplate). The EVSE load in the service calculation is:","o":["3,600 VA","1,440 VA","1,800 VA","7,200 VA"],"a":3,"e":"The load is 7,200 W or the nameplate rating, whichever is larger, so 7,200 VA.","r":"NEC 220.57","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"The minimum cover for direct-buried cable supplying a GFCI-protected 120-volt, 20-ampere residential branch circuit for a portable EV charger is:","o":["18 in.","12 in.","24 in.","6 in."],"a":1,"e":"Table 300.5 allows 12 in. for residential branch circuits of 120 V or less with GFCI protection and maximum 20 A overcurrent protection.","r":"NEC Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":1,"q":"Electric vehicle supply equipment is installed specifically for the purpose of:","o":["Charging only portable tools","Lighting the parking area","Supplying the building service","Transferring energy between the premises wiring and the electric vehicle"],"a":3,"e":"EVSE is the equipment that transfers energy between premises wiring and the vehicle.","r":"NEC 625.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":1,"q":"Article 630 applies to which equipment?","o":["Electric arc welders, resistance welders, and similar welding equipment connected to an electric supply system","Electrolytic cells","Electric furnaces for melting metal","Only portable gas welding torches"],"a":0,"e":"Article 630 covers electric arc welders, resistance welders, and similar welding equipment connected to a supply circuit.","r":"NEC 630.1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":1,"q":"For a transformer-type (non-motor-generator) arc welder, the Table 630.11(A) multiplier for a 100 percent duty cycle is:","o":["0.71","0.95","1.00","0.84"],"a":2,"e":"At 100 percent duty cycle the multiplier is 1.00.","r":"NEC 630.11(A), Table 630.11(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":2,"q":"For a transformer-type arc welder, the Table 630.11(A) multiplier for a 50 percent duty cycle is:","o":["0.71","0.63","0.84","0.55"],"a":0,"e":"The multiplier at a 50 percent duty cycle is 0.71.","r":"NEC 630.11(A), Table 630.11(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":2,"q":"For a transformer-type arc welder, the Table 630.11(A) multiplier for a 70 percent duty cycle is:","o":["0.78","0.89","0.71","0.84"],"a":3,"e":"The multiplier at 70 percent duty cycle is 0.84.","r":"NEC 630.11(A), Table 630.11(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":3,"q":"A transformer-type arc welder has a rated primary current of 60 A and a 60 percent duty cycle (multiplier 0.78). The minimum supply conductor ampacity is:","o":["52.2 A","36.0 A","60.0 A","46.8 A"],"a":3,"e":"60 A x 0.78 = 46.8 A.","r":"NEC 630.11(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":3,"q":"A transformer-type arc welder has a rated primary current of 40 A and a 30 percent duty cycle (multiplier 0.55). The minimum supply conductor ampacity is:","o":["28 A","22 A","12 A","40 A"],"a":1,"e":"40 A x 0.55 = 22 A.","r":"NEC 630.11(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":2,"q":"For conductors supplying a group of arc welders, the ampacity is based on which percentages of the individual welder currents?","o":["100% of the two largest, 85% of the third, 70% of the fourth, 60% of the rest","100% of the largest and 50% of the rest","100% of all welders","80% of all welders"],"a":0,"e":"Section 630.11(B) uses 100, 100, 85, 70 percent for the four largest and 60 percent for the remainder.","r":"NEC 630.11(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":3,"q":"Four arc welders each have a determined supply current of 40 A. The minimum ampacity of the conductors supplying the group is:","o":["142 A","96 A","160 A","120 A"],"a":0,"e":"40 + 40 + (0.85 x 40) + (0.70 x 40) = 40 + 40 + 34 + 28 = 142 A.","r":"NEC 630.11(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":2,"q":"Overcurrent protection for an arc welder must be rated or set at not more than what percentage of the welder's rated primary current (I1max)?","o":["200 percent","150 percent","125 percent","300 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Arc welders are protected at not more than 200 percent of I1max or the rated primary current. The 300 percent figure applies to resistance welders.","r":"NEC 630.12(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":3,"q":"An arc welder has a rated primary current of 45 A. What is the maximum standard overcurrent device size?","o":["80 A","90 A","70 A","100 A"],"a":1,"e":"200 percent of 45 A = 90 A, which is a standard size.","r":"NEC 630.12(A), 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":3,"q":"An arc welder has a rated primary current of 42 A. 200 percent is 84 A, which is not a standard size. What may be used?","o":["80 A only","125 A","The next higher standard size, 90 A","100 A"],"a":2,"e":"Where the calculated value does not correspond to a standard rating, the next higher standard rating is permitted: 90 A.","r":"NEC 630.12(A), 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":2,"q":"A disconnecting means for an arc welder must be provided:","o":["Only inside the welding cable","Only for motor-generator welders","Only if the welder is over 100 A","In the supply circuit, unless the welder has an integral disconnect"],"a":3,"e":"A disconnect (switch or circuit breaker) is required in the supply circuit for each arc welder not equipped with an integral disconnecting means.","r":"NEC 630.13","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":2,"q":"Which of the following must be marked on an arc welder nameplate?","o":["Maximum open-circuit voltage","Installer's name","Operator license number","Installation date"],"a":0,"e":"The nameplate includes the manufacturer, frequency, phases, primary voltage, rated primary current, maximum open-circuit voltage, and rated secondary current.","r":"NEC 630.14","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":2,"q":"The supply conductors for an individual seam or automatically fed resistance welder must have ampacity of at least what percentage of rated primary current (when duty cycle is not otherwise established)?","o":["85 percent","50 percent","100 percent","70 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Seam and automatically fed welders use 70 percent of the rated primary current.","r":"NEC 630.31(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":2,"q":"The supply conductors for an individual manually operated nonautomatic resistance welder must have ampacity of at least what percentage of rated primary current (when duty cycle is not otherwise established)?","o":["60 percent","100 percent","50 percent","70 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Manually operated nonautomatic welders use 50 percent of the rated primary current.","r":"NEC 630.31(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":2,"q":"A seam resistance welder has a rated primary current of 60 A and no established duty cycle. The minimum supply conductor ampacity is:","o":["42 A","60 A","30 A","48 A"],"a":0,"e":"Seam welders use 70 percent: 60 x 0.70 = 42 A.","r":"NEC 630.31(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":2,"q":"For a resistance welder with a known 25 percent duty cycle, the Table 630.31(A)(2) multiplier is:","o":["0.50","0.45","0.63","0.39"],"a":0,"e":"Table 630.31(A)(2) lists 0.50 for a 25 percent duty cycle.","r":"NEC 630.31(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":2,"q":"For a resistance welder with a known 10 percent duty cycle, the Table 630.31(A)(2) multiplier is:","o":["0.32","0.22","0.27","0.45"],"a":0,"e":"Table 630.31(A)(2) lists 0.32 for a 10 percent duty cycle.","r":"NEC 630.31(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":2,"q":"Conductors supplying two or more resistance welders must have ampacity of at least:","o":["The largest welder's value plus 60 percent of the others","The largest welder's value only","The sum of all values at 100 percent","75 percent of the sum"],"a":0,"e":"Section 630.31(B) uses 100 percent of the largest welder plus 60 percent of the values for all others.","r":"NEC 630.31(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":3,"q":"Three resistance welders have determined supply values of 50 A, 30 A, and 20 A. The minimum ampacity of the conductors supplying all three is:","o":["60 A","100 A","80 A","70 A"],"a":2,"e":"50 + 0.60 x (30 + 20) = 50 + 30 = 80 A.","r":"NEC 630.31(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":2,"q":"Overcurrent protection for a resistance welder must be rated or set at not more than what percentage of the welder's rated primary current?","o":["300 percent","125 percent","400 percent","200 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Resistance welders are protected at not more than 300 percent of the rated primary current.","r":"NEC 630.32","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":3,"q":"A resistance welder has a rated primary current of 50 A. The maximum overcurrent device rating is:","o":["200 A","150 A","100 A","125 A"],"a":1,"e":"300 percent of 50 A = 150 A, a standard size.","r":"NEC 630.32, 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":2,"q":"The ampere rating of the disconnecting means for a resistance welder must be at least:","o":["200 percent of the primary current","50 percent of the welder primary current","The welder secondary current","The supply conductor ampacity determined by 630.31"],"a":3,"e":"The disconnect rating may not be less than the supply conductor ampacity determined under 630.31.","r":"NEC 630.33","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":2,"q":"When may the supply circuit switch serve as the resistance welder's disconnecting means?","o":["When the circuit supplies only one welder","When the welder is portable","Never","When the circuit is over 480 volts"],"a":0,"e":"The supply circuit switch is permitted as the welder disconnect where the circuit supplies only one welder.","r":"NEC 630.33","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 630 Electric Welders","d":1,"q":"A resistance welder nameplate must include the rated kVA at what duty cycle?","o":["25 percent","100 percent","75 percent","50 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Resistance welder nameplates list rated kVA at 50 percent duty cycle.","r":"NEC 630.34","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":1,"q":"An audio loudspeaker is to be installed in a fire-resistance-rated ceiling assembly. Which condition must be met?","o":["The loudspeaker must be listed for the purpose, or installed in an enclosure or recess that maintains the fire-resistance rating","The loudspeaker must be installed in a metal raceway extending to the next floor","Loudspeakers are prohibited in fire-resistance-rated assemblies","Any loudspeaker is permitted if the opening is no larger than 100 mm (4 in)"],"a":0,"e":"Loudspeakers in fire-resistant-rated partitions, walls, or ceilings must be listed for the purpose or installed in an enclosure or recess that maintains the rating.","r":"NEC 640.25","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":1,"q":"What wiring methods are permitted for the power-supply wiring to audio amplification equipment?","o":["The wiring methods of Chapter 3 that apply to the conditions of installation","Only flexible cord, regardless of the location","Only audio cable listed under Article 640","Only Class 2 cables from Article 725"],"a":0,"e":"Power-supply wiring for audio equipment is installed with the Chapter 3 wiring methods; Article 725 methods apply to the amplifier output circuits.","r":"NEC 640.9(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":2,"q":"The output wiring that connects the amplifier to the loudspeakers in a permanent audio system is installed using Class 2 wiring methods. Which Article governs those wiring methods?","o":["Article 725","Article 690","Article 800","Article 760"],"a":0,"e":"Amplifier output wiring may be installed as Class 1, 2, or 3 wiring under Article 725 when the amplifier output meets the Class limits, or with Chapter 3 methods.","r":"NEC 640.9(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":1,"q":"Which parts of audio signal processing equipment must be grounded?","o":["Only equipment mounted in racks","Exposed metal parts of equipment likely to become energized","Only the amplifier output terminals","None, because audio equipment operates at low voltage"],"a":1,"e":"Exposed metal parts of audio equipment that may become energized must be connected to the equipment grounding conductor, regardless of the signal level.","r":"NEC 640.7(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":1,"q":"What does the NEC require regarding the location of amplifiers, loudspeakers, and other audio equipment?","o":["They must be installed at least 1.5 m (5 ft) from all power wiring","They must be located or protected so as to protect them from physical damage likely to result in a fire or shock hazard","They must be located at least 3 m (10 ft) above the floor","They must be installed only in enclosed rooms with locked doors"],"a":1,"e":"Article 640 requires location or protection to guard against physical damage that could create a fire or shock hazard.","r":"NEC 640.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":2,"q":"Audio equipment is installed above a suspended ceiling with removable tiles. Over time a bundle of speaker wiring is added across the opening. What does the NEC say about the accumulation of cables?","o":["Access to the equipment is required only for the first year after installation","Accumulation of cables is allowed if tagged with the date of installation","Access to equipment must not be denied by an accumulation of wires and cables that prevents removal of panels, including suspended ceiling panels","Cables may be bundled across any ceiling opening as long as they are bundled with cable ties"],"a":2,"e":"Wiring must not be installed so as to block removal of panels, including suspended ceiling panels, that give access to equipment.","r":"NEC 640.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":1,"q":"The working space and clearances about electrical equipment in an audio installation, such as a power distribution panel, are governed by which general rule?","o":["Section 725.24","Section 110.26","Section 300.4","Section 410.16"],"a":1,"e":"Article 640 refers back to 110.26 for spaces about electrical equipment, as it does for other electrical equipment operating at 1000 V or less.","r":"NEC 640.3, 110.26","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":1,"q":"What is the general workmanship requirement for audio system wiring and equipment?","o":["No installation standard applies to low-voltage audio","They must be installed exposed to permit inspection","They shall be installed in a neat and workmanlike manner","They must be installed by a manufacturer's representative"],"a":2,"e":"Mechanical execution of work in Article 640 requires neat and workmanlike installation, with cables supported by approved means.","r":"NEC 640.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":2,"q":"An audio amplifier output circuit is marked Class 2. Which of the following installation approaches is permitted for the loudspeaker wiring?","o":["Install the wiring as communications circuits under Article 805","Install the wiring with Type NM cable","Install the wiring in accordance with Article 725 requirements for Class 2 circuits","Install the wiring only in rigid metal conduit"],"a":2,"e":"Amplifier output wiring is permitted to be installed per Article 725 when it complies with the Class 1, 2, or 3 requirements.","r":"NEC 640.9(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":2,"q":"An audio equipment rack contains several pieces of cord-and-plug-connected equipment. The rack frame is metal. What is required for the rack?","o":["The frame must be connected to a separate driven ground rod only","No grounding is required if all equipment is double insulated","The metal frame must be grounded, either through an equipment grounding conductor or by bonding to a grounded path","The frame must be left floating to avoid hum"],"a":2,"e":"Metal parts of equipment racks that are likely to become energized must be grounded; hum is dealt with by approved methods, not by omitting grounding.","r":"NEC 640.7(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":2,"q":"To reduce noise in a sensitive audio system, a technician proposes to use isolated ground receptacles. Where permitted, how must the isolated ground receptacle be installed?","o":["It must not be grounded at all","It must be installed in accordance with 250.146(D), with an insulated equipment grounding conductor run with the circuit conductors","It must be connected to a separate ground rod with no connection to the equipment grounding conductor","It must be installed only in a nonmetallic box with no grounding"],"a":1,"e":"Isolated ground receptacles are permitted for noise reduction when installed per 250.146(D); the equipment grounding conductor is still required.","r":"NEC 640.7, 250.146(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":1,"q":"Article 640 applies to which type of equipment?","o":["Equipment and wiring for audio signal generation, recording, processing, amplification, and reproduction","Only equipment used in motion picture and television studios","Only equipment operating at less than 30 V","Only equipment installed in places of assembly"],"a":0,"e":"Article 640 covers permanent and portable audio systems used for generation, recording, processing, amplification, and reproduction of audio signals.","r":"NEC 640.1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":1,"q":"In Article 640, what is a loudspeaker?","o":["A device that combines input signals into a single output","A device that changes audio voltage levels by transformer action","A device that records electrical signals","A device that converts an electrical audio signal into acoustic output"],"a":3,"e":"A loudspeaker (speaker) converts audio signal energy into sound output; a mixer combines signals and a transformer changes levels.","r":"NEC 640.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":1,"q":"In Article 640, which device combines two or more input audio signals into one or more combined outputs?","o":["Loudspeaker","Audio autotransformer","Mixer","Microphone"],"a":2,"e":"A mixer combines input signals and is classified as audio signal processing equipment.","r":"NEC 640.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":2,"q":"Power-supply conductors and amplifier output (loudspeaker) conductors operating as Class 2 circuits are to be run in the same raceway in a permanent installation. What is the general approach under Article 640 and Article 725?","o":["They may share a raceway only if the raceway is nonmetallic","They may always be run together in the same raceway without restriction","They must be installed per the Article 725 separation requirements unless the Class 2 conductors are insulated for the highest voltage present","They may never be run in the same raceway under any condition"],"a":2,"e":"Class 2 and Class 3 circuit conductors are generally separated from power conductors unless rated for the highest voltage involved and permitted by 725.136.","r":"NEC 640.9, 725.136","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":2,"q":"Audio equipment is to be installed near a swimming pool. Which Article provides the controlling requirements for the equipment installed in or adjacent to the pool?","o":["Article 640 alone","Article 600","Article 680, in addition to Article 640","Article 725 alone"],"a":2,"e":"Audio equipment installed near pools, spas, and fountains must also comply with the applicable requirements of Article 680.","r":"NEC 640.10, 680","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":2,"q":"A loudspeaker line from a Class 2 amplifier output is to pass through a plenum space in a building. What is required for the cable?","o":["Any general-purpose cable is permitted in a plenum","Only Type NM cable is permitted in a plenum","It must be of a type permitted for plenums, such as CL2P or a type permitted by 725.154","Cable is not permitted in plenums"],"a":2,"e":"Class 2 cables installed in a plenum must be plenum-rated or installed in a wiring method permitted for plenums.","r":"NEC 640.9(C), 725.154","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":3,"q":"A technician installs a rack-mounted amplifier fed by a 120-V, 15-A branch circuit. The amplifier chassis is metal and bolted to a metal rack that is not otherwise connected to any grounding path. What should be done?","o":["Ground the rack to a water pipe only, with no connection to the supply EGC","Nothing, since amplifier outputs are low voltage","Bond the rack and chassis to the equipment grounding conductor of the supply circuit","Connect the rack to the grounded (neutral) conductor at the rack"],"a":2,"e":"Exposed metal parts that may become energized must be connected to the equipment grounding conductor; grounded-conductor connections at the load side are not permitted.","r":"NEC 640.7(A), 250.142","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":2,"q":"Which term describes audio system installations that are fixed in place and not routinely moved?","o":["Portable audio system installations","Mobile audio system installations","Temporary audio system installations","Permanent audio system installations"],"a":3,"e":"Article 640 distinguishes permanent audio system installations from portable and temporary installations.","r":"NEC 640.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":2,"q":"An installer is routing loudspeaker cable in an accessible attic. How should the cable be supported?","o":["It may be left lying loose on the insulation","It may be supported by attaching to the plumbing vents","By approved means, so that it is secured and not sagging against other cables or structural members","It must be supported at intervals not exceeding 100 mm (4 in)"],"a":2,"e":"Cables must be installed and supported in a neat and workmanlike manner by approved supports, not left unsecured.","r":"NEC 640.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":2,"q":"A loudspeaker is mounted in a location where it can be struck by moving equipment. What does Article 640 require?","o":["Loudspeakers must always be mounted at least 2.5 m (8 ft) above the floor","The loudspeaker must be marked with a warning sign only","No protection is required if the loudspeaker is fused","The loudspeaker must be located or protected to avoid physical damage likely to cause a fire or shock hazard"],"a":3,"e":"Equipment must be protected from physical damage likely to cause fire or shock hazards.","r":"NEC 640.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":1,"q":"Under Article 640, which of the following is permitted for loudspeaker output wiring from a Class 3 amplifier?","o":["Wiring installed under Article 800 only","Wiring installed under Article 760 only","Wiring installed under Article 690 only","Wiring installed under Article 725 as a Class 3 circuit"],"a":3,"e":"Class 3 amplifier output circuits may be wired according to the Article 725 rules for Class 3 circuits.","r":"NEC 640.9(C), 725","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":1,"q":"Why must wiring in a suspended ceiling not prevent removal of ceiling panels over audio equipment?","o":["Panels must be removable so the cables can be re-pulled each year","Access to the equipment must be maintained for service and inspection","Ceiling panels are required to be removed annually","To permit running additional power conductors above the ceiling"],"a":1,"e":"The NEC requires that access to equipment not be denied by cable accumulation preventing removal of panels, including suspended ceiling panels.","r":"NEC 640.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":2,"q":"A recessed ceiling loudspeaker is to be installed in a 2-hour fire-resistance-rated ceiling. Which installation satisfies the NEC?","o":["A loudspeaker listed for installation in a fire-rated ceiling, or one installed in an enclosure that maintains the 2-hour rating","A loudspeaker supported only by the ceiling tiles","A loudspeaker with its opening left open to the plenum","Any standard loudspeaker with a plastic baffle"],"a":0,"e":"Loudspeakers in fire-resistance-rated ceilings must be listed for the purpose or installed in an enclosure or recess that maintains the rating.","r":"NEC 640.25","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing","d":1,"q":"Which Article's wiring methods may be used for amplifier output circuits that meet Class 1, 2, or 3 limits?","o":["Article 300 only","Article 424","Article 800","Article 725"],"a":3,"e":"Amplifier output wiring may be installed per Article 725 when it complies with the Class circuit requirements.","r":"NEC 640.9(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":2,"q":"A branch circuit supplies several units of information technology equipment in an IT equipment room installed under Article 645. The total connected load is 40 A. What is the minimum ampacity of the branch-circuit conductors?","o":["50 A","60 A","44 A","40 A"],"a":0,"e":"Branch-circuit conductors supplying one or more units of IT equipment must have an ampacity of at least 125 percent of the total connected load: 40 x 1.25 = 50 A.","r":"NEC 645.5(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum length of a power-supply cord for cord-and-plug connected IT equipment under 645.5?","o":["6.0 m (20 ft)","4.5 m (15 ft)","3.0 m (10 ft)","1.8 m (6 ft)"],"a":1,"e":"Power-supply cords of IT equipment may not exceed 4.5 m (15 ft) in length.","r":"NEC 645.5(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is required for an information technology equipment room to use the alternative wiring methods of Article 645?","o":["A disconnecting means complying with 645.10","An engineered halon fire suppression system","A generator providing standby power","A fire sprinkler system in every room"],"a":0,"e":"645.4 lists conditions for use of Article 645, including a disconnecting means complying with 645.10, listed IT equipment, a separate HVAC system or protected dampers, and fire-rated separation.","r":"NEC 645.4(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is one of the conditions in 645.4 that an IT equipment room must meet for Article 645 to apply?","o":["The room must have no windows","The room must have a raised floor","The room must be located in a basement","The room is separated from other occupancies by fire-resistant-rated walls, floors, and ceilings with protected openings"],"a":3,"e":"645.4 requires the room to be separated from other occupancies by fire-resistant-rated construction with protected openings.","r":"NEC 645.4(5)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":2,"q":"The HVAC system serving an IT equipment room is also used to serve other occupancies in the building. Under 645.4, what is needed for the room to meet the conditions of Article 645?","o":["The HVAC unit must be located inside the room","The HVAC must be 100 percent outside air","Fire/smoke dampers must be installed at the room boundaries if the HVAC is not a separate dedicated system","Nothing, as shared HVAC is always permitted"],"a":2,"e":"The room must have a separate HVAC system, or fire/smoke dampers must be provided where the HVAC serves other occupancies.","r":"NEC 645.4(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":1,"q":"Where must the disconnecting means for IT equipment and HVAC systems in an Article 645 room be located?","o":["Only inside the IT equipment cabinets","Only at the service entrance of the building","At readily accessible locations at the principal exit doors","In the HVAC mechanical room only"],"a":2,"e":"The disconnecting means must be readily accessible at the principal exit doors and grouped and identified.","r":"NEC 645.10(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":2,"q":"When the disconnecting means for an Article 645 IT equipment room is operated, what else must occur?","o":["The HVAC serving the room must be disconnected and any required fire/smoke dampers must close","Nothing else is required","The fire alarm must be silenced","Only the lighting must turn off"],"a":0,"e":"The disconnecting means must also disconnect HVAC serving the room and close required fire/smoke dampers.","r":"NEC 645.10(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":3,"q":"An IT equipment room is built to Article 645. The designer proposes a single disconnect that serves both the IT equipment and HVAC at the principal exit door. Is this permitted?","o":["Yes, but only if located inside the room next to the equipment","No, the HVAC may never be disconnected by the same means","No, separate disconnects are mandatory at different doors","Yes, a single means is permitted to control both if grouped and identified"],"a":3,"e":"A single means to control both the electronic equipment and HVAC is permitted and the disconnects are to be grouped and identified.","r":"NEC 645.10(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":2,"q":"Under a raised floor in an Article 645 room, which cabling is permitted?","o":["Power cables, communications cables, connecting cables, interconnecting cables, and receptacles, if the floor is of suitable construction","Only listed communications cables","Only Type NM cable","No cables are permitted under a raised floor"],"a":0,"e":"645.5 allows these cables and receptacles under the raised floor where the area complies with the requirements (such as listing, ventilation, and protection).","r":"NEC 645.5(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":3,"q":"Cables pass through openings in a raised floor of an IT equipment room. What protection is required at the openings?","o":["Openings must be protected against abrasion of cables, such as by grommets or other approved means","Openings must be sealed solid with foam","Openings must be fitted with metal covers bolted down","No protection is required if cables are listed"],"a":0,"e":"Openings in raised floors for cables must be protected against abrasion and minimize the entrance of debris.","r":"NEC 645.5(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":2,"q":"Abandoned supply circuits and interconnecting cables in an IT equipment room covered by Article 645 must be:","o":["Left in place indefinitely","Removed unless contained in a raceway","Cut to 1.8 m (6 ft) and capped","Tagged and left in place under the floor"],"a":1,"e":"The abandoned cables must be removed unless they are contained in a raceway.","r":"NEC 645.5(G)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":1,"q":"How must exposed non-current-carrying metal parts of IT equipment systems be grounded?","o":["In accordance with Article 250 and applicable Chapter 2 requirements, through the equipment grounding conductor","They must be connected to the grounded conductor at the unit","They must be grounded only if the room is below grade","They need not be grounded if the equipment is listed"],"a":0,"e":"IT equipment metal parts that may become energized must be grounded and bonded in accordance with Article 250.","r":"NEC 645.15","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":2,"q":"Which protection must be provided for critical operations data systems under Article 645?","o":["Capacitor banks at the service","Arc-flash relays at every panel","Only a UPS system","Surge-protective devices at the service and distribution levels"],"a":3,"e":"Critical operations data systems must have surge protection provided per 645.18.","r":"NEC 645.18","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":3,"q":"In a critical operations data system, how must overcurrent protective devices be arranged?","o":["Be of the same rating throughout the system","Be omitted at feeders","Selectively coordinated with all supply-side overcurrent protective devices","Have instantaneous trip settings only"],"a":2,"e":"Overcurrent devices in critical operations data systems must be selectively coordinated with the supply-side devices.","r":"NEC 645.27","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":1,"q":"Which equipment must be installed in an IT equipment room under 645.4?","o":["Only unlisted equipment","Any electrical equipment from the building","Storage of combustible materials","Only listed information technology equipment and equipment needed for the operation of the IT equipment"],"a":3,"e":"645.4 requires listed IT equipment and limits the room to electrical equipment needed for IT operations.","r":"NEC 645.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":3,"q":"An IT room has a branch circuit with continuous loading from four servers drawing a total of 24 A. What is the minimum conductor ampacity under 645.5(A)?","o":["30 A","27 A","36 A","24 A"],"a":0,"e":"125 percent of 24 A = 30 A for Article 645 branch circuit conductors.","r":"NEC 645.5(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":2,"q":"Interconnecting cables between IT units are installed in an IT room covered by Article 645. What is required?","o":["Cables must be listed as suitable for the purpose, or enclosed in an approved wiring method","They must be buried","They must be installed in rigid metal conduit","Any unlisted cable is permitted"],"a":0,"e":"Interconnecting cables must be listed or installed in permitted raceways or wiring methods.","r":"NEC 645.5(C)","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":3,"q":"A room houses IT equipment and also a storage area for office supplies. Can the room use Article 645?","o":["Yes, storage is allowed","Yes, if the storage is under 10 percent of the area","No, 645.4 limits the room to IT equipment and equipment needed for its operation","Yes, if sprinklers are provided"],"a":2,"e":"The room is limited to IT equipment and electrical equipment necessary for operation.","r":"NEC 645.4","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":2,"q":"Branch-circuit conductors supply IT equipment in an Article 645 room with a total connected load of 32 A. What is the minimum conductor ampacity?","o":["32 A","40 A","48 A","35 A"],"a":1,"e":"Branch-circuit conductors must have ampacity not less than 125 percent of the total connected load: 32 x 1.25 = 40 A.","r":"NEC 645.5(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":1,"q":"Which equipment must be listed to qualify an IT equipment room for Article 645?","o":["The information technology equipment","The lighting fixtures","The building elevators","The roofing materials"],"a":0,"e":"645.4 requires listed information technology equipment.","r":"NEC 645.4(2)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":1,"q":"An IT equipment room under Article 645 requires disconnecting means to disconnect power to which items?","o":["Only the lighting","Only the receptacles","All electronic equipment in the room and the HVAC serving the room","Only the HVAC"],"a":2,"e":"645.10 requires means to disconnect power to all electronic equipment in the room and the HVAC.","r":"NEC 645.10(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum permitted length of a power-supply cord for IT equipment, in meters?","o":["1.5 m","6.0 m","3.0 m","4.5 m"],"a":3,"e":"The power cord must not exceed 4.5 m (15 ft).","r":"NEC 645.5(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":3,"q":"A 20-A, 120-V branch circuit supplies IT equipment in an Article 645 room. The load totals 18 A. Is a 14 AWG copper conductor (ampacity 15 A) at 60 degrees C acceptable?","o":["Yes, if GFCI protected","Yes, if copper-clad","No, 18 A x 125 percent = 22.5 A requires a larger conductor","Yes, 14 AWG is acceptable on 20-A circuits"],"a":2,"e":"Conductors must have ampacity of at least 125 percent of connected load, i.e., 22.5 A, so 14 AWG is inadequate.","r":"NEC 645.5(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","d":3,"q":"A critical operations data system is installed under Article 645. Which of the following is required?","o":["Capacitor banks","Isolated ground only","Surge-protective devices for the system","Cord lengths of 10 m"],"a":2,"e":"Surge protection is required for critical operations data systems.","r":"NEC 645.18","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"Under Article 680, a pool constructed on or above the ground that can hold water to a maximum depth of 1.0 m (42 in) is classified as which type?","o":["Hydromassage pool","Storable pool","Permanently installed pool","Therapeutic pool"],"a":1,"e":"A storable pool is on or above the ground with a maximum water depth of 1.0 m (42 in), or has nonmetallic, molded polymeric, or inflatable fabric walls regardless of dimension.","r":"NEC 680.2","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"An in-ground swimming pool at a dwelling holds water to a depth of 1.2 m (4 ft). How is it classified?","o":["Storable pool","Fountain","Spa or hot tub","Permanently installed pool"],"a":3,"e":"Pools constructed in the ground or partially in the ground, or any pool capable of holding water deeper than 1.0 m (42 in), and all pools inside a building, are permanently installed pools.","r":"NEC 680.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"What is the low voltage contact limit for sinusoidal AC under Article 680?","o":["12 V","15 V (RMS)","50 V","30 V"],"a":1,"e":"The low voltage contact limit is 15 V RMS for sinusoidal AC, 21.2 V peak for nonsinusoidal AC, and 30 V for continuous DC.","r":"NEC 680.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"Which term describes a structure intended for the support of a wet-niche luminaire, mounted in a pool wall?","o":["Forming shell","Bonding grid","Dry-niche enclosure","Junction shell"],"a":0,"e":"A forming shell is the structure designed to support a wet-niche luminaire assembly.","r":"NEC 680.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A luminaire is installed in a pool wall niche that is not filled with water and is designed so that service personnel can access it from a space behind the wall. What type of luminaire is this?","o":["Dry-niche luminaire","Wet-niche luminaire","Through-wall luminaire","No-niche luminaire"],"a":0,"e":"A dry-niche luminaire is intended for installation in the wall of a pool in a niche that is sealed against the entrance of water.","r":"NEC 680.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"Under Article 680, which one of the following is included in the definition of a fountain?","o":["Ornamental pools, display pools, and reflection pools","Irrigation canals","Drinking fountains","Swimming pools"],"a":0,"e":"Fountains include ornamental, display, and reflection pools; drinking fountains are not included.","r":"NEC 680.2","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A factory-fabricated unit consists of a spa vessel with water-circulating, heating, and control equipment all integral to the unit. What is it called?","o":["Field-assembled spa","Packaged spa equipment assembly","Hydromassage bathtub","Self-contained spa or hot tub"],"a":3,"e":"A self-contained spa has all circulating, heating, and control equipment integral to the unit.","r":"NEC 680.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A spa or hot tub is not drained and refilled after each use, whereas a bathtub designed for recirculating water is drained after each use. Which is correct?","o":["The tub drained after each use is a hydromassage bathtub","Both are spas","The tub drained after each use is a spa","Neither is addressed by Article 680"],"a":0,"e":"A hydromassage bathtub is designed to accept, circulate, and discharge water after each use; spas and hot tubs are not drained after each use.","r":"NEC 680.2, 680.70","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"Which of the following must be grounded under 680.6 for a permanently installed pool?","o":["Only electrical equipment in the dwelling","Only the underwater luminaire","Only the pool pump","All electrical equipment located within 1.5 m (5 ft) of the inside wall of the pool"],"a":3,"e":"Electrical equipment within 1.5 m (5 ft) of the inside wall, equipment associated with the recirculating system, junction boxes, transformers, GFCIs, and certain panelboards must be grounded.","r":"NEC 680.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A panelboard that is not service equipment is installed in a garage and supplies a pool pump and pool lighting. What does 680.6 require?","o":["No grounding is required if the panel is in a garage","It must be connected to the pool water","It must be grounded, since it supplies electrical equipment associated with the pool","It must have a separate ground rod only"],"a":2,"e":"Panelboards that are not part of the service equipment and supply pool equipment must be grounded.","r":"NEC 680.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A cord-and-plug connected fixed or stationary pool equipment is permitted. What is the maximum cord length and the required protection?","o":["4.5 m (15 ft), with AFCI protection","1.8 m (6 ft), with no GFCI","6.0 m (20 ft), with GFCI","3.0 m (10 ft), with GFCI protection"],"a":3,"e":"Cord-and-plug-connected pool equipment (other than underwater luminaires) is limited to 3.0 m (10 ft) and must be GFCI-protected.","r":"NEC 680.7","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"Insulated cables rated 0 to 750 V to ground are supported on and cabled together with a solidly grounded bare messenger. What is the minimum clearance in any direction to the water level and edge of an in-ground pool?","o":["6.9 m (22.5 ft)","4.5 m (15 ft)","3.7 m (12 ft)","7.5 m (25 ft)"],"a":0,"e":"Table 680.9(A) requires 6.9 m (22.5 ft) for such cables, and 7.5 m (25 ft) for all other cables 0 to 15 kV.","r":"NEC 680.9(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"What is the minimum clearance in any direction to the water level of a pool for open overhead conductors rated 0 to 15 kV to ground (not supported on a grounded messenger)?","o":["6.9 m (22.5 ft)","4.5 m (15 ft)","9.0 m (30 ft)","7.5 m (25 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"Table 680.9(A) specifies 7.5 m (25 ft) for all other conductors 0 to 15 kV.","r":"NEC 680.9(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"When is underground wiring permitted under a pool or within 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally of its inside wall?","o":["When necessary to supply pool equipment permitted by Article 680, or where space limits require it and a complete approved raceway is used","Never under any circumstance","Whenever the wiring is Type UF cable","Whenever buried 300 mm (12 in)"],"a":0,"e":"Underground wiring is not permitted in this area unless required for permitted pool equipment, with raceway systems used where space limitations exist.","r":"NEC 680.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"Space limitations prevent routing an underground feeder 1.5 m (5 ft) from a pool. Which installation is permitted?","o":["Direct buried MC cable","Direct buried Type UF cable","A complete raceway of rigid metal conduit, intermediate metal conduit, or nonmetallic raceway, with metal conduit corrosion resistant","Direct buried Type NM cable"],"a":2,"e":"Where space limitations prevent routing 1.5 m (5 ft) away, wiring is permitted in a complete raceway system of RMC, IMC, or a nonmetallic raceway, with burial per Table 300.5.","r":"NEC 680.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"What is required of rooms or pits housing electrical equipment for a pool, such as a pump room?","o":["The room must be located on the second floor","The room must have a floor of wood","The room must have no ventilation","Adequate drainage to prevent water accumulation during normal operation or filter maintenance"],"a":3,"e":"Electrical equipment cannot be installed in rooms or pits that lack adequate drainage to prevent water accumulation.","r":"NEC 680.11","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum horizontal distance from the inside wall of a pool for the maintenance disconnecting means (unless separated by a permanent barrier)?","o":["0.9 m (3 ft)","1.8 m (6 ft)","1.5 m (5 ft)","3.0 m (10 ft)"],"a":2,"e":"A maintenance disconnect must be readily accessible, within sight of the equipment, and at least 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally from the inside wall, unless separated by a permanent barrier providing a 1.5 m (5 ft) reach path.","r":"NEC 680.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is correct for the maintenance disconnecting means for pool utilization equipment other than lighting?","o":["It may be located remotely out of sight","It is not required for pool pumps","It need only disconnect one ungrounded conductor","It must simultaneously disconnect all ungrounded conductors and be readily accessible and within sight of the equipment"],"a":3,"e":"The disconnect must simultaneously open all ungrounded conductors and be readily accessible and within sight.","r":"NEC 680.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A pool equipment room has an unsealed circulation pump motor and chemical storage with limited ventilation. How is this area treated?","o":["As a Class I, Division 1 location","As an ordinary location with no special rules","As a corrosive environment, requiring wiring methods and equipment listed and identified for such areas","As a dry location"],"a":2,"e":"Areas where pool sanitation chemicals are stored and areas with unsealed pump motors with limited ventilation are corrosive environments.","r":"NEC 680.14","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Which wiring method is suited to a pool equipment room considered a corrosive environment?","o":["Bare copper conductors","Electrical metallic tubing with set-screw fittings","Rigid PVC conduit, RTRC, or other wiring methods listed and identified for the environment","Type AC cable"],"a":2,"e":"Wiring methods must be listed and identified for use in corrosive environments, such as PVC, RTRC, or other suitable methods.","r":"NEC 680.14","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A flexible connection to a pool pump motor is made with liquidtight flexible metal conduit. What is the maximum length where flexibility is necessary?","o":["3.0 m (10 ft)","0.9 m (3 ft)","1.8 m (6 ft)","4.5 m (15 ft)"],"a":2,"e":"Flexible connections to pool pumps using LFNC or LFMC are limited to 1.8 m (6 ft) for LFMC and LFNC where flexibility is necessary.","r":"NEC 680.21(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum length of the cord on a cord-and-plug connected permanently installed pool pump motor?","o":["1.5 m (5 ft)","6.0 m (20 ft)","4.5 m (15 ft)","3.0 m (10 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"Cord-and-plug-connected pool pumps are limited to 3.0 m (10 ft) and must have a grounding-type plug.","r":"NEC 680.21(A)(5)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"Outlets supplying pool pump motors on 15- or 20-A single-phase branch circuits rated 120 V through 240 V must have which type of protection?","o":["AFCI protection only","GFCI protection","Overload protection only","No protection is required"],"a":1,"e":"Outlets supplying pool pump motors on such circuits must be GFCI-protected, whether by receptacle or direct connection.","r":"NEC 680.21(C)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A 240-V, 20-A single-phase pool pump motor is directly connected (hardwired) to its branch circuit. Is GFCI protection required?","o":["Yes, but only if the pump is cord-connected","No, GFCI applies only at 120 V","Yes, because 680.21 requires GFCI protection whether by receptacle or direct connection","No, GFCI applies only to receptacle outlets"],"a":2,"e":"GFCI protection for pool pump motors applies to receptacle and direct-connected outlets on 15- or 20-A 120–240 V single-phase circuits.","r":"NEC 680.21(C)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A dwelling unit has a permanently installed pool. A general-purpose receptacle is required to be installed within what distance range from the inside wall of the pool?","o":["At least 0.9 m (3 ft) and not more than 4.5 m (15 ft)","At least 1.83 m (6 ft) and not more than 6.0 m (20 ft)","At least 1.5 m (5 ft) and not more than 3.0 m (10 ft)","At least 3.0 m (10 ft) and not more than 7.5 m (25 ft)"],"a":1,"e":"At least one 125-V, 15- or 20-A receptacle on a general-purpose circuit must be located 1.83 m (6 ft) to 6.0 m (20 ft) from the inside wall of the pool at a dwelling.","r":"NEC 680.22(A)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Which receptacles located within 6.0 m (20 ft) of the inside walls of a permanently installed pool require GFCI protection?","o":["All 15- and 20-A, 125-V through 250-V receptacles","Only 125-V receptacles rated 30 A","Only receptacles on the pool deck","Only receptacles outdoors"],"a":0,"e":"All 15- and 20-A, 125- through 250-V receptacles within 6.0 m (20 ft) of the inside wall of the pool must be GFCI-protected.","r":"NEC 680.22(A)(5)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum horizontal distance from the inside walls of a pool for a wall switch, unless separated by a solid fence, wall, or other permanent barrier?","o":["1.5 m (5 ft)","0.9 m (3 ft)","1.83 m (6 ft)","3.0 m (10 ft)"],"a":0,"e":"Switching devices must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally from the inside walls of the pool unless separated by a permanent barrier.","r":"NEC 680.22(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"An outdoor luminaire is to be installed over a new in-ground pool. What is the minimum height above the maximum water level if the luminaire is installed within 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally of the pool?","o":["4.5 m (15 ft)","3.0 m (10 ft)","3.7 m (12 ft)","2.3 m (7 ft 6 in)"],"a":2,"e":"Outdoor luminaires, lighting outlets, and ceiling-suspended fans over the pool or within 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally must be at least 3.7 m (12 ft) above the maximum water level.","r":"NEC 680.22(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A ceiling-suspended (paddle) fan is proposed for a covered patio, and its blades would be 3.0 m (10 ft) above the maximum water level directly over the pool edge. Is it permitted for a new outdoor installation?","o":["Yes, if the fan motor is listed for wet locations","No, it must be at least 3.7 m (12 ft) above the maximum water level","Yes, if GFCI protected","Yes, since it is over 2.5 m (8 ft)"],"a":1,"e":"New outdoor ceiling-suspended fans over the pool or within 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally need to be at least 3.7 m (12 ft) above the maximum water level.","r":"NEC 680.22(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"Underwater pool luminaires must not be installed for operation at more than what voltage?","o":["300 V","120 V","150 V between conductors","50 V"],"a":2,"e":"Luminaires must be installed for operation at 150 V or less between conductors.","r":"NEC 680.23(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A wet-niche luminaire operates at 120 V. What protection is required for its branch circuit?","o":["No protection, since it is listed","Overvoltage protection only","Arc-fault protection only","GFCI protection"],"a":3,"e":"Luminaires operating above 15 V (up to 150 V) must be supplied by a GFCI-protected circuit.","r":"NEC 680.23(A)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"What construction feature is required of a transformer supplying a 12-V underwater pool luminaire?","o":["It needs only a single winding","It must be nonlisted if under 100 VA","It must be listed for swimming pool and spa use, with an isolating winding with a grounded metal barrier or equivalent","It can be a standard autotransformer"],"a":2,"e":"Transformers for pool luminaires must be listed for the purpose with an isolating winding having a grounded metal barrier or equivalent.","r":"NEC 680.23(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"What is the minimum depth below the normal water level for the top of the lens of a wall-mounted underwater luminaire not identified for use at lesser depths?","o":["300 mm (12 in)","450 mm (18 in)","600 mm (24 in)","100 mm (4 in)"],"a":1,"e":"The top of the luminaire lens must be at least 450 mm (18 in) below the normal water level unless identified for use at lesser depths, and never less than 100 mm (4 in).","r":"NEC 680.23(A)(5)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A junction box serving a wet-niche luminaire is installed on a deck. What is the minimum elevation of the box above ground level or maximum water level, whichever gives the greater elevation?","o":["50 mm (2 in) above ground","100 mm (4 in) above ground or 200 mm (8 in) above the maximum water level","None; they may be flush","300 mm (12 in) above ground or water level"],"a":1,"e":"Junction boxes must be not less than 100 mm (4 in) above ground level or 200 mm (8 in) above maximum water level, whichever provides greater elevation.","r":"NEC 680.24(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Unless separated from the pool by a solid fence, wall, or other permanent barrier, how far must a junction box for a wet-niche luminaire be from the inside wall of the pool?","o":["At least 1.2 m (4 ft)","At least 0.6 m (2 ft)","At least 1.5 m (5 ft)","At least 3.0 m (10 ft)"],"a":0,"e":"The junction box must be not less than 1.2 m (4 ft) from the inside wall of the pool unless separated by a permanent barrier.","r":"NEC 680.24(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A feeder runs to a panelboard in a separate building to supply pool equipment. What equipment grounding conductor type is required?","o":["A bare aluminum conductor","The raceway alone","The grounded conductor","An insulated copper equipment grounding conductor installed with the feeder"],"a":3,"e":"Feeders to a separate building supplying pool equipment require an insulated copper EGC.","r":"NEC 680.25(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"What is the purpose of the equipotential bonding required around a swimming pool?","o":["To reduce voltage drop in the pump circuit","To reduce voltage gradients in the pool area","To provide a path for lightning","To increase the available fault current"],"a":1,"e":"Equipotential bonding is intended to reduce voltage gradients in the pool area.","r":"NEC 680.26(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum size and type of conductor used to make equipotential bonding connections for pool structural elements?","o":["No. 12 AWG insulated copper","No. 8 AWG or larger solid copper","No. 6 AWG aluminum","No. 10 AWG stranded copper"],"a":1,"e":"Bonding conductors must be solid copper, insulated, covered, or bare, not smaller than 8 AWG.","r":"NEC 680.26(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Perimeter surfaces to be bonded extend how far horizontally beyond the inside walls of the pool?","o":["1.5 m (5 ft)","3.0 m (10 ft)","0.6 m (2 ft)","1 m (3 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"The perimeter surfaces extend 1 m (3 ft) horizontally beyond the inside walls of the pool.","r":"NEC 680.26(B)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A copper conductor grid is used to bond the perimeter surface of a pool. What are the installation requirements?","o":["8 AWG or larger bare solid copper, 450–600 mm (18–24 in) from the inside wall and 100–150 mm (4–6 in) below the subgrade","10 AWG, 300 mm (12 in) from the wall and 300 mm below the subgrade","12 AWG, 1.5 m from the wall at the surface","6 AWG aluminum at the wall surface"],"a":0,"e":"The perimeter bonding conductor must follow the contour, 450–600 mm (18–24 in) from the inside wall and 100–150 mm (4–6 in) below subgrade.","r":"NEC 680.26(B)(2)(b)","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A conductive surface in contact with the pool water is required as part of the equipotential bonding. What is the minimum surface area?","o":["9000 mm2 (14 in2)","5800 mm2 (9 in2)","1450 mm2 (2.25 in2)","3200 mm2 (5 in2)"],"a":1,"e":"An intentional bond of a minimum conductive surface area of 5800 mm2 (9 in2) must be in contact with the pool water.","r":"NEC 680.26(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"Which fixed metal parts must be bonded within the pool area?","o":["All parts within 7.5 m (25 ft)","Fixed metal parts within 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally of the inside walls of the pool or within 3.7 m (12 ft) vertically above the maximum water level","Only parts within 0.6 m (2 ft) of the pool","Only the ladder and rails"],"a":1,"e":"Fixed metal parts within 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally or 3.7 m (12 ft) vertically above the maximum water level must be bonded.","r":"NEC 680.26(B)(7)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Which pool equipment is exempt from equipotential bonding?","o":["Metal ladders","Metal pool cover motors","Metal handrails","Listed double-insulated pool pump motors"],"a":3,"e":"Double-insulated pool pump motors are exempted, while metal ladders and rails must be bonded.","r":"NEC 680.26(B)(6)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A small metal part within the pool area is less than 100 mm (4 in) in any dimension, does not penetrate into the pool structure more than 25 mm (1 in), and is not connected to metal parts. Does it need to be bonded?","o":["Only if it is a ladder","Yes, all metal parts must be bonded","No, such small parts are exempt","Yes, if it is within 1.5 m (5 ft) of the pool"],"a":2,"e":"Small metal parts that meet the size and penetration limits are exempt.","r":"NEC 680.26(B)(5)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"An underwater audio speaker is installed in a permanently installed pool. What is required?","o":["Any waterproof speaker is permitted","The speaker must be listed for the purpose and installed in an approved metal forming shell, with proper wiring","The speaker must be plugged into a receptacle at the pool edge","The speaker must operate on 120 V"],"a":1,"e":"Underwater audio equipment must be identified for the purpose and installed in an approved forming shell.","r":"NEC 680.27(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Where must the motor and controller of an electrically operated pool cover be located relative to the pool?","o":["Within the pool water","At least 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally from the inside wall of the pool unless separated by a wall, cover, or other permanent barrier","At least 3.0 m (10 ft)","At least 0.6 m (2 ft)"],"a":1,"e":"The motor and controller must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally from the inside wall of the pool unless separated by a barrier.","r":"NEC 680.27(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Circuits for gas-fired pool water heaters operating above the low voltage contact limit require what protection?","o":["GFCI protection","AFCI protection","No special protection","Surge protection"],"a":0,"e":"Circuits for gas-fired water heaters operating at voltages above the low voltage contact limit must be GFCI-protected.","r":"NEC 680.28","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A cord-connected filter pump for a storable pool must incorporate what feature?","o":["An approved system of double insulation, with an equipment grounding conductor in the cord to ground only internal non-accessible metal parts","A bare metal housing with no insulation","A two-wire cord with no grounding conductor","A cord longer than 4.5 m (15 ft) for flexibility"],"a":0,"e":"Cord-connected filter pumps for storable pools must have double insulation and a grounding conductor in the cord that terminates in a grounding-type plug.","r":"NEC 680.31","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"What type of protection is required for electrical equipment connected to the branch circuits supplying a storable pool?","o":["GFCI protection","No protection is required","Surge protection only","Arc-fault protection only"],"a":0,"e":"Electrical equipment used with storable pools must be supplied by GFCI-protected circuits.","r":"NEC 680.32","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum distance from the inside wall of a storable pool to a receptacle?","o":["0.9 m (3 ft)","1.5 m (5 ft)","3.0 m (10 ft)","1.83 m (6 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"Receptacles must be at least 1.83 m (6 ft) from the inside walls of a storable pool.","r":"NEC 680.34","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A luminaire is installed in the wall of a storable pool. What is required?","o":["A luminaire is not permitted","A hardwired luminaire fed by Type NM cable","It must be part of a cord-and-plug-connected lighting assembly listed for the purpose, with a sealed lens and an attachment plug","Any wet-niche luminaire is permitted"],"a":2,"e":"Luminaires in or on the wall of storable pools must be part of a listed cord-and-plug-connected assembly.","r":"NEC 680.33","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Where must the emergency shutoff for a commercial spa or hot tub (not at a single-family dwelling) be located?","o":["Inside the spa pump room only","On the roof","Within 0.6 m (2 ft) of the spa","Readily accessible to the users, adjacent to and within sight of the spa, and at least 1.5 m (5 ft) away horizontally"],"a":3,"e":"A clearly labeled emergency shutoff for the recirculation and jet systems must be readily accessible, within sight, and at least 1.5 m (5 ft) away.","r":"NEC 680.41","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"Is the emergency shutoff switch for spas required at a single-family dwelling?","o":["Yes, always","Yes, only if over 50 A","No, the requirement does not apply at a single-family dwelling","Yes, only if outdoors"],"a":2,"e":"The emergency switch requirement for spas and hot tubs does not apply to those installed at a single-family dwelling.","r":"NEC 680.41","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum length of the cord on a cord-and-plug-connected self-contained spa or hot tub?","o":["7.5 m (25 ft)","4.6 m (15 ft)","1.8 m (6 ft)","3.0 m (10 ft)"],"a":1,"e":"Listed self-contained spas and hot tubs may be cord-and-plug-connected with a cord not longer than 4.6 m (15 ft), with GFCI protection.","r":"NEC 680.42(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A cord-and-plug-connected spa at a dwelling is installed outdoors. What protection is required for the receptacle?","o":["GFCI protection","Overvoltage protection","AFCI only","None, since it is listed"],"a":0,"e":"The cord-and-plug connection of a spa must be GFCI-protected.","r":"NEC 680.42(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"For an indoor spa, what is the minimum distance from the inside wall of the spa to a receptacle?","o":["1.83 m (6 ft)","1.5 m (5 ft)","3.0 m (10 ft)","0.9 m (3 ft)"],"a":0,"e":"Receptacles near indoor spas must be at least 1.83 m (6 ft) from the inside walls.","r":"NEC 680.43(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"An indoor spa is installed in a bathroom. A receptacle is located 2.4 m (8 ft) from the spa. Which is correct?","o":["It must be 3.0 m (10 ft) away","It is permitted without GFCI","It is permitted and must be GFCI-protected","It is prohibited"],"a":2,"e":"Receptacles at least 1.83 m (6 ft) away are permitted, and those within 3.0 m (10 ft) must be GFCI-protected.","r":"NEC 680.43(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Switches near an indoor spa or hot tub must be at least how far from the inside wall of the spa?","o":["0.6 m (2 ft)","1.0 m (3 ft)","2.0 m (6.5 ft)","1.5 m (5 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"Switches must be located at least 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally from the inside walls of the spa.","r":"NEC 680.43(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A 240-V, 40-A single-phase packaged spa equipment assembly is installed. What protection is required for the circuit?","o":["No protection, as the load is greater than 30 A","GFCI protection","Surge protection","AFCI protection only"],"a":1,"e":"Outlets supplying self-contained or packaged spas must be GFCI-protected.","r":"NEC 680.44","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"Which fountain installations are covered by Part V of Article 680?","o":["Natural lakes","Irrigation canals","Drinking fountains","Fountains, ornamental pools, display pools, and reflection pools"],"a":3,"e":"Part V of Article 680 covers fountains and similar installations, not drinking fountains.","r":"NEC 680.50, 680.2","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Fountain luminaires and submersible pumps operating above 15 V must have what protection?","o":["No protection","AFCI protection","Overvoltage protection","GFCI protection"],"a":3,"e":"Luminaires, submersible pumps, and other submersible equipment must be GFCI-protected unless they operate at 15 V or less.","r":"NEC 680.51(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A fountain includes submersible luminaires. What is the maximum operating voltage between conductors?","o":["50 V","300 V","240 V","150 V"],"a":3,"e":"Submersible fountain luminaires operate at 150 V or less between conductors.","r":"NEC 680.51(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Which metal parts of a fountain must be bonded?","o":["None, as fountains are not bonded","All metal piping systems and metal fittings within or attached to the fountain structure","Only the pump casing","Only the metal lamps"],"a":1,"e":"All metal piping and fittings within or attached to the fountain structure must be bonded.","r":"NEC 680.53","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Which equipment in a fountain installation must be grounded?","o":["Electrical equipment within the fountain or within 1.5 m (5 ft) of its inside wall, and equipment associated with the recirculating system","No equipment needs grounding","Only equipment submerged","Only the lighting"],"a":0,"e":"Equipment within or within 1.5 m (5 ft) of the fountain's inside wall, and recirculating system equipment, must be grounded.","r":"NEC 680.54","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"What type of flexible cord is required for cord-connected fountain equipment that is immersed in water?","o":["Parallel zip cord","Cord is not permitted","Extra-hard-usage type listed for the purpose","Light-duty lamp cord"],"a":2,"e":"Cords for fountain equipment must be extra-hard-usage.","r":"NEC 680.56","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"How must a hydromassage bathtub be supplied?","o":["By a circuit without GFCI protection","By an individual branch circuit protected by a GFCI","By a 30-A circuit only","By a shared circuit with other loads"],"a":1,"e":"Hydromassage bathtubs and their associated electrical components must be supplied by individual branch circuits protected by GFCI.","r":"NEC 680.71","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"What accessibility is required for electrical equipment of a hydromassage bathtub?","o":["It must be accessible without damaging the building structure or finish","It must be located inside the tub","It may be sealed permanently in the wall","No access is required"],"a":0,"e":"Hydromassage bathtub equipment must be accessible without damaging the structure or finish.","r":"NEC 680.73","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"Which bonding conductor size is used to bond metal piping and metal parts of a hydromassage bathtub?","o":["Solid copper not smaller than 8 AWG","14 AWG copper","12 AWG stranded copper","6 AWG aluminum"],"a":0,"e":"Metal parts must be bonded using a solid copper conductor not smaller than 8 AWG.","r":"NEC 680.74","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"What is the definition of the low voltage contact limit for continuous DC under Article 680?","o":["15 V","12 V","30 V","50 V"],"a":2,"e":"The low voltage contact limit is 15 V RMS sinusoidal AC, 21.2 V peak nonsinusoidal AC, and 30 V continuous DC.","r":"NEC 680.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A pool has a depth of 0.8 m (32 in), nonmetallic molded polymeric walls, and is 6 m (20 ft) across, sitting on the ground in a yard. How is it classified?","o":["Spa","Storable pool","Permanently installed pool","Fountain"],"a":1,"e":"A pool with nonmetallic, molded polymeric, or inflatable walls constructed on or above the ground is a storable pool regardless of dimension if the depth is within the limit.","r":"NEC 680.2","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A pool installed inside a building is 0.6 m (2 ft) deep. How is it classified?","o":["Hydromassage bathtub","Storable pool","Permanently installed pool","Fountain"],"a":2,"e":"All pools installed inside a building are permanently installed pools regardless of water depth.","r":"NEC 680.2","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"What is a wet-niche luminaire?","o":["A luminaire with a battery","A luminaire installed in a dry space behind the wall","A luminaire intended for installation in a forming shell mounted in a pool wall, completely surrounded by water","A luminaire installed above the water"],"a":2,"e":"A wet-niche luminaire is installed in a forming shell and is surrounded by water.","r":"NEC 680.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"What is a packaged spa or hot tub equipment assembly?","o":["A plumbing kit with no electrical equipment","A factory-fabricated unit with water-circulating, heating, and control equipment mounted on a common base, intended to operate a spa or hot tub","A spa vessel with all equipment integral","A bathtub with air jets"],"a":1,"e":"A packaged assembly includes pumps, heaters, blowers, and controls on a common base.","r":"NEC 680.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"Which of the following must be grounded under 680.6?","o":["Junction boxes and transformer or power supply enclosures for pool luminaires","Only the metal fence","Only the pool cover","Only the dwelling service"],"a":0,"e":"Junction boxes, transformer enclosures, GFCI enclosures, and related equipment must be grounded.","r":"NEC 680.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"Cord-and-plug-connected fixed pool equipment, other than underwater luminaires, must have which of the following?","o":["A grounding-type attachment plug and GFCI protection","A cord longer than 6 m","A two-prong plug","No GFCI if listed"],"a":0,"e":"Such equipment must have a grounding conductor and a grounding-type plug, with GFCI protection and cord length not exceeding 3.0 m (10 ft).","r":"NEC 680.7","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A service drop with insulated conductors on a grounded messenger rated 240 V crosses a yard that includes a pool. What is the minimum clearance to the pool water level?","o":["4.5 m (15 ft)","6.9 m (22.5 ft)","7.5 m (25 ft)","3.7 m (12 ft)"],"a":1,"e":"Per Table 680.9(A), insulated cables 0–750 V supported on a grounded messenger require 6.9 m (22.5 ft) to the water level.","r":"NEC 680.9(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"An electrician wants to bury a branch circuit feeding a garden luminaire under the pool deck 1.2 m (4 ft) from the pool wall. It is not required to serve pool equipment. Is it permitted?","o":["Yes, if GFCI protected","Yes, if buried 600 mm","No, because the wiring is within 1.5 m (5 ft) and does not serve pool equipment","Yes, if in conduit"],"a":2,"e":"Underground wiring in the restricted area is only for pool equipment (or limited exceptions).","r":"NEC 680.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A pool pump disconnect is located 1.2 m (4 ft) from the pool but is separated by a solid permanent fence that provides a reach path of 2.0 m (6.5 ft) to the pool. Is it permitted?","o":["No, it must be 3.0 m","No, it must always be 1.5 m horizontally","Yes, only if GFCI protected","Yes, because it is separated by a permanent barrier providing at least a 1.5 m (5 ft) reach path"],"a":3,"e":"A barrier that provides a 1.5 m (5 ft) or greater reach path allows closer installation.","r":"NEC 680.12","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"Which areas are considered corrosive environments under 680.14?","o":["Areas where pool sanitation chemicals are stored and areas with unsealed pump motors with limited ventilation","Any room with a dehumidifier","Garage interiors","All outdoor decks"],"a":0,"e":"Chemical storage and unsealed pump motor areas with limited ventilation are corrosive.","r":"NEC 680.14","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Which wiring methods are permitted to supply a pool pump motor outdoors?","o":["Type NM cable","Rigid metal conduit, intermediate metal conduit, rigid PVC conduit, RTRC, or Type MC cable listed for the location, among others","Bare conductors","Type NMC cable"],"a":1,"e":"Wiring to pool motors uses approved raceways or cable types listed for the location.","r":"NEC 680.21(A)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A 120-V, 15-A cord-and-plug connected pool pump has a 4.5 m (15 ft) cord. Is it compliant?","o":["Yes, up to 4.5 m is allowed","No, the cord is limited to 3.0 m (10 ft)","Yes, up to 6 m is allowed","Yes, if GFCI protected"],"a":1,"e":"The cord is limited to 3.0 m (10 ft) with a grounding-type plug.","r":"NEC 680.21(A)(5)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Outdoor lighting is to be installed over an in-ground pool at a dwelling. A luminaire is 4.0 m (13 ft) above the maximum water level directly over the pool. Is it permitted?","o":["No, it must be 6.9 m (22.5 ft)","Yes, since it is at least 3.7 m (12 ft) above the maximum water level","No, luminaires are never permitted over pools","No, it must be 4.5 m (15 ft)"],"a":1,"e":"A luminaire at least 3.7 m (12 ft) above the maximum water level is permitted over the pool.","r":"NEC 680.22(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A new switch controlling area lighting is installed 1.2 m (4 ft) from the pool wall with no barrier. Is it compliant?","o":["Yes, if rated for wet locations","No, it must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft) from the inside wall unless separated by a permanent barrier","Yes, if on a GFCI circuit","Yes, if the switch is listed"],"a":1,"e":"Switches must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft) from the pool inside wall unless separated by a barrier.","r":"NEC 680.22(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"A 20-A, 240-V receptacle for a pool pump is installed 4.5 m (15 ft) from the pool. What protection is required?","o":["No protection","GFCI protection","Combination AFCI/GFCI only","AFCI only"],"a":1,"e":"Receptacles rated 15 or 20 A, 125–250 V within 6.0 m (20 ft) require GFCI protection.","r":"NEC 680.22(A)(5)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A through-wall luminaire is installed so the top of its lens is 150 mm (6 in) below the normal water level. The luminaire is not identified for use at lesser depths. Is this acceptable?","o":["No, the top of the lens must be at least 450 mm (18 in) below the normal water level","Yes, the minimum is 100 mm (4 in)","Yes, the minimum is 150 mm (6 in)","Yes, any depth is acceptable"],"a":0,"e":"Unless identified for lesser depth, the lens must be at least 450 mm (18 in) below water level; no luminaire may be less than 100 mm (4 in) below.","r":"NEC 680.23(A)(5)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum depth below normal water level at which any underwater luminaire may operate, even if identified for lesser depths?","o":["25 mm (1 in)","450 mm (18 in)","50 mm (2 in)","100 mm (4 in)"],"a":3,"e":"No luminaire may be installed for operation at less than 100 mm (4 in) below the normal water level.","r":"NEC 680.23(A)(5)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":1,"q":"A junction box for a pool luminaire is installed at least how far above ground level?","o":["50 mm (2 in)","25 mm (1 in)","300 mm (12 in)","100 mm (4 in)"],"a":3,"e":"The box must be not less than 100 mm (4 in) above ground level or 200 mm (8 in) above the maximum water level, whichever is greater.","r":"NEC 680.24(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Which type of grounding conductor is required with feeders to a separate building panelboard that supplies pool equipment?","o":["Raceway only","Grounded conductor only","Insulated copper equipment grounding conductor","Bare aluminum"],"a":2,"e":"An insulated copper EGC is required with the feeder.","r":"NEC 680.25(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A pool has a metal fence 1.2 m (4 ft) from its inside wall, and the fence is separated by a permanent barrier so contact cannot occur. Must the fence be bonded?","o":["Required only for aluminum","Not required if it is isolated by a permanent barrier that prevents contact","Always required","Required only if over 2 m tall"],"a":1,"e":"Fixed metal parts separated from the pool by a permanent barrier are exempt.","r":"NEC 680.26(B)(7)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"What is required for an electrically operated pool cover?","o":["No GFCI is required","The motor must be 3.0 m away","The motor must be inside the pool","The motor and controller must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally from the pool, unless separated by a barrier; the circuit must be GFCI protected"],"a":3,"e":"Pool cover motors have a 1.5 m (5 ft) location requirement and GFCI protection.","r":"NEC 680.27(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Under Article 680, what is the maximum water level?","o":["The level 150 mm (6 in) below the deck","The highest level that water can reach before it spills out","The level at which the pool is normally kept","The level of the skimmer opening only"],"a":1,"e":"Maximum water level is defined as the highest level that water can reach before it spills out.","r":"NEC 680.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A 240-V, 20-A, single-phase pool pump is hardwired in a pump house with no receptacle. Which statement is correct?","o":["Only an overload relay is required","The circuit must be GFCI-protected","GFCI is not required because there is no receptacle","Only AFCI protection is required"],"a":1,"e":"GFCI protection applies to pool pump outlets on 15- or 20-A 120–240 V single-phase circuits whether by receptacle or direct connection.","r":"NEC 680.21(C)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A dwelling has a permanently installed pool. The only general-purpose receptacle outdoors is located 7.5 m (25 ft) from the inside wall of the pool. Does this satisfy the required receptacle?","o":["No, it must be within 1.5 m (5 ft)","No, one must be located between 1.83 m (6 ft) and 6.0 m (20 ft) from the inside wall","Yes, any distance is acceptable","Yes, if GFCI protected"],"a":1,"e":"At a dwelling, at least one receptacle must be within 6.0 m (20 ft) of the pool and at least 1.83 m (6 ft) away.","r":"NEC 680.22(A)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"At a dwelling, an electrician installs the required pool-area receptacle 1.5 m (5 ft) from the pool's inside wall. Is it compliant?","o":["Yes, if GFCI protected","Yes, if on a 20-A circuit","No, it must be at least 1.83 m (6 ft) from the inside wall","Yes, 1.5 m (5 ft) is the minimum"],"a":2,"e":"The required receptacle must be at least 1.83 m (6 ft) from the inside wall and not more than 6.0 m (20 ft).","r":"NEC 680.22(A)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A maintenance disconnect for a pool heater is installed on a wall 0.9 m (3 ft) from the inside wall of an in-ground pool with no fence or barrier between them. What is the result?","o":["Compliant since it is within sight","Compliant if weatherproof","Compliant if GFCI protected","Not compliant; it must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally unless separated by a permanent barrier"],"a":3,"e":"The maintenance disconnect must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft) from the inside wall unless separated by a permanent barrier.","r":"NEC 680.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A pool luminaire junction box is located 0.9 m (3 ft) from the inside wall of the pool with no barrier. What is the result?","o":["Compliant if the box is metal","Compliant if raintight","Compliant if 100 mm (4 in) above grade","Not compliant; it must be at least 1.2 m (4 ft) from the inside wall unless separated by a solid fence or wall"],"a":3,"e":"The junction box must be at least 1.2 m (4 ft) from the inside wall unless separated by a permanent barrier.","r":"NEC 680.24(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A junction box for a pool luminaire is mounted on the deck 4 ft from the pool. The maximum water level is 300 mm (12 in) below the deck, and the box bottom is 150 mm (6 in) above the deck. Is the elevation compliant?","o":["No, it must be 600 mm above the water","No, it must be flush with the deck","Yes, because it is more than 100 mm (4 in) above ground and more than 200 mm (8 in) above the maximum water level","No, it must be 300 mm above ground"],"a":2,"e":"The box is 150 mm above grade and 450 mm above maximum water level, exceeding both minimums.","r":"NEC 680.24(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"Open overhead conductors at 13.2 kV to ground cross a yard with an in-ground pool. The nearest clearance to the water level is 6.1 m (20 ft). Is it compliant?","o":["Yes, 4.5 m (15 ft) is required","Yes, 3.7 m (12 ft) is required","No, 7.5 m (25 ft) is required","Yes, 6.9 m (22.5 ft) is required"],"a":2,"e":"Table 680.9(A) requires 7.5 m (25 ft) for all other conductors 0–15 kV.","r":"NEC 680.9(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A pump motor cord is 3.7 m (12 ft) long and plugged into a grounded receptacle. Is it compliant?","o":["No, the cord may not exceed 3.0 m (10 ft)","Yes, up to 4.5 m (15 ft)","Yes, up to 6.0 m (20 ft)","Yes, if GFCI protected"],"a":0,"e":"The cord of a cord-and-plug connected pool pump is limited to 3.0 m (10 ft).","r":"NEC 680.21(A)(5)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A commercial hot tub at a hotel has its emergency shutoff switch 0.9 m (3 ft) from the spa. Is it compliant?","o":["Yes, if in the equipment room","Yes, if keyed","No, it must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft) away, within sight, and readily accessible","Yes, 0.9 m is acceptable"],"a":2,"e":"The emergency shutoff must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft) from the spa measured horizontally and within sight.","r":"NEC 680.41","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A 120-V self-contained spa has a 6.0 m (20 ft) power cord. Is this permitted?","o":["Yes, up to 9.0 m (30 ft)","Yes, any length if GFCI protected","Yes, up to 7.5 m (25 ft)","No, the cord may not exceed 4.6 m (15 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"Cord-and-plug-connected spas are limited to 4.6 m (15 ft) cords.","r":"NEC 680.42(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"An indoor spa has a receptacle located 1.2 m (4 ft) from its inside wall. What is the result?","o":["Compliant if listed","Compliant if 20 A","Compliant if GFCI protected","Not compliant; receptacles must be at least 1.83 m (6 ft) from the spa"],"a":3,"e":"Indoor spa receptacles must be at least 1.83 m (6 ft) from the inside wall.","r":"NEC 680.43(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A wall switch is installed 0.9 m (3 ft) from the inside wall of an indoor spa. What is the result?","o":["Compliant if rated 15 A","Compliant if GFCI protected","Not compliant; switches must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft) from the spa","Compliant if listed"],"a":2,"e":"Switches must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally from the inside walls of the spa.","r":"NEC 680.43(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"The bonding grid conductor used under the perimeter surface of a pool is 10 AWG solid copper. What is the result?","o":["Compliant, 10 AWG is acceptable","Compliant if bare","Compliant if aluminum","Not compliant; it must be 8 AWG or larger solid copper"],"a":3,"e":"Bonding conductors must be solid copper not smaller than 8 AWG.","r":"NEC 680.26(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"An aluminum ladder in contact with pool water provides the only water bond. Its contact surface with the water is 3200 mm2 (5 in2). Is it compliant?","o":["Yes, 1450 mm2 (2.25 in2) is the minimum","Yes, any contact is acceptable","Yes, 3200 mm2 is sufficient","No, at least 5800 mm2 (9 in2) in contact with the pool water is required"],"a":3,"e":"A minimum conductive surface of 5800 mm2 (9 in2) must be in contact with the pool water.","r":"NEC 680.26(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A metal fence post is located 1.2 m (4 ft) from the inside wall of a pool with no barrier to isolate it. What is required?","o":["Bonding only if the post is above 2 m tall","No bonding is required","It must be bonded, because it is a fixed metal part within 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally of the pool","Grounding only"],"a":2,"e":"Fixed metal parts within 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally must be bonded unless isolated by a permanent barrier or exempt.","r":"NEC 680.26(B)(7)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A receptacle is installed 1.2 m (4 ft) from a storable pool for a cord-connected pump. Is it compliant?","o":["Yes, if 20 A","No, it must be at least 1.83 m (6 ft) from the inside wall of the pool","Yes, if GFCI protected","Yes, 1.5 m (5 ft) is the minimum"],"a":1,"e":"Storable pool receptacles must be at least 1.83 m (6 ft) from the inside wall.","r":"NEC 680.34","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"How are structural reinforcing steel elements of a conductive pool shell typically bonded together?","o":["By nonconductive plastic ties","By steel tie wires or the equivalent, or by an 8 AWG bonding conductor","They are not bonded","By aluminum foil"],"a":1,"e":"Reinforcing steel is interconnected by steel tie wires or the equivalent per 680.26.","r":"NEC 680.26(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":2,"q":"In a land area subject to tidal fluctuation, where is the electrical datum plane located?","o":["A horizontal plane 600 mm (2 ft) above the highest high tide level for the area","300 mm (12 in) above the highest high tide","750 mm (30 in) above the water level","At the highest high tide level"],"a":0,"e":"In tidal areas the electrical datum plane is 600 mm (2 ft) above the highest high tide (100-year storm) level.","r":"NEC 682.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":2,"q":"In a land area not subject to tidal fluctuation, where is the electrical datum plane located?","o":["A horizontal plane 600 mm (2 ft) above the highest water level for the area, including flood stage","At the normal water level","1.5 m (5 ft) above the shoreline","300 mm (12 in) above the normal water level"],"a":0,"e":"For non-tidal areas, the EDP is 600 mm (2 ft) above the highest water level, including flood stage.","r":"NEC 682.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":3,"q":"For a floating pier, where is the electrical datum plane located?","o":["At the deck level","300 mm (12 in) above the water level","600 mm (2 ft) above the highest high tide","A horizontal plane 750 mm (30 in) above the water level at the pier, and at least 300 mm (12 in) above the deck"],"a":3,"e":"For floating structures and piers, the EDP is 750 mm (30 in) above the water level and a minimum of 300 mm (12 in) above the deck.","r":"NEC 682.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":1,"q":"Article 682 applies to the installation of electrical wiring and equipment in, on, under, or adjacent to which of the following?","o":["Natural or artificially made bodies of water such as lakes, streams, ponds, and canals","Swimming pools only","Marinas and boatyards only","Hot tubs only"],"a":0,"e":"Article 682 covers equipment in, on, under, or adjacent to natural or artificially made bodies of water; pools and marinas are covered elsewhere.","r":"NEC 682.1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":2,"q":"Which type of facility is NOT covered by Article 682 because it is covered by another article?","o":["Irrigation canals","Swimming pools covered by Article 680","Lakes","Ponds"],"a":1,"e":"Swimming pools, fountains, and similar installations are covered by Article 680.","r":"NEC 682.1, 680","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":1,"q":"What is the definition of the shoreline in Article 682?","o":["The edge of the dock","The property line","The normal water level","The farthest extent of standing water under the applicable conditions that determine the electrical datum plane"],"a":3,"e":"Shoreline is the farthest extent of standing water under the conditions that determine the EDP.","r":"NEC 682.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":1,"q":"What is submersible electrical equipment in Article 682?","o":["Equipment installed on shore","Equipment installed on a dock","Equipment intended to be operated while submerged in water","Equipment that is waterproof only"],"a":2,"e":"Submersible equipment is intended to be operated while submerged.","r":"NEC 682.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum horizontal distance from the shoreline for service equipment serving floating structures or submersible electrical equipment?","o":["3.0 m (10 ft)","0.6 m (2 ft)","7.5 m (25 ft)","1.5 m (5 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"Service equipment must be located no closer than 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally from the shoreline.","r":"NEC 682.11","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":2,"q":"All electrical connections, regardless of voltage, must be located at least how far above the electrical datum plane (unless listed for submersion)?","o":["900 mm (36 in)","150 mm (6 in)","300 mm (12 in)","600 mm (24 in)"],"a":2,"e":"Electrical connections must be at least 300 mm (12 in) above the EDP; submersible connections must be listed for the purpose.","r":"NEC 682.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":3,"q":"A junction box with wire splices at a lakeside pump station is mounted with its lower edge 150 mm (6 in) above the electrical datum plane and has no submersion listing. Is it compliant?","o":["Yes, if painted","Yes, if the box is rated NEMA 3R","Yes, 150 mm is adequate","No, connections must be at least 300 mm (12 in) above the EDP"],"a":3,"e":"All electrical connections must be at least 300 mm (12 in) above the EDP unless the connections are listed for submersion.","r":"NEC 682.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":2,"q":"What type and minimum size of equipment grounding conductor is required for circuits under Article 682?","o":["Bare copper 14 AWG","Insulated copper, sized per 250.122 but not smaller than 12 AWG","Insulated 14 AWG copper","Aluminum 10 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"EGCs must be insulated copper conductors sized per 250.122 but not less than 12 AWG.","r":"NEC 682.31","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":2,"q":"Which receptacles installed outdoors in or adjacent to a body of water require GFCI protection for personnel?","o":["Only 30-A receptacles","Only 240-V receptacles","Single-phase 15- and 20-A, 125-V receptacles","None"],"a":2,"e":"GFCI protection for personnel is required for 125-V 15- and 20-A receptacles.","r":"NEC 682.15","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":3,"q":"A 20-A, 125-V receptacle is installed on a pole beside a pond to power an aerator. What protection is required?","o":["None because it is outdoors","Surge protection only","AFCI protection only","GFCI protection for personnel"],"a":3,"e":"Receptacles of this type adjacent to bodies of water need GFCI protection for personnel.","r":"NEC 682.15","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":2,"q":"Electrical equipment and transformers installed in, on, or adjacent to bodies of water must be:","o":["Fused at 30 A only","Specifically approved for the intended location","Any general-purpose type","Painted white"],"a":1,"e":"Equipment and transformers must be specifically approved for the intended condition and location.","r":"NEC 682.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":1,"q":"Which flexible cord type is permitted for connection of portable equipment under Article 682?","o":["Extra-hard-usage portable power cable","Parallel zip cord","Lamp cord","Heater cord"],"a":0,"e":"Flexible cords used for portable equipment around bodies of water must be extra-hard usage.","r":"NEC 682.13","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":2,"q":"Which of the following wiring methods is permitted above the electrical datum plane under Article 682?","o":["Type NM cable","Type AC cable only","Rigid PVC conduit","Open wiring on insulators"],"a":2,"e":"Wiring methods such as rigid metal conduit, IMC, RTRC, PVC, and listed MC are permitted.","r":"NEC 682.13","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":2,"q":"Which grounding approach is required for the metal parts of equipment near a lake?","o":["No connection is needed","They must be floating","Non-current-carrying metal parts must be connected to an equipment grounding conductor","They must be connected to the grounded conductor only"],"a":2,"e":"Metal parts of equipment likely to become energized must be grounded.","r":"NEC 682.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":3,"q":"A metal part in contact with the water near a dock is not intended to carry current. What does Article 682 require?","o":["It must be connected to the neutral","It must be left unbonded","It must be insulated","It must be bonded to the equipment grounding conductor"],"a":3,"e":"Metal parts in contact with the water that are not intended to carry current must be bonded.","r":"NEC 682.33","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":3,"q":"A service pedestal for a floating dock is proposed 0.6 m (2 ft) horizontally from the shoreline, mounted 450 mm (18 in) above the electrical datum plane. Is the location compliant?","o":["No, it must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally from the shoreline","Yes, if GFCI protected","Yes, if grounded","Yes, it is above the EDP"],"a":0,"e":"Service equipment must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft) from the shoreline and its enclosure bottom at least 300 mm (12 in) above the EDP.","r":"NEC 682.11","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":3,"q":"A pond is in a non-tidal area. The highest flood stage elevation is 3.0 m (10 ft) above the normal water level. Where is the electrical datum plane?","o":["3.0 m (10 ft) above normal","3.6 m (12 ft) above the normal water level","0.6 m (2 ft) above normal","3.3 m (11 ft) above normal"],"a":1,"e":"The EDP is 600 mm (2 ft) above the highest water level including flood stage: 3.0 m + 0.6 m = 3.6 m.","r":"NEC 682.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is an artificially made body of water under Article 682?","o":["A bathtub","An irrigation canal","A fountain","A swimming pool"],"a":1,"e":"Irrigation canals are among the artificially made bodies of water covered by Article 682.","r":"NEC 682.1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":2,"q":"Which connections are permitted below the electrical datum plane?","o":["Submersible connections listed for the purpose","Taped splices","Any wire nut connection","Only soldered splices"],"a":0,"e":"Connections below the EDP must be listed as submersible.","r":"NEC 682.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":3,"q":"A feeder supplies a separate structure on a dock. What conductor must be installed with the feeder?","o":["An insulated copper equipment grounding conductor","No grounding conductor","Only the grounded conductor","A bare aluminum conductor"],"a":0,"e":"An insulated copper EGC is required with the feeder.","r":"NEC 682.32","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","d":2,"q":"A dock is in a tidal area where the highest high tide is 1.5 m (5 ft) above the mean water level. Where is the electrical datum plane?","o":["1.8 m (6 ft) above mean","1.5 m (5 ft) above mean","2.4 m (8 ft) above mean","2.1 m (7 ft) above the mean water level"],"a":3,"e":"EDP is 600 mm (2 ft) above the highest high tide level: 1.5 m + 0.6 m = 2.1 m.","r":"NEC 682.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A PV source circuit is a single string of series-connected modules. The module nameplate shows a short-circuit current (Isc) of 9.2 A. What is the maximum circuit current for this circuit under 690.8(A)?","o":["14.4 A","9.2 A","10.1 A","11.5 A"],"a":3,"e":"The maximum current of a PV source circuit is the sum of the parallel module Isc values multiplied by 125 percent: 9.2 x 1.25 = 11.5 A. The 14.4 A figure wrongly applies the 125 percent factor twice.","r":"NEC 690.8(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Three PV strings are connected in parallel to form a PV output circuit. Each string has a module Isc of 8.0 A. What is the maximum circuit current of the output circuit under 690.8(A)?","o":["24 A","37.5 A","27 A","30 A"],"a":3,"e":"Sum the Isc values (3 x 8.0 = 24 A) and multiply by 125 percent to get 30 A. Applying the 125 percent factor twice (37.5 A) is only done later when sizing conductors.","r":"NEC 690.8(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Two PV strings, each with a module Isc of 11 A, are connected in parallel. What is the maximum circuit current of the combined circuit per 690.8(A)?","o":["22 A","13.75 A","27.5 A","34.4 A"],"a":2,"e":"(11 A + 11 A) x 1.25 = 27.5 A. This is the 690.8(A) value before any 125 percent conductor or overcurrent device factor is applied.","r":"NEC 690.8(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"Five PV source circuits, each with a module Isc of 6.5 A, are combined in parallel at a combiner. What is the maximum circuit current of the PV output circuit under 690.8(A)?","o":["37.5 A","32.5 A","40.6 A","50.8 A"],"a":2,"e":"5 x 6.5 A = 32.5 A, multiplied by 1.25 gives 40.6 A. The 50.8 A answer applies a second 125 percent factor, which belongs to conductor ampacity sizing.","r":"NEC 690.8(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A single PV string has a module Isc of 10 A. Before applying any ambient temperature correction or conductor bundling adjustment, what is the minimum conductor ampacity required by 690.8(B)?","o":["15.6 A","20 A","10 A","12.5 A"],"a":0,"e":"The 690.8(A) maximum current is 10 x 1.25 = 12.5 A, and conductors must have an ampacity of at least 125 percent of that value before adjustment and correction: 12.5 x 1.25 = 15.6 A.","r":"NEC 690.8(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A single PV string has a module Isc of 9 A. What is the minimum conductor ampacity, before correction and adjustment factors, required by 690.8(B)?","o":["9 A","14.1 A","18 A","11.3 A"],"a":1,"e":"9 A x 1.25 = 11.25 A (maximum circuit current), then x 1.25 again = 14.06 A for the before-adjustment ampacity test.","r":"NEC 690.8(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"A PV output circuit combines four parallel strings, each with a module Isc of 10 A. What minimum conductor ampacity is required before ambient temperature correction and conductor adjustment factors are applied?","o":["78.1 A","50 A","40 A","62.5 A"],"a":3,"e":"Maximum circuit current is 4 x 10 x 1.25 = 50 A. The conductor ampacity before correction and adjustment must be at least 125 percent of that, which is 62.5 A.","r":"NEC 690.8(A), 690.8(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"The maximum circuit current of a PV source circuit, calculated per 690.8(A), is 20 A. If the conductors are evaluated using ampacity after ambient temperature correction and conductor adjustment, what is the minimum required ampacity after those factors are applied?","o":["31.3 A","20 A","16 A","25 A"],"a":1,"e":"690.8(B) gives two tests: 125 percent of the 690.8(A) current before adjustment, or 100 percent of the 690.8(A) current after correction and adjustment. The 690.8(A) value already includes the 125 percent Isc factor.","r":"NEC 690.8(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"A PV source circuit uses 10 AWG PV wire with a 90 C insulation rating (40 A in Table 310.16). The ambient temperature is 65 C (correction factor 0.65) and there are 4 current-carrying conductors in the raceway (adjustment factor 0.80). What is the adjusted ampacity of the conductor?","o":["32 A","40 A","20.8 A","26 A"],"a":2,"e":"Ampacity = 40 A x 0.65 x 0.80 = 20.8 A. This value is then compared with the 690.8(A) maximum circuit current.","r":"NEC 690.8(B), 310.15(B), 310.15(C)(1), Table 310.16","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"12 AWG PV wire rated 90 C (30 A in Table 310.16) is installed in conduit in a 50 C ambient (correction factor 0.82) with 6 current-carrying conductors (adjustment factor 0.80). What is its adjusted ampacity?","o":["24.6 A","30 A","19.7 A","24.0 A"],"a":2,"e":"30 A x 0.82 x 0.80 = 19.7 A. Using only one of the two factors overstates the ampacity.","r":"NEC 690.8(B), 310.15(B), 310.15(C)(1), Table 310.16","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"8 AWG conductors rated 90 C (55 A in Table 310.16) carry PV circuits in a raceway with 8 current-carrying conductors (adjustment factor 0.70) in a 45 C ambient (correction factor 0.87). What is the adjusted ampacity?","o":["38.5 A","55 A","47.9 A","33.5 A"],"a":3,"e":"55 A x 0.87 x 0.70 = 33.5 A.","r":"NEC 690.8(B), 310.15(B), 310.15(C)(1), Table 310.16","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A single PV string has a module Isc of 12.8 A. What is the minimum overcurrent device rating, as a calculated value, required by 690.9(B) if overcurrent protection is used?","o":["25 A","20 A","12.8 A","16 A"],"a":1,"e":"Overcurrent devices must be rated not less than 125 percent of the 690.8(A) current. 12.8 x 1.25 = 16 A, and 16 x 1.25 = 20 A.","r":"NEC 690.9(B), 690.8(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Two parallel PV strings each have a module Isc of 11 A. What is the minimum calculated overcurrent protective device rating for the PV output circuit under 690.9(B)?","o":["22 A","43 A","27.5 A","34.4 A"],"a":3,"e":"The 690.8(A) current is 27.5 A, and the overcurrent device must be at least 125 percent of it: 27.5 x 1.25 = 34.4 A.","r":"NEC 690.9(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Under 690.9(B), the rating of an overcurrent protective device in a PV system dc circuit must be at least what percentage of the maximum circuit current calculated under 690.8(A)?","o":["80 percent","125 percent","100 percent","156 percent"],"a":1,"e":"PV overcurrent devices are sized at not less than 125 percent of the 690.8(A) maximum current, unless the assembly is listed for continuous operation at 100 percent of its rating.","r":"NEC 690.9(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A PV system has one string of series-connected modules connected to an inverter. There are no batteries, no parallel strings, and no backfeed sources, and the module short-circuit current does not exceed the conductor ampacity or the module maximum series fuse rating. Is an overcurrent device required in the string circuit?","o":["Yes, a circuit breaker is required at the roof","Yes, but only in the positive conductor","No, overcurrent protection is not required under these conditions","Yes, a fuse is required in each string conductor"],"a":2,"e":"690.9(A) does not require overcurrent protection where there are no external sources of fault current and all available short-circuit currents are within the conductor ampacity and the module maximum series fuse rating.","r":"NEC 690.9(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"Two PV strings are connected in parallel. Each string has an Isc of 9 A, the conductors are rated 30 A, and the module maximum series fuse rating is 20 A. What does 690.9(A) require for string overcurrent protection?","o":["A circuit breaker rated 30 A on each string","A 15 A fuse in the positive conductor only","A fuse in each string rated 20 A","None, because the fault current from the other string (9 A) does not exceed the conductor ampacity or the module series fuse rating"],"a":3,"e":"The most backfeed current into a faulted string is the Isc of the one other string (9 A), which is less than both the 30 A conductor ampacity and the 20 A module rating, so overcurrent protection is not required.","r":"NEC 690.9(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"Three PV strings are paralleled. Each has an Isc of 10 A and the module nameplate maximum series fuse rating is 15 A. Under 690.9(A), is string overcurrent protection required?","o":["Yes, because backfeed from the two other strings (20 A) exceeds the 15 A module rating","No, because the conductors are 10 AWG","Yes, but only if the inverter is transformerless","No, because each string Isc is only 10 A"],"a":0,"e":"A faulted string can receive the Isc of the other two strings, 2 x 10 = 20 A, which exceeds the 15 A maximum series fuse rating of the module, so overcurrent protection is required.","r":"NEC 690.9(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"Overcurrent protective devices (fuses or circuit breakers) used in the dc portion of a PV system must be:","o":["listed for use in PV systems and rated for the dc voltage","any ac-rated circuit breaker of adequate ampere rating","rated for at least 120 volts ac","of the plug-in type only"],"a":0,"e":"Dc overcurrent devices in PV systems must be listed for PV use and rated for the dc voltage and interrupting duty; ac-only rated devices are not suitable.","r":"NEC 690.9(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"PV system dc circuits operating at a maximum system voltage of at least what value between any two conductors require PV arc-fault circuit protection?","o":["80 volts","150 volts","30 volts","50 volts"],"a":0,"e":"690.11 requires listed PV arc-fault circuit protection for dc circuits operating at 80 volts or greater between any two conductors.","r":"NEC 690.11","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"What type of protection satisfies the 690.11 dc arc-fault requirement for a rooftop PV system with a maximum voltage of 400 volts dc?","o":["A ground-fault circuit interrupter","A surge protective device","A standard 210.12 combination-type AFCI circuit breaker","A listed PV arc-fault circuit interrupter, or other listed equipment providing equivalent protection"],"a":3,"e":"690.11 calls for a listed PV arc-fault circuit interrupter or equivalent listed protection. Branch-circuit AFCIs under 210.12 are designed for ac circuits.","r":"NEC 690.11","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A PV system installed on a dwelling roof has a maximum dc system voltage of 400 volts. Is dc arc-fault circuit protection required?","o":["No, because the circuits are on the roof","No, because the system is under 600 volts","Yes, but only for the PV output circuit","Yes, because the maximum voltage is 80 volts or greater"],"a":3,"e":"The 80 volt threshold, not the 600 volt dwelling limit, triggers 690.11. The protection applies to PV source circuits, PV output circuits, or both.","r":"NEC 690.11","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A small PV system on a building has a maximum dc system voltage of 60 volts between any two conductors. Under 690.11, is PV arc-fault circuit protection required?","o":["No, because the voltage is below 80 volts","Yes, all PV systems on buildings require it","Yes, if the system is over 30 volts","Yes, if the array exceeds 1 kW"],"a":0,"e":"690.11 applies to dc circuits at 80 volts or greater between any two conductors. A 60 volt system is below the threshold.","r":"NEC 690.11","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"Under 690.12, controlled conductors outside the array boundary must be limited to not more than what voltage and power within 30 seconds of rapid shutdown initiation?","o":["80 volts and 240 volt-amperes","30 volts and 240 volt-amperes","50 volts and 500 volt-amperes","30 volts and 100 volt-amperes"],"a":1,"e":"Controlled conductors outside the array boundary must drop to 30 volts and 240 volt-amperes within 30 seconds. The 80 volt figure applies only to certain inside-the-array methods.","r":"NEC 690.12(B)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"How quickly after rapid shutdown initiation must controlled conductors outside the array boundary reach the required reduced voltage and power?","o":["Within 10 seconds","Within 30 seconds","Within 5 minutes","Within 60 seconds"],"a":1,"e":"690.12(B) gives 30 seconds from initiation of the rapid shutdown function.","r":"NEC 690.12(B)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"For purposes of 690.12, the array boundary is defined as a plane segment extending how far from the array in all directions?","o":["3 ft (1 m)","10 ft (3 m)","5 ft (1.5 m)","1 ft (305 mm)"],"a":3,"e":"The array boundary is 1 ft (305 mm) from the array in all directions. Inside a building, controlled conductors are measured from the point of entry (3 ft).","r":"NEC 690.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Inside a building, rapid shutdown limits apply to controlled PV conductors that are more than what distance from the point of entry into the building?","o":["5 ft (1.5 m)","3 ft (1 m)","10 ft (3 m)","1 ft (305 mm)"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors are controlled conductors if they are outside the array boundary or more than 1 m (3 ft) from the point of entry inside a building.","r":"NEC 690.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Which of the following PV installations is exempt from the rapid shutdown requirements of 690.12 under Exception No. 2?","o":["A PV system on a non-enclosed, detached structure such as a solar carport","A PV array on an enclosed detached garage","A PV array on the roof of a one-family dwelling","A PV array on the roof of a warehouse"],"a":0,"e":"2023 Exception No. 2 exempts PV equipment and circuits on non-enclosed detached structures such as carports. Enclosed buildings, including detached garages, are not exempt.","r":"NEC 690.12 Exception No. 2","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Which of the following may serve as the initiation device for the rapid shutdown function?","o":["A smoke detector tied to the inverter","A fused disconnect located at the array","Any branch-circuit breaker in the main panel","A readily accessible switch that plainly indicates whether it is in the on or off position"],"a":3,"e":"Initiation devices are the service disconnecting means, the PV system disconnecting means, or a readily accessible switch that plainly indicates on and off.","r":"NEC 690.12(C)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"For a one-family dwelling with a rooftop PV system required to have rapid shutdown, where must the initiation device be located?","o":["Inside the garage only","At a readily accessible location outside the building","Inside the attic near the inverter","At the array on the roof"],"a":1,"e":"For one- and two-family dwellings, an initiation device must be at a readily accessible location outside the building so emergency responders can operate it.","r":"NEC 690.12(C)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"Rapid shutdown requirements in 690.12 apply to controlled conductors of PV system circuits installed:","o":["only in systems above 600 volts","only on ground-mounted arrays","on or in buildings","only in underground raceways"],"a":2,"e":"690.12 is intended to reduce shock hazards for emergency responders and applies to PV system circuits installed on or in buildings.","r":"NEC 690.12(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"The PV system disconnecting means must be installed at a location that is:","o":["readily accessible","on the roof","behind a locked door only","within 6 ft of the array"],"a":0,"e":"690.13 requires the PV system disconnecting means to be installed at a readily accessible location.","r":"NEC 690.13(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A PV system disconnecting means must disconnect the PV system conductors from:","o":["only the utility service","only the grounded conductor","all other wiring systems, including power systems, energy storage systems, and utilization equipment","only the inverter"],"a":2,"e":"The PV system disconnecting means isolates the PV system from all other wiring systems, and must simultaneously disconnect the conductors that are not solidly grounded.","r":"NEC 690.13","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is required of the PV system disconnecting means under 690.13?","o":["It must be located on the load side of the inverter only","It must be externally operable without exposing the operator to contact with live parts and plainly indicate open or closed position","It must be a plug-and-receptacle connection","It must be a fused switch only"],"a":1,"e":"The disconnect must be a manually operable switch or circuit breaker, externally operable without exposing the operator to live parts, and plainly indicate its position.","r":"NEC 690.13(E)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"How must the PV system disconnecting means be marked?","o":["With a temporary tag during inspection","No marking is required if it is near the inverter","Permanently marked to identify it as a PV system disconnect","With a red handle only"],"a":2,"e":"690.13 requires each PV system disconnecting means to be permanently marked as a PV system disconnect.","r":"NEC 690.13(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A PV system disconnecting means must have ratings sufficient for:","o":["the maximum circuit current, the available fault current, and the voltage at the terminals","the module open-circuit voltage only","the inverter nameplate wattage only","the 125 percent value only"],"a":0,"e":"690.13 requires ratings sufficient for the maximum circuit current, available fault current, and voltage that is available at the terminals.","r":"NEC 690.13(D)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"If the line and load terminals of a PV system disconnecting means may both be energized in the open position, what must be provided?","o":["A GFCI device","A locked enclosure rated NEMA 4X","A second disconnect in series","A permanent warning marking indicating that both terminals may be energized in the open position"],"a":3,"e":"690.13 requires a warning at the disconnecting means where the terminals can remain energized when open.","r":"NEC 690.13(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"Equipment such as inverters, batteries, and charge controllers in a PV system must have an isolating means so the equipment can be:","o":["tested while energized","bypassed during load testing","isolated from all ungrounded conductors for servicing","grounded through the isolating device"],"a":2,"e":"690.15 requires a means to isolate equipment from all ungrounded conductors of all sources.","r":"NEC 690.15","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"An isolating device in a PV system that is not rated to interrupt the circuit current must be:","o":["marked to indicate not to open under load, or an equivalent warning","rated for 1000 volts","installed only inside the inverter","a molded-case circuit breaker"],"a":0,"e":"An isolating device is not required to have an interrupting rating, but where not rated for interrupting the circuit current it must be marked not to be opened under load.","r":"NEC 690.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is acceptable as an isolating device under 690.15?","o":["A plug-in cord set","A finger-safe fuse holder","A standard cartridge fuse in a bare clip","An ordinary snap switch rated 15 A"],"a":1,"e":"Acceptable isolating devices include listed connectors, finger-safe fuse holders, isolating switches that require a tool to open, and devices listed for the purpose.","r":"NEC 690.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"Which equipment must be listed or field labeled for PV application under 690.4(B)?","o":["Only the rooftop junction boxes","Only the array mounting rails","Only the utility meter","Inverters, PV modules, dc-to-dc converters, and PV charge controllers"],"a":3,"e":"690.4(B) requires listing or field labeling of PV-specific equipment such as inverters, PV modules, ac modules, dc-to-dc converters, and charge controllers.","r":"NEC 690.4(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"Who is permitted to install PV system equipment and its associated wiring and interconnections?","o":["Any homeowner","Only licensed roofers","Only qualified persons","Only utility employees"],"a":2,"e":"690.4 requires the installation to be performed only by qualified persons.","r":"NEC 690.4(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"May a PV system supply a building that is also served by a utility service?","o":["Yes, but only if the utility service is disconnected","Yes, but only in stand-alone mode","No, a building may have only one source of supply","Yes, a PV system may supply a building in addition to any other supply"],"a":3,"e":"690.4(A) permits PV systems to supply a building or structure in addition to any other supply system(s).","r":"NEC 690.4(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"Where must PV equipment and disconnecting means NOT be installed?","o":["In bathrooms","In garages","On roofs","In attics"],"a":0,"e":"690.4 prohibits PV equipment and disconnecting means from being installed in bathrooms.","r":"NEC 690.4","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"May multiple PV systems be installed on a single building?","o":["Yes, but only if all are the same size","Yes, but each must have its own service","No, only one PV system per building","Yes, and if they are remote from each other a directory is required per 705.10"],"a":3,"e":"Multiple PV systems are permitted on one building. When remote from each other, a directory in accordance with 705.10 is required.","r":"NEC 690.4","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"How are the inverter and dc wiring inside an ac module treated under Article 690?","o":["The dc wiring must be in metal raceway","The ac module output is treated as an inverter output circuit, and the internal wiring is not subject to the PV source circuit rules","The ac module must be wired as a bipolar system","The ac module needs separate dc arc-fault protection at each module"],"a":1,"e":"An ac module combines modules and an inverter, so the PV source circuit and inverter are internal to the module and the output is treated as an inverter output circuit.","r":"NEC 690.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"Which single-conductor cable types are permitted in exposed outdoor locations for PV module interconnections within the array?","o":["Type NM-B","Type THHN","Type USE-2 or listed PV wire/PV cable","Type SPT-2"],"a":2,"e":"690.31(C) allows USE-2 and PV wire or cable for exposed outdoor PV source circuit module interconnections within the array.","r":"NEC 690.31(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"PV system dc circuits that run inside a building must be contained in what, from the point of penetration to the first readily accessible disconnecting means?","o":["Metal raceways, Type MC cable, or metal enclosures","PVC conduit","Type UF cable","Type NM cable"],"a":0,"e":"Inside buildings, dc PV circuits must be in metal raceways, Type MC cable complying with 250.118(10), or metal enclosures.","r":"NEC 690.31(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"May PV system dc circuit conductors share a raceway or junction box with feeder conductors of a non-PV system?","o":["Only if rated 600 V","Yes, always","Yes, if they are the same color","Not unless the conductors are separated by a partition or are connected together"],"a":3,"e":"690.31(B) prohibits mixing PV dc circuits with conductors of other systems in the same raceway or box unless a partition separates them or they are connected together.","r":"NEC 690.31(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Where conductors of more than one PV system occupy the same junction box or raceway with a removable cover, how must each system be grouped?","o":["Only at the ends","With tape at every 12 in.","By cable ties or similar means at least once and then at intervals not exceeding 6 ft (1.8 m)","No grouping is required"],"a":2,"e":"Conductors of each PV system must be grouped at least once, then at intervals not exceeding 6 ft.","r":"NEC 690.31(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Flexible cords and cables used to connect the moving parts of a tracking PV array must be:","o":["any flat cord rated 300 V","Type SPT-1","listed for extra-hard usage, outdoor use, water resistance, and sunlight resistance","Type NM-B"],"a":2,"e":"Flexible cords and cables for tracking arrays must comply with Article 400 and be extra-hard usage and listed for outdoor use, water resistant, and sunlight resistant.","r":"NEC 690.31","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Junction, pull, and outlet boxes located behind PV modules must be installed so that the wiring in them can be rendered accessible by:","o":["cutting the roof deck","removing the roofing material","no access is required","displacement of a module secured by removable fasteners and connected by a flexible wiring system"],"a":3,"e":"690.34 requires boxes behind modules to be accessible directly or by displacement of a module secured by removable fasteners and connected by a flexible wiring system.","r":"NEC 690.34","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Readily accessible PV connectors in circuits operating at over 30 volts dc must require what for opening?","o":["A tool","No tool, they must be hand-separable","A two-person operation","A key interlock only"],"a":0,"e":"Readily accessible connectors in circuits over 30 volts dc or 15 volts ac must be of latching or locking type requiring a tool for opening.","r":"NEC 690.33","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"PV system connectors must be:","o":["unpolarized","interchangeable with ac receptacles","of the plug-and-cord type only","polarized and noninterchangeable with receptacles in other wiring systems on the premises"],"a":3,"e":"690.33 requires connectors to be polarized and configured so they are noninterchangeable with receptacles of other electrical systems on the premises.","r":"NEC 690.33","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"PV connectors must be constructed and installed so as to:","o":["be accessible with bare hands when energized","guard against inadvertent contact with live parts by persons","permit connection to an ac receptacle","omit locking features"],"a":1,"e":"Connectors must be guarded against inadvertent contact with live parts.","r":"NEC 690.33","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"If a PV connector includes a grounding member, it must be:","o":["the last to make and first to break","separate from the connector body","the first to make and last to break contact with the mating connector","omitted from the connector"],"a":2,"e":"A connector with a grounding member must be arranged so that the grounding member is first-make, last-break.","r":"NEC 690.33","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A PV connector that is not rated for interrupting current must:","o":["be installed only indoors","require a tool to open and be marked not to disconnect under load","be hand-disconnected under load","be rated 30 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"Connectors must either be rated for interrupting current or require a tool for opening and be marked accordingly, such as Do Not Disconnect Under Load.","r":"NEC 690.33","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"Mating PV connectors from different manufacturers may be used together only if:","o":["both are rated for 600 volts","they are installed in a raceway","they are listed and identified as intermatable","both have the same color"],"a":2,"e":"Where mating connectors are not of the identical type and brand, they must be listed and identified for intermatability.","r":"NEC 690.33","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"To determine the maximum PV system voltage, the open-circuit voltage of series-connected modules is corrected for:","o":["the highest expected ambient temperature","the average annual temperature","the lowest expected ambient temperature","the module operating temperature at noon"],"a":2,"e":"Module Voc rises as temperature falls, so 690.7 requires correction for the lowest expected ambient temperature.","r":"NEC 690.7(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A string has 12 modules in series, each with a rated Voc of 40 V. The temperature correction factor for the lowest expected ambient is 1.10. What is the maximum PV system voltage?","o":["528 V","480 V","576 V","440 V"],"a":0,"e":"12 x 40 V = 480 V, multiplied by 1.10 = 528 V.","r":"NEC 690.7(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"A string has 10 modules, each with Voc of 45 V at 25 C and a Voc temperature coefficient of -0.30 percent per C. The lowest expected ambient is -25 C. What is the maximum PV system voltage?","o":["567 V","450 V","477 V","517.5 V"],"a":3,"e":"Temperature change = 25 - (-25) = 50 C, so the voltage rise is 50 x 0.30 = 15 percent. 45 x 1.15 = 51.75 V per module, x 10 = 517.5 V.","r":"NEC 690.7(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum dc PV system voltage permitted on or inside a one- or two-family dwelling?","o":["300 volts","150 volts","600 volts","1000 volts"],"a":2,"e":"690.7 limits dc circuits on or inside one- and two-family dwellings to 600 volts or less.","r":"NEC 690.7","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"A string of 14 modules has a rated Voc of 37.2 V each and a lowest-temperature correction factor of 1.12. Is the string permitted on a one-family dwelling, and what is its maximum voltage?","o":["Yes, about 583 V, which does not exceed 600 V","No, about 650 V","Yes, about 520 V","No, about 730 V"],"a":0,"e":"14 x 37.2 = 520.8 V; x 1.12 = 583.3 V, which is within the 600 V dwelling limit.","r":"NEC 690.7","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"A string has 16 modules, each with a rated Voc of 36 V and a lowest-temperature correction factor of 1.14. Can this string be installed on a one-family dwelling?","o":["No, the maximum voltage is about 657 V, which exceeds 600 V","No, 1000 V is the maximum","Yes, 600 V is the maximum","Yes, 576 V is the maximum"],"a":0,"e":"16 x 36 = 576 V; x 1.14 = 656.6 V. That exceeds the 600 V limit for one- and two-family dwellings.","r":"NEC 690.7","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"On a one-family dwelling, the corrected Voc of each module is 50 V. What is the maximum number of modules permitted in a series string?","o":["11","10","13","12"],"a":3,"e":"600 V / 50 V = 12 modules, which results in exactly 600 V and is permitted.","r":"NEC 690.7","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"A PV string with an Isc of 8 A has no overcurrent device. For EGC sizing under 690.45, an assumed overcurrent device is used. Using the 690.9(B) value, what size copper EGC is required?","o":["12 AWG","14 AWG","8 AWG","10 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"8 x 1.25 x 1.25 = 12.5 A assumed device. Table 250.122 gives 14 AWG for a rating up to 15 A, and 14 AWG is also the 690.45 minimum.","r":"NEC 690.45, Table 250.122","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"Two parallel strings, each with an Isc of 10.5 A, have no overcurrent protection. Using the assumed overcurrent device value from 690.9(B) in Table 250.122, which copper EGC size applies?","o":["12 AWG","10 AWG","14 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"10.5 x 2 x 1.25 = 26.25 A, and x 1.25 = 32.8 A assumed device rating. Table 250.122 requires 10 AWG for devices up to 60 A.","r":"NEC 690.45, Table 250.122","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"A PV output circuit combines four strings each with an Isc of 10 A and has no overcurrent device. What copper EGC size applies using the assumed overcurrent device value of 690.9(B)?","o":["6 AWG","12 AWG","8 AWG","10 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"4 x 10 x 1.25 = 50 A; x 1.25 = 62.5 A assumed rating. That exceeds 60 A in Table 250.122, which requires 8 AWG copper.","r":"NEC 690.45, Table 250.122","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Must the PV equipment grounding conductor be increased in size to compensate for voltage drop?","o":["Yes, if installed in conduit","No, increases for voltage drop are not required","Yes, if the circuit exceeds 100 ft","Yes, always one size"],"a":1,"e":"690.45 states that increases in EGC size to address voltage drop considerations are not required.","r":"NEC 690.45","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Exposed non-current-carrying metal parts of PV module frames, electrical equipment, and conductor enclosures must be connected to an equipment grounding conductor:","o":["only above 150 volts","regardless of voltage","only on dwellings","only above 50 volts"],"a":1,"e":"690.43 requires bonding of exposed metal parts to an EGC regardless of system voltage.","r":"NEC 690.43(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Where PV circuit conductors leave the vicinity of the PV array, the equipment grounding conductor must be:","o":["run separately along the structure","omitted","run with the PV circuit conductors","replaced by a grounding electrode"],"a":2,"e":"The array EGC must be contained within the same raceway or cable, or otherwise run with the PV array circuit conductors, when they leave the array vicinity.","r":"NEC 690.43(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Which of the following may be used to bond PV modules to the mounting structure?","o":["Devices identified and listed for bonding PV modules","Painted rail clips","Unlisted self-tapping screws","Plastic spacers"],"a":0,"e":"Listed devices that bond module frames to mounting structures are permitted for equipment bonding.","r":"NEC 690.43","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"An equipment grounding conductor smaller than 6 AWG that is not routed with the circuit conductors must be protected from physical damage by:","o":["nothing, if it is bare","a raceway or cable armor, unless installed in hollow spaces of framing and not subject to damage","tape alone","a plastic sleeve"],"a":1,"e":"EGCs smaller than 6 AWG not run with the circuit conductors must be in an identified raceway or cable armor unless in framing hollow spaces and not subject to damage.","r":"NEC 250.120(C), 690.43","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Are auxiliary grounding electrodes permitted at ground-mounted or roof-mounted PV arrays?","o":["Yes, but they may not be used as a substitute for equipment bonding or grounding","Yes, and they replace the EGC","Only for systems above 1000 volts","No, they are prohibited"],"a":0,"e":"Auxiliary electrodes at arrays are permitted but do not take the place of required equipment grounding conductors.","r":"NEC 690.47","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A dc grounding electrode conductor for a PV system that is connected to a ground rod is not required to be larger than:","o":["8 AWG copper","3/0 AWG copper","6 AWG copper","4 AWG copper"],"a":2,"e":"250.166 limits the required size of a dc grounding electrode conductor to 6 AWG copper where connected to a rod, pipe, or plate electrode.","r":"NEC 250.166, 690.47","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A dc grounding electrode conductor for a PV system that is connected to a concrete-encased electrode is not required to be larger than:","o":["2 AWG copper","1/0 AWG copper","6 AWG copper","4 AWG copper"],"a":3,"e":"250.166 limits the required size of the dc grounding electrode conductor to 4 AWG copper where connected to a concrete-encased electrode.","r":"NEC 250.166, 690.47","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"A PV source circuit charges a 400 amp-hour battery and the maximum charging current multiplied by one hour is below 3 percent of the battery capacity. What is the maximum charging current that qualifies as self-regulated?","o":["Less than 8 A","Less than 16 A","Less than 40 A","Less than 12 A"],"a":3,"e":"3 percent of 400 Ah is 12 Ah. If charging current x 1 hour is below that, the source is considered self-regulated, provided it is within the battery manufacturer's recommended charge rate.","r":"NEC 690.72","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"A PV source circuit has a maximum charging current of 20 A for a 500 Ah battery. Is it considered self-regulated under 690.72?","o":["No, because 20 A for one hour exceeds 3 percent of 500 Ah (15 Ah)","Yes, if the battery is lead-acid","Yes, because 20 A is under 50 A","Yes, because 20 A is under 10 percent"],"a":0,"e":"3 percent of 500 Ah is 15 Ah, and 20 A x 1 hour = 20 Ah exceeds that limit, so charge control is needed.","r":"NEC 690.72","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"In a PV system, the circuit conductors between the PV source circuits and the inverter or dc utilization equipment are called the:","o":["branch circuit","inverter output circuit","feeder","PV output circuit"],"a":3,"e":"The PV output circuit is the circuit between the PV source circuit(s) and the inverter or dc utilization equipment.","r":"NEC Art. 100 (PV output circuit)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"A PV source circuit is the circuit conductors between:","o":["the battery and the load","the modules and from modules to the common connection point(s) of the dc system","the service point and the meter","the inverter and the service panel"],"a":1,"e":"A PV source circuit connects modules together and to the common connection point(s) of the dc system.","r":"NEC Art. 100 (PV source circuit)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"The total components, circuits, and equipment up to and including the PV system disconnecting means that convert solar energy into electric energy is called a:","o":["microgrid","PV array","PV source circuit","photovoltaic (PV) system"],"a":3,"e":"A PV system includes everything up to and including the PV system disconnecting means.","r":"NEC Art. 100 (Photovoltaic (PV) system)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"A complete, environmentally protected unit consisting of solar cells, an inverter, and other components designed to generate ac power when exposed to sunlight is a(n):","o":["PV array","ac module","dc-to-dc converter","charge controller"],"a":1,"e":"An ac module integrates the PV module and inverter into one unit with an ac output.","r":"NEC Art. 100 (alternating-current module)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A PV system dc circuit that has an electrical reference to ground that is not solidly grounded is described as:","o":["ungrounded","bipolar","functionally grounded","solidly grounded"],"a":2,"e":"A functionally grounded PV system has an electrical reference to ground that is not a solid connection to ground, such as through a ground-fault protection device.","r":"NEC Art. 100 (functionally grounded PV system)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"A device installed in the PV source or output circuit that provides an output dc voltage and current at a higher or lower value than the input is a:","o":["rectifier","dc-to-dc converter","charge controller only","isolation transformer"],"a":1,"e":"A dc-to-dc converter changes dc voltage and current levels between its input and output.","r":"NEC Art. 100 (dc-to-dc converter)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Four PV strings, each with a module Isc of 8.7 A, are paralleled in a combiner. What is the maximum circuit current of the PV output circuit under 690.8(A)?","o":["34.8 A","54.4 A","43.5 A","37.5 A"],"a":2,"e":"4 x 8.7 A = 34.8 A, multiplied by 125 percent equals 43.5 A.","r":"NEC 690.8(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Six PV source circuits, each with an Isc of 5.5 A, are combined in parallel. What is the maximum circuit current of the PV output circuit under 690.8(A)?","o":["27.5 A","41.25 A","51.6 A","33 A"],"a":1,"e":"6 x 5.5 = 33 A; x 1.25 = 41.25 A.","r":"NEC 690.8(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"Three parallel strings each have a module Isc of 9 A. What is the minimum calculated overcurrent device rating for the PV output circuit under 690.9(B)?","o":["52.7 A","33.75 A","42.2 A","27 A"],"a":2,"e":"3 x 9 = 27 A; x 1.25 = 33.75 A (690.8(A) maximum current); x 1.25 = 42.2 A minimum overcurrent device rating.","r":"NEC 690.9(B), 690.8(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"10 AWG conductors rated 90 C (40 A in Table 310.16) are installed in a raceway with 7 current-carrying conductors (adjustment factor 0.70) in a 40 C ambient (correction factor 0.91). What is the adjusted ampacity?","o":["36.4 A","40 A","28 A","25.5 A"],"a":3,"e":"40 A x 0.91 x 0.70 = 25.5 A.","r":"NEC 690.8(B), 310.15(B), 310.15(C)(1), Table 310.16","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"12 AWG PV conductors rated 90 C (30 A in Table 310.16) are in a raceway on a roof with a 60 C ambient (correction factor 0.71) and 4 current-carrying conductors (adjustment factor 0.80). What is the adjusted ampacity?","o":["24.0 A","21.3 A","17.0 A","30 A"],"a":2,"e":"30 A x 0.71 x 0.80 = 17.0 A. Both the correction and adjustment factors must be applied.","r":"NEC 690.8(B), 310.15(B), 310.15(C)(1), Table 310.16","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"6 AWG conductors rated 90 C (75 A in Table 310.16) are in a raceway with 8 current-carrying conductors (adjustment factor 0.70) in a 55 C ambient (correction factor 0.76). What is the adjusted ampacity?","o":["39.9 A","75 A","57 A","52.5 A"],"a":0,"e":"75 A x 0.76 x 0.70 = 39.9 A.","r":"NEC 690.8(B), 310.15(B), 310.15(C)(1), Table 310.16","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"A PV source circuit has a maximum circuit current of 16 A under 690.8(A). Which minimum conductor ampacity applies before correction and adjustment factors are applied?","o":["25 A","16 A","20 A","12.8 A"],"a":2,"e":"The 690.8(A) current of 16 A already includes the 125 percent Isc factor. The before-adjustment test requires another 125 percent: 16 x 1.25 = 20 A.","r":"NEC 690.8(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"A single PV string with an Isc of 6 A has no overcurrent device. What copper EGC size applies under 690.45 using the assumed overcurrent device value of 690.9(B)?","o":["10 AWG","12 AWG","16 AWG","14 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"6 x 1.25 x 1.25 = 9.4 A, so Table 250.122 gives 14 AWG, which is also the minimum EGC size allowed in a PV circuit.","r":"NEC 690.45, Table 250.122","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"A string of 20 modules each has a rated Voc of 30 V and a lowest-temperature correction factor of 1.18. May this string be installed on a one-family dwelling?","o":["Yes, 700 V is the limit","Yes, 600 V is the maximum","No, the maximum voltage is 708 V, which exceeds 600 V","No, because the limit is 150 V"],"a":2,"e":"20 x 30 = 600 V; x 1.18 = 708 V, which exceeds the 600 V limit for one- and two-family dwellings.","r":"NEC 690.7","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A string of 8 modules each has a rated Voc of 48 V and a temperature correction factor of 1.13. What is the maximum PV system voltage?","o":["434 V","384 V","480 V","542 V"],"a":0,"e":"8 x 48 = 384 V; x 1.13 = 433.9 V, or about 434 V.","r":"NEC 690.7(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A rooftop PV circuit on a commercial building enters the building through a wall. Inside the building, a conductor segment is 2 ft from the point of entry. Is it a controlled conductor under 690.12?","o":["Yes, because it is inside the building","Yes, if the wall is masonry","Yes, because it is more than 1 ft from the array","No, because it is within 3 ft (1 m) of the point of entry"],"a":3,"e":"Inside a building, only conductors more than 1 m (3 ft) from the point of entry are controlled conductors; the first 3 ft are not.","r":"NEC 690.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"An initiation device for the rapid shutdown function may be which of the following?","o":["The service disconnecting means","A GFCI receptacle","A 20 A lighting circuit breaker","An ordinary light switch"],"a":0,"e":"The service disconnecting means, the PV system disconnecting means, or a readily accessible on-off switch qualify as initiation devices.","r":"NEC 690.12(C)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"Which of the following PV installations requires rapid shutdown under 690.12?","o":["A rooftop PV array on a one-family dwelling","A ground-mounted array not attached to any building","A solar carport that is a non-enclosed detached structure","A ground-mounted array with circuits terminating outside and installed per 230.6"],"a":0,"e":"Rooftop PV circuits on buildings require rapid shutdown. Ground-mounted arrays and non-enclosed detached structures have exceptions.","r":"NEC 690.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A PV system disconnecting means must simultaneously disconnect which conductors?","o":["Only the grounded conductor","Only the equipment grounding conductor","Only the positive conductors","All PV system conductors that are not solidly grounded"],"a":3,"e":"The disconnect must open all PV system conductors that are not solidly grounded at the same time.","r":"NEC 690.13(E)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A PV isolating device that is a connector must meet which requirement?","o":["It must be a connector complying with 690.33 and listed for the application","It can be any twist-on wire connector","It must be a plug and receptacle listed for ac use","It must be a molded-case breaker"],"a":0,"e":"A connector meeting 690.33 is one of the permitted isolating device types in 690.15(C).","r":"NEC 690.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"Which article governs the connection of a PV system to other power sources such as the utility?","o":["Article 705","Article 480","Article 700","Article 725"],"a":0,"e":"690.59 sends PV source connections to Parts I and II of Article 705, which covers interconnected power production sources.","r":"NEC 690.59","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"Where a building has a PV system, which section governs the required plaque or directory identifying power sources?","o":["690.53","705.10","230.2","110.21"],"a":1,"e":"690.56 directs identification of power sources to the directory requirements of 705.10.","r":"NEC 690.56, 705.10","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A listed combiner uses fuses that are rated for the dc voltage of the PV system. Which statement is correct?","o":["Fuses are never allowed in PV circuits","AC-rated fuses may be used if the amp rating is correct","Only plug fuses are allowed","Fuses used on the dc side must be listed for PV use and rated for the dc voltage"],"a":3,"e":"Dc PV overcurrent devices must be PV-listed and rated for the dc voltage, since interrupting dc is harder than interrupting ac.","r":"NEC 690.9(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"Single-conductor PV wire used in exposed outdoor array wiring must be identified as which of the following?","o":["Type NM","Type TW","Type MTW","Listed PV wire or Type USE-2"],"a":3,"e":"Exposed outdoor array wiring uses PV wire or USE-2, which are sunlight and moisture resistant.","r":"NEC 690.31(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"A PV dc circuit from the array passes through a wall into a building. From the point of penetration to the first readily accessible disconnecting means, which wiring method is NOT permitted?","o":["A metal enclosure","Metal raceway","Type NM-B cable","Type MC cable"],"a":2,"e":"Interior dc PV circuits must be in metal raceways, Type MC cable, or metal enclosures from penetration to the first readily accessible disconnect.","r":"NEC 690.31(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"Why must PV connectors be noninterchangeable with receptacles of other systems on the premises?","o":["To allow connection to ac receptacles","To eliminate the need for grounding","To reduce the cost of the connector","To prevent PV dc circuits from being connected to other wiring systems by mistake"],"a":3,"e":"Noninterchangeable connectors prevent misconnection of dc PV conductors to ac or other circuits.","r":"NEC 690.33","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"For a PV array mounted on a building that has a grounding electrode system for its ac service, how should the PV grounding be connected?","o":["To a separate isolated electrode only","It is not grounded","To a gas pipe","To the building grounding electrode system"],"a":3,"e":"The PV dc grounding electrode conductor and array EGC connect to the building's existing grounding electrode system.","r":"NEC 690.47, 250.166","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"Equipment used in PV systems that is not specifically listed for the application must be:","o":["installed outdoors only","field labeled or otherwise evaluated for PV use","installed in a locked enclosure","used only below 50 V"],"a":1,"e":"690.4(B) requires listing or field labeling for equipment such as inverters and PV modules.","r":"NEC 690.4(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":1,"q":"Which type of device provides the dc arc-fault protection that 690.11 requires for PV circuits operating at 80 volts or more?","o":["A surge arrester","A listed PV arc-fault circuit interrupter or equivalent listed equipment","A ground-fault circuit interrupter","A residual-current detector"],"a":1,"e":"Dc arc-fault protection must be provided by a listed PV arc-fault circuit interrupter or other listed equipment with equivalent protection.","r":"NEC 690.11","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":2,"q":"The Voc rating of PV modules used to calculate maximum system voltage is normally taken from:","o":["the highest ambient temperature","the inverter nameplate","the Imp rating","the module nameplate, corrected for temperature using the manufacturer's coefficients or Table 690.7(A)"],"a":3,"e":"690.7 uses module Voc from the nameplate, corrected for the lowest ambient temperature using manufacturer coefficients or the table.","r":"NEC 690.7(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":1,"q":"A fuel cell is best described as:","o":["a device that burns fuel to spin a generator","an electrochemical system that consumes fuel to produce an electric current","a transformer that converts fuel to dc","a battery that stores energy from the utility"],"a":1,"e":"A fuel cell is an electrochemical system that consumes fuel to produce an electric current. The main reaction is not combustion.","r":"NEC 692.2 / Art. 100 (fuel cell)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":1,"q":"The complete aggregate of equipment used to convert chemical fuel into usable electricity, typically including a reformer, stack, power inverter, and auxiliary equipment, is called a:","o":["wind turbine","microgrid","PV array","fuel cell system"],"a":3,"e":"A fuel cell system is the complete aggregate of equipment, not just the stack.","r":"NEC 692.2 / Art. 100 (fuel cell system)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":1,"q":"May a fuel cell system supply a building that is also supplied by a utility service?","o":["Only if the fuel cell is under 1 kW","Only if the utility is disconnected","Yes, a fuel cell system may supply a building in addition to any other supply","No, a building may have only one supply"],"a":2,"e":"Fuel cell systems are permitted to supply a building or structure in addition to any other electrical supply system(s).","r":"NEC 692.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":1,"q":"Fuel cell systems must be:","o":["evaluated and listed for the intended application","field built without listing","installed without a nameplate","approved only by the manufacturer"],"a":0,"e":"692.6 requires fuel cell systems to be evaluated and listed for the intended application.","r":"NEC 692.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":1,"q":"The rated current shown on the fuel cell system nameplate is used as the:","o":["grounding conductor size","available fault current","nameplate-rated circuit current for sizing","short-circuit current"],"a":2,"e":"692.8(A) defines the nameplate-rated circuit current as the rated current on the fuel cell system nameplate.","r":"NEC 692.8(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":2,"q":"The ampacity of feeder conductors from a fuel cell system to the premises wiring must not be less than the greater of the nameplate-rated circuit current or:","o":["50 percent of the nameplate current","the rating of the fuel cell system overcurrent protective device","the service ampacity","the fuel line pressure rating"],"a":1,"e":"692.8(B) requires feeder conductors to have an ampacity at least equal to the greater of the nameplate-rated circuit current or the rating of the fuel cell overcurrent device.","r":"NEC 692.8(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":3,"q":"A fuel cell system nameplate shows a rated circuit current of 52 A and the fuel cell system is protected by a 60 A overcurrent device. What is the minimum ampacity of the feeder conductors to the premises wiring under 692.8(B)?","o":["75 A","60 A","52 A","65 A"],"a":1,"e":"The feeder ampacity must be at least the greater of the nameplate-rated current (52 A) or the overcurrent device rating (60 A), so 60 A.","r":"NEC 692.8(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":1,"q":"A means must be provided to disconnect a fuel cell system from:","o":["only the fuel supply","only the utility meter","all other conductors in a building or structure","only the grounding electrode"],"a":2,"e":"692.13 requires a means to disconnect all current-carrying conductors of the fuel cell system from all other conductors in the building.","r":"NEC 692.13","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":2,"q":"Equipment grounding conductors for a fuel cell system are sized in accordance with:","o":["310.16","250.122","250.166","250.66"],"a":1,"e":"692.45 sizes fuel cell equipment grounding conductors per 250.122, which is based on the overcurrent device rating.","r":"NEC 692.45, 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":3,"q":"A fuel cell inverter with a 32 A continuous output is connected through a dedicated breaker to a 200 A busbar protected by a 200 A main breaker. Under the 120 percent rule, what is the largest overcurrent device that may be installed for the fuel cell connection?","o":["60 A","40 A","32 A","50 A"],"a":1,"e":"120 percent of 200 A = 240 A; 240 - 200 = 40 A maximum source breaker. The inverter needs 125 percent of 32 A = 40 A, so a 40 A breaker is the largest allowed and is adequate.","r":"NEC 705.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":1,"q":"An interactive fuel cell system must be interconnected with the utility according to the requirements of:","o":["Article 480","Article 695","Article 725","Article 705"],"a":3,"e":"Article 705 covers the interconnection of power production sources including fuel cells operating in parallel with the primary source.","r":"NEC 705.1, Art. 692","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":2,"q":"A building has a fuel cell system installed in addition to the utility service. Where must the permanent plaque or directory listing the power sources be installed?","o":["Only at the fuel cell","Only at the meter base","Only in the electrical room door","At each service equipment location and the other required locations of the sources"],"a":3,"e":"705.10 requires a permanent plaque or directory at each service equipment location and at power production source locations.","r":"NEC 705.10","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":2,"q":"An interactive fuel cell system loses utility voltage. What must the fuel cell output do?","o":["Switch to the grounded conductor","Operate unchanged in parallel","Continue to export power to the utility","Automatically de-energize from the primary source and not re-energize until the primary source is restored"],"a":3,"e":"705.40 requires interactive sources to automatically disconnect from the primary source on loss of voltage and stay off until restored.","r":"NEC 705.40","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":1,"q":"May a wind electric system supply a building that is also served by a utility?","o":["Only if under 1 kW","Yes, in addition to any other supply system","No, wind systems must be the sole supply","Only if the utility is a 3-phase system"],"a":1,"e":"694.7(A) allows wind electric systems to supply a building in addition to any other supply.","r":"NEC 694.7(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":1,"q":"Inverters, wind generators, and charge controllers used in a wind electric system must be:","o":["of any manufacturer","installed outdoors","listed or field labeled for wind electric use","rated for 120 V only"],"a":2,"e":"694.7(B) requires equipment such as inverters, motor generators, wind generators, and controllers to be listed or labeled for the wind application.","r":"NEC 694.7(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":1,"q":"Who may install wind electric system equipment, wiring, and interconnections?","o":["Any owner","Only qualified persons","Only licensed riggers","Only utility staff"],"a":1,"e":"694.7(C) requires qualified persons for the installation of the equipment and wiring.","r":"NEC 694.7(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum circuit current of a wind inverter output circuit under 694.12(A)?","o":["The service ampacity","80 percent of the nameplate wattage","The turbine peak gust rating","The continuous output current rating marked on the inverter nameplate"],"a":3,"e":"The inverter output circuit current is the inverter's continuous output current rating.","r":"NEC 694.12(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":2,"q":"Conductors and overcurrent devices for wind turbine output circuits are sized at not less than what percentage of the maximum currents calculated in 694.12(A)?","o":["125 percent","100 percent","80 percent","156 percent"],"a":0,"e":"694.12(B) requires conductors and overcurrent devices to be rated at not less than 125 percent of the maximum current.","r":"NEC 694.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":3,"q":"A wind turbine output circuit has a maximum current of 32 A. What is the minimum ampacity of the circuit conductors under 694.12(B)?","o":["50 A","32 A","40 A","25.6 A"],"a":2,"e":"32 A x 1.25 = 40 A.","r":"NEC 694.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":3,"q":"A wind turbine output circuit has a maximum current of 18 A. What is the minimum overcurrent device rating, as a calculated value, under 694.12(B)?","o":["22.5 A","18 A","28.1 A","14.4 A"],"a":0,"e":"18 A x 1.25 = 22.5 A.","r":"NEC 694.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":2,"q":"The disconnecting means for a wind electric system must disconnect:","o":["only the grounded conductor","all current-carrying conductors of the wind system from all other conductors in the building","only the tower guy wires","only the dc side"],"a":1,"e":"694.22(A) requires a means to disconnect all current-carrying conductors of the wind source from all other conductors in a building or structure.","r":"NEC 694.22(A)","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":1,"q":"The disconnecting means for a wind electric system must be:","o":["marked with a temporary tag","permanently marked to identify it as a wind electric system disconnect","painted yellow","unmarked"],"a":1,"e":"694.22(C) requires permanent marking identifying the disconnect as a wind electric system disconnect.","r":"NEC 694.22(C)","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":2,"q":"Flexible cords used to connect moving parts of a wind turbine must be:","o":["any cord rated 300 V","listed for extra-hard usage and water and sunlight resistant","Type SPT-1 lamp cord","Type NM-B"],"a":1,"e":"Flexible cords for moving parts must comply with Article 400, be extra-hard usage, water resistant, and sunlight resistant.","r":"NEC 694.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":3,"q":"A wind turbine inverter with a continuous output of 35 A is interconnected on the load side of a service on a 225 A busbar with a 225 A main breaker. What is the maximum rating of the overcurrent device that may protect the inverter breaker under the 120 percent rule?","o":["56 A","35 A","45 A","60 A"],"a":2,"e":"120 percent of 225 A = 270 A; 270 - 225 = 45 A maximum source breaker. The 35 A inverter requires 125 percent = 43.75 A, which fits.","r":"NEC 705.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 692 and 694 Fuel Cell Systems and Wind Electric Systems","d":2,"q":"Which of the following must be bonded to the equipment grounding system of a wind electric system?","o":["Only the turbine blades","Exposed non-current-carrying metal parts of towers, nacelles, and other equipment","Only the guy wire anchors","Nothing, towers are exempt"],"a":1,"e":"694.40 requires non-current-carrying metal parts of towers and equipment to be bonded in accordance with Article 250.","r":"NEC 694.40","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is an acceptable power source for an electric motor-driven fire pump?","o":["A branch circuit from a lighting panel","A panelboard on the load side of the main service disconnect","A connection ahead of, and not within, the service disconnecting means","A receptacle outlet"],"a":2,"e":"695.3(A) permits a separate utility service or a connection made ahead of the service disconnecting means, an on-site power production facility, or a dedicated feeder from a service connection.","r":"NEC 695.3(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"If a single power source for a fire pump is not reliable, 695.3(B) requires:","o":["two or more of the permitted sources, or one permitted source plus an on-site standby generator","a single larger service","no alternate source if a generator is nearby","a UPS only"],"a":0,"e":"Where reliability of a single source cannot be assured, an alternate source such as a second utility source or on-site generator must be provided.","r":"NEC 695.3(B)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is prohibited as a power source for an electric fire pump?","o":["A dedicated feeder from a service connection","An on-site standby generator","A utility service","A phase converter"],"a":3,"e":"Phase converters are not permitted to supply fire pump motors.","r":"NEC 695.3","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":1,"q":"Under 695.4(A), supply conductors for a fire pump may directly connect the power source to:","o":["a listed fire pump controller or a listed combination fire pump controller and power transfer switch","a lighting contactor","a variable frequency drive only","a motor starter in a general-purpose panelboard"],"a":0,"e":"695.4(A) allows direct connection of the supply to a listed fire pump controller or listed combination controller and power transfer switch.","r":"NEC 695.4(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"The overcurrent protective device in the fire pump supply circuit must be selected or set to carry indefinitely the sum of:","o":["the full-load current of the fire pump motor only","the locked-rotor current of the fire pump and pressure maintenance motors plus the full-load current of the accessory equipment","the service ampacity","125 percent of the fire pump motor full-load current"],"a":1,"e":"695.4(B) sizes the overcurrent device to carry indefinitely locked-rotor currents of the pump motors plus accessory equipment so that it does not open during starting.","r":"NEC 695.4(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":3,"q":"A fire pump motor has a locked-rotor current of 600 A, the pressure maintenance pump has a locked-rotor current of 50 A, and the accessory equipment is 5 A. If the calculated value is not a standard rating, what overcurrent device rating applies under 695.4(B)?","o":["700 A","655 A","800 A","600 A"],"a":0,"e":"600 + 50 + 5 = 655 A, which is not a standard rating. The next standard size per 240.6(A) is 700 A.","r":"NEC 695.4(B), 240.6(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":1,"q":"The fire pump disconnecting means must be marked with the words 'Fire Pump Disconnecting Means' in letters at least how tall?","o":["1 in. (25 mm)","2 in. (50 mm)","1/4 in. (6 mm)","1/2 in. (13 mm)"],"a":0,"e":"695.4(B) requires the marking in letters at least 25 mm (1 in.) high, visible without opening doors or covers.","r":"NEC 695.4(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"The fire pump disconnecting means must be capable of being:","o":["operated only by a key held by the utility","locked in the closed position","operated by a push button","locked in the open position only"],"a":1,"e":"The disconnect must be lockable in the closed position so the pump supply is not shut off unintentionally.","r":"NEC 695.4(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"Where must the fire pump disconnecting means be located relative to other building disconnects?","o":["Within the fire pump motor","Directly adjacent to the main service disconnect","Inside the controller enclosure that it feeds","Sufficiently remote from other building disconnects so that inadvertent simultaneous operation is unlikely"],"a":3,"e":"The pump disconnect must be remote from other building disconnects and must not be located within equipment that feeds loads other than the fire pump.","r":"NEC 695.4(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"Which method is permitted to supervise the fire pump disconnecting means in the closed position?","o":["A warning tag only","A sign on the panel door","Painting an arrow on the handle","Locking the disconnecting means in the closed position"],"a":3,"e":"Permitted supervision includes central-station or remote signaling, local audible signaling, locking closed, or sealing with weekly recorded inspection.","r":"NEC 695.4(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":1,"q":"A transformer supplying a fire pump controller must be rated at not less than what percentage of the sum of the fire pump and pressure maintenance pump motor loads, plus 100 percent of the fire pump accessory equipment?","o":["250 percent","125 percent","150 percent","100 percent"],"a":1,"e":"695.5(A) requires the transformer to be rated at not less than 125 percent of the pump motor loads and 100 percent of accessory equipment.","r":"NEC 695.5(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":3,"q":"A transformer supplies a fire pump with a full-load current of 40 A, a pressure maintenance pump of 5 A, and accessory equipment of 3 A. What is the minimum transformer secondary current rating?","o":["56.25 A","59.25 A","48 A","75 A"],"a":1,"e":"125 percent of (40 + 5) = 56.25 A, plus 100 percent of 3 A accessory equipment = 59.25 A.","r":"NEC 695.5(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"Overcurrent protection on the secondary of a transformer supplying a fire pump controller is:","o":["permitted only for fuses","required at 250 percent","required at 125 percent","not permitted"],"a":3,"e":"695.5(B) prohibits secondary overcurrent protection. The primary device must carry the locked-rotor currents indefinitely.","r":"NEC 695.5(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"If fire pump supply conductors must pass through a building, which protection is permitted?","o":["Burial in 6 in. of sand","No protection is required","Encasement in at least 2 in. (50 mm) of concrete","Encasement in 1/2 in. of plaster"],"a":2,"e":"Supply conductors routed through a building must be encased in 2 in. of concrete or brick, or be in a fire-rated enclosure or listed protective system.","r":"NEC 695.6(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"Conductors supplying only one fire pump motor must have an ampacity of at least what percentage of the motor full-load current?","o":["115 percent","150 percent","125 percent","100 percent"],"a":2,"e":"695.6(B) requires conductors to a single fire pump motor to be rated at least 125 percent of the motor full-load current per 430.22.","r":"NEC 695.6(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":3,"q":"A 100 hp, 460 V, 3-phase fire pump motor has a table full-load current of 124 A (Table 430.250). What is the minimum conductor ampacity supplying only this motor?","o":["124 A","186 A","155 A","143 A"],"a":2,"e":"124 A x 1.25 = 155 A.","r":"NEC 695.6(B), Table 430.250","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":3,"q":"A fire pump motor has a full-load current of 65 A, the jockey pump is 7.6 A, and the accessories draw 3 A, all supplied from the same conductors. What is the minimum conductor ampacity?","o":["109 A","75.6 A","93.75 A","81.25 A"],"a":2,"e":"125 percent of (65 + 7.6) = 90.75 A, plus 100 percent of 3 A = 93.75 A.","r":"NEC 695.6(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":1,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted between the fire pump controller and the fire pump motor?","o":["Type AC cable","Type NM-B cable","Type SE cable","Rigid metal conduit"],"a":3,"e":"Pump wiring from the controller must be in rigid metal conduit, intermediate metal conduit, liquidtight conduit, or Type MI cable.","r":"NEC 695.6(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"Ground-fault protection of equipment in a fire pump circuit is:","o":["not permitted","required at 1200 A","required at 30 mA","permitted only on feeders"],"a":0,"e":"Ground-fault protection of equipment is prohibited in fire pump circuits so the pump runs even with a fault.","r":"NEC 695.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"During motor starting, the voltage at the fire pump controller line terminals must not drop more than what percentage below the controller's rated voltage?","o":["10 percent","15 percent","5 percent","20 percent"],"a":1,"e":"695.7(A) limits starting voltage drop at the controller line terminals to 15 percent below the controller rated voltage.","r":"NEC 695.7(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"While the fire pump motor runs at 115 percent of its full-load current, the voltage at the controller load terminals must not drop more than what percentage below the motor's rated voltage?","o":["15 percent","10 percent","5 percent","3 percent"],"a":2,"e":"695.7(B) limits running voltage drop to 5 percent below the motor voltage rating at 115 percent of full-load current.","r":"NEC 695.7(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":3,"q":"A fire pump controller is rated 480 V. What is the lowest voltage permitted at the controller line terminals during motor starting?","o":["384 V","432 V","408 V","456 V"],"a":2,"e":"480 V x 0.85 = 408 V.","r":"NEC 695.7(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":3,"q":"A fire pump motor is rated 460 V. What is the lowest voltage permitted at the controller load terminals when the motor runs at 115 percent of full-load current?","o":["437 V","455 V","391 V","414 V"],"a":0,"e":"460 V x 0.95 = 437 V.","r":"NEC 695.7(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":1,"q":"Fire pump controllers and transfer switches must be:","o":["listed for fire pump service","NEMA 1 enclosures only","listed as general-purpose motor starters","approved by the pump manufacturer only"],"a":0,"e":"695.10 requires listed equipment for fire pump service.","r":"NEC 695.10","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":1,"q":"Fire pump controllers and transfer switches must be located:","o":["on the roof","behind a locked door","in the main electrical room","as close as practicable to the motors they control and within sight of the motors"],"a":3,"e":"695.12 requires controllers and transfer switches to be close to and within sight of the pump motors.","r":"NEC 695.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"Current-carrying parts of fire pump equipment must be at least how far above the floor level?","o":["18 in. (450 mm)","6 in. (150 mm)","24 in. (600 mm)","12 in. (300 mm)"],"a":3,"e":"Energized parts must be at least 300 mm (12 in.) above the floor level.","r":"NEC 695.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"Fire pump control equipment must be located or protected so that:","o":["it is hidden from view","it is not damaged by water escaping from pumps or pump connections","it is exposed to rain","it is below grade"],"a":1,"e":"Controllers and transfer switches must be located or protected against water escaping from pumps or connections.","r":"NEC 695.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"External control circuits that extend outside the fire pump room must be arranged so that:","o":["an open circuit prevents the pump from starting","remote devices can block automatic start","failure of the external circuit does not prevent operation of the pump from other means","a short circuit disables the controller"],"a":2,"e":"A failure in an external control circuit must not prevent the pump from starting from other internal or remote means.","r":"NEC 695.14","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"A listed surge protective device must be installed:","o":["only at the motor terminals","only at the service entrance","only in the generator","in or on the fire pump controller"],"a":3,"e":"695.15 requires a listed SPD in or on the fire pump controller.","r":"NEC 695.15","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 695 Fire Pumps","d":2,"q":"Fire pump supply conductors on the load side of the final disconnecting means and overcurrent device must be:","o":["run in the same raceway as other loads","kept independent of all other wiring","shared with lighting circuits","combined with building feeders"],"a":1,"e":"Fire pump supply conductors must be independent of other wiring and serve only the fire pump loads.","r":"NEC 695.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":1,"q":"Equipment that operates in parallel with power production sources must be:","o":["of any type","rated for 120 V only","installed in a locked room","listed for the required interactive function"],"a":3,"e":"705.6 requires equipment used for interconnection with power production sources to be listed for the required interactive function.","r":"NEC 705.6","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":1,"q":"What does 705.10 require at the service equipment location of a premises with a power production source?","o":["No marking if the source is under 10 kW","A plaque showing only the utility meter number","A sign stating the date of installation","A permanent plaque or directory identifying all electric power sources and their disconnects"],"a":3,"e":"A permanent plaque or directory denoting all electric power sources on or in the premises must be at each service equipment location and other readily visible approved locations.","r":"NEC 705.10","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":1,"q":"A supply-side source connection is made:","o":["on the load side of a branch-circuit breaker","at a receptacle outlet","ahead of the service disconnecting means","on the load side of the main service disconnect"],"a":2,"e":"A supply-side connection taps the service conductors or service equipment before the service disconnecting means.","r":"NEC 705.11","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum size of supply-side source connection conductors under 705.11?","o":["8 AWG copper","12 AWG copper","10 AWG copper","6 AWG copper or 4 AWG aluminum or copper-clad aluminum"],"a":3,"e":"Supply-side conductors must also have an ampacity not less than 125 percent of the continuous current, and not be smaller than 6 AWG copper or 4 AWG aluminum.","r":"NEC 705.11(B)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"Splices and taps made to service conductors for a supply-side source connection must comply with:","o":["230.46","210.8","250.64","300.5"],"a":0,"e":"705.11(C) refers supply-side splices and taps on service conductors to 230.46.","r":"NEC 705.11(C)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"A supply-side connection made inside a meter socket enclosure that is used exclusively by the utility is permitted only:","o":["if the source is under 5 kW","with the approval of the serving utility","with no approval","if the enclosure is NEMA 3R"],"a":1,"e":"Supply-side connections inside utility-exclusive meter sockets require utility approval.","r":"NEC 705.11(C)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"Metal enclosures and raceways containing supply-side source conductors must be:","o":["bonded in accordance with Article 250","left unbonded","grounded to a separate rod only","insulated from the service equipment"],"a":0,"e":"Supply-side conductor metal raceways and enclosures must be bonded per the Article 250 requirements for service conductors.","r":"NEC 705.11(E)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"Under the busbar rule for load-side connections, the sum of 125 percent of the power source output circuit current and the rating of the overcurrent device protecting the busbar may not exceed:","o":["150 percent of the busbar ampacity","120 percent of the busbar ampacity","125 percent of the busbar ampacity","100 percent of the busbar ampacity"],"a":1,"e":"705.12 limits the sum to 120 percent of the busbar rating.","r":"NEC 705.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":3,"q":"A panelboard has a 200 A busbar protected by a 200 A main breaker. What is the largest power source breaker permitted under the 120 percent rule?","o":["40 A","60 A","20 A","50 A"],"a":0,"e":"200 A x 1.2 = 240 A; 240 - 200 = 40 A.","r":"NEC 705.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":3,"q":"A 225 A busbar is protected by a 225 A main breaker. What is the maximum inverter output current permitted by the 120 percent rule?","o":["30 A","45 A","36 A","56 A"],"a":2,"e":"225 x 1.2 = 270 A; 270 - 225 = 45 A maximum breaker. The inverter current is limited to 45 A / 1.25 = 36 A.","r":"NEC 705.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":3,"q":"A 100 A busbar is protected by a 100 A main breaker. What is the largest source breaker permitted under the 120 percent rule?","o":["30 A","10 A","25 A","20 A"],"a":3,"e":"100 x 1.2 = 120 A; 120 - 100 = 20 A.","r":"NEC 705.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":3,"q":"A 400 A busbar is protected by a 400 A main breaker. What is the maximum inverter output current under the 120 percent rule?","o":["64 A","100 A","50 A","80 A"],"a":0,"e":"400 x 1.2 = 480 A; 480 - 400 = 80 A maximum breaker; 80 / 1.25 = 64 A.","r":"NEC 705.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":3,"q":"A 125 A busbar is protected by a 100 A main breaker. What is the largest source breaker allowed under the 120 percent rule?","o":["20 A","50 A","25 A","100 A"],"a":1,"e":"125 x 1.2 = 150 A; 150 - 100 = 50 A.","r":"NEC 705.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is required of a source disconnecting means under 705.20?","o":["It must be inside the inverter only","It must be externally operable without exposing the operator to live parts and plainly indicate open or closed","It must be a plug and cord","It must be unmarked"],"a":1,"e":"705.20 requires an externally operable disconnect that plainly indicates its position and has ratings sufficient for the circuit.","r":"NEC 705.20","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"A single disconnecting means may be used for:","o":["only one inverter","the combined ac output of one or more power production sources in a single system","only the dc side","all loads in the building"],"a":1,"e":"A single disconnecting means is permitted for the combined ac output of one or more sources in a single system.","r":"NEC 705.20","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":1,"q":"Wiring methods for power production source output circuits must comply with:","o":["the wiring methods of Chapter 3","Article 725","no specific rules","Article 400 only"],"a":0,"e":"705.25 permits Chapter 3 wiring methods for power source output circuits.","r":"NEC 705.25","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":3,"q":"An inverter has a nameplate continuous output current of 48 A. What is the minimum ampacity of the output circuit conductors before correction and adjustment?","o":["75 A","48 A","60 A","50 A"],"a":2,"e":"48 A x 1.25 = 60 A.","r":"NEC 705.28(A)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":3,"q":"Two inverters feed the same output circuit with nameplate currents of 24 A and 16 A. What is the minimum conductor ampacity before correction and adjustment?","o":["62.5 A","40 A","50 A","32 A"],"a":2,"e":"24 + 16 = 40 A, x 1.25 = 50 A.","r":"NEC 705.28(A)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"After applying correction and adjustment factors, output conductors from a power production source must have an ampacity of at least:","o":["100 percent of the nameplate current rating","125 percent of the nameplate","50 percent of the nameplate","80 percent of the nameplate"],"a":0,"e":"705.28 requires 125 percent before adjustment, or 100 percent after correction and adjustment.","r":"NEC 705.28(A)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":3,"q":"An energy management system limits the combined output of two inverters to 30 A. What current is used for sizing the output conductors?","o":["The service rating","The 30 A energy management system setting","The sum of the inverter nameplates","The module Isc"],"a":1,"e":"Where an energy management system limits current, the output current is the system's current setting.","r":"NEC 705.28(A)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":3,"q":"An inverter has a 32 A continuous output. What is the minimum calculated overcurrent device rating?","o":["50 A","35 A","32 A","40 A"],"a":3,"e":"32 x 1.25 = 40 A.","r":"NEC 705.30, 705.28","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":1,"q":"Overcurrent protection for circuits and equipment supplied by power production sources must consider:","o":["only the inverter","only the loads","only the utility","current from all sources"],"a":3,"e":"705.30 requires protection for all sources feeding the circuit.","r":"NEC 705.30(A)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"Equipment containing overcurrent devices in circuits supplied by multiple sources must be:","o":["painted red","marked only with the utility","left unmarked","marked to indicate the presence of all sources"],"a":3,"e":"705.30 requires marking where more than one source feeds the equipment.","r":"NEC 705.30","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"Which circuit breakers are considered suitable for backfeed?","o":["Breakers not marked Line and Load","Breakers marked Line and Load, in all cases","No breakers","Any breaker"],"a":0,"e":"Breakers marked Line and Load are suitable only if specifically rated for backfeed; unmarked breakers are suitable.","r":"NEC 705.30","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"Where ground-fault protection is used, the output of an interactive system must be connected to:","o":["the supply side of the ground-fault protection","the load side of the ground-fault protection","the grounded conductor","the equipment grounding conductor"],"a":0,"e":"The output is connected to the supply side unless equipment ground-fault protection covers all sources.","r":"NEC 705.32","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"Upon loss of voltage in the primary source, an electric power production source must:","o":["switch to the grounded conductor","operate in parallel","automatically disconnect from the primary source and not re-energize until the source is restored","continue to export power"],"a":2,"e":"705.40 prohibits energizing the primary source when it has lost voltage.","r":"NEC 705.40","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"A power control system (PCS) under 705.13:","o":["limits the current or loading on busbars and conductors but does not replace required overcurrent protection","is required for every inverter","allows unlimited backfeed","replaces all overcurrent devices"],"a":0,"e":"A power control system limits current but overcurrent protection is still required.","r":"NEC 705.13","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":1,"q":"An electric power production system operating in parallel with and capable of delivering energy to an electric primary source supply system is called a(n):","o":["battery system","stand-alone system","interactive system","isolated system"],"a":2,"e":"An interactive system operates in parallel with the primary source and can deliver energy to it.","r":"NEC Art. 100 (interactive system)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":1,"q":"A premises electrical power system that includes one or more power sources, loads, and can operate in grid-connected or island mode is a:","o":["feeder","separately derived system","service","microgrid system"],"a":3,"e":"A microgrid system can operate connected to the primary source or in island mode.","r":"NEC Art. 100 (microgrid system)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":1,"q":"A device that allows a microgrid system to separate and isolate itself from the primary source and reconnect is a:","o":["ground-fault interrupter","dc-to-dc converter","transfer switch for fire pumps","microgrid interconnect device"],"a":3,"e":"The microgrid interconnect device (MID) separates and reconnects the microgrid to the primary source.","r":"NEC Art. 100 (microgrid interconnect device)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":1,"q":"A microgrid that is operating while disconnected from the primary source is in:","o":["bypass mode","island mode","standby mode only","interactive mode"],"a":1,"e":"When disconnected from the primary source, a microgrid operates in island mode.","r":"NEC Art. 100, 705.50","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"A microgrid interconnect device must provide:","o":["overcurrent protection for all sources","only ground-fault protection","only surge protection","no protection"],"a":0,"e":"705.70 requires the MID to meet the requirements for overcurrent protection from all sources.","r":"NEC 705.70","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"Where must the 705.10 plaque or directory be installed?","o":["Only at the meter","At each service equipment location and at locations of power production sources capable of being interconnected","Only at the main panel","On the inverter"],"a":1,"e":"705.10 requires the directory at service equipment and at source locations.","r":"NEC 705.10","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"A plug-in type back-fed breaker is used for a power source connection in a panelboard. Which statement applies?","o":["The breaker must be suitable for backfeed","Any breaker may be backfed","Fuses may not be used","Breakers marked Line and Load are always suitable"],"a":0,"e":"Circuit breakers used for backfeed must be suitable for it.","r":"NEC 705.30","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"Supply-side source connections must have a disconnecting means that complies with:","o":["Article 230, Parts VI through VIII","Article 440","Article 424","Article 430"],"a":0,"e":"705.11(D) requires a disconnecting means complying with the service disconnect requirements.","r":"NEC 705.11(D)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":1,"q":"In interactive mode a microgrid system may:","o":["never be connected to the utility","supply loads together with the utility and other sources","not supply loads","operate only in island mode"],"a":1,"e":"In interactive mode the microgrid operates with the primary source.","r":"NEC 705.50","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"An inverter has a nameplate continuous output current of 24 A. What is the minimum conductor ampacity before correction and adjustment for its output circuit?","o":["27 A","24 A","30 A","37.5 A"],"a":2,"e":"24 A x 1.25 = 30 A.","r":"NEC 705.28(A)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":3,"q":"A 225 A busbar is protected by a 200 A main breaker. What is the largest source breaker permitted under the 120 percent rule?","o":["45 A","70 A","90 A","25 A"],"a":1,"e":"225 x 1.2 = 270 A; 270 - 200 = 70 A.","r":"NEC 705.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"An inverter has a continuous output current of 20 A. What is the minimum calculated overcurrent device rating?","o":["20 A","15 A","25 A","30 A"],"a":2,"e":"20 A x 1.25 = 25 A.","r":"NEC 705.30, 705.28","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":1,"q":"Article 706 applies to energy storage systems having a capacity greater than what amount?","o":["0.5 kWh","10 kWh","1 kWh (3.6 MJ)","5 kWh"],"a":2,"e":"Article 706 covers ESS with capacity greater than 3.6 MJ (1 kWh) that are stand-alone or interactive with other power sources.","r":"NEC 706.1","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":1,"q":"Article 706 covers permanently installed energy storage systems that operate at over what voltage?","o":["Over 24 volts ac or dc","Over 600 volts ac or dc","Over 50 volts ac or 60 volts dc","Over 100 volts ac or dc"],"a":2,"e":"The Article 706 scope is ESS operating at over 50 V ac or 60 V dc.","r":"NEC 706.1","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":1,"q":"Under Article 706, energy storage systems must be:","o":["Built to a manufacturer's own standard without third-party evaluation","Approved by the installer only","Field-labeled by the electrician","Listed"],"a":3,"e":"706.5 requires that energy storage systems be listed.","r":"NEC 706.5","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":1,"q":"Who is permitted to install and maintain an energy storage system under Article 706?","o":["Qualified persons","The system owner only","Any licensed general contractor","Utility personnel only"],"a":0,"e":"706.3 limits installation and maintenance of ESS to qualified persons.","r":"NEC 706.3","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":1,"q":"May more than one energy storage system be installed on a single premises?","o":["No, only one ESS is permitted per premises","Only with written approval from the utility for each additional unit","Yes, multiple ESS are permitted on a premises","Only if each is on a separate service"],"a":2,"e":"706.6 permits multiple energy storage systems to be installed in or on a single premises.","r":"NEC 706.6","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"A commercial building has a newly installed energy storage system (not a one- or two-family dwelling). Which statement is correct about commissioning?","o":["The ESS must be commissioned on installation","Commissioning is required only after the first year of operation","Commissioning is required only for systems above 1 MWh","Commissioning is prohibited until the AHJ energizes it"],"a":0,"e":"706.7(A) requires ESS other than those in one- and two-family dwellings to be commissioned on installation.","r":"NEC 706.7(A)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"For an ESS in a commercial building, what must be kept regarding repairs and replacements?","o":["Nothing, since records are the utility's responsibility","Only a verbal report to the AHJ","A written record of repairs and replacements","A record only if the repair exceeded 10 percent of system cost"],"a":2,"e":"706.7(B) requires the ESS to be maintained and a written record (which may be computerized) of repair and replacements to be kept.","r":"NEC 706.7(B)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":1,"q":"The disconnecting means required for an ESS must allow the ESS to be isolated from which of the following?","o":["Only the battery cells","All wiring systems, including other power systems, utilization equipment and premises wiring","Only the utility service","Only the building's grounding system"],"a":1,"e":"706.15(A) requires a disconnecting means that disconnects the ESS from all wiring systems including other power systems, utilization equipment and its associated premises wiring.","r":"NEC 706.15(A)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"An ESS disconnect is located more than 10 ft from the ESS and out of sight. Which condition makes this acceptable?","o":["The disconnect is rated at least 125 percent of the ESS current","The disconnect is capable of being locked in accordance with 110.25","A label on the ESS stating where the disconnect is","The disconnect is installed above 6.5 ft"],"a":1,"e":"706.15(B) permits a disconnect not within sight if it is lockable per 110.25.","r":"NEC 706.15(B), 110.25","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"A one-family dwelling has an ESS. Which additional function must the ESS include under 706.15(B)?","o":["An automatic fire sprinkler interlock","A shutdown switch inside the garage only","An emergency shutdown function that stops power export to premises wiring, with the initiating device readily accessible outside the building","A rapid shutdown device at each battery module"],"a":2,"e":"For one- and two-family dwellings, 706.15(B) requires an emergency shutdown function with a readily accessible initiating device outside the building, clearly marked on/off.","r":"NEC 706.15(B)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"Which of these must be durably marked on the ESS disconnecting means for an ESS that is not in a one- or two-family dwelling?","o":["Total installed cost","Available fault current derived from the ESS and the date the calculation was performed","Battery chemistry and cell count","Name of the installing electrician"],"a":1,"e":"706.15(C) requires marking with nominal battery voltage, the available fault current derived from the ESS, an arc-flash label, and the date the calculation was performed.","r":"NEC 706.15(C)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"What must the ESS disconnecting means clearly indicate?","o":["The battery state of charge","The number of charge cycles","Whether it is in the open (off) or closed (on) position","The inverter output frequency"],"a":2,"e":"706.15(C) requires the disconnect to plainly indicate whether it is in the open or closed position.","r":"NEC 706.15(C)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":3,"q":"A battery system is separate from the power electronics and is subject to field servicing. The battery circuit is 480 volts dc nominal. What capability must be provided?","o":["Nothing further, since 480 V dc is within the limit","A second disconnect at the inverter only","A means to split strings into segments of 50 volts dc or less","A means to split the series-connected strings into segments of 240 volts dc nominal or less"],"a":3,"e":"706.15(E) requires battery circuits over 240 V dc nominal between conductors or to ground to have provisions to disconnect series-connected strings into segments not exceeding 240 V dc nominal.","r":"NEC 706.15(E)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"A battery that is separate from the ESS electronics and subject to field servicing requires a disconnecting means. Where must it be located?","o":["Readily accessible and within sight of the battery","At the inverter only","Within 50 ft of the main service","Inside the battery enclosure only"],"a":0,"e":"706.15(E) requires a readily accessible disconnecting means within sight of the battery for batteries separate from the electronics and subject to field servicing.","r":"NEC 706.15(E)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"If the line and load terminals of an ESS disconnect may remain energized in the open position, what is required?","o":["A field-applied warning label at the disconnect, per 110.21(B)","A lockout lock permanently installed","A red enclosure only","No marking, because the switch is open"],"a":0,"e":"Where terminals remain energized with the disconnect open, 110.21(B) field-applied warning labeling is required, as referenced by 706.15.","r":"NEC 706.15, 110.21(B)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"What does 110.25 require of a disconnect that is required to be lockable?","o":["The provisions for locking must remain in place with or without the lock installed","The lock must be a keyed padlock supplied by the utility","The lock must remain installed at all times","The disconnect must be locked in the closed position only"],"a":0,"e":"110.25 requires that locking provisions remain in place whether or not the lock is installed.","r":"NEC 110.25","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"An ESS has a maximum calculated circuit current of 80 A. The overcurrent device is not listed for operation at 100 percent of its rating. What is the minimum OCPD rating?","o":["90 A","80 A","100 A","125 A"],"a":2,"e":"706.31 requires overcurrent devices to be not less than 125 percent of the maximum current, so 80 A x 1.25 = 100 A.","r":"NEC 706.31, 706.30(A)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"The maximum current of an ESS circuit is 40 A. What is the minimum calculated rating of the overcurrent device if it is not 100-percent rated?","o":["60 A","45 A","40 A","50 A"],"a":3,"e":"40 A x 125 percent = 50 A.","r":"NEC 706.31","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":3,"q":"An ESS circuit has a maximum calculated current of 70 A. The OCPD is not listed for 100-percent continuous operation. What is the smallest standard OCPD rating that complies?","o":["70 A","90 A","100 A","80 A"],"a":1,"e":"70 A x 1.25 = 87.5 A; the next standard size per 240.6(A) is 90 A.","r":"NEC 706.31, 240.6(A)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":3,"q":"The maximum circuit current of an ESS output is 64 A. What is the minimum ampacity of the output conductors based on the 125 percent requirement?","o":["64 A","90 A","80 A","72 A"],"a":2,"e":"64 A x 1.25 = 80 A.","r":"NEC 706.31","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"When may the OCPD for an ESS circuit be rated at 100 percent of the maximum current instead of 125 percent?","o":["Whenever the circuit is protected by a fuse","Whenever the ESS is outdoors","Whenever the circuit is under 50 volts","When the assembly, including the overcurrent devices, is listed for operation at 100 percent of its rating"],"a":3,"e":"The 706.31 exception allows 100 percent sizing where the assembly including the OCPD is listed for operation at 100 percent of its rating.","r":"NEC 706.31","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":1,"q":"Which article governs the interconnection of an energy storage system with a utility?","o":["Article 690","Article 705","Article 725","Article 480"],"a":1,"e":"706.16 directs interactive ESS connections to the interconnected power production source requirements of Article 705.","r":"NEC 706.16, Art. 705","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"An ESS operates in stand-alone mode supplying premises wiring. Which article provides the requirements for stand-alone systems?","o":["Article 760","Article 705","Article 692","Article 710"],"a":3,"e":"706.16 refers stand-alone operation to Article 710.","r":"NEC 706.16, Art. 710","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum depth of working space in front of ESS equipment on a 120/240-volt single-phase system (150 volts or less to ground) (Condition 1: exposed live parts on one side, none on the other)?","o":["1.5 m (5 ft)","1.2 m (4 ft)","900 mm (3 ft)","600 mm (2 ft)"],"a":2,"e":"Table 110.26(A)(1): 0-150 V to ground requires 3 ft for Condition 1.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(1)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"ESS equipment is on a 480Y/277-volt system, with exposed live parts on one side of the working space and grounded parts on the other (Condition 2). What is the minimum working space depth?","o":["1.2 m (4 ft)","1.5 m (5 ft)","900 mm (3 ft)","1.07 m (3.5 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"Table 110.26(A)(1): 151-600 V to ground, Condition 2 is 3.5 ft.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(1)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum width of working space in front of ESS electrical equipment?","o":["30 in. or the width of the equipment, whichever is greater","Exactly the width of the equipment","36 in. fixed","24 in. fixed"],"a":0,"e":"110.26(A)(2) requires width of at least 30 in. or the width of the equipment, whichever is greater.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(2)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum headroom for working space about ESS equipment rated 1000 V or less?","o":["2.5 m (8 ft)","2.3 m (7.5 ft)","1.8 m (6 ft)","2.0 m (6.5 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"110.26(A)(3) requires headroom of at least 6.5 ft.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(3)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":1,"q":"Which description best matches the Article 100/706 definition of an energy storage system?","o":["One or more components assembled together capable of storing energy for use at a future time","A utility meter with a backup battery","A device that only converts dc to ac","A generator driven by an engine"],"a":0,"e":"The definition of an energy storage system is one or more components assembled together capable of storing energy for use at a future time.","r":"NEC 706.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"Which condition best describes a disconnecting means that is 'readily accessible'?","o":["Located behind a permanently locked door with no key available","Located in a space requiring a tool to remove a cover only","Capable of being reached quickly without needing to climb over or remove obstacles or use portable ladders","Located above a suspended ceiling"],"a":2,"e":"Article 100 defines readily accessible as capable of being reached quickly for operation without having to climb over or remove obstacles or resort to portable ladders.","r":"NEC Art. 100, 706.15(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":3,"q":"A lithium-ion ESS is installed in a one-family dwelling garage. The installer places the emergency shutdown initiating switch only inside the garage next to the ESS. Why is this arrangement noncompliant?","o":["It must be a lockable disconnect","The initiating device must be at a readily accessible location outside the building","It must be located in the attic","It must be rated at least 600 V"],"a":1,"e":"706.15(B) requires the emergency shutdown initiation device to be readily accessible outside the building for one- and two-family dwellings.","r":"NEC 706.15(B)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":3,"q":"An ESS in a one-family dwelling includes an emergency shutdown function. Which statement about what this function does is correct?","o":["It disconnects the utility service","It de-energizes the batteries internal cells","It replaces the ESS disconnecting means","It stops power export from the ESS to other premises wiring"],"a":3,"e":"The emergency shutdown stops power export to other premises wiring; it is not the disconnecting means itself.","r":"NEC 706.15(B)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"Where must the overcurrent protection for ESS battery conductors be located?","o":["At the far end of the conductor","Only at the main service panel","As close as practicable to the storage battery terminals","Anywhere within 100 ft"],"a":2,"e":"240.21(H) permits battery conductor overcurrent protection as close as practicable to the battery terminals in a nonhazardous location, as referenced by 706.15(E).","r":"NEC 240.21(H), 706.15(E)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":2,"q":"What type of arc-flash information must appear on the ESS disconnect for a non-dwelling installation?","o":["An arc-flash label in accordance with acceptable industry practice","A label stating NFPA 70E is not applicable","No arc-flash label, since ESS is dc","An arc-flash label only if over 1000 V"],"a":0,"e":"706.15(C) requires an arc-flash label applied in accordance with acceptable industry practice for non-dwelling ESS.","r":"NEC 706.15(C)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":1,"q":"If the ESS disconnecting means is within the ESS itself (integral), does it satisfy the disconnect requirement?","o":["Yes, a disconnecting means integral to the listed ESS can satisfy the requirement","Only in dwelling units","Only if it is rated above 1000 V","No, a separate external disconnect is always required"],"a":0,"e":"706.15 permits the disconnecting means to be located within the ESS.","r":"NEC 706.15(A)-(B)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":1,"q":"Article 710 applies to power production systems that operate in which manner?","o":["Stand-alone, not interactive with an electric supply network","Only above 1000 volts","Only in parallel with the utility at all times","Only as emergency systems for fire pumps"],"a":0,"e":"Article 710 covers stand-alone systems, which supply power independent of an electric power production and distribution network.","r":"NEC 710.1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":2,"q":"Under Article 710, how must the capacity of a stand-alone supply compare with the largest single piece of utilization equipment connected?","o":["It must be equal to or greater than the load of the largest single utilization equipment","It may be any size, even smaller than the largest load","It must be at least twice the largest load","It must be at least 125 percent of the total connected load"],"a":0,"e":"710.15(A) permits the stand-alone supply to have less capacity than the calculated load, but not less than the largest single utilization equipment.","r":"NEC 710.15(A)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":3,"q":"A stand-alone inverter supplies a workshop and has a continuous output of 5 kW. The largest single piece of utilization equipment is a 7 kW load. Which statement is correct?","o":["The supply complies because it only needs to meet 125 percent of the smallest load","The supply complies if a plaque is installed","The supply is too small, because it must be at least equal to the largest single load","The supply complies because stand-alone supplies have no capacity requirement"],"a":2,"e":"710.15(A) requires the stand-alone supply capacity to be equal to or greater than the largest single utilization equipment, which here is 7 kW.","r":"NEC 710.15(A)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":3,"q":"A stand-alone supply provides 12 kW. Premises loads total 20 kW calculated, with the largest single load being 6 kW. Is the supply sizing permitted?","o":["No, because it must be at least 125 percent of 20 kW","No, because it must match the full 20 kW calculated load","Yes, because stand-alone supply may be less than calculated load if not less than the largest single load","Yes, but only if a generator is also installed"],"a":2,"e":"710.15(A) allows a stand-alone supply smaller than the calculated load so long as it is not less than the largest single utilization equipment (6 kW here).","r":"NEC 710.15(A)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":1,"q":"What must be installed where a stand-alone system is present at a building, to identify the power sources?","o":["A temporary paper tag","A permanent plaque or directory denoting all electric power sources on or in the premises","A plaque only at the utility pole","No marking, since the system is private"],"a":1,"e":"710.10 requires a permanent plaque or directory identifying all electric power sources on or in the premises.","r":"NEC 710.10","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":2,"q":"How is the maximum current of a stand-alone inverter input circuit determined?","o":["The continuous input current rating of the inverter when producing rated power at the lowest input voltage","The sum of the PV module Isc ratings times 1.25 only","The inverter output current at nominal voltage","The ampere rating of the main breaker"],"a":0,"e":"710.12 bases the stand-alone inverter input circuit current on the inverter's continuous input current rating at rated power and the lowest input voltage.","r":"NEC 710.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":2,"q":"Where a plug-in-type circuit breaker is back-fed by a stand-alone supply in a panelboard, what is required?","o":["The panelboard must be at least 400 A","The breaker must be replaced with a fuse","Nothing special beyond normal installation","The breaker must be secured in place by an additional fastener beyond the normal bus clamping"],"a":3,"e":"408.36(D) requires plug-in-type back-fed devices to be secured by an additional fastener, as referenced by Article 710.","r":"NEC 408.36(D), 710.15","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":1,"q":"Article 712 covers which type of system?","o":["Alternating-current microgrids only","Emergency lighting only","Direct-current microgrids","Fire alarm power sources"],"a":2,"e":"Article 712 contains the requirements for dc microgrids.","r":"NEC 712.1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":1,"q":"What type of marking is required at a building supplied by or containing a dc microgrid to identify its power sources?","o":["No marking is required","A handwritten tag at the main panel","A label on the utility meter only","A permanent plaque or directory denoting all electric power sources on or in the premises"],"a":3,"e":"712.10 requires a permanent plaque or directory identifying all power sources on or in the premises.","r":"NEC 712.10","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":1,"q":"A dc microgrid is best described as a power distribution system consisting of what?","o":["More than one interconnected dc power source, and dc loads, that may operate in conjunction with or independently of other sources","Only a PV array and no storage or loads","An ac transformer and a rectifier only","A single battery connected to one load"],"a":0,"e":"A dc microgrid consists of more than one interconnected dc power source, with loads, capable of operating with or independent of other power sources.","r":"NEC 712.2","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":2,"q":"In a dc microgrid, a bipolar system is one that has what characteristic?","o":["Two monopole circuits with opposite polarities with respect to a common reference point","A single grounded conductor at zero volts only","Only one conductor","Two ac phases"],"a":0,"e":"A bipolar dc system has two monopole circuits of opposite polarity with respect to a common reference point.","r":"NEC 712.2","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":2,"q":"In Article 712, a dc-to-dc converter can best be described as a device that does what?","o":["Only measures current","Converts ac to dc","Converts dc to ac","Changes one dc voltage level to another dc level"],"a":3,"e":"A dc-to-dc converter outputs a dc voltage or current different from its dc input.","r":"NEC 712.2, 690.2","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":1,"q":"A Class 1 power-limited circuit must have a power source with a rated output not exceeding what?","o":["30 volts and 1000 volt-amperes","12 volts and 100 volt-amperes","120 volts and 2000 volt-amperes","60 volts and 500 volt-amperes"],"a":0,"e":"Class 1 power-limited circuits are supplied by a source limited to 30 V and 1000 VA.","r":"NEC 725.41(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum voltage permitted for a Class 1 remote-control or signaling circuit?","o":["1000 volts","150 volts","30 volts","600 volts"],"a":3,"e":"Class 1 remote-control and signaling circuits are limited to 600 volts; only the power-limited version is limited to 30 V.","r":"NEC 725.41(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":1,"q":"What is the smallest copper conductor size permitted for a Class 1 circuit?","o":["14 AWG","20 AWG","18 AWG","22 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Class 1 circuit conductors may be 18 AWG or 16 AWG when they meet the conditions of 725.49(A); larger sizes are used otherwise.","r":"NEC 725.49(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum overcurrent protection for 18 AWG Class 1 circuit conductors?","o":["5 amperes","7 amperes","15 amperes","10 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"725.43 limits overcurrent protection to 7 A for 18 AWG and 10 A for 16 AWG Class 1 conductors.","r":"NEC 725.43","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum overcurrent protection for 16 AWG Class 1 circuit conductors?","o":["10 amperes","15 amperes","7 amperes","20 amperes"],"a":0,"e":"725.43 limits overcurrent protection to 7 A for 18 AWG and 10 A for 16 AWG conductors.","r":"NEC 725.43","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":3,"q":"A Class 1 circuit run in a raceway uses 18 AWG conductors to supply a 7 ampere load. Is this installation permitted?","o":["Yes, because the OCPD is 7 amperes","No, because 18 AWG may supply loads only up to its fixture-wire ampacity of 6 amperes","No, because 18 AWG is never permitted in Class 1 circuits","Yes, because Class 1 circuits have no load limit"],"a":1,"e":"725.49(A) allows 18 AWG only for loads not exceeding the 402.5 ampacities, which is 6 A for 18 AWG.","r":"NEC 725.49(A), 402.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"What minimum voltage rating must the insulation of Class 1 circuit conductors have?","o":["150 volts","600 volts","300 volts","1000 volts"],"a":1,"e":"725.49(B) requires Class 1 conductor insulation to be rated at least 600 V.","r":"NEC 725.49(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"Under what condition may Class 1 circuit conductors share a raceway with power-supply conductors?","o":["Only when the raceway is nonmetallic","The equipment powered is functionally associated","Only when the power conductors are 480 volts","Never, under any condition"],"a":1,"e":"725.48 permits Class 1 and power-supply conductors in the same raceway where the equipment they serve is functionally associated.","r":"NEC 725.48(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"A 24-volt thermostat Class 2 cable is to be pulled into the same EMT as 120-volt lighting branch-circuit conductors. What is the result?","o":["Permitted if the EMT is at least 1 in.","Permitted if the cable has a 300 volt rating","Permitted if the lighting is on a GFCI circuit","Not permitted, since Class 2 conductors may not share a raceway with power conductors"],"a":3,"e":"725.136(A) prohibits Class 2 and 3 conductors in the same raceway as electric light and power conductors, subject only to listed exceptions that do not apply here.","r":"NEC 725.136(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"What minimum separation applies between open Class 2 conductors and open power conductors where no raceway, cable or barrier is used?","o":["6 mm (1/4 in.)","25 mm (1 in.)","50 mm (2 in.)","300 mm (12 in.)"],"a":2,"e":"725.136(B) requires open Class 2 and 3 conductors to be separated by at least 50 mm (2 in.) from light and power conductors unless a special equally protective method is used.","r":"NEC 725.136(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":1,"q":"May Type CL3 cable be used for a Class 2 circuit?","o":["Yes, Type CL3 may be used for Class 2 circuits","No, only CL2 may be used for Class 2","Only in dwelling units","Only if installed in a plenum"],"a":0,"e":"Class 3 cable has a higher voltage rating and may substitute for Class 2 cable.","r":"NEC 725.154, 725.179","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"May Type CL2 cable be used for a Class 3 circuit?","o":["Yes, but only below 24 volts","Yes, if it is installed in conduit","No, CL2 cable is not permitted for Class 3 circuits","Yes, always"],"a":2,"e":"Class 2 cable is rated for lower voltage and cannot substitute for Class 3 cable.","r":"NEC 725.154, 725.179","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":1,"q":"What does the suffix P indicate in Class 2 cable such as CL2P?","o":["The cable is for plastic raceways only","The cable is listed for use in plenums","The cable is for direct burial","The cable is power-limited tray cable"],"a":1,"e":"CL2P and CL3P are plenum-rated Class 2 and Class 3 cables.","r":"NEC 725.179, 725.154","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"Which Class 2 cable type is appropriate for a vertical run passing through a floor penetration in a riser?","o":["CL2X","CL2 only if lengths are over 10 ft","CL2R","CL2"],"a":2,"e":"Type CL2R (riser) or CL2P (plenum) is required for riser runs; general-purpose CL2 is not.","r":"NEC 725.154, 725.179","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"Which Class 2 cable may be installed in an air-handling plenum without a metal raceway?","o":["CL2P","CL2X","CL2","CL2R"],"a":0,"e":"Plenum-rated Type CL2P is permitted in other spaces used for environmental air such as plenums.","r":"NEC 725.154, 300.22(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"Which Type may substitute for CL2R cable in a riser?","o":["CM","CMX","CL2X","CMR (communications riser)"],"a":3,"e":"Type CMR may be used in place of CL2R, per the cable substitution table.","r":"NEC 725.154","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"What is required of the accessible portion of abandoned Class 2 and Class 3 cables?","o":["They may be left indefinitely","They must be cut off at the first junction box","They must be removed","They must be tagged only"],"a":2,"e":"725.25 requires the accessible portion of abandoned Class 2, Class 3 and PLTC cables to be removed.","r":"NEC 725.25","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"May Class 2 cable be strapped or taped to the outside of a conduit for support?","o":["No, Class 2 and 3 conductors may not be supported by attaching to a raceway exterior","Yes, if the conduit is EMT","Yes, using listed straps","Yes, if the circuit is under 24 volts"],"a":0,"e":"725.143 prohibits strapping, taping or attaching Class 2 and 3 conductors to the exterior of any conduit or raceway as a means of support.","r":"NEC 725.143","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":1,"q":"Class 1, Class 2 and Class 3 circuits must be identified at terminal and junction locations to prevent what?","o":["Corrosion","Overheating of the raceway","Unintentional interference with other circuits during testing and servicing","Voltage drop"],"a":2,"e":"725.30 requires identification so that other circuits are not unintentionally interfered with during testing and servicing.","r":"NEC 725.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is a permitted power source for Class 2 circuits?","o":["Any 120 volt branch circuit","A listed Class 2 transformer","An unlisted step-down transformer","A 480 volt feeder"],"a":1,"e":"725.121 permits a listed Class 2 or Class 3 transformer or power supply, or listed equipment marked as a Class 2 source.","r":"NEC 725.121(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"What limits the output power of a Class 2 or Class 3 power source?","o":["The limits in Chapter 9 Tables 11(A) and 11(B)","Table 250.122","Table 430.250","Table 310.16"],"a":0,"e":"Class 2 and 3 power sources are limited as shown in Chapter 9 Tables 11(A) and 11(B).","r":"NEC 725.121, Ch. 9 Tables 11(A) and 11(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":1,"q":"What wiring rules apply to conductors on the supply side of a Class 2 power source?","o":["Article 725 Part III only","Article 800","Article 760 only","Chapters 1 through 4 of the NEC"],"a":3,"e":"725.127 requires supply-side wiring to comply with Chapters 1 through 4.","r":"NEC 725.127","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"May two or more Class 2 circuits be installed in the same cable or raceway?","o":["Only if each is under 12 volts","Yes","No, each Class 2 circuit needs its own raceway","Only in dwellings"],"a":1,"e":"725.139 permits two or more Class 2 circuits in the same cable, enclosure or raceway.","r":"NEC 725.139(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":1,"q":"For which use is Type PLTC (power-limited tray cable) specifically listed?","o":["Direct burial in all cases","Plenum use without markings","Installation in cable trays","Branch circuits"],"a":2,"e":"PLTC is listed for use in cable trays in power-limited circuits.","r":"NEC 725.154, 725.179","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":3,"q":"A 24-volt control circuit is supplied by a transformer rated 24 V, 1500 VA. Which classification applies?","o":["Class 1 power-limited","Class 1 remote-control circuit, since the source exceeds 1000 VA","Class 2","Class 3"],"a":1,"e":"A Class 1 power-limited source is limited to 30 V and 1000 VA; at 1500 VA this is a Class 1 remote-control circuit, with 600 V maximum.","r":"NEC 725.41","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":1,"q":"How must exposed Class 2 cables on ceilings and sidewalls be installed?","o":["Laid loosely on the ceiling","Held up by adjacent pipes","Supported by the building structure so they will not be damaged by normal building use","Supported only by the ceiling grid wires"],"a":2,"e":"725.24 requires exposed cables to be supported by the building structure and secured with fittings that do not damage the cable.","r":"NEC 725.24","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":2,"q":"Where must overcurrent protection for a Class 1 circuit be located in general?","o":["At the load only","Anywhere on the circuit","At the point where the conductor to be protected receives its supply","At the service entrance only"],"a":2,"e":"725.45 requires Class 1 overcurrent protection at the point where the conductor receives its supply, subject to listed exceptions.","r":"NEC 725.45","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum rated output voltage of a power source for a nonpower-limited fire alarm (NPLFA) circuit?","o":["600 volts","150 volts","30 volts","1000 volts"],"a":0,"e":"760.41(A) limits NPLFA power sources to 600 volts rated output.","r":"NEC 760.41(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":1,"q":"The branch circuit supplying fire alarm equipment may supply which other loads?","o":["Exit signs only","Emergency lighting only","Receptacles in the same room","No other loads"],"a":3,"e":"760.41(B) requires the fire alarm branch circuit to supply no other loads.","r":"NEC 760.41(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":2,"q":"May the branch circuit supplying a fire alarm control unit be protected by a GFCI or AFCI device?","o":["Yes, AFCI is required in dwellings","No, it must not be supplied through GFCI or AFCI protection","Yes, but only a combination type","Yes, GFCI is required"],"a":1,"e":"760.41(B) states that the fire alarm branch circuit shall not be supplied through ground-fault circuit interrupters or arc-fault circuit interrupters.","r":"NEC 760.41(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":2,"q":"How must the circuit disconnecting means for a fire alarm branch circuit be identified?","o":["No special identification is required","Yellow identification and marked CONTROL","With red identification and marked FIRE ALARM CIRCUIT","Blue identification and marked EMERGENCY"],"a":2,"e":"760.41(B) requires red identification and marking FIRE ALARM CIRCUIT, accessible only to qualified personnel.","r":"NEC 760.41(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":2,"q":"Where must the location of the overcurrent device for the fire alarm branch circuit be identified?","o":["At the nearest exit door","In the service panel directory only","At the fire alarm control unit","At the utility meter"],"a":2,"e":"760.41(B) requires the location of the branch-circuit overcurrent protective device to be permanently identified at the fire alarm control unit.","r":"NEC 760.41(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum overcurrent protection for 18 AWG conductors of an NPLFA circuit?","o":["15 amperes","3 amperes","10 amperes","7 amperes"],"a":3,"e":"760.43 refers to 760.45 limits: 7 A for 18 AWG and 10 A for 16 AWG.","r":"NEC 760.43, 760.45","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum overcurrent protection for 16 AWG conductors of an NPLFA circuit?","o":["10 amperes","7 amperes","15 amperes","20 amperes"],"a":0,"e":"760.45 limits 16 AWG NPLFA conductors to 10 A.","r":"NEC 760.43, 760.45","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum conductor size for NPLFA circuits?","o":["12 AWG","18 AWG","20 AWG","22 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"NPLFA conductors must be at least 18 AWG.","r":"NEC 760.49(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":2,"q":"What voltage rating must the insulation of NPLFA circuit conductors have?","o":["150 volts","1000 volts","600 volts","300 volts"],"a":2,"e":"760.49(B) requires conductors to have insulation rated for 600 volts.","r":"NEC 760.49(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":1,"q":"Which cable type is listed for power-limited fire alarm circuits in a plenum?","o":["FPLR","FPL","FPLP","NPLFR"],"a":2,"e":"Type FPLP is listed for plenum use, FPLR for riser, and FPL for general use.","r":"NEC 760.179, 760.154","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":1,"q":"Which power-limited fire alarm cable is listed for use in vertical runs through floors?","o":["FPLR","NPLF","CMX","FPL"],"a":0,"e":"Type FPLR (riser) is suitable for vertical runs in a shaft or from floor to floor.","r":"NEC 760.179, 760.154","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":2,"q":"May Type FPLR cable be used in place of Type FPL cable?","o":["Only outdoors","Yes","Only in dwellings","No, never"],"a":1,"e":"The cable substitution table allows FPLR (and FPLP) to substitute for FPL; higher-rated types may replace lower-rated ones.","r":"NEC 760.154","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":2,"q":"A power-limited fire alarm circuit runs through an air-handling plenum without a raceway. Which cable is permitted?","o":["CL2","FPLR","FPL","FPLP"],"a":3,"e":"Plenum-rated FPLP (or equivalent plenum cable per the substitution table) is required in plenums not in a raceway.","r":"NEC 760.154, 300.22(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":2,"q":"May power-limited fire alarm conductors be installed in the same raceway as 120-volt branch-circuit power conductors?","o":["Yes, if 14 AWG","No, not in the same raceway as electric light and power conductors","Yes, if the circuit is under 24 volts","Yes, if the raceway is metal"],"a":1,"e":"760.136(A) prohibits power-limited fire alarm circuits in the same raceway as electric light, power, Class 1 or NPLFA conductors.","r":"NEC 760.136(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":2,"q":"What separation is required between open power-limited fire alarm conductors and open power conductors with no barrier?","o":["25 mm (1 in.)","150 mm (6 in.)","6 mm (1/4 in.)","50 mm (2 in.)"],"a":3,"e":"760.136(B) requires at least 50 mm (2 in.) of separation unless another equally protective method is used.","r":"NEC 760.136(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":3,"q":"A PLFA circuit and an NPLFA circuit are to share one conduit. How does the Code treat this?","o":["Not permitted, since PLFA circuits may not share a raceway with NPLFA conductors","Permitted if the PLFA conductors are FPLP","Permitted if over 50 volts","Permitted if both are 14 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"760.136(A) prohibits PLFA conductors in the same raceway as NPLFA conductors.","r":"NEC 760.136(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":2,"q":"What is required for the accessible portion of abandoned fire alarm cables?","o":["They may be left in place","They must be taped to supports","They must be capped","They must be removed"],"a":3,"e":"760.25 requires removal of the accessible portion of abandoned fire alarm cables.","r":"NEC 760.25","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":1,"q":"At terminal and junction locations, fire alarm circuits must be identified in a manner that does what?","o":["Shows manufacturer","Indicates cable length","Prevents unintentional interference with other circuits during testing and servicing","Indicates age of the cable"],"a":2,"e":"760.30 requires identification to prevent unintentional interference during testing and servicing.","r":"NEC 760.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":1,"q":"What requirements apply to wiring on the supply side of the power source of a PLFA circuit?","o":["None","Article 725 only","Chapters 1 through 4","Article 800"],"a":2,"e":"760.127 requires supply-side wiring to comply with Chapters 1 through 4.","r":"NEC 760.127","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":2,"q":"What limits the output of a power source for a power-limited fire alarm circuit?","o":["Chapter 9 Tables 12(A) and 12(B)","Table 250.66","Table 430.250","Table 310.16"],"a":0,"e":"PLFA power sources are limited per Chapter 9 Tables 12(A) and 12(B).","r":"NEC 760.121, Ch. 9 Tables 12(A), 12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":1,"q":"How must exposed fire alarm cables on ceilings and sidewalls be supported?","o":["By hanging loosely","By adjacent plumbing","By the suspended ceiling grid only","By the building structure, secured so as not to damage the cable"],"a":3,"e":"760.24 requires exposed cables to be supported by the building structure and not damaged by normal building use.","r":"NEC 760.24","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":1,"q":"When fire alarm cables pass through a fire-rated wall, what must be done to the opening?","o":["Leave it open for service","Fill with insulation only","Firestop it to maintain the fire-resistance rating","Nothing, since fire alarm is low voltage"],"a":2,"e":"760.3 requires penetrations through fire-resistance-rated assemblies to be firestopped per 300.21.","r":"NEC 760.3, 300.21","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":3,"q":"A fire alarm panel is fed from a 20 A branch circuit with an AFCI breaker that also feeds two office receptacles. How many NEC violations are present?","o":["Two: it supplies other loads and it is AFCI protected","None","Three: the circuit must also be 30 A","One: only the AFCI"],"a":0,"e":"760.41(B) prohibits other loads on the fire alarm branch circuit and prohibits GFCI/AFCI protection.","r":"NEC 760.41(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":2,"q":"Which suffix on fire alarm cable indicates it is a circuit integrity cable?","o":["CIP","CI","PL","HF"],"a":1,"e":"Cables marked with the suffix CI are circuit integrity cables that maintain circuit operation for a specified time in a fire.","r":"NEC 760.176, 760.179","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","d":2,"q":"Which cable type is listed for nonpower-limited fire alarm circuits in a plenum?","o":["CL3P","NPLFR","NPLFP","FPLP"],"a":2,"e":"NPLFP is the plenum-rated nonpower-limited fire alarm cable; FPLP is for power-limited circuits.","r":"NEC 760.176, 760.179","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":2,"q":"An optical fiber cable will be installed without a raceway in a plenum (a space used for environmental air). Which cable type is listed for that use?","o":["OFNG","OFNR","OFNP","OFN"],"a":2,"e":"Types OFNP and OFCP are the plenum-rated optical fiber cables. Riser and general-purpose types are not permitted exposed in a plenum.","r":"NEC 770.154(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":1,"q":"In optical fiber cable type designations such as OFNR and OFCR, what does the letter N indicate?","o":["The cable has no outer jacket","The cable is network-powered","The cable is nonconductive, with no metallic members or other electrically conductive materials","The cable is non-flame-propagating only"],"a":2,"e":"N means nonconductive and C means conductive. The R, G and P letters indicate riser, general-purpose and plenum.","r":"NEC 770.2, 770.179","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":1,"q":"An optical fiber cable that contains a non-current-carrying metallic strength member is classified as which type?","o":["Plenum optical fiber cable","Conductive optical fiber cable","Composite optical fiber cable","Nonconductive optical fiber cable"],"a":1,"e":"A conductive cable has non-current-carrying conductive members such as metallic strength members or armor. A composite cable has current-carrying electrical conductors.","r":"NEC 770.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":1,"q":"An optical fiber cable that contains both optical fibers and current-carrying electrical conductors is called a:","o":["Composite optical fiber cable","Cable routing assembly","Conductive optical fiber cable","Nonconductive optical fiber cable"],"a":0,"e":"Composite cables combine optical fibers with current-carrying electrical conductors. Conductive cables only have non-current-carrying metallic members.","r":"NEC 770.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":2,"q":"A listed optical fiber cable runs vertically through three floors in an open shaft that is not a fireproof shaft. Which of these is the minimum cable type permitted?","o":["OFNR","OFNG","OFN","OFCG"],"a":0,"e":"Cables in vertical runs penetrating more than one floor must be riser rated (OFNR or OFCR) or plenum rated. General-purpose types are not permitted for that use.","r":"NEC 770.154(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":2,"q":"Which cable type may be installed in place of Type OFNR cable under the cable substitution rules?","o":["OFNP","OFNG","OFN","OFCG"],"a":0,"e":"A higher-rated cable may substitute for a lower-rated one: plenum (OFNP) may replace riser (OFNR), but general-purpose cable may not replace riser cable.","r":"NEC 770.154, Table 770.154(b)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":2,"q":"A nonconductive optical fiber cable is to be pulled into the same raceway as 480-volt branch-circuit conductors. Under Article 770 this is:","o":["Permitted, because the cable is nonconductive","Not permitted under any circumstance","Permitted only if a barrier is installed inside the raceway","Permitted only if the cable is OFNP"],"a":0,"e":"Nonconductive optical fiber cables may occupy the same raceway or cable tray with electric light and power conductors. Conductive cables are treated differently.","r":"NEC 770.133(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":2,"q":"The bonding conductor or grounding electrode conductor for the metallic member of a conductive optical fiber cable is not required to be larger than:","o":["2 AWG","10 AWG","4 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"The conductor must be at least 14 AWG with ampacity about equal to the metallic member, but it need not exceed 6 AWG.","r":"NEC 770.100","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":1,"q":"Under Article 770, an installed optical fiber cable that is not terminated at equipment other than a connector and is not identified for future use with a tag is considered:","o":["A spare cable","A limited-use cable","Abandoned optical fiber cable","A cable routing assembly"],"a":2,"e":"Cable that is not terminated and not tagged for future use meets the definition of abandoned cable.","r":"NEC 770.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":2,"q":"What does Article 770 require for the accessible portion of abandoned optical fiber cables?","o":["It must be left in place and capped","It must be removed","It must be grounded at both ends","It must be tagged and left in place"],"a":1,"e":"Abandoned cable that is accessible must be removed so it does not add fire load or clutter. Cable tagged for future use is not abandoned.","r":"NEC 770.25","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":1,"q":"Where optical fiber cable passes through a fire-resistance-rated wall, the opening around the cable must be:","o":["Sleeved with PVC and left unsealed","Firestopped using an approved method to maintain the fire-resistance rating","Filled with loose fiberglass insulation","Left open for ventilation"],"a":1,"e":"Openings through fire-rated assemblies must be sealed with approved methods so the rating is maintained.","r":"NEC 770.26","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":2,"q":"Above a suspended ceiling, optical fiber cables are installed near electrical equipment that is reached by removing ceiling panels. What is required of the cable installation?","o":["Panels must be permanently fastened","Cables may be laid on top of the ceiling panels","Cables must be laid in a spiral above each panel","Accumulated cables must not prevent removal of the panels that give access to the equipment"],"a":3,"e":"Access to electrical equipment behind panels designed to allow access must not be denied by an accumulation of cables.","r":"NEC 770.21","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size of the bonding conductor or grounding electrode conductor for the metallic member of a conductive optical fiber cable entering a building?","o":["12 AWG","18 AWG","14 AWG","10 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"The minimum size is 14 AWG, with ampacity about equal to that of the metallic member.","r":"NEC 770.100","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":2,"q":"In a one-family dwelling, the bonding conductor or grounding electrode conductor for an optical fiber cable's metallic member must be as short as practicable and not longer than:","o":["15 m (50 ft)","6 m (20 ft)","3 m (10 ft)","9 m (30 ft)"],"a":1,"e":"For one- and two-family dwellings the length limit is 6 m (20 ft).","r":"NEC 770.100","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":3,"q":"In a one-family dwelling the 20 ft conductor length cannot be met, so a separate grounding electrode is installed for the optical fiber cable's metallic member. What connection is also required?","o":["A bonding jumper of at least 6 AWG copper (or equivalent) to the power grounding electrode system","A bonding jumper to the service neutral at the first outlet","No connection, because the electrode is separate by design","A 14 AWG conductor to the nearest water pipe"],"a":0,"e":"Separate electrodes must be bonded together with a jumper not smaller than 6 AWG copper or equivalent, so all electrodes are at the same potential.","r":"NEC 770.100","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":2,"q":"A conductive optical fiber cable with a metallic strength member enters a building from outdoors. The non-current-carrying metallic member must be:","o":["Left floating inside the building","Grounded, or interrupted by an insulating joint, as close as practicable to the point of entrance","Bonded to the neutral at the first panelboard","Grounded only at the far end of the cable"],"a":1,"e":"The metallic member must be grounded or interrupted close to where the cable enters, to keep outside faults from being carried inside.","r":"NEC 770.93","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":2,"q":"Unlisted nonconductive optical fiber cable (not in conduit) enters a building from outside and ends at an enclosure. What is the maximum length of the cable inside the building, measured from the point of entrance?","o":["30 m (100 ft)","3 m (10 ft)","6 m (20 ft)","15 m (50 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"Unlisted outside-plant nonconductive cable may enter and run no more than 15 m (50 ft) inside before terminating.","r":"NEC 770.48(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":1,"q":"Optical fiber cables installed within a building must be:","o":["Of any type if run in conduit","Listed as suitable for the purpose and marked with the cable type","Approved by the building owner","Listed only if operating above 600 volts"],"a":1,"e":"Cables installed in buildings are required to be listed and marked per Article 770.","r":"NEC 770.113, 770.179","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":2,"q":"Optical fiber cable is to be installed in a Class I, Division 1 location. Which requirement applies?","o":["Only Article 770 applies","The installation is exempt because it carries no current","Only Article 725 applies","Articles 500 through 516 must be met in addition to Article 770"],"a":3,"e":"Optical fiber cable installed in classified locations must comply with the applicable hazardous location articles.","r":"NEC 770.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":2,"q":"Optical fiber cable installed exposed on a ceiling surface must be:","o":["Supported by structural components, using fittings designed so the cable is not damaged","Supported only by its own metallic strength member","Laid loosely on top of the ceiling grid","Secured with nails driven through the jacket"],"a":0,"e":"Cable must be installed neatly and supported so normal building use will not damage it.","r":"NEC 770.24","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":3,"q":"A contractor has Type OFNR cable but the pathway is a plenum. Which approach is permitted?","o":["Install it exposed, because riser is fire rated","Install the cable in a metal raceway such as EMT of a type permitted by 300.22","Wrap it in fire-retardant tape","Install it in PVC conduit"],"a":1,"e":"Non-plenum cable can be used in a plenum only when installed in the metal raceways recognized for environmental air spaces. PVC is not permitted there.","r":"NEC 770.154(A), 300.22(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":1,"q":"The bonding conductor or grounding electrode conductor for a conductive optical fiber cable should be:","o":["Bare aluminum only","Insulated","Uninsulated steel","Bare copper or aluminum, either one"],"a":1,"e":"The conductor is required to be insulated and made of copper or another suitable corrosion-resistant conductive material.","r":"NEC 770.100","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":1,"q":"How should the bonding or grounding electrode conductor for an optical fiber cable be routed to the electrode?","o":["In as straight a line as practicable","With loops every 10 ft","Coiled at the entrance","Through a ferrous raceway that is not bonded"],"a":0,"e":"The conductor is run in as straight a line as practicable.","r":"NEC 770.100","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":2,"q":"A communications intersystem bonding termination is installed at the service. What is the minimum number of bonding conductor connections its terminals must accommodate?","o":["One","Two","Six","Three"],"a":3,"e":"The intersystem bonding termination must have terminals able to accept at least three intersystem bonding conductors.","r":"NEC 250.94(A), 770.100","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","d":1,"q":"Which description fits an optical fiber cable?","o":["A twisted pair carrying 48 volts dc","A factory assembly of one or more optical fibers with an overall covering","A single bare glass strand without a covering","A coaxial cable with a glass dielectric"],"a":1,"e":"An optical fiber cable is a factory assembly of one or more optical fibers having an overall covering.","r":"NEC 770.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":1,"q":"Which communications cable type is listed for installation in a plenum without a raceway?","o":["CMP","CMX","CMG","CMR"],"a":0,"e":"Type CMP is plenum-rated. CMR is riser, CMG and CM are general-purpose, and CMX is limited-use.","r":"NEC 800.154(A), 800.179","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"A communications cable runs vertically through more than one floor in an open shaft that is not fire-rated. Which is the minimum cable type?","o":["CMG","CMR","CM","CMX"],"a":1,"e":"Vertical runs through floors need riser (CMR) or plenum (CMP) cable. General-purpose and limited-use types are not suitable.","r":"NEC 800.154(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"Which communications cable type may be installed in place of Type CMR under the substitution rules?","o":["CMP","CMX","CM","CMG"],"a":0,"e":"Plenum cable (CMP) has higher fire performance than riser cable and may substitute for it. The reverse is not allowed.","r":"NEC 800.154, Table 800.154(b)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":1,"q":"Which communications cable type is the limited-use type, permitted only in specified applications such as dwellings?","o":["CMP","CMR","CMX","CMG"],"a":2,"e":"CMX is limited-use cable with reduced fire-resistance performance.","r":"NEC 800.179, 800.154","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"A communications cable runs along a wall parallel to loose 120-volt power conductors that are not in a raceway or cable sheath, with no barrier between them. What minimum separation is required?","o":["25 mm (1 in.)","150 mm (6 in.)","50 mm (2 in.)","12 mm (1/2 in.)"],"a":2,"e":"Communications conductors must be separated at least 50 mm (2 in.) from light and power conductors unless separated by a barrier or the power conductors are in a raceway or sheathed cable.","r":"NEC 800.133(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"Communications cables are to be installed in the same raceway as electric light and power conductors. What is generally required?","o":["The communications cables must be larger than 14 AWG","The raceway must be at least 2 in. trade size","Nothing, if the cables are CMP","A permanent barrier or listed divider separating them"],"a":3,"e":"Communications conductors may not share a raceway or enclosure with power conductors unless they are separated by a permanent barrier or listed divider (with limited exceptions).","r":"NEC 800.133(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"A technician wants to strap communications cable to the outside of an electrical conduit as its means of support. Under the NEC this is:","o":["Not permitted","Permitted if cable ties are used","Permitted if the conduit is EMT","Permitted if the run is under 10 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Communications cables may not be strapped, taped, or attached to the exterior of a raceway as a means of support.","r":"NEC 800.133","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"Which communications circuit requires a listed primary protector?","o":["A fiber-only circuit","A circuit entirely in a conduit inside a building","Any circuit shorter than 100 ft","A circuit run partly or entirely in aerial wire or cable not confined within a block"],"a":3,"e":"Primary protectors are required on circuits that are aerial and not confined within a block, or otherwise exposed to lightning or contact with power conductors over 300 volts to ground.","r":"NEC 800.90(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"Where must the primary protector for a communications circuit entering a dwelling be located?","o":["At the farthest outlet in the dwelling","In, on, or immediately adjacent to the building and as close as practicable to where the exposed conductors enter or attach","In the attic","At the central office only"],"a":1,"e":"The protector is located at the point of entrance, so its grounding conductor can be short.","r":"NEC 800.90(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":1,"q":"Primary protectors for communications circuits shall not be located:","o":["In any hazardous (classified) location or in the vicinity of easily ignitible material","In a utility room","Inside a listed enclosure","On the exterior of a building"],"a":0,"e":"Protectors can arc, so they are kept out of classified locations and away from easily ignitible materials.","r":"NEC 800.90(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size of the bonding conductor or grounding electrode conductor for a communications primary protector?","o":["14 AWG","8 AWG","18 AWG","12 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"The conductor must be insulated, not smaller than 14 AWG, with ampacity about equal to the protector grounding conductor, and need not exceed 6 AWG.","r":"NEC 800.100(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"In a one-family dwelling, the primary protector bonding or grounding electrode conductor must be as short as practicable and not exceed:","o":["3 m (10 ft)","9 m (30 ft)","15 m (50 ft)","6 m (20 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"The 6 m (20 ft) limit applies to one- and two-family dwellings.","r":"NEC 800.100(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"Communications and power systems use separate grounding electrodes at a building. What bonding is required between them?","o":["No bonding when the electrodes are separate","A 14 AWG insulated conductor","A jumper only if the building has a metal roof","A bonding jumper not smaller than 6 AWG copper or equivalent"],"a":3,"e":"A 6 AWG copper bonding jumper must connect the communications electrode to the power grounding electrode system.","r":"NEC 800.100(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":1,"q":"The bonding conductor or grounding electrode conductor for a communications primary protector must be:","o":["Bare copper only","Insulated","Bare aluminum","Uninsulated steel"],"a":1,"e":"The bonding conductor or GEC must be insulated and of copper or another suitable corrosion-resistant conductive material.","r":"NEC 800.100(A)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"A communications cable with a non-current-carrying metallic sheath enters a building. What must be done with the metallic sheath?","o":["Ground it, or interrupt it with an insulating joint, as close as practicable to the point of entrance","Leave it floating in the building","Ground it at the far end only","Connect it to the neutral at the service"],"a":0,"e":"Metallic sheath members must be grounded or interrupted near the entrance to keep fault voltage out of the building.","r":"NEC 800.93","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"Where practicable, what separation should be maintained between communications cables on a building and lightning protection conductors?","o":["1.8 m (6 ft)","0.3 m (1 ft)","3 m (10 ft)","0.6 m (2 ft)"],"a":0,"e":"A separation of at least 1.8 m (6 ft) is required where practicable.","r":"NEC 800.53","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"An overhead communications cable crosses a flat, accessible roof. What is the minimum vertical clearance above the roof, without exceptions applying?","o":["3.7 m (12 ft)","2.5 m (8 ft)","0.9 m (3 ft)","1.5 m (5 ft)"],"a":1,"e":"Overhead communications wires and cables need 2.5 m (8 ft) above roofs, with limited exceptions such as at roof edges and overhangs.","r":"NEC 800.44","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":1,"q":"Under Article 800, an installed communications cable that is not terminated at equipment other than a connector and not identified for future use with a tag is:","o":["Listed by default","Limited-use cable","A spare","Abandoned"],"a":3,"e":"Unterminated, untagged cable is abandoned.","r":"NEC 800.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"What must be done with the accessible portion of abandoned communications cable?","o":["Leave it and cap the ends","Leave it if it is CMP","Remove it","Bond it at both ends"],"a":2,"e":"The accessible portion of abandoned cable must be removed.","r":"NEC 800.25","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":1,"q":"Communications cable passes through a fire-rated floor. The opening must be:","o":["Sleeved with plastic and left unsealed","Firestopped using an approved method","Filled with insulation only","Left open for air circulation"],"a":1,"e":"Penetrations of fire-resistance-rated assemblies must be sealed to maintain the rating.","r":"NEC 800.26","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"Communications cables are bundled above a suspended ceiling that gives access to electrical equipment. What is required?","o":["Cables must be removed from the building","The cables must not accumulate in a way that prevents removal of the access panels","Cables must be hung on the ceiling panels","The panels must be glued in place"],"a":1,"e":"Access to electrical equipment must not be denied by an accumulation of cables.","r":"NEC 800.21","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":1,"q":"Communications cable is installed in a Class I, Division 2 location. Which requirement applies?","o":["The applicable Chapter 5 hazardous location requirements apply","No special requirements apply to communications cable","Only Article 725 applies","Only plenum cable may be used anywhere"],"a":0,"e":"Communications circuits in classified locations must meet the applicable requirements of Articles 500 through 516.","r":"NEC 800.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"Unlisted outside-plant communications cable (not in conduit) enters a building and ends at a protector or enclosure. What is the maximum length inside the building, measured from the point of entrance?","o":["15 m (50 ft)","3 m (10 ft)","30 m (100 ft)","6 m (20 ft)"],"a":0,"e":"Unlisted outside-plant cable may only run 15 m (50 ft) inside the building before terminating, unless enclosed in rigid or intermediate metal conduit.","r":"NEC 800.48","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"A building has a communications intersystem bonding termination installed per 250.94. Where is the primary protector bonding conductor connected?","o":["To the intersystem bonding termination","To a separate driven rod only","To the neutral at the meter","To the cold water pipe at the kitchen sink"],"a":0,"e":"In buildings with an intersystem bonding termination, the bonding conductor connects to it.","r":"NEC 800.100(B), 250.94","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 800 Communications Circuits","d":2,"q":"Communications cable run exposed on a ceiling surface must be:","o":["Secured with nails through the jacket","Laid on top of the ceiling grid","Supported only by electrical conduit it is strapped to","Supported by structural components and secured with fittings that do not damage the cable"],"a":3,"e":"Cables must be installed neatly and supported so normal building use will not damage them.","r":"NEC 800.24","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":1,"q":"Which coaxial cable type is listed for use in plenums without a raceway?","o":["CATVP","CATVR","CATV","CATVX"],"a":0,"e":"CATVP is plenum-rated. CATVR is riser, CATV is general-purpose and CATVX is limited-use.","r":"NEC 820.154(A), 820.179","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":2,"q":"Coaxial cable runs vertically through more than one floor in an open shaft that is not fire-rated. Which is the minimum type?","o":["None, any listed coax is permitted","CATV","CATVR","CATVX"],"a":2,"e":"Riser (CATVR) or plenum (CATVP) cable is needed for vertical runs through floors.","r":"NEC 820.154(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":2,"q":"Which coaxial cable type may be installed in place of Type CATVR?","o":["CATV","CATVG","CATVX","CATVP"],"a":3,"e":"Plenum cable may substitute for riser cable. General-purpose and limited-use cable may not.","r":"NEC 820.154, Table 820.154(b)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":1,"q":"Which coaxial cable type is limited-use cable?","o":["CATVR","CATV","CATVX","CATVP"],"a":2,"e":"CATVX is limited-use coaxial cable.","r":"NEC 820.179, 820.154","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":2,"q":"Coaxial cable may carry power to equipment directly associated with the RF distribution system if the voltage does not exceed:","o":["24 volts","250 volts","120 volts","60 volts, from a current-limited source"],"a":3,"e":"Power over coaxial cable is limited to 60 volts and must come from a power-limited source.","r":"NEC 820.15","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":2,"q":"Coaxial cable is to share a raceway with electric light and power conductors. What is generally required?","o":["The coax must be bonded to the power neutral","A raceway at least 2 in. trade size","A permanent barrier or listed divider separating them","Nothing, if the coax is CATVP"],"a":2,"e":"Coaxial cable may not share a raceway or enclosure with power conductors unless separated by a permanent barrier or listed divider (with limited exceptions).","r":"NEC 820.133(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":2,"q":"Coaxial cable runs on a wall surface parallel to loose power conductors not in a raceway or sheath, with no barrier. What minimum separation is required?","o":["12 mm (1/2 in.)","150 mm (6 in.)","25 mm (1 in.)","50 mm (2 in.)"],"a":3,"e":"Coaxial cables must be separated by at least 50 mm (2 in.) from light and power conductors unless a barrier is installed or the power conductors are enclosed.","r":"NEC 820.133(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size of the grounding conductor for the outer shield of a coaxial cable?","o":["14 AWG","8 AWG","18 AWG","12 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"The conductor must be at least 14 AWG, with ampacity about equal to the cable shield, and need not exceed 6 AWG.","r":"NEC 820.100(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":2,"q":"In a one-family dwelling, the grounding conductor for a coaxial cable shield must be as short as practicable and not exceed:","o":["15 m (50 ft)","9 m (30 ft)","3 m (10 ft)","6 m (20 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"The limit is 6 m (20 ft) for one- and two-family dwellings.","r":"NEC 820.100(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":2,"q":"The grounding conductor for a coaxial cable shield is not required to be larger than:","o":["10 AWG","6 AWG","14 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Its ampacity should be about that of the shield, but it need not exceed 6 AWG.","r":"NEC 820.100(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":1,"q":"The grounding conductor for the shield of a coaxial cable should be:","o":["Any conductor, bare or insulated","Bare aluminum","Insulated copper or another corrosion-resistant conductive material","Uninsulated steel"],"a":2,"e":"It must be insulated and of copper or other suitable corrosion-resistant conductive material.","r":"NEC 820.100(A)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":2,"q":"A coaxial grounding electrode and the power grounding electrode system at a building are separate. What bonding is required?","o":["No bonding","A bonding jumper not smaller than 6 AWG copper or equivalent","A jumper only if the building is metal","A 14 AWG conductor"],"a":1,"e":"The electrodes must be bonded together with a 6 AWG copper jumper or equivalent.","r":"NEC 820.100(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":2,"q":"Where must the outer conductive shield of a coaxial cable entering a building be grounded?","o":["Never, because it carries signal","At the far end only","At the first outlet after entering","As close to the point of entrance as practicable"],"a":3,"e":"Shields are grounded close to where the cable enters the building.","r":"NEC 820.93","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":1,"q":"Coaxial cable is installed in a Class I, Division 2 location. Which requirement applies?","o":["The applicable hazardous location requirements of Chapter 5 apply","Only plenum cable may be used anywhere","Only Article 820 applies","No special requirements apply"],"a":0,"e":"Coaxial systems in classified locations must meet the applicable requirements of Articles 500 through 516.","r":"NEC 820.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":1,"q":"Under Article 820, an installed coaxial cable not terminated at equipment other than a coaxial connector and not identified for future use with a tag is:","o":["A spare","Abandoned","Limited-use cable","Plenum cable"],"a":1,"e":"Unterminated, untagged cable is abandoned.","r":"NEC 820.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":2,"q":"What must be done with the accessible portion of abandoned coaxial cable?","o":["Remove it","Bond it at both ends","Leave it and cap the ends","Leave it if it is CATVR"],"a":0,"e":"The accessible portion of abandoned coaxial cable must be removed.","r":"NEC 820.25","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":1,"q":"Coaxial cable passes through a fire-rated wall. The opening must be:","o":["Firestopped using an approved method","Sleeved with plastic and left unsealed","Left open","Filled with insulation only"],"a":0,"e":"Penetrations of fire-resistance-rated assemblies must be sealed to maintain the rating.","r":"NEC 820.26","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":2,"q":"Coaxial cable is bundled above a suspended ceiling that gives access to electrical equipment. What is required?","o":["Cables should rest on the ceiling panels","Cables must be removed","Accumulated cable must not prevent removal of the access panels","The panels must be glued"],"a":2,"e":"Access to electrical equipment must not be denied by an accumulation of cables.","r":"NEC 820.21","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":2,"q":"Coaxial cable run exposed on a ceiling surface must be:","o":["Supported by structural components and secured so the cable is not damaged","Supported by the exterior of a conduit","Secured with staples driven through the jacket","Laid on the ceiling grid"],"a":0,"e":"Cable must be installed neatly and supported so normal building use will not damage it.","r":"NEC 820.24","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":1,"q":"Which describes a coaxial cable?","o":["Two twisted insulated conductors","A cylindrical assembly of a center conductor inside a metallic tubular insulation shield","A flat unshielded cable with four conductors","A cable of glass fibers"],"a":1,"e":"Coaxial cable has a center conductor surrounded by a tubular shield.","r":"NEC 820.2","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":2,"q":"A building has an intersystem bonding termination installed per 250.94. Where is the coaxial shield grounding conductor connected?","o":["To the intersystem bonding termination","To the neutral at the meter","To a separate driven rod only","To the nearest plumbing fixture"],"a":0,"e":"In buildings with an intersystem bonding termination, the grounding conductor connects to it.","r":"NEC 820.100(B), 250.94","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":1,"q":"How should the grounding conductor for a coaxial cable shield be run to its electrode?","o":["In as straight a line as practicable","Coiled at the entrance","With loops every 10 ft","Through an unbonded ferrous raceway"],"a":0,"e":"The conductor is run in as straight a line as practicable.","r":"NEC 820.100(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":2,"q":"Coaxial cables installed within a building must be:","o":["Approved by the installer","Listed and marked as suitable for the use","Of any type if run in conduit","Listed only above 60 volts"],"a":1,"e":"Coaxial cables in buildings must be listed and marked.","r":"NEC 820.113, 820.179","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":3,"q":"A contractor wants to use Type CATVR coaxial cable in a plenum. Which approach is permitted?","o":["Wrap it in fire-retardant tape","Install it in a metal raceway of a type permitted by 300.22","Install it in PVC conduit","Install it exposed, since riser cable is fire rated"],"a":1,"e":"Non-plenum coax may be used in plenums only when installed in the metal raceways recognized for environmental air spaces.","r":"NEC 820.113, 300.22(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":1,"q":"Article 810 covers which of the following?","o":["Only cable television headend equipment","Carrier-current equipment coupling signals onto power lines","Only commercial broadcast transmitters","Radio and television receiving equipment, and amateur radio transmitting and receiving equipment"],"a":3,"e":"Article 810 covers receiving equipment and amateur transmitting and receiving equipment, but not equipment that couples carrier current to power lines.","r":"NEC 810.1","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is an acceptable material for outdoor antennas and lead-in conductors?","o":["Soft-drawn aluminum","Uncoated mild steel","Hard-drawn copper, bronze, aluminum alloy or copper-clad steel","Lead-covered copper"],"a":2,"e":"Antennas and lead-ins must be of hard-drawn copper, bronze, aluminum alloy, copper-clad steel, or other high-strength corrosion-resistant material.","r":"NEC 810.11","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":2,"q":"Soft-drawn or medium-drawn copper may be used for lead-in conductors only where the maximum span between points of support is less than:","o":["11 m (35 ft)","3 m (10 ft)","30 m (100 ft)","6 m (20 ft)"],"a":0,"e":"Soft or medium-drawn copper is weaker, so it is limited to lead-in spans under 11 m (35 ft).","r":"NEC 810.11","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":2,"q":"Where may an outdoor antenna or lead-in be attached?","o":["To the service mast if on insulators","To the service mast if under 20 ft","It may not be attached to the electric service mast","To the service mast if grounded"],"a":2,"e":"Outdoor antennas and lead-ins must be securely supported and may not be attached to the electric service mast.","r":"NEC 810.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":2,"q":"Antenna supports may not be attached to poles or similar structures carrying open electric light or power wires or trolley wires of more than:","o":["600 volts","250 volts between conductors","50 volts","120 volts"],"a":1,"e":"Antennas and lead-ins may not be attached to poles or structures carrying open conductors over 250 volts between conductors.","r":"NEC 810.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":2,"q":"How must outdoor antennas and lead-ins be installed relative to open electric light or power conductors?","o":["Directly over them for support","So they do not cross over the open conductors and avoid accidental contact","Tied to them with insulators","Wrapped around them for stability"],"a":1,"e":"Antennas and lead-ins must not cross over open light and power conductors and must be kept clear to avoid accidental contact.","r":"NEC 810.13","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":2,"q":"Splices and joints in antenna spans must be:","o":["Mechanically secure and electrically conductive","Taped only","Made with twist-on connectors only","Left slack for movement"],"a":0,"e":"Splices must be mechanically and electrically sound, using approved splicing devices or means that do not weaken the conductors.","r":"NEC 810.14","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":1,"q":"Masts and metal structures that support antennas must be:","o":["Connected to the building roof drain","Bonded to the neutral at the antenna","Grounded in accordance with 810.21","Left ungrounded"],"a":2,"e":"Metal antenna supports are grounded per 810.21.","r":"NEC 810.15","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":2,"q":"Each conductor of a lead-in from an outdoor receiving antenna must be provided with:","o":["A GFCI","A voltage regulator","An antenna discharge unit","A fuse"],"a":2,"e":"An antenna discharge unit is required unless the lead-in is in a grounded continuous metal shield.","r":"NEC 810.20(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":2,"q":"Where must an antenna discharge unit for a receiving station be located?","o":["Anywhere in the attic","At the radio set only","Outside or inside the building, as near as practicable to the point of entrance","At the far end of the cable"],"a":2,"e":"The unit is located near where the lead-in enters so the discharge path is short.","r":"NEC 810.20(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":1,"q":"The antenna discharge unit is grounded in accordance with:","o":["725.121","300.5","250.50","810.21"],"a":3,"e":"Antenna discharge units are grounded in accordance with 810.21.","r":"NEC 810.20(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size of the grounding conductor for a receiving station, if it is copper?","o":["10 AWG","6 AWG","12 AWG","14 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"A receiving station grounding conductor must be at least 10 AWG copper, 8 AWG aluminum, or 17 AWG copper-clad steel or bronze.","r":"NEC 810.21","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":2,"q":"If an aluminum grounding conductor for a receiving station is used outdoors, it may not be installed within what distance of the earth?","o":["450 mm (18 in.)","900 mm (36 in.)","300 mm (12 in.)","150 mm (6 in.)"],"a":0,"e":"Aluminum may not be in direct contact with masonry or earth, and outdoors may not be within 450 mm (18 in.) of the earth.","r":"NEC 810.21","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":1,"q":"Aluminum or copper-clad aluminum grounding conductors for a receiving station may not be used:","o":["With a listed clamp","Where attached to a mast","Indoors","In direct contact with masonry or the earth"],"a":3,"e":"These conductors are not permitted in direct contact with masonry or the earth, or where subject to corrosive conditions.","r":"NEC 810.21","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":2,"q":"Is insulation required on the grounding conductor for a receiving station?","o":["Yes, always insulated","No, it may be bare","Yes, if copper","Yes, if over 30 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Insulation is not required on the grounding conductor of a receiving station.","r":"NEC 810.21","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":1,"q":"Receiving station grounding conductors should be run:","o":["In as straight a line as practicable from the mast and discharge unit to the electrode","Through the attic in a coil","Along the longest path","With a loop every 6 ft"],"a":0,"e":"A short, straight path reduces impedance during lightning surges.","r":"NEC 810.21","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":2,"q":"Separate grounding electrodes serve the radio or television equipment and the power system at a building. What bonding jumper is required between them?","o":["Not smaller than 6 AWG copper or equivalent","10 AWG aluminum","3/0 copper","14 AWG copper"],"a":0,"e":"A 6 AWG copper jumper or equivalent is needed between the two electrode systems.","r":"NEC 810.21","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":3,"q":"A receiving station grounding conductor is run in a metal raceway for protection. What is required?","o":["The raceway must be insulated","Both ends of the raceway must be bonded to the grounding conductor or its terminal or electrode","Only the top end bonded","No bonding is needed"],"a":1,"e":"A grounding conductor in a metal raceway must have both ends of the raceway bonded to it, so the raceway does not act as a choke.","r":"NEC 810.21","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":2,"q":"Antenna conductors for transmitting stations attached to a building must be mounted on nonabsorbent insulating supports and be clear of the building surface by at least:","o":["50 mm (2 in.)","75 mm (3 in.)","150 mm (6 in.)","25 mm (1 in.)"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors must be firmly mounted at least 75 mm (3 in.) clear of the building surface.","r":"NEC 810.54","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":3,"q":"A transmitting station lead-in (not metal shielded) enters a building through an opening provided for the purpose. What clearance must the conductors have from the opening?","o":["100 mm (4 in.)","25 mm (1 in.)","12 mm (1/2 in.)","At least 50 mm (2 in.)"],"a":3,"e":"The entrance conductors are firmly secured with at least 50 mm (2 in.) clearance, or enter through an insulating tube or bushing or a drilled window pane.","r":"NEC 810.55","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":2,"q":"Each lead-in conductor for an outdoor transmitting antenna must be provided with:","o":["A fuse","A voltage regulator","A GFCI","An antenna discharge unit or other suitable means to drain static charges"],"a":3,"e":"Antenna discharge units or other suitable means are needed on transmitting station lead-ins.","r":"NEC 810.57","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":2,"q":"The protective grounding conductor for an amateur transmitting station must be as large as the lead-in but not smaller than:","o":["6 AWG copper","10 AWG copper, bronze or copper-clad steel","12 AWG copper","14 AWG copper"],"a":1,"e":"The protective grounding conductor must be at least as large as the lead-in and not smaller than 10 AWG.","r":"NEC 810.58","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":1,"q":"Radio noise suppressors connected to power supply leads must be:","o":["Unlisted but fused","Mounted outside the enclosure","Rated 600 volts","Of a listed type and not exposed to physical damage"],"a":3,"e":"Radio interference eliminators, interference capacitors and noise suppressors connected to power-supply leads must be listed and protected from physical damage.","r":"NEC 810.5","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum ac voltage for a lighting system under Article 411?","o":["50 volts","12 volts","120 volts","30 volts"],"a":3,"e":"Article 411 lighting systems may not exceed 30 volts ac or 60 volts dc.","r":"NEC 411.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum dc voltage for a lighting system under Article 411?","o":["30 volts","24 volts","60 volts","120 volts"],"a":2,"e":"The limits are 30 volts ac or 60 volts dc.","r":"NEC 411.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":2,"q":"Where wet contact is likely to occur, what is the maximum ac voltage for an Article 411 lighting system?","o":["12 volts","60 volts","30 volts","15 volts"],"a":3,"e":"Where wet contact is likely, the limits drop to 15 volts ac or 30 volts dc.","r":"NEC 411.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":2,"q":"Where wet contact is likely to occur, what is the maximum dc voltage for an Article 411 lighting system?","o":["15 volts","120 volts","30 volts","60 volts"],"a":2,"e":"Wet contact limits are 15 volts ac and 30 volts dc.","r":"NEC 411.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":1,"q":"The output circuits of the isolating power supply in an Article 411 system are rated for not more than:","o":["15 amperes","10 amperes","25 amperes","30 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"Article 411 systems have power supplies with output rated not more than 25 amperes.","r":"NEC 411.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":2,"q":"Under all load conditions, the output of the isolating power supply in an Article 411 ac system may not exceed:","o":["60 volts (84 volts peak)","24 volts (34 volts peak)","50 volts (70 volts peak)","30 volts (42.4 volts peak)"],"a":3,"e":"The ac output limit includes the 42.4 volt peak value.","r":"NEC 411.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":2,"q":"Which statement about the listing of 30-volt-or-less lighting systems is correct?","o":["They must be listed as a complete system or assembled from listed parts","Listing is required only for outdoor use","Only the power supply must be listed","Any unlisted components are acceptable"],"a":0,"e":"Article 411 requires a listed complete system or an assembly of listed parts.","r":"NEC 411.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":2,"q":"The secondary circuit of an Article 411 lighting system:","o":["Shall not be grounded","Must be grounded at the farthest luminaire","Must be grounded at the power supply","Must be bonded to the neutral"],"a":0,"e":"Secondary circuits of these systems are not grounded.","r":"NEC 411.6, 250.22","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":2,"q":"How must the secondary circuit of an Article 411 lighting system be separated from the branch circuit supply?","o":["By an isolating transformer","By a fuse","By a surge protector","By a 30 mA GFCI"],"a":0,"e":"Secondary circuits must be isolated from the branch circuit by an isolating transformer in the power supply.","r":"NEC 411.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":2,"q":"Exposed bare conductors and current-carrying parts of an Article 411 system are permitted:","o":["Outdoors if the voltage is 12 volts","Only on dc systems","For indoor installations only","In wet locations"],"a":2,"e":"Exposed bare conductors are permitted for indoor installations only.","r":"NEC 411.5(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":2,"q":"Bare conductors of an Article 411 system may not be installed less than what height above the finished floor, unless specifically listed for a lower height?","o":["2.5 m (8 ft)","1.5 m (5 ft)","1.8 m (6 ft)","2.1 m (7 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"Bare conductors must be at least 2.1 m (7 ft) above the floor unless listed for a lower installation height.","r":"NEC 411.5(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":3,"q":"A bare-conductor 12-volt track lighting system is installed indoors at 6 ft above the floor. When is this permitted?","o":["If a GFCI protects the primary","Never, the minimum is always 7 ft","If the system is specifically listed for a lower installation height","If the system is outdoor-rated"],"a":2,"e":"The 7 ft requirement can be reduced if the system is specifically listed for a lower installation height.","r":"NEC 411.5(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":2,"q":"The branch circuit supplying the isolating power supply of an Article 411 system may not exceed:","o":["40 amperes","20 amperes","30 amperes","15 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"The supply branch circuit is limited to 20 amperes.","r":"NEC 411.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":2,"q":"A 12-volt isolating power supply has an output rated 25 amperes. What is the maximum load it can serve?","o":["750 volt-amperes","150 volt-amperes","25 volt-amperes","300 volt-amperes"],"a":3,"e":"12 V multiplied by 25 A is 300 VA.","r":"NEC 411.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":3,"q":"A 12-volt, 25-ampere power supply feeds 20-watt lamps at 12 volts. What is the maximum number of lamps on it without exceeding the output rating?","o":["12","20","25","15"],"a":3,"e":"25 A at 12 V is 300 VA, and 300/20 is 15 lamps.","r":"NEC 411.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":3,"q":"Which supply can be used for an Article 411 system where wet contact is likely?","o":["A 24-volt ac supply","A 12-volt ac supply","A 60-volt dc supply","A 40-volt dc supply"],"a":1,"e":"Wet contact limits are 15 V ac and 30 V dc, and only the 12 V ac supply is within them.","r":"NEC 411.3","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":1,"q":"Article 411 covers lighting systems operating at:","o":["50 volts to 150 volts","Over 600 volts","120 volts only","30 volts or less ac, or 60 volts dc or less, and their associated components"],"a":3,"e":"Article 411 applies to lighting systems at 30 V ac or 60 V dc or less.","r":"NEC 411.1, 411.3","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":2,"q":"A low-voltage landscape lighting system (30 volts or less) uses Type UF cable buried under a lawn. Minimum cover per Table 300.5?","o":["150 mm (6 in.)","600 mm (24 in.)","300 mm (12 in.)","450 mm (18 in.)"],"a":0,"e":"Table 300.5 allows 150 mm (6 in.) cover for landscape lighting circuits limited to 30 volts with Type UF or identified cable or raceway.","r":"NEC Table 300.5","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":1,"q":"Which article contains additional requirements for lighting installed in or near swimming pools and fountains?","o":["Article 410","Article 680","Article 725","Article 400"],"a":1,"e":"Informational notes direct readers to Article 680 for pools, fountains, and similar installations.","r":"NEC 411.3 Informational Note, 680.1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":2,"q":"A 12-volt lighting system is installed in a Class I, Division 1 location. Which statement is correct?","o":["Only Article 411 applies","The classified location requirements of Chapter 5 must still be met","The low voltage exempts it from Chapter 5","Only a listed power supply is needed"],"a":1,"e":"Chapter 5 rules supplement and modify general requirements, and low voltage does not exempt equipment from classified-location rules.","r":"NEC 90.3, 500.1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is within the dc limit for an Article 411 lighting system in a dry location?","o":["75 volts dc","48 volts dc","40 volts ac","48 volts ac"],"a":1,"e":"Dry location limits are 30 V ac and 60 V dc.","r":"NEC 411.3","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":2,"q":"A 30-volt isolating power supply has an output rated 25 amperes. What is its maximum volt-ampere output?","o":["1,000 volt-amperes","750 volt-amperes","300 volt-amperes","600 volt-amperes"],"a":1,"e":"30 V multiplied by 25 A is 750 VA.","r":"NEC 411.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":1,"q":"An Article 411 lighting system consists of:","o":["A standard 120-volt luminaire with a dimmer","Any luminaire on a 12-volt battery","An isolating power supply, low-voltage luminaires and associated equipment identified for the use","A cord-and-plug night light"],"a":2,"e":"The system is made up of an isolating power supply, luminaires and associated equipment.","r":"NEC 411.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":3,"q":"A landscape designer uses a 12-volt transformer rated 300 VA with 50-watt lamps. What is the maximum number of lamps?","o":["6","4","5","8"],"a":0,"e":"300 VA / 50 W is 6 lamps.","r":"NEC 411.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less","d":3,"q":"An installer bonds the secondary of a 12-volt landscape lighting transformer to the grounded conductor and a ground rod. What is the result?","o":["Required by Article 250","Correct practice for safety","Permitted if bonded to a rod only","A violation, since secondary circuits of Article 411 systems must not be grounded"],"a":3,"e":"Secondary circuits of Article 411 systems are not grounded.","r":"NEC 411.6, 250.22","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is a type of heating system recognized by Article 426?","o":["Infrared lamp system","Skin-effect heating system","Dielectric heating system","Microwave heating system"],"a":1,"e":"Article 426 covers resistance, impedance and skin-effect heating systems.","r":"NEC 426.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":2,"q":"A fixed outdoor snow-melting cable system draws 20 amperes. What is the minimum ampacity of its branch-circuit conductors, ignoring other adjustments?","o":["20 amperes","30 amperes","25 amperes","22 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"Deicing and snow-melting equipment is a continuous load, so the branch-circuit conductors are sized at 125 percent: 20 x 1.25 = 25 A.","r":"NEC 426.4, 210.19(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":3,"q":"A snow-melting system rated 4,800 watts at 240 volts, single-phase, is on a branch circuit. What is the minimum branch-circuit conductor ampacity?","o":["16 amperes","25 amperes","30 amperes","20 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"4,800 W / 240 V is 20 A. As a continuous load, 20 x 1.25 = 25 A.","r":"NEC 426.4, 210.19(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":1,"q":"External surfaces of deicing or snow-melting equipment operating above what temperature must be physically guarded, isolated, or thermally insulated?","o":["90 C (194 F)","60 C (140 F)","40 C (104 F)","50 C (122 F)"],"a":1,"e":"Surfaces over 60 C (140 F) must be protected against contact by persons.","r":"NEC 426.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":1,"q":"How must the presence of fixed outdoor deicing and snow-melting equipment be made evident?","o":["No marking is required","By painting the area red","With caution signs or markings at visible locations","By a buried warning tape only"],"a":2,"e":"Caution signs or markings must be posted where visible.","r":"NEC 426.13","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":2,"q":"Using electric heating equipment for an application not described in Article 426 requires:","o":["A GFCI only","Nothing, if listed","Written owner approval only","Special permission"],"a":3,"e":"Other uses are allowed only by special permission of the authority having jurisdiction.","r":"NEC 426.14, 90.4","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":2,"q":"Deicing and snow-melting equipment must be identified as suitable for:","o":["Use at 600 volts only","Use above 90 F only","Indoor use only","The chemical, thermal and physical environment, and be installed per manufacturer's drawings"],"a":3,"e":"Equipment must suit its environment and be installed per the manufacturer's instructions.","r":"NEC 426.10","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":2,"q":"How must heating elements be connected to non-heating leads?","o":["With connectors identified for the purpose","With any splice kit","With wire nuts","Twisted and taped"],"a":0,"e":"Only connections identified for joining heating and non-heating elements are used.","r":"NEC 426.24(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":2,"q":"Splices and terminations in deicing and snow-melting equipment installations are made:","o":["In unmarked locations","In a box or fitting that complies with 110.14 and 300.15","Without any enclosure","Directly in the pavement"],"a":1,"e":"Splices and terminations must be in a box or fitting per 110.14 and 300.15.","r":"NEC 426.24(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":2,"q":"Metal raceways and fittings for snow-melting equipment installed in earth or other severely corrosive conditions must be:","o":["Left bare","Painted red","Made of aluminum only","Protected against corrosion"],"a":3,"e":"Corrosion protection is required for raceways and fittings in earth or severe corrosion.","r":"NEC 426.26","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":2,"q":"What protection is required for resistance-type fixed outdoor deicing and snow-melting equipment?","o":["Surge protection","Ground-fault protection of equipment","Arc-fault protection","None"],"a":1,"e":"Ground-fault protection of equipment is required, with a limited exception.","r":"NEC 426.28","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":3,"q":"Which installation is exempt from the GFPE requirement for deicing and snow-melting equipment?","o":["Mineral-insulated, metal-sheathed cable embedded in a noncombustible medium","Cable-and-plug equipment in a driveway","Any cable under 20 A","PVC-sheathed cable embedded in asphalt"],"a":0,"e":"MI cable embedded in a noncombustible medium is the exception.","r":"NEC 426.28","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":2,"q":"An impedance heating system must be supplied through:","o":["A direct connection to the service","An autotransformer","A buck-boost transformer","An isolation transformer with a grounded shield between primary and secondary windings"],"a":3,"e":"Isolation transformers with a grounded shield are required.","r":"NEC 426.31","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":2,"q":"Pull boxes for skin-effect heating systems must be:","o":["Unmarked","Buried under pavement","Made of wood","Accessible without excavation, and watertight if outdoors"],"a":3,"e":"Pull boxes must be accessible without excavation and watertight outdoors.","r":"NEC 426.41","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":2,"q":"The ferromagnetic envelope of a skin-effect system must be:","o":["Bonded to the neutral","Grounded at one end only","Bonded to the equipment grounding conductor at each end","Left ungrounded"],"a":2,"e":"The envelope is bonded at each end.","r":"NEC 426.44","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":1,"q":"The disconnecting means for fixed outdoor deicing and snow-melting equipment must:","o":["Disconnect only the neutral","Be non-indicating","Be a fuse","Simultaneously disconnect all ungrounded conductors, be indicating, and be lockable in the open position"],"a":3,"e":"Disconnects must open all ungrounded conductors simultaneously, be indicating, and be lockable open.","r":"NEC 426.50(A)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":2,"q":"An attachment plug and receptacle may serve as the disconnecting means for deicing equipment if rated:","o":["50 amperes or less and 150 volts","20 amperes or less and 150 volts or less to ground","30 amperes or less and 250 volts","15 amperes or less and 120 volts"],"a":1,"e":"The limits are 20 amperes or less and 150 volts or less to ground.","r":"NEC 426.50(B)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":2,"q":"Cord-and-plug-connected deicing or snow-melting equipment must be:","o":["Rated 600 volts","Listed","Of any type","Unlisted but fused"],"a":1,"e":"Cord-and-plug equipment must be listed.","r":"NEC 426.54","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum watt density of heating elements in embedded snow-melting equipment?","o":["2600 W/m2 (240 W/ft2)","1950 W/m2 (180 W/ft2)","1300 W/m2 (120 W/ft2) of heated area","650 W/m2 (60 W/ft2)"],"a":2,"e":"The limit is 120 W/ft2 of heated area.","r":"NEC 426.20","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum spacing between adjacent runs of embedded heating cable?","o":["100 mm (4 in.) on centers","12 mm (1/2 in.) on centers","50 mm (2 in.) on centers","25 mm (1 in.) on centers"],"a":3,"e":"Adjacent runs are spaced not less than 25 mm (1 in.) on centers.","r":"NEC 426.20","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":1,"q":"Non-heating leads of embedded heating cables must have:","o":["No grounding","Only a neutral","A plastic jacket only","A grounded metal braid, sheath, or other approved means of grounding"],"a":3,"e":"Non-heating leads have a grounded sheath or braid.","r":"NEC 426.22","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":3,"q":"An installer crosses one run of embedded heating cable over another. Is this allowed?","o":["Yes, if taped","Yes, if under 12 V","No, cables must not cross over themselves or each other","Yes, at corners"],"a":2,"e":"Heating cables are not allowed to cross over themselves or each other.","r":"NEC 426.20","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":2,"q":"Cord-and-plug-connected deicing equipment that is identified for the use must comply with:","o":["Article 430","Article 480","Article 422","Article 410"],"a":2,"e":"Such equipment is covered by Article 422.","r":"NEC 426.3","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 426 Fixed Outdoor Electric Deicing and Snow-Melting Equipment","d":1,"q":"Fixed outdoor deicing and snow-melting equipment must be protected from:","o":["Sunlight only","Rainfall only","Vibration only","Physical damage"],"a":3,"e":"Equipment must be protected from physical damage where subject to it.","r":"NEC 426.11","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":1,"q":"Which coaxial cable type is the general-purpose type?","o":["CATVX","CATVP","CATV","CATVR"],"a":2,"e":"CATV is the general-purpose coaxial cable type.","r":"NEC 820.179, 820.154","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment","d":2,"q":"Wiring from the power source to and between devices connected to the interior wiring system of radio and television equipment must comply with:","o":["Chapters 1 through 4","Article 810 only","Article 725 only","Chapter 8 only"],"a":0,"e":"Interior power wiring follows the general Chapters 1 through 4 unless Article 810 modifies it.","r":"NEC 810.3","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":1,"q":"A feeder supplies both continuous and noncontinuous loads. Before the application of adjustment and correction factors, what is the minimum feeder conductor ampacity?","o":["100 percent of the sum of all loads","125 percent of the sum of all loads","125 percent of the continuous load plus 100 percent of the noncontinuous load","100 percent of the continuous load plus 125 percent of the noncontinuous load"],"a":2,"e":"The feeder must carry 125 percent of the continuous load plus 100 percent of the noncontinuous load unless it is part of a 100 percent rated listed assembly.","r":"NEC 215.2(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":2,"q":"A feeder (not a 100 percent rated assembly) supplies a 120 A continuous load and a 60 A noncontinuous load. What is the minimum feeder conductor ampacity before adjustment and correction factors?","o":["210 A","225 A","180 A","195 A"],"a":0,"e":"125 percent of 120 A is 150 A, plus 60 A noncontinuous equals 210 A.","r":"NEC 215.2(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":1,"q":"For the purposes of feeder sizing, a continuous load is one where the maximum current is expected to continue for at least how long?","o":["1 hour","10 minutes","8 hours","3 hours"],"a":3,"e":"Article 100 defines a continuous load as one in which the maximum current is expected to continue for 3 hours or more.","r":"NEC Art. 100; 215.2(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":2,"q":"A feeder with its overcurrent device is a single assembly listed for operation at 100 percent of its rating and supplies a 160 A continuous load with no other load. What is the minimum permitted feeder conductor ampacity?","o":["200 A","175 A","225 A","160 A"],"a":3,"e":"For an assembly including the overcurrent device that is listed for 100 percent operation, the feeder ampacity may equal the sum of the continuous and noncontinuous loads, here 160 A.","r":"NEC 215.2(A)(1) Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":1,"q":"Unless the assembly is listed for 100 percent operation, the rating of the overcurrent device protecting a feeder with continuous loads must be not less than what?","o":["The sum of all loads only","The noncontinuous load plus 125 percent of the continuous load","125 percent of the noncontinuous load plus the continuous load","80 percent of the conductor ampacity"],"a":1,"e":"The OCPD must be at least the noncontinuous load plus 125 percent of the continuous load.","r":"NEC 215.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":2,"q":"A feeder (no 100 percent rated assembly) supplies a 160 A continuous load and a 40 A noncontinuous load. What is the minimum standard rating of the overcurrent device?","o":["250 A","200 A","300 A","225 A"],"a":0,"e":"The required minimum is 125 percent of 160 A plus 40 A = 240 A. Since 240 A is not a standard size and 225 A is too small, the next standard rating, 250 A, is used.","r":"NEC 215.3; 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum number of sets of 3-wire feeders permitted to use a common neutral?","o":["Two","Six","Three","Four"],"a":2,"e":"Up to three sets of 3-wire feeders, or two sets of 4-wire or 5-wire feeders, may use a common neutral.","r":"NEC 215.4(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":1,"q":"How many sets of 4-wire feeders are permitted to utilize a common neutral?","o":["Four","Three","Two","One"],"a":2,"e":"Only two sets of 4-wire or 5-wire feeders may share a common neutral.","r":"NEC 215.4(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":2,"q":"Feeders with a common neutral are installed in metal raceways. What is required?","o":["The common neutral must be one size smaller","All conductors of the feeders using the common neutral must be in the same raceway or enclosure","Each set may be run in a separate raceway as long as the neutral is bonded","The common neutral must be run in a separate nonmetallic raceway"],"a":1,"e":"When installed in metal raceway or enclosure, all conductors of feeders with a common neutral must be enclosed in the same raceway to avoid inductive heating.","r":"NEC 215.4(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":1,"q":"Where a feeder supplies branch circuits that require equipment grounding conductors, what must the feeder provide?","o":["An equipment grounding conductor as described in 250.118, run with the feeder","Only a grounded conductor","Nothing, since the branch circuits carry their own","A grounding electrode conductor to each panel"],"a":0,"e":"Feeders supplying branch circuits that require equipment grounding conductors must include or provide an EGC per 250.118, sized per 250.122.","r":"NEC 215.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":2,"q":"A 100 A feeder with copper conductors is protected by a 100 A overcurrent device. What is the minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor?","o":["4 AWG","8 AWG","10 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Table 250.122 requires 8 AWG copper for a 100 A overcurrent device.","r":"NEC 215.6; 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":3,"q":"A 200 A feeder has 3/0 AWG copper ungrounded conductors. The neutral carries only a calculated 20 A unbalanced load. Using only the feeder rules for a copper neutral, what is the minimum neutral size?","o":["8 AWG","6 AWG","12 AWG","10 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"The feeder grounded conductor may not be smaller than the equipment grounding conductor required by Table 250.122, which is 6 AWG copper for a 200 A device, even though 20 A of load alone would need less.","r":"NEC 215.2(A)(2); 250.122","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":1,"q":"In place of branch-circuit GFCI protection, which of the following is permitted for 15 and 20 A receptacle branch circuits?","o":["Feeder protected by a listed ground-fault circuit interrupter","A feeder with ground-fault protection of equipment","Surge protective device on the feeder","A feeder with an AFCI"],"a":0,"e":"Feeders supplying 15 and 20 A receptacle branch circuits may be protected by a listed feeder GFCI in lieu of the branch-circuit GFCI requirements.","r":"NEC 215.9","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":1,"q":"Ground-fault protection of equipment is required for a feeder disconnect rated at or above what amperage (on qualifying systems)?","o":["1000 A","2000 A","800 A","400 A"],"a":0,"e":"Each feeder disconnect rated 1000 A or more on a solidly grounded wye system over 150 V to ground but not over 1000 V phase-to-phase requires GFPE.","r":"NEC 215.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":2,"q":"Which feeder disconnect requires ground-fault protection of equipment under the feeder rules (no exceptions apply)?","o":["1200 A on a 480Y/277 V solidly grounded wye system","1200 A on a 208Y/120 V system","1200 A on a 480 V ungrounded delta system","800 A on a 480Y/277 V solidly grounded wye system"],"a":0,"e":"GFPE applies to solidly grounded wye systems with more than 150 V to ground and not over 1000 V phase-to-phase, with disconnects of 1000 A or more. 208Y/120 V is only 120 V to ground, delta is not wye, and 800 A is under 1000 A.","r":"NEC 215.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":2,"q":"A building has feeders supplied from more than one nominal voltage system. How must ungrounded feeder conductors be identified?","o":["By phase or line and by system at all termination, connection, and splice points","Only on the panel directory","No identification is required if sized differently","Only by color at the service"],"a":0,"e":"Each ungrounded conductor must be identified by phase or line and system at all terminations, connections, and splice points.","r":"NEC 215.12(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":1,"q":"An insulated equipment grounding conductor of 6 AWG or smaller in a feeder must be identified by which color?","o":["Continuous orange","Continuous green or green with one or more yellow stripes","Continuous gray","Continuous white"],"a":1,"e":"Insulated EGCs 6 AWG or smaller must be continuous green or green with yellow stripes; white and gray are reserved for grounded conductors.","r":"NEC 215.12(B); 250.119","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":2,"q":"Which method may identify a 250 kcmil feeder grounded conductor?","o":["Continuous green insulation","No identification is needed for conductors over 6 AWG","Red tape at each termination","A distinctive white marking at each termination, applied at the time of installation"],"a":3,"e":"Grounded conductors larger than 6 AWG may be identified with a white or gray finish, three white stripes, or a distinctive white marking at terminations.","r":"NEC 215.12(A); 200.6(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":2,"q":"A feeder carries the total load supplied by service conductors that have an ampacity of 55 A or less. What is the minimum feeder conductor ampacity?","o":["It must be at least equal to the ampacity of the service conductors","Any size that carries the load","80 percent of the service conductors","125 percent of the service conductors"],"a":0,"e":"Where the feeder carries the total load of service conductors with ampacity of 55 A or less, the feeder ampacity must not be less than that of the service conductors.","r":"NEC 215.2(A)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":2,"q":"For a single-phase 120/240 V dwelling unit feeder, 310.12 allows feeder conductors to have an ampacity not less than what percentage of the feeder rating?","o":["100 percent","83 percent","90 percent","75 percent"],"a":1,"e":"Dwelling unit feeders at 120/240 V single phase, 100 through 400 A, may be sized at 83 percent of the feeder rating under 310.12.","r":"NEC 215.2(A)(4); 310.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":1,"q":"What maximum voltage drop on feeders is recommended (informational note) to provide reasonable efficiency?","o":["3 percent","2 percent","5 percent","1 percent"],"a":0,"e":"A voltage drop of not more than 3 percent at the farthest outlet for feeders is recommended.","r":"NEC 215.2 Informational Note","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":1,"q":"What maximum total voltage drop on feeders plus branch circuits to the farthest outlet is recommended (informational note)?","o":["3 percent","10 percent","2 percent","5 percent"],"a":3,"e":"The combined feeder and branch-circuit voltage drop should not exceed 5 percent.","r":"NEC 215.2 Informational Note","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":3,"q":"A feeder is designed with a 4 percent voltage drop and a branch circuit with a 2 percent drop to the farthest outlet. Based on NEC informational recommendations, which statement is correct?","o":["The feeder exceeds 3 percent and the total exceeds 5 percent, so recommendations are not met","Only the total is a problem","Everything is within recommendations","The NEC mandates 3 percent as a hard limit and this is a violation, but 5 percent total is met"],"a":0,"e":"The informational note recommends 3 percent max on the feeder and 5 percent total; 4 percent exceeds feeder, and 6 percent exceeds total. These are recommendations, not mandatory rules.","r":"NEC 215.2 Informational Note","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":2,"q":"A two-wire ac circuit is tapped from the ungrounded conductors of a 3-phase, 4-wire grounded-neutral circuit. What must a switching device in the tapped circuit have?","o":["No poles, since it is a tap","A pole in the neutral only","A pole in the grounded conductor and one ungrounded","A pole in each ungrounded conductor"],"a":3,"e":"Switching devices in each tapped circuit must have a pole in each ungrounded conductor.","r":"NEC 215.7","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":1,"q":"What is a feeder, as covered by Article 215?","o":["Conductors only within a panelboard","Conductors from the utility transformer to the meter","Circuit conductors between the service equipment (or other source) and the final branch-circuit overcurrent device","Conductors between the final overcurrent device and the outlet"],"a":2,"e":"Feeders are all circuit conductors between the service equipment, source of a separately derived system, or other power supply and the final branch-circuit OCPD.","r":"NEC Art. 100; 215.1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":1,"q":"For load calculation purposes, which value is used as the nominal voltage of the line-to-neutral portion of a 480Y/277 V system?","o":["277 V","270 V","265 V","240 V"],"a":0,"e":"Section 220.5(A) lists the nominal voltages for calculations, including 480Y/277.","r":"NEC 220.5(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":1,"q":"When rounding calculations, how are decimal fractions of an ampere treated?","o":["All fractions are dropped","Fractions of 0.25 A or more are rounded up","All fractions are always rounded up","Fractions smaller than 0.5 A are dropped; 0.5 A or larger is rounded up"],"a":3,"e":"Calculations may be rounded to the nearest whole ampere, dropping fractions smaller than 0.5.","r":"NEC 220.5(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":1,"q":"What general lighting unit load in volt-amperes per square foot is used for a dwelling unit?","o":["2 VA","3.5 VA","1 VA","3 VA"],"a":3,"e":"Table 220.12 gives 3 VA/ft2 for dwelling units.","r":"NEC 220.12, Table 220.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":1,"q":"What general lighting unit load per square foot does Table 220.12 give for a warehouse (storage)?","o":["2 VA","0.25 VA","3 VA","1 VA"],"a":1,"e":"Warehouses (storage) are 0.25 VA/ft2 in Table 220.12.","r":"NEC Table 220.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":1,"q":"What general lighting unit load per square foot does Table 220.12 give for a hospital?","o":["3 VA","1 VA","3.5 VA","2 VA"],"a":3,"e":"Hospitals are calculated at 2 VA/ft2.","r":"NEC Table 220.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"In a one-family dwelling, how are the general-use 15 and 20 A receptacle outlets required by 210.52 treated in the load calculation?","o":["Added at 1500 VA each","Added at 180 VA each","Included in the general lighting load and not added separately","Added at 600 VA per room"],"a":2,"e":"The general-use receptacles and lighting outlets of dwellings are covered by the unit load of 220.12 and are not further added.","r":"NEC 220.14(J)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"A retail store (not a bank or office) has 10 duplex receptacles, each on its own yoke. What load is added for these receptacles in a feeder calculation, before demand factors?","o":["3,600 VA","1,800 VA","1,080 VA","1,500 VA"],"a":1,"e":"Non-dwelling receptacle outlets are counted at 180 VA per single or multiple receptacle on one yoke: 10 x 180 = 1,800 VA.","r":"NEC 220.14(I)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"A 20,000 ft2 office building has 50 receptacles, each on a separate yoke. For the receptacle load, which value applies?","o":["30,000 VA","20,000 VA","9,000 VA","10,000 VA"],"a":1,"e":"For banks and office buildings the load is the larger of 180 VA per yoke (9,000 VA) or 1 VA/ft2 (20,000 VA), so 20,000 VA.","r":"NEC 220.14(K)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"A 15 ft fixed multioutlet assembly in a store is used where appliances are not likely to be used simultaneously. What load is calculated?","o":["540 VA","1,500 VA","2,700 VA","180 VA"],"a":0,"e":"Each 5 ft or fraction counts as 180 VA when not used simultaneously: 15 ft = 3 x 180 = 540 VA.","r":"NEC 220.14(H)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"A 10 ft fixed multioutlet assembly is in a workshop where multiple appliances are likely to be used simultaneously. What load is calculated?","o":["540 VA","360 VA","1,800 VA","3,600 VA"],"a":2,"e":"Where used simultaneously, each 1 ft or fraction counts as 180 VA: 10 x 180 = 1,800 VA.","r":"NEC 220.14(H)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum load that must be used for each required sign or outline lighting branch circuit?","o":["600 VA","2,400 VA","1,200 VA","1,500 VA"],"a":2,"e":"Sign and outline lighting outlets are calculated at not less than 1,200 VA for each required branch circuit.","r":"NEC 220.14(F); 600.5(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":1,"q":"For a specific appliance or load not covered elsewhere in 220.14, what is used in the calculation?","o":["1,500 VA per appliance","The ampere rating of the appliance or load","180 VA per appliance","3 VA per square foot"],"a":1,"e":"The ampere rating of the appliance or load is used for specific appliances or loads not otherwise covered.","r":"NEC 220.14(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":1,"q":"What minimum load is used for show windows in a store?","o":["200 VA per linear foot","100 VA per linear foot","50 VA per linear foot","300 VA per linear foot"],"a":0,"e":"Show windows are calculated at not less than 200 VA per linear foot of show window, measured horizontally along the base.","r":"NEC 220.43(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"Nondwelling track lighting totals 12 ft of track. What additional load is calculated for it?","o":["600 VA","1,200 VA","2,400 VA","900 VA"],"a":3,"e":"150 VA is added for every 2 ft (or fraction) of track: 6 x 150 = 900 VA.","r":"NEC 220.43(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":1,"q":"For a dwelling unit, how many small-appliance branch circuits are included in the load calculation, and at what rating each?","o":["One at 1,500 VA","Two at 3,000 VA each","Two or more at 1,500 VA each","Three at 1,200 VA each"],"a":2,"e":"Two or more 20 A small-appliance circuits are each calculated at 1,500 VA.","r":"NEC 220.52(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":1,"q":"What load is used for the dwelling unit laundry branch circuit?","o":["1,500 VA","1,000 VA","2,000 VA","3,000 VA"],"a":0,"e":"At least one laundry circuit is included at 1,500 VA.","r":"NEC 220.52(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"A dwelling has five fastened-in-place appliances (a dishwasher, disposal, water heater, compactor and attic fan, none a range, dryer, heating or AC). What demand factor may be applied to the nameplate loads?","o":["75 percent","65 percent","100 percent","50 percent"],"a":0,"e":"A 75 percent demand factor applies where four or more appliances fastened in place are on the same feeder or service.","r":"NEC 220.53","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"A household electric clothes dryer has a nameplate rating of 4,500 W. What minimum load is used for the dryer in the calculation?","o":["4,500 W","5,500 W","6,000 W","5,000 W"],"a":3,"e":"The load is 5,000 W or the nameplate rating, whichever is larger.","r":"NEC 220.54","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"What demand factor applies to four household electric dryers on the same feeder?","o":["85 percent","75 percent","100 percent","50 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Table 220.54 gives 100 percent for one through four dryers.","r":"NEC Table 220.54","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"What is the demand in kW for one 12 kW household electric range in a feeder calculation?","o":["12 kW","9.6 kW","10 kW","8 kW"],"a":3,"e":"Table 220.55, Column C gives 8 kW for one range rated up to 12 kW.","r":"NEC Table 220.55","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"What is the demand for one 14 kW household range (Column C base of 8 kW)?","o":["8.0 kW","8.8 kW","9.6 kW","14 kW"],"a":1,"e":"Column C is increased 5 percent for each kW over 12: 2 kW x 5 percent = 10 percent, 8 kW x 1.10 = 8.8 kW.","r":"NEC Table 220.55, Note 1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":1,"q":"At what demand factor are fixed electric space-heating loads calculated?","o":["65 percent","75 percent","100 percent of the total connected load","50 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Feeder and service loads for fixed electric space heating are 100 percent of the connected load.","r":"NEC 220.51","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"A building has 20 kW of electric heating and 12 kW of air conditioning, interlocked so they cannot operate simultaneously. What load is used from these two?","o":["32 kW","16 kW","20 kW","12 kW"],"a":2,"e":"Noncoincident loads: only the largest of the two is included.","r":"NEC 220.60","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"A 3-phase, 4-wire feeder (with linear loads only) has a maximum unbalanced neutral load of 300 A. What neutral load is used?","o":["300 A","240 A","210 A","270 A"],"a":3,"e":"The first 200 A is at 100 percent; 70 percent of the portion over 200 A: 200 + 0.7(100) = 270 A.","r":"NEC 220.61(C)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"Which neutral load receives no 70 percent reduction on the portion over 200 A?","o":["Portion consisting of electric discharge lighting on a single-phase system","Portion consisting of nonlinear loads supplied from a 4-wire wye system","Portion consisting of linear loads","Portion that is from a 2-wire circuit"],"a":1,"e":"No neutral reduction is allowed for the nonlinear-load portion supplied from a 4-wire, 3-phase wye system.","r":"NEC 220.61(C)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"A dwelling has 13,000 VA of general lighting load (including small-appliance and laundry loads). What demand-factored load results from Table 220.42?","o":["6,500 VA","9,100 VA","13,000 VA","4,550 VA"],"a":0,"e":"First 3,000 VA at 100 percent plus the remaining 10,000 VA at 35 percent: 3,000 + 3,500 = 6,500 VA.","r":"NEC Table 220.42","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"A hospital has 60,000 VA of general lighting load. What demand-factored load results from Table 220.42?","o":["24,000 VA","60,000 VA","22,000 VA","30,000 VA"],"a":2,"e":"First 50,000 VA at 40 percent = 20,000 plus remaining 10,000 VA at 20 percent = 2,000, total 22,000 VA.","r":"NEC Table 220.42","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"A warehouse has 20,000 VA of general lighting load. What demand-factored load results from Table 220.42?","o":["10,000 VA","20,000 VA","12,500 VA","16,250 VA"],"a":3,"e":"First 12,500 VA at 100 percent plus remaining 7,500 VA at 50 percent: 12,500 + 3,750 = 16,250 VA.","r":"NEC Table 220.42","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"A commercial building has 30 kVA of receptacle load (other than dwelling, 220.14(I) loads). What demand-factored load is used under Table 220.44?","o":["15 kVA","20 kVA","25 kVA","30 kVA"],"a":1,"e":"First 10 kVA at 100 percent plus remainder at 50 percent: 10 + 10 = 20 kVA.","r":"NEC Table 220.44","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"A commercial kitchen has five pieces of thermostatically controlled electric cooking equipment on one feeder. What demand factor applies per Table 220.56?","o":["90 percent","65 percent","80 percent","70 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Table 220.56 gives 70 percent for five units (100 percent for 1-2, 90 for 3, 80 for 4, 65 for 6 or more); the demand cannot be less than the two largest units.","r":"NEC Table 220.56","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"What load is used in a calculation for each electric vehicle supply equipment (EVSE) outlet?","o":["11,500 W per outlet","Nameplate rating at 80 percent","7,200 W or the nameplate rating, whichever is larger","3,300 W per outlet"],"a":2,"e":"The EVSE load is the greater of 7,200 W or the nameplate rating.","r":"NEC 220.57","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Using the dwelling optional calculation, the general loads (lighting, small-appliance, laundry, appliances, etc.) total 30 kVA. What is the demand-factored general load?","o":["22 kVA","18 kVA","12 kVA","30 kVA"],"a":1,"e":"First 10 kVA at 100 percent plus the remainder at 40 percent: 10 + 8 = 18 kVA.","r":"NEC 220.82(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"For an existing dwelling unit where no additional air conditioning or heating is being installed, the loads total 20 kVA. What is the demand-factored load using the existing-dwelling method?","o":["8 kVA","12.8 kVA","20 kVA","14 kVA"],"a":1,"e":"First 8 kVA at 100 percent plus the remainder at 40 percent: 8 + 4.8 = 12.8 kVA.","r":"NEC 220.83(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"The optional method for multifamily dwellings applies to buildings with at least how many dwelling units?","o":["Five","Ten","Three","Two"],"a":2,"e":"The optional calculation for multifamily dwellings is for three or more dwelling units.","r":"NEC 220.84(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 220 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"An existing feeder load is determined from one year of maximum demand data. What factor is applied to that demand before adding the new load?","o":["125 percent","150 percent","100 percent","110 percent"],"a":0,"e":"The load is taken as 125 percent of the maximum demand plus the new load.","r":"NEC 220.87","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size of open individual overhead copper conductors (1000 V or less) for a span of up to 50 ft?","o":["12 AWG","14 AWG","10 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"For 1000 V or less, open individual conductors must be at least 10 AWG copper or 8 AWG aluminum for spans up to 50 ft.","r":"NEC 225.6(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"Open individual overhead copper conductors (1000 V or less, no messenger) span 75 ft between supports. What is the minimum size?","o":["6 AWG","10 AWG","12 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Spans longer than 50 ft require at least 8 AWG copper (6 AWG aluminum) unless supported by a messenger wire.","r":"NEC 225.6(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size of overhead festoon lighting conductors unless supported by a messenger wire?","o":["14 AWG","10 AWG","12 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Overhead conductors for festoon lighting may not be smaller than 12 AWG unless supported by a messenger wire.","r":"NEC 225.6(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"Open individual overhead conductors of 1000 V or less pass within 10 ft of a building (not supporting poles). What covering is required, other than as permitted for grounded and equipment grounding conductors elsewhere in the Code?","o":["They must be in a metal raceway","They may be bare","They must be insulated or covered","They must be 4 AWG or larger"],"a":2,"e":"Open individual overhead conductors within 10 ft of a building or structure must be insulated or covered.","r":"NEC 225.4","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"120/240 V conductors cabled together with a grounded bare messenger run over a sidewalk accessible to pedestrians only. What is the minimum clearance from final grade?","o":["8 ft","10 ft","15 ft","12 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Clearance of 10 ft applies over areas or sidewalks accessible only to pedestrians for conductors supported on and cabled with a grounded bare messenger where voltage does not exceed 150 V to ground.","r":"NEC 225.18(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"Overhead 120/240 V conductors cross a residential driveway. What minimum clearance above the driveway is required?","o":["15 ft","12 ft","10 ft","18 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Over residential property and driveways, and commercial areas not subject to truck traffic, 12 ft applies where voltage does not exceed 300 V to ground.","r":"NEC 225.18(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":1,"q":"What minimum clearance applies for overhead conductors of 1000 V or less over public streets, alleys and roads?","o":["12 ft","15 ft","20 ft","18 ft"],"a":3,"e":"Over public streets, alleys, roads, parking areas subject to truck traffic, and non-residential driveways, 18 ft is required.","r":"NEC 225.18(4)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":3,"q":"600Y/347 V overhead conductors cross a residential driveway. What minimum clearance is required?","o":["12 ft","15 ft","18 ft","10 ft"],"a":1,"e":"With more than 300 V to ground (347 V), the areas that otherwise permit 12 ft require 15 ft.","r":"NEC 225.18(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":3,"q":"480Y/277 V overhead feeders cross a commercial parking lot subject to truck traffic. What minimum clearance is required?","o":["12 ft","15 ft","18 ft","20 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Areas subject to truck traffic such as non-residential driveways and parking areas require 18 ft regardless of the 277 V to ground.","r":"NEC 225.18(4)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":1,"q":"What is the general minimum vertical clearance of overhead conductors (1000 V or less) above a roof surface?","o":["10 ft","8 ft","3 ft","12 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Overhead conductors must have a vertical clearance of not less than 8 ft above the roof surface, unless an exception applies.","r":"NEC 225.19(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"The voltage between overhead conductors is 240 V and the roof has a slope of 4 in 12. What reduced clearance above the roof is permitted?","o":["3 ft","8 ft","5 ft","18 in."],"a":0,"e":"Where the voltage between conductors does not exceed 300 V and the roof slope is 4 in 12 or more, the clearance may be reduced to 3 ft.","r":"NEC 225.19(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":3,"q":"Conductors of 240 V pass over no more than 4 ft of a roof overhang and terminate at a through-the-roof raceway. What is the minimum clearance above the overhanging portion only?","o":["3 ft","12 in.","18 in.","8 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Over the overhanging portion only, clearance may be reduced to 18 in. if conductors do not pass over more than 4 ft of overhang and terminate at a through-the-roof raceway or approved support.","r":"NEC 225.19(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"What minimum clearance is required (vertical, diagonal and horizontal) between overhead conductors and signs, chimneys, antennas and tanks?","o":["3 ft","5 ft","1 ft","8 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Clearances from signs, chimneys, radio and TV antennas, tanks and other nonbuilding structures are not less than 3 ft.","r":"NEC 225.19(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"Final spans of overhead conductors attach to a building. What clearance is required from windows designed to be opened, doors, porches and fire escapes?","o":["8 ft","6 ft","1 ft","3 ft"],"a":3,"e":"Final spans must clear windows that open, doors, porches, balconies, ladders, stairs and fire escapes by at least 3 ft.","r":"NEC 225.19(D)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":1,"q":"Raceways installed on the exterior surface of a building must be:","o":["Sealed with sealant at every fitting","Raintight and arranged to drain","Installed at least 8 ft above grade","Nonmetallic only"],"a":1,"e":"Raceways on exterior surfaces must be raintight and arranged to drain.","r":"NEC 225.22","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"Outdoor lampholders are attached as pendants. How must the connections to the circuit wires be made?","o":["Connections must be staggered","Connections must be soldered only","Wires must be twisted together","Lampholders must be bonded"],"a":0,"e":"Where outdoor lampholders are attached as pendants, the connections to the circuit wires must be staggered. Lampholders with puncturing terminals also require specific provisions.","r":"NEC 225.24","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"Where must lamps for outdoor lighting be located in relation to energized conductors, unless clearance or other safeguards are provided for relamping?","o":["Above all energized conductors","At least 10 ft from poles","Below all energized conductors, transformers and other utilization equipment","At the same level as energized conductors"],"a":2,"e":"Locations of outdoor lamps must be below all energized conductors, transformers or other utilization equipment unless safeguards are provided for relamping.","r":"NEC 225.25","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"May a tree be used to support overhead conductor spans for a permanent installation?","o":["Yes, up to 600 V","No, vegetation such as trees may not be used for support of overhead conductor spans","Yes, if the tree is within 10 ft of the building","Yes, if the conductors are insulated"],"a":1,"e":"Vegetation such as trees is not permitted for support of overhead conductor spans, except temporary wiring covered in Article 590.","r":"NEC 225.26","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"A raceway enters a building from an underground distribution system. What must be done at the raceway?","o":["Install an expansion fitting","Nothing for conduit under 2 in.","Seal it in accordance with 300.5(G), including spare or unused raceways","Bond the raceway to the water pipe"],"a":2,"e":"Raceways entering from underground distribution systems must be sealed per 300.5(G), and spare or unused raceways must also be sealed.","r":"NEC 225.27","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":1,"q":"In general, how many supplies (feeders or branch circuits) may serve a building or structure?","o":["Two","One","As many as needed","Three"],"a":1,"e":"A building or structure is generally served by only one feeder or branch circuit; 225.30 lists exceptions.","r":"NEC 225.30","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"Which is a permitted reason for an additional supply to a building under 225.30?","o":["Reduced conductor size","A fire pump","Long conductor runs","Convenience of the contractor"],"a":1,"e":"Special conditions such as fire pumps, emergency systems, legally required standby systems and optional standby systems allow additional supplies.","r":"NEC 225.30","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":1,"q":"A building disconnecting means for a feeder must disconnect which conductors?","o":["Only the ungrounded conductors serving lighting","Only the equipment grounding conductor","Only the grounded conductor","All ungrounded conductors that supply or pass through the building"],"a":3,"e":"A means must be provided for disconnecting all ungrounded conductors that supply or pass through the building or structure.","r":"NEC 225.31","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"Where must the building or structure feeder disconnecting means be located?","o":["At the far end of the building","Only inside a bathroom","Only outdoors","At a readily accessible location nearest the point of entrance of the supply conductors, inside or outside"],"a":3,"e":"The disconnecting means is installed inside or outside at a readily accessible location nearest the point of entrance of the conductors.","r":"NEC 225.32","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum number of switches or circuit breakers permitted to serve as the disconnecting means for each supply to a building?","o":["Eight","Six","Two","Four"],"a":1,"e":"The disconnecting means for each supply consists of not more than six switches or six circuit breakers grouped in one location.","r":"NEC 225.33(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"In a multiple-occupancy building, who must have access to the supply disconnecting means (outside of building management exceptions)?","o":["Only a licensed electrician","Only the utility","Only the building owner","Each occupant must have access to their own supply disconnecting means"],"a":3,"e":"In a multiple-occupancy building each occupant must have access to the occupant's supply disconnecting means, with a limited exception for continuous building management supervision.","r":"NEC 225.35","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"For a detached garage on residential property, which device may serve as the building disconnecting means?","o":["A lampholder","Only a service-listed enclosed switch","A snap switch or set of three-way or four-way snap switches","Only a circuit breaker rated 100 A"],"a":2,"e":"Garages and outbuildings on residential property may use a snap switch, or a set of 3-way or 4-way snap switches, as the disconnecting means. Other buildings need equipment suitable for use as service equipment.","r":"NEC 225.36 Exception","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"A building is supplied by more than one feeder or branch circuit. What identification is required?","o":["A tag on each conductor","A permanent plaque or directory at each disconnect location denoting all other supplies and the areas served","A label inside the panelboard door only","None"],"a":1,"e":"A permanent plaque or directory must be installed at each feeder and branch-circuit disconnect location denoting all other feeders/circuits supplying the building and the area served by each.","r":"NEC 225.37","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"A building disconnecting means of multiple poles must be designed so that:","o":["Only one ungrounded conductor opens","Each pole is operated separately","All ungrounded conductors open with a single operation of the hand or simultaneous operation","The grounded conductor opens first"],"a":2,"e":"All ungrounded conductors must open simultaneously with a single operation.","r":"NEC 225.38(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum rating of the feeder disconnecting means for a building supplied by only one branch circuit?","o":["15 A","20 A","30 A","60 A"],"a":0,"e":"A one-circuit installation requires a disconnecting means rated at not less than 15 A.","r":"NEC 225.39(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum rating of the disconnecting means for a one-family dwelling feeder, 3-wire?","o":["200 A","30 A","60 A","100 A"],"a":3,"e":"For a one-family dwelling the feeder disconnecting means must be rated at least 100 A, 3-wire.","r":"NEC 225.39(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum rating of the building disconnecting means for installations other than those with one circuit, two circuits or a one-family dwelling?","o":["30 A","200 A","100 A","60 A"],"a":3,"e":"All other installations require a disconnecting means of not less than 60 A.","r":"NEC 225.39(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"In general, how many services may supply a building or other structure?","o":["Three","One per floor","One","Two"],"a":2,"e":"A building or structure is supplied by only one service unless one of the listed exceptions applies.","r":"NEC 230.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"A building requires 2,500 A at 480 V. Under what condition does the NEC permit an additional service?","o":["Never, regardless of capacity","Only if the utility requests it","Where capacity requirements exceed 2000 A at 1000 V or less","Only if the load is over 5000 A"],"a":2,"e":"Additional services are permitted where capacity requirements are in excess of 2000 A at a supply voltage of 1000 V or less.","r":"NEC 230.2(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"A fire pump in a building requires its own supply. How does 230.2 treat it?","o":["It must be supplied from the main service","It is supplied only from a feeder tap inside the building","A separate service is prohibited","A separate service is permitted for the fire pump"],"a":3,"e":"Fire pumps are among the special conditions where an additional service is permitted.","r":"NEC 230.2(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"A building requires a 120/208 V supply for general loads and a separate 480 V supply for a large process. What does 230.2 allow?","o":["A service only if the utility agrees to give three","Only one service of one voltage","Separate services only for dwellings","Separate services for different voltages or classes of use"],"a":3,"e":"Additional services are permitted for different voltages, frequencies, phases or classes of use.","r":"NEC 230.2(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"May service conductors supplying one building pass through the interior of another building?","o":["Yes, if in conduit","No, service conductors supplying a building must not pass through the interior of another building","Yes, if under 100 A","Yes, if the buildings share an owner"],"a":1,"e":"Service conductors supplying a building or structure shall not pass through the interior of another building or structure.","r":"NEC 230.3","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Service conductors installed under not less than what depth of earth beneath a building are considered outside the building?","o":["18 in.","12 in.","6 in.","24 in."],"a":0,"e":"Conductors installed under not less than 18 in. of earth beneath a building, or in a raceway encased in at least 2 in. of concrete or brick, are considered outside.","r":"NEC 230.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is permitted in a service raceway along with the service conductors?","o":["Feeder conductors for a subpanel","Fire alarm circuit conductors","Branch-circuit conductors for a nearby receptacle","Grounding electrode conductors and bonding jumpers"],"a":3,"e":"Only service conductors may be in a service raceway, with exceptions such as grounding and bonding conductors.","r":"NEC 230.7","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"A service raceway enters a building from an underground distribution system. What is required of the raceway?","o":["It must be sealed only if over 2 in. trade size","It must be sealed per 300.5(G), including spare or unused raceways","Nothing is required","Only metal raceways are sealed"],"a":1,"e":"Service raceways entering from underground must be sealed, and spare or unused raceways must also be sealed.","r":"NEC 230.8","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"What minimum clearance must be maintained between service conductors and windows designed to be opened, doors, porches and fire escapes?","o":["8 ft","3 ft","5 ft","1 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Service conductors installed as open conductors or multiconductor cable without an overall outer jacket must have a clearance of not less than 3 ft from these locations.","r":"NEC 230.9(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Where must overhead service conductors not be installed with respect to building openings?","o":["Beside a masonry wall","Above the roof","Beneath openings through which materials may be moved, such as in farm and commercial buildings","Over a window with a fixed sash"],"a":2,"e":"Overhead service conductors may not be installed beneath openings through which materials may be moved, or where they obstruct such openings.","r":"NEC 230.9(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"May vegetation, such as a tree, be used to support overhead service conductors?","o":["Yes, if insulated","Yes, if under 150 V","No, vegetation must not be used for support of overhead service conductors","Yes, if the tree is dead"],"a":2,"e":"Vegetation such as trees may not be used for support of overhead service conductors.","r":"NEC 230.10","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Individual overhead service conductors must be:","o":["Insulated or covered","Aluminum only","In a nonmetallic raceway","Bare for ease of cooling"],"a":0,"e":"Individual conductors must be insulated or covered (the grounded conductor of a multiconductor cable may be bare).","r":"NEC 230.22","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size of overhead service-drop conductors (copper), in general?","o":["6 AWG","10 AWG","12 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Conductors must not be smaller than 8 AWG copper or 6 AWG aluminum or copper-clad aluminum.","r":"NEC 230.23(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Overhead service conductors supply only a limited load of a single branch circuit, such as controlled water heating. What is the smallest permitted?","o":["10 AWG copper","14 AWG copper","12 AWG hard-drawn copper or equivalent","8 AWG copper"],"a":2,"e":"For installations supplying only limited loads of a single branch circuit, the conductors must not be smaller than 12 AWG hard-drawn copper or equivalent.","r":"NEC 230.23(B) Exception","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"What is the general vertical clearance of overhead service conductors above a roof surface?","o":["8 ft","3 ft","12 ft","6 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Conductors must have a vertical clearance of not less than 8 ft above the roof surface, over the roof area and 3 ft beyond.","r":"NEC 230.24(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Overhead service conductors of 240 V pass over a roof with a 5 in 12 slope. What reduced clearance above the roof surface is permitted?","o":["8 ft","18 in.","5 ft","3 ft"],"a":3,"e":"Where voltage between conductors does not exceed 300 V and the roof slope is 4 in 12 or more, clearance may be reduced to 3 ft.","r":"NEC 230.24(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Overhead 120/240 V service conductors cross a residential driveway. What is the minimum clearance above the driveway?","o":["18 ft","10 ft","15 ft","12 ft"],"a":3,"e":"Over residential property and driveways where voltage is not over 300 V to ground, 12 ft applies.","r":"NEC 230.24(B)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum clearance for overhead service conductors over public streets, alleys and roads?","o":["12 ft","15 ft","22 ft","18 ft"],"a":3,"e":"Public streets, alleys, roads and truck-traffic parking areas require 18 ft.","r":"NEC 230.24(B)(4)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"480Y/277 V overhead service conductors cross a residential driveway. What minimum clearance applies?","o":["10 ft","12 ft","15 ft","18 ft"],"a":1,"e":"The 277 V to ground does not exceed 300 V, so the 12 ft clearance applies.","r":"NEC 230.24(B)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum height above finished grade for the point of attachment of service-drop conductors to a building?","o":["15 ft","8 ft","10 ft","12 ft"],"a":2,"e":"The point of attachment must provide the required clearances and in no case be less than 10 ft above finished grade.","r":"NEC 230.26","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Which conductors may be attached to a service mast used for support?","o":["Service conductors and cable TV drop","Service conductors and a telephone drop","Any overhead conductor","Only power service-drop or overhead service conductors"],"a":3,"e":"Only power service-drop or overhead service conductors may be attached to a service mast.","r":"NEC 230.28","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"In general, how many sets of service-entrance conductors are permitted for each service drop or lateral?","o":["Six","Two","Three","One"],"a":3,"e":"Each service drop or lateral supplies only one set of service-entrance conductors, subject to listed exceptions.","r":"NEC 230.40","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"A service supplies continuous and noncontinuous loads (no 100 percent rated assembly). The service-entrance conductor ampacity must be at least:","o":["125 percent of all loads","Noncontinuous load plus 125 percent of the continuous load","100 percent of the continuous load only","Noncontinuous load plus 80 percent of the continuous load"],"a":1,"e":"Service conductors must carry noncontinuous load plus 125 percent of continuous load, before adjustment.","r":"NEC 230.42(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted for service-entrance conductors?","o":["Type MC cable","Type UF cable","Type NM cable","Type NMC cable"],"a":0,"e":"230.43 permits Type MC, SE, RMC, IMC, EMT and other listed methods, but not Type NM or UF.","r":"NEC 230.43","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"Which is permitted for service-entrance conductors?","o":["Rigid metal conduit","Type NM-B cable","Type UF-B cable","Open wiring in attics"],"a":0,"e":"RMC is permitted. Nonmetallic-sheathed and underground feeder cables are not on the 230.43 list.","r":"NEC 230.43","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Service-entrance conductors may be spliced or tapped in accordance with which?","o":["Only in a junction box with a blank cover","110.14, 300.5(E), 300.13 and 300.15","Only inside the meter socket","Never, they must be continuous"],"a":1,"e":"Service-entrance conductors may be spliced or tapped per 110.14, 300.5(E), 300.13 and 300.15.","r":"NEC 230.46","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Service-entrance cable on the exterior of a building is subject to physical damage. Which is an acceptable protection?","o":["Rigid metal conduit, IMC, Schedule 80 PVC, RTRC-XW or EMT","Type NM cable cover","No protection if above 6 ft","A strap every 6 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Where subject to physical damage, service-entrance conductors must be protected by RMC, IMC, Schedule 80 PVC, RTRC-XW, EMT or other approved means.","r":"NEC 230.50(B)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Service-entrance cable (not in a raceway) must be supported within what distance of every service head and at what maximum intervals?","o":["6 in. and 24 in.","24 in. and 48 in.","12 in. and 30 in.","18 in. and 36 in."],"a":2,"e":"Cables must be supported within 12 in. of every service head, gooseneck, or raceway/enclosure connection and at intervals not exceeding 30 in.","r":"NEC 230.51(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"Service raceways exposed to weather must be:","o":["Painted white","Raintight and arranged to drain","Sealed at both ends","Nonmetallic"],"a":1,"e":"Service raceways exposed to weather must be raintight and arranged to drain.","r":"NEC 230.53","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Service heads on raceways must be located above the point of attachment of the service-drop conductors. Where this is impractical, how far may it be?","o":["Within 24 in.","Within 12 in.","Within 36 in.","Within 6 in."],"a":0,"e":"Where impractical, the service head must be within 24 in. of the point of attachment.","r":"NEC 230.54(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"What must be formed on individual service-entrance conductors at the service head to prevent moisture entry?","o":["Expansion loops","Sealing compound","Splice kits","Drip loops"],"a":3,"e":"Drip loops must be formed on individual conductors, and the connection to the service drop must be below the level of the service head or termination of the cable sheath.","r":"NEC 230.54(F)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"On a 120/240 V, 3-phase, 4-wire delta service, how must the conductor with the higher voltage to ground (208 V) be identified?","o":["Blue","Red tape only at the meter","White","Orange outer finish or other effective means at each termination or junction"],"a":3,"e":"The service conductor having the higher phase voltage to ground must be durably marked by an orange outer finish or other effective means at each termination or junction point.","r":"NEC 230.56","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Which statement about marking of service equipment is correct?","o":["All metal enclosures are service equipment","Marking is optional","Service equipment must be listed and marked suitable for use as service equipment; individual meter socket enclosures are not considered service equipment","Meter sockets are service equipment"],"a":2,"e":"Service equipment must be listed or field evaluated and marked as suitable for use as service equipment; individual meter socket enclosures are not considered service equipment.","r":"NEC 230.66","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"A new service supplies a one-family dwelling unit. What is required?","o":["No SPD","A Type 1 or Type 2 surge-protective device","A GFCI service disconnect","A Type 4 SPD only"],"a":1,"e":"All services supplying dwelling units must have a Type 1 or Type 2 SPD.","r":"NEC 230.67(A) and (C)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Where is the required service SPD for a dwelling installed?","o":["At the utility pole only","At the farthest receptacle","Integral to the service equipment or immediately adjacent to it","In the attic"],"a":2,"e":"The SPD must be an integral part of the service equipment or located immediately adjacent.","r":"NEC 230.67(B)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Where must the service disconnecting means be located?","o":["At the farthest point from entrance","Anywhere in the building","Only in a utility room","At a readily accessible location outside the building or inside nearest the point of entrance of the service conductors"],"a":3,"e":"The service disconnect is installed at a readily accessible location either outside or inside nearest the point of entrance of the service conductors.","r":"NEC 230.70(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"Where is a service disconnecting means not permitted to be installed?","o":["Outdoors","Garages","Basements","Bathrooms"],"a":3,"e":"Service disconnecting means may not be installed in bathrooms.","r":"NEC 230.70(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"Each service disconnecting means must be:","o":["Located within 3 ft of the meter","Painted red","Permanently marked to identify it as a service disconnecting means","Locked at all times"],"a":2,"e":"Each service disconnect must be permanently marked to identify it as a service disconnecting means.","r":"NEC 230.70(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum number of switches or circuit breakers permitted to comprise the service disconnecting means for each service?","o":["Four","Eight","Two","Six"],"a":3,"e":"The service disconnecting means for each service consists of not more than six switches or six circuit breakers.","r":"NEC 230.71(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Which service disconnects may be located remotely from the grouped main disconnects?","o":["Those for receptacles","Those for lighting","Those for fire pumps, emergency, legally required standby and optional standby systems","None"],"a":2,"e":"Additional disconnects for fire pumps, emergency, legally required standby or optional standby systems may be remote from the grouped service disconnects.","r":"NEC 230.72(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"In a multiple-occupancy building, who must have access to a service disconnecting means (absent management-only exception)?","o":["Only the building owner","Each occupant to the occupant's service disconnect","Only an electrician","Only the utility"],"a":1,"e":"Each occupant must have access to the occupant's service disconnecting means, with an exception for buildings under continuous management supervision.","r":"NEC 230.72(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"A service disconnect with multiple ungrounded conductors must:","o":["Disconnect the grounded conductor first","Open only the highest phase","Simultaneously disconnect all ungrounded service conductors","Open each pole individually"],"a":2,"e":"Each service disconnect must simultaneously disconnect all ungrounded service conductors it controls.","r":"NEC 230.74","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"If the service disconnect does not disconnect the grounded service conductor, what must be provided?","o":["A terminal or bus in the service equipment to which the grounded conductor can be attached by a pressure connector","A second disconnect outdoors","A fused neutral switch","Nothing"],"a":0,"e":"Another means must be provided in the service equipment enclosure for disconnecting the grounded conductor from the premises wiring, such as a terminal or bus for a pressure connector.","r":"NEC 230.75","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"A service disconnect is power operated. What must be possible?","o":["It may not be opened manually","It must close automatically","It must be able to be opened by hand in the event of supply failure","It must be remote only"],"a":2,"e":"Power-operated service disconnects must be able to be opened by hand if electric supply fails.","r":"NEC 230.76","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"A service disconnecting means must:","o":["Plainly indicate whether it is in the open or closed position","Have a nameplate with load details only","Be painted","Be located outdoors"],"a":0,"e":"The service disconnect must plainly indicate whether it is open (off) or closed (on).","r":"NEC 230.77","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum rating of the service disconnecting means for a one-circuit installation?","o":["60 A","20 A","30 A","15 A"],"a":3,"e":"One-circuit installations require not less than 15 A.","r":"NEC 230.79(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum rating of the service disconnecting means for a one-family dwelling?","o":["100 A","60 A","150 A","200 A"],"a":0,"e":"A one-family dwelling service disconnect must be rated not less than 100 A, 3-wire.","r":"NEC 230.79(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"What minimum rating applies to a service disconnecting means for a two-circuit installation?","o":["15 A","60 A","30 A","100 A"],"a":2,"e":"A two-circuit installation requires not less than 30 A.","r":"NEC 230.79(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Which equipment may be connected to the supply side of the service disconnecting means?","o":["A general-purpose receptacle circuit","Lighting branch circuits","A subpanel feeder","Meters and meter sockets"],"a":3,"e":"Only listed equipment such as cable limiters, meters and meter sockets, and instrument transformers may be connected on the supply side.","r":"NEC 230.82","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"What is required for a one-family dwelling service?","o":["Nothing outdoors","A disconnect only in the attic","A shunt-trip only","A readily accessible outdoor emergency disconnecting means"],"a":3,"e":"Services for one- and two-family dwellings must have a readily accessible outdoor emergency disconnecting means.","r":"NEC 230.85","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"What wording must mark the emergency disconnect for a dwelling service?","o":["ELECTRIC SERVICE","EMERGENCY DISCONNECT, SERVICE DISCONNECT","SHUT OFF","MAIN POWER"],"a":1,"e":"The disconnect must be marked 'EMERGENCY DISCONNECT, SERVICE DISCONNECT'.","r":"NEC 230.85","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"Each ungrounded service conductor must have:","o":["GFCI protection","Overload protection","Only a fuse","No protection"],"a":1,"e":"Each ungrounded service conductor must have overload protection.","r":"NEC 230.90(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Which overcurrent device may be placed in series with a grounded service conductor?","o":["A fuse","A circuit breaker that simultaneously opens all conductors of the circuit","A single-pole circuit breaker","None"],"a":1,"e":"No overcurrent device may be in series with a grounded service conductor other than a breaker that opens all conductors simultaneously.","r":"NEC 230.90(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Where must the service overcurrent device be located?","o":["Outdoors on a pole","Anywhere in the building","Integral with the service disconnecting means or immediately adjacent to it","At the meter"],"a":2,"e":"The service OCPD must be an integral part of the service disconnect or immediately adjacent to it.","r":"NEC 230.91","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"At what service disconnect rating is ground-fault protection of equipment required (solidly grounded wye, over 150 V to ground, not over 1000 V phase-to-phase)?","o":["400 A or more","1000 A or more","800 A or more","2000 A or more"],"a":1,"e":"Each service disconnect rated 1000 A or more requires ground-fault protection of equipment.","r":"NEC 230.95","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum ground-fault protection setting permitted?","o":["1200 A","600 A","3000 A","1000 A"],"a":0,"e":"The maximum setting of the ground-fault protection is 1200 A.","r":"NEC 230.95(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"For ground-fault currents equal to or greater than 3000 A, the maximum time delay of the ground-fault protection is:","o":["5 seconds","10 seconds","1 second","0.5 second"],"a":2,"e":"The maximum time delay is 1 second for ground-fault currents of 3000 A or more.","r":"NEC 230.95(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"When must a ground-fault protection system for a service be performance tested?","o":["Only annually","Never","When first installed on site, with a written record","After 5 years"],"a":2,"e":"The system must be performance tested when first installed on site by a qualified person(s), and a written record made.","r":"NEC 230.95(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Overhead 120/240 V service-drop conductors are cabled together with a grounded bare messenger. What is the minimum clearance at the service entrance to the building, measured at the lowest point of the drip loop?","o":["15 ft","8 ft","12 ft","10 ft"],"a":3,"e":"At the electrical service entrance to buildings, at the lowest point of the drip loop, 10 ft is required for such cabled conductors of not over 150 V to ground.","r":"NEC 230.24(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":1,"q":"Conductors (other than flexible cords, flexible cables and fixture wires) must generally be protected against overcurrent in accordance with:","o":["The length of the circuit","The load only","Their ampacities as specified in 310.14","The size of the raceway"],"a":2,"e":"240.4 requires protection at the conductor ampacities from 310.14 (and other listed provisions).","r":"NEC 240.4(A)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"A 2 AWG copper conductor has an ampacity of 115 A at its termination temperature. For a feeder (not a multioutlet receptacle circuit), what is the maximum standard overcurrent device permitted under the next-higher-size rule?","o":["110 A","125 A","150 A","115 A"],"a":1,"e":"For devices 800 A or less, the next higher standard rating (125 A) is permitted when the ampacity does not correspond to a standard rating.","r":"NEC 240.4(B); 240.6(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"A 4 AWG copper conductor has a 75 C ampacity of 85 A. For a feeder with no multioutlet receptacle loads, what is the maximum standard overcurrent device rating permitted?","o":["80 A","90 A","100 A","85 A"],"a":1,"e":"85 A is not a standard rating, so the next higher standard rating, 90 A, is permitted.","r":"NEC 240.4(B); 240.6(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Which condition prohibits use of the next higher standard overcurrent device rating?","o":["The conductors are copper","The circuit is a feeder","The conductors are part of a branch circuit supplying more than one receptacle for cord-and-plug-connected portable loads","The device rating is 100 A"],"a":2,"e":"The next-higher-size rule does not apply where conductors supply more than one receptacle for cord-and-plug portable loads; the device may not exceed 800 A and the ampacity must not match a standard size.","r":"NEC 240.4(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"A conductor is protected by a device rated over 800 A. What is required of the conductor ampacity?","o":["It may be 125 percent of the device rating","It may be 80 percent of the device rating","It must be equal to or greater than the rating of the overcurrent device","It may be any ampacity above 800 A"],"a":2,"e":"Over 800 A, the conductor ampacity must be equal to or greater than the device rating; the next-higher-size rule does not apply.","r":"NEC 240.4(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum overcurrent protection for 14 AWG copper conductors (after correction factors)?","o":["10 A","15 A","20 A","30 A"],"a":1,"e":"Small conductor limits: 14 AWG copper is 15 A, 12 AWG copper 20 A, 10 AWG copper 30 A.","r":"NEC 240.4(D)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum overcurrent protection for 10 AWG copper conductors under the small-conductor rule?","o":["20 A","25 A","40 A","30 A"],"a":3,"e":"10 AWG copper is limited to 30 A.","r":"NEC 240.4(D)(5)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"What is the maximum overcurrent protection for 10 AWG aluminum conductors under the small-conductor rule?","o":["20 A","30 A","35 A","25 A"],"a":3,"e":"10 AWG aluminum or copper-clad aluminum is limited to 25 A; 12 AWG aluminum is limited to 15 A.","r":"NEC 240.4(D)(7)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is a standard ampere rating for fuses and inverse time circuit breakers?","o":["140 A","110 A","130 A","120 A"],"a":1,"e":"110 A is a standard rating; 120, 130 and 140 are not.","r":"NEC 240.6(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is NOT a standard rating for inverse time circuit breakers?","o":["70 A","60 A","75 A","80 A"],"a":2,"e":"The standard sizes are 60, 70, 80 and 90; 75 A is not on the list.","r":"NEC 240.6(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Which additional ampere rating is a standard rating for fuses only?","o":["1100 A","225 A","75 A","601 A"],"a":3,"e":"Additional standard fuse ratings are 1, 3, 6, 10 and 601 A.","r":"NEC 240.6(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"May two circuit breakers be connected in parallel to feed one load?","o":["No, except factory-assembled in parallel and listed as a unit","Yes, if less than 100 A","Yes, if on same bus","Yes, always"],"a":0,"e":"Fuses or circuit breakers in parallel are not permitted except as factory assembled in parallel and listed as a unit.","r":"NEC 240.8","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Thermal relays and similar devices not designed to open short circuits or ground faults:","o":["May be used for feeder short-circuit protection","May be used to protect motor branch-circuit conductors from overload if protected per 430.40, but not for short-circuit protection of conductors","May serve as branch-circuit overcurrent protection","May replace a circuit breaker"],"a":1,"e":"Such devices cannot protect conductors from short circuits or ground faults but may protect motor branch-circuit conductors from overload per 430.40.","r":"NEC 240.9","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Supplementary overcurrent protection in luminaires and appliances:","o":["Must be 20 A","Is not a substitute for required branch-circuit overcurrent protection","Replaces the branch-circuit breaker","Is not permitted"],"a":1,"e":"Supplementary protection may not be used as a substitute for required branch-circuit overcurrent devices.","r":"NEC 240.10","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Where an orderly shutdown is required to minimize hazards, what system is permitted?","o":["A single main only","A system of coordination based on coordinated short-circuit protection and overload indication","Removing grounding","Bypassing overcurrent devices"],"a":1,"e":"Electrical system coordination is permitted with coordinated short-circuit protection and overload indication.","r":"NEC 240.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"On a 480Y/277 V solidly grounded wye system, what rating of building main disconnecting device triggers ground-fault protection of equipment under 240.13?","o":["600 A","Any rating","800 A","1000 A or more"],"a":3,"e":"GFPE per 230.95 is required for each device used as a building main disconnect rated 1000 A or more on solidly grounded wye systems over 150 V to ground and up to 1000 V phase-to-phase.","r":"NEC 240.13","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":1,"q":"An overcurrent device must be placed in series with:","o":["The equipment grounding conductor","The grounded conductor only","Only one conductor","Each ungrounded conductor"],"a":3,"e":"An overcurrent device is required in series with each ungrounded conductor.","r":"NEC 240.15(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Individual single-pole circuit breakers with identified handle ties are permitted to protect:","o":["Service conductors only","Multiwire branch circuits that serve only line-to-neutral loads","Line-to-line loads of a multiwire branch circuit only","Any 3-phase motor load"],"a":1,"e":"Individual single-pole breakers with identified handle ties may protect multiwire branch circuits supplying only line-to-neutral loads, among other permitted applications.","r":"NEC 240.15(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Which overcurrent device is permitted in series with a grounded conductor?","o":["A single-pole breaker","Any fuse","One that opens all conductors of the circuit simultaneously","A thermal relay"],"a":2,"e":"No OCPD may be in series with a grounded conductor unless it opens all conductors simultaneously (or is for motor overload per 430.36/430.37).","r":"NEC 240.22","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"A field-installed feeder tap is not over 10 ft long. What minimum ampacity relative to the feeder overcurrent device is required (in addition to load requirements)?","o":["1/10 of the rating of the feeder OCPD","1/3 of the rating","1/2 of the rating","The full rating"],"a":0,"e":"For field-installed 10 ft taps the tap conductor ampacity must be not less than one-tenth of the feeder OCPD rating.","r":"NEC 240.21(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"A feeder tap is not over 25 ft long. What minimum ampacity relative to the feeder OCPD is required?","o":["1/10 of the rating","1/3 of the rating","The full rating","1/2 of the rating"],"a":1,"e":"For taps up to 25 ft the tap conductors must have ampacity not less than one-third of the feeder OCPD rating.","r":"NEC 240.21(B)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"A 480 V primary transformer is protected by a 100 A device and supplies 240 V secondary conductors not over 25 ft long. What minimum ampacity applies to the secondary conductors under the 25 ft rule?","o":["67 A","33 A","200 A","100 A"],"a":0,"e":"The ampacity must be at least the primary-to-secondary voltage ratio times one-third of the primary OCPD rating: 2 x 33.3 = 66.7 A.","r":"NEC 240.21(C)(6)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":1,"q":"The center of the grip of the operating handle of a switch or breaker in its highest position may not be more than what height above the floor or working platform?","o":["5 ft","7 ft","6 ft","6 ft 7 in."],"a":3,"e":"The maximum height is 6 ft 7 in. (2.0 m) above the floor or working platform.","r":"NEC 240.24(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":1,"q":"Overcurrent devices must not be located in:","o":["Garages","Bathrooms of dwelling units","Basements","Hallways"],"a":1,"e":"OCPDs may not be located in bathrooms of dwelling units, dormitories, or guest rooms/suites of hotels and motels.","r":"NEC 240.24(E)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":1,"q":"Overcurrent devices must not be located:","o":["In utility rooms","Outdoors","In basements","In the vicinity of easily ignitible material, such as in clothes closets"],"a":3,"e":"OCPDs must not be located in the vicinity of easily ignitible material, such as in clothes closets.","r":"NEC 240.24(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"In a multiple-occupancy building, who must have ready access to the overcurrent devices protecting conductors that supply their occupancy (general rule)?","o":["Each occupant","Only the utility","Only a licensed electrician","Only the owner"],"a":0,"e":"Each occupant must have ready access to all OCPDs protecting the conductors supplying that occupancy, with limited exceptions.","r":"NEC 240.24(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"How must enclosures for overcurrent devices generally be mounted?","o":["At any angle","In a vertical position, unless impracticable","Flat on floors","Horizontally"],"a":1,"e":"Enclosures must be mounted vertically unless impracticable; breaker enclosures may be horizontal under 240.81 conditions.","r":"NEC 240.33","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"When must a disconnecting means be provided on the supply side of a cartridge fuse?","o":["Never","Only in 600 V circuits","In circuits over 150 V to ground, and for circuits where accessible to other than qualified persons","Only for fuses over 600 A"],"a":2,"e":"A disconnecting means is required on the supply side where fuses are accessible to other than qualified persons and for all fuses in circuits over 150 V to ground.","r":"NEC 240.40","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"How are plug fuses rated 15 A and lower identified to distinguish them from higher rated fuses?","o":["Color-coded blue","Hexagonal window, cap or other prominent part","Square cap","Round window"],"a":1,"e":"Plug fuses of 15 A and lower are identified by a hexagonal configuration of the window, cap or other prominent part.","r":"NEC 240.50(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"A plug fuseholder's screw shell must be connected to:","o":["The equipment ground","The load side of the circuit","The supply side","The grounded conductor"],"a":1,"e":"The screw shell of a plug-fuse holder must be connected to the load side of the circuit.","r":"NEC 240.50(E)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Edison-base plug fuses may be used:","o":["Only in 240 V circuits","Only for replacements in existing installations where there is no evidence of overfusing or tampering","Only above 30 A","In all new installations"],"a":1,"e":"Edison-base fuses are limited to replacements in existing installations with no overfusing or tampering.","r":"NEC 240.51(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Type S fuses are classified at not over 125 V and in which ampere ranges?","o":["0-20 A and 21-40 A","0-15 A, 16-20 A and 21-30 A","0-10 A and 11-30 A","0-30 A only"],"a":1,"e":"Type S fuses are classified at 0-15 A, 16-20 A and 21-30 A, and are noninterchangeable between classes.","r":"NEC 240.53(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Once inserted in a fuseholder, a Type S adapter:","o":["Can be replaced with an Edison fuse","Can be removed with a screwdriver","Must not be removable","Is for 240 V only"],"a":2,"e":"Type S adapters must be designed so that once inserted in a fuseholder they cannot be removed.","r":"NEC 240.54(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"300-V type cartridge fuses may be used in which circuit?","o":["Any 600 V circuit","Not exceeding 300 V between conductors on single-phase line-to-line circuits or line-to-neutral of solidly grounded 3-phase, 4-wire systems not over 300 V","Only 120 V circuits","Any 480 V circuit"],"a":1,"e":"300-V fuses are limited to circuits not exceeding 300 V between conductors on single-phase line-to-line, or 300 V or less line-to-neutral on solidly grounded 3-phase 4-wire.","r":"NEC 240.60(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Cartridge fuses must be marked with their interrupting rating where it is other than:","o":["10,000 A","50,000 A","20,000 A","5,000 A"],"a":0,"e":"Fuses must be marked with interrupting rating where other than 10,000 A.","r":"NEC 240.60(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Class H cartridge fuses of the renewable type are permitted:","o":["Only for replacement in existing installations where there is no evidence of overfusing or tampering","Only above 600 A","Only for motors","In all new installations"],"a":0,"e":"Renewable fuses are limited to replacement in existing installations with no overfusing or tampering.","r":"NEC 240.60(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":1,"q":"A fuse rated 600 V nominal may be used on a circuit having a voltage:","o":["Only 120 V","Above its voltage rating","Only exactly 600 V","At or below its voltage rating"],"a":3,"e":"Fuses rated 600 V or less may be used for voltages at or below their ratings.","r":"NEC 240.61","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":1,"q":"If a circuit breaker handle is operated vertically, which position is the closed (on) position?","o":["Down","Either","Center","Up"],"a":3,"e":"When operated vertically, the up position is the closed (on) position.","r":"NEC 240.81","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"A circuit breaker must be designed so that alteration of its trip point:","o":["Can be done with a screwdriver","Is permitted by the user","Requires dismantling or breaking a seal (other than intended adjustments)","Is unlimited"],"a":2,"e":"Breakers must be nontamperable.","r":"NEC 240.82","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"A circuit breaker rated 100 A or less and 600 V or less must have its ampere rating marked where?","o":["Only on the panel schedule","Molded, stamped, etched or similarly marked into the handle or escutcheon area","On the box cover only","Only on the label inside the door"],"a":1,"e":"The ampere rating must be molded, stamped, etched or similarly marked on the handle or escutcheon area.","r":"NEC 240.83(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"The interrupting rating must be shown on a circuit breaker when it is other than:","o":["5,000 A","10,000 A","1,000 A","22,000 A"],"a":0,"e":"Circuit breakers with an interrupting rating other than 5000 A must have it marked (not required for supplementary protectors).","r":"NEC 240.83(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"A circuit breaker is used as a switch in 120 V fluorescent lighting circuits. What marking is required?","o":["HACR","GFCI","SWD","CU-AL"],"a":2,"e":"Breakers used as switches in 120 V and 277 V fluorescent lighting circuits must be listed and marked SWD or HID.","r":"NEC 240.83(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"A 120/240 V slash-rated circuit breaker is applied in a solidly grounded 208Y/120 V system. Is this permitted?","o":["Only on ungrounded systems","No, slash-rated breakers are only for 480 V","Yes, because 120 V to ground does not exceed the lower rating and 208 V line-to-line does not exceed the higher","No, because 208 V exceeds 120 V"],"a":2,"e":"A slash-rated breaker may be applied in a solidly grounded system if the voltage to ground does not exceed the lower rating and line-to-line does not exceed the higher.","r":"NEC 240.85","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"A two-pole circuit breaker may protect a 3-phase corner-grounded delta circuit only if:","o":["It is a GFCI","It is marked 1Φ-3Φ","It is marked SWD","It is rated 100 A or less"],"a":1,"e":"A two-pole breaker may not be used in a 3-phase corner-grounded delta circuit unless marked 1Φ-3Φ.","r":"NEC 240.85","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"A series-rated combination is installed. What marking is required on the end-use equipment?","o":["A caution that the equipment is a series combination system rated at a given amperes and an identified replacement component is required","The panel schedule only","UL logo","None"],"a":0,"e":"End-use equipment must be field-marked with the series combination system rating and a statement that identified replacement components are required.","r":"NEC 240.86(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Arc energy reduction means are required for circuit breakers rated or adjustable to what amperage or higher?","o":["2000 A","1000 A","1200 A","800 A"],"a":2,"e":"Where a breaker is rated or can be adjusted to 1200 A or higher, one of the listed arc-energy reduction methods must be provided.","r":"NEC 240.87","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Arc energy reduction is required for fuses rated:","o":["800 A or higher","1200 A or higher","1600 A or higher","1000 A or higher"],"a":1,"e":"Where fuses rated 1200 A or higher are installed, an arc-energy reduction method is required.","r":"NEC 240.67","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"In a grounded premises wiring system, can the earth be relied on as the effective ground-fault current path?","o":["Yes, if the ground rod measures 25 ohms or less","No. The earth is not considered an effective ground-fault current path","Yes, if two or more electrodes are installed","Yes, in a building supplied by a single branch circuit"],"a":1,"e":"A low-impedance metallic path back to the source is needed so the overcurrent device opens. Earth alone is too high in impedance, regardless of electrode resistance.","r":"NEC 250.4(A)(5)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"What is the main bonding jumper?","o":["The conductor that bonds the water piping to the service","The conductor that connects the service equipment to the grounding electrode","The connection between the grounded circuit conductor and the equipment grounding conductor at the service","The connection between the grounded conductor and the equipment grounding conductor at a separately derived system"],"a":2,"e":"The main bonding jumper is at the service. The same connection at a separately derived system is the system bonding jumper, and the conductor to the electrode is the grounding electrode conductor.","r":"NEC 250.2","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"What is a ground fault?","o":["An overload that persists for more than three hours","An intentional connection between the grounded conductor and earth at the service","An unintentional, electrically conducting connection between an ungrounded conductor and a normally non-current-carrying conductor, metal enclosure, metal raceway, metal equipment, or earth","An unintentional connection between two ungrounded conductors of different phases"],"a":2,"e":"A line-to-line fault is a short circuit, not a ground fault. A ground fault involves contact between an ungrounded conductor and grounded or grounding parts or earth.","r":"NEC 250.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Which action is a recognized way to stop objectionable current on grounding connections of a premises wiring system?","o":["Disconnect all grounding connections","Replace the grounded conductor with a smaller conductor","Remove the equipment grounding conductors from the affected circuits","Discontinue one or more, but not all, of the grounding connections"],"a":3,"e":"Corrective measures include discontinuing some (but never all) grounding connections, relocating them, or interrupting continuity of the conductor carrying the objectionable current. Removing all grounding is not permitted.","r":"NEC 250.6(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"Which fastener is not permitted for connecting grounding and bonding conductors and equipment?","o":["Machine screws secured with a nut","Thread-forming machine screws engaging at least two threads","Sheet-metal screws","Machine screws engaging at least two threads"],"a":2,"e":"Sheet-metal screws are not permitted to connect grounding or bonding conductors or equipment to the enclosure. The other fasteners are recognized in 250.8(A).","r":"NEC 250.8(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"Ground clamps or other grounding fittings that are exposed to physical damage must be:","o":["Covered with at least two layers of electrical tape","Enclosed in metal, wood, or equivalent protective covering","Buried at least 6 in below grade","Painted with a corrosion-resistant coating"],"a":1,"e":"Exposed clamps and fittings must be given protective enclosure so damage cannot break the grounding connection.","r":"NEC 250.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"Before making a grounding or bonding connection to painted equipment, what must be done?","o":["Nothing, because paint is thin enough to conduct fault current","Remove nonconductive coatings such as paint, lacquer, and enamel from threads and contact surfaces, or use fittings designed to make removal unnecessary","Apply conductive paint over the connection","Use only a listed green wire nut"],"a":1,"e":"Nonconductive coatings must be removed from contact surfaces (or penetrated by fittings designed for the purpose) to ensure electrical continuity.","r":"NEC 250.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A 24-volt ac control system is supplied by a transformer whose primary is fed from a 480Y/277-volt system. Under the NEC, the 24-volt system:","o":["Is not permitted to be grounded","Must be grounded only if it is run overhead","Must be grounded, because the transformer supply system exceeds 150 volts to ground","Need not be grounded because it is less than 50 volts"],"a":2,"e":"AC systems under 50 volts must be grounded when supplied by a transformer whose supply system exceeds 150 volts to ground (277 V here), when supply is ungrounded, or when run overhead outside buildings.","r":"NEC 250.20(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Which premises wiring system of 50 to 1000 volts is required to be grounded?","o":["A 480-volt, three-phase, 3-wire ungrounded delta system","A 480-volt, three-phase, 3-wire delta with no neutral load","A three-phase, 4-wire wye system in which the neutral conductor is used as a circuit conductor","Any system in a building with a metal frame"],"a":2,"e":"Three-phase 4-wire wye systems using the neutral as a circuit conductor must be grounded. Ungrounded systems are allowed by 250.21 under specified conditions.","r":"NEC 250.20(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A 240/120-volt, three-phase, 4-wire delta system has the midpoint of one phase winding used as a circuit conductor. What does the NEC require?","o":["The system must be grounded, with the midpoint conductor as the grounded conductor","Grounding is optional if the neutral carries less than 100 amperes","The system must remain ungrounded","The high-leg conductor must be grounded"],"a":0,"e":"A 3-phase 4-wire delta system where the midpoint of one phase is used as a circuit conductor must be grounded. The grounded conductor is that midpoint conductor, not the high leg.","r":"NEC 250.20(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"At a service, where may the grounding electrode conductor connection to the grounded service conductor be made?","o":["At any panelboard in the building","At any accessible point from the load end of the service drop or lateral to and including the service disconnecting means","Only at the utility transformer","Only at the first branch-circuit panel after the service disconnect"],"a":1,"e":"The system grounding connection may be made anywhere accessible from the load end of the service conductors up to and including the service disconnect.","r":"NEC 250.24(A)(1), 250.24(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"Where an ac service is grounded, the grounded conductor must be routed:","o":["Separately from the ungrounded conductors in its own raceway","With the ungrounded conductors to each service disconnecting means and connected to its grounded-conductor terminal or bus","To the main panel only, not to additional service disconnects","Directly to the electrode and not to the disconnect"],"a":1,"e":"The grounded service conductor is routed with the ungrounded conductors to each service disconnecting means.","r":"NEC 250.24(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A neutral conductor in a subpanel fed from the service equipment by a feeder in the same building must be:","o":["Isolated from the enclosure and equipment grounding conductor; the neutral-to-ground bond is made only at the service","Bonded to the subpanel enclosure with a main bonding jumper","Bonded to the enclosure only if the load exceeds 100 amperes","Connected to a ground rod at the subpanel"],"a":0,"e":"A grounded conductor must not be reconnected to ground or to the equipment grounding conductor on the load side of the service disconnect, except as specifically permitted (such as separately derived systems).","r":"NEC 250.24(A)(5), 250.142(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"A service has a largest ungrounded service-entrance conductor of 1500 kcmil copper. Considering only the grounding rule for the grounded conductor, what is its minimum size?","o":["2/0 AWG copper","3/0 AWG copper","1/0 AWG copper","4/0 AWG copper"],"a":3,"e":"Where ungrounded service conductors exceed 1100 kcmil copper, the grounded conductor must be at least 12.5 percent of the largest ungrounded conductor: 187.5 kcmil, so 4/0 (211.6 kcmil). 3/0 is only 167.8 kcmil.","r":"NEC 250.24(C)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"For a single-phase, 3-wire ac premises wiring system, which conductor is the one that must be grounded?","o":["No conductor, because the system is ungrounded","Either ungrounded conductor","Both ungrounded conductors","The neutral conductor"],"a":3,"e":"On a single-phase 3-wire system the neutral is the grounded conductor.","r":"NEC 250.26","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"A main bonding jumper may be which of the following?","o":["A wire, bus, screw, or similar suitable conductor of copper, aluminum, or other corrosion-resistant material","Only a listed pressure connector","Any steel strap that fits the enclosure","Only a copper wire"],"a":0,"e":"Main bonding jumpers may be wire, bus, screw, or similar suitable conductor of copper, aluminum, or another corrosion-resistant material.","r":"NEC 250.28(A), 250.28(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A service has 4/0 AWG copper ungrounded conductors. Using Table 250.102(C)(1), what is the minimum size copper main bonding jumper?","o":["2 AWG","6 AWG","1/0 AWG","4 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"The main bonding jumper is sized from Table 250.102(C)(1) (same values as Table 250.66). 4/0 copper falls in 'over 3/0 through 350 kcmil', which gives 2 AWG copper.","r":"NEC 250.28(D), Table 250.102(C)(1)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"For a separately derived system with a grounded conductor, the system bonding jumper is installed:","o":["At each load served by the system","At a single point, at the source or at the first system disconnecting means","At every panelboard fed from the system","Only at the grounding electrode"],"a":1,"e":"The system bonding jumper ties the grounded conductor to the supply-side bonding jumper or equipment grounding conductor at one point so neutral current cannot parallel the grounding path.","r":"NEC 250.30(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A detached garage is supplied by a single branch circuit that includes an equipment grounding conductor. Is a grounding electrode required at the garage?","o":["No, a grounding electrode is not required where only one branch circuit supplies the building and it includes an equipment grounding conductor","Yes, but only if the circuit is over 20 amperes","Yes, a ground ring is required","Yes, always, with a 25-ohm measurement"],"a":0,"e":"An exception to 250.32(A) removes the electrode requirement where one branch circuit (including a multiwire branch circuit) supplies the structure and includes an equipment grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 250.32(A) Exception","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A feeder supplies a panelboard in a detached building and an equipment grounding conductor is run with the feeder. At the detached building the neutral conductor must be:","o":["Bonded to the building grounding electrode","Connected to the metal water pipe","Bonded to the panelboard enclosure","Isolated from the equipment grounding conductor and enclosure, with no neutral-to-ground bond"],"a":3,"e":"When an equipment grounding conductor is run with the supply to a separate building, the grounded conductor is not bonded to the equipment grounding conductor or electrode there.","r":"NEC 250.32(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A 60-ampere feeder breaker supplies a panelboard in a detached building by copper conductors, and an equipment grounding conductor is run with them. What is the minimum copper equipment grounding conductor size?","o":["10 AWG","12 AWG","8 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"The equipment grounding conductor is sized from Table 250.122 using the overcurrent device ahead of the feeder; 60 A calls for 10 AWG copper.","r":"NEC 250.32(B)(1), Table 250.122","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A portable generator is not required to have a grounding electrode when:","o":["It supplies only equipment mounted on the generator and/or cord-and-plug equipment through receptacles on the generator, with non-current-carrying metal parts and receptacle grounding terminals bonded to the generator frame","It supplies a transfer switch connected to the building wiring","It is used outdoors in dry weather","It is rated less than 10 kW"],"a":0,"e":"The conditions in 250.34(A) allow the generator frame to serve as the grounding reference without an electrode. A generator feeding a building through a transfer switch is not covered.","r":"NEC 250.34(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"High-impedance grounded neutral systems under Article 250 are permitted for which of the following?","o":["Any system that serves line-to-neutral loads at 277 volts","Three-phase 208Y/120-volt systems serving receptacle loads","Any single-phase 120/240-volt dwelling service","Three-phase ac systems of 480 volts to 1000 volts where only qualified persons service the installation, ground detectors are installed, and line-to-neutral loads are not served"],"a":3,"e":"High-impedance grounding is limited to 480 to 1000 V three-phase systems with qualified maintenance, ground detectors, and no line-to-neutral loads.","r":"NEC 250.36","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"If a building has a metal underground water pipe, a concrete-encased electrode, and a ground ring present, what must be done with them?","o":["Only the one with the lowest resistance must be used","Only the water pipe must be used","They must be kept separate to avoid circulating current","All must be bonded together to form the grounding electrode system"],"a":3,"e":"All electrodes of the types in 250.52(A)(1) through (A)(7) that are present at each building must be bonded together to form the grounding electrode system.","r":"NEC 250.50","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"To qualify as a grounding electrode, a metal underground water pipe must be in direct contact with the earth for at least:","o":["8 feet","20 feet","10 feet","5 feet"],"a":2,"e":"A metal underground water pipe in direct contact with earth for 10 ft or more is a grounding electrode, and it must be supplemented by another electrode.","r":"NEC 250.52(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is NOT permitted to be used as a grounding electrode?","o":["Metal underground gas piping system","Ground ring","Driven rod","Concrete-encased electrode"],"a":0,"e":"Metal underground gas piping and aluminum electrodes are not permitted as grounding electrodes.","r":"NEC 250.52(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A concrete-encased electrode uses reinforcing steel. What are the minimum requirements?","o":["At least 5 ft of 1/2 in rebar encased in 2 in of concrete","At least 20 ft of one or more steel reinforcing bars not less than 1/2 in diameter, encased in at least 2 in of concrete near the bottom of a foundation or footing in direct contact with earth","At least 10 ft of 3/8 in rebar encased in 1 in of concrete","At least 20 ft of 3/8 in rebar encased in 3 in of concrete"],"a":1,"e":"The concrete-encased electrode requires 20 ft of 1/2 in (minimum) bare or zinc-coated steel rebar, or 20 ft of bare copper, within at least 2 in of concrete.","r":"NEC 250.52(A)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A concrete-encased electrode is built with bare copper instead of rebar. What is the minimum?","o":["At least 20 ft of bare copper not smaller than 8 AWG","At least 30 ft of bare copper not smaller than 2 AWG","At least 10 ft of bare copper not smaller than 6 AWG","At least 20 ft of bare copper conductor not smaller than 4 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"The copper alternative is at least 20 ft of bare copper conductor not smaller than 4 AWG, encased in at least 2 in of concrete.","r":"NEC 250.52(A)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"What are the minimum size and length requirements for a ground ring electrode encircling a building?","o":["Bare copper not smaller than 2 AWG, at least 10 ft long","Bare copper not smaller than 6 AWG, at least 10 ft long","Bare copper not smaller than 4 AWG, at least 20 ft long","Bare copper conductor not smaller than 2 AWG, at least 20 ft long"],"a":3,"e":"A ground ring must be at least 20 ft of bare copper not smaller than 2 AWG encircling the building in direct contact with earth.","r":"NEC 250.52(A)(4)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"Unless listed otherwise, the minimum diameter of a steel or stainless steel ground rod electrode is:","o":["3/8 in","3/4 in","1/2 in","5/8 in"],"a":3,"e":"Rod-type electrodes of stainless steel or zinc-coated steel must be at least 5/8 in in diameter unless listed; listed rods must be at least 1/2 in.","r":"NEC 250.52(A)(5)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"Pipe or conduit used as a grounding electrode must be not smaller than which trade size?","o":["1","1-1/4","1/2","3/4"],"a":3,"e":"Pipe electrodes must be trade size 3/4 or larger, and iron or steel pipe must be galvanized or otherwise metal-coated for corrosion protection.","r":"NEC 250.52(A)(5)(a)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"What minimum surface area must a plate electrode expose to the soil?","o":["6 square feet","1 square foot","4 square feet","2 square feet"],"a":3,"e":"Plate electrodes must present at least 0.186 m2 (2 sq ft) of surface to exterior soil.","r":"NEC 250.52(A)(7)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"What minimum thickness applies to an iron or steel plate electrode?","o":["1/4 in","1/8 in","1/2 in","1/16 in"],"a":0,"e":"Iron or steel plate electrodes must be at least 6.4 mm (1/4 in) thick; nonferrous plates must be at least 1.5 mm (0.06 in).","r":"NEC 250.52(A)(7)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A single driven rod measures 40 ohms to earth. What does the NEC require?","o":["It must be replaced by a 4 AWG conductor","It must be replaced by a rod of larger diameter","Nothing, because there is no required resistance value","It must be augmented by one additional electrode"],"a":3,"e":"A single rod, pipe, or plate that does not have 25 ohms or less to ground must be augmented by one additional electrode of the types in 250.52(A)(2) through (A)(8).","r":"NEC 250.53(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"When two or more rod electrodes are installed to form part of the grounding electrode system, what is the minimum spacing between them?","o":["8 ft","10 ft","6 ft","2 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Multiple rod, pipe, or plate electrodes must be at least 1.8 m (6 ft) apart, and more is better.","r":"NEC 250.53(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum length of a rod electrode that must be in contact with the soil?","o":["4 ft","6 ft","8 ft","10 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Rod and pipe electrodes must have at least 2.44 m (8 ft) of length in contact with the soil.","r":"NEC 250.53(G)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A ground rod hits rock at 4 ft. Which installation is permitted?","o":["Drive it at an angle of 60 degrees from vertical","Drive it at an oblique angle not exceeding 45 degrees from vertical, or bury it in a trench at least 30 in deep","Bury it in a trench at least 12 in deep","Cut it off at 4 ft and use it as is"],"a":1,"e":"Where rock bottom prevents full depth, the rod may be driven at up to 45 degrees from vertical or buried horizontally in a trench at least 30 in deep.","r":"NEC 250.53(G)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"How far below the surface of the earth must a plate electrode be installed?","o":["At least 8 ft","At least 30 in","At least 12 in","At least 18 in"],"a":1,"e":"Plate electrodes must be installed not less than 750 mm (30 in) below the surface of the earth.","r":"NEC 250.53(H)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"The top end of a driven ground rod and its grounding electrode conductor connection are subject to physical damage. What must be done?","o":["Cover the clamp with duct tape","Protect the above-ground end and the connection against physical damage","Nothing, because ground rods are always allowed to stick out","Cut the rod off below grade and use a splice"],"a":1,"e":"The upper end must be flush with or below ground level unless the above-ground end and the conductor attachment are protected against physical damage.","r":"NEC 250.53(G)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A building's grounding electrode system includes only a metal underground water pipe that is 20 ft in contact with earth. What else is required?","o":["A gas pipe connection","Nothing; the water pipe alone is sufficient","A second water pipe connection","It must be supplemented by an additional electrode of a type listed in 250.52(A)(2) through (A)(8)"],"a":3,"e":"A metal underground water pipe electrode must be supplemented by an additional electrode.","r":"NEC 250.53(D)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Several buildings on a property each have services. At one building the ac system is grounded to an electrode. What does the NEC require for the electrode used for conductor enclosures and equipment in or on that building?","o":["The same electrode must be used to ground the conductor enclosures and equipment in or on that building","A separate electrode must be used for equipment","A separate electrode must be installed for each circuit","No electrode is permitted for conductor enclosures"],"a":0,"e":"A common grounding electrode is required for the ac system and the equipment/enclosures in or on a building to prevent voltage differences between separate grounding systems.","r":"NEC 250.58","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Where may an aluminum grounding electrode conductor be installed outside above ground?","o":["Embedded in concrete","Directly buried in earth","Within 6 in of the earth if painted","Not within 18 in of the earth and not in direct contact with masonry"],"a":3,"e":"Aluminum or copper-clad aluminum grounding electrode conductors cannot be in direct contact with masonry or earth and cannot be terminated within 18 in of the earth outdoors.","r":"NEC 250.64(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"An 8 AWG copper grounding electrode conductor is run on the surface of a building. How must it be installed?","o":["Run bare through open framing","Exposed and stapled to the surface","In a raceway or cable armor, since conductors smaller than 6 AWG must be protected","Buried 6 in deep without protection"],"a":2,"e":"Grounding electrode conductors smaller than 6 AWG must be installed in rigid metal conduit, IMC, PVC, RTRC, EMT, or cable armor.","r":"NEC 250.64(B)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A 6 AWG copper grounding electrode conductor is free from exposure to physical damage. How may it be installed?","o":["Only in rigid metal conduit","Along the surface of the building construction without metal covering, securely fastened","Only inside EMT","Only buried underground"],"a":1,"e":"A 6 AWG conductor free of exposure to physical damage may run along the surface securely fastened; 4 AWG or larger must be protected if exposed to damage; smaller than 6 AWG must be in a raceway or armor.","r":"NEC 250.64(B)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Grounding electrode conductors must be installed in one continuous length. Which is a permitted splice?","o":["Solder only","Twist-on wire connectors","Irreversible compression-type connectors listed for the purpose, or exothermic welding","Screw-on wire nuts listed for 600 volts"],"a":2,"e":"Splices in a grounding electrode conductor are limited to listed irreversible compression-type connectors or exothermic welding (and busbar connections).","r":"NEC 250.64(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A grounding electrode conductor is installed in a ferrous metal raceway. How must the raceway be treated?","o":["It must be bonded at one end only","Nothing special is required","It must be insulated from the conductor","It must be electrically continuous and bonded to the grounding electrode conductor at each end"],"a":3,"e":"Ferrous metal raceways enclosing a grounding electrode conductor must be bonded to the conductor at both ends to avoid choke effect.","r":"NEC 250.64(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Service-entrance conductors are 4/0 AWG copper. What is the minimum size copper grounding electrode conductor (to a water pipe electrode)?","o":["4 AWG","6 AWG","1/0 AWG","2 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Table 250.66: 4/0 copper falls in 'over 3/0 through 350 kcmil', requiring 2 AWG copper.","r":"NEC 250.66, Table 250.66","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Service-entrance conductors are 500 kcmil copper. What is the minimum size copper grounding electrode conductor to a metal water pipe electrode?","o":["3/0 AWG","1/0 AWG","2 AWG","2/0 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Table 250.66: 500 kcmil copper falls in 'over 350 through 600 kcmil', requiring 1/0 AWG copper.","r":"NEC 250.66, Table 250.66","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"Service-entrance conductors are 2 AWG copper. What is the minimum size copper grounding electrode conductor (to a metal water pipe electrode)?","o":["4 AWG","10 AWG","6 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Table 250.66: 2 AWG or smaller copper requires 8 AWG copper.","r":"NEC 250.66, Table 250.66","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"A service uses 1/0 AWG aluminum ungrounded conductors. What is the minimum size copper grounding electrode conductor to a water pipe electrode?","o":["6 AWG copper","4 AWG copper","2 AWG copper","8 AWG copper"],"a":3,"e":"Table 250.66: 1/0 aluminum or smaller falls in the first row, which requires 8 AWG copper (6 AWG aluminum).","r":"NEC 250.66, Table 250.66","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Service conductors are 500 kcmil copper and the grounding electrode conductor runs to a single driven ground rod. What is the largest copper GEC required for that connection?","o":["2 AWG","4 AWG","6 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Where the grounding electrode conductor connects to a rod, pipe, or plate electrode, it is not required to be larger than 6 AWG copper.","r":"NEC 250.66(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Service conductors are 500 kcmil copper and the grounding electrode conductor runs only to a concrete-encased electrode. What is the largest copper GEC required for that connection?","o":["1/0 AWG","6 AWG","2 AWG","4 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"The portion of a GEC that connects to a concrete-encased electrode is not required to be larger than 4 AWG copper.","r":"NEC 250.66(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A grounding electrode conductor connects to a ground ring made of 2 AWG copper. The largest GEC required for that connection is:","o":["1/0 AWG copper","2 AWG copper, the size of the ring conductor","4 AWG copper","6 AWG copper"],"a":1,"e":"A GEC to a ground ring need not be larger than the conductor used for the ground ring.","r":"NEC 250.66(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Which grounding electrode conductor connections are not required to be accessible?","o":["Connections encased in concrete or buried","Connections to the water pipe","Connections to the service equipment","Connections made with listed clamps"],"a":0,"e":"Encased or buried connections to electrodes are exempt from the accessibility requirement.","r":"NEC 250.68(A) Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A metal water pipe serves as a grounding electrode and a water meter is installed in the line. How is continuity ensured?","o":["No bonding is required because the meter is brass","By bonding the meter to the gas line","By bonding around the water meter with a bonding jumper long enough to allow its removal","By relying on the water inside the pipe"],"a":2,"e":"Piping continuity must be maintained by bonding around insulated joints and around equipment likely to be disconnected for repair or replacement, such as water meters.","r":"NEC 250.68(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Interior metal water piping located more than ____ from the point of entrance to the building cannot be used as part of the grounding electrode system or as a conductor to interconnect electrodes.","o":["5 ft","10 ft","3 ft","20 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Only the first 5 ft of interior metal water pipe from the entrance may be used as part of the grounding electrode system or as an interconnecting conductor.","r":"NEC 250.68(C)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"How many grounding electrode conductors may be connected to an electrode with a single clamp?","o":["Two, always","Three, if the electrode is a rod","Only one, unless the clamp or fitting is listed for multiple conductors","Any number if tightened with a torque wrench"],"a":2,"e":"Not more than one conductor per clamp or fitting is permitted unless the fitting is listed for multiple conductors.","r":"NEC 250.70","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is an acceptable method to ensure continuity of bonding for metal service raceways?","o":["A plastic bushing on the raceway end","Threaded couplings or hubs made up wrenchtight","A standard locknut on each side of the enclosure knockout","Nonmetallic couplings"],"a":1,"e":"Threaded couplings/hubs made wrenchtight, threadless couplings made up tight, bonding jumpers, and listed bonding devices (such as bonding-type locknuts or bushings) are allowed. Standard locknuts are not a listed bonding device.","r":"NEC 250.92(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"An intersystem bonding termination must be capable of connecting at least how many intersystem bonding conductors?","o":["One","Six","Three","Two"],"a":2,"e":"The termination must provide for connection of not fewer than three intersystem bonding conductors and must not interfere with opening of any enclosure.","r":"NEC 250.94(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum size of the conductor connecting an intersystem bonding termination to the service enclosure or electrode conductor?","o":["6 AWG copper","14 AWG copper","10 AWG copper","2 AWG copper"],"a":0,"e":"The intersystem bonding termination conductor must be at least 6 AWG copper.","r":"NEC 250.94(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"For a circuit operating at over 250 volts to ground (for example, a 480Y/277-volt circuit) in a metal raceway, what may not be the sole means of bonding at knockouts?","o":["Listed bonding jumpers","Bonding-type locknuts with jumpers","Threaded couplings made up wrenchtight","Standard locknuts or bushings"],"a":3,"e":"For circuits over 250 V to ground, standard locknuts or bushings shall not be the sole means of bonding; bonding jumpers or bonding-type devices are needed.","r":"NEC 250.97","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"How must expansion fittings and telescoping sections of metal raceways be made electrically continuous?","o":["By PVC couplings","By using a larger raceway","With equipment bonding jumpers or other means","No bonding is needed, since they are gasketed"],"a":2,"e":"Expansion fittings and telescoping sections of metal raceways must be made electrically continuous by equipment bonding jumpers or other means.","r":"NEC 250.98","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"In a Class I, Division 1 hazardous (classified) location, metal raceways and equipment enclosures are bonded:","o":["Only if the raceway is aluminum","Only if the circuit voltage exceeds 600 volts","Only if the circuit voltage exceeds 250 volts","Regardless of voltage, using threaded couplings or bonding jumpers around loose-jointed fittings"],"a":3,"e":"The electrical continuity of non-current-carrying metal parts in hazardous locations must be ensured regardless of voltage by bonding.","r":"NEC 250.100","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"A service has four parallel sets of 3 x 350 kcmil copper ungrounded conductors, one set per raceway, and a copper supply-side bonding jumper is run in each raceway. What is the minimum size of each bonding jumper?","o":["3/0 AWG copper","6 AWG copper","2 AWG copper","1/0 AWG copper"],"a":2,"e":"Where conductors are paralleled in multiple raceways, the supply-side bonding jumper in each raceway is sized from Table 250.102(C)(1) based on the ungrounded conductors in that raceway; 350 kcmil gives 2 AWG copper.","r":"NEC 250.102(C)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A bonding jumper installed on the outside of a raceway to bond the raceway ends is limited to what length?","o":["10 ft","3 ft","6 ft","25 ft"],"a":2,"e":"An equipment bonding jumper on the outside of a raceway must be no longer than 1.8 m (6 ft) and routed with the raceway.","r":"NEC 250.102(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"A dwelling has 4/0 AWG copper service conductors. What is the minimum size copper bonding jumper required for the interior metal water piping system under the general rule?","o":["6 AWG","2 AWG","4 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Metal water piping is bonded using Table 250.102(C)(1) based on the service conductors; 4/0 copper gives 2 AWG copper.","r":"NEC 250.104(A), Table 250.102(C)(1)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Metal gas piping is likely to be energized by a 20-ampere branch circuit. What is the minimum size copper bonding jumper required for the gas piping?","o":["8 AWG, based on the service size","6 AWG, as for a ground rod","14 AWG, as the smallest copper wire","12 AWG, per Table 250.122 for the 20-ampere circuit"],"a":3,"e":"Other metal piping likely to become energized is bonded with a conductor sized by Table 250.122 using the rating of the circuit likely to energize the piping; 20 A gives 12 AWG copper.","r":"NEC 250.104(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"How must a lightning protection system be connected to the building grounding electrode system?","o":["It must be kept separate by at least 6 ft","It must be bonded to the building grounding electrode system","It must be connected only to the gas piping","It may replace the electrode system"],"a":1,"e":"Lightning protection system ground terminals must be bonded to the building grounding electrode system; a separate lightning electrode cannot replace the required electrode.","r":"NEC 250.106","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"In a dwelling, which cord-and-plug connected equipment must be grounded (with equipment grounding conductor) regardless of voltage?","o":["Plug-in clocks","Double-insulated table lamps","Dishwashers","Handheld hair dryers with double insulation"],"a":2,"e":"Refrigerators, freezers, air conditioners, clothes washers and dryers, dishwashers, information technology equipment, sump pumps, and electrical aquarium equipment require grounding in residential occupancies.","r":"NEC 250.114","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is recognized as an equipment grounding conductor?","o":["Liquidtight flexible nonmetallic conduit","Rigid PVC conduit","Metal underground gas piping","Electrical metallic tubing"],"a":3,"e":"EMT, RMC, and IMC are recognized equipment grounding conductors. Nonmetallic raceways and gas piping are not.","r":"NEC 250.118","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A 20-ampere circuit is run in trade size 3/4 flexible metal conduit (FMC) with listed fittings and no separate equipment grounding conductor. The FMC run is 10 ft. Is the FMC acceptable as the equipment grounding conductor?","o":["Yes, any length is allowed if listed fittings are used","Yes, for circuits up to 60 amperes","No, because the total length of flexible metal conduit in the ground return path may not exceed 6 ft","Yes, if the run is over 5 ft"],"a":2,"e":"FMC can serve as the EGC only where the total length in the ground return path does not exceed 6 ft, with listed fittings and circuit conductors protected at 20 A or less.","r":"NEC 250.118(5)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"An insulated equipment grounding conductor 6 AWG or smaller must be identified by:","o":["Continuous white","Continuous gray","Red with a green stripe","Continuous green or continuous green with one or more yellow stripes"],"a":3,"e":"Insulated EGCs 6 AWG or smaller must be identified by a continuous outer finish of green or green with one or more yellow stripes.","r":"NEC 250.119","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"An insulated equipment grounding conductor 4 AWG or larger that is not green may be permanently identified as an EGC by:","o":["Stripping insulation along its length, coloring it green, or marking it with green tape or labels at each end and at accessible points","Painting it yellow","Using white tape at the terminations","Leaving it unmarked if it is in a raceway"],"a":0,"e":"Insulated EGCs 4 AWG and larger may be identified at each end and every accessible point by stripping, green coloring, or green tape or adhesive labels.","r":"NEC 250.119(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Where is an aluminum equipment grounding conductor not permitted?","o":["In a cable assembly","Within a panelboard","Inside a metal raceway","In direct contact with masonry or earth, or terminated within 18 in of the earth outdoors"],"a":3,"e":"Aluminum or copper-clad aluminum EGCs cannot be in direct contact with masonry or earth or subject to corrosive conditions, nor terminated within 18 in of the earth outdoors.","r":"NEC 250.120(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for a circuit protected by a 20-ampere overcurrent device?","o":["10 AWG","12 AWG","8 AWG","14 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 12 AWG copper for a 20 A overcurrent device.","r":"NEC 250.122, Table 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for a circuit protected by a 100-ampere overcurrent device?","o":["6 AWG","8 AWG","10 AWG","4 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 8 AWG copper for a 100 A overcurrent device.","r":"NEC 250.122, Table 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for a circuit protected by a 200-ampere overcurrent device?","o":["4 AWG","3 AWG","8 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 6 AWG copper for a 200 A overcurrent device.","r":"NEC 250.122, Table 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for a circuit protected by a 400-ampere overcurrent device?","o":["3 AWG","2 AWG","4 AWG","1 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 3 AWG copper for a 400 A overcurrent device.","r":"NEC 250.122, Table 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for a circuit protected by a 60-ampere overcurrent device?","o":["6 AWG","8 AWG","10 AWG","12 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 10 AWG copper for 60 A (and 30 A, 40 A).","r":"NEC 250.122, Table 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for a feeder protected by a 600-ampere overcurrent device?","o":["1/0 AWG","3 AWG","1 AWG","2 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 1 AWG copper for a 600 A overcurrent device.","r":"NEC 250.122, Table 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for a feeder protected by an 800-ampere overcurrent device?","o":["1/0 AWG","2/0 AWG","2 AWG","1 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 1/0 AWG copper for an 800 A overcurrent device.","r":"NEC 250.122, Table 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for a feeder protected by a 1000-ampere overcurrent device?","o":["2/0 AWG","3/0 AWG","1/0 AWG","4/0 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 2/0 AWG copper for a 1000 A overcurrent device.","r":"NEC 250.122, Table 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum size aluminum equipment grounding conductor for a circuit protected by a 100-ampere overcurrent device?","o":["6 AWG aluminum","4 AWG aluminum","8 AWG aluminum","10 AWG aluminum"],"a":0,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 6 AWG aluminum (or copper-clad aluminum) for a 100 A overcurrent device.","r":"NEC 250.122, Table 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"A 20-ampere branch circuit uses 10 AWG copper conductors, upsized from 12 AWG for voltage drop. The equipment grounding conductor is originally 12 AWG. What size must it be?","o":["8 AWG","10 AWG, increased proportionally in circular mil area to the ungrounded conductors","14 AWG","12 AWG, no change is required"],"a":1,"e":"If ungrounded conductors are increased in size beyond the minimum, the EGC must be proportionally increased in circular mil area. 10 AWG is 10,380 cmil, equal to the 12 AWG (6,530 cmil) scaled by the same ratio.","r":"NEC 250.122(B)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Three circuits with 20 A, 30 A, and 60 A overcurrent devices share one raceway and are to be served by a single copper equipment grounding conductor. What is the minimum size?","o":["8 AWG","12 AWG, based on the smallest device","14 AWG","10 AWG, based on the largest (60 A) overcurrent device"],"a":3,"e":"When multiple circuits are in the same raceway, one EGC may serve them, sized by Table 250.122 for the largest overcurrent device in the raceway.","r":"NEC 250.122(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"A 600-ampere feeder breaker feeds three parallel sets of conductors in three separate raceways with copper EGCs run in each raceway. What is the minimum size of the copper EGC in each raceway?","o":["1/0 AWG","6 AWG, one-third of the circuit","1 AWG, sized from the 600 A overcurrent device","3 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Where conductors are paralleled in multiple raceways, an EGC is installed in each raceway and each is sized by Table 250.122 using the rating of the overcurrent device protecting the circuit (600 A gives 1 AWG).","r":"NEC 250.122(F)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"A wiring device's EGC terminal can be identified by:","o":["A brass slotted screw","A white hex-head screw","A red terminal screw","A green, not readily removable terminal screw with a hexagonal head"],"a":3,"e":"The terminal for the equipment grounding conductor is identified by a green hex-head screw, green hex nut, green pressure wire connector, or marked 'G', 'GR', 'GND', 'green', or 'ground' or the ground symbol.","r":"NEC 250.126","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"A two-wire receptacle outlet with no equipment grounding conductor in the circuit is replaced with a grounding-type receptacle. Which is an allowed grounding connection for a new EGC from the receptacle?","o":["A nearby gas pipe","The neutral terminal of the receptacle","The ungrounded conductor of another circuit","Any accessible point on the grounding electrode system or on the grounding electrode conductor"],"a":3,"e":"The EGC may be run to the grounding electrode system, the GEC, the equipment grounding bar where the circuit originates, or (for grounded systems) the grounded service conductor within the service equipment enclosure.","r":"NEC 250.130(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"In a new installation, how is the frame of an electric range grounded?","o":["By connection to a nearby water pipe only","By connection to the equipment grounding conductor","By connection to the grounded (neutral) conductor","It need not be grounded"],"a":1,"e":"New installations require the frames of ranges, wall-mounted ovens, counter-mounted cooking units, and clothes dryers to be connected to the equipment grounding conductor (four-wire circuit). Use of the neutral is limited to certain existing installations.","r":"NEC 250.140","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"On the supply side of the service disconnect, the grounded conductor may be used to ground:","o":["Only the load-side panelboards","Non-current-carrying metal parts of equipment such as the meter enclosure and service raceway","Nothing; the grounded conductor may never ground equipment","Only portable equipment"],"a":1,"e":"On the supply side of the service disconnecting means, the grounded conductor is permitted to ground non-current-carrying metal parts. On the load side, it generally is not.","r":"NEC 250.142(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"An isolated ground receptacle is used for sensitive electronic equipment. The grounding terminal is purposely insulated from the mounting yoke. The equipment grounding conductor from it may:","o":["Terminate at a ground rod","Be connected to the box with a green screw","Be connected to the neutral bar","Pass through one or more panelboards without a connection to the panelboard enclosure to terminate at the equipment grounding terminal of the applicable derived system or service"],"a":3,"e":"An EGC to an isolated ground receptacle may pass through boxes and panelboards without connecting to them, terminating at the service or separately derived system grounding point.","r":"NEC 250.146(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"When circuit conductors are spliced in a box containing the EGCs of more than one circuit, how must the EGCs be arranged?","o":["Connected to the device terminals in series","Spliced or joined within the box so that removal of a device does not interrupt continuity of the other EGCs","Connected to the neutral splice","Left unspliced and capped"],"a":1,"e":"EGCs must be spliced so that disconnection of a receptacle or device does not interrupt grounding continuity for other devices.","r":"NEC 250.148","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"A current transformer has a primary connected to a 480-volt circuit with 277 volts to ground and is not on a switchboard. Must its secondary be grounded?","o":["Yes, if the secondary is under 50 volts","No, instrument transformers are never grounded","Yes, any primary voltage requires grounding","No; secondary grounding is required where the primary is 300 volts or more to ground, or for any voltage on switchboards"],"a":3,"e":"Instrument transformer secondary circuits must be grounded where primary windings are connected to circuits of 300 V or more to ground, and on switchboards regardless of voltage.","r":"NEC 250.170","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Which circuit must NOT be grounded?","o":["A 120/208-volt receptacle circuit","Isolated power system circuits in patient care wet procedure locations","The grounded conductor of a 120/240 volt residence","A 277-volt lighting circuit"],"a":1,"e":"Circuits in health care facilities covered by 517.20 and 517.160 (isolated power in wet procedure locations) are among those not to be grounded.","r":"NEC 250.22","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"What is the system bonding jumper?","o":["The conductor from the service equipment to the grounding electrode","The bonding jumper around a water meter","The connection between the grounded conductor and the equipment grounding conductor at the service","The connection between the grounded circuit conductor and the supply-side bonding jumper or equipment grounding conductor (or both) at a separately derived system"],"a":3,"e":"A system bonding jumper is used at separately derived systems. The equivalent connection at the service is the main bonding jumper.","r":"NEC 250.2","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"Which is a purpose of system grounding?","o":["To eliminate the need for overcurrent devices","To limit voltage imposed by lightning, line surges, or unintentional contact with higher-voltage lines, and to stabilize voltage to ground during normal operation","To reduce the ampacity needed for branch circuits","To make the earth the path that clears ground faults"],"a":1,"e":"System grounding limits voltage and stabilizes it relative to earth. The fault-clearing path must be a low-impedance bonded path back to the source, not earth.","r":"NEC 250.4(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Service-entrance conductors are 3/0 AWG copper. What is the minimum copper grounding electrode conductor to a metal water pipe electrode?","o":["2 AWG","6 AWG","4 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Table 250.66: 2/0 or 3/0 copper requires 4 AWG copper.","r":"NEC 250.66, Table 250.66","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"May a lightning rod conductor and its ground rod be used as the grounding electrode for the building electrical system in place of the electrodes required by 250.50?","o":["No; lightning protection conductors and electrodes cannot be used in place of the grounding electrodes required for the electrical system","Yes, if it is driven 8 ft","Yes, if it is within 6 ft of the service","Yes, if the lightning rod is copper"],"a":0,"e":"Air terminals and their down conductors and electrodes are separate from the electrical grounding electrode system, though they must be bonded to it.","r":"NEC 250.60","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for a circuit protected by a 15-ampere overcurrent device?","o":["10 AWG","14 AWG","12 AWG","16 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Table 250.122 specifies 14 AWG copper for 15 A.","r":"NEC 250.122, Table 250.122","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"A separately derived 3-phase transformer system has 4/0 AWG copper derived ungrounded conductors. What is the minimum size copper grounding electrode conductor to the electrode?","o":["1/0 AWG","4 AWG","2 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"The grounding electrode conductor for a separately derived system is sized from Table 250.66 using the derived ungrounded conductors; 4/0 copper requires 2 AWG copper.","r":"NEC 250.30(A)(5), Table 250.66","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":1,"q":"Under the NEC, a surge-protective device (SPD) installed on a premises wiring system must be:","o":["A listed device","Field-tested to 25 ohms","Installed only by the utility","Rated at least 100 kA"],"a":0,"e":"SPDs must be listed. Content of former Article 285 is located in Article 242 of the 2023 NEC.","r":"NEC 242.6","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":1,"q":"An SPD must be marked with which of the following?","o":["The utility service voltage","A short-circuit current rating","The wire gauge of the lead conductors only","The name of the installer"],"a":1,"e":"SPDs must be marked with a short-circuit current rating and cannot be installed where available fault current exceeds that rating.","r":"NEC 242.8","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":2,"q":"Which SPD is exempt from the short-circuit current rating marking requirement?","o":["Surge arresters on a 12-kV line","Type 2 SPDs in panelboards","Receptacles incorporating SPD protection","Type 1 SPDs at service equipment"],"a":2,"e":"The short-circuit current rating marking requirement does not apply to receptacles.","r":"NEC 242.8","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":3,"q":"An SPD marked with a 20-kA short-circuit current rating is proposed for a panelboard where the available fault current is 35 kA. Is this installation permitted?","o":["Yes, if installed outdoors","No, because the SPD may not be installed where the available fault current exceeds its short-circuit current rating","Yes, because the SPD rating is only for lightning surges","Yes, if the leads are kept short"],"a":1,"e":"An SPD may not be installed at a point where the available fault current exceeds its marked short-circuit current rating.","r":"NEC 242.8","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":1,"q":"SPDs under the 1000-volt-or-less rules are not permitted on which type of system unless specifically listed for it?","o":["Ungrounded systems","Single-phase 120/240-volt systems","Systems with a grounded neutral at the service","Solidly grounded wye systems"],"a":0,"e":"SPDs are not permitted on ungrounded, impedance-grounded, or corner-grounded delta systems unless listed for the use.","r":"NEC 242.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":3,"q":"A 240-volt, three-phase corner-grounded delta system needs surge protection. An SPD is available that is not listed for corner-grounded delta systems. May it be installed?","o":["Yes, if it is installed outdoors","Yes, if it is rated for 600 volts","Yes, if it is a Type 2","No, SPDs are not permitted on corner-grounded delta systems unless listed for that use"],"a":3,"e":"Listing for the specific system configuration is required for corner-grounded delta systems.","r":"NEC 242.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":3,"q":"A 480Y/277-volt solidly grounded system needs an SPD. A candidate SPD has a maximum continuous operating voltage rating of 150 volts line-to-ground. Is this device acceptable?","o":["Yes, if it is a Type 3 only","Yes, because 150 volts is the 120-volt standard","No, because the SPD voltage rating is lower than the maximum continuous phase-to-ground voltage of 277 volts","Yes, because it is a listed SPD"],"a":2,"e":"An SPD cannot be used where its voltage rating is less than the maximum continuous phase-to-ground power frequency voltage at the point of application.","r":"NEC 242.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":2,"q":"Surge-protective devices under the 1000-volt-or-less provisions are not permitted on circuits of what voltage?","o":["Over 250 volts","Over 1000 volts","Over 120 volts","Over 600 volts"],"a":1,"e":"SPD rules apply to systems of 1000 V or less. Systems over 1 kV use surge arresters.","r":"NEC 242.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":1,"q":"A Type 1 SPD is permitted to be installed:","o":["Between the secondary of the service transformer and the line side of the service disconnect overcurrent device","Only on the load side of branch-circuit protection","Only inside plug-in strips","Only at the point of utilization"],"a":0,"e":"Type 1 SPDs are permanently connected devices installed on the supply side of the service disconnect, and may also be installed on the load side.","r":"NEC 242.13","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":2,"q":"A Type 1 SPD is listed to be installed without:","o":["A short-circuit current rating","An external overcurrent protective device","A listing","Grounding"],"a":1,"e":"A Type 1 SPD is intended to be installed without an external overcurrent protective device.","r":"NEC 242.13; NEC Article 100 (Surge-Protective Device Type 1)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":2,"q":"Where is a Type 1 SPD permitted to be connected in relation to the service disconnect?","o":["On the supply side of the service disconnect (as allowed by 230.82)","Only on the load side of a branch-circuit breaker","Only at a receptacle","Only inside a luminaire"],"a":0,"e":"Type 1 SPDs may be connected on the supply side of the service disconnecting means, as permitted by 230.82.","r":"NEC 242.13(A); NEC 230.82","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":1,"q":"Where must a Type 2 SPD be installed in a service-supplied building?","o":["On the line side of the service disconnect only","Anywhere on the load side of the service disconnect overcurrent device","Only at the point of utilization","Only at the utility transformer"],"a":1,"e":"Type 2 SPDs are installed on the load side of the service disconnect overcurrent device, including at branch panelboards.","r":"NEC 242.14","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":2,"q":"A building is fed by a feeder from another building. Where may a Type 2 SPD be connected at the second building?","o":["Anywhere on the load side of the first overcurrent device at that building","Only outside the building","Only on the line side of the first overcurrent device","Only at the receptacle outlets"],"a":0,"e":"In a feeder-supplied building a Type 2 SPD may be connected anywhere on the load side of the first overcurrent device at the building.","r":"NEC 242.14","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":3,"q":"A dwelling's panelboard is to be protected with an SPD installed inside the panelboard on the load side of the main breaker. Which type of SPD fits this installation?","o":["Type 5","Type 2","Type 3","Type 4"],"a":1,"e":"Type 2 SPDs are permanently connected devices installed on the load side of the service disconnect overcurrent device, including at branch panels.","r":"NEC 242.14","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":1,"q":"A Type 3 SPD is installed:","o":["Only at the utility pole","On the line side of the service","Between the service transformer and the meter","At the point of utilization, on the load side of branch-circuit overcurrent protection"],"a":3,"e":"Type 3 SPDs are point-of-utilization devices such as cord-connected, direct plug-in, and receptacle-type SPDs.","r":"NEC 242.16","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":2,"q":"Who is permitted to install a Type 4 component assembly SPD?","o":["Any licensed electrician at a panelboard","The equipment manufacturer only, within listed equipment","Any homeowner","The utility only"],"a":1,"e":"Type 4 and other component SPDs may be installed only by the equipment manufacturer.","r":"NEC 242.18","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":2,"q":"Where may an SPD be located?","o":["Outdoors only","Indoors only","Indoors or outdoors, but inaccessible to unqualified persons unless listed for installation in accessible locations","Only in hazardous (classified) locations"],"a":2,"e":"SPDs may be indoors or outdoors, but must be made inaccessible to unqualified persons unless listed for accessible installation.","r":"NEC 242.22","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":1,"q":"How should the conductors connecting an SPD to the line or bus and to ground be routed?","o":["As long as possible to dissipate the surge","As short as practical, avoiding unnecessary bends","Coiled to add inductance","In a ferrous conduit with sharp bends"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors must be no longer than necessary and avoid unnecessary bends to keep the clamping voltage low.","r":"NEC 242.24","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size copper line and grounding conductor for connecting an SPD?","o":["16 AWG","14 AWG","10 AWG","12 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"SPD line and grounding conductors must be at least 14 AWG copper or 12 AWG aluminum or copper-clad aluminum.","r":"NEC 242.28","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum size aluminum line and grounding conductor for connecting an SPD?","o":["8 AWG","14 AWG","12 AWG","10 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"The minimum is 14 AWG copper or 12 AWG aluminum or copper-clad aluminum.","r":"NEC 242.28","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":2,"q":"Between which conductors is an SPD permitted to be connected?","o":["Only between the neutral and the grounding electrode conductor","Only between ungrounded conductors","Only between a phase conductor and the grounded conductor","Any two conductors: ungrounded, grounded, equipment grounding, or grounding electrode conductor"],"a":3,"e":"SPDs may be connected between any two conductors, as long as the grounded conductor and equipment grounding conductor are bonded together only by the normal bonding jumper at the service or separately derived system.","r":"NEC 242.30","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":2,"q":"Which description best fits a Type 4 SPD?","o":["A device installed in a meter socket only","A component assembly consisting of one or more Type 5 components with a disconnect, or a means of complying with the limited current tests","A device installed ahead of the service disconnect","A receptacle-type SPD at the point of utilization"],"a":1,"e":"Type 4 SPDs are component assemblies; Type 5 SPDs are discrete components such as MOVs.","r":"NEC 242.2; NEC Article 100","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":1,"q":"What is the function of a surge-protective device?","o":["To regulate the steady-state voltage","To interrupt overcurrent permanently","To limit transient voltages by diverting or limiting surge current while remaining capable of repeating this function","To provide a ground-fault path"],"a":2,"e":"An SPD limits transient voltage surges by diverting or limiting surge current and prevents continued flow of follow current.","r":"NEC Article 100 definition; NEC 242.2","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum size conductor connecting a surge arrester (over 1 kV) to the line and ground?","o":["14 AWG copper","10 AWG copper","6 AWG copper or aluminum","12 AWG aluminum"],"a":2,"e":"Surge arrester conductors must be at least 6 AWG copper or aluminum, unlike low-voltage SPD leads (14 AWG copper).","r":"NEC 242.52","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 242/285 Overvoltage and surge protection","d":3,"q":"A 480-volt to 208Y/120-volt transformer feeds a panelboard as a separately derived system. Where may a Type 2 SPD be connected?","o":["Only at the receptacles","Only at the utility service","Only on the primary side of the transformer","Anywhere on the load side of the first overcurrent device of the separately derived system"],"a":3,"e":"For separately derived systems, Type 2 SPDs are connected on the load side of the first overcurrent device.","r":"NEC 242.14","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":1,"q":"Where must all conductors of a circuit, including the grounded and equipment grounding conductors, be installed?","o":["In the same raceway, cable, trench, cord, or cable tray, with limited exceptions","In separate raceways to reduce heating","Only in a metal raceway","In any raceway of the same building"],"a":0,"e":"Conductors of the same circuit are installed together to limit inductive heating and keep impedance low for fault current.","r":"NEC 300.3(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"120-volt and 277-volt circuits are installed in the same conduit. What is required of the insulation?","o":["Nothing, as long as all are THHN","Each conductor needs only its own circuit voltage insulation rating","The 120-volt conductors must be 300-volt rated","All conductors must have an insulation rating at least equal to the maximum circuit voltage applied to any conductor in the enclosure"],"a":3,"e":"Circuits of 1000 V or less may share a raceway or enclosure if all conductors are insulated for at least the maximum voltage applied to any conductor within.","r":"NEC 300.3(C)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"In general, conductors over 1000 volts may occupy the same raceway as conductors of 1000 volts or less:","o":["Always, in dry locations","Not unless specific exceptions apply","Always, if insulated for 5 kV","Always, if in rigid metal conduit"],"a":1,"e":"Primary conductors over 1000 volts may not occupy the same equipment wiring enclosure, cable, or raceway as conductors of 1000 volts or less, with limited exceptions.","r":"NEC 300.3(C)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"A cable is installed through holes bored in wood studs and the holes are 1 in from the nearest edge of the stud. What is required for protection?","o":["A 1/32 in plastic sleeve","A steel plate or sleeve at least 1/16 in thick must protect the cable","Nothing, because holes are protected by the stud","Nothing, because it is below 5 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Where holes are less than 1-1/4 in from the nearest edge of the framing member, protection by a steel plate or sleeve at least 1/16 in thick is required.","r":"NEC 300.4(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":1,"q":"What minimum distance is required from the nearest edge of a wood stud to a bored hole for a nonmetallic-sheathed cable, without additional protection?","o":["1-1/4 in","2 in","1 in","3/4 in"],"a":0,"e":"Holes must be at least 1-1/4 in from the nearest edge of the framing member, or the cable must be protected by a 1/16 in steel plate or sleeve.","r":"NEC 300.4(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"A cable is laid in a notch cut into a wood stud. What protection is required?","o":["A 1/32 in sleeve","A nail plate on one side only","No protection if the notch is deeper than 1 in","A steel plate at least 1/16 in thick, installed before the finish is applied"],"a":3,"e":"Where notches are cut in wood framing, the cable must be protected by a steel plate, sleeve, or equivalent at least 1/16 in thick.","r":"NEC 300.4(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Nonmetallic-sheathed cable is run through factory-punched openings in metal studs. What is required?","o":["Listed bushings or grommets covering all metal edges, securely fastened in the opening prior to installing the cable","Duct tape around the cable","No protection, because the studs are smooth","A steel plate on the outside of the stud"],"a":0,"e":"Cable wiring methods through metal framing must be protected by listed bushings or grommets covering all metal edges.","r":"NEC 300.4(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"A cable is run parallel to wood framing and installed in a bored hole within the stud. How far must it be from the nearest edge of the framing member without extra protection?","o":["At least 2 in","At least 1-1/4 in","At least 6 in","At least 3/4 in"],"a":1,"e":"Cables or raceways parallel to framing members must be at least 1-1/4 in from the nearest edge or protected by a 1/16 in steel plate.","r":"NEC 300.4(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Type MC cable is installed below metal-corrugated sheet roof decking. What is the minimum distance from the lowest surface of the roof decking to the top of the cable?","o":["1-1/2 in","1 in","2-1/2 in","3/4 in"],"a":0,"e":"Cables and raceways installed in or under roof decking must be at least 1-1/2 in from the lowest surface of the decking to the top of the cable or raceway to protect from roof screws and fasteners.","r":"NEC 300.4(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"A cable is installed in a shallow groove cut in a wall that will be covered by wallboard. How must it be protected?","o":["No protection is needed","By a 1/16 in steel plate, sleeve, or equivalent, or at least 1-1/4 in of free space for the length of the groove","By a coat of paint","By a 1/8 in plastic cover"],"a":1,"e":"Cable or raceway wiring in a groove to be covered by finish materials must be protected by a 1/16 in steel plate or sleeve or have at least 1-1/4 in free space.","r":"NEC 300.4(F)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Insulated 2 AWG conductors in a conduit enter a cabinet. What protection is required at the entry?","o":["A plastic wire nut","A hole saw cut smoothed with a file","A fitting with a smooth, rounded insulating surface (insulated bushing), unless separated from the fitting by substantial insulating material","None, because 2 AWG is a large conductor"],"a":2,"e":"Where raceways contain 4 AWG or larger insulated conductors entering a cabinet, box, or enclosure, the conductors must be protected by a substantial fitting that provides a smooth rounded insulating surface.","r":"NEC 300.4(G)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"A raceway crosses a structural joint intended for expansion, contraction, or deflection. What is required?","o":["A listed expansion/deflection fitting or other approved means","Nothing special","A rigid coupling","A threadless set-screw coupling"],"a":0,"e":"Structural joints require an expansion, expansion-deflection, or deflection fitting or other approved means.","r":"NEC 300.4(H)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"How is 'cover' for underground installations measured?","o":["The shortest distance from the top surface of the direct-buried conductor, cable, conduit, or raceway to the top surface of finished grade, concrete, or similar cover","From the bottom of the trench to the finished grade","From the top of the backfill before finishing","From the center of the raceway to grade"],"a":0,"e":"Cover is measured from the top of the cable or raceway to the top surface of finished grade, concrete, or similar cover.","r":"NEC 300.5(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum cover for direct-buried Type UF cable (not under a building, street, or driveway, and not a GFCI-protected residential circuit)?","o":["12 in","6 in","18 in","24 in"],"a":3,"e":"Table 300.5 requires 24 in of cover for direct-buried cables in general locations.","r":"NEC 300.5(A), Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum cover for Schedule 80 PVC conduit buried outdoors in a general location (no concrete encasement)?","o":["6 in","12 in","18 in","24 in"],"a":2,"e":"Table 300.5 requires 18 in of cover for nonmetallic raceways listed for direct burial without concrete encasement.","r":"NEC 300.5(A), Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum cover for rigid metal conduit (RMC) buried in a general outdoor location?","o":["24 in","6 in","18 in","12 in"],"a":1,"e":"Table 300.5 requires 6 in of cover for rigid metal conduit or intermediate metal conduit in general locations.","r":"NEC 300.5(A), Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"A 120-volt, 20-ampere residential branch circuit with GFCI protection is run in direct-buried Type UF cable to a detached shed. What is the minimum cover?","o":["18 in","6 in","24 in","12 in"],"a":3,"e":"Table 300.5 permits 12 in of cover for residential branch circuits rated 120 V or less with GFCI protection and maximum 20 A overcurrent protection.","r":"NEC 300.5(A), Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"A 120-volt, 20-ampere branch circuit with no GFCI protection is run in direct-buried Type UF cable to an outbuilding. What is the minimum cover?","o":["12 in","24 in","18 in","6 in"],"a":1,"e":"The 12 in reduction applies only if GFCI protection is provided; otherwise the 24 in direct-burial cover applies.","r":"NEC 300.5(A), Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"A raceway is buried under a street, highway, road, alley, driveway, or parking lot. What is the typical minimum cover?","o":["12 in","18 in","6 in","24 in"],"a":3,"e":"Table 300.5 requires 24 in of cover under streets, highways, roads, alleys, driveways, and parking lots for the listed methods.","r":"NEC 300.5(A), Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Schedule 40 PVC conduit is buried under a one-family dwelling's driveway used only for dwelling-related purposes. What is the minimum cover?","o":["24 in","18 in","12 in","6 in"],"a":1,"e":"Table 300.5 allows 18 in of cover for nonmetallic raceways under one- and two-family dwelling driveways and outdoor parking areas used only for dwelling-related purposes.","r":"NEC 300.5(A), Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"A 24-volt landscape lighting circuit limited to not more than 30 volts is run in Type UF cable direct buried. What is the minimum cover?","o":["6 in","24 in","18 in","12 in"],"a":0,"e":"Table 300.5 permits 6 in for irrigation and landscape lighting control circuits limited to 30 V installed with Type UF or other identified cable or raceway.","r":"NEC 300.5(A), Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":1,"q":"The interior of enclosures or raceways installed underground is considered to be:","o":["An unclassified indoor location","A wet location","A damp location","A dry location"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors in underground raceways must be listed for wet locations (such as type THWN or XHHW).","r":"NEC 300.5(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Type UF cable is installed under a building. What is required?","o":["It must be buried 6 in deep","Nothing special is required","Direct burial is permitted with 24 in of cover","It must be in a raceway that extends beyond the outside walls of the building"],"a":3,"e":"Underground cable installed under a building must be in a raceway extending past the outside walls (MC cable listed for direct burial has a limited exception).","r":"NEC 300.5(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Direct-buried cable emerges from grade near a building. How far must it be protected by a raceway or enclosure?","o":["Up to 2 ft above grade","From the minimum required cover below grade up to 8 ft above finished grade, but not required to exceed 18 in below grade","Up to 12 ft above grade","Up to 4 ft above grade"],"a":1,"e":"Direct-buried conductors emerging from grade must be protected from the minimum cover depth to at least 8 ft above grade, but protection need not extend more than 18 in below finished grade.","r":"NEC 300.5(D)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"May a splice be made in a direct-buried cable without a splice box?","o":["Yes, with wire nuts rated for dry locations","Yes, with tape only","No, a box is always required","Yes, if the splice uses materials listed for direct burial"],"a":3,"e":"Direct-buried conductors and cables can be spliced and tapped without a splice box using methods and materials listed for direct burial.","r":"NEC 300.5(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Backfill for an underground raceway trench contains large rocks and cinders. What does the NEC say?","o":["It is acceptable if the conduit is PVC","It is acceptable if the cable is Type UF","It is acceptable if the trench is under 12 in deep","Such backfill must not be placed where it could damage the raceway or cable; protection or selected fill is required"],"a":3,"e":"Backfill that could damage raceways, cables, or other substructures or prevent adequate compaction must not be used; protection such as granular or selected material is required.","r":"NEC 300.5(F)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"An underground raceway crosses ground that may move from frost or settlement. What does the NEC require?","o":["A shallower trench","Nothing special","A direct-buried splice","The installation must be arranged to prevent damage, for example with expansion/deflection fittings"],"a":3,"e":"Underground installations subject to movement by settlement or frost must be arranged to prevent damage to the enclosed conductors or equipment.","r":"NEC 300.5(J)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":1,"q":"Cables or raceways installed using directional boring must be:","o":["Metal only","Approved for the purpose","Limited to 10 ft","Installed with a copper strip"],"a":1,"e":"Directional boring installations require cables or raceways approved for the purpose.","r":"NEC 300.5(K)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Aluminum raceways or equipment embedded in concrete or in direct contact with earth must have:","o":["No protection because aluminum does not corrode","Supplementary corrosion protection","Insulating tape only","A copper wrap"],"a":1,"e":"Aluminum metal equipment or raceways embedded in masonry or concrete or in direct contact with earth require supplementary corrosion protection.","r":"NEC 300.6(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"In an indoor wet location such as a dairy processing area, how must exposed metal conduit systems be mounted?","o":["With a 2 in airspace","With at least 1/4 in airspace between the raceway and the wall or supporting surface, using corrosion-resistant supports","With silicone caulk against the wall","Flush against the wall"],"a":1,"e":"In such locations the raceway system must have at least 1/4 in airspace from the wall and be supported with corrosion-resistant hardware.","r":"NEC 300.6(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"A raceway passes between a heated area and a cold storage freezer. What does the NEC require?","o":["It must be sealed (e.g., with an approved material) to prevent the circulation of warm air into the colder section","A plastic bushing only","Nothing, because condensation is normal","A larger raceway"],"a":0,"e":"Where a raceway or sleeve is exposed to widely different temperatures, it must be sealed to prevent circulation of warm air to a colder section.","r":"NEC 300.7(A)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Where must expansion fittings be used in a raceway?","o":["Never, in dry locations","Every 10 ft","Where necessary to compensate for thermal expansion and contraction","Only in underground installations"],"a":2,"e":"Expansion fittings are required wherever thermal expansion and contraction are likely to be a problem.","r":"NEC 300.7(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":1,"q":"Raceways containing electrical conductors may not also contain:","o":["Neutral conductors","Equipment grounding conductors","More than one circuit","Any pipe or tube for steam, water, air, gas, drainage, or other nonelectrical service"],"a":3,"e":"Raceways and cable trays containing electrical conductors must not contain pipes, tubes, or equivalent for non-electrical services.","r":"NEC 300.8","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Conductors are installed in rigid metal conduit exposed to weather above grade. What must be true of the conductors?","o":["They must be solid copper","They must be listed for use in wet locations, since the interior of the raceway is a wet location","Dry-location conductors are fine because the conduit is sealed","Any insulation is acceptable"],"a":1,"e":"The interior of raceways installed in wet locations above grade is considered a wet location, and insulated conductors must comply with 310.10(C) (e.g., THWN, XHHW).","r":"NEC 300.9","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":1,"q":"What is required of metal raceways, cable armor, and other metal enclosures for conductors?","o":["They need only be continuous at the panel","They may be isolated if painted","They must be metallically joined together into a continuous electrical conductor and connected to boxes, fittings, and cabinets","They must be insulated at each joint"],"a":2,"e":"Electrical continuity of metal raceways and enclosures is required to provide an effective fault-current path.","r":"NEC 300.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Can a raceway or cable be supported by the ceiling grid support wires in a suspended ceiling?","o":["Yes, with Type MC only","Yes, if the cable is under 20 lb","Yes, if tie-wrapped","No; raceways and cables must not be supported by ceiling grids. Separate independent supports are required"],"a":3,"e":"Cables and raceways cannot be supported by the ceiling grid. Independent support wires may be used if secured at both ends and distinguishable.","r":"NEC 300.11(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Under what condition may a raceway be used as a means of support for other raceways or cables?","o":["Whenever the other cable is under 15 amperes","Where the raceway or support means is identified for the purpose","Whenever it is metallic","Whenever it is within 2 ft of a box"],"a":1,"e":"A raceway may support other raceways or cables only where identified as a means of support, or in narrow exceptions such as Class 2 control cables to equipment.","r":"NEC 300.11(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":1,"q":"What does 300.12 require of metal or nonmetallic raceways, cable armors, and cable sheaths?","o":["They may be interrupted every 10 ft","They must be insulated from enclosures","They must be grounded at both ends only","They must be continuous between cabinets, boxes, fittings, or other enclosures or outlets"],"a":3,"e":"Mechanical continuity of the wiring method between boxes and enclosures is required.","r":"NEC 300.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"On a multiwire branch circuit, what rule applies to the grounded (neutral) conductor at devices such as receptacles?","o":["It may be omitted at devices","It may pass through the device terminals in series","It may be spliced with a twist only","Continuity of the grounded conductor must not depend on the device connection; removing a device must not interrupt it"],"a":3,"e":"On multiwire branch circuits, the grounded conductor must not depend on a device connection, so removing a receptacle cannot open the neutral for other devices downstream.","r":"NEC 300.13(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum length of free conductor at outlet, junction, and switch points for splices or terminations?","o":["4 in","12 in","3 in","6 in, measured from the point in the box where the conductor emerges from its cable sheath or raceway"],"a":3,"e":"At least 6 in of free conductor is required, and where the box opening is less than 8 in in any dimension, each conductor must extend at least 3 in outside the opening.","r":"NEC 300.14","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"A box opening is 6 in in its largest dimension. What is the minimum length conductors must extend outside the box opening?","o":["3 in","1 in","8 in","6 in"],"a":0,"e":"Where the opening to an outlet, junction, or switch point is less than 8 in in any dimension, each conductor must be long enough to extend at least 3 in outside the opening.","r":"NEC 300.14","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Where is a box or conduit body required in a raceway or cable wiring system?","o":["Only at the beginning of the run","At each conductor splice point, outlet, switch point, junction point, termination point, or pull point","Only at outlets","Only at splice points"],"a":1,"e":"A box or conduit body is required at each splice, outlet, switch, junction, termination, or pull point unless otherwise permitted.","r":"NEC 300.15","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum conduit fill when installing three or more conductors in a raceway?","o":["60 percent","31 percent","40 percent of the cross-sectional area","53 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Chapter 9 Table 1 limits fill to 40 percent for more than two conductors, 31 percent for two, and 53 percent for one. Raceways must permit installation and withdrawal without damage.","r":"NEC 300.17; Chapter 9 Table 1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"When must a raceway system be installed relative to conductor installation?","o":["Complete runs of raceway must be installed before conductors are pulled in, except where specifically permitted","Only after the walls are finished","Conductors must be installed first","Only in underground installations"],"a":0,"e":"Raceway runs are completed before conductors are installed, except where permitted by 376.56 and 378.56.","r":"NEC 300.18(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"May metal raceways be supported or terminated by welding to the raceway?","o":["No, unless specifically designed or otherwise permitted by the NEC","Yes, if the weld is 1 in long","Yes, for aluminum only","Yes, any metal raceway may be welded"],"a":0,"e":"Welding to metal raceways is prohibited unless the raceway is designed for it.","r":"NEC 300.18(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Why must all conductors of an ac circuit be installed together in the same ferrous metal raceway?","o":["To avoid heating the metal by induction","To simplify labeling","To reduce voltage drop","To increase ampacity"],"a":0,"e":"Arranging all phase, grounded, and equipment grounding conductors in the same raceway cancels magnetic fields and prevents induction heating of ferrous metal.","r":"NEC 300.20(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":3,"q":"Single conductors of an ac circuit pass through a steel plate (magnetic metal). How can the inductive effect be minimized?","o":["Insulate the plate edges only","Ground the plate at one end","Cut slots between the individual holes, or pass all circuit conductors through an insulating wall","Use a larger hole for each conductor"],"a":2,"e":"300.20(B) allows cutting slots between holes or passing all conductors of the circuit through an insulating wall large enough for all.","r":"NEC 300.20(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Cables pass through a fire-rated wall. What must be done to the opening around the penetration?","o":["It must be firestopped using approved methods to maintain the fire-resistance rating","No action is needed","It must be left open for ventilation","It must be sealed with silicone only"],"a":0,"e":"Openings around electrical penetrations through fire-resistant-rated assemblies must be firestopped with approved methods.","r":"NEC 300.21","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":1,"q":"Which wiring may be installed in a duct used for the removal of vapor from commercial cooking equipment?","o":["Mineral-insulated cable","Type MC cable","No wiring system of any type","Rigid metal conduit"],"a":2,"e":"No wiring system of any type may be installed in ducts used to transport dust, loose stock, or flammable vapors, or in ducts for commercial cooking vapor removal.","r":"NEC 300.22(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted in a duct specifically fabricated for environmental air handling?","o":["Type UF cable","Type NM cable","Type MI cable","Rigid PVC conduit"],"a":2,"e":"Only EMT, Type MI, Type MC without overall nonmetallic covering, Type AC, rigid metal conduit, or other specifically listed cable are permitted in such ducts.","r":"NEC 300.22(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":3,"q":"Flexible metal conduit is installed inside a fabricated environmental air duct to connect physically adjustable equipment. What is the maximum length?","o":["6 ft","2 ft","4 ft","10 ft"],"a":2,"e":"FMC and liquidtight FMC not exceeding 4 ft may be used within the duct to connect physically adjustable equipment and devices.","r":"NEC 300.22(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Cables and raceways behind suspended-ceiling panels designed to allow access must be:","o":["Hidden above the grid with no access","Arranged and secured so the panels can be removed and the equipment accessed","Installed only above 12 ft","Tie-wrapped to the grid"],"a":1,"e":"Wiring behind removable panels must be arranged and secured to allow removal of panels and access to equipment.","r":"NEC 300.23","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum cover for direct-buried cables operating at over 1000 volts in a general location?","o":["18 in","30 in","24 in","12 in"],"a":1,"e":"Table 300.50 requires 30 in of cover for direct-buried cables over 1000 V in general locations.","r":"NEC 300.50(A), Table 300.50","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum cover for rigid metal conduit used for underground installations over 1000 volts in a general location?","o":["6 in","12 in","24 in","18 in"],"a":0,"e":"Table 300.50 requires 6 in of cover for rigid metal conduit and intermediate metal conduit.","r":"NEC 300.50(A), Table 300.50","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":1,"q":"The allowable ampacities in Table 310.16 for conductors in raceway, cable, or earth are based on what ambient temperature, with not more than three current-carrying conductors?","o":["20 degrees C (68 degrees F)","40 degrees C (104 degrees F)","25 degrees C (77 degrees F)","30 degrees C (86 degrees F)"],"a":3,"e":"Table 310.16 is based on a 30 degree C ambient and no more than three current-carrying conductors. Other ambient temperatures need correction factors, and more conductors need adjustment factors.","r":"NEC Table 310.16; 310.14","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":1,"q":"What adjustment factor applies to the ampacity of conductors when 4 through 6 current-carrying conductors are installed in a single raceway?","o":["80 percent","60 percent","90 percent","70 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Table 310.15(C)(1) requires 80 percent for 4 to 6 current-carrying conductors. 70 percent applies to 7 to 9 conductors.","r":"NEC Table 310.15(C)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":1,"q":"What adjustment factor applies when 12 current-carrying conductors are installed in one raceway (no other correction needed)?","o":["40 percent","70 percent","50 percent","60 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Table 310.15(C)(1) gives 50 percent for 10 through 20 current-carrying conductors.","r":"NEC Table 310.15(C)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"A raceway contains the two ungrounded conductors and the neutral of a 120/240-volt, single-phase, 3-wire multiwire branch circuit serving incandescent and resistive loads. How many current-carrying conductors are counted for ampacity adjustment?","o":["3","4","2","1"],"a":2,"e":"The neutral that carries only the unbalanced current of the same circuit is not counted, so only the two ungrounded conductors count.","r":"NEC 310.15(E)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"A raceway contains three phase conductors and the neutral of a 3-phase, 4-wire wye system where the major portion of the load is nonlinear (electronic ballasts and power supplies). How many current-carrying conductors are counted for adjustment?","o":["3","5","2","4"],"a":3,"e":"On a 3-phase, 4-wire wye system with a major portion of nonlinear load, harmonic currents flow in the neutral, so the neutral is counted as a current-carrying conductor.","r":"NEC 310.15(E)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"A conduit contains three ungrounded phase conductors, a neutral that carries only unbalanced current of a 3-phase, 4-wire wye linear-load circuit, and an equipment grounding conductor. How many conductors are counted for ampacity adjustment?","o":["4","5","2","3"],"a":3,"e":"The neutral carrying only unbalanced current and the equipment grounding conductor are not counted. Only the three phase conductors are counted.","r":"NEC 310.15(E)(1), 310.15(F)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"Adjustment factors for more than three current-carrying conductors are NOT required to be applied to conductors installed in a raceway nipple of what maximum length?","o":["36 in.","12 in.","24 in.","48 in."],"a":2,"e":"Adjustment factors do not apply to conductors in raceway nipples not exceeding 600 mm (24 in.) in length.","r":"NEC 310.15(C)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":1,"q":"What is the smallest conductor size permitted to be installed in parallel for general power circuits (unless a specific exception applies)?","o":["4 AWG","2 AWG","1/0 AWG","3/0 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Paralleled conductors must be 1/0 AWG or larger, with limited exceptions such as certain control and DC applications.","r":"NEC 310.10(H)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"Two sets of 250 kcmil conductors are to be paralleled per phase. Which arrangement meets the requirements for the sets?","o":["One set copper and one set aluminum, both the same length","Both sets copper, same insulation type, same length, same termination method","One set 250 kcmil THWN and one set 300 kcmil THWN","Both sets copper, but one set 60 ft long and the other 75 ft long"],"a":1,"e":"Paralleled conductors must be the same length, same conductor material, same size in circular mil area, same insulation type, and terminated in the same manner.","r":"NEC 310.10(H)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":3,"q":"A 400-ampere feeder uses two parallel sets of conductors in two separate metal raceways. How must the equipment grounding conductor be installed?","o":["One 3 AWG copper equipment grounding conductor in only one of the raceways","No equipment grounding conductor, because the metal raceways serve as the sole path","A 6 AWG copper equipment grounding conductor in each raceway (half the size)","A full-size equipment grounding conductor sized from Table 250.122 for 400 A (3 AWG copper) in each raceway"],"a":3,"e":"Where conductors are paralleled in multiple raceways, an equipment grounding conductor is installed in each raceway, sized from Table 250.122 based on the overcurrent device rating.","r":"NEC 250.122(F); 310.10(H)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"Paralleled sets of conductors for a feeder are to be run in two raceways. Which requirement applies to the raceways?","o":["One may be EMT and the other PVC if both are the same trade size","They must be the same trade size but may contain different numbers of conductors","They may be of different lengths if the conductors have the same size","They must have the same physical characteristics and the same number of conductors"],"a":3,"e":"Conductors in parallel must be installed in raceways with the same physical characteristics (same material, length, and so on), and each raceway must contain the same number of conductors.","r":"NEC 310.10(H)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"Which conductor insulation type is suitable for installation in underground conduit, a wet location?","o":["THHN","THWN-2","TFN","XHH"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors in wet locations must be listed for wet use, such as THWN or THWN-2. THHN, XHH, and TFN are dry and damp location types only.","r":"NEC 310.10(C); Table 310.104(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum operating temperature of Type THWN-2 insulated conductors?","o":["90 degrees C, wet or dry","105 degrees C","75 degrees C wet, 90 degrees C dry","60 degrees C"],"a":0,"e":"THWN-2 is rated 90 degrees C in wet and dry locations. THWN (without -2) is rated 75 degrees C.","r":"NEC Table 310.104(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum operating temperature of Type TW insulated conductors?","o":["105 degrees C","90 degrees C","60 degrees C","75 degrees C"],"a":2,"e":"Type TW is rated 60 degrees C in wet and dry locations.","r":"NEC Table 310.104(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":1,"q":"When installed in raceways, conductors of what size and larger must be stranded unless specifically permitted otherwise?","o":["8 AWG","10 AWG","4 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"Conductors 8 AWG and larger in raceways must be stranded, unless specifically permitted or required elsewhere in the Code.","r":"NEC 310.3(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":1,"q":"What is the smallest aluminum conductor size permitted for general wiring under Article 310?","o":["8 AWG","10 AWG","14 AWG","12 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Table 310.106(A) sets 14 AWG as the minimum for copper and 12 AWG for aluminum and copper-clad aluminum.","r":"NEC 310.106(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"Which marking is permitted to identify a 10 AWG insulated grounded (neutral) conductor?","o":["A continuous gray outer finish","A continuous natural gray finish only at the terminations","A continuous green finish with a yellow stripe","White tape applied at the terminations at installation"],"a":0,"e":"Grounded conductors 6 AWG or smaller must be identified by a continuous white or gray outer finish or three continuous white stripes on other than green insulation. Marking at terminations is allowed only for 4 AWG and larger.","r":"NEC 200.6(A); 310.110(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"Which insulated grounded conductor may be identified at installation by distinctive white marking at its terminations?","o":["8 AWG","6 AWG","2 AWG","10 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Only insulated grounded conductors 4 AWG or larger may be identified by white or gray marking at the terminations at the time of installation.","r":"NEC 200.6(B); 310.110(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":1,"q":"Which identification is permitted for a 6 AWG insulated equipment grounding conductor?","o":["Continuous white","Three continuous white stripes","Continuous green, or green with one or more yellow stripes","Continuous gray"],"a":2,"e":"Insulated equipment grounding conductors are identified by green or green with yellow stripe(s). White and gray are reserved for grounded conductors.","r":"NEC 250.119(A); 310.110(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":1,"q":"Which of the following must be marked on the surface of insulated conductors under Article 310?","o":["Maximum rated voltage","Maximum ampacity at 30 degrees C","Name of the installing electrician","Date of installation"],"a":0,"e":"Required surface marking includes maximum rated voltage, type letter(s), manufacturer's name or trademark, and AWG size or circular mil area. Ampacity is not marked.","r":"NEC 310.120(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"Insulated conductors that are exposed to the direct rays of the sun must meet which requirement?","o":["Be listed or marked as sunlight resistant, or be covered with listed sunlight-resistant tape or sleeving","Be at least 6 AWG","Be rated 90 degrees C or higher","Be installed with a white outer finish only"],"a":0,"e":"Conductors and cables exposed to direct sunlight must be listed or marked sunlight resistant, or covered with insulating material such as tape or sleeving that is listed or identified as sunlight resistant.","r":"NEC 310.10(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"Which single-conductor type is suitable for direct burial in earth without a raceway?","o":["Type MTW","Type USE-2","Type THHN","Type TFFN"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors installed in direct burial must be listed for the purpose, such as Type USE. THHN, MTW, and TFFN are not listed for direct burial.","r":"NEC 310.10; 338.10(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"For a 120/240-volt, single-phase dwelling service of 100 A through 400 A that supplies the entire load, 310.12 permits the conductor ampacity to be not less than what percentage of the service rating?","o":["83 percent","75 percent","80 percent","90 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Section 310.12 allows service and main power feeder conductors for single-phase dwelling services to have an ampacity of not less than 83 percent of the service rating.","r":"NEC 310.12","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":3,"q":"A single-family dwelling has a 200-ampere, 120/240-volt, single-phase service supplying the entire load. Using 310.12 and the 75 degree C column of Table 310.16, what is the smallest copper conductor size permitted for the service conductors?","o":["1/0 AWG","2/0 AWG","2 AWG","3/0 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"83 percent of 200 A is 166 A. In the 75 degree C copper column, 1/0 AWG is 150 A (too low) and 2/0 AWG is 175 A, which is the smallest that meets 166 A.","r":"NEC 310.12; Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":3,"q":"A 50-ft circuit uses conductors that have a lower ampacity for an 8-ft section where they pass through a hot area. May the higher ampacity of the rest of the circuit be used for the entire circuit length?","o":["No, because the lowest ampacity may never be exceeded for any length","Yes, because the section is less than 10 ft","No, because the 8-ft section is more than the lesser of 10 ft or 10 percent of the circuit length (5 ft)","Yes, because the section is less than 20 percent of the circuit length"],"a":2,"e":"The exception allows the higher ampacity only if the lower-ampacity length does not exceed the lesser of 10 ft or 10 percent of the circuit length. Ten percent of 50 ft is 5 ft, and 8 ft exceeds it.","r":"NEC 310.14(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"What ambient temperature correction factor from Table 310.15(B)(1) applies to conductors with 90 degree C insulation in a 40 degree C (104 degree F) ambient (36 to 40 degree C range)?","o":["0.88","0.82","0.91","0.96"],"a":2,"e":"For the 36 to 40 degree C ambient range the 90 degree C insulation column factor is 0.91. The 75 degree C factor is 0.88 and the 60 degree C factor is 0.82.","r":"NEC Table 310.15(B)(1)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":1,"q":"What is the ampacity of a 3 AWG copper conductor in the 75 degree C column of Table 310.16?","o":["110 A","85 A","115 A","100 A"],"a":3,"e":"Table 310.16 lists 3 AWG copper at 100 A in the 75 degree C column. 115 A is 2 AWG, 85 A is 4 AWG.","r":"NEC Table 310.16","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"What is the ampacity of a 1/0 AWG aluminum conductor in the 75 degree C column of Table 310.16?","o":["150 A","135 A","100 A","120 A"],"a":3,"e":"Table 310.16 lists 1/0 AWG aluminum at 120 A in the 75 degree C column. 150 A is the copper value.","r":"NEC Table 310.16","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"What is the ampacity of a 4 AWG copper conductor in the 90 degree C column of Table 310.16?","o":["85 A","95 A","75 A","100 A"],"a":1,"e":"Table 310.16 lists 4 AWG copper at 95 A in the 90 degree C column and 85 A in the 75 degree C column.","r":"NEC Table 310.16","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":1,"q":"Part III of Article 310 covers conductors rated within what voltage range?","o":["1000 volts through 69,000 volts","5000 volts through 46,000 volts","2001 volts through 35,000 volts","601 volts through 2000 volts"],"a":2,"e":"Parts I and II cover conductors rated up to 2000 volts, and Part III covers medium voltage conductors rated 2001 volts to 35,000 volts.","r":"NEC 310.1; Part III Article 310","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum operating temperature of Type THHW insulation in a wet location?","o":["90 degrees C","75 degrees C","60 degrees C","105 degrees C"],"a":1,"e":"Type THHW is rated 75 degrees C in wet locations and 90 degrees C in dry locations.","r":"NEC Table 310.104(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"A 6 AWG copper THHN conductor terminates on a 60-ampere circuit breaker whose terminals are not marked for a higher temperature rating. What ampacity must be used for the conductor at this termination?","o":["65 A","55 A","75 A","60 A"],"a":1,"e":"For circuits of 100 A or less, termination provisions are presumed to be 60 degrees C unless marked otherwise. 6 AWG copper in the 60 degree C column is 55 A.","r":"NEC 110.14(C)(1); 310.14(A)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum operating temperature of Type XHHW-2 insulation in a wet location?","o":["75 degrees C","105 degrees C","90 degrees C","60 degrees C"],"a":2,"e":"XHHW-2 is rated 90 degrees C in wet and dry locations.","r":"NEC Table 310.104(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum overcurrent protection for 10 AWG copper conductors supplying a general (non-motor) branch circuit, per the small-conductor rule?","o":["30 A","25 A","35 A","40 A"],"a":0,"e":"Section 240.4(D) limits 10 AWG copper to 30 A overcurrent protection regardless of the higher table ampacity.","r":"NEC 240.4(D); 310.16","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":1,"q":"What adjustment factor applies when 8 current-carrying conductors are installed in one raceway?","o":["80 percent","70 percent","50 percent","60 percent"],"a":1,"e":"Table 310.15(C)(1) lists 70 percent for 7 through 9 current-carrying conductors.","r":"NEC Table 310.15(C)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"Where insulated conductors are exposed to oils, greases, vapors, gases, fumes, or liquids having a deleterious effect on the insulation, what is required?","o":["Conductors rated 600 volts or higher","Conductors of a type suitable for the exposure","Conductors of at least 6 AWG","Conductors installed in cable tray only"],"a":1,"e":"Insulation must be suitable for the conditions of the location, with a type listed or identified as suitable for the exposure.","r":"NEC 310.10","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":1,"q":"Which enclosure is defined as designed for surface or flush mounting with a frame, mat, or trim in which a swinging door or doors are or can be hung?","o":["Junction box","Meter socket enclosure","Cabinet","Cutout box"],"a":2,"e":"A cabinet has a frame, mat, or trim for hanging swinging doors and can be surface or flush mounted. A cutout box is surface mounted only.","r":"NEC Art. 100 (Cabinet); 312.1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":1,"q":"Which enclosure is defined as designed for surface mounting with swinging doors or covers secured directly to and telescoping with the walls of the box proper?","o":["Cabinet","Device box","Pull box","Cutout box"],"a":3,"e":"The definition of cutout box includes doors or covers that telescope with the box walls, and it is designed for surface mounting only.","r":"NEC Art. 100 (Cutout Box); 312.1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":1,"q":"A surface-mounted metal cabinet is installed in a wet location. What minimum airspace is required between the enclosure and the wall or supporting surface?","o":["1/2 in.","1/4 in.","1/8 in.","1 in."],"a":1,"e":"Surface-type enclosures in damp or wet locations must be mounted with at least 6 mm (1/4 in.) airspace between the enclosure and the supporting surface.","r":"NEC 312.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":2,"q":"Which enclosure is permitted to be mounted in a wet location directly against a concrete or masonry wall without the 1/4 in. airspace?","o":["A nonmetallic enclosure","A metal enclosure painted with rust-resistant paint","A metal enclosure with a gasketed cover","Any listed Type 3R enclosure"],"a":0,"e":"The exception allows nonmetallic enclosures to be installed without the airspace on a concrete, masonry, tile, or similar surface.","r":"NEC 312.2 Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":2,"q":"A raceway enters the top of a meter socket enclosure installed in a wet location, above the level of the uninsulated live parts. What is required at the entry?","o":["A grounding bushing","A fitting listed for wet locations on each conduit or cable","A concrete seal around the raceway","A standard locknut and bushing only"],"a":1,"e":"Raceways or cables entering above the level of uninsulated live parts in a wet location must use a fitting listed for wet locations for each conduit or cable.","r":"NEC 312.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":1,"q":"Which enclosure type is suitable for outdoor use to provide protection against rain, sleet, and ice formation, such as a typical outdoor meter socket?","o":["Type 1","Type 12","Type 2","Type 3R"],"a":3,"e":"Type 3R enclosures are intended for outdoor use against falling rain, sleet, and external ice formation. Type 1 is for indoor, nonhazardous use.","r":"NEC Table 110.28; 312.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":1,"q":"Enclosures within the scope of Article 312 that are installed in wet locations must be which of the following?","o":["Fire-resistive","Vapor-tight only","Explosion-proof","Weatherproof"],"a":3,"e":"Enclosures installed in wet locations must be weatherproof and prevent moisture from entering and accumulating inside.","r":"NEC 312.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":1,"q":"A flush-mounted cabinet is installed in a concrete wall. How far may the front edge be set back from the finished surface?","o":["Not more than 1/2 in.","Not more than 1/8 in.","Not more than 1/4 in.","Not more than 1 in."],"a":2,"e":"In walls of concrete, tile, or other noncombustible material, the front edge may be set back not more than 6 mm (1/4 in.) from the finished surface.","r":"NEC 312.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":1,"q":"A flush-mounted cabinet is installed in a wall of combustible material (wood framing with a wood finish). How must the front edge of the cabinet be positioned?","o":["Flush with the finished surface or projecting from it","Set back up to 1/8 in.","Set back up to 1/2 in.","Set back up to 1/4 in."],"a":0,"e":"In walls of combustible material, cabinets must be flush with the finished surface or project from it.","r":"NEC 312.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":1,"q":"Plaster or drywall around a flush-mounted cabinet is damaged. The surface must be repaired so there are no gaps or open spaces greater than what at the edge of the cabinet?","o":["1/8 in.","1/16 in.","1/4 in.","1/2 in."],"a":0,"e":"Gaps around flush-type cabinets must be repaired so that no gap exceeds 3 mm (1/8 in.) at the edge of the cabinet.","r":"NEC 312.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":1,"q":"What is required for unused openings in a cabinet, cutout box, or meter socket enclosure?","o":["They must be covered with electrical tape","They may remain open if located at the bottom","They may remain open if the enclosure is in a dry location","They must be effectively closed to afford protection substantially equivalent to the wall of the enclosure"],"a":3,"e":"Unused openings must be closed with listed plugs, knockout seals, or similar means that provide protection equivalent to the enclosure wall.","r":"NEC 312.5(A); 110.12(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":2,"q":"Nonmetallic-sheathed cable enters a metal cabinet through a knockout with a listed connector. How far must the cable assembly, including the sheath, extend into the enclosure?","o":["Not less than 1/2 in.","Not less than 1/4 in.","Not less than 1 in.","Flush with the inside of the enclosure"],"a":1,"e":"The nonmetallic sheath must extend into the cabinet not less than 6 mm (1/4 in.) through the knockout.","r":"NEC 312.5(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":1,"q":"Where cable is used to enter a cabinet, which general requirement applies?","o":["Cables must be secured only in dry locations","Cables must be secured only if larger than 6 AWG","Cables need not be secured if less than 6 ft long","Each cable must be secured to the cabinet"],"a":3,"e":"Each cable must be secured to the cabinet, cutout box, or meter socket enclosure, subject to the exception for cables entering through a raceway sleeve.","r":"NEC 312.5(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":2,"q":"Nonmetallic-sheathed cables enter the top of a surface-mounted cabinet through a nonflexible raceway sleeve instead of being secured to the cabinet. What is the permitted length of the sleeve?","o":["Not less than 18 in. and not more than 10 ft","Not less than 12 in. and not more than 6 ft","Not less than 6 in. and not more than 3 ft","Not less than 24 in. and not more than 25 ft"],"a":0,"e":"The exception requires a nonflexible raceway of not less than 450 mm (18 in.) and not more than 3 m (10 ft) in length, with other conditions met.","r":"NEC 312.5(C) Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":2,"q":"Under the exception to securing cables at a surface-mounted cabinet through a raceway sleeve, how close to the outer end of the raceway must each cable be fastened, measured along the sheath?","o":["Within 12 in.","Within 6 in.","Within 24 in.","Within 18 in."],"a":0,"e":"Each cable must be fastened within 300 mm (12 in.), measured along the sheath, of the outer end of the raceway.","r":"NEC 312.5(C) Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":3,"q":"For cables entering a surface-mounted cabinet through a raceway sleeve under the exception to 312.5(C), which condition applies to the raceway?","o":["It must be flexible metal conduit","It must be left open at the outer end for drainage","It extends directly above the enclosure, does not penetrate a structural ceiling, and is sealed or plugged at the outer end","It may enter from the bottom of the enclosure and penetrate a structural ceiling"],"a":2,"e":"The raceway must extend directly above the enclosure, must not penetrate a structural ceiling, and must be sealed or plugged at the outer end to prevent access to the enclosure.","r":"NEC 312.5(C) Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":3,"q":"A conduit sleeve is used under the exception to 312.5(C) to bring nonmetallic-sheathed cables into a cabinet. How is the cable fill in the sleeve limited?","o":["Fill may not exceed 25 percent","It may not exceed the fill permitted for complete conduit or tubing systems by Chapter 9, Table 1 and its notes","Fill may not exceed 60 percent","Fill is not limited because the sleeve is short"],"a":1,"e":"Where installed as conduit or tubing, cable fill may not exceed that permitted for complete conduit or tubing systems by Table 1 of Chapter 9 and its notes.","r":"NEC 312.5(C) Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":2,"q":"4 AWG insulated conductors in a conduit enter a metal cabinet. What protection is required at the entry?","o":["No additional protection if a locknut is installed","A fitting providing a smoothly rounded insulating surface, such as an insulating bushing","A grounding bushing only","A listed seal-off fitting"],"a":1,"e":"Insulated circuit conductors 4 AWG and larger entering a cabinet must be protected by a substantial fitting with a smoothly rounded insulating surface, unless separated by substantial insulating material.","r":"NEC 300.4(G); 312.5","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum wire-bending space at the terminals for one 4 AWG conductor per terminal in a cabinet, per Table 312.6(A)?","o":["3-1/2 in.","2 in.","1-1/2 in.","3 in."],"a":1,"e":"Table 312.6(A) requires 2 in. for 4 AWG and 3 AWG conductors with one conductor per terminal.","r":"NEC Table 312.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum wire-bending space at the terminals for one 1/0 AWG conductor per terminal in a cabinet, per Table 312.6(A)?","o":["3-1/2 in.","4 in.","3 in.","2 in."],"a":0,"e":"Table 312.6(A) requires 3-1/2 in. for 1/0 and 2/0 AWG conductors with one conductor per terminal.","r":"NEC Table 312.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum wire-bending space at the terminals for one 3/0 AWG conductor per terminal, per Table 312.6(A)?","o":["3 in.","3-1/2 in.","4 in.","5 in."],"a":2,"e":"Table 312.6(A) requires 4 in. for 3/0 and 4/0 AWG conductors with one conductor per terminal.","r":"NEC Table 312.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":2,"q":"An enclosure for switches or overcurrent devices is used to carry feed-through conductors to other equipment. Conductors may not fill the wiring space at any cross section to more than what percentage of that space?","o":["60 percent","40 percent","75 percent","20 percent"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors in the wiring space of switch or overcurrent device enclosures may not fill any cross section to more than 40 percent of its area.","r":"NEC 312.8(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":2,"q":"In the wiring space of a switch or overcurrent device enclosure, the total area of all conductors, splices, and taps at any cross section may not exceed what percentage of the cross-sectional area of that space?","o":["50 percent","60 percent","75 percent","40 percent"],"a":2,"e":"The total of conductors, splices, and taps is limited to 75 percent, while conductors alone are limited to 40 percent.","r":"NEC 312.8(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":1,"q":"Article 312 enclosures installed in damp or wet locations must be placed or equipped to do what?","o":["Maintain a 3 ft working clearance on all sides","Prevent moisture or water from entering and accumulating within the enclosure","Be painted a light color to reflect heat","Allow free ventilation through the bottom of the enclosure"],"a":1,"e":"Surface-type enclosures in damp or wet locations must prevent moisture or water from entering and accumulating inside.","r":"NEC 312.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":3,"q":"The wiring space of a switch enclosure used for feed-through conductors has a cross-sectional area of 20 sq in. at its narrowest point. What is the maximum cross-sectional area that the conductors may occupy at that section?","o":["10 sq in.","15 sq in.","12 sq in.","8 sq in."],"a":3,"e":"The 40 percent limit for conductors gives 0.40 x 20 = 8 sq in. The 75 percent limit (15 sq in.) applies only to splices and taps.","r":"NEC 312.8(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":1,"q":"What is the volume of a standard 4 in. x 1-1/4 in. deep round or octagonal metal box in Table 314.16(A)?","o":["18.0 cu in.","10.0 cu in.","12.5 cu in.","15.5 cu in."],"a":2,"e":"Table 314.16(A) lists 12.5 cu in. for a 4 x 1-1/4 in. round or octagonal box. The 1-1/2 in. deep box is 15.5 cu in.","r":"NEC Table 314.16(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":1,"q":"What is the volume of a standard 4 in. x 1-1/2 in. deep square metal box in Table 314.16(A)?","o":["25.5 cu in.","18.0 cu in.","21.0 cu in.","30.3 cu in."],"a":2,"e":"Table 314.16(A) lists 21.0 cu in. for a 4 x 1-1/2 in. square box. The 1-1/4 in. deep box is 18.0 cu in., and the 2-1/8 in. deep box is 30.3 cu in.","r":"NEC Table 314.16(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":1,"q":"What is the volume of a standard 3 in. x 2 in. x 2-1/2 in. deep device box in Table 314.16(A)?","o":["14.0 cu in.","10.0 cu in.","12.5 cu in.","18.0 cu in."],"a":2,"e":"Table 314.16(A) lists 12.5 cu in. for a 3 x 2 x 2-1/2 in. device box. A 2 in. deep box is 10.0 cu in., and a 2-3/4 in. deep box is 14.0 cu in.","r":"NEC Table 314.16(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":1,"q":"What volume allowance in Table 314.16(B) is required for each 14 AWG conductor counted in a box?","o":["2.00 cu in.","1.50 cu in.","2.50 cu in.","2.25 cu in."],"a":0,"e":"Table 314.16(B) gives 2.00 cu in. for 14 AWG, 2.25 cu in. for 12 AWG, and 2.50 cu in. for 10 AWG.","r":"NEC Table 314.16(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":1,"q":"What volume allowance in Table 314.16(B) is required for each 6 AWG conductor counted in a box?","o":["3.00 cu in.","2.50 cu in.","4.00 cu in.","5.00 cu in."],"a":3,"e":"Table 314.16(B) lists 5.00 cu in. for 6 AWG, 3.00 cu in. for 8 AWG, and 2.50 cu in. for 10 AWG.","r":"NEC Table 314.16(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"A box contains one single-pole switch (on one yoke) with 12 AWG conductors connected to it. How many 12 AWG volume allowances does the switch add to the box fill?","o":["One","Two","None, because the device is not a conductor","Three"],"a":1,"e":"Each yoke or strap containing devices requires a double volume allowance based on the largest conductor connected to the device.","r":"NEC 314.16(B)(4)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"A box contains three 14 AWG equipment grounding conductors and one 12 AWG equipment grounding conductor, all connected together. How is the box fill for the equipment grounding conductors counted?","o":["No allowance, because grounding conductors are exempt","One 14 AWG allowance (2.00 cu in.) for all of them","Four allowances of 14 AWG and 12 AWG","One 12 AWG allowance (2.25 cu in.) for all of them"],"a":3,"e":"All equipment grounding conductors together receive a single volume allowance based on the largest equipment grounding conductor present.","r":"NEC 314.16(B)(5)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"A box has internal cable clamps, and the largest conductor in the box is 12 AWG. How are the clamps counted for box fill?","o":["One 12 AWG allowance for each clamp","One 12 AWG allowance for all clamps together","One 14 AWG allowance for each clamp","They are not counted"],"a":1,"e":"One or more internal clamps are counted as a single volume allowance based on the largest conductor present. No allowance is needed for clamps outside the box.","r":"NEC 314.16(B)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"A 4 in. square x 1-1/2 in. deep metal box (21.0 cu in.) contains two 12/2 NM cables with ground (4 circuit conductors and 2 equipment grounding conductors), a single-pole switch, and no internal clamps. How many additional 12 AWG conductors could be added without exceeding the box volume?","o":["4","1","2","3"],"a":2,"e":"Count: 4 conductors + 1 EGC allowance + 2 for the switch = 7 x 2.25 = 15.75 cu in. The remaining 5.25 cu in. allows 2 more 12 AWG conductors (4.5 cu in.).","r":"NEC 314.16(B)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"An unbroken 12 AWG conductor is looped through a box and is at least twice the minimum free-conductor length required by 300.14. How many times is it counted for box fill?","o":["Not counted","Once","Three times","Twice"],"a":3,"e":"A looped, unbroken conductor not less than twice the minimum free length required in 300.14 is counted twice.","r":"NEC 314.16(B)(1); 300.14","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"A short 14 AWG jumper conductor is installed entirely within a box, connecting two devices, and no part of it leaves the box. How is it counted for box fill?","o":["Once","Twice","It is not counted","Once, but at a 12 AWG allowance"],"a":2,"e":"A conductor, no part of which leaves the box, is not counted for box fill.","r":"NEC 314.16(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":1,"q":"Each conductor that passes through a box without splice or termination is counted how many times for box fill?","o":["Not at all","Twice","Once","Once for each end"],"a":2,"e":"Each conductor originating outside the box and terminating or spliced inside, and each unbroken pass-through conductor, is counted once.","r":"NEC 314.16(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Which of the following is not required to be counted for box fill when entering a box from a domed luminaire canopy and terminating in the box?","o":["A neutral conductor","An equipment grounding conductor and up to four fixture wires smaller than 14 AWG","Up to four fixture wires of 12 AWG","Any number of 14 AWG fixture wires"],"a":1,"e":"The exception exempts an equipment grounding conductor, or not more than four fixture wires smaller than 14 AWG, or both, that enter from a domed luminaire or similar canopy and terminate in the box.","r":"NEC 314.16(B)(1) Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"A conduit body contains splices of 10 AWG conductors. What marking is required on the conduit body?","o":["The manufacturer's durable, legible marking of its volume in cubic inches","The maximum number of splices permitted","The maximum voltage rating only","No marking, because conduit bodies are exempt"],"a":0,"e":"Conduit bodies enclosing splices, taps, or devices must be marked with their volume, and the fill is calculated like a box.","r":"NEC 314.16(C)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"A conduit body with no splices, taps, or devices encloses 8 AWG conductors. How is the number of conductors limited?","o":["By a maximum of three conductors regardless of size","By the volume allowances in Table 314.16(B)","There is no limit for conduit bodies","By the conduit fill allowed in Chapter 9, Table 1 for the attached conduit, and the body must have a cross-sectional area at least twice that of the largest attached conduit"],"a":3,"e":"For conduit bodies enclosing 6 AWG and smaller conductors with no splices, the cross-sectional area must be at least twice that of the largest conduit attached, and the maximum number of conductors follows Chapter 9, Table 1.","r":"NEC 314.16(C)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"A short-radius conduit body such as a capped elbow encloses 10 AWG conductors. Which statement applies?","o":["It may contain splices if the conductors are 10 AWG or smaller","It may not contain splices, taps, or devices","It may contain one splice per conductor","It may contain devices if the cover is gasketed"],"a":1,"e":"Short-radius conduit bodies such as capped elbows and service-entrance elbows are only intended to facilitate raceway and conductor installation and may not contain splices, taps, or devices.","r":"NEC 314.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"A nonmetallic outlet box is to be used with EMT. Under what condition is this permitted?","o":["Never; nonmetallic boxes are prohibited with metal raceways","If the box is listed for wet locations","If the box has internal bonding means between all entries","If an equipment grounding conductor is not installed"],"a":2,"e":"Nonmetallic boxes may be used with metal raceways or metal-armored cables only where internal bonding means are provided between all entries, or integral bonding means with a provision for attaching an equipment bonding jumper.","r":"NEC 314.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":1,"q":"Metal boxes, conduit bodies, and fittings must be connected to an equipment grounding conductor in accordance with which Article?","o":["Article 300","Article 110","Article 250","Article 408"],"a":2,"e":"Metal boxes, conduit bodies, and fittings must be grounded in accordance with Part IV of Article 250.","r":"NEC 314.4; Art. 250 Part IV","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":1,"q":"Boxes and fittings installed in a wet location must be which of the following?","o":["Listed for dry locations only","Made of cast metal only","Listed for use in wet locations","Mounted flush with the finished surface"],"a":2,"e":"Boxes, conduit bodies, outlet box hoods, and fittings in wet locations must be listed for wet locations and be installed to prevent moisture from entering or accumulating.","r":"NEC 314.15","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"Nonmetallic-sheathed cable enters a nonmetallic outlet box through a cable knockout. How far must the cable assembly, including the sheath, extend into the box?","o":["Not less than 3/8 in.","Not less than 1 in.","Not less than 1/4 in.","Not less than 1/2 in."],"a":2,"e":"The cable assembly, including the sheath, must extend into the box not less than 6 mm (1/4 in.) through the cable knockout.","r":"NEC 314.17(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"NM cable enters a single-gang nonmetallic box (not larger than a nominal 2-1/4 x 4 in.) without a cable clamp. Under what condition is it not required to be secured to the box?","o":["The cable is fastened within 8 in. of the box, measured along the sheath, and extends at least 1/4 in. into the box","The cable is fastened within 4-1/2 ft of the box","The cable is fastened within 12 in. of the box","The cable is fastened within 18 in. of the box"],"a":0,"e":"For single-gang nonmetallic boxes, securing the cable to the box is not required if the cable is fastened within 200 mm (8 in.) of the box and the sheath extends 1/4 in. into the box.","r":"NEC 314.17(C) Exception; 334.30(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":1,"q":"What is required for unused openings in boxes and conduit bodies?","o":["They may remain open if smaller than 1/2 in.","They must be taped","They may remain open in dry locations","They must be effectively closed"],"a":3,"e":"Unused openings in boxes, conduit bodies, and fittings must be effectively closed to afford protection substantially equivalent to the wall of the box or fitting.","r":"NEC 314.17(A); 110.12(A)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":1,"q":"In a wall of concrete or tile, how far may the front edge of a flush-mounted box be set back from the finished surface?","o":["Not more than 1 in.","Not more than 1/8 in.","Not more than 1/4 in.","Not more than 1/2 in."],"a":2,"e":"In walls of concrete, tile, or other noncombustible material, the front edge may be set back not more than 6 mm (1/4 in.).","r":"NEC 314.20","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":1,"q":"In walls of wood or other combustible material, how must the front edge of a flush-mounted box be positioned?","o":["Flush with the finished surface or projecting from it","Set back up to 1/8 in.","Set back up to 1/4 in.","Set back up to 1/2 in."],"a":0,"e":"In walls of combustible material, boxes must be flush with the finished surface or project from it.","r":"NEC 314.20","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":1,"q":"Gaps in a plaster or drywall surface around a box must be repaired so there is no gap greater than what at the edge of the box?","o":["1/16 in.","1/4 in.","1/2 in.","1/8 in."],"a":3,"e":"Surfaces damaged or incomplete around a box must be repaired so no gap or open space exceeds 3 mm (1/8 in.) at the edge of the box.","r":"NEC 314.21","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"Nails or screws used to mount a box by passing through the interior of the box must be located within what distance of the back or ends of the box?","o":["1/8 in.","1/2 in.","1/4 in.","3/4 in."],"a":2,"e":"Nails and screws passing through the interior of a box must be within 6 mm (1/4 in.) of the back or ends of the box, unless brackets on the outside are used.","r":"NEC 314.23(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum internal depth of an outlet box that encloses a flush device such as a receptacle?","o":["15/16 in.","1/2 in.","3/4 in.","1-1/4 in."],"a":0,"e":"Outlet boxes enclosing flush devices must have a minimum internal depth of 23.8 mm (15/16 in.).","r":"NEC 314.24(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"What minimum weight must a ceiling outlet box used exclusively for lighting be able to support?","o":["25 lb","35 lb","70 lb","50 lb"],"a":3,"e":"Ceiling boxes for luminaires must support a luminaire weighing a minimum of 23 kg (50 lb). Heavier luminaires must be supported independently unless the box is listed and marked for the greater weight.","r":"NEC 314.27(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"An outlet box is to be used as the sole support of a ceiling-suspended (paddle) fan. What is the maximum fan weight such a box may support?","o":["100 lb","70 lb","35 lb","50 lb"],"a":1,"e":"Boxes used as the sole support of a ceiling-suspended (paddle) fan must be listed and marked for the purpose and cannot support a fan weighing more than 32 kg (70 lb).","r":"NEC 314.27(C)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"An outlet box is designed to support ceiling-suspended (paddle) fans that weigh more than 35 lb. What additional marking is required?","o":["The manufacturer's installation date","The maximum blade diameter","The maximum weight to be supported","The motor horsepower"],"a":2,"e":"For boxes designed to support fans weighing more than 16 kg (35 lb), the required marking must include the maximum weight to be supported.","r":"NEC 314.27(C)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":1,"q":"What is required of boxes installed in a floor?","o":["They must be listed specifically for the application","They must be cast metal only","They must be at least 4 in. square","They must be installed flush with the floor with a gasket"],"a":0,"e":"Boxes in floors must be listed specifically for the application.","r":"NEC 314.27(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"A straight pull is made through a pull box using a 3 in. raceway containing 2/0 AWG conductors. What is the minimum length of the box?","o":["30 in.","24 in.","12 in.","18 in."],"a":1,"e":"In a straight pull, the box length must be at least 8 times the trade diameter of the largest raceway: 8 x 3 = 24 in.","r":"NEC 314.28(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"An angle pull in a pull box uses two 2 in. raceways entering the same wall in the same row, each carrying 1/0 AWG conductors. What is the minimum distance from the raceway entries to the opposite wall?","o":["14 in.","18 in.","12 in.","16 in."],"a":0,"e":"For angle pulls, the distance is 6 times the trade diameter of the largest raceway plus the sum of the other raceways on the same wall and row: 6 x 2 + 2 = 14 in.","r":"NEC 314.28(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"In a pull box angle pull with 3 in. raceways, what is the minimum distance between a raceway entry and the exit of the raceway enclosing the same conductors?","o":["12 in.","24 in.","9 in.","18 in."],"a":3,"e":"The distance between raceway entries enclosing the same conductor must be at least 6 times the trade diameter of the largest raceway: 6 x 3 = 18 in.","r":"NEC 314.28(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":1,"q":"The minimum size rules for pull and junction boxes in 314.28(A) apply to raceways containing conductors of what size or larger?","o":["8 AWG","6 AWG","4 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"The sizing rules for pull and junction boxes apply where conductors of 4 AWG or larger are pulled.","r":"NEC 314.28(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"In a pull or junction box having any dimension over 6 ft, how must the conductors be arranged?","o":["Cabled or racked up in an approved manner","Grouped by circuit with tape only","Left loose if properly identified","Installed in separate raceways inside the box"],"a":0,"e":"In boxes with any dimension over 1.8 m (6 ft), all conductors must be cabled or racked up in an approved manner.","r":"NEC 314.28(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"A junction box is installed above a permanently installed drywall ceiling with no access panel. Why is this not permitted?","o":["Boxes must be accessible without removing any part of the building structure or finish","Boxes must be in sight of the panelboard","The box would be too deep","Junction boxes must be metallic"],"a":0,"e":"Boxes and conduit bodies must be installed so the wiring can be rendered accessible without removing any part of the building or structure.","r":"NEC 314.29","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"A handhole enclosure cover must meet which requirement against unauthorized access?","o":["It must require tools to open, or weigh over 100 lb","It must be locked with a padlock","It must be bolted with at least eight bolts","It must weigh over 25 lb"],"a":0,"e":"Handhole enclosure covers must require the use of tools to open or weigh over 45 kg (100 lb).","r":"NEC 314.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":1,"q":"What must handhole enclosure covers be marked with?","o":["The name of the utility","The voltage of the circuits inside","The date of installation","An identifying mark or logo that prominently identifies the function, such as ELECTRIC"],"a":3,"e":"Handhole enclosure covers must have an identifying mark or logo prominently identifying the function of the enclosure, such as electric.","r":"NEC 314.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"Where a permanent barrier is installed inside a pull or junction box, how is each section treated?","o":["As a separate box","As part of one box with combined volume","As exempt from sizing rules","As a barrier only, with no box requirements"],"a":0,"e":"Where permanent barriers are installed in a box, each section is considered a separate box.","r":"NEC 314.28","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":1,"q":"In which of the following structures is Type NM cable permitted to be used?","o":["A commercial garage with Class I hazardous locations","An elevator hoistway in a dwelling","A Type I construction high-rise apartment building","A multifamily dwelling permitted to be of Type III, IV, or V construction"],"a":3,"e":"Type NM is permitted in one- and two-family dwellings, multifamily dwellings of Types III, IV, and V construction, and other structures permitted to be Types III, IV, or V construction.","r":"NEC 334.10","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":1,"q":"Which location prohibits the use of Type NM cable?","o":["Concealed in a wood-framed ceiling in a dwelling","Concealed in the walls of a one-family dwelling","Run through bored holes in wood joists","Embedded in poured concrete"],"a":3,"e":"Type NM, NMC, and NMS may not be embedded in poured cement, concrete, or aggregate.","r":"NEC 334.12(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":2,"q":"Type NM cable (not NMC) is NOT permitted in which of the following?","o":["Normally dry concealed locations of a dwelling","Exposed work in a dwelling basement, protected as required","Attics of one-family dwellings","Wet or damp locations"],"a":3,"e":"Types NM and NMS may not be used in wet or damp locations or where exposed to corrosive fumes. Type NMC is the type for damp and corrosive locations.","r":"NEC 334.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":2,"q":"Which type of nonmetallic-sheathed cable has a corrosion-resistant jacket and is permitted in dry, moist, damp, or corrosive locations?","o":["Type NMC","Type NM","Type UF only","Type NMS"],"a":0,"e":"Type NMC has a moisture, fungus, and corrosion-resistant covering and is permitted in dry, moist, damp, or corrosive locations. Type NM is limited to normally dry locations.","r":"NEC 334.10(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":1,"q":"May Type NM cable be installed in an elevator hoistway?","o":["Yes, if exposed at a height of at least 7 ft","No, it is prohibited","Yes, if rated 90 degrees C","Yes, if installed in conduit only"],"a":1,"e":"Type NM, NMC, and NMS cables are not permitted in hoistways or on elevators or escalators.","r":"NEC 334.12(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":2,"q":"Type NM cable is installed exposed in a dropped (suspended) ceiling in a commercial building of Type V construction. Is this permitted?","o":["Yes, if supported every 4-1/2 ft","No, exposed NM in dropped or suspended ceilings is not permitted except in dwelling occupancies","Yes, if the cable is 12 AWG or larger","Yes, if the ceiling is accessible"],"a":1,"e":"Exposed Type NM in dropped or suspended ceilings is not permitted in other than one- and two-family and multifamily dwellings.","r":"NEC 334.12(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":1,"q":"May Type NM cable be used as service-entrance cable?","o":["Yes, if run in conduit","Yes, if 6 AWG or larger","No","Yes, for single-family dwellings only"],"a":2,"e":"Types NM, NMC, and NMS may not be used as service-entrance cable.","r":"NEC 334.12(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":1,"q":"Which room is a location where Type NM cable is NOT permitted?","o":["Dwelling attics","Storage battery rooms","Dwelling living rooms","Dwelling kitchens"],"a":1,"e":"Types NM, NMC, and NMS are not permitted in storage battery rooms.","r":"NEC 334.12(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":2,"q":"Exposed Type NM cable passes through a floor in a dwelling. To what height above the floor must it be enclosed in a raceway or otherwise protected?","o":["At least 3 in.","At least 4 ft","At least 12 in.","At least 6 in."],"a":3,"e":"Where exposed NM passes through a floor, it must be enclosed in RMC, IMC, EMT, Schedule 80 PVC, RTRC, or other approved means extending at least 150 mm (6 in.) above the floor.","r":"NEC 334.15(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":3,"q":"In an unfinished basement, Type NM cable runs at an angle across the bottom of the joists. Which cable may be secured directly to the lower edges of the joists?","o":["Any size cable","Two 6 AWG or three 8 AWG conductors, or larger","Three 10 AWG conductors","Two 12 AWG conductors"],"a":1,"e":"Cables not smaller than two 6 AWG or three 8 AWG may be secured directly to the lower edges of joists. Smaller cables must go through bored holes or on running boards.","r":"NEC 334.15(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":1,"q":"How must exposed runs of Type NM cable generally be installed on a building surface?","o":["Only on the underside of joists","With loops every 10 ft","Spaced at least 1 in. from the surface","Closely following the surface of the building finish or running boards"],"a":3,"e":"Exposed runs of cable must closely follow the surface of the building finish or running boards.","r":"NEC 334.15(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":2,"q":"Exposed Type NM cable in a dwelling garage is subject to physical damage on a wall. What protection is required?","o":["No protection if the cable is 10 AWG or larger","Ordinary Schedule 40 PVC rated for outdoor use only","RMC, IMC, EMT, Schedule 80 PVC, RTRC, or other approved means","Protection only up to 12 in. above grade"],"a":2,"e":"Where exposed NM cable is subject to physical damage, it must be protected by RMC, IMC, EMT, Schedule 80 PVC, RTRC, or other approved means.","r":"NEC 334.15(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":2,"q":"Type NM cable is run through bored holes in wood studs. What is the minimum distance from the nearest edge of the stud to the hole, unless the cable is protected by a steel plate?","o":["1-1/4 in.","2 in.","3/4 in.","1 in."],"a":0,"e":"Bored holes must be not less than 32 mm (1-1/4 in.) from the nearest edge of the framing member, or the cable must be protected by a steel plate or sleeve at least 1/16 in. thick.","r":"NEC 334.17; 300.4(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":3,"q":"A hole in a wood stud for Type NM cable is only 3/4 in. from the nearest edge. Which protection satisfies the Code?","o":["No protection is required for NM cable","A plastic strip 1/32 in. thick","A steel plate or bushing at least 1/16 in. thick over the area of the cable","Electrical tape wrapped around the cable"],"a":2,"e":"Where the hole is closer than 1-1/4 in. to the edge, a steel plate, sleeve, or equivalent at least 1/16 in. thick must protect the cable.","r":"NEC 300.4(A)(1); 334.17","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":2,"q":"Type NM cable passes through factory-punched holes in metal studs. What is required?","o":["Taping over the edges after the cable is installed","Listed bushings or grommets covering all metal edges, securely fastened in the opening before installation","Nothing, because the holes are factory made","A steel plate over the hole"],"a":1,"e":"Where cable passes through metal framing members, bushings or grommets that cover all edges must be installed and securely fastened before the cable is installed.","r":"NEC 300.4(B)(1); 334.17","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":2,"q":"In an attic accessible by permanent stairs, Type NM cable runs across the top of the floor joists. How must it be protected?","o":["By guard strips at least as high as the cable","By a metal raceway for the entire run","By 6 in. of separation from the joists","It need not be protected"],"a":0,"e":"In accessible attics, NM cable run across the top of floor joists must be protected by substantial guard strips at least as high as the cable.","r":"NEC 334.23; 320.23","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":3,"q":"An attic is NOT accessible by permanent stairs or ladders. Type NM cable runs across the top of the joists. Where is protection against physical damage required?","o":["Along the entire cable run","Within 10 ft of the nearest wall","Within 6 ft of the nearest edge of the attic entrance","Only within 3 ft of the attic entrance"],"a":2,"e":"Where the attic is not accessible by permanent stairs or ladders, protection is required only within 1.8 m (6 ft) of the nearest edge of the scuttle hole or attic entrance.","r":"NEC 334.23; 320.23","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum bending radius of the inner edge of any bend in Type NM cable?","o":["10 times the diameter of the cable","3 times the diameter of the cable","5 times the diameter of the cable","8 times the diameter of the cable"],"a":2,"e":"The radius of the curve of the inner edge of any bend in NM cable must not be less than five times the cable diameter.","r":"NEC 334.24","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum spacing between supports for Type NM cable?","o":["6 ft","3 ft","4-1/2 ft","8 ft"],"a":2,"e":"NM cable must be secured at intervals not exceeding 1.4 m (4-1/2 ft) and within 300 mm (12 in.) of every cable entry into a box, conduit body, or fitting.","r":"NEC 334.30","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":1,"q":"Type NM cable enters a metal box with a clamp. Within what distance of the box must the cable be secured?","o":["12 in.","24 in.","18 in.","6 in."],"a":0,"e":"NM cable must be secured within 300 mm (12 in.) of every cable entry into a box, conduit body, or fitting, measured along the sheath.","r":"NEC 334.30","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":2,"q":"NM cable is used with a single-gang nonmetallic box with no integral clamp. The cable is fastened within what distance of the box, measured along the sheath?","o":["8 in.","6 in.","18 in.","12 in."],"a":0,"e":"With single-gang nonmetallic boxes not larger than a nominal 2-1/4 x 4 in., the cable may be fastened within 200 mm (8 in.) of the box and need not be secured to the box.","r":"NEC 334.30(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":2,"q":"NM cable is fished through a finished wall between two access points where supporting is impractical. What is permitted?","o":["The cable may be unsupported","The cable must be installed in conduit","The cable must be supported every 18 in.","The cable must be supported every 3 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Cables fished between access points through concealed spaces in finished buildings, where supporting is impractical, are not required to be supported.","r":"NEC 334.30(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":2,"q":"Which listed devices of insulating material may be used with exposed NM cable wiring without boxes?","o":["Self-contained switches and receptacles","Splices with wire nuts","Any junction connection","Only luminaires"],"a":0,"e":"Listed self-contained switches, self-contained receptacles, and NM interconnector devices of insulating material may be used without boxes in exposed cable wiring and for repair wiring in existing buildings where the cable is concealed.","r":"NEC 334.40(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":3,"q":"Three 10/2 Type NM-B cables with 10 AWG copper (90 degree C rated) conductors run together through the same opening in wood framing sealed with spray foam, for six current-carrying conductors. What is the maximum ampacity of each conductor?","o":["32 A","30 A","35 A","40 A"],"a":1,"e":"The 90 degree C ampacity of 10 AWG is 40 A, and 80 percent is 32 A. The final derated ampacity may not exceed the 60 degree C value of 30 A, so 30 A applies.","r":"NEC 334.80","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":1,"q":"The ampacity of Type NM cable may not exceed that of what temperature rating of conductor?","o":["75 degrees C","90 degrees C","105 degrees C","60 degrees C"],"a":3,"e":"The ampacity of NM, NMC, and NMS cable is limited to that of a 60 degree C conductor, although 90 degrees C may be used for derating and correction.","r":"NEC 334.80","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":2,"q":"What is the largest copper conductor size within Type NM cable?","o":["1/0 AWG","4 AWG","6 AWG","2 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Type NM cable conductors are 14 AWG through 2 AWG copper, or 12 AWG through 2 AWG aluminum or copper-clad aluminum.","r":"NEC 334.104","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":1,"q":"What must Type NM cable contain in addition to the insulated circuit conductors?","o":["An insulated, covered, or bare equipment grounding conductor","A metal bonding strip","A drain wire","A shield"],"a":0,"e":"Type NM cable must have an insulated, covered, or bare equipment grounding conductor in addition to the insulated conductors.","r":"NEC 334.108","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":3,"q":"Type NM cable is installed in an office building of Type V construction. Which condition applies to the installation?","o":["The cable may be exposed in the ceiling plenum","The cable must be concealed within walls, floors, or ceilings providing a thermal barrier with at least a 15-minute finish rating","NM cable is not permitted in Type V construction","The cable must be enclosed in EMT throughout"],"a":1,"e":"In structures permitted to be Type III, IV, or V construction (other than one- and two-family dwellings), the cable must be concealed within walls, floors, or ceilings providing a thermal barrier of at least a 15-minute finish rating.","r":"NEC 334.10","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":2,"q":"Type NM cable is to be installed in a shallow chase in masonry covered with plaster. Is this permitted?","o":["No, it is not permitted","Yes, if the cable is 14 AWG","Yes, if the cable is Type NMC","Yes, if the chase is at least 1 in. deep"],"a":0,"e":"Types NM and NMS are not permitted embedded in masonry, concrete, adobe, fill, or plaster, or in a shallow chase in masonry covered with plaster.","r":"NEC 334.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"Where is Type AC cable permitted to be installed?","o":["In damp locations where subject to condensation","In dry locations, exposed or concealed","Embedded in poured concrete","In wet locations when exposed"],"a":1,"e":"Type AC cable is permitted for feeders and branch circuits in dry locations, exposed or concealed, and in cable trays. It is not permitted in damp or wet locations.","r":"NEC 320.10; 320.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"Where is Type AC cable NOT permitted?","o":["In a cable tray","In concealed work in dry locations","In damp or wet locations","In the air voids of dry masonry block walls"],"a":2,"e":"Type AC cable may not be used in damp or wet locations, where exposed to corrosive fumes or vapors, or where subject to physical damage.","r":"NEC 320.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"May Type AC cable be installed where subject to physical damage?","o":["Yes, if 10 AWG or larger","No","Yes, if exposed","Yes, if within 6 ft of the floor"],"a":1,"e":"Type AC cable is not permitted where subject to physical damage.","r":"NEC 320.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"How must exposed runs of Type AC cable be installed on a building surface?","o":["Closely following the surface of the building finish or running boards","Supported at intervals of 10 ft","With 1 in. spacing from the surface","Only in conduit"],"a":0,"e":"Exposed runs of AC cable must closely follow the surface of the building finish or running boards.","r":"NEC 320.15","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum radius of the inner edge of a bend in Type AC cable?","o":["5 times the diameter of the cable","10 times the diameter of the cable","7 times the diameter of the cable","3 times the diameter of the cable"],"a":0,"e":"AC cable bends must have the inner edge radius not less than five times the cable diameter.","r":"NEC 320.24","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"Type AC cable must be secured at intervals not exceeding what distance?","o":["3 ft","6 ft","8 ft","4-1/2 ft"],"a":3,"e":"AC cable must be secured at intervals not exceeding 1.4 m (4-1/2 ft) and within 300 mm (12 in.) of every box, cabinet, or fitting.","r":"NEC 320.30(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"A flexible connection of Type AC cable runs from the last support to a luminaire in an accessible ceiling. What is the maximum unsupported length permitted?","o":["2 ft","10 ft","4-1/2 ft","6 ft"],"a":3,"e":"Unsupported lengths up to 1.8 m (6 ft) are permitted from the last point of support to a luminaire or equipment in an accessible ceiling.","r":"NEC 320.30(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"At the termination of Type AC cable at a box, what must be provided between the conductors and the armor?","o":["A seal-off fitting","An insulating bushing (anti-short)","A fire-rated sleeve","A ground bushing"],"a":1,"e":"At points where the cable terminates, a fitting protecting the wires from abrasion is required, and an insulating bushing or its equivalent must be provided between the conductors and the armor.","r":"NEC 320.40","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"Type AC cable with 90 degree C conductors is installed in thermal insulation. What ampacity basis applies?","o":["The 60 degree C ampacity of the conductors","The 105 degree C ampacity","The 75 degree C ampacity","The 90 degree C ampacity"],"a":0,"e":"Where AC cable is installed in thermal insulation, the ampacity is that of 60 degree C conductors; 90 degree C values may be used only for derating.","r":"NEC 320.80(A)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"What internal component must Type AC cable have in intimate contact with the armor for its entire length?","o":["A drain wire","A bonding strip of copper or aluminum","A paper wrap","A bare copper conductor of 14 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Type AC cable has an internal bonding strip of copper or aluminum in intimate contact with the armor.","r":"NEC 320.100","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"How is a fault-current path provided by Type AC cable?","o":["The armor alone serves as the ground","The armor and internal bonding strip together serve as the equipment grounding conductor","A separate green conductor is always required","AC cable cannot provide a ground path"],"a":1,"e":"Type AC cable must provide an adequate path for fault current, and its armor and internal bonding strip act as the equipment grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 320.108; 250.118(8)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"Type MC cable is permitted for which of the following?","o":["Services, feeders, and branch circuits","Branch circuits only","Feeders only","Control circuits only"],"a":0,"e":"Type MC cable is permitted for services, feeders, and branch circuits, for power, lighting, control, and signal circuits.","r":"NEC 330.10(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":3,"q":"Which Type MC cable construction is acceptable for use in a wet location?","o":["Any MC cable if installed vertically","Insulated conductors listed for wet locations beneath the metal covering, plus a corrosion-resistant jacket over the metal sheath","Interlocked armor with dry-rated conductors and a sealed end","Interlocked armor with THHN conductors and no outer jacket"],"a":1,"e":"In wet locations, MC cable needs an impervious metal covering, a lead sheath or moisture-impervious jacket under the metal covering, or wet-listed conductors with a corrosion-resistant jacket over the sheath.","r":"NEC 330.10(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"Under what condition may Type MC cable be directly buried in earth?","o":["It is identified for the use","It is installed in a dry location","It is 10 AWG or larger","It has interlocked armor and is at least 12 in. deep"],"a":0,"e":"MC cable may be directly buried only where identified for such use.","r":"NEC 330.10(A); 330.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"Where may Type MC cable NOT be used?","o":["Exposed in a dry location","In a cable tray","In concealed work in a dwelling","Where subject to physical damage"],"a":3,"e":"MC cable may not be used where subject to physical damage or exposed to destructive corrosive conditions unless identified.","r":"NEC 330.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum bending radius for smooth-sheath Type MC cable with an external diameter of 3/4 in. or less?","o":["10 times the external diameter of the sheath","12 times the external diameter","5 times the external diameter","7 times the external diameter"],"a":0,"e":"Smooth-sheath MC cable of 3/4 in. diameter or less has a minimum bend radius of 10 times the external diameter of the sheath.","r":"NEC 330.24(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum bending radius for interlocked-armor Type MC cable?","o":["5 times the external diameter","12 times the external diameter","7 times the external diameter of the metallic sheath","10 times the external diameter"],"a":2,"e":"Interlocked-type armor or corrugated sheath MC cable requires a bend radius of at least 7 times the external diameter of the sheath.","r":"NEC 330.24(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"Type MC cable must be secured at intervals not exceeding what distance?","o":["3 ft","10 ft","4-1/2 ft","6 ft"],"a":3,"e":"MC cable must be secured at intervals not exceeding 1.8 m (6 ft).","r":"NEC 330.30(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"Type MC cable with four or fewer conductors, none larger than 10 AWG, must be secured within what distance of every box or fitting?","o":["24 in.","18 in.","6 in.","12 in."],"a":3,"e":"Cables with four or fewer conductors no larger than 10 AWG must be secured within 300 mm (12 in.) of every box, cabinet, fitting, or termination.","r":"NEC 330.30(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum unsupported length of Type MC cable from the last point of support to a luminaire in an accessible ceiling?","o":["4-1/2 ft","8 ft","6 ft","2 ft"],"a":2,"e":"MC cable may be unsupported for up to 1.8 m (6 ft) from the last point of support to a luminaire or equipment in an accessible ceiling.","r":"NEC 330.30(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"Fittings used to connect Type MC cable to boxes, cabinets, or other equipment must be what?","o":["Made of plastic only","Installed with bonding wire","Rated for wet locations only","Listed and identified for such use"],"a":3,"e":"Fittings for MC cable must be listed and identified for the use.","r":"NEC 330.40","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"What is the smallest copper conductor size permitted in Type MC cable?","o":["12 AWG","18 AWG","16 AWG","14 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Type MC conductors may be as small as 18 AWG copper, nickel, or nickel-coated copper, and 12 AWG aluminum or copper-clad aluminum.","r":"NEC 330.104","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"Where may Type USE cable be used?","o":["In interior wiring","Exposed on a building as a branch circuit","As aerial cable without a messenger","Underground, including direct burial"],"a":3,"e":"Type USE service-entrance cable is permitted underground, including direct burial.","r":"NEC 338.10(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"For which use is Type USE cable NOT permitted?","o":["Direct burial in earth","Interior wiring","Emerging from the ground to an outdoor enclosure","Underground service conductors"],"a":1,"e":"Type USE may not be used for interior wiring or for aboveground installations except where it emerges from the ground and terminates at an enclosure.","r":"NEC 338.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"Type SE cable is used for an interior branch circuit or feeder. Which requirements apply to its installation?","o":["Article 330 installation requirements","No specific requirements","The installation requirements of Part II of Article 334 (except 334.80)","The requirements for service conductors only"],"a":2,"e":"Interior SE branch-circuit and feeder installations must comply with Part II of Article 334, excluding 334.80.","r":"NEC 338.10(B)(4)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"For interior Type SE branch-circuit cable, what is the maximum spacing between supports?","o":["8 ft","3 ft","4-1/2 ft","6 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Interior SE cable follows Article 334 Part II, so it must be secured at intervals not exceeding 4-1/2 ft and within 12 in. of boxes.","r":"NEC 338.10(B)(4); 334.30","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"Type SE cable is permitted for which of the following uses?","o":["As direct burial cable underground","As aerial cable without a messenger","As service-entrance cable","In hazardous locations without further restrictions"],"a":2,"e":"Type SE cable is permitted as service-entrance cable. USE is the type for underground installation.","r":"NEC 338.10(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum bending radius of the inner edge of a bend in Type SE or USE cable?","o":["8 times the diameter","10 times the diameter","5 times the diameter of the cable","3 times the diameter"],"a":2,"e":"SE and USE cable bends must have the inner edge radius not less than five times the cable diameter.","r":"NEC 338.24","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":3,"q":"Type USE multiconductor cable is used as aerial cable. What is required?","o":["It may be run free-hanging up to 10 ft","It must be at least 4 AWG","USE is never permitted aerially","It must be identified for aboveground use and installed as messenger-supported wiring"],"a":3,"e":"USE is permitted as aerial cable only if it is a multiconductor cable identified for aboveground use and installed as messenger-supported wiring.","r":"NEC 338.12(B)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"Type SE cable is installed in a location subject to physical damage. What is required?","o":["The cable must be 6 AWG or larger","Burial in sand","Protection as required in 230.50(B)","Nothing, because SE is armored"],"a":2,"e":"SE cable may not be used where subject to physical damage unless protected as in 230.50(B).","r":"NEC 338.12(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"Where is Type UF cable permitted?","o":["Embedded in poured concrete","As service-entrance cable","Underground, including direct burial","In commercial garages"],"a":2,"e":"Type UF cable is permitted underground, including direct burial, as feeder or branch-circuit cable.","r":"NEC 340.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"May Type UF cable be used as service-entrance cable?","o":["Yes, underground only","Yes, for dwellings only","Yes, if 4 AWG or larger","No"],"a":3,"e":"UF cable may not be used as service-entrance cable.","r":"NEC 340.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"Type UF cable is installed exposed to the direct rays of the sun. What is required?","o":["Nothing; UF is always suitable","The cable must be identified as sunlight resistant","The cable must be run in a trough","The cable must be 10 AWG or larger"],"a":1,"e":"UF cable may not be used where exposed to direct sunlight unless identified as sunlight resistant.","r":"NEC 340.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"Type UF cable is used as overhead cable. When is this permitted?","o":["Anywhere if supported every 4-1/2 ft","Never in any case","Only when 6 AWG or larger","Only as messenger-supported wiring in accordance with Article 396"],"a":3,"e":"UF cable may not be used as overhead cable except where installed as messenger-supported wiring.","r":"NEC 340.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"May Type UF cable be embedded in poured concrete?","o":["Yes, if supported every 6 ft","Yes, if the concrete is 3 in. thick","Yes, if 12 AWG","No"],"a":3,"e":"UF cable may not be embedded in poured cement, concrete, or aggregate, with limited exceptions for nonheating leads.","r":"NEC 340.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"The ampacity of Type UF cable is based on which conductor temperature rating?","o":["75 degrees C","90 degrees C","60 degrees C","105 degrees C"],"a":2,"e":"The ampacity of UF cable is that of 60 degree C conductors per 310.14.","r":"NEC 340.80","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum bending radius for Type UF cable?","o":["10 times the diameter","8 times the diameter","5 times the diameter of the cable","3 times the diameter"],"a":2,"e":"UF cable bends must have an inner edge radius not less than five times the cable diameter.","r":"NEC 340.24","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"Type UF multiconductor cable is installed in a dry location as a substitute for NM cable. Which rules apply to the installation?","o":["Article 338","No installation rules","Article 330","Parts II and III of Article 334"],"a":3,"e":"Where UF is installed as nonmetallic-sheathed cable, it must comply with Parts II and III of Article 334 and be the multiconductor type.","r":"NEC 340.10(4)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"Type UF cable is direct buried in a general location (not under driveways) as a 240-volt, 30-ampere circuit. What is the minimum cover?","o":["24 in.","18 in.","6 in.","12 in."],"a":0,"e":"Table 300.5 requires 24 in. for direct-buried cables in general locations. Reduced cover applies to certain GFCI-protected 120 V, 20 A or less dwelling circuits.","r":"NEC 340.10; Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":3,"q":"A 120-volt, 20-ampere GFCI-protected residential branch circuit is run in direct-buried Type UF cable. What is the minimum cover permitted?","o":["24 in.","6 in.","12 in.","18 in."],"a":2,"e":"Table 300.5 permits 12 in. cover for residential branch circuits rated 120 volts or less with GFCI protection and maximum overcurrent protection of 20 amperes.","r":"NEC Table 300.5; 340.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"Direct-buried Type UF cable must be spliced underground. What is required?","o":["A junction box must be used above grade","The splice may be taped with electrical tape","The splice must use materials listed for direct burial","Splicing underground is prohibited"],"a":2,"e":"Direct-buried cables may be spliced or tapped without a splice box if the splice is made with materials listed for direct burial.","r":"NEC 300.5(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"Direct-buried Type UF cable emerges from the ground. Up to what height must it be protected by an enclosure or raceway?","o":["At least 8 ft above finished grade","At least 6 ft above finished grade","At least 18 in. above finished grade","At least 4 ft above finished grade"],"a":0,"e":"Direct-buried cable emerging from grade must be protected from the minimum cover depth to a point at least 2.5 m (8 ft) above finished grade.","r":"NEC 300.5(D)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 320/330/338/340 AC, MC, SE/USE and UF Cables","d":2,"q":"What is the largest conductor size within Type UF cable?","o":["500 kcmil","2 AWG","4/0 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Type UF conductors are 14 AWG copper (12 AWG aluminum) through 4/0 AWG.","r":"NEC 340.104","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum trade size of electrical metallic tubing (EMT) permitted by the NEC?","o":["2 in.","6 in.","4 in.","3 in."],"a":2,"e":"EMT is permitted in trade sizes 1/2 through 4 in. Trade size 6 in. is available only in rigid metal conduit and PVC.","r":"NEC 358.20(B)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"Under the general rule for EMT, what is the maximum distance between supports for a straight horizontal run?","o":["6 ft","5 ft","8 ft","10 ft"],"a":3,"e":"EMT must be supported at intervals not exceeding 10 ft, and also within 3 ft of each box or termination.","r":"NEC 358.30(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"A run of EMT terminates at a junction box. Structural members make it impractical to support the EMT within 3 ft of the box. What is the greatest distance from the box at which the NEC permits the first support?","o":["6 ft","10 ft","5 ft","4 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Where structural members do not readily permit fastening within 3 ft of a box, fastening is permitted within 5 ft.","r":"NEC 358.30(A) Exception No. 1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum total degrees of bends permitted in an EMT run between pull points (for example, between boxes)?","o":["90 degrees","270 degrees","180 degrees","360 degrees"],"a":3,"e":"There shall be no more than the equivalent of four quarter bends (360 degrees total) between pull points.","r":"NEC 358.26","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum number of 12 AWG THHN conductors permitted in a 1/2 in. trade size EMT?","o":["16","9","7","12"],"a":1,"e":"Conduit fill is 40 percent for more than two conductors. 1/2 in. EMT has 0.304 sq in. total area, so 40 percent is 0.122 sq in. A 12 AWG THHN is 0.0133 sq in., giving 9 conductors. Annex C Table C.1 confirms 9.","r":"NEC Chapter 9 Table 1, Table 4, Table 5; Annex C Table C.1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"Which statement about EMT as an equipment grounding conductor is correct?","o":["EMT is permitted as an equipment grounding conductor","EMT is permitted only for circuits of 20 amperes or less","EMT is never permitted as an equipment grounding conductor","EMT is permitted only if it is 1 in. or larger"],"a":0,"e":"EMT is listed in 250.118 as an acceptable equipment grounding conductor and 358.60 states that EMT is permitted as an equipment grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 358.60; 250.118(4)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"EMT couplings and connectors that will be buried in masonry or concrete must be of which type?","o":["Concretetight type","Any type if painted","Compression type with a gasket only","Set-screw type only"],"a":0,"e":"Couplings and connectors used with EMT must be made up tight, and where buried in masonry or concrete they must be of the concretetight type.","r":"NEC 358.42","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"What is the smallest trade size of EMT generally permitted by the NEC?","o":["1/2 in.","1 in.","3/4 in.","3/8 in."],"a":0,"e":"EMT is not smaller than metric designator 16 (trade size 1/2 in.). 3/8 in. applies to certain flexible conduits only.","r":"NEC 358.20(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":3,"q":"What is the smallest trade size of EMT that may contain ten 12 AWG THHN conductors (all the same size, no other conductors) under the 40 percent fill limit?","o":["1 in.","1/2 in.","3/4 in.","1-1/4 in."],"a":2,"e":"Ten 12 AWG THHN total 10 x 0.0133 = 0.133 sq in. 1/2 in. EMT allows only 0.122 sq in. (40 percent of 0.304); 3/4 in. EMT allows 0.213 sq in. (40 percent of 0.533), so 3/4 in. is the smallest.","r":"NEC Chapter 9 Table 1, Table 4, Table 5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":3,"q":"Three 4 AWG THHN conductors (0.0824 sq in. each) will be installed in EMT with no other conductors. What is the smallest trade size of EMT that meets the 40 percent fill limit?","o":["1-1/2 in.","1 in.","1-1/4 in.","3/4 in."],"a":1,"e":"Total conductor area is 3 x 0.0824 = 0.2472 sq in. 3/4 in. EMT allows 0.213 sq in. (not enough); 1 in. EMT allows 0.346 sq in. (40 percent of 0.864), so 1 in. is the minimum.","r":"NEC Chapter 9 Table 1, Table 4, Table 5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"What range of trade sizes is rigid metal conduit (RMC) permitted to be used in under the NEC?","o":["3/4 in. through 6 in.","1/2 in. through 6 in.","1/2 in. through 4 in.","1 in. through 8 in."],"a":1,"e":"RMC is recognized in trade sizes 1/2 through 6 in. (minimum 1/2 in., maximum 6 in.).","r":"NEC 344.20","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"RMC must be securely fastened within what distance of each outlet box, junction box, device box, conduit body or other conduit termination (general rule)?","o":["2 ft","1 ft","3 ft","5 ft"],"a":2,"e":"RMC must be securely fastened within 3 ft of each termination. The 5 ft distance applies only where structural members do not readily permit fastening within 3 ft.","r":"NEC 344.30(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":3,"q":"A straight horizontal run of 2 in. rigid metal conduit is made up with threaded couplings. What is the maximum support spacing permitted for this run under the NEC (assume the supports are rigid and the conduit is fastened within 3 ft of the terminations)?","o":["10 ft","16 ft","12 ft","20 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Where RMC is made up with threaded couplings, Table 344.30(B)(2) permits increased spacing: 10 ft for 1/2 and 3/4 in., 12 ft for 1 in., 14 ft for 1-1/4 and 1-1/2 in., 16 ft for 2 and 2-1/2 in., and 20 ft for 3 in. and larger.","r":"NEC 344.30(B)(2), Table 344.30(B)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"After RMC is cut, what must be done to the cut end before installation?","o":["It must be reamed to remove rough edges","It must be wrapped with tape","It must be filled with sealing compound","It must be threaded a second time"],"a":0,"e":"All cut ends of RMC must be reamed or otherwise finished to remove rough edges.","r":"NEC 344.28","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum cover for rigid metal conduit buried directly in earth in a trench under a lawn (an all-locations area not otherwise listed, no concrete)?","o":["24 in.","6 in.","18 in.","12 in."],"a":1,"e":"Table 300.5 requires 6 in. of cover for rigid metal conduit or intermediate metal conduit in the general all-locations row.","r":"NEC 300.5(A), Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"Aluminum rigid metal conduit that is encased in concrete or in direct contact with the earth requires which of the following?","o":["Supplementary corrosion protection approved for the condition","Nothing additional","A larger trade size","A listed expansion fitting at every coupling"],"a":0,"e":"Aluminum RMC encased in concrete or in direct contact with earth must be provided with approved supplementary corrosion protection.","r":"NEC 344.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"What range of trade sizes is intermediate metal conduit (IMC) permitted in?","o":["1/2 in. through 2 in.","1/2 in. through 6 in.","1/2 in. through 4 in.","3/4 in. through 4 in."],"a":2,"e":"IMC is recognized in trade sizes 1/2 through 4 in.","r":"NEC 342.20","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"For a straight horizontal run of IMC with standard couplings, which support spacing complies with the NEC?","o":["Within 3 ft of each box and not more than 6 ft between supports","Within 1 ft of each box and not more than 4-1/2 ft between supports","Within 3 ft of each box and not more than 10 ft between supports","Within 5 ft of each box and not more than 20 ft between supports"],"a":2,"e":"IMC is supported like RMC: fastened within 3 ft of each termination and supported at intervals not exceeding 10 ft.","r":"NEC 342.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":3,"q":"A run of IMC between two pull boxes already contains three 90-degree bends and one 45-degree bend. What is the greatest additional bend the NEC allows in that run?","o":["135 degrees","45 degrees","None; the run is already at the limit","90 degrees"],"a":1,"e":"The limit is the equivalent of four quarter bends (360 degrees) between pull points. Existing bends total 315 degrees, leaving 45 degrees.","r":"NEC 342.26","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"What range of trade sizes is rigid PVC conduit (Type PVC) permitted in?","o":["1 in. through 8 in.","1/2 in. through 6 in.","3/4 in. through 6 in.","1/2 in. through 4 in."],"a":1,"e":"Rigid PVC conduit is recognized in trade sizes 1/2 through 6 in.","r":"NEC 352.20","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum support spacing for 1 in. trade size rigid PVC conduit in a horizontal run?","o":["3 ft","6 ft","5 ft","2 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Table 352.30: 1/2 through 1 in. = 3 ft; 1-1/4 through 2 in. = 5 ft; 2-1/2 and 3 in. = 6 ft; 3-1/2 through 5 in. = 7 ft; 6 in. = 8 ft.","r":"NEC 352.30(B), Table 352.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum support spacing for 2 in. trade size rigid PVC conduit in a horizontal run?","o":["6 ft","3 ft","5 ft","7 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Table 352.30 allows 5 ft for trade sizes 1-1/4 through 2 in.","r":"NEC 352.30(B), Table 352.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum support spacing for 3 in. trade size rigid PVC conduit in a horizontal run?","o":["8 ft","6 ft","5 ft","10 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Table 352.30 allows 6 ft for trade sizes 2-1/2 and 3 in.","r":"NEC 352.30(B), Table 352.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"Expansion fittings are required for rigid PVC conduit where the expected length change in a straight run between securely mounted items is at least what amount?","o":["1/2 in.","1/8 in.","1 in.","1/4 in."],"a":3,"e":"Expansion fittings must be provided where the calculated thermal expansion or contraction is expected to be 1/4 in. or more in a straight run between securely mounted items.","r":"NEC 352.44","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":3,"q":"Using the NEC expansion table for PVC (4.06 in. per 100 ft for a 100 degree F change; 2.03 in. per 100 ft for a 50 degree F change), what is the expected length change in a 200 ft straight run of PVC conduit subject to a 50 degree F temperature change?","o":["4.06 in.","2.03 in.","8.12 in.","1.01 in."],"a":0,"e":"Table 352.44 gives 2.03 in. per 100 ft for a 50 degree F change; the run is 200 ft, so 2 x 2.03 = 4.06 in.","r":"NEC 352.44, Table 352.44","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"Exposed rigid PVC conduit where it is subject to physical damage must be which of the following, unless identified otherwise?","o":["Schedule 40","Schedule 80","Schedule 40 with a metal cover","Any schedule if painted"],"a":1,"e":"Schedule 80 PVC is identified for use where subject to physical damage; Schedule 40 is not.","r":"NEC 352.10(F)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"How may bends in rigid PVC conduit be made in the field?","o":["Only with a hydraulic bender for metal conduit","Only with bending equipment identified for bending PVC conduit","Bends are not permitted in PVC","With an open flame torch"],"a":1,"e":"Bends must be made only with bending equipment identified for the purpose of bending PVC conduit.","r":"NEC 352.24","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"Where an equipment grounding conductor is required in a run of rigid PVC conduit, what must be done?","o":["The conduit serves as the equipment grounding conductor","No grounding conductor is required for nonmetallic conduit","A bare conductor is run on the outside of the conduit","A separate equipment grounding conductor must be installed in the conduit"],"a":3,"e":"Rigid PVC is nonmetallic and cannot serve as an equipment grounding conductor; a separate conductor must be installed in the conduit.","r":"NEC 352.60","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum cover for PVC conduit (listed for direct burial, no concrete encasement) in a trench in an all-locations area not otherwise listed in Table 300.5?","o":["12 in.","18 in.","6 in.","24 in."],"a":1,"e":"Table 300.5 requires 18 in. cover for nonmetallic raceways listed for direct burial without concrete encasement in the general row.","r":"NEC 300.5(A), Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"A 120 volt, 20 ampere residential branch circuit with GFCI protection is run in PVC conduit directly buried outdoors in a trench in an area not otherwise listed. What is the minimum cover?","o":["12 in.","24 in.","6 in.","18 in."],"a":0,"e":"Table 300.5 permits 12 in. cover for residential branch circuits rated 120 volts or less, GFCI protected, with overcurrent protection of 20 amperes or less.","r":"NEC 300.5(A), Table 300.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"Underground conductors in PVC conduit emerge from grade on a pole. How far above finished grade must the raceway or enclosure protection extend?","o":["2 ft","6 ft","8 ft","4 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Direct-buried conductors and cables emerging from grade must be protected by enclosures or raceways extending from at least 18 in. below grade to 8 ft above finished grade.","r":"NEC 300.5(D)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"What range of trade sizes is electrical nonmetallic tubing (ENT) permitted in?","o":["1/2 in. through 2 in.","1/2 in. through 4 in.","3/8 in. through 1 in.","3/4 in. through 3 in."],"a":0,"e":"ENT is recognized in trade sizes 1/2 through 2 in.","r":"NEC 362.20","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"Which support spacing complies with the NEC for horizontal ENT?","o":["Within 3 ft of each box and at intervals not exceeding 3 ft","Within 3 ft of each box and at intervals not exceeding 10 ft","Within 12 in. of each box and at intervals not exceeding 4-1/2 ft","Within 5 ft of each box and at intervals not exceeding 6 ft"],"a":0,"e":"ENT must be secured within 3 ft of each outlet box, junction box, cabinet or fitting, and supported at intervals not exceeding 3 ft.","r":"NEC 362.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"ENT is not permitted where the ambient temperature exceeds what value, unless it is listed for a higher temperature?","o":["60 degrees C (140 degrees F)","75 degrees C (167 degrees F)","50 degrees C (122 degrees F)","40 degrees C (104 degrees F)"],"a":2,"e":"ENT is not permitted in ambient temperatures in excess of 50 degrees C (122 degrees F) unless listed otherwise.","r":"NEC 362.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"In a building exceeding three floors above grade, ENT is permitted only when concealed within walls, floors and ceilings that provide a thermal barrier with at least what finish rating?","o":["15 minutes","30 minutes","1 hour","5 minutes"],"a":0,"e":"For buildings over three floors above grade, ENT must be concealed in walls, floors and ceilings that provide a thermal barrier with at least a 15 minute finish rating.","r":"NEC 362.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum total of bends in an ENT run between pull points?","o":["180 degrees","360 degrees","90 degrees","270 degrees"],"a":1,"e":"There shall be no more than the equivalent of four quarter bends (360 degrees) between pull points.","r":"NEC 362.26","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"Flexible metal conduit (FMC) must be secured within what distance of each box, and at what maximum interval thereafter (general rule)?","o":["18 in. and every 6 ft","12 in. and every 4-1/2 ft","12 in. and every 3 ft","3 ft and every 10 ft"],"a":1,"e":"FMC must be securely fastened in place within 12 in. of each box or fitting and supported at intervals not exceeding 4-1/2 ft.","r":"NEC 348.30(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"Which installation allows trade size 3/4 in. FMC to serve as the equipment grounding conductor?","o":["Total length of 15 ft, circuit protected at 20 A","Total length in the ground-return path of 5 ft, circuit protected at 20 A","Total length of 10 ft, circuit protected at 20 A","Total length of 5 ft, circuit protected at 60 A"],"a":1,"e":"FMC may be used as an EGC only where the total length in the ground-return path does not exceed 6 ft, the conduit is trade size 1-1/4 in. or smaller, the circuit conductors are protected by overcurrent devices of 20 A or less, and flexibility is not required after installation.","r":"NEC 250.118(5); 348.60","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":3,"q":"A 1/2 in. FMC run of 10 ft supplies a 20 A, 120 V circuit to fixed equipment. The FMC is the only possible grounding path unless something is added. What does the NEC require?","o":["The conduit must be replaced with EMT only","A grounding conductor only if the circuit is over 30 A","A wire-type equipment grounding conductor installed in the conduit","Nothing; the FMC is an acceptable equipment grounding conductor"],"a":2,"e":"FMC may serve as the EGC only if the total length in the ground-return path is 6 ft or less. At 10 ft a wire-type EGC is required.","r":"NEC 250.118(5); 348.60","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"Flexible metal conduit is not permitted in which of the following locations?","o":["Behind a suspended ceiling","Concealed in a dry wall space","Embedded in poured concrete","In a dry accessible location"],"a":2,"e":"FMC is not permitted for direct burial in earth or embedded in poured concrete or aggregate.","r":"NEC 348.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum total degrees of bends in a run of FMC between pull points?","o":["540 degrees","270 degrees","180 degrees","360 degrees"],"a":3,"e":"The NEC limits bends to the equivalent of four quarter bends (360 degrees) between pull points.","r":"NEC 348.26","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"Under what condition may FMC be installed in a wet location?","o":["Only if the trade size is 2 in. or larger","If the conductors are approved for the conditions and the installation keeps liquid from entering the raceway or enclosures","Only if painted","It is never allowed"],"a":1,"e":"FMC is not permitted in wet locations unless the conductors are approved for the specific conditions and the installation is such that liquid is not likely to enter the raceway or the enclosures to which it is connected.","r":"NEC 348.12(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"Liquidtight flexible metal conduit (LFMC) must be secured within what distance of each box, and at what maximum interval thereafter (general rule)?","o":["12 in. and every 3 ft","3 ft and every 10 ft","6 in. and every 6 ft","12 in. and every 4-1/2 ft"],"a":3,"e":"LFMC must be securely fastened within 12 in. of each box or fitting and supported at intervals not exceeding 4-1/2 ft.","r":"NEC 350.30(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"What is the largest trade size of LFMC permitted by the NEC?","o":["3 in.","6 in.","4 in.","2 in."],"a":2,"e":"LFMC is recognized in trade sizes up to 4 in. (minimum 1/2 in., with 3/8 in. permitted in limited cases).","r":"NEC 350.20","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"Which use is permitted for LFMC?","o":["Where subject to physical damage without any listing","Embedded in poured concrete in all cases","Direct burial where listed and marked for the purpose","In lengths over 6 ft only"],"a":2,"e":"LFMC listed and marked for direct burial may be buried. Other conduit types have their own rules, and it is not permitted where subject to severe physical damage.","r":"NEC 350.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":3,"q":"A 1/2 in. LFMC whip, 8 ft long, supplies a 20 A circuit. Can the LFMC serve as the sole equipment grounding path?","o":["Yes, for any 20 A circuit","Yes, if listed","No; the total length in the ground-return path exceeds 6 ft, so a wire-type EGC is required","Yes, if the trade size is 1/2 in. or smaller regardless of length"],"a":2,"e":"Listed LFMC may be an EGC only where the total length in the ground-return path is 6 ft or less (plus the trade-size and overcurrent limits). At 8 ft, a wire-type EGC is required.","r":"NEC 250.118(6)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"Liquidtight flexible nonmetallic conduit (LFNC) must be secured within what distance of each box, and at what maximum interval thereafter (general rule)?","o":["3 ft and every 10 ft","6 in. and every 5 ft","12 in. and every 4-1/2 ft","12 in. and every 3 ft"],"a":3,"e":"LFNC must be securely fastened within 12 in. of each box or fitting and supported at intervals not exceeding 3 ft.","r":"NEC 356.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"What range of trade sizes is LFNC permitted in?","o":["3/8 in. through 4 in.","3/4 in. through 4 in.","3/8 in. through 2 in.","1/2 in. through 6 in."],"a":0,"e":"LFNC is recognized in trade sizes 3/8 through 4 in.","r":"NEC 356.20","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"Which statement about ENT in concrete is correct?","o":["It may be encased in poured concrete or embedded in a concrete slab on grade when fittings listed for the purpose are used","It may be embedded only if the slab is more than 8 in. thick","It may be encased in concrete only with threaded couplings","It may never be installed in concrete"],"a":0,"e":"ENT is permitted encased in poured concrete or in a concrete slab on grade (on sand or approved screenings) if listed fittings are used.","r":"NEC 362.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum conduit or tubing fill, as a percentage of cross-sectional area, for three or more conductors?","o":["60 percent","53 percent","40 percent","31 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Chapter 9 Table 1: 53 percent for one conductor, 31 percent for two, and 40 percent for over two.","r":"NEC Chapter 9 Table 1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum conduit fill percentage for exactly two conductors (non-lead-sheathed)?","o":["53 percent","40 percent","31 percent","60 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Chapter 9 Table 1 limits fill to 31 percent for two conductors.","r":"NEC Chapter 9 Table 1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum conduit fill percentage for a single conductor installed in a raceway?","o":["53 percent","40 percent","31 percent","60 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Chapter 9 Table 1 permits 53 percent fill for one conductor.","r":"NEC Chapter 9 Table 1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"A 20 in. long conduit nipple connects two enclosures. To what percentage of its cross-sectional area may it be filled, and do the 310.15(C)(1) adjustment factors apply?","o":["60 percent; adjustment factors do not apply","53 percent; adjustment factors do not apply","40 percent; adjustment factors apply","60 percent; adjustment factors apply"],"a":0,"e":"Conduit or tubing nipples not exceeding 24 in. may be filled to 60 percent, and the adjustment factors need not be applied.","r":"NEC Chapter 9 Note 4","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"At an outlet box, what is the minimum length of free conductor required, and how far must it extend outside the box opening?","o":["6 in. total, 6 in. outside the opening","3 in. total, 1 in. outside the opening","12 in. total, 6 in. outside the opening","6 in. total free conductor, 3 in. outside the opening"],"a":3,"e":"At least 6 in. of free conductor is required at each outlet, junction or switch point, with at least 3 in. extending outside the opening of the box.","r":"NEC 300.14","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"Where 4 AWG or larger insulated circuit conductors enter a box through a raceway, what protection is required at the entry?","o":["A listed expansion fitting","A grounding bushing only","A substantial fitting providing a smoothly rounded insulating surface (insulated bushing) unless otherwise separated by insulating material","None for conductors in metal conduit"],"a":2,"e":"For 4 AWG and larger insulated conductors entering a cabinet, box or enclosure, a fitting with a smoothly rounded insulating surface is required.","r":"NEC 300.4(G)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":1,"q":"What must be true of a raceway system before conductors are pulled in?","o":["It must be supported only at its ends","It must be complete between outlet, junction and splicing points","It must be painted","It must be filled with compound"],"a":1,"e":"Raceways must be installed complete between outlet, junction or splicing points before the installation of conductors, except for prewired assemblies.","r":"NEC 300.18(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"Raceways installed in a wet location above grade: how is the interior of the raceway classified?","o":["Wet location","Damp location","Dry location","Corrosive location"],"a":0,"e":"The interior of raceways installed in wet locations above grade is considered a wet location, and conductors must be suitable for wet locations.","r":"NEC 300.9","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 342-362 Conduits and Tubing","d":2,"q":"Which of these raceways is permitted in a Class I, Division 1 location?","o":["Liquidtight flexible nonmetallic conduit","Electrical metallic tubing","Rigid PVC conduit above ground","Threaded rigid metal conduit"],"a":3,"e":"Class I, Division 1 allows threaded rigid metal conduit or threaded steel intermediate metal conduit (and certain others); EMT is not permitted.","r":"NEC 501.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum percentage of the interior cross-sectional area of a metal wireway that the sum of all contained conductors may fill at any cross section?","o":["50 percent","25 percent","40 percent","20 percent"],"a":3,"e":"The sum of the cross-sectional areas of all contained conductors at any cross section of a wireway may not exceed 20 percent of the interior cross-sectional area.","r":"NEC 376.22(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"Splices and taps are made inside an accessible metal wireway. At the splice point, conductors including splices and taps may fill no more than what percentage of the wireway area?","o":["75 percent","40 percent","20 percent","100 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Splices and taps are permitted in a wireway if accessible, and the conductors including splices and taps may not fill the wireway to more than 75 percent of its area at that point.","r":"NEC 376.56(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"Adjustment factors of 310.15(C)(1) apply to conductors in a metal wireway only when the number of current-carrying conductors exceeds how many?","o":["9","20","3","30"],"a":3,"e":"Derating adjustment factors apply to wireway conductors only where the number of current-carrying conductors exceeds 30 at any cross section.","r":"NEC 376.22(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"For a horizontal run of metal wireway, which support arrangement complies with the NEC, unless the wireway is listed for other intervals?","o":["At each end and at intervals not exceeding 5 ft, with no more than 10 ft between supports","Every 15 ft","At each end and every 10 ft only","Every 3 ft with no maximum between joints"],"a":0,"e":"Horizontal wireway must be supported at each end and at intervals not exceeding 5 ft (or at each end or joint of lengths longer than 5 ft), unless listed otherwise, and the distance between supports may not exceed 10 ft.","r":"NEC 376.30(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum support interval for a vertical run of metal wireway, and how many joints may be between supports?","o":["10 ft, with two joints","15 ft, with no more than one joint","20 ft, with any number of joints","5 ft, with one joint"],"a":1,"e":"Vertical runs of wireway must be securely supported at intervals not exceeding 15 ft and may not have more than one joint between supports.","r":"NEC 376.30(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":1,"q":"What is required at the end of a wireway that is not continued?","o":["A pull box","A grounding bushing","The dead end must be closed","Nothing"],"a":2,"e":"Dead ends of wireways must be closed.","r":"NEC 376.58","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"Where are sheet metal wireways permitted to be installed?","o":["Concealed inside walls","Embedded in concrete","Underground","Exposed work"],"a":3,"e":"Sheet metal wireways are permitted for exposed work (and in wet locations only if of a raintight type).","r":"NEC 376.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":3,"q":"A metal wireway has an interior of 4 in. by 4 in. (16 sq in.). Each conductor has a cross-sectional area (with insulation) of 0.3970 sq in. What is the maximum number of these conductors permitted at one cross section under the 20 percent fill limit?","o":["12","6","9","8"],"a":3,"e":"20 percent of 16 sq in. is 3.2 sq in. Eight conductors total 3.176 sq in., which fits; nine total 3.573 sq in., which exceeds the limit.","r":"NEC 376.22(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":3,"q":"A metal wireway has an interior of 4 in. by 4 in. At a splice point, what is the largest total cross-sectional area of conductors, splices and taps permitted?","o":["3.2 sq in.","12 sq in.","6.4 sq in.","16 sq in."],"a":1,"e":"At splices and taps the fill limit is 75 percent: 0.75 x 16 sq in. = 12 sq in. The 3.2 sq in. figure is the 20 percent limit that applies elsewhere in the wireway.","r":"NEC 376.56(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":1,"q":"A sheet metal auxiliary gutter supplements a switchboard. In general, how far may it extend beyond the equipment it supplements?","o":["10 ft","30 ft","50 ft","100 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Auxiliary gutters may not extend more than 30 ft beyond the equipment they supplement (elevator work under 620.35 is the exception, up to 100 ft).","r":"NEC 366.12(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"An auxiliary gutter supplements equipment for elevator installations as permitted in 620.35. What is the maximum distance it may extend beyond the equipment it supplements?","o":["30 ft","100 ft","75 ft","50 ft"],"a":1,"e":"The exception to 366.12 permits an auxiliary gutter to extend up to 100 ft beyond the equipment it supplements where used as permitted by 620.35 for elevators.","r":"NEC 366.12 Exception; 620.35","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum fill of a sheet metal auxiliary gutter by conductors at any cross section?","o":["75 percent","20 percent","25 percent","40 percent"],"a":1,"e":"The sum of the cross-sectional areas of all contained conductors at any cross section may not exceed 20 percent of the interior cross-sectional area.","r":"NEC 366.22(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum support interval for a sheet metal auxiliary gutter?","o":["3 ft","5 ft","8 ft","10 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Sheet metal auxiliary gutters must be supported and secured at intervals not exceeding 5 ft.","r":"NEC 366.30(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":3,"q":"Bare copper bus bars in a sheet metal auxiliary gutter have a cross section of 0.25 in. by 2 in. What is the maximum ampacity the NEC allows for these bars?","o":["1000 A","350 A","500 A","700 A"],"a":2,"e":"The cross-sectional area is 0.5 sq in., and the NEC limits bare copper bars to 1000 A per square inch, so 500 A. (700 A per square inch applies to aluminum bars.)","r":"NEC 366.23(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum support interval for a busway, unless the busway is designed and marked for other spacing?","o":["10 ft","3 ft","5 ft","8 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Busways must be securely supported at intervals not exceeding 5 ft unless otherwise designed and marked.","r":"NEC 368.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":3,"q":"A 1200 A busway is protected by a 1200 A overcurrent device. A shorter section of smaller busway, 40 ft long, free of contact with combustible material, is connected to it. What is the lowest ampere rating the smaller busway may have without its own overcurrent protection at the reduction?","o":["600 A","400 A","200 A","300 A"],"a":1,"e":"Overcurrent protection at a reduction in busway ampacity is not required where the smaller busway is 50 ft or less, has a rating at least one-third of the protecting device (1200 / 3 = 400 A), and is free from contact with combustible material.","r":"NEC 368.17(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"Where may an unbroken length of busway extend vertically through a dry floor?","o":["Never","If totally enclosed (unventilated) where it passes through and for a minimum of 6 ft above the floor","If enclosed for 12 in. above the floor","If painted"],"a":1,"e":"Busways may extend vertically through dry floors if totally enclosed where passing through and for a minimum of 6 ft above the floor to protect against physical damage.","r":"NEC 368.10(C)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":1,"q":"Which location is a busway not permitted to be installed in?","o":["Above a suspended ceiling where accessible","A hoistway","A dry commercial building","A switchgear room"],"a":1,"e":"Busways are not permitted in hoistways (and not where subject to severe physical damage or corrosive vapors, or in hazardous locations unless specifically permitted).","r":"NEC 368.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":1,"q":"What is the smallest size single-conductor power cable generally permitted in a cable tray?","o":["1 AWG","1/0 AWG","4/0 AWG","4 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Single-conductor cables in cable tray must be 1/0 AWG or larger (and listed and marked for tray use).","r":"NEC 392.10(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"Under what condition may metal cable tray sections and fittings be used as an equipment grounding conductor?","o":["Always, with no conditions","Only with an added bare copper conductor","When the tray sections and fittings are identified for grounding, marked, and bonded as required","Never"],"a":2,"e":"Metal cable trays may serve as an equipment grounding conductor where they are identified for it, marked with the cross-sectional area, meet the Table 392.60(A) requirements and are bonded in accordance with 250.96.","r":"NEC 392.60(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":1,"q":"Cable tray systems are not permitted in which location?","o":["Above a suspended ceiling","Industrial establishments","Hoistways","Outdoors in a listed outdoor tray"],"a":2,"e":"Cable tray systems may not be used in hoistways or where subject to severe physical damage.","r":"NEC 392.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"Cable tray passing through a wall must be installed according to which requirement?","o":["300.21, spread of fire or products of combustion","Only the manufacturer's color code","Article 680","Section 230.50"],"a":0,"e":"Cable trays may extend transversely through partitions and walls, or vertically through platforms and floors, where installed in accordance with 300.21, so fire-rated assemblies are not compromised.","r":"NEC 392.18(D); 300.21","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":3,"q":"1/0 through 4/0 AWG single-conductor cables are installed in a ladder cable tray. What maximum rung spacing applies?","o":["12 in.","9 in.","6 in.","18 in."],"a":1,"e":"Where 1/0 through 4/0 AWG single-conductor cables are installed in ladder tray, the maximum rung spacing is 9 in.","r":"NEC 392.22(B)(1)(c)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"May a cable splice be located in a cable tray?","o":["Only in the bottom rails","Never","Yes, if made and insulated by approved methods, and it is permitted to project above the side rails when not subject to physical damage","Only if it is made with wire nuts for 4/0 and larger"],"a":2,"e":"Cable splices made and insulated by approved methods are permitted in cable tray and may project above the side rails if not subject to physical damage.","r":"NEC 392.56","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum fill of a strut-type channel raceway by conductors?","o":["40 percent","75 percent","25 percent","20 percent"],"a":2,"e":"The size and number of conductors may not exceed 25 percent of the interior cross-sectional area of the strut-type channel raceway, and adjustment factors apply.","r":"NEC 384.22","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"Strut-type channel raceway running horizontally must be supported within what distance of each outlet box or fitting, and at what maximum interval?","o":["12 in. and every 4-1/2 ft","1 ft and every 3 ft","5 ft and every 20 ft","3 ft and every 10 ft"],"a":3,"e":"Strut-type channel raceways must be secured within 3 ft of each box or fitting and supported at intervals not exceeding 10 ft.","r":"NEC 384.30(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"Metal surface raceway is not permitted where the voltage between conductors is 300 volts or more, unless the metal has what minimum nominal thickness?","o":["0.062 in.","0.125 in.","0.040 in.","0.020 in."],"a":2,"e":"At 300 volts or more between conductors, the metal of a surface metal raceway must be at least 0.040 in. (1.0 mm) nominal thick.","r":"NEC 386.12(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"Nonmetallic wireways run horizontally must be supported at intervals not exceeding what, with what absolute maximum between supports (unless listed for other intervals)?","o":["10 ft; 20 ft","5 ft; 10 ft","3 ft; 10 ft","5 ft; 15 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Nonmetallic wireways are supported at intervals not exceeding 3 ft and at each end or joint, and the distance between supports may not exceed 10 ft.","r":"NEC 378.30(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":2,"q":"Expansion fittings are required in a straight run of nonmetallic wireway where the expected length change is how much or more?","o":["1/4 in.","1 in.","1/2 in.","1/8 in."],"a":0,"e":"Expansion fittings must be provided where the thermal expansion or contraction is expected to be 1/4 in. or more in a straight run.","r":"NEC 378.44","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 366-392 Wireways, Busways and Cable Trays","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum fill of a nonmetallic wireway by conductors at any cross section?","o":["20 percent","50 percent","40 percent","25 percent"],"a":0,"e":"The sum of the cross-sectional areas of all contained conductors at any cross section may not exceed 20 percent of the interior cross-sectional area.","r":"NEC 378.22","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is a use of flexible cord that the NEC does not permit?","o":["To connect a portable lamp to a receptacle","To supply a pendant luminaire","To connect an appliance made to be removed for repair","As a substitute for the fixed wiring of a structure"],"a":3,"e":"Flexible cords and cables may not be used as a substitute for the fixed wiring of a structure.","r":"NEC 400.12(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":2,"q":"A contractor wants to run a flexible cord through a hole in a wall to power an outlet in the next room. What does the NEC say?","o":["Permitted for circuits of 15 amperes or less","Permitted if the cord is Type SO","Not permitted; flexible cords may not run through holes in walls, ceilings or floors","Permitted if the hole is bushed"],"a":2,"e":"Flexible cords and cables may not be run through holes in walls, structural ceilings, suspended ceilings, dropped ceilings or floors, regardless of cord type.","r":"NEC 400.12(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":2,"q":"Which installation of flexible cord violates the NEC?","o":["A cord feeding a pendant","A cord connecting a portable appliance to a receptacle","A cord run through a doorway or window opening to a fixed outlet","A cord connecting a portable lamp"],"a":2,"e":"Flexible cords and cables may not be run through doorways, windows or similar openings.","r":"NEC 400.12(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":2,"q":"Flexible cord stapled to the surface of a building wall to supply a fixed ceiling fan is:","o":["Not permitted, since cords may not be attached to building surfaces","Permitted for 120 volt circuits","Permitted if stapled every 4-1/2 ft","Permitted if the cord is Type SO"],"a":0,"e":"Flexible cords and cables may not be attached to building surfaces (with limited exceptions).","r":"NEC 400.12(4)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":1,"q":"May flexible cord be concealed behind building walls, floors or ceilings?","o":["No, flexible cords may not be concealed behind walls, floors or ceilings","Yes, if the circuit is 15 amperes or less","Yes, if in a raceway only","Yes, if Type SJO"],"a":0,"e":"Flexible cords and cables may not be concealed by walls, floors or ceilings or located above suspended or dropped ceilings.","r":"NEC 400.12(5)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":1,"q":"Flexible cords are generally required to be installed:","o":["With splices in junction boxes","In continuous lengths without splice or tap","With splices only at the plug","With one splice per 25 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Flexible cord must be used only in continuous lengths without splice or tap.","r":"NEC 400.13","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":2,"q":"Which cord may be repaired by splicing, provided the splice retains the insulation, outer sheath and usage characteristics of the cord?","o":["Any 18 AWG cord","Hard service cord, 14 AWG or larger","Any parallel cord","Only elevator cable"],"a":1,"e":"Hard service cord and junior hard service cord, 14 AWG and larger, may be repaired by splicing if the repair retains the properties of the original cord.","r":"NEC 400.13 Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":2,"q":"How must a flexible cord be connected to a device or fitting?","o":["With the cord jacket removed entirely","So the conductors carry the weight of the cord","With solder only","So that tension is not transmitted to joints or terminals"],"a":3,"e":"Cords must be connected so that tension is not transmitted to joints or terminals, such as by knotting, tape winding or fittings designed for the purpose.","r":"NEC 400.14","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":2,"q":"A flexible cord passes through a hole in a metal outlet box cover for an approved use. What protection is required at the hole?","o":["A conduit nipple 12 in. long","None if the cord is Type SO","A sealing compound","Bushing or fitting to protect the cord"],"a":3,"e":"Flexible cords must be protected by bushings or fittings where passing through holes in covers, outlet boxes or similar enclosures.","r":"NEC 400.17","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":1,"q":"Which color identifies the grounded conductor of a flexible cord?","o":["Red","Green","Black","White or gray"],"a":3,"e":"A grounded conductor in a flexible cord must be white or gray (or have three continuous white stripes on other than green insulation).","r":"NEC 400.22","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":1,"q":"How is the equipment grounding conductor of a flexible cord identified?","o":["White","Orange","Green or green with one or more yellow stripes","Gray"],"a":2,"e":"The EGC of a flexible cord must be green or green with one or more yellow stripes, and no other conductor may use these colors.","r":"NEC 400.23","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":2,"q":"Which cord type listed in Table 400.4 is a hard-service cord rated at 600 volts?","o":["Type SV","Type SPT-2","Type SO","Type SJ"],"a":2,"e":"Type S and SO are hard service cords rated 600 V; SJ, SJO and SJT (junior hard service) are rated 300 V.","r":"NEC Table 400.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":1,"q":"In Table 400.4 cord type letters, what does the suffix W indicate?","o":["Water-soluble insulation","Wall-mounted use only","Suitable for outdoor use, with sunlight resistance and wet-location suitability","Wire only, no jacket"],"a":2,"e":"Cord types with a W suffix are suitable for weather-exposed (outdoor) use, being sunlight resistant and suited for wet locations.","r":"NEC Table 400.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":3,"q":"A flexible cord has six current-carrying conductors and a base ampacity of 20 amperes from the cord ampacity table. What is the allowable ampacity after applying the adjustment factor for number of conductors?","o":["16 A","20 A","14 A","10 A"],"a":0,"e":"Table 400.5(A)(3) applies 80 percent for 4 through 6 current-carrying conductors: 20 A x 0.80 = 16 A.","r":"NEC 400.5(A)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":3,"q":"A flexible cord has eight current-carrying conductors and a base ampacity of 20 amperes from the cord ampacity table. What is the allowable ampacity after applying the adjustment factor?","o":["14 A","10 A","16 A","18 A"],"a":0,"e":"Table 400.5(A)(3) applies 70 percent for 7 through 9 current-carrying conductors: 20 A x 0.70 = 14 A.","r":"NEC 400.5(A)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":2,"q":"A 3-conductor flexible cord has two current-carrying conductors and an equipment grounding conductor. How is the EGC treated for adjustment factors?","o":["It is counted only for outdoor use","It is counted at half value","It is counted","It is not counted as a current-carrying conductor"],"a":3,"e":"A conductor used only to connect to the equipment grounding conductor is not counted as a current-carrying conductor for the adjustment factors.","r":"NEC 400.5(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":1,"q":"Which is a permitted use of flexible cord?","o":["Pendants","Concealed in a wall","Run through a wall","Run above a suspended ceiling"],"a":0,"e":"Flexible cords are permitted for pendants, wiring of luminaires, connection of portable luminaires and appliances, and other uses listed in 400.10(A).","r":"NEC 400.10(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":2,"q":"Flexible cord may be used to connect a stationary appliance when which condition applies?","o":["The cord is 100 ft long","The appliance is over 30 amperes","The appliance is in a wet location","The fastening means and connections are designed to permit removal for maintenance and repair"],"a":3,"e":"Flexible cord is permitted for appliances whose fastening means and mechanical connections are specifically designed to permit removal for maintenance and repair.","r":"NEC 400.10(A)(8)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":2,"q":"Flexible cord is permitted to connect utilization equipment in which situation?","o":["To replace conduit in an attic","To connect fixed lighting inside walls","To facilitate frequent interchange","To avoid installing a branch circuit"],"a":2,"e":"Cords are permitted for connection of utilization equipment to facilitate frequent interchange.","r":"NEC 400.10(A)(6)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":3,"q":"What is the smallest flexible cord conductor size permitted in a field-assembled extension cord set supplied from a 20 ampere branch circuit?","o":["18 AWG","20 AWG","14 AWG","16 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"For field-assembled extension cord sets protected by a 20 A overcurrent device, 16 AWG or larger conductors are required.","r":"NEC 240.5(B)(4)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":2,"q":"How must flexible cords be marked?","o":["Only with the manufacturer's color","With a stamped label inside the plug only","No marking is required","Durably marked on the surface at intervals with type designation, size and number of conductors"],"a":3,"e":"Flexible cords must be durably marked on the surface at intervals not exceeding 24 in. with type letters, size and number of conductors.","r":"NEC 400.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":2,"q":"Which statement about flexible cord in a raceway is correct?","o":["Cord may be installed in any metal raceway but not in PVC","Cord may be installed in raceways only if 10 AWG","Cord may always be installed in raceways","Cord generally may not be installed in raceways, except as specifically permitted"],"a":3,"e":"Flexible cords and cables may not be installed within raceways except as otherwise permitted by the NEC, such as for protection at the point where they enter (400.17).","r":"NEC 400.12(6)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":3,"q":"A flexible cord is used where it will be subject to physical damage and is not protected. Is this permitted?","o":["Yes, if lengths do not exceed 10 ft","No, flexible cords may not be used where subject to physical damage","Yes, if painted","Yes, with Type SJ"],"a":1,"e":"Flexible cords may not be used where subject to physical damage.","r":"NEC 400.12(7)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":2,"q":"Flexible cord is permitted for the wiring of which of the following?","o":["Underground feeders","Service entrance conductors","Concealed ceiling outlets","Cranes and hoists"],"a":3,"e":"Wiring of cranes and hoists is one of the uses listed for flexible cords and cables.","r":"NEC 400.10(A)(5)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 400 Flexible Cords and Cables","d":2,"q":"Flexible cord is permitted in which of the following elevator applications?","o":["As elevator cables","As concealed wiring in the hoistway wall","As the feeder from the elevator disconnect to the main panel","As the branch circuit to the machine room lighting"],"a":0,"e":"Elevator cables are one of the permitted uses of flexible cords and cables listed in 400.10(A); the other options are fixed-wiring uses.","r":"NEC 400.10(A)(4)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum height above the floor or working platform for the center of the grip of a switch operating handle in its highest position (general rule)?","o":["7 ft","5 ft 6 in.","6 ft 7 in.","6 ft"],"a":2,"e":"The center of the grip of the operating handle of a switch or circuit breaker in its highest position may not be more than 6 ft 7 in. above the floor or working platform.","r":"NEC 404.8(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"Snap switches may be ganged in the same enclosure with other snap switches or receptacles without a barrier only if the voltage between adjacent devices does not exceed what value?","o":["150 volts","600 volts","300 volts","120 volts"],"a":2,"e":"Snap switches may not be grouped with other devices unless the voltage between adjacent devices does not exceed 300 volts, or identified barriers are installed.","r":"NEC 404.8(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":1,"q":"For a vertically operated toggle switch, which handle position must be the closed (on) position?","o":["Either, if marked","Up","Center","Down"],"a":1,"e":"Where switch handles are operated vertically, the up position must be the closed (on) position.","r":"NEC 404.7","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":1,"q":"In 3-way and 4-way switch wiring, switching must be done in which conductor?","o":["In the equipment grounding conductor","Only in the ungrounded (hot) conductors","Only in the grounded conductor","In either conductor"],"a":1,"e":"Three-way and four-way switches must be wired so that all switching is done only in the ungrounded circuit conductor.","r":"NEC 404.2(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"May a switch disconnect the grounded conductor of a circuit?","o":["No, unless it simultaneously disconnects all conductors or opens the grounded conductor only after all ungrounded ones are disconnected","Yes, always","Yes, if the switch is rated 30 amperes","Yes, for 277 volt circuits only"],"a":0,"e":"Switches may not disconnect the grounded conductor unless all conductors are disconnected simultaneously or the grounded conductor cannot be disconnected until all ungrounded conductors are.","r":"NEC 404.2(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"In a new installation, a switch controlling a lighting load is installed in a box with no applicable exception. What conductor must be provided at the switch location?","o":["A 10 AWG conductor","A grounded (neutral) conductor","Nothing additional","A second equipment grounding conductor"],"a":1,"e":"Switches controlling lighting loads must have a grounded circuit conductor provided at the switch location, unless one of the listed exceptions applies.","r":"NEC 404.2(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":1,"q":"Where may a snap switch NOT be installed in a bathroom?","o":["On the wall near a door","Next to the vanity","Next to the exhaust fan","Within a tub or shower space (unless part of a listed tub or shower assembly)"],"a":3,"e":"Switches may not be installed within tub or shower spaces unless installed as part of a listed tub or shower assembly.","r":"NEC 404.4(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"A surface-mounted snap switch is installed in a wet location. What type of enclosure is required?","o":["No enclosure if GFCI protected","Any plastic box","A standard utility box","A weatherproof enclosure or cabinet"],"a":3,"e":"A surface-mounted switch or circuit breaker in a wet location must be enclosed in a weatherproof enclosure or cabinet.","r":"NEC 404.4(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":1,"q":"Faceplates provided for snap switches must be installed so that they do which of the following?","o":["Be mounted with only one screw","Cover only the switch yoke","Completely cover the opening and seat against the finished surface","Leave a 1/4 in. gap around the box"],"a":2,"e":"Faceplates must completely cover the opening and seat against the finished surface.","r":"NEC 404.9(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"Snap switches, including dimmers, must provide which of the following?","o":["A neutral terminal on every switch","A bare ground wire only","A means to connect an equipment grounding conductor","A fuse in the housing"],"a":2,"e":"Snap switches including dimmers must be effectively grounded and have a means to connect an EGC, with limited exceptions.","r":"NEC 404.9(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"Circuit breakers used as switches in 120 volt and 277 volt fluorescent lighting circuits must be listed and marked with which designation?","o":["GFCI","SWD","HACR","CO/ALR"],"a":1,"e":"Circuit breakers used as switches in 120 V and 277 V fluorescent lighting circuits must be listed and marked SWD.","r":"NEC 404.11","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"A circuit breaker used as a switch for a high-intensity discharge (HID) lighting circuit must be listed and marked as:","o":["AFCI","HACR","HID","SWD"],"a":2,"e":"Circuit breakers used as switches in high-intensity discharge lighting circuits must be listed and marked HID.","r":"NEC 404.11","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"A general-use ac snap switch is rated 30 A. What is the maximum motor load (full-load current) it may control?","o":["24 A","37.5 A","15 A","30 A"],"a":0,"e":"General-use ac snap switches may control motor loads not exceeding 80 percent of the switch ampere rating at its rated voltage: 0.80 x 30 A = 24 A.","r":"NEC 404.14(A)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":3,"q":"A 15 A, 120 V general-use ac snap switch is considered to control a motor. What is the largest motor full-load current permitted?","o":["12 A","15 A","8 A","18.75 A"],"a":0,"e":"Motor loads may not exceed 80 percent of the switch rating: 0.80 x 15 A = 12 A.","r":"NEC 404.14(A)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":3,"q":"A 20 A, 120/277 V general-use ac snap switch is proposed to control tungsten-filament lamp loads at 277 V. What does the NEC say?","o":["It is permitted at 80 percent of 20 A","It is not permitted; tungsten-filament lamp loads are limited to the switch ampere rating at 120 V","It is permitted only with a fuse","It is permitted up to 20 A at 277 V"],"a":1,"e":"General-use ac snap switches may control tungsten-filament lamp loads only up to the ampere rating at 120 volts.","r":"NEC 404.14(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"A general-use ac snap switch rated 20 A may control resistive and inductive loads up to what limit?","o":["The ampere rating at the applied voltage","125 percent of the ampere rating","50 percent of the ampere rating","80 percent of the ampere rating"],"a":0,"e":"Ac general-use snap switches may control resistive and inductive loads not exceeding the ampere rating at the applied voltage.","r":"NEC 404.14(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"A general-use ac-dc snap switch may control inductive loads up to what portion of its ampere rating at the applied voltage?","o":["25 percent","80 percent","100 percent","50 percent"],"a":3,"e":"AC-DC general-use snap switches may control inductive loads not exceeding 50 percent of the ampere rating at the applied voltage.","r":"NEC 404.14(B)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"A 20 A or less switch is directly connected to aluminum conductors. It must be listed and marked with which designation?","o":["AL-CU only","HID","SWD","CO/ALR"],"a":3,"e":"Snap switches rated 20 A or less directly connected to aluminum conductors must be listed and marked CO/ALR.","r":"NEC 404.14(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"Single-throw knife switches must be placed so that:","o":["Gravity does not tend to close them","They are always facing down","They are always horizontal","Gravity tends to close them"],"a":0,"e":"Single-throw knife switches must be placed so gravity will not tend to close them, unless fitted with a locking device.","r":"NEC 404.6(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"A double-throw knife switch is mounted in a vertical position. What must be provided?","o":["A fuse in each pole","Nothing","A locking device that holds the blades in the open position when so set","A cover"],"a":2,"e":"Double-throw knife switches may be mounted vertically or horizontally, but vertical mounting requires a locking means to hold the blades in the open position.","r":"NEC 404.6(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"Single-throw knife switches and switches with butt contacts must be connected so that, when open, the blades are:","o":["Connected to ground","De-energized (dead)","Tied together","Energized from the line side"],"a":1,"e":"They must be connected such that the blades are de-energized when the switch is in the open position.","r":"NEC 404.6(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":1,"q":"Metal enclosures for switches and circuit breakers must be connected to:","o":["Nothing if less than 50 volts","The water pipe only","A grounded conductor only","An equipment grounding conductor"],"a":3,"e":"Metal enclosures for switches or circuit breakers must be connected to an equipment grounding conductor as required in Part IV of 250.","r":"NEC 404.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":3,"q":"A multipole general-use snap switch is fed from two different circuits. What must be true?","o":["Nothing, any multipole switch can be fed from two circuits","It must be 30 amperes","It must be listed and marked as a two-circuit (or three-circuit) switch, or have a voltage rating not less than the nominal line-to-line voltage","It must have a neutral terminal"],"a":2,"e":"A multipole snap switch may not be fed from more than a single circuit unless it is listed and marked as a two-circuit or three-circuit switch, or its voltage rating is at least the nominal line-to-line voltage of the system supplying the circuit.","r":"NEC 404.8(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"A switch has a marked OFF position. In the off position, it must:","o":["Disconnect only the neutral","Disconnect only one ungrounded conductor","Be marked ON as well","Completely disconnect all ungrounded conductors to the load it controls"],"a":3,"e":"A switch with a marked OFF position must completely disconnect all ungrounded conductors to the load it controls.","r":"NEC 404.15(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":3,"q":"A knife switch is rated 1500 A at 240 volts. How may it be used?","o":["Only on dc circuits","Only as an isolating switch, not opened under load","As a load-break switch","As a motor starter"],"a":1,"e":"Knife switches rated over 1200 A at 250 V or less (and over 600 A at 251-600 V) may be used only as isolating switches and not opened under load.","r":"NEC 404.13(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":1,"q":"Receptacles installed on 15 ampere and 20 ampere branch circuits must be which type?","o":["Grounding type","Nongrounding type","Locking type","Hospital grade"],"a":0,"e":"Receptacles installed on 15 A and 20 A branch circuits must be of the grounding type, and the grounding contacts must be effectively grounded.","r":"NEC 406.4(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":1,"q":"How must an isolated ground receptacle be identified?","o":["By a green dot on the box","By an orange triangle on the face of the receptacle","By the letters IG on the faceplate","By a red face"],"a":1,"e":"Receptacles with an isolated grounding conductor connection to reduce electrical noise must be identified by an orange triangle on the face.","r":"NEC 406.3(E)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"A nonlocking, 125 volt, 20 ampere receptacle is controlled by an automatic control device for energy management. What marking is required?","o":["No marking","The orange triangle","The letters WR","The controlled receptacle symbol on the face of the receptacle"],"a":3,"e":"Receptacles controlled by an automatic control device, or with control features that remove power for energy management or building automation, must be marked with the controlled-receptacle symbol.","r":"NEC 406.3(F)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"Receptacles rated 20 A or less and designed for direct connection of aluminum conductors must be marked:","o":["SWD","AL/CU","TR","CO/ALR"],"a":3,"e":"Receptacles rated 20 A or less intended for direct connection of aluminum conductors must be marked CO/ALR.","r":"NEC 406.3(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"Push-in terminals on a 15 A receptacle may be used with which conductors?","o":["10 AWG aluminum","12 AWG stranded copper","14 AWG solid copper","Any 14 AWG conductor including stranded"],"a":2,"e":"Push-in terminals are limited to 15 A maximum circuits and 14 AWG solid copper (unless future listing permits other types).","r":"NEC 406.3(D)(3)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":1,"q":"When replacing a receptacle in a box that has an equipment grounding conductor, what type must be used?","o":["A nongrounding type","A GFCI only","A grounding-type receptacle connected to the EGC","Either type"],"a":2,"e":"If the box contains an EGC, a grounding-type receptacle connected to it must be used.","r":"NEC 406.4(D)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":3,"q":"A GFCI-required bathroom receptacle in a dwelling is being replaced. The box has no equipment grounding means. Which replacement is permitted?","o":["A GFCI receptacle marked 'No Equipment Ground'","A standard nongrounding receptacle with no GFCI","A standard grounding receptacle with a bonding jumper to the water pipe","A grounding receptacle connected to the neutral"],"a":0,"e":"Where there is no EGC in the box, the options are a nongrounding receptacle, a GFCI receptacle marked 'No Equipment Ground', or a grounding receptacle GFCI-protected and marked accordingly. Where GFCI is required, the GFCI options apply.","r":"NEC 406.4(D)(2),(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"A receptacle that requires GFCI protection is being replaced. What does the NEC require of the replacement?","o":["It must be a nongrounding type","No special protection","It must be GFCI protected","It must be tamper-resistant only"],"a":2,"e":"Where GFCI protection is required, a replacement receptacle must be GFCI protected.","r":"NEC 406.4(D)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"A replacement receptacle is installed at a location where tamper resistance is required. It must be:","o":["A hospital-grade receptacle","A listed tamper-resistant receptacle","A GFCI receptacle only","Any receptacle with a cover plate"],"a":1,"e":"Where tamper-resistant receptacles are required by 406.12, replacement receptacles must be listed tamper-resistant.","r":"NEC 406.4(D)(5)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"A replacement receptacle is installed at a location where weather-resistant receptacles are required. It must be:","o":["A hospital grade receptacle","A listed weather-resistant receptacle","Any receptacle in an in-use cover","A GFCI receptacle only"],"a":1,"e":"Where weather-resistant receptacles are required, replacements must be listed weather-resistant.","r":"NEC 406.4(D)(6)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"A receptacle outlet requires AFCI protection under 210.12. When it is replaced, which is acceptable?","o":["A GFCI receptacle only","A standard receptacle with no AFCI","A listed outlet branch-circuit type AFCI receptacle, a receptacle protected by an AFCI receptacle upstream, or a combination-type AFCI breaker","A nongrounding receptacle"],"a":2,"e":"Where AFCI protection is required, replacement receptacles must be a listed AFCI receptacle, be protected by a listed AFCI receptacle, or be protected by a listed combination-type AFCI breaker.","r":"NEC 406.4(D)(4)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":1,"q":"The grounding contacts of receptacles must be connected to what?","o":["The grounded conductor","A water pipe only","Nothing","An equipment grounding conductor per 250.146"],"a":3,"e":"Grounding contacts must be connected to an equipment grounding conductor in accordance with 250.146.","r":"NEC 406.4(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"A receptacle mounted on a box cover must be secured by what?","o":["One screw","A cover plate only","At least two screws, or be a listed assembly","Adhesive"],"a":2,"e":"Receptacles mounted to and supported by a cover must be secured by more than one screw or be a device assembly or box cover listed and identified for the application.","r":"NEC 406.5(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"Receptacles in dwelling unit countertops may not be installed in which position unless listed for the purpose?","o":["Vertical on a backsplash","Face-up","Face-down","Under a cabinet"],"a":1,"e":"Receptacles may not be installed in a face-up position in or on countertops unless listed as part of an assembly identified for the purpose.","r":"NEC 406.5(G)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"A box installed in a noncombustible wall surface may be set back from the finished surface by no more than what distance?","o":["1/8 in.","1/4 in.","1/2 in.","1/16 in."],"a":1,"e":"Boxes in noncombustible surfaces may be set back no more than 1/4 in. In combustible surfaces they must be flush.","r":"NEC 314.20","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"Surface finish gaps at the edge of a box must not exceed what?","o":["1/8 in.","3/8 in.","1/16 in.","1/4 in."],"a":0,"e":"Gaps in plaster, drywall or plasterboard surfaces around the box edge may not be more than 1/8 in.","r":"NEC 314.21","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":3,"q":"A ferrous metal faceplate must have a minimum thickness of what?","o":["0.020 in.","0.040 in.","0.030 in.","0.062 in."],"a":2,"e":"Ferrous metal faceplates must be at least 0.030 in. thick; nonferrous metal faceplates at least 0.040 in.","r":"NEC 406.6(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":3,"q":"An insulating faceplate that is not formed or reinforced must be noncombustible and have a minimum thickness of what?","o":["0.06 in.","0.25 in.","0.04 in.","0.10 in."],"a":3,"e":"Insulating faceplates must be noncombustible and at least 0.10 in. thick, unless formed or reinforced for adequate strength.","r":"NEC 406.6(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"A receptacle is installed in a damp location. What enclosure is required?","o":["A hospital-grade enclosure","None","One that is weatherproof only when a plug is inserted","One that is weatherproof when the receptacle is covered (attachment plug not inserted)"],"a":3,"e":"Receptacles in damp locations require an enclosure weatherproof when the receptacle is covered (no plug inserted).","r":"NEC 406.9(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"A 15 A or 20 A, 125 V receptacle is installed in a wet location. The enclosure must be weatherproof:","o":["Only if the circuit is GFCI protected","Only in winter","Whether or not the attachment plug is inserted (extra-duty cover)","Only when no plug is inserted"],"a":2,"e":"15 A and 20 A receptacles in wet locations require an enclosure that is weatherproof whether or not a plug is inserted, and the cover must be listed and identified as extra duty.","r":"NEC 406.9(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":1,"q":"Nonlocking 15 A and 20 A, 125 V receptacles installed in damp or wet locations must be which type?","o":["Hospital grade","Isolated ground","Listed weather-resistant","Tamper-resistant only"],"a":2,"e":"Nonlocking 15 A and 20 A, 125 V receptacles in damp or wet locations must be listed weather-resistant.","r":"NEC 406.9(A),(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"A receptacle may not be installed within what zone measured from the top of the bathtub rim or shower stall threshold?","o":["3 ft horizontally and 8 ft vertically","3 ft horizontally and 12 ft vertically","1 ft horizontally and 4 ft vertically","6 ft horizontally and 8 ft vertically"],"a":0,"e":"Receptacles may not be installed within a zone measured 3 ft horizontally and 8 ft vertically from the top of the bathtub rim or shower stall threshold, subject to exceptions.","r":"NEC 406.9(C)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":3,"q":"A bathroom receptacle is on a wall with the nearest edge of the tub 4 ft horizontally from the receptacle. The receptacle is 3 ft above the floor. Is it within the prohibited zone?","o":["Yes; it is within 8 ft vertically","No; GFCI protection is not required","No; 4 ft horizontal exceeds the 3 ft limit (and it must still be GFCI protected)","Yes; any receptacle in a bathroom with a tub is prohibited"],"a":2,"e":"The restricted zone extends 3 ft horizontally from the tub rim, so a receptacle 4 ft away is outside it. The 8 ft is a vertical measurement, and GFCI is still required in bathrooms.","r":"NEC 406.9(C); 210.8","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":1,"q":"In dwelling units, which receptacles must be listed tamper-resistant?","o":["None","Only those within 6 ft of a sink","Only those in bedrooms","All nonlocking-type 125 V, 15 A and 20 A receptacles"],"a":3,"e":"All nonlocking-type 125 V, 15 A and 20 A receptacles in dwelling units must be listed tamper-resistant.","r":"NEC 406.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"Tamper-resistant receptacles are required in which of the following?","o":["Parking garages","Warehouses","Outdoor lighting poles","Child care facilities and preschool and elementary education facilities"],"a":3,"e":"406.12 lists child care facilities and preschools/elementary education facilities, as well as dwelling units, guest rooms, and certain clinic and office areas.","r":"NEC 406.12","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"Which receptacle in a dwelling unit is exempt from the tamper-resistant requirement?","o":["A receptacle located more than 5-1/2 ft above the floor","A garage receptacle","A bedroom receptacle","A kitchen countertop receptacle"],"a":0,"e":"Receptacles located more than 5-1/2 ft above the floor are exempt, along with receptacles that are part of a luminaire or appliance and certain dedicated appliance receptacles.","r":"NEC 406.12 Exception","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"Tamper-resistant receptacles are required in which part of a hotel or motel?","o":["Boiler rooms","Maintenance sheds","Parking structures","Guest rooms and guest suites"],"a":3,"e":"Guest rooms and guest suites of hotels and motels are included among the areas requiring tamper-resistant receptacles.","r":"NEC 406.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"Tamper-resistant receptacles are required in which areas of clinics, medical offices and dental offices?","o":["Operating rooms only","Waiting rooms and public business office areas","Exterior lighting","Mechanical rooms"],"a":1,"e":"Waiting rooms and public business office areas of clinics, medical and dental offices (and similar outpatient facilities) are listed in 406.12.","r":"NEC 406.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"A 20 A branch circuit supplies multiple receptacles. Which receptacle ratings are permitted?","o":["20 A only","15 A only","30 A","15 A or 20 A"],"a":3,"e":"On a 20 A circuit with two or more outlets, receptacles may be rated 15 A or 20 A.","r":"NEC 210.21(B)(3), Table 210.21(B)(3); 406.4(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":3,"q":"A single receptacle (not duplex) is installed on an individual 20 A branch circuit. What rating must the receptacle have?","o":["Any rating","Not less than 20 A","30 A","15 A"],"a":1,"e":"A single receptacle on an individual branch circuit must have an ampere rating not less than that of the branch circuit.","r":"NEC 210.21(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"Receptacle mounting screws must be which type?","o":["The type furnished with a listed receptacle, or machine screws with 32 threads per inch","Sheet metal screws","Any wood screw","6-32 or 10-32 screws of any length"],"a":0,"e":"Screws for mounting must be the type provided with a listed receptacle, or machine screws having 32 threads per inch.","r":"NEC 406.5","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":3,"q":"A dwelling unit living room receptacle is controlled by a wall switch to provide the required lighting outlet. Does the controlled-receptacle marking apply?","o":["Yes; always","Yes; only if GFCI","Yes; only if 20 A","No; an exception covers receptacles controlled by a wall switch that provides the required room lighting"],"a":3,"e":"Receptacles controlled by a wall switch that supplies the required lighting outlet under 210.70(A)(1) Exception No. 2 are not required to be marked as controlled receptacles.","r":"NEC 406.3(F); 210.70(A)(1) Exception No. 2","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":1,"q":"Metal faceplates must be:","o":["Rated for 600 V","Connected to an equipment grounding conductor","Painted","Isolated from ground"],"a":1,"e":"Metal faceplates must be grounded.","r":"NEC 406.6(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":1,"q":"Faceplates for receptacles must do what with respect to the wall opening?","o":["Leave a gap for ventilation","Cover half of the opening","Be installed with one screw","Completely cover the opening and seat against the wall surface"],"a":3,"e":"Faceplates must completely cover the opening and seat against the wall surface.","r":"NEC 406.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"The grounding terminal of an isolated ground receptacle is connected to:","o":["The metal box","An insulated equipment grounding conductor run with the circuit conductors, not to the box","The mounting yoke","The grounded conductor"],"a":1,"e":"The isolated ground receptacle's grounding terminal is insulated from the mounting strap and connected to an insulated EGC run with the circuit conductors to the applicable grounding point.","r":"NEC 250.146(D); 406.3(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":1,"q":"A panelboard is being installed where the available fault current is 22,000 amperes. What is required of the panelboard's short-circuit current rating?","o":["It must be at least half of the available fault current","No rating is required if the panelboard has a main breaker","It must be at least 10,000 amperes regardless of the available fault current","It must be equal to or greater than the available fault current"],"a":3,"e":"Switchboards, switchgear and panelboards must have a short-circuit current rating not less than the available fault current. A main breaker does not remove this requirement.","r":"NEC 408.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":1,"q":"A circuit breaker is removed from a panelboard and the opening is left empty. How must the unused opening be treated?","o":["Closed using identified closures or other means providing protection substantially equivalent to the enclosure wall","Closed only if the panelboard is in a wet location","Left open if the bus is insulated","Covered with electrical tape"],"a":0,"e":"Unused openings for circuit breakers and switches must be closed with identified closures or equivalent means. Tape is not an identified closure.","r":"NEC 408.7","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":1,"q":"A panelboard in a commercial building is supplied by a feeder from a distribution panel in another room. What marking is required on the panelboard?","o":["Marking indicating the device or equipment where its power supply originates","No marking is required for panelboards","Marking of the feeder conductor insulation type","Marking showing the available fault current only"],"a":0,"e":"Switchboards, switchgear and panelboards supplied by feeders in other than one- or two-family dwellings must be marked to indicate where the power supply originates.","r":"NEC 408.4(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":1,"q":"Which information must the manufacturer durably mark on a panelboard?","o":["The voltage and phase only","Voltage, current rating, number of phases, and the manufacturer's name or trademark","Only the manufacturer's name","Only the voltage and current rating"],"a":1,"e":"Panelboards must be durably marked by the manufacturer with the voltage, current rating, number of phases, and the manufacturer's name or trademark.","r":"NEC 408.58","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":1,"q":"For a new three-phase switchboard or panelboard (not a four-wire delta), what is the required phase arrangement of the busbars when viewed from the front?","o":["C, B, A from front to back, top to bottom, left to right","Any arrangement if phases are identified","B, A, C from front to back","A, B, C from front to back, top to bottom, or left to right"],"a":3,"e":"Three-phase buses must be arranged A, B, C from front to back, top to bottom, or left to right as viewed from the front.","r":"NEC 408.3(E)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"In a three-phase, four-wire delta system with the midpoint of one phase grounded (high-leg delta), which bus phase must be the high leg in a panelboard?","o":["A phase","Any phase if marked orange","B phase","C phase"],"a":2,"e":"The high leg (the phase with the higher voltage to ground) must be the B phase in a panelboard or switchboard, in the middle position.","r":"NEC 408.3(E)(2); 110.15","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":1,"q":"On a 120/240-volt, three-phase, four-wire delta system, how must the conductor or busbar with the higher voltage to ground be identified?","o":["Orange color or other effective means","Blue color only","Red color only","It need not be identified in a panelboard"],"a":0,"e":"The phase with the higher voltage to ground (208 V) must be durably and permanently marked orange or by other effective means.","r":"NEC 110.15","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"A switchboard that is not totally enclosed is installed below a combustible ceiling with no fire-resistant shield. What is the minimum space between the top of the switchboard and the ceiling?","o":["1 foot","6 inches","2 feet","3 feet"],"a":3,"e":"A space of at least 900 mm (3 ft) is required between the top of a switchboard that is not totally enclosed and a combustible ceiling, unless a fire-resistant shield is provided.","r":"NEC 408.18(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"How must grounded (neutral) conductors be terminated in a panelboard?","o":["In an individual terminal not also used for another conductor, unless the terminal is identified for more than one conductor","On the equipment grounding terminal bar","Two grounded conductors under one screw if the same size","Spliced together under a wire connector inside the panelboard"],"a":0,"e":"Each grounded conductor must terminate in an individual terminal that is not also used for another conductor, except where the terminal is identified for multiple conductors.","r":"NEC 408.41","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"A panelboard in a building is supplied by a four-wire feeder from the service equipment in the same building. How must the panelboard's neutral bar and equipment grounding terminal bar be arranged?","o":["Both bars isolated from the enclosure","Neutral bar isolated from the enclosure, grounding bar bonded to the enclosure","Both bars bonded to the enclosure","Neutral bar bonded to the enclosure, grounding bar isolated"],"a":1,"e":"The neutral is bonded to ground only at the service (or separately derived system), so downstream panelboards keep the neutral bar isolated while the equipment grounding bar is bonded to the enclosure.","r":"NEC 408.40; 250.142","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum rating of the overcurrent protective device that protects a panelboard on its supply side?","o":["Not greater than the rating of the panelboard","150 percent of the panelboard rating","Any rating if the feeder is sized correctly","125 percent of the panelboard rating"],"a":0,"e":"Each panelboard must be protected by an overcurrent device with a rating not greater than that of the panelboard (subject to listed exceptions).","r":"NEC 408.36","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"A plug-in circuit breaker is back-fed to terminate field-installed ungrounded supply conductors in a panelboard (for example, a PV or generator connection). What additional securing is required?","o":["A tie wrap on the conductors","A label only","An additional fastener that requires other than a pull to release the breaker from the panelboard","None; the plug-in connection is sufficient"],"a":2,"e":"Plug-in back-fed devices used to terminate field-installed ungrounded supply conductors must be secured by an additional fastener that requires more than a pull to release.","r":"NEC 408.36(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"What must a panelboard provide regarding the number of overcurrent devices that can be installed?","o":["No limit on the number of devices","A directory that lists the maximum count","Physical means to prevent installation of more overcurrent devices than the panelboard was designed, rated and approved for","A limit of 42 devices in all panelboards"],"a":2,"e":"Panelboards must have physical means preventing installation of more overcurrent devices than they are designed, rated, and approved for. The old 42-circuit limit no longer applies.","r":"NEC 408.54","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"Which table governs the minimum wire-bending space at the top and bottom of an enclosure containing a panelboard?","o":["Table 312.6(A) only","Table 314.16(A)","Table 312.6(B)","Table 300.19"],"a":2,"e":"Wire-bending space within an enclosure containing a panelboard must comply with the dimensions in Table 312.6(B) for the conductor size at the terminals.","r":"NEC 408.55","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes how circuits in a panelboard directory must be identified?","o":["Identified only if the panelboard is in a commercial building","Identified only by circuit number","Each circuit identified with sufficient detail to distinguish it from all others, not dependent on transient conditions of occupancy","Identified by room occupant's name for ease of use"],"a":2,"e":"Every circuit and modification must be legibly identified as to purpose, in enough detail to distinguish it, and not based on transient occupancy conditions such as tenant names.","r":"NEC 408.4(A)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum ampere rating of a panelboard?","o":["The rating of the largest branch-circuit device","100 amperes","125 percent of the continuous load","Not less than the minimum feeder capacity required for the calculated load per Article 220"],"a":3,"e":"All panelboards must have a rating not less than the minimum feeder capacity required for the load calculated under Article 220.","r":"NEC 408.30","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"A surface-mounted nonwatertight panelboard enclosure is installed in a damp location. What mounting requirement applies?","o":["Mount with at least a 1/4 inch airspace between the enclosure and the wall","Mount with at least 1 inch airspace","Mount flush against the wall","Mount with at least 2 inches of airspace"],"a":0,"e":"Enclosures in damp or wet locations must prevent moisture from entering and surface-type enclosures must have at least 6 mm (1/4 in) airspace between the enclosure and the wall.","r":"NEC 408.37; 312.2(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"Which statement about the enclosure of a panelboard is correct?","o":["It may be any pull box","It must be dead front only if above 300 volts","It must be mounted in a cabinet, cutout box or enclosure designed for the purpose and be dead front","It may be an open frame if mounted high"],"a":2,"e":"Panelboards must be mounted in cabinets, cutout boxes, or enclosures designed for the purpose and be dead front, with exceptions for panelboards other than the externally-operable type.","r":"NEC 408.38","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"A panelboard in a commercial building contains circuit breakers that are readily accessible. What is the maximum height of the center of the grip of the operating handle of the highest breaker when in its highest position?","o":["2.5 m (8 ft)","2.0 m (6 ft 7 in) above the floor or working platform","1.7 m (5 ft 6 in)","1.2 m (4 ft)"],"a":1,"e":"Overcurrent devices must be readily accessible and the center of the grip of the operating handle, at its highest position, must not be more than 2.0 m (6 ft 7 in) above the floor or working platform.","r":"NEC 240.24(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":1,"q":"Where may a panelboard containing overcurrent devices NOT be installed in a dwelling unit?","o":["In a basement","In a bathroom","In a hallway","In a garage"],"a":1,"e":"Overcurrent devices must not be located in bathrooms of dwelling units, or guest rooms or suites of hotels and motels.","r":"NEC 240.24(E)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"A homeowner wants to place a panelboard in a bedroom clothes closet. What does the NEC require?","o":["It is not allowed because overcurrent devices must not be located in the vicinity of easily ignitible material such as clothes closets","It is allowed with a 3-ft clearance","It is allowed if the panel is in a dead-front enclosure","It is allowed if protected by AFCI breakers"],"a":0,"e":"Overcurrent devices must not be located in the vicinity of easily ignitible material, such as in clothes closets.","r":"NEC 240.24(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"A 480Y/277-volt panelboard has exposed live parts on one side of the working space and grounded concrete wall on the opposite side (Condition 2). What is the minimum depth of working space?","o":["3 ft","3 ft 6 in","6 ft","4 ft"],"a":1,"e":"For 151-600 volts to ground, Condition 2 requires 1.0 m (3 ft 6 in) of working space depth.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(1), Table 110.26(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum width of the working space in front of a 20-inch-wide panelboard?","o":["20 inches","30 inches, or the width of the equipment, whichever is greater","36 inches","The width of the equipment plus 6 inches"],"a":1,"e":"The working space width must be at least 762 mm (30 in) or the width of the equipment, whichever is greater. Here 30 in is greater than 20 in.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"What dedicated electrical space is required above a panelboard?","o":["Equal to the width and depth of the equipment, extending from the floor to 6 ft above the equipment or to the structural ceiling, whichever is lower","None above the panelboard","Space 18 inches above","Space 3 ft above for piping"],"a":0,"e":"The dedicated space equals the width and depth of the equipment and extends from the floor to a height of 1.8 m (6 ft) above the equipment or to the structural ceiling, whichever is lower. Foreign piping and ducts are not allowed in it.","r":"NEC 110.26(E)(1)(a)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"A switchboard rated 2000 A and 8 ft wide contains overcurrent devices. What entrance requirement applies to the working space?","o":["A single entrance is sufficient","Two entrances at the same end","No entrance required if open","One entrance of the required size at each end of the working space"],"a":3,"e":"For equipment rated 1200 A or more and over 1.8 m (6 ft) wide that contains overcurrent, switching or control devices, one entrance is required at each end of the working space (with exceptions).","r":"NEC 110.26(C)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":1,"q":"May the required working space in front of a panelboard be used for storage?","o":["Yes, if materials are movable","Yes, if the panelboard is dead-front","No, it must be kept clear and not used for storage","Yes, if a path 18 inches wide remains"],"a":2,"e":"Working space must not be used for storage, and live parts there must be guarded if work is required while energized and the space is occupied.","r":"NEC 110.26(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum headroom of the working space for a panelboard 5 ft tall?","o":["6 ft","6 1/2 ft (2.0 m) or the height of the equipment, whichever is greater","5 ft","8 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Working space height must be clear for at least 2.0 m (6 1/2 ft) or the height of the equipment, whichever is greater.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":1,"q":"Which statement about illumination of the working space around panelboards installed indoors is correct?","o":["Illumination is not required","Illumination is required but must be on a timer","Illumination is required and must not be controlled by automatic means only","Illumination is required only in commercial buildings"],"a":2,"e":"Illumination is required for working spaces about service equipment, switchboards, switchgear, panelboards and motor control centers installed indoors, and it must not be controlled by automatic means only.","r":"NEC 110.26(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":3,"q":"A 100-ampere main-lug-only panelboard (no main breaker) is to be fed by a feeder protected by a 125-ampere breaker at the source, with no other overcurrent device ahead of the panelboard. Is this permitted?","o":["No, because the panelboard must be protected by an overcurrent device rated not more than the panelboard rating","Yes, because the panelboard has a main lug rating","Yes, with a label","Yes, if the conductors are sized for 125 A"],"a":0,"e":"A panelboard must be protected by an overcurrent device with a rating not greater than the panelboard's rating; 125 A exceeds the 100 A panelboard rating.","r":"NEC 408.36","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear and Panelboards","d":3,"q":"A 480-volt panelboard has exposed live parts on its front, and another switchboard with exposed live parts directly across the aisle (Condition 3). What is the minimum aisle working space depth?","o":["4 ft","3 ft 6 in","3 ft 9 in","3 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Condition 3 (exposed live parts on both sides of the working space) at 151-600 volts requires 1.2 m (4 ft).","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(1), Table 110.26(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":1,"q":"What does the NEC require regarding listing of luminaires, lampholders and retrofit kits?","o":["Only outdoor luminaires must be listed","They must be listed","Listing is optional if installed by a licensed electrician","Only luminaires over 100 watts must be listed"],"a":1,"e":"Luminaires, lampholders, decorative lighting products and retrofit kits must be listed.","r":"NEC 410.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":1,"q":"Which statement about live parts of luminaires, lampholders and receptacles used in luminaires is correct?","o":["They may be exposed if the voltage is 120 volts or less","They may be exposed if marked with a warning label","They must have no live parts normally exposed to contact, with limited exceptions","They may be exposed if above 8 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Luminaires, lampholders, lamps and rosettes must have no live parts normally exposed to contact, with narrow exceptions such as cleat-type lampholders located at least 2.5 m (8 ft) above the floor.","r":"NEC 410.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"What is the zone around a bathtub within which luminaires must be marked suitable for damp or wet locations, unless otherwise permitted?","o":["Within 1 ft horizontally and 8 ft vertically","Within 5 ft horizontally and 10 ft vertically of the tub rim","Within 3 ft horizontally and 8 ft vertically of the top of the bathtub rim or shower stall threshold","Within 6 ft horizontally and 6 ft vertically"],"a":2,"e":"No parts of cord-connected luminaires, chain-, cable-, or cord-suspended luminaires, lighting track, pendants or ceiling fans may be located within a zone measured 900 mm (3 ft) horizontally and 2.5 m (8 ft) vertically from the top of the bathtub rim or shower threshold. Luminaires within the zone must be marked for damp locations, or wet locations if subject to shower spray.","r":"NEC 410.10(D)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":1,"q":"A luminaire installed outdoors where it is exposed to direct rain must be marked:","o":["Suitable for Dry Locations","Suitable for Damp Locations","Raintight only","Suitable for Wet Locations"],"a":3,"e":"Luminaires installed in wet locations must be marked 'Suitable for Wet Locations'. Damp-location luminaires are for protected locations such as covered porches.","r":"NEC 410.10(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"A luminaire is installed under a roofed open porch, protected from direct rain but subject to moisture. At minimum, how must it be marked?","o":["Suitable for Dry Locations","No marking is required","Suitable for Damp Locations","Suitable for Corrosive Locations"],"a":2,"e":"Luminaires in damp locations (partially protected locations such as roofed porches) must be marked suitable for damp locations or wet locations.","r":"NEC 410.10(A); Art. 100 Location, Damp","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"A surface-mounted incandescent luminaire with a completely enclosed light source is installed in a clothes closet. What is the minimum clearance from the nearest point of the storage space?","o":["18 inches","3 inches","6 inches","12 inches"],"a":3,"e":"Surface-mounted incandescent or LED luminaires with a completely enclosed light source require 300 mm (12 in) clearance to the storage space.","r":"NEC 410.16(C)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"A surface-mounted fluorescent luminaire is installed in a clothes closet. What is the minimum clearance from the nearest point of the storage space?","o":["3 inches","6 inches","12 inches","18 inches"],"a":1,"e":"Surface-mounted fluorescent luminaires require 150 mm (6 in) clearance from the storage space.","r":"NEC 410.16(C)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":1,"q":"Which luminaire type is not permitted in a clothes closet?","o":["Surface-mounted fluorescent luminaire","Recessed fluorescent luminaire","Pendant luminaires or lampholders, and luminaires with open or partially enclosed incandescent lamps","Recessed luminaire with a completely enclosed lamp"],"a":2,"e":"Incandescent luminaires with open or partially enclosed lamps and pendant luminaires or lampholders are not permitted in clothes closets.","r":"NEC 410.16(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"For the purposes of luminaire clearances, the closet storage space extends horizontally from the sides and back walls of the closet by what distance (at heights up to 6 ft or the highest rod)?","o":["24 inches (600 mm)","6 inches (150 mm)","36 inches (900 mm)","12 inches (300 mm)"],"a":0,"e":"The storage space is bounded by planes at 600 mm (24 in) from the side and back walls up to 1.8 m (6 ft) or the highest rod, then 300 mm (12 in) or the shelf width at the ceiling.","r":"NEC 410.2 (Closet Storage Space); 410.16(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":1,"q":"A luminaire weighing more than what weight, or exceeding what dimension, may not be supported by the screw shell of a lampholder?","o":["3 lb or 12 in","6 lb (3 kg) or 16 in (400 mm)","10 lb or 24 in","1 lb or 8 in"],"a":1,"e":"A luminaire weighing more than 3 kg (6 lb) or exceeding 400 mm (16 in) in any dimension must not be supported by the screw shell of a lampholder.","r":"NEC 410.30(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"A non-IC recessed luminaire is installed in a combustible ceiling. What is the minimum spacing between the recessed luminaire and the surrounding combustible material, except at points of support?","o":["1 inch","1/2 inch","1/8 inch","1/4 inch"],"a":1,"e":"Recessed luminaires must be installed so that adjacent combustible material is not subject to temperatures above 90 degrees C, and non-Type IC luminaires must be spaced at least 13 mm (1/2 in) from combustible materials except at points of support.","r":"NEC 410.116(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"Thermal insulation may not be installed within what distance above a recessed luminaire enclosure, wiring compartment or ballast, unless the luminaire is identified as Type IC?","o":["6 inches","3 inches (75 mm)","12 inches","1 inch"],"a":1,"e":"Thermal insulation must not be installed above a recessed luminaire or within 75 mm (3 in) of the enclosure, wiring compartment or ballast, unless it is identified for contact with insulation (Type IC).","r":"NEC 410.116(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":1,"q":"Combustible ceiling finish is exposed between the edge of a luminaire canopy and the outlet box. What is required?","o":["Nothing is required","It must be covered with noncombustible material","It must be covered with electrical tape","It must be painted with fire-retardant paint"],"a":1,"e":"Combustible wall or ceiling finish exposed between the edge of a luminaire canopy or pan and the outlet box must be covered with noncombustible material.","r":"NEC 410.23","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"In a completed installation, an outlet box that contains no luminaire or lampholder fixture must be:","o":["Marked with a label only","Filled with insulating foam","Covered with a cover unless covered by a luminaire canopy, lampholder, receptacle or similar device","Left open for future use"],"a":2,"e":"In completed installations, each outlet box must have a cover, faceplate or luminaire canopy, except where covered by a lampholder, receptacle, or similar device.","r":"NEC 410.22","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"How must a luminaire be fastened to the framing members of a suspended ceiling?","o":["By adhesive","Securely fastened to the ceiling framing member by mechanical means such as bolts, screws or rivets, or by identified clips","By hanging it from the tile with wire","By resting it on the ceiling tile"],"a":1,"e":"Luminaires must be securely fastened to ceiling framing members by mechanical means; identified clips are also permitted.","r":"NEC 410.36(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":1,"q":"May a luminaire be used as a raceway for circuit conductors?","o":["Only for 120-volt circuits","Only if listed and marked for use as a raceway","Only in dwelling units","Yes, for any circuit"],"a":1,"e":"Luminaires must not be used as a raceway for circuit conductors unless listed and marked for use as a raceway.","r":"NEC 410.64","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"Feeder or branch-circuit conductors within 3 inches (75 mm) of a ballast must have an insulation temperature rating of at least:","o":["60 degrees C","90 degrees C unless the luminaire is marked otherwise","105 degrees C only","75 degrees C in all cases"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors within 75 mm (3 in) of a ballast, LED driver, power supply or transformer must have insulation rated not lower than 90 degrees C, unless the luminaire is listed and marked as suitable for a different temperature.","r":"NEC 410.68","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"A screw-shell lampholder is supplied by a circuit with a grounded conductor. Where must the grounded conductor connect?","o":["To the equipment grounding conductor only","To either contact","To the center contact","To the screw shell"],"a":3,"e":"Where supplied by a circuit having a grounded conductor, the grounded conductor must be connected to the screw shell.","r":"NEC 410.90","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"A 4-foot section of lighting track is installed. Unless identified for greater spacing, how many supports are required?","o":["Three","Two","Four","One"],"a":1,"e":"A single section of track 1.2 m (4 ft) or shorter must have at least two supports. Longer continuous runs need one additional support per 1.2 m (4 ft) section.","r":"NEC 410.154","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is a location where lighting track is not permitted?","o":["Where concealed","In a retail store","On a wall of a living room","On a ceiling in an office"],"a":0,"e":"Lighting track must not be installed where concealed, where subject to physical damage, corrosive vapors, wet or damp locations, hazardous locations, or within the bathtub zone.","r":"NEC 410.151(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":1,"q":"What may be installed on lighting track?","o":["Only luminaires over 5 ft high","Any listed device","Only lighting track fittings","Any receptacle or fitting"],"a":2,"e":"Only lighting track fittings shall be installed on lighting track.","r":"NEC 410.151(B)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"Fluorescent luminaires with ballasts installed indoors (other than simple reactance ballasts) must have what protection?","o":["Integral thermal protection","A GFCI","A surge protector","A fuse only"],"a":0,"e":"Fluorescent ballasts installed indoors, except simple reactance ballasts, must have integral thermal protection.","r":"NEC 410.130(E)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":3,"q":"A bathroom ceiling-mounted luminaire marked only 'Suitable for Dry Locations' is to be installed 7 feet above the top of a bathtub rim and 2 feet horizontally from the rim. Is this acceptable?","o":["Yes, since it is ceiling mounted","Yes, if on a GFCI circuit","Yes, if above 6 ft","No, it is inside the 3 ft by 8 ft zone and must be marked for damp or wet locations"],"a":3,"e":"The zone extends 3 ft horizontally and 8 ft vertically from the top of the tub rim. Luminaires there must be marked for damp locations (wet if subject to shower spray).","r":"NEC 410.10(D)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":3,"q":"An installer places a surface-mounted incandescent luminaire with a completely enclosed lamp 8 inches from the nearest point of the storage space in a clothes closet. Is this compliant?","o":["No, a minimum 12 inch clearance is required","Yes, any enclosed fixture is allowed","Yes, since the lamp is enclosed","No, 24 inches is required"],"a":0,"e":"A 12 in clearance to the storage space is required for surface-mounted incandescent luminaires with completely enclosed sources. 8 in is insufficient.","r":"NEC 410.16(C)(1)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":3,"q":"A non-IC recessed luminaire is installed in an attic where blown-in insulation will be added. Which statement is correct?","o":["Insulation may cover the luminaire","Insulation may touch the sides but not the top","Insulation must be kept at least 3 inches from the luminaire enclosure, wiring compartment and ballast, and must not be above it","Insulation may be placed anywhere if the lamp is LED"],"a":2,"e":"Non-IC recessed luminaires must not have insulation above them or within 75 mm (3 in) of the enclosure, wiring compartment or ballast.","r":"NEC 410.116(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":3,"q":"An 8-pound pendant luminaire with a 20-inch globe is proposed to hang from a lampholder screw shell. Is this acceptable?","o":["Yes, if on a GFCI circuit","Yes, if the ceiling is non-combustible","Yes, the lampholder is listed","No, because it exceeds 6 lb and 16 in so it may not be supported by the screw shell"],"a":3,"e":"A luminaire over 3 kg (6 lb) or over 400 mm (16 in) in any dimension must not be supported by the screw shell of a lampholder. This one exceeds both.","r":"NEC 410.30(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"Luminaires with exposed conductive parts must be:","o":["Connected to an equipment grounding conductor","Wired with a neutral only","Insulated from ground","Painted"],"a":0,"e":"Luminaires with exposed conductive parts must be connected to an equipment grounding conductor, with limited exceptions.","r":"NEC 410.42","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":1,"q":"Luminaires designed for incandescent lamps must be marked with:","o":["Maximum allowable lamp wattage","The installer's license number","The circuit number","Only the manufacturer's name"],"a":0,"e":"Luminaires using incandescent lamps must be marked with the maximum allowable lamp wattage, in a location visible during relamping.","r":"NEC 410.120","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"A luminaire installed within a shower stall where it is subject to shower spray must be marked:","o":["No marking is required if GFCI protected","Suitable for Damp Locations only","Suitable for Dry Locations","Suitable for Wet Locations"],"a":3,"e":"Luminaires subject to shower spray directly must be listed for wet locations.","r":"NEC 410.10(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":1,"q":"Appliances must have no live parts normally exposed to contact, except for which type of part?","o":["Motor windings","Switch contacts","Terminal lugs","Open-resistance heating elements such as the element of a toaster"],"a":3,"e":"The only exception is parts functioning as open-resistance heating elements, such as a toaster element, which are necessarily exposed.","r":"NEC 422.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":1,"q":"What does the NEC require regarding listing of appliances?","o":["Only cord-and-plug appliances must be listed","Listing is optional for fixed appliances","Appliances must be listed","Only appliances over 1000 watts must be listed"],"a":2,"e":"Appliances must be listed by a qualified electrical testing laboratory unless otherwise permitted.","r":"NEC 422.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":1,"q":"What type of protection is required for cord-and-plug-connected high-pressure spray washing machines?","o":["A surge protective device","A GFCI for personal protection","No special protection","AFCI protection"],"a":1,"e":"High-pressure spray washing machines are among the appliances requiring GFCI protection for personnel.","r":"NEC 422.5(A)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"A cord-and-plug-connected vending machine manufactured after 2004 must have GFCI protection provided where?","o":["No GFCI is required","In the branch-circuit breaker only","Integral with the attachment plug or in the power supply cord within 12 in of the plug","At the service equipment only"],"a":2,"e":"Newer vending machines must include a GFCI as an integral part of the attachment plug or within 300 mm (12 in) of it in the cord. Older machines must be plugged into a GFCI-protected outlet.","r":"NEC 422.51","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum rating of an individual branch circuit supplying a single appliance?","o":["15 amperes","125 percent of the marked rating in all cases","20 amperes","Not less than the marked rating of the appliance"],"a":3,"e":"The rating of an individual branch circuit must not be less than the marked rating of the appliance or appliance load.","r":"NEC 422.10(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"A nonmotor appliance rated 13.3 amperes or less has no marked maximum overcurrent protection. What is the maximum rating of the overcurrent device protecting its branch circuit?","o":["20 amperes","30 amperes","25 amperes","15 amperes"],"a":0,"e":"For appliances rated not over 13.3 A with no marked protective device size, the branch-circuit overcurrent device must not exceed 20 A.","r":"NEC 422.11(E)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"A nonmotor appliance is rated 20 amperes and is not marked with a maximum overcurrent device size. What is the maximum branch-circuit overcurrent protection permitted?","o":["25 amperes","40 amperes","30 amperes","20 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"For appliances rated over 13.3 A with no marking, protection must not exceed 150 percent of the appliance rating: 20 x 1.5 = 30 A.","r":"NEC 422.11(E)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"Which of the following may be supplied from the same branch circuit as central heating equipment?","o":["Basement lighting","Auxiliary equipment such as pumps, valves, humidifiers and electrostatic air cleaners directly associated with the heating equipment","The refrigerator","A bedroom receptacle"],"a":1,"e":"Central heating equipment must be on an individual branch circuit, except that directly associated auxiliary equipment may be connected to the same circuit.","r":"NEC 422.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"The branch-circuit rating for a fixed storage-type water heater with a capacity of 120 gallons or less is not less than what percent of the nameplate rating?","o":["100 percent","125 percent","115 percent","150 percent"],"a":1,"e":"A storage-type water heater of 450 L (120 gal) or less is considered a continuous load, so its branch circuit must be rated at least 125 percent of the nameplate rating.","r":"NEC 422.13","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":3,"q":"A 50-gallon storage water heater is rated 4500 watts at 240 volts, single phase. What is the minimum branch-circuit ampere rating required?","o":["18.75 A","22.5 A","30 A","23.4 A"],"a":3,"e":"I = 4500/240 = 18.75 A. Times 125 percent gives 23.4 A minimum branch-circuit rating.","r":"NEC 422.13","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"A cord-and-plug-connected kitchen waste disposer (garbage disposal) is installed. What is the permitted length of its flexible cord?","o":["12 to 24 inches","3 to 6 feet","6 to 10 feet","18 to 36 inches"],"a":3,"e":"Cords for garbage disposers must be 450 mm to 900 mm (18 in to 36 in) long, with the receptacle accessible.","r":"NEC 422.16(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"A built-in dishwasher is connected with a flexible cord and plug. What is the permitted cord length?","o":["6 to 8 feet","Any length up to 15 feet","3 to 4 feet","18 to 36 inches"],"a":2,"e":"Built-in dishwashers and trash compactors may use a cord 900 mm to 1.2 m (3 ft to 4 ft) long if the receptacle is accessible and other conditions are met.","r":"NEC 422.16(B)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":3,"q":"An installer connects a garbage disposer with a 4-foot flexible cord. Is this compliant?","o":["Yes, since 4 ft is common","No, a cord is never permitted","No, the maximum cord length is 36 inches","Yes, if the cord is 14 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Garbage disposer cords must be between 18 in and 36 in. A 4-ft (48 in) cord is too long.","r":"NEC 422.16(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"An outlet box used as the sole support of a ceiling-suspended (paddle) fan must be listed, marked as suitable for the purpose, and must not support fans weighing more than:","o":["50 pounds","100 pounds","35 pounds","70 pounds"],"a":3,"e":"Boxes used as sole support for paddle fans must not support fans that weigh more than 32 kg (70 lb).","r":"NEC 422.18; 314.27(C)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"An outlet box listed to support a ceiling fan heavier than 35 pounds must be marked with what additional information?","o":["The maximum fan speed","The date of manufacture","The maximum weight to be supported","The installer's name"],"a":2,"e":"For boxes designed to support more than 16 kg (35 lb), the marking must include the maximum weight to be supported.","r":"NEC 422.18; 314.27(C)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":3,"q":"A 40-pound ceiling fan is to be hung from an outlet box marked 'suitable for fan support, 35 lb max.' Is this acceptable?","o":["No, the box is not marked for the fan's weight","Yes, if the fan is on a dimmer","Yes, since it is marked for fan support","Yes, if the box is metal"],"a":0,"e":"Boxes designed to support more than 35 lb must be marked with their maximum weight. A box marked 35 lb max cannot support a 40 lb fan.","r":"NEC 422.18; 314.27(C)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"For a permanently connected appliance rated not over 300 volt-amperes or 1/8 horsepower, what may serve as the disconnecting means?","o":["The service disconnect only","A pull-out fuse block only","None is required","The branch-circuit overcurrent device"],"a":3,"e":"For permanently connected appliances not over 300 VA or 1/8 hp, the branch-circuit switch or circuit breaker may serve as the disconnecting means.","r":"NEC 422.31(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"A permanently connected motor-driven appliance rated over 1/8 horsepower has its disconnecting means where?","o":["Within 50 ft but not locked","At the service equipment only","In sight from the appliance, or capable of being locked in the open position","Anywhere in the building"],"a":2,"e":"For permanently connected motor-driven appliances over 1/8 hp, the disconnecting means must be within sight of the motor controller or be capable of being locked open.","r":"NEC 422.31(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":1,"q":"For a cord-and-plug-connected appliance, what may serve as the disconnecting means?","o":["A wall switch only","Nothing; a separate switch is always required","The attachment plug and receptacle","The main breaker only"],"a":2,"e":"For cord-and-plug-connected appliances, an accessible attachment plug and receptacle may serve as the disconnecting means.","r":"NEC 422.33(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":1,"q":"What must the nameplate of an electrical appliance show?","o":["The identifying name and the rating in volts and amperes, or in volts and watts","Only the weight","Only the serial number","Only the price"],"a":0,"e":"Each appliance must have a nameplate with its identifying name and rating in volts and amperes, or volts and watts, plus frequency if specific.","r":"NEC 422.60(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":1,"q":"What does the NEC require for each appliance with respect to disconnecting?","o":["Only a pilot light","A means to disconnect it from all ungrounded conductors","A disconnect at the service only","No disconnecting means"],"a":1,"e":"Each appliance must be provided with a means to disconnect it from all ungrounded conductors as specified in Part III of Article 422.","r":"NEC 422.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":3,"q":"A permanently connected 1/2 horsepower motor-driven appliance is located where the branch-circuit breaker panel is out of sight. What must be done to meet the disconnecting-means rule?","o":["Label the appliance","Nothing, the panel breaker is sufficient","Install a disconnect within sight, or provide a means to lock the panel breaker in the open position","Install an additional fuse"],"a":2,"e":"The disconnecting means must be in sight from the appliance or be capable of being locked in the open position.","r":"NEC 422.31(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"If an appliance requires motor overload protection that is external to the appliance, what must the nameplate show?","o":["The utility name","Nothing special","That external overload protection is required","The wattage only"],"a":2,"e":"Where motor overload protection external to the appliance is required, the appliance must be so marked.","r":"NEC 422.60(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"The exposed heating element of a toaster is permitted to be uninsulated because:","o":["It has a GFCI plug","It is below 50 volts","Toasters are low voltage","Open-resistance heating elements are the only live parts allowed to be exposed"],"a":3,"e":"Parts functioning as open-resistance heating elements, such as the toaster element, are necessarily exposed and are the exception to the no-exposed-live-parts rule.","r":"NEC 422.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":3,"q":"A permanently connected 120-volt appliance draws 250 volt-amperes (no motor over 1/8 hp). Which disconnecting means is acceptable?","o":["The branch-circuit circuit breaker","None is required","Only a separate fused switch","Only a motor-circuit switch"],"a":0,"e":"At 300 VA or less, the branch-circuit breaker or switch is permitted to be the disconnecting means.","r":"NEC 422.31(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"An appliance nameplate states a maximum overcurrent device size of 15 amperes. What is the effect on the branch-circuit protection?","o":["The marking is advisory","It must not exceed the marked 15 A","It may be 125 percent of the marking","It may be protected at up to 20 A"],"a":1,"e":"Where the appliance is marked with a maximum OCPD size, the branch-circuit protection must not exceed that rating.","r":"NEC 422.11(E)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"Cord-and-plug-connected electrically heated appliances over 50 watts that produce surface temperatures above 250 degrees F where the cord may contact must use:","o":["Heater cords of an approved type","Zip cord","Armored cable","Any flexible cord"],"a":0,"e":"Such appliances must have an approved heater cord from Table 400.4.","r":"NEC 422.43(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":3,"q":"A 1500-watt, 120-volt portable heater with no marked maximum overcurrent device size is connected to a 20-ampere branch circuit. Is this permitted?","o":["Yes only if GFCI","No, a 25 A circuit is required","No, 15 A is the maximum","Yes, because the rated current is 12.5 A (13.3 A or less) and 20 A is the maximum"],"a":3,"e":"1500/120 = 12.5 A, below 13.3 A, so the branch-circuit protection may be up to 20 A.","r":"NEC 422.11(E)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":2,"q":"A single branch circuit supplies two or more outlets for fixed electric space-heating equipment in a dwelling. What is the maximum rating of that branch circuit?","o":["50 amperes","20 amperes","30 amperes","40 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"Branch circuits supplying two or more outlets for fixed electric space-heating equipment must be rated not over 30 A.","r":"NEC 424.3(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":2,"q":"In other than a dwelling unit, what is the maximum branch-circuit rating that may supply two or more outlets of fixed infrared heating equipment?","o":["20 amperes","30 amperes","50 amperes","60 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"Fixed infrared heating equipment in other than dwelling units is permitted on circuits rated not over 50 A.","r":"NEC 424.3(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":1,"q":"Branch-circuit conductors and overcurrent devices supplying fixed electric space-heating equipment containing a motor must be sized at not less than what percent of the total load of the motors and heaters?","o":["100 percent","150 percent","115 percent","125 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Fixed electric space-heating loads are treated as continuous, so conductors and OCPDs are sized at not less than 125 percent of the total load of motors and heaters.","r":"NEC 424.3(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":3,"q":"A fixed electric unit heater has 10 amperes of resistance heating load and a 2-ampere blower motor, with no other loads. What is the minimum ampere rating the branch-circuit conductors must be sized for, based on this rule?","o":["14 A","15 A","12 A","20 A"],"a":1,"e":"Total load is 12 A; 12 x 1.25 = 15 A minimum.","r":"NEC 424.3(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":1,"q":"The disconnecting means for fixed electric space-heating equipment must disconnect the heater, motor controller and supplementary overcurrent devices from:","o":["The neutral only","The equipment grounding conductor","All ungrounded conductors","Only one ungrounded conductor"],"a":2,"e":"The disconnecting means must open all ungrounded conductors of the heater, its motor controllers and supplementary overcurrent protective devices.","r":"NEC 424.19","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":1,"q":"For a cord-and-plug-connected fixed electric space-heating unit, what may serve as the disconnecting means?","o":["A separable connection such as an attachment plug and receptacle","Nothing; a fused switch is required","The service disconnect","A thermostat only"],"a":0,"e":"For cord-and-plug-connected fixed space heaters, a separable connection such as a plug and receptacle may serve as the disconnecting means.","r":"NEC 424.19","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":2,"q":"A thermostatically controlled switching device may serve as both the controller and the disconnecting means only if it has:","o":["A marked 'off' position that directly opens all ungrounded conductors when manually placed there","A rating of at least 60 A","A pilot light","A mercury contact"],"a":0,"e":"It must have a marked off position that directly opens all ungrounded conductors and cannot automatically reenergize the circuit after being placed in off.","r":"NEC 424.20(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum overcurrent protection for resistance-type heating elements in electric space-heating equipment?","o":["48 amperes","60 amperes","100 amperes","30 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"Resistance-type heating elements in electric space-heating equipment must be protected at not more than 60 A.","r":"NEC 424.22(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":2,"q":"Fixed electric space-heating equipment rated more than what ampere value must have its heating elements subdivided, with each subdivided load not exceeding that value?","o":["30 amperes","48 amperes","60 amperes","100 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"Equipment rated more than 48 A must have heating elements subdivided into loads of not more than 48 A each.","r":"NEC 424.22(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":3,"q":"A fixed electric resistance heater has a total heating load of 72 amperes. What must be done to comply?","o":["Use a 125 A breaker","No subdivision is required","Protect the entire load with one 90 A device","The load must be subdivided so no subdivided load exceeds 48 A"],"a":3,"e":"Equipment with heating loads over 48 A must have the elements subdivided into loads not exceeding 48 A, each protected at not more than 60 A.","r":"NEC 424.22(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":1,"q":"What must the nameplate of each unit of fixed electric space-heating equipment show?","o":["The date installed","The identifying name and normal rating in volts and watts, or volts and amperes","The price","The contractor's name"],"a":1,"e":"Each unit must have a nameplate with its identifying name and normal rating in volts and watts or volts and amperes.","r":"NEC 424.28(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":2,"q":"If fixed electric space-heating equipment requires supply conductors with insulation rated higher than 60 degrees C, what is required?","o":["It must be clearly and permanently marked, visible after installation","Nothing special","A separate disconnect","A special permit"],"a":0,"e":"Such equipment must be clearly and permanently marked, and the marking must be plainly visible after installation (it may be adjacent to the field connection box).","r":"NEC 424.11","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":2,"q":"Fixed electric space-heating equipment installed in a damp or wet location must be:","o":["Installed only on GFCI circuits of 15 A","Listed for the location and installed so water cannot enter or accumulate in wired sections or components","Any listed indoor type","Covered with a tarp"],"a":1,"e":"Heaters in damp or wet locations must be listed for such locations and constructed and installed to prevent water entering or accumulating.","r":"NEC 424.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":1,"q":"Fixed electric space-heating equipment must be installed with what regarding adjacent combustible material?","o":["No spacing","Contact with the material","Spacing only in commercial buildings","The spacing required between the heater and adjacent combustible material"],"a":3,"e":"Heaters must be installed to provide the required spacing between the heater and adjacent combustible material, per the listing and instructions.","r":"NEC 424.13","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":1,"q":"Exposed noncurrent-carrying metal parts of fixed electric space-heating equipment must be:","o":["Grounded as required by Article 250","Bonded to the neutral only","Left isolated","Painted"],"a":0,"e":"Exposed noncurrent-carrying metal parts of fixed electric space-heating equipment must be grounded as required in Article 250.","r":"NEC 424.14","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":2,"q":"Wiring located above a heated ceiling must be spaced at least how far above the heated ceiling?","o":["1 inch","12 inches","6 inches","2 inches"],"a":3,"e":"Wiring above heated ceilings must be spaced at least 50 mm (2 in) above the ceiling and considered as operating at a 50 degrees C ambient.","r":"NEC 424.36","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":2,"q":"The ampacity of conductors located above a heated ceiling (in the space above) is calculated on the basis of what ambient temperature?","o":["50 degrees C","30 degrees C","40 degrees C","60 degrees C"],"a":0,"e":"Wiring above heated ceilings is considered to operate at an ambient of 50 degrees C (122 degrees F).","r":"NEC 424.36","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":1,"q":"Heating cables for space heating must not extend beyond:","o":["The room or area in which they originate","10 feet","The first wall","The building footprint"],"a":0,"e":"Heating cables shall not extend beyond the room or area in which they originate.","r":"NEC 424.38(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":2,"q":"Electric heating cables embedded in a bathroom floor must be provided with what protection?","o":["A surge protective device","No special protection","AFCI protection","GFCI protection for personnel"],"a":3,"e":"GFCI protection for personnel is required for cables installed in electrically heated bathroom floors and in hydromassage bathtub locations.","r":"NEC 424.44","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":1,"q":"What does the NEC require regarding listing of fixed electric space-heating equipment?","o":["Only when portable","It must be listed","Only if over 1500 W","Listing is not required"],"a":1,"e":"Fixed electric heating equipment must be listed.","r":"NEC 424.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":1,"q":"Fixed electric space-heating equipment subject to physical damage must be:","o":["Unprotected if above 6 ft","Moved","Protected in an approved manner","Painted"],"a":2,"e":"Where subject to physical damage, fixed electric space-heating equipment must be protected in an approved manner.","r":"NEC 424.12(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":1,"q":"A switch or circuit breaker used as a disconnecting means for fixed electric space-heating equipment must be:","o":["Single-pole only","Indicating type","Non-indicating","Mercury type"],"a":1,"e":"Switches and circuit breakers used as disconnecting means must be of the indicating type.","r":"NEC 424.21","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":3,"q":"A line-voltage thermostat has a marked 'off' position, but it can energize the heater automatically after being set to 'off'. Can it be the disconnecting means?","o":["Yes, if labeled","Yes, if the heater is under 10 A","Yes, always","No, a disconnecting device must not allow the circuit to be energized automatically after being manually turned off"],"a":3,"e":"A thermostat used as a disconnecting means must be designed so the circuit cannot be energized automatically after being manually placed in off.","r":"NEC 424.20(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":2,"q":"May the receptacle outlet on an electric baseboard heater be supplied from the heater circuit?","o":["Yes","Yes, if GFCI","Yes, up to 15 A","No, receptacle outlets must not be connected to the heater circuits"],"a":3,"e":"Receptacle outlets, whether factory-installed in baseboard heaters or provided as a listed assembly, must not be connected to the heater circuits.","r":"NEC 424.9","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","d":2,"q":"A listed electric baseboard heater has a factory-installed receptacle outlet. May it be counted toward the required wall-space receptacle outlets in a dwelling room?","o":["Yes, for the wall space utilized by the heater","Only in garages","No, never","Only in bathrooms"],"a":0,"e":"Baseboard heaters with factory-installed receptacles, or listed receptacle assemblies, may be counted as the required outlet for the wall space the heater uses.","r":"NEC 424.9","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":1,"q":"For a typical three-phase squirrel-cage induction motor, what current value is used to size branch-circuit conductors and short-circuit protection (other than for overload protection)?","o":["The full-load current from the NEC motor tables (such as Table 430.250)","The service factor current","The nameplate full-load current","The locked-rotor current"],"a":0,"e":"Ampacity of conductors, disconnect ratings and short-circuit/ground-fault protection are based on the NEC table FLC values, not the nameplate current.","r":"NEC 430.6(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":1,"q":"Separate motor overload protection is selected based on which current?","o":["The motor nameplate current rating","The table full-load current","The branch-circuit rating","The locked-rotor current"],"a":0,"e":"Separate overload devices are selected from the motor nameplate current, not the table value.","r":"NEC 430.6(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Branch-circuit conductors supplying a single continuous-duty motor must have an ampacity of not less than what percentage of the motor's table full-load current?","o":["125 percent","150 percent","115 percent","100 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Conductors supplying a single motor used in continuous duty must have an ampacity of at least 125 percent of the motor FLC rating.","r":"NEC 430.22(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Conductors supplying several motors must have an ampacity at least equal to the sum of the full-load currents of all motors plus what additional amount?","o":["100 percent of the largest motor FLC","25 percent of the smallest motor FLC","25 percent of the highest rated motor FLC in the group","10 percent of the sum"],"a":2,"e":"Per 430.24 the ampacity is the sum of the FLCs plus 25 percent of the highest rated motor FLC in the group.","r":"NEC 430.24","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"A continuous-duty motor has a nameplate service factor of 1.15 and is protected by a separate overload device. The overload device must trip at no more than what percent of the nameplate current?","o":["150 percent","140 percent","125 percent","115 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Motors with a marked service factor of 1.15 or greater, or a marked temperature rise of 40 degrees C or less, require overload protection set at no more than 125 percent of nameplate current.","r":"NEC 430.32(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"A continuous-duty motor rated over 1 hp has no marked service factor and a marked temperature rise of 55 degrees C. The overload device must trip at no more than what percent of nameplate current?","o":["100 percent","125 percent","115 percent","130 percent"],"a":2,"e":"All other motors (not SF 1.15 or more and not temp rise 40 degrees C or less) are limited to 115 percent of nameplate current.","r":"NEC 430.32(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":3,"q":"A motor with a 1.15 service factor trips its overload relay set at 125 percent while starting a high-inertia load, even though the relay is correctly selected. What is the maximum the trip setting may be increased to?","o":["200 percent of nameplate current","140 percent of nameplate current","150 percent of nameplate current","130 percent of nameplate current"],"a":1,"e":"If the selected overload device is not sufficient to start or carry the load, a higher size may be used, not exceeding 140 percent for motors with SF 1.15 or more or temperature rise 40 degrees C or less (130 percent for others).","r":"NEC 430.32(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum rating of an inverse time circuit breaker for motor branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault protection of a three-phase squirrel-cage motor, as a percentage of table full-load current (before exceptions)?","o":["250 percent","175 percent","150 percent","300 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Table 430.52 permits inverse time breakers up to 250 percent of FLC for these motors.","r":"NEC 430.52(C)(1); Table 430.52","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum rating of a dual-element (time-delay) fuse for motor branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault protection of a three-phase squirrel-cage motor, as a percentage of table full-load current (before exceptions)?","o":["125 percent","300 percent","175 percent","250 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Table 430.52 allows time-delay (dual-element) fuses up to 175 percent of FLC.","r":"NEC 430.52(C)(1); Table 430.52","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum rating of a non-time-delay fuse for motor branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault protection of a three-phase squirrel-cage motor, as a percentage of table full-load current (before exceptions)?","o":["250 percent","175 percent","800 percent","300 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Non-time-delay fuses are permitted up to 300 percent of FLC under Table 430.52.","r":"NEC 430.52(C)(1); Table 430.52","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"An instantaneous-trip circuit breaker may be used for motor branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault protection only if it is:","o":["Part of a listed combination motor controller with coordinated overload and short-circuit protection in each conductor","Rated at 15 A or less","Installed in any panelboard","Used on single-phase motors only"],"a":0,"e":"Instantaneous-trip breakers are allowed only if adjustable and part of a listed combination motor controller with coordinated protection.","r":"NEC 430.52(C)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":3,"q":"The calculated rating for a motor branch-circuit protective device does not correspond to a standard size or rating. What is permitted?","o":["The next higher standard size is permitted","The motor must be reconnected","The next lower standard size must be used","Any non-standard device may be used"],"a":0,"e":"Exception No. 1 to 430.52(C)(1): where the values for branch-circuit protective devices determined by Table 430.52 do not correspond to standard sizes, the next higher standard size is permitted.","r":"NEC 430.52(C)(1) Exception No. 1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":3,"q":"A 250 percent inverse time breaker will not allow a motor with a full-load current of 80 amperes to start. What is the maximum rating the breaker may be increased to under the exception?","o":["300 percent of FLC","350 percent of FLC","500 percent of FLC","400 percent of FLC"],"a":3,"e":"Exception No. 2: where the rating is not sufficient to start the motor, an inverse time breaker may be increased but not above 400 percent of FLC for motors with FLC of 100 A or less (300 percent for over 100 A).","r":"NEC 430.52(C)(1) Exception No. 2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Where fuses are used for motor overload protection, in which conductors must they be inserted?","o":["Only one ungrounded conductor","Only the grounded conductor","The neutral only","Each ungrounded conductor, and also the grounded conductor if the system is 3-wire, 3-phase ac with one conductor grounded"],"a":3,"e":"Fuses used for motor overload protection must be in each ungrounded conductor and also in the grounded conductor of a 3-phase, 3-wire ac system with one conductor grounded.","r":"NEC 430.36","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"How many overload units (such as overload relay elements) are required for a three-phase ac motor?","o":["One","Three, one in each phase","Two","Only one in the neutral"],"a":1,"e":"Table 430.37 requires three overload units, one in each phase, for three-phase ac motors.","r":"NEC 430.37; Table 430.37","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":1,"q":"When is an overload device that automatically restarts the motor after tripping NOT permitted?","o":["Where automatic restarting can result in injury to persons","On motors over 100 hp","In dry locations","On single-phase motors"],"a":0,"e":"A motor overload device that can restart the motor automatically after tripping must not be installed if automatic restarting can result in injury to persons.","r":"NEC 430.43","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"A motor drives a critical process where an immediate shutdown would introduce additional hazards. What does the NEC allow?","o":["Using a larger breaker only","Eliminating the overload protection","Doubling the conductor size","Connecting the overload sensing device to a supervised alarm instead of interrupting the motor circuit"],"a":3,"e":"Where immediate shutdown would increase hazards, overload sensing devices may be connected to a supervised alarm instead of interrupting the circuit.","r":"NEC 430.44","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"May motor branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault protection and motor overload protection be combined in a single device?","o":["Never","Only for motors over 50 hp","Yes, if the device's rating or setting is selected in accordance with 430.32","Only on dc motors"],"a":2,"e":"They may be combined in a single device if the rating or setting complies with 430.32.","r":"NEC 430.55","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":3,"q":"A feeder supplies several motors. What is the maximum rating of the feeder short-circuit and ground-fault protective device?","o":["150 percent of the sum of all FLCs","125 percent of the largest motor FLC","The largest branch-circuit short-circuit device rating (per 430.52) plus the sum of the FLCs of the other motors","The sum of all branch-circuit device ratings"],"a":2,"e":"The feeder device may not exceed the largest rating of any motor branch-circuit protective device permitted by 430.52, plus the sum of the FLCs of the other motors in the group.","r":"NEC 430.62(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":1,"q":"Which of the following must be marked on a motor nameplate?","o":["The installer's name","The date of installation","Rated full-load current, rated voltage, frequency, and number of phases","The purchase price"],"a":2,"e":"Motor nameplates must show the manufacturer's name, rated volts and full-load current, frequency, phases, speed, time rating, temperature rise or insulation class, and horsepower (1/8 hp and larger).","r":"NEC 430.7(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"A motor controller must be marked with its manufacturer's name, voltage, current or horsepower rating, and:","o":["Price","Short-circuit current rating","Size of the bushing","Wiring diagram only"],"a":1,"e":"Controllers must be marked with the manufacturer's name, voltage, current or horsepower rating, short-circuit current rating, and other data needed to indicate the motor for which they are suitable.","r":"NEC 430.8","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"What controller arrangement does the NEC require for motors?","o":["Each motor needs two controllers","Each motor has an individual controller, with exceptions such as a group controller for motors of 1 hp or less in specific cases","One controller can serve any number of motors","Controllers are optional"],"a":1,"e":"Each motor must be provided with an individual controller, with limited exceptions for a single controller serving a group.","r":"NEC 430.87","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"For a stationary motor rated 1/8 hp or less, normally left running and constructed so it cannot be damaged by overload or failure to start (such as a clock motor), what may serve as the controller?","o":["A contactor only","The branch-circuit overcurrent protective device","A magnetic starter only","A variable frequency drive"],"a":1,"e":"The branch-circuit protective device may serve as the controller for such motors of 1/8 hp or less.","r":"NEC 430.81(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"For a portable motor rated 1/3 hp or less, what may serve as the controller?","o":["The attachment plug and receptacle (cord and plug)","The overload relay","A fuse in the cord","A nonlockable switch"],"a":0,"e":"For portable motors of 1/3 hp or less, the attachment plug and receptacle may serve as the controller.","r":"NEC 430.81(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"A stationary motor of 2 hp or less and 300 volts or less may use a general-use switch as its disconnecting means if the switch has an ampere rating of not less than:","o":["115 percent of the FLC","Three times the FLC","Twice the full-load current of the motor","The FLC"],"a":2,"e":"For stationary motors of 2 hp or less and 300 V or less, a general-use switch rated at least twice the motor FLC is permitted.","r":"NEC 430.109(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"The ampere rating of a motor disconnecting means must be at least what percent of the motor's full-load current rating?","o":["125 percent","115 percent","150 percent","100 percent"],"a":1,"e":"The disconnecting means ampere rating must be at least 115 percent of the motor full-load current rating.","r":"NEC 430.110(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":1,"q":"A motor circuit disconnecting means must open which conductors?","o":["All ungrounded supply conductors, with no pole operating independently","The neutral only","The grounded conductor only","One ungrounded conductor"],"a":0,"e":"The disconnecting means must open all ungrounded supply conductors and be designed so that no pole can be operated independently.","r":"NEC 430.103","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":1,"q":"A motor disconnecting means must be:","o":["Capable only of operation by tool","Marked to plainly indicate whether it is in the open (off) or closed (on) position","Concealed","Mercury-type"],"a":1,"e":"The disconnecting means must plainly indicate whether it is open or closed.","r":"NEC 430.104","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":1,"q":"For a motor circuit with multiple disconnecting means, what must be true of accessibility?","o":["At least one of the disconnecting means must be readily accessible","All must be locked","All must be above 6 ft","None needs to be accessible"],"a":0,"e":"At least one of the disconnecting means required must be readily accessible.","r":"NEC 430.107","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Where must the disconnecting means for a motor controller be located (generally)?","o":["Only at the service","Within 100 ft","Within sight from the controller location","Anywhere in the building"],"a":2,"e":"An individual disconnecting means must be provided for each controller and be located in sight from the controller location, with limited exceptions.","r":"NEC 430.102(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"In addition to the controller disconnect, where must a disconnecting means for a motor be located?","o":["Within 100 feet","Only in the control room","In sight from the motor location and the driven machinery location","Anywhere on the premises"],"a":2,"e":"A disconnecting means must be in sight from the motor and driven machinery locations; the controller disconnect may serve if it is in sight of both.","r":"NEC 430.102(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":1,"q":"For the purpose of 'within sight' requirements for motor disconnects, the equipment must be visible and not more than how far from the other?","o":["50 feet","100 feet","10 feet","25 feet"],"a":0,"e":"'In sight from' means visible and not more than 15 m (50 ft) distant from the other.","r":"NEC Art. 100 (Within Sight From); 430.102","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":3,"q":"A motor's controller disconnect is not located in sight of the motor, and relocating it would be impracticable or introduce additional hazards. What does the NEC permit?","o":["Nothing; it must always be in sight","A disconnecting means that is not in sight, provided it is capable of being locked in the open position and meets the exception's conditions","Removing the disconnect entirely","Using a fuse as the disconnect"],"a":1,"e":"The exception to 430.102(B) allows the disconnect to be out of sight where it is impracticable or hazardous, provided a lockable disconnecting means is provided as specified.","r":"NEC 430.102(B) Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"For a stationary motor of 1/8 horsepower or less, what is permitted to serve as the disconnecting means?","o":["A snap switch with 1/2 ampere rating","Only a lockable disconnect","The branch-circuit overcurrent device","Nothing"],"a":2,"e":"For stationary motors of 1/8 hp or less, the branch-circuit overcurrent device may serve as the disconnecting means.","r":"NEC 430.109(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Under which condition may a single disconnecting means serve a group of motors?","o":["Only for dc motors","Only if all motors are over 10 hp","Never","When the group of motors is under the protection of one set of branch-circuit protective devices, among other listed conditions"],"a":3,"e":"A single disconnect may serve a group if motors drive parts of one machine, are protected by one set of branch-circuit devices, or are in a single room within sight of the disconnect.","r":"NEC 430.112","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Where one conductor of a motor control circuit is grounded, the control circuit must be arranged so a ground fault in the control circuit remote from the motor controller will:","o":["Trip the service","Sound an alarm only","Start the motor","Not start the motor and not bypass manual shutdown or automatic safety shutdown devices"],"a":3,"e":"A ground fault in a grounded control circuit must not start the motor or bypass shutdown devices.","r":"NEC 430.74","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Motor control circuits must be arranged so that:","o":["Only the neutral is opened","They are only de-energized at the panel","They are disconnected from all sources of supply when the disconnecting means is in the open position","They remain energized when the disconnect is open"],"a":2,"e":"Control circuits must be disconnected from all sources of supply when the disconnecting means is open (with a permitted exception for separate disconnects with a warning).","r":"NEC 430.75(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Exposed live parts of motors operating at 50 volts or more must be guarded by enclosure or location. Which location qualifies?","o":["Elevated 8 feet (2.5 m) or more above the floor","Behind a 3 ft rail","On the floor in an open area","Behind a curtain"],"a":0,"e":"Guarding by location includes elevation of 2.5 m (8 ft) or more above the floor, or in a room accessible only to qualified persons.","r":"NEC 430.232","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Where live parts of motors or controllers over 150 volts to ground are guarded only by location and attendance is needed during operation, what must be provided?","o":["Nothing","Suitable insulating mats or platforms","A warning light","A second disconnect"],"a":1,"e":"Insulating mats or platforms must be provided so an attendant cannot readily touch live parts unless standing on the mats or platforms.","r":"NEC 430.233","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Which condition requires the frame of a stationary motor to be grounded?","o":["Motor is under 1 hp","Motor is single-phase","Motor operates with any terminal at over 150 volts to ground","Motor is in a dry room"],"a":2,"e":"Stationary motor frames must be grounded if supplied by metal-enclosed wiring, in wet locations not isolated or guarded, in hazardous locations, or if operating with any terminal over 150 V to ground.","r":"NEC 430.242","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Motor controller enclosures must be connected to the equipment grounding conductor:","o":["Regardless of voltage","Only above 300 volts","Only for three-phase","Only above 150 volts"],"a":0,"e":"Controller enclosures must be connected to the equipment grounding conductor regardless of voltage, except lined covers of snap switches.","r":"NEC 430.244","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":1,"q":"Motors must be located so that:","o":["Adequate ventilation is provided and maintenance such as lubrication can be readily accomplished","They are hidden from view","They are in a closet","They are inaccessible"],"a":0,"e":"Motors must be located for adequate ventilation and so that maintenance can be readily accomplished.","r":"NEC 430.14(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":1,"q":"Open motors with commutators or collector rings must be located so that:","o":["They contact walls","Sparks cannot reach adjacent combustible material","They are in direct sunlight","They are covered with cloth"],"a":1,"e":"Open motors must be located so sparks cannot reach adjacent combustible material, though they may be on wooden floors or supports.","r":"NEC 430.14(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Where dust or flying material collects on motors in quantities that interfere with ventilation and cooling, what type of motor must be used?","o":["Motors painted white","Suitable totally enclosed or otherwise suitable motors that will not overheat under the prevailing conditions","Any motor with a larger fuse","Open motors"],"a":1,"e":"Suitable types of enclosed motors that will not overheat under the prevailing conditions must be used.","r":"NEC 430.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":3,"q":"A standard part-winding start induction motor uses separate overload devices for each winding half. What is the maximum branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault protection for each winding connection?","o":["One-half the value specified by 430.52","The full value permitted by 430.52","125 percent of the value in 430.52","Twice the value in 430.52"],"a":0,"e":"Each motor-winding connection must have short-circuit and ground-fault protection rated at not more than one-half that specified in 430.52.","r":"NEC 430.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Conductors connecting the secondary of a wound-rotor ac motor to its controller, for continuous duty, must have an ampacity of not less than what percent of full-load secondary current?","o":["150 percent","125 percent","110 percent","100 percent"],"a":1,"e":"For continuous duty, wound-rotor secondary conductors must be sized at not less than 125 percent of the full-load secondary current.","r":"NEC 430.23(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Circuit conductors supplying power conversion equipment (such as an adjustable speed drive) included as part of an adjustable speed drive system must have an ampacity of not less than what percent of the rated input current of the drive?","o":["125 percent","100 percent","115 percent","150 percent"],"a":0,"e":"The conductors must have an ampacity not less than 125 percent of the rated input current of the power conversion equipment.","r":"NEC 430.122(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"In determining the highest rated motor in a group for feeder sizing, which values are used?","o":["The locked-rotor current","The full-load current ratings from Tables 430.247 through 430.250","The nameplate full-load current","The nameplate horsepower"],"a":1,"e":"The highest rated motor is the one with the highest FLC rating selected from Tables 430.247-430.250.","r":"NEC 430.17","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":1,"q":"Motor overload protection (Part III) is intended to protect against:","o":["Short circuits","Excessive heating due to motor overloads and failure to start","Lightning strikes","Ground faults only"],"a":1,"e":"Overload protection protects motors, control apparatus and branch-circuit conductors from excessive heating due to overloads and failure to start; it does not protect against short circuits.","r":"NEC 430.31","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Overload relays that cannot open short-circuit currents must be protected by:","o":["A surge protector","A GFCI","Nothing","A fuse or circuit breaker with a rating per 430.52 or a motor short-circuit protector"],"a":3,"e":"Overload relays and devices not capable of opening short circuits must be protected by fuses or circuit breakers selected per 430.52, or by a motor short-circuit protector.","r":"NEC 430.40","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"For a multispeed motor, the ampacity of conductors on the line side of the controller is based on:","o":["The table value for the lowest speed","The lowest nameplate current","The average of the nameplate currents","The highest of the FLC ratings shown on the motor nameplate"],"a":3,"e":"For multispeed motors, line-side conductor ampacity is based on the highest of the FLC ratings on the nameplate.","r":"NEC 430.22(B); 430.6(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Several motors, each not over 1 hp, may be on one 120-volt branch circuit protected at not more than 20 A if the full-load rating of each motor does not exceed:","o":["10 amperes","12 amperes","2 amperes","6 amperes"],"a":3,"e":"Each motor must have a full-load rating not exceeding 6 A, the protective device rating must not exceed that marked on any controller, and individual overload protection must comply with 430.32.","r":"NEC 430.53(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Conductors supplying multimotor or combination-load equipment (such as an HVAC unit) that has a nameplate with minimum supply circuit conductor ampacity are sized:","o":["From the main breaker","From the nameplate minimum circuit ampacity","At 100 percent of connected hp","From the largest motor only"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors must have ampacity not less than the minimum supply circuit conductor ampacity marked on the nameplate of the multimotor or combination-load equipment.","r":"NEC 430.25","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"For a torque motor, which current is used to determine conductor ampacity, disconnect rating and short-circuit protection?","o":["Locked-rotor current marked on the nameplate","Table full-load current","Average running current","Service factor current"],"a":0,"e":"For torque motors the rated current is the locked-rotor current marked on the nameplate and is used for these determinations.","r":"NEC 430.6(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"For ac adjustable voltage, variable torque drive motors, which current is used to determine conductor ampacity and short-circuit protection?","o":["Locked-rotor current","The maximum operating current marked on the motor or controller nameplate","Table full-load current","Service factor current"],"a":1,"e":"For these motors, the maximum operating current marked on the nameplate of the motor or controller is used instead of the table FLC.","r":"NEC 430.6(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":3,"q":"A 460-volt three-phase motor has a table full-load current of 65 A, a nameplate current of 62 A and a service factor of 1.15. For selecting the separate overload relay, which value is the maximum trip setting?","o":["77.5 A (125 percent of nameplate)","71.3 A (115 percent of nameplate)","93 A (150 percent of nameplate)","81.25 A (125 percent of table FLC)"],"a":0,"e":"Overload devices are based on nameplate current, not table FLC, and SF 1.15 allows 125 percent: 62 x 1.25 = 77.5 A.","r":"NEC 430.6(A)(2); 430.32(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":3,"q":"A continuous-duty three-phase motor has a table FLC of 34 amperes and a nameplate current of 32 amperes. What is the minimum ampacity of the branch-circuit conductors?","o":["42.5 A","40 A","32 A","34 A"],"a":0,"e":"Conductors are sized at 125 percent of the table FLC: 34 x 1.25 = 42.5 A. Nameplate current is not used for this.","r":"NEC 430.6(A)(1); 430.22(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Which of these must a motor nameplate show for a motor rated 1/8 horsepower or larger?","o":["The rated horsepower","The wiring method","The conduit size","The utility rate"],"a":0,"e":"Motors of 1/8 hp or more must have the rated horsepower marked on the nameplate along with other required data.","r":"NEC 430.7(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":1,"q":"How do the provisions of Article 440 relate to those of Article 430 for air-conditioning equipment with hermetic motor-compressors?","o":["Article 440 is in addition to or amendatory of Article 430 and other articles, which still apply except as modified","Article 440 replaces Article 430 entirely","Article 440 applies only to residential equipment","Article 430 never applies"],"a":0,"e":"Article 440 provisions are in addition to or amendatory of Article 430 and other articles, which apply except as modified by Article 440.","r":"NEC 440.3(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":1,"q":"What is the 'rated-load current' of a hermetic refrigerant motor-compressor?","o":["The current resulting when the motor-compressor is operated at the rated load, rated voltage and rated frequency of the equipment it serves","The current at locked rotor","The current at no load","The service factor current"],"a":0,"e":"Rated-load current is the current when the compressor operates at the rated load, voltage and frequency of the equipment it serves.","r":"NEC 440.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"The 'branch-circuit selection current' of a hermetic motor-compressor is always:","o":["Equal to or greater than the marked rated-load current","Less than the rated-load current","Equal to locked-rotor current","Unrelated to rated-load current"],"a":0,"e":"Branch-circuit selection current is used in place of rated-load current when the running overload device permits a sustained current above the specified percentage, and it is always equal to or greater than the marked RLC.","r":"NEC 440.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":1,"q":"A hermetic refrigerant motor-compressor nameplate must include the rated-load current and what other current?","o":["Service factor current","No-load current","Locked-rotor current","Inrush of the fan"],"a":2,"e":"The nameplate must show the rated-load current and locked-rotor current of the motor-compressor, along with voltage, phase, frequency and other data.","r":"NEC 440.4(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"Multimotor and combination-load equipment such as an air conditioner must have a visible nameplate marked with the minimum supply circuit conductor ampacity and:","o":["The installer's name","The maximum rating of the branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault protective device","The refrigerant price","Only the horsepower"],"a":1,"e":"The nameplate must show the minimum supply circuit conductor ampacity and the maximum rating of the branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault protective device, among other data.","r":"NEC 440.4(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"In addition to voltage, frequency, phase, minimum supply circuit conductor ampacity and maximum branch-circuit protective device rating, what rating must be marked on the nameplate of multimotor and combination-load equipment?","o":["Weight","Sound level","Duct size","Short-circuit current rating of the motor controller or industrial control panel"],"a":3,"e":"The nameplate must also include the short-circuit current rating of the motor controller or industrial control panel.","r":"NEC 440.4(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":1,"q":"For a hermetic motor-compressor, what value is used to determine conductor ampacity, disconnect rating and branch-circuit protection?","o":["The locked-rotor current","The nameplate rated-load current or branch-circuit selection current, whichever is greater","The fan motor current only","The horsepower from Table 430.250"],"a":1,"e":"The marked RLC or BCSC, whichever is greater, is used instead of the motor horsepower rating from the NEC tables.","r":"NEC 440.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"For purposes of sizing conductors and feeder protection, which motor is the 'highest rated (largest) motor' in an air-conditioning installation?","o":["The motor with the highest rated-load current","The motor with the highest locked-rotor current","The first motor installed","The motor with the highest horsepower"],"a":0,"e":"The largest motor is the one with the highest rated-load current. If two have the same highest RLC, only one is considered the highest.","r":"NEC 440.7","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"The ampere rating of a disconnecting means for a hermetic motor-compressor must be at least what percent of the nameplate RLC or branch-circuit selection current, whichever is greater?","o":["140 percent","115 percent","125 percent","100 percent"],"a":1,"e":"The disconnecting means must have an ampere rating of at least 115 percent of the greater of the nameplate RLC or BCSC.","r":"NEC 440.12(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"For cord-connected equipment such as room air conditioners, household refrigerators and freezers, and drinking water coolers, what may serve as the disconnecting means?","o":["Nothing is required","An attachment plug and receptacle or cord connector","Only a fused switch","The branch-circuit breaker only if lockable"],"a":1,"e":"For cord-connected equipment of these types, an attachment plug and receptacle or cord connector may serve as the disconnecting means.","r":"NEC 440.13","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":1,"q":"A disconnecting means for air-conditioning or refrigerating equipment must be located:","o":["Anywhere in the building","Within 100 ft","At the service entrance only","Within sight from, and readily accessible from, the equipment"],"a":3,"e":"The disconnect must be within sight from and readily accessible from the air-conditioning or refrigerating equipment, with limited exceptions.","r":"NEC 440.14","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"Where may the disconnecting means be installed relative to the air-conditioning equipment?","o":["On or within the equipment, but not on panels designed to be removed for access to the equipment","Only inside the building","Only remotely","Only on removable access panels"],"a":0,"e":"The disconnect may be on or within the equipment, but must not be on panels designed to allow access to the equipment by removal of the panels.","r":"NEC 440.14","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":3,"q":"A contractor mounts the disconnect for a rooftop unit on the removable service access panel of the unit. Is this compliant?","o":["Yes, if lockable","Yes, since it is within sight","Yes, if the unit is under 5 tons","No, the disconnect must not be located on panels designed to be removed for access to the equipment"],"a":3,"e":"The disconnect must not be located on panels designed to allow access to the equipment by removal of the panels.","r":"NEC 440.14","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"A disconnect for a split-system outdoor unit is installed on the wall behind a fence 100 feet away from the unit and not visible from it. Which statement is correct?","o":["It is allowed because the unit is outdoors","It meets the code if labeled","It does not meet the within-sight requirement","It meets the code if readily accessible"],"a":2,"e":"The disconnect must be within sight from the equipment (visible and not more than 50 ft away), subject only to the specific exception.","r":"NEC 440.14; Art. 100","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum rating of the branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault protective device for a single hermetic motor-compressor, before any increase to allow starting?","o":["175 percent of RLC or BCSC, whichever is greater","125 percent of RLC or BCSC","250 percent of RLC","300 percent of RLC"],"a":0,"e":"The device must be capable of carrying starting current and must not exceed 175 percent of the RLC or BCSC, whichever is greater.","r":"NEC 440.22(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":3,"q":"The protective device selected at 175 percent will not carry the starting current of a hermetic motor-compressor. What is the maximum the rating may be increased to?","o":["250 percent","300 percent","225 percent","200 percent"],"a":2,"e":"If the protective device is not sufficient for starting, its rating may be increased but must not exceed 225 percent of the RLC or BCSC, whichever is greater.","r":"NEC 440.22(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":3,"q":"An air conditioner is marked 'Minimum circuit ampacity 22 A; Maximum overcurrent protective device 30 A'. An installer wants to use a 40-ampere breaker. Is this permitted?","o":["Yes, up to 175 percent of MCA","Yes, the conductors are sized for 22 A","No, the protective device must not exceed the maximum marked on the equipment","Yes, for outdoor units"],"a":2,"e":"The protective device may not exceed the manufacturer's marked maximum rating on the equipment nameplate.","r":"NEC 440.22; 440.4(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"Branch-circuit conductors supplying a single motor-compressor must have an ampacity of at least what percent of the RLC or BCSC, whichever is greater?","o":["115 percent","125 percent","175 percent","100 percent"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors supplying a single motor-compressor must be sized at not less than 125 percent of the RLC or BCSC, whichever is greater.","r":"NEC 440.32","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"Conductors supplying a motor-compressor and other motors on the same circuit must have an ampacity of at least:","o":["The sum of nameplate hp","125 percent of the largest RLC plus the sum of the other loads","200 percent of the largest motor","The sum of locked-rotor currents"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors must have an ampacity of at least 125 percent of the largest motor-compressor RLC/BCSC (or largest motor FLC) plus the sum of the other motor loads and other loads.","r":"NEC 440.33","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"Supply conductors for multimotor or combination-load equipment bearing the nameplate marking of minimum supply circuit conductor ampacity must have an ampacity:","o":["Half the marked value","Not less than the marked minimum ampacity","Based on the largest motor only","Equal to the breaker size"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors for such equipment must have an ampacity not less than the minimum supply circuit conductor ampacity marked on the nameplate.","r":"NEC 440.35","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"A controller for a hermetic motor-compressor must have both a continuous-duty full-load current rating and a locked-rotor current rating that are:","o":["Half the compressor values","Unrelated to the compressor","Not less than the nameplate RLC or BCSC and locked-rotor current of the compressor","Equal to the breaker rating"],"a":2,"e":"The controller ratings must be not less than the compressor nameplate RLC (or BCSC) and locked-rotor current.","r":"NEC 440.41(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"A separate overload relay for a motor-compressor must be selected to trip at no more than what percent of the compressor's rated-load current?","o":["125 percent","115 percent","140 percent","175 percent"],"a":2,"e":"A separate overload relay must trip at not more than 140 percent of the motor-compressor rated-load current.","r":"NEC 440.52(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"A cord-and-plug-connected room air conditioner is on a branch circuit that supplies no other loads. The total marked rating of the air conditioner must not exceed what percent of the branch-circuit rating?","o":["50 percent","125 percent","80 percent","100 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Where no other loads are supplied, the air conditioner's marked rating must not exceed 80 percent of the branch-circuit rating.","r":"NEC 440.62(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"A room air conditioner shares a branch circuit with lighting units or other appliances. The air conditioner's marked rating must not exceed what percent of the branch-circuit rating?","o":["100 percent","80 percent","125 percent","50 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Where other loads are also supplied, the total marked rating of the air conditioner must not exceed 50 percent of the branch-circuit rating.","r":"NEC 440.62(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":3,"q":"A 120-volt room air conditioner is marked 13 amperes and will be the only load on a 15-ampere branch circuit. Is this permitted?","o":["Yes, 13 A is under 15 A","No, 13 A exceeds 80 percent of 15 A (12 A)","Yes, if cord-connected","No, it needs a 30 A circuit"],"a":1,"e":"80 percent of 15 A is 12 A, and 13 A exceeds this limit.","r":"NEC 440.62(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":3,"q":"A room air conditioner marked 8 amperes shares a 15-ampere circuit with lighting. Is this permitted?","o":["Yes, 8 A is under 12 A","Yes, if lighting is LED","No, 8 A exceeds 50 percent of 15 A (7.5 A)","No, a 20 A circuit is always required"],"a":2,"e":"With other loads on the circuit, the air conditioner must not exceed 50 percent of the circuit rating: 7.5 A for a 15 A circuit.","r":"NEC 440.62(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"A single-phase room air conditioner rated 250 volts or less is cord-and-plug connected. The attachment plug and receptacle may serve as the disconnecting means if the manual controls are readily accessible and located within what height of the floor?","o":["10 feet","3 feet","6 feet (1.8 m)","8 feet"],"a":2,"e":"The controls must be readily accessible and within 1.8 m (6 ft) of the floor, or an approved manual disconnect must be installed in sight and readily accessible.","r":"NEC 440.63","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":1,"q":"For a 120-volt room air conditioner, what is the maximum length of the flexible supply cord?","o":["6 feet (1.8 m)","15 feet","25 feet","10 feet (3.0 m)"],"a":3,"e":"For 120-volt room air conditioners, the cord must not exceed 3.0 m (10 ft).","r":"NEC 440.64","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"For a 240-volt room air conditioner, what is the maximum length of the flexible supply cord?","o":["6 feet (1.8 m)","10 feet","15 feet","3 feet"],"a":0,"e":"For 208- or 240-volt room air conditioners, the cord must not exceed 1.8 m (6 ft).","r":"NEC 440.64","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":2,"q":"Single-phase cord-and-plug-connected room air conditioners must be provided with which factory-installed protection?","o":["A surge protector only","LCDI or AFCI protection","No protection","GFCI only"],"a":1,"e":"Single-phase cord-and-plug-connected room air conditioners must have factory-installed leakage-current detector-interrupter (LCDI) or arc-fault circuit-interrupter (AFCI) protection.","r":"NEC 440.65","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders and Lamps","d":2,"q":"A recessed incandescent luminaire with a completely enclosed light source is installed in a clothes closet. What is the minimum clearance from the nearest point of the storage space?","o":["24 inches","6 inches","12 inches","3 inches"],"a":1,"e":"Recessed incandescent or LED luminaires with a completely enclosed light source require 150 mm (6 in) clearance to the storage space.","r":"NEC 410.16(C)(2)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":2,"q":"Branch-circuit conductors supplying an individual appliance must have an ampacity of not less than:","o":["The breaker size only","The marked rating of the appliance","15 amperes","50 percent of the marked rating"],"a":1,"e":"The branch-circuit rating for an individual appliance must not be less than the marked rating of the appliance.","r":"NEC 422.10(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":1,"q":"How must central heating equipment (other than fixed electric space-heating equipment) be supplied?","o":["By a shared kitchen circuit","By an individual branch circuit","By a bathroom circuit","By a general-purpose lighting circuit"],"a":1,"e":"Central heating equipment must be supplied by an individual branch circuit, though directly associated auxiliary equipment may share it.","r":"NEC 422.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"A motor-circuit switch used as a motor disconnecting means must be:","o":["Unlisted","Rated in horsepower","A general-use snap switch only","Rated only in volts"],"a":1,"e":"A motor-circuit switch is a listed switch rated in horsepower.","r":"NEC 430.109(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Motor equipment receives energy from more than one source. What is required?","o":["One disconnect for the primary source only","A fuse in each source","A disconnecting means from each source of energy, unless interlocked as permitted","No disconnect"],"a":2,"e":"Equipment receiving energy from more than one source must have a disconnecting means from each source (with an exception for interlocked disconnects).","r":"NEC 430.113","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":1,"q":"A generator nameplate shows a rated output current of 100 amperes, and the generator design allows overloading. What is the minimum ampacity of the conductors from the generator terminals to the first distribution device?","o":["150 amperes","115 amperes","100 amperes","125 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"Generator output conductors must have an ampacity of at least 115 percent of the nameplate current rating. 125 percent is the continuous-load or motor rule, not the generator rule.","r":"NEC 445.13(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 480-volt generator is rated 100 kVA. Using the nameplate current, what is the minimum ampacity of the output conductors between the generator terminals and the first overcurrent device?","o":["120.3 amperes","125 amperes","138.3 amperes","150.4 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"Nameplate current is 100,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 120.3 A. Multiply by 115 percent to get 138.3 A. 120.3 A omits the 115 percent factor.","r":"NEC 445.13(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":2,"q":"A generator is designed so that it cannot be overloaded, and its nameplate current is 80 amperes. What is the minimum ampacity of its output conductors?","o":["92 amperes","115 amperes","100 amperes","80 amperes"],"a":3,"e":"Where the generator design does not allow overloading, the 115 percent multiplier is not required and 100 percent of nameplate current is the minimum.","r":"NEC 445.13(A) Exception","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":3,"q":"A single-phase, 240-volt generator is rated 25 kVA and can be overloaded. What is the minimum ampacity of the conductors from the generator terminals to the first overcurrent device?","o":["104.2 amperes","130.2 amperes","119.8 amperes","156.3 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"Nameplate current is 25,000 / 240 = 104.2 A, and 115 percent of that is 119.8 A. 130.2 A would be 125 percent, which is not the generator rule.","r":"NEC 445.13(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 208-volt generator is rated 30 kVA and can be overloaded. What is the minimum ampacity of its output conductors from the terminals to the first overcurrent device?","o":["91.6 amperes","95.8 amperes","104.1 amperes","83.3 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"Nameplate current is 30,000 / (1.732 x 208) = 83.3 A. Multiplying by 1.15 gives 95.8 A.","r":"NEC 445.13(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 480-volt generator is rated 150 kVA and can be overloaded. What is the minimum ampacity of the output conductors from the generator terminals to the first overcurrent device?","o":["207.5 amperes","250.0 amperes","225.5 amperes","180.4 amperes"],"a":0,"e":"Nameplate current is 150,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 180.4 A, and 115 percent is 207.5 A.","r":"NEC 445.13(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":3,"q":"A single-phase, 240-volt generator is rated 20 kVA and can be overloaded. What is the minimum ampacity of its output conductors from the terminals to the first overcurrent device?","o":["104.2 amperes","83.3 amperes","95.8 amperes","91.7 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"Nameplate current is 20,000 / 240 = 83.3 A; 115 percent gives 95.8 A.","r":"NEC 445.13(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":2,"q":"Conductors from generator terminals that are sized according to the generator output conductor rule may be protected against overload in which way?","o":["By a ground-fault protection device only","By the generator overload protection required for the generator","Only by a fuse located at the generator terminals","They must never be protected by the generator itself"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors from generator terminals that meet the 445.13 size requirement are permitted to be protected against overload by the generator overload protection required in 445.12.","r":"NEC 240.21(G); 445.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is a permitted way to provide overload protection for a constant-voltage generator (other than an ac generator exciter)?","o":["No protection is permitted because it could interrupt the load","Inherent design, circuit breakers, fuses, protective relays or other identified overcurrent protective means","Only a dedicated fuse mounted on the generator frame","Ground-fault protection of equipment only"],"a":1,"e":"Constant-voltage generators, other than ac exciters, must be protected from overload by inherent design, circuit breakers, fuses, protective relays or other identified overcurrent protective means suitable for the conditions of use.","r":"NEC 445.12(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":1,"q":"Which of the following must be shown on a generator nameplate?","o":["Power factor","Muffler type","Cranking time","Fuel tank capacity"],"a":0,"e":"The nameplate must show the manufacturer name, rated frequency, number of phases, kW or kVA rating, power factor, normal volts and amperes, and other electrical and thermal data. Fuel capacity is not an electrical nameplate item.","r":"NEC 445.11","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":2,"q":"In addition to manufacturer name, frequency, phases, kW or kVA rating, power factor, and normal volts and amperes, which information must a generator nameplate also give?","o":["Date of last inspection","Sound level in decibels and shipping weight","Rated ambient temperature or rated temperature rise, and insulation system class","Fuel type and tank size"],"a":2,"e":"Section 445.11 requires rated ambient temperature or rated temperature rise and the insulation system class on the nameplate.","r":"NEC 445.11","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":1,"q":"Live parts of generators operating at more than what voltage to ground must not be exposed to accidental contact where accessible to unqualified persons (ac systems)?","o":["30 volts","50 volts","150 volts","120 volts"],"a":1,"e":"Live parts of generators operating at more than 50 volts ac (or 60 volts dc) to ground must not be exposed to accidental contact where accessible to unqualified persons.","r":"NEC 445.14","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":2,"q":"A 480-volt generator is installed in a space that unqualified persons can enter. Which statement is correct regarding its live parts?","o":["They must be guarded or enclosed so they are not exposed to accidental contact","Only the neutral must be enclosed","They may be exposed because 480 volts is a low-voltage system","They are acceptable if marked with a warning label only"],"a":0,"e":"Because the voltage to ground exceeds 50 volts ac, live parts accessible to unqualified persons must not be exposed to accidental contact. A label alone is not protection.","r":"NEC 445.14","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":1,"q":"Where field wiring passes through an opening in a generator enclosure, conduit box, or barrier, what must be used to protect the conductors from abrasion?","o":["A bushing","A fiber washer taped in place","A listed sealing fitting in all cases","An expansion fitting"],"a":0,"e":"A bushing must be used to protect conductors from the edge of the opening where field wiring passes through enclosing walls or barriers.","r":"NEC 445.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":2,"q":"A generator that is not bidirectional is required to have a disconnecting means. What must that means be able to disconnect from the circuits supplied by the generator, unless a listed exception applies?","o":["Only the prime mover fuel supply","Only the grounded conductor","Only the load conductors","The generator and all protective devices and control apparatus"],"a":3,"e":"The disconnecting means must allow the generator and all protective devices and control apparatus to be entirely disconnected from the circuits supplied.","r":"NEC 445.18(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":2,"q":"Generators must be located so that what two conditions are met, as required through the motor location rule referenced in the generator article?","o":["Direct burial and corrosion protection only","Attached to a combustible surface for stability","Adequate ventilation and ready maintenance","Maximum possible distance from the building and no ventilation"],"a":2,"e":"Generators must be suitable for their location and meet 430.14, which requires locating the machine so adequate ventilation is provided and maintenance such as lubrication and replacing brushes can be readily accomplished.","r":"NEC 445.10; 430.14(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":2,"q":"An open generator with a commutator or collector rings is being installed. Which statement reflects the rule on sparks?","o":["It must be placed on a metal floor only","It must be located or protected so sparks cannot reach adjacent combustible material","No protection is ever required","It must be installed only in a hazardous location"],"a":1,"e":"Open machines having commutators or collector rings must be located or protected so that sparks cannot reach adjacent combustible material. They may be mounted on wooden floors or supports.","r":"NEC 445.10; 430.14(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":2,"q":"Under what condition is the frame of a portable generator NOT required to be connected to a grounding electrode?","o":["When the generator supplies only equipment mounted on it or cord-and-plug equipment through receptacles on the generator, and the non-current-carrying metal parts and receptacle grounding terminals are bonded to the generator frame","Whenever the generator has a transfer switch of any type","When the generator is rated over 15 kW","When the generator is used outdoors only"],"a":0,"e":"The frame of a portable generator need not be connected to a grounding electrode if it supplies only on-board equipment or cord-and-plug equipment through generator receptacles, with equipment metal parts and receptacle grounding terminals bonded to the frame.","r":"NEC 250.34(A)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":2,"q":"A portable generator powers only a cord-and-plug drill and a light through receptacles mounted on the generator. The receptacle equipment grounding terminals are bonded to the generator frame. Is a ground rod required for the generator?","o":["Yes, one ground rod is required","Yes, two ground rods are required","Only if the generator is over 5 kW","No, a grounding electrode is not required under these conditions"],"a":3,"e":"These conditions meet the portable generator provisions, so connecting the frame to a grounding electrode is not required.","r":"NEC 250.34(A)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":2,"q":"A portable generator is a component of a separately derived system. What must be done with the system conductor that is required to be grounded?","o":["It must be left floating","It must be omitted from the generator","It must be bonded to the generator frame","It must be connected to a separate isolated ground rod only"],"a":2,"e":"For a portable generator that is a separately derived system, the system conductor required to be grounded must be bonded to the generator frame.","r":"NEC 250.34","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":3,"q":"A permanently installed generator is connected to a 3-wire premises system through a 3-pole transfer switch that switches only the ungrounded conductors. The neutral is solidly connected between the utility and the generator. Which statement is correct?","o":["A second grounding electrode system is required at the generator","The generator is not a separately derived system and the neutral must not be bonded again at the generator","The generator is a separately derived system and the neutral must be bonded at the generator","The generator frame must never be bonded to the equipment grounding conductor"],"a":1,"e":"When the transfer equipment does not switch the grounded conductor, the system is not separately derived. The neutral is already bonded at the service, so bonding it again at the generator would create parallel neutral current paths.","r":"NEC 250.35(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":3,"q":"A permanently installed generator supplies a building through a 4-pole transfer switch that also switches the neutral. How is the generator system treated?","o":["As a utility interactive source","As an ungrounded system only","As a separately derived system, with bonding and grounding per the separately derived system rules","As a service"],"a":2,"e":"When the transfer switch switches the grounded conductor, the generator is a separately derived system and 250.30 applies to its bonding and grounding.","r":"NEC 250.35(B); 250.30","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":2,"q":"For a separately derived generator system, where may the system bonding jumper be installed?","o":["At the grounding electrode only","At any number of points to ensure continuity","Only at each load receptacle","At one point only, at the source or the first system disconnecting means"],"a":3,"e":"A separately derived system bonding jumper is made at one point only, either at the source or at the first disconnecting means, to avoid neutral current on equipment grounding paths.","r":"NEC 250.30(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":2,"q":"Which of the following generators is permitted in a Class I, Division 1 location?","o":["A standard weatherproof generator","A generator identified for Class I, Division 1 locations","An open drip-proof generator","A general-purpose totally enclosed fan-cooled generator"],"a":1,"e":"Rotating machinery in Class I, Division 1 must be identified for the location (or be one of a few specified pressurized or inert-gas-filled types). General-purpose enclosures do not qualify.","r":"NEC 501.125(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":1,"q":"Article 445 covers which of the following?","o":["Only the engines that drive generators","Only portable cord sets","Only utility power plants","Installation and other requirements for generators"],"a":3,"e":"Article 445 contains installation and other requirements for generators.","r":"NEC 445.1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":1,"q":"A 480-volt primary transformer rated 1000 volts or less has primary rated current of 9 amperes or more and is protected on the primary side only. What is the maximum primary overcurrent device rating as a percent of rated primary current?","o":["167 percent","100 percent","125 percent","250 percent"],"a":2,"e":"For transformers of 1000 volts or less with primary-only protection and rated primary current of 9 A or more, the maximum is 125 percent, with the next higher standard size permitted where 125 percent is not a standard rating.","r":"NEC 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":3,"q":"A 45-kVA, 3-phase transformer has a 480-volt primary and is protected only on the primary side. What is the maximum standard primary overcurrent device permitted?","o":["100 amperes","80 amperes","70 amperes","60 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"Primary current is 45,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 54.1 A. At 125 percent that is 67.7 A, and the next higher standard rating (70 A) is permitted.","r":"NEC 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B); 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":3,"q":"A 112.5-kVA, 3-phase transformer with a 480-volt primary is protected on the primary side only (no secondary overcurrent protection). What is the maximum standard primary overcurrent device permitted?","o":["150 amperes","200 amperes","225 amperes","175 amperes"],"a":3,"e":"Primary current is 112,500 / (1.732 x 480) = 135.3 A. 125 percent is 169.1 A, and the next higher standard size is 175 A.","r":"NEC 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B); 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":3,"q":"A 75-kVA, 3-phase transformer has a 480-volt primary and a 208Y/120-volt secondary. If secondary overcurrent protection is provided, what is the maximum standard secondary overcurrent device permitted?","o":["300 amperes","225 amperes","350 amperes","250 amperes"],"a":0,"e":"Secondary current is 75,000 / (1.732 x 208) = 208.2 A. At 125 percent, that is 260 A, and the next higher standard size is 300 A.","r":"NEC 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B); 240.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"A 12,470-volt primary transformer has rated impedance of 5.75 percent and primary-only protection by fuses. What is the maximum fuse rating as a percent of rated primary current?","o":["300 percent","125 percent","250 percent","600 percent"],"a":0,"e":"For transformers over 1000 volts with impedance not over 6 percent and primary protection only, fuses may be rated at up to 300 percent and circuit breakers at up to 600 percent.","r":"NEC 450.3(A), Table 450.3(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"Voltage (potential) transformers installed indoors or enclosed must be protected how?","o":["With no overcurrent protection at all","With a secondary circuit breaker only","With ground-fault protection only","With primary fuses"],"a":3,"e":"Potential transformers installed indoors or enclosed require primary fuses.","r":"NEC 450.3(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":3,"q":"An autotransformer is used to supply a branch circuit in an ordinary commercial building. Which condition must be met?","o":["The autotransformer must be isolated from all grounded conductors","The autotransformer output must be ungrounded","The autotransformer may be used on any system without conditions","The supplied system must have a grounded conductor electrically connected to the grounded conductor of the supplying system"],"a":3,"e":"Autotransformers may be used to extend or add a branch circuit only if the grounded conductor of the supplied system is electrically connected to the grounded conductor of the supplying system, subject to limited exceptions.","r":"NEC 210.9; 450.4","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":1,"q":"What information must be marked on a transformer's live parts exposed in an area with voltage warning requirements?","o":["The manufacturer's phone number","The date of installation","The weight of the transformer","The operating voltage of the live parts"],"a":3,"e":"A voltage warning, with the operating voltage of the live parts, must be provided in a manner visible to persons before they contact live parts.","r":"NEC 450.8(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"Dry-type transformers are required to be provided with what type of case or enclosure?","o":["A watertight case in all locations","A combustible case to reduce weight","An open frame accessible to all","A noncombustible, moisture-resistant case or enclosure that protects against accidental contact with live parts"],"a":3,"e":"Dry-type transformers must have a noncombustible, moisture-resistant case or enclosure that provides protection against accidental contact with live parts.","r":"NEC 450.8(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":1,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the ventilating openings of a transformer?","o":["They are never required","They may be covered when painting","They must not be blocked by walls or other obstructions, and required clearances must be maintained","They must be sealed in dusty areas"],"a":2,"e":"Ventilation must not be obstructed, and the clearances marked on the transformer (450.11) must be maintained.","r":"NEC 450.9","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"For which transformers must the nameplate show the impedance?","o":["Only oil-filled transformers","Transformers rated 25 kVA and larger","Only transformers over 1000 kVA","All transformers regardless of size"],"a":1,"e":"Nameplates must show the impedance of transformers 25 kVA and larger, along with kVA, frequency and voltages.","r":"NEC 450.11(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":1,"q":"What insulation-related marking is required on the nameplate of a dry-type transformer?","o":["The temperature class for the insulation system","The oil capacity in gallons","The paint color code","The shipping weight"],"a":0,"e":"Dry-type transformer nameplates must state the temperature class of the insulation system; liquid-filled units show the amount and kind of liquid.","r":"NEC 450.11(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"A dry-type transformer of 1000 volts or less is installed in the open on a wall. What accessibility is required?","o":["It must be hidden above a permanent ceiling","No accessibility is required","It must be readily accessible to qualified personnel for inspection","It must be accessible only by removing structure"],"a":2,"e":"Dry-type transformers of 1000 V or less located in the open on walls, columns, or structures must be readily accessible to qualified personnel for inspection.","r":"NEC 450.13(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum rating of a 1000-volt-or-less dry-type transformer permitted in a hollow space of a building not permanently closed in by structure, if ventilation and other requirements are met?","o":["25 kVA","112.5 kVA","75 kVA","50 kVA"],"a":3,"e":"Dry-type transformers of 1000 volts or less and not exceeding 50 kVA may be installed in hollow spaces that are accessible, provided ventilation and separation requirements are met.","r":"NEC 450.13(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"Where must the transformer disconnecting means be located?","o":["Inside the transformer enclosure only","In sight of the transformer, or in a readily accessible location with the disconnect location field marked on the transformer","Anywhere in the building without marking","At the utility pole"],"a":1,"e":"The disconnect must be in sight of the transformer; otherwise it must be in a readily accessible location and the location field marked on the transformer.","r":"NEC 450.14","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"An indoor dry-type transformer rated 75 kVA is installed near combustible material with no fire-resistant barrier. What minimum separation from combustible material is generally required?","o":["6 inches","36 inches","12 inches","18 inches"],"a":2,"e":"Indoor dry-type transformers of 112.5 kVA or less must be at least 300 mm (12 in.) from combustible material unless separated by a fire-resistant, heat-insulating barrier or rated 1000 V or less and completely enclosed except for ventilating openings.","r":"NEC 450.21(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"A 150-kVA dry-type transformer with 150C insulation rise is installed indoors. What is the general installation requirement?","o":["It must be installed in a transformer room of fire-resistant construction with a minimum 1-hour fire rating","It may be installed in an ordinary closet","It may be hung on any wall","It must be in a vault with a 3-hour rating always"],"a":0,"e":"Dry-type transformers over 112.5 kVA must be installed in a fire-resistant transformer room with a minimum 1-hour fire rating, subject to exceptions for higher temperature-class insulation with specified separation.","r":"NEC 450.21(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":1,"q":"Oil-insulated transformers installed indoors must in general be installed in what?","o":["A weatherproof cabinet only","An open hallway","A suspended ceiling space","A vault constructed per the vault requirements"],"a":3,"e":"Oil-insulated transformers installed indoors must be installed in a vault complying with Part III, subject to limited exceptions.","r":"NEC 450.26","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"Which is an acceptable safeguard for an outdoor oil-insulated transformer near combustible material or building openings?","o":["No safeguard is ever required","Fire-resistant barriers or space separation, automatic extinguishing systems, or oil-confining enclosures","A decorative fence only","A cloth cover"],"a":1,"e":"Combustible materials, buildings and openings must be safeguarded from fires originating in outdoor oil-insulated transformers by space separation, barriers, extinguishing systems or oil-confining enclosures.","r":"NEC 450.27","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":1,"q":"Where practicable, transformer vaults must be located where they can be ventilated how?","o":["Without any ventilation","To the outside air without using flues or ducts","Only through a flue","Through the building HVAC system"],"a":1,"e":"Vaults should be located where they can be ventilated to the outside air without flues or ducts wherever practicable.","r":"NEC 450.41","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum fire resistance rating of the walls and roof of a transformer vault, without the reduction for fire suppression systems?","o":["4 hours","1 hour","3 hours","2 hours"],"a":2,"e":"Walls, roofs and floors of vaults must have a minimum fire resistance of 3 hours; 1 hour is permitted where the transformers are protected by an automatic extinguishing system.","r":"NEC 450.42","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"A vault floor is in contact with the earth. What is the minimum floor thickness?","o":["8 inches of concrete","2 inches of concrete","4 inches of concrete","6 inches of concrete"],"a":2,"e":"Floors in contact with the earth must be concrete not less than 100 mm (4 in.) thick.","r":"NEC 450.42","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum fire rating of a tight-fitting door leading from a transformer vault into the building interior, without a suppression-system reduction?","o":["1 hour","3 hours","No rating is required","2 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Doorways from the interior must have tight-fitting doors with a minimum fire rating of 3 hours, or 1 hour where the vault is protected by an automatic extinguishing system.","r":"NEC 450.43(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"A door sill or curb is required at a vault doorway to confine oil from the largest transformer. What is its minimum height?","o":["2 inches","12 inches","4 inches","6 inches"],"a":2,"e":"The sill or curb must be high enough to confine the oil of the largest transformer and never less than 100 mm (4 in.).","r":"NEC 450.43(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"Which describes personnel doors on a transformer vault?","o":["They must swing inward and be kept unlocked","They must be locked with access limited to qualified persons, swing out, and have panic bars or pressure plates","They must be fixed shut","They may be sliding doors without latches"],"a":1,"e":"Vault doors must be equipped with locks, with access limited to qualified persons. Personnel doors must swing out and be equipped with panic bars, pressure plates or similar devices normally latched but openable under simple pressure.","r":"NEC 450.43(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"Where practicable, a vault containing more than what transformer capacity must have a drain or other means to remove accumulated oil or water?","o":["50 kVA","25 kVA","100 kVA","500 kVA"],"a":2,"e":"Vaults containing more than 100 kVA of transformer capacity must, where practicable, have a drain or other means to remove oil or water.","r":"NEC 450.46","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":2,"q":"Which piping may pass through a transformer vault?","o":["Natural gas piping serving the building","Sanitary drain lines","Any water supply to a nearby sink","Piping or ducts foreign to the electrical installation must not enter or pass through it"],"a":3,"e":"Piping or other facilities foreign to the electrical installation must not enter or pass through a transformer vault; only piping for vault fire protection or transformer cooling is allowed.","r":"NEC 450.47","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 450 Transformers","d":3,"q":"After an existing transformer is modified in a way that changes its capacity, what must be done regarding overcurrent protection?","o":["The overcurrent protection must be verified as compliant with the transformer overcurrent rules","The protection may be doubled","Nothing; the original protection remains valid","The protection must be removed"],"a":0,"e":"When modifications change the transformer capacity, the overcurrent protection must be verified as being in compliance with 450.3.","r":"NEC 450.28","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":1,"q":"Article 480 applies to which kind of battery installations?","o":["All stationary installations of storage batteries","Only batteries in vehicles","Only primary (non-rechargeable) cells","Only batteries under 12 volts"],"a":0,"e":"Article 480 applies to all stationary installations of storage batteries.","r":"NEC 480.1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":1,"q":"A storage battery as defined in Article 480 is made of one or more cells of which type?","o":["Only disposable alkaline cells","Only solar cells","Only fuel cells","Rechargeable cells such as lead-acid, nickel-cadmium or other rechargeable electrochemical types"],"a":3,"e":"A storage battery is comprised of one or more rechargeable cells of the lead-acid, nickel-cadmium, or other rechargeable electrochemical types.","r":"NEC 480.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":1,"q":"What does the term nominal battery voltage mean in Article 480?","o":["The voltage of the charger only","A value assigned to a cell or battery of a given voltage class for convenient designation, with the actual operating voltage able to vary above or below it","The exact voltage at all times under load","The highest voltage ever measured"],"a":1,"e":"Nominal battery voltage is a designation value; the operating voltage may vary above or below it.","r":"NEC 480.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":1,"q":"Which describes a sealed cell or battery?","o":["One with no provision for routine addition of water or electrolyte or for external measurement of electrolyte specific gravity","One with removable caps for testing","One that is open to the air and refilled monthly","One that is a primary cell"],"a":0,"e":"A sealed cell or battery has no provision for routine addition of water or electrolyte or for external specific gravity measurement.","r":"NEC 480.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":1,"q":"What is an intercell connector?","o":["An electrical conductor used to connect adjacent cells","A device that disconnects the battery from the load","A fuse inside the charger","The part of the container that holds electrolyte"],"a":0,"e":"An intercell connector is an electrical conductor used to connect adjacent cells.","r":"NEC 480.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":2,"q":"Storage batteries and battery management equipment are required to be what, where required elsewhere in the Code?","o":["Listed","Located in a vault","Painted","Grounded at each cell"],"a":0,"e":"Storage batteries and battery management equipment must be listed where required elsewhere in the Code.","r":"NEC 480.3","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":2,"q":"Wiring and equipment supplied from storage batteries are generally subject to which provisions?","o":["Only the manufacturer's instructions","Only Article 480","No Code provisions because it is dc","The Code provisions for wiring and equipment operating at the same voltage"],"a":3,"e":"Wiring and equipment supplied from storage batteries are subject to the Code provisions that apply to wiring and equipment operating at the same voltage.","r":"NEC 480.5","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":2,"q":"Where must the battery system disconnecting means be located?","o":["Readily accessible and within sight of the battery system","Anywhere on the property","In a locked room away from the battery","Only inside the battery enclosure"],"a":0,"e":"The disconnecting means must be readily accessible and located within sight of the battery system.","r":"NEC 480.7(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":2,"q":"A 125-volt dc stationary battery system supplies a load. How many of the ungrounded conductors derived from the battery require the disconnect?","o":["Only the positive conductor","All ungrounded conductors","Only the conductor with the highest current","None because the voltage is low"],"a":1,"e":"A disconnecting means must be provided for all ungrounded conductors derived from a stationary battery system over 60 volts dc.","r":"NEC 480.7(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":2,"q":"If the battery disconnecting means is remotely controlled, what feature is required?","o":["It must be hidden from workers","It must be capable of being locked in the open position, and its location must be field marked on the battery disconnect","It must be automatically closed on loss of power","It must be a plug-in fuse"],"a":1,"e":"A remote-controlled disconnect must be capable of being locked in the open position, with the remote location field marked on the battery disconnect.","r":"NEC 480.7(B)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":2,"q":"What must be provided for rooms or areas where batteries produce gas?","o":["Sufficient diffusion and ventilation of gases to prevent accumulation of an explosive mixture","Unvented cabinets only","A sealed airtight room","Open flames for gas detection"],"a":0,"e":"Provisions must be made for sufficient diffusion and ventilation of gases from the battery to prevent accumulation of an explosive mixture.","r":"NEC 480.10(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":2,"q":"For battery racks, what minimum clearance is required between a cell container and any wall or structure on the side not requiring access for maintenance?","o":["1 inch","1/4 inch","6 inches","12 inches"],"a":0,"e":"There must be at least 25 mm (1 in.) clearance between a cell container and a wall or structure on the side not requiring maintenance access. Stands may touch walls if the shelf has 90 percent free air space along its length.","r":"NEC 480.10(C)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":2,"q":"Battery stands are permitted to contact adjacent walls or structures provided the shelf has what?","o":["A steel plate behind it","A waterproof coating","Free air space for 10 percent of its length","Free air space for not less than 90 percent of its length"],"a":3,"e":"Battery stands may contact adjacent walls or structures if the shelf has free air space for not less than 90 percent of its length.","r":"NEC 480.10(C)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":2,"q":"Working space about a battery system is measured from what?","o":["The ceiling","The centerline of the room","The nearest panelboard","The edge of the battery cabinet, rack or tray"],"a":3,"e":"Working space about battery systems must comply with 110.26 and is measured from the edge of the battery cabinet, rack or tray.","r":"NEC 480.10(C)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":2,"q":"Where must lighting outlets for a battery system working space not be located?","o":["Near the door","On the ceiling of the room","Directly above the battery system","On any wall"],"a":2,"e":"Illumination must be provided for working spaces about battery systems, and lighting outlets must not be located directly above the battery system.","r":"NEC 480.10(F)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":2,"q":"Personnel doors for rooms designated as battery rooms must meet which requirement?","o":["Be fixed shut with a window","Be sliding doors","Open in the direction of egress and be equipped with listed panic hardware","Open inward and be locked from inside"],"a":2,"e":"Doors for entrance to and egress from battery rooms must open in the direction of egress and have listed panic hardware.","r":"NEC 480.10(D)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":2,"q":"Each vented cell must be equipped with what?","o":["A bleed valve to atmosphere without protection","A tightly sealed lid with no vent","A fuse on each vent","A flame arrester designed to prevent destruction of the cell from ignition of gases outside the cell"],"a":3,"e":"Vented cells must have a flame arrester to prevent destruction of the cell due to ignition of gases outside the cell.","r":"NEC 480.11","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":2,"q":"Sealed cells must be equipped with or designed with what?","o":["A magnetic seal","A pressure-release vent, or a design that prevents scattering of cell parts in a cell explosion","An open top for water addition","A flame arrester only"],"a":1,"e":"Sealed cells must have a pressure-release vent or be designed to prevent scattering of cell parts if the cell explodes.","r":"NEC 480.11","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":3,"q":"Flexible cables permitted within a battery enclosure from battery terminals to a nearby junction box must be what minimum size?","o":["6 AWG","2/0 AWG","2 AWG","4/0 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Flexible cables as identified in Article 400, in sizes 2/0 AWG and larger, are permitted within the battery enclosure from the battery terminals to a nearby junction box.","r":"NEC 480.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":2,"q":"Flexible battery cables used between cells within a battery enclosure must be what?","o":["Ordinary lamp cord","Bare solid copper wire","Unlisted welding cable of any type","Listed for hard usage and identified as moisture resistant"],"a":3,"e":"Flexible battery cables must be listed for hard usage and identified as moisture-resistant.","r":"NEC 480.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":3,"q":"A flexible cable is run from a battery terminal to a junction box and from cell to cell inside the battery enclosure. Where is the flexible cable permitted?","o":["Only in direct burial","Along the entire building feeder","Within the battery enclosure from terminals to a nearby junction box and between cells","Only outdoors"],"a":2,"e":"Flexible cables are allowed within the enclosure from terminals to a nearby junction box, where they connect to an approved wiring method, and between cells.","r":"NEC 480.12","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":2,"q":"Ground-fault detection is required for what kind of battery system?","o":["Only 12-volt systems","Ungrounded battery systems","Grounded battery systems only","None"],"a":1,"e":"Ground-fault detection must be provided for ungrounded battery systems.","r":"NEC 480.13","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":3,"q":"A solidly grounded dc battery system and an ungrounded dc battery system are being installed. Which one requires battery ground-fault detection under Article 480?","o":["Only the ungrounded system","Both systems","Neither system","Only the solidly grounded system"],"a":0,"e":"Ground-fault detection is required for ungrounded battery systems.","r":"NEC 480.13","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":1,"q":"Article 490 covers general requirements for equipment operating at more than what nominal voltage?","o":["5000 volts","1000 volts","600 volts","2400 volts"],"a":1,"e":"Article 490 applies to equipment operating at more than 1000 volts, nominal.","r":"NEC 490.1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum height of a fence enclosing an outdoor installation of equipment operating over 1000 volts, if the fence is of the plain type without an extension?","o":["7 feet","6 feet","5 feet","8 feet"],"a":0,"e":"Fences must be not less than 7 ft in height, or a combination of 6 ft or more of fence fabric and a 1 ft or more extension using three or more strands of barbed wire or equivalent.","r":"NEC 110.31","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":2,"q":"Which fence arrangement meets the minimum for enclosing an outdoor over-1000-volt installation?","o":["4 ft of fence fabric with a 3 ft extension","6 ft of fence fabric with no extension","5 ft of fence fabric with a 1 ft extension of barbed wire","6 ft of fence fabric with a 1 ft extension of three or more strands of barbed wire"],"a":3,"e":"The alternative to a 7 ft fence is 6 ft or more of fence fabric plus a 1 ft or more extension of three or more strands of barbed wire or equivalent.","r":"NEC 110.31","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum fire rating for the walls, roof and floor of an electrical vault housing equipment over 1000 volts, without a reduction for suppression systems?","o":["2 hours","1 hour","3 hours","4 hours"],"a":2,"e":"Vault walls, roof and floors must have adequate structural strength and a minimum fire rating of 3 hours.","r":"NEC 110.31(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":2,"q":"The floor of an electrical vault for over-1000-volt equipment is in contact with the earth. What is required?","o":["Wood planks","Concrete not less than 4 inches thick","Gravel at least 2 inches deep","Concrete not less than 12 inches thick"],"a":1,"e":"Floors in contact with the earth must be concrete not less than 100 mm (4 in.) thick.","r":"NEC 110.31(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":2,"q":"Which describes the personnel doors of an electrical vault containing equipment over 1000 volts?","o":["Open inward and have no locks","Fixed shut","Sliding doors with a deadbolt only","Equipped with locks, kept locked, swing out and have panic bars or pressure plates that open under simple pressure"],"a":3,"e":"Vault personnel doors must be locked with access limited to qualified persons and swing out with panic bars, pressure plates or similar devices.","r":"NEC 110.31(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":1,"q":"What wording must the permanent warning signs read at entrances to rooms containing exposed live parts over 1000 volts?","o":["AUTHORIZED PERSONNEL ONLY","CAUTION - ELECTRICAL ROOM","DANGER - HIGH VOLTAGE - KEEP OUT","WARNING - WET FLOOR"],"a":2,"e":"Where the voltage exceeds 1000 volts, permanent and conspicuous warning signs reading DANGER - HIGH VOLTAGE - KEEP OUT must be provided.","r":"NEC 110.34(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":2,"q":"Entrances to rooms containing exposed live parts over 1000 volts must be handled how?","o":["Covered with a curtain","Left open for ventilation","Equipped with a doorbell only","Kept locked unless under observation of a qualified person at all times"],"a":3,"e":"Entrances must be kept locked unless they are under the observation of a qualified person at all times, and warning signs must be posted.","r":"NEC 110.34(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":2,"q":"Equipment at a nominal voltage to ground of 2400 volts has exposed live parts on one side and no live or grounded parts on the other side (Condition 1). What is the minimum depth of clear working space?","o":["5 feet","4 feet","3 feet","6 feet"],"a":2,"e":"For 1001-2500 volts to ground, Condition 1 requires 3 ft, Condition 2 requires 4 ft, and Condition 3 requires 5 ft.","r":"NEC 110.34(A), Table 110.34(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":2,"q":"Equipment is at a nominal 7200 volts to ground. Exposed live parts are on one side of the working space and grounded parts are on the other (Condition 2). What is the minimum clear working space depth?","o":["4 feet","9 feet","5 feet","6 feet"],"a":2,"e":"For 2501-9000 volts to ground, Condition 1 is 4 ft, Condition 2 is 5 ft and Condition 3 is 6 ft.","r":"NEC 110.34(A), Table 110.34(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":3,"q":"Switchgear at 7200 volts to ground has exposed live parts on both sides of the working space (Condition 3). What is the minimum depth of clear working space?","o":["5 feet","4 feet","9 feet","6 feet"],"a":3,"e":"For 2501-9000 volts to ground, Condition 3 (live parts on both sides) requires 6 ft.","r":"NEC 110.34(A), Table 110.34(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":3,"q":"A 13.8 kV (13,800 volt) delta system with nominal voltage to ground of 8000 volts has exposed live parts opposite grounded parts (Condition 2). What is the minimum working space depth?","o":["9 feet","4 feet","5 feet","6 feet"],"a":2,"e":"8000 volts to ground falls in the 2501-9000 volt row; Condition 2 requires 5 ft.","r":"NEC 110.34(A), Table 110.34(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":3,"q":"Equipment has a nominal voltage to ground of 14,400 volts, with exposed live parts on both sides of the working space (Condition 3). What is the minimum working space depth?","o":["9 feet","8 feet","5 feet","6 feet"],"a":0,"e":"For 9001-25,000 volts to ground, the depths are 5 ft, 6 ft and 9 ft for Conditions 1, 2 and 3.","r":"NEC 110.34(A), Table 110.34(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":2,"q":"Unguarded live parts of a 4160-volt (between phases) installation above the working space must be elevated at least how high?","o":["7 feet","9 feet","10 feet","8 feet"],"a":1,"e":"For nominal voltages of 1001-7500 volts between phases, the minimum elevation of unguarded live parts above working space is 9 ft.","r":"NEC 110.34(E), Table 110.34(E)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":2,"q":"Lighting for the working space about over-1000-volt equipment must be arranged how?","o":["So that no lighting is needed","So the lights are directly above exposed live parts","So the lights are turned on only during maintenance by a hidden switch","So persons changing lamps or making repairs are not endangered by live parts"],"a":3,"e":"Illumination must be provided and lighting outlets arranged so persons changing lamps or making repairs on the lighting system are not endangered by live parts.","r":"NEC 110.34(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":2,"q":"Switches operating at 480 volts are installed in the same room as exposed live parts operating at 4160 volts. What is required?","o":["Nothing, because 480 volts is lower","All equipment must be ungrounded","The two must be on the same bus","Effective separation by a suitable partition, fence or screen, unless the low-voltage equipment serves only the high-voltage enclosure and is accessible to qualified persons only"],"a":3,"e":"High-voltage equipment must be effectively separated from low-voltage equipment by a partition, fence or screen, with an exception for low-voltage equipment serving only the high-voltage enclosure and accessible to qualified persons only.","r":"NEC 110.34(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":1,"q":"Where energized parts over 1000 volts are exposed, the minimum clear work space must be at least how high and wide?","o":["5 ft high and 2 ft wide","6 ft high and 30 in. wide","8 ft high and 4 ft wide","6.5 ft high and 3 ft wide"],"a":3,"e":"Section 110.32 requires clear work space not less than 6.5 ft high and 3 ft wide (parallel to the equipment).","r":"NEC 110.32","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":2,"q":"At least one entrance to the working space about over-1000-volt equipment must be what minimum size?","o":["36 inches wide and 6 feet high","24 inches wide and 6.5 feet high","12 inches wide and 6 feet high","30 inches wide and 7 feet high"],"a":1,"e":"At least one entrance not less than 610 mm (24 in.) wide and 1.98 m (6.5 ft) high must provide access to the working space.","r":"NEC 110.33(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is a permitted method for circuit conductors over 1000 volts?","o":["Only flat cable assemblies","Only open knob and tube wiring","Raceways, cable trays, metal-clad cable, bare busbars, or Type MV cable as permitted","Only extension cords"],"a":2,"e":"Circuit conductors over 1000 volts may be installed in raceways, cable trays, as metal-clad cable, as bare wire, cable and busbars, or as Type MV cables as provided in the applicable sections.","r":"NEC 110.36","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum cover for direct-buried cables rated 1001 volts to 22 kV in a general location under Table 300.50?","o":["24 inches","30 inches","18 inches","12 inches"],"a":1,"e":"Table 300.50 requires 750 mm (30 in.) of cover for direct-buried cables rated 1001 V to 22 kV.","r":"NEC 300.50, Table 300.50","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum cover for rigid metal conduit enclosing conductors rated 1001 volts to 22 kV in a general location?","o":["12 inches","6 inches","18 inches","30 inches"],"a":1,"e":"Table 300.50 allows 150 mm (6 in.) of cover over rigid metal conduit or IMC for 1001 V to 22 kV circuits.","r":"NEC 300.50, Table 300.50","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":2,"q":"As a general rule, may conductors of a circuit rated over 1000 volts occupy the same raceway with conductors of a 480-volt circuit?","o":["No, not as a general rule","Yes, if the 480-volt circuit is a lighting circuit","Yes, if both are copper","Yes, always"],"a":0,"e":"Circuits over 1000 volts must not occupy the same enclosure, cable or raceway with circuits of 1000 volts or less, except as permitted by specific exceptions.","r":"NEC 300.3(C)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":1,"q":"What is required on all boxes, fittings and similar enclosures in over-1000-volt wiring?","o":["Suitable covers to prevent accidental contact with energized parts or physical damage","Open tops for heat dissipation","Fabric covers","Warning stickers instead of covers"],"a":0,"e":"Suitable covers must be installed on all boxes, fittings and similar enclosures to prevent accidental contact with energized parts or physical damage to parts or insulation.","r":"NEC 300.31","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":1,"q":"Emergency systems are those systems that are:","o":["Legally required and classed as emergency by codes or governmental agencies, automatically supplying illumination, power or both when normal supply fails","Used only for stage lighting","Installed at the owner's option to protect property and production","Required only for temporary construction sites"],"a":0,"e":"Emergency systems are legally required systems intended to automatically supply illumination, power or both to designated areas and equipment on failure of the normal supply. Optional standby is Article 702.","r":"NEC 700.1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":1,"q":"On failure of the normal supply, an emergency system must make power available within what maximum time?","o":["30 seconds","60 seconds","10 seconds","1 hour"],"a":2,"e":"Emergency illumination or power must be available within the time required for the application but not to exceed 10 seconds.","r":"NEC 700.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":1,"q":"A storage battery used as the emergency source must be able to supply the total emergency load for at least how long?","o":["30 minutes","1-1/2 hours","4 hours","1 hour"],"a":1,"e":"A storage battery must supply and maintain the total load for at least 1-1/2 hours.","r":"NEC 700.12(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"During the required 1-1/2 hour period, the emergency battery must maintain the voltage applied to the load at not less than what percent of nominal?","o":["87-1/2 percent","95 percent","80 percent","75 percent"],"a":0,"e":"The battery must maintain not less than 87-1/2 percent of the system voltage for the load of the circuits for the required period.","r":"NEC 700.12(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":1,"q":"Which type of battery is NOT permitted as an emergency system source?","o":["Lead-acid battery designed for emergency service","Nickel-cadmium battery designed for emergency service","Alkali-type battery designed for emergency service","Automotive-type battery"],"a":3,"e":"Batteries must be designed and constructed for emergency service, and automotive-type batteries are not permitted.","r":"NEC 700.12(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"An internal combustion engine drives the emergency generator. What on-site fuel supply is required?","o":["Enough for 30 minutes","Enough for at least 2 hours of full-demand operation","None if the building has a gas line","Enough for 8 hours"],"a":1,"e":"An on-premises fuel supply sufficient for not less than 2 hours of full-demand operation of the system is required.","r":"NEC 700.12(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"A fuel transfer pump delivers fuel to the day tank of an emergency generator. How must that pump be supplied?","o":["From a separate optional standby system","From the normal system only","It needs no power connection","It must be connected to the emergency system"],"a":3,"e":"Where power is needed for fuel transfer pumps that deliver fuel to the day tank, the pump must be connected to the emergency system.","r":"NEC 700.12(B)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":3,"q":"An outdoor emergency generator set has a readily accessible disconnect within sight of the building it supplies. Ungrounded conductors serve the building. Is an additional disconnect required at the building?","o":["Yes, always","Yes, but only if over 600 volts","No, an additional disconnecting means is not required","Yes, only if the generator is over 100 kW"],"a":2,"e":"Where an outdoor generator has a readily accessible disconnecting means within sight of the building supplied, no additional disconnect is required where ungrounded conductors serve or pass through the building.","r":"NEC 700.12(B)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"A second utility service is proposed as the emergency source. What conditions apply?","o":["It is always permitted if it comes from the same transformer","It is never permitted","It must be approved by the AHJ and be sufficiently remote electrically and physically from the normal service to minimize simultaneous interruption","It is permitted if connected to the same service drop"],"a":2,"e":"A separate service may be used as an emergency source only where approved by the AHJ, and it must be sufficiently remote electrically and physically from the normal service.","r":"NEC 700.12(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"A unit equipment emergency light is cord-and-plug connected. What is the maximum cord length?","o":["1 foot","6 feet","3 feet","10 feet"],"a":2,"e":"Flexible cord-and-plug connection of unit equipment is permitted if the cord does not exceed 900 mm (3 ft) in length.","r":"NEC 700.12(F)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"The branch circuit feeding emergency unit equipment must be which circuit?","o":["A dedicated 30-ampere circuit with a switch","Any receptacle circuit in the building","A circuit controlled by a local wall switch","The same branch circuit that serves the normal lighting in the area, connected ahead of any local switches"],"a":3,"e":"The unit equipment branch circuit must be the same circuit as the normal lighting in the area and connected ahead of any local switches.","r":"NEC 700.12(F)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":1,"q":"How must emergency unit equipment be installed?","o":["Hung by its cord","Plugged into any convenient outlet and movable","Permanently fixed in place, not portable","On wheels"],"a":2,"e":"Unit equipment must be permanently fixed in place (not portable) and wired per Chapter 3 or via a short cord and plug.","r":"NEC 700.12(F)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":1,"q":"Emergency system transfer equipment must be designed and installed to prevent what?","o":["Inadvertent interconnection of the normal and emergency sources in any operation of the transfer equipment","Any use of the emergency source","Use of automatic controls","Operation of the normal source"],"a":0,"e":"Transfer equipment must prevent inadvertent interconnection of normal and emergency sources of supply.","r":"NEC 700.5(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"Automatic transfer switches used in emergency systems must be what?","o":["Manually operated only","Magnetically held only","Mechanically operated and electrically held","Electrically operated and mechanically held"],"a":3,"e":"Automatic transfer switches must be electrically operated and mechanically held.","r":"NEC 700.5","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"Emergency system transfer equipment may supply which loads?","o":["Only emergency loads","Any building load","Emergency and optional standby loads","Only lighting loads"],"a":0,"e":"Transfer equipment in an emergency system must supply only emergency loads.","r":"NEC 700.5(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":1,"q":"Transfer equipment for an emergency system must be what in terms of listing/identification?","o":["Unlisted equipment","Automatic and identified for emergency use","Manual and general purpose","Any listed switch"],"a":1,"e":"Transfer equipment must be automatic, identified for emergency use, and approved by the AHJ.","r":"NEC 700.5(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":1,"q":"Where practicable, audible and visual signals are required on an emergency system to indicate which condition?","o":["Door open","The normal lights are on","The building is occupied","Derangement of the emergency source"],"a":3,"e":"Signal devices are required to indicate derangement of the emergency source, that the source is carrying load, that the battery charger is not functioning, and ground fault where applicable.","r":"NEC 700.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"Where a battery is the emergency source, a signal is required to indicate what?","o":["That the battery is fully charged","That the battery charger is not functioning","That the room is ventilated","That the battery has been installed 1 year"],"a":1,"e":"A signal must indicate that the battery charger is not functioning where a battery is the emergency source.","r":"NEC 700.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":3,"q":"An emergency system is a solidly grounded wye 480Y/277-volt system with a 1200-ampere main device at the emergency source. What ground-fault provision is required?","o":["Automatic GFPE tripping of the emergency source","No ground-fault indication of any kind","A ground-fault indication at the emergency source; automatic GFPE disconnection is not permitted ahead of the source","Indication is required only for 120-volt systems"],"a":2,"e":"Solidly grounded wye emergency systems over 150 V to ground with devices of 1000 A or more require ground-fault indication (not automatic disconnection by GFPE).","r":"NEC 700.6(D); 700.31","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"The alternate source for an emergency system must NOT include what?","o":["A transfer switch","Ground-fault protection of equipment with automatic disconnecting means","Overcurrent protection","Short-circuit protection"],"a":1,"e":"The emergency source must not include ground-fault protection of equipment with automatic disconnecting means; ground-fault indication is used instead.","r":"NEC 700.31","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"A sign is required at the service-entrance equipment of a building with an on-site emergency source. What must it indicate?","o":["The date of the last test","The type and location of the on-site emergency power source","The names of all occupants","The utility account number"],"a":1,"e":"A sign must indicate the type and location of the on-site emergency power sources, unless the source is within sight of the service.","r":"NEC 700.7(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":1,"q":"What device must be installed in or on all emergency system switchboards and panelboards?","o":["An AFCI breaker","A listed surge protective device","A GFCI breaker","A capacitor bank"],"a":1,"e":"A listed SPD must be installed in or on all emergency systems switchboards and panelboards.","r":"NEC 700.8","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":1,"q":"How must boxes and enclosures such as transfer switches, generators and power panels of an emergency system be identified?","o":["Permanently marked as a component of an emergency circuit or system","Painted yellow only","With a temporary paper tag","They need no marking"],"a":0,"e":"All boxes and enclosures, including transfer switches, generators and power panels, must be permanently marked as components of an emergency circuit or system.","r":"NEC 700.10(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"Emergency wiring must be kept entirely independent of other wiring. Which is a permitted exception for shared enclosures?","o":["Transfer equipment enclosures","Panelboards with ordinary branch circuits","Any junction box with normal circuits","Any raceway with normal lighting"],"a":0,"e":"Emergency wiring may enter the same enclosure as other wiring only in transfer equipment enclosures, in exit or emergency luminaires supplied from two sources, and in a common junction box attached to such luminaires.","r":"NEC 700.10(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":3,"q":"An exit sign is supplied from both a normal source and an emergency source. May both circuits be present inside the luminaire?","o":["Yes, in exit or emergency luminaires supplied from two sources","Only in a separate box","Only if both are the same voltage and phase","No, never"],"a":0,"e":"Circuits of emergency and normal sources may enter exit or emergency luminaires supplied from two sources, and a common junction box attached to them.","r":"NEC 700.10(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"Emergency system wiring must be designed and located to minimize hazards from what?","o":["Ordinary wear only","Noise","Hot weather only","Flooding, fire, icing, vandalism and other adverse conditions"],"a":3,"e":"Wiring must be designed and located to minimize hazards that might cause failure due to flooding, fire, icing, vandalism and other adverse conditions.","r":"NEC 700.10(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":3,"q":"An assembly occupancy has an occupant load of 1500 persons. Which feeder wiring for the emergency system satisfies the fire-protection requirement?","o":["A listed system with a 30-minute fire rating","Type NM cable","Cable that is a listed electrical circuit protective system with a minimum 2-hour fire rating","THHN in EMT in an unsprinklered space"],"a":2,"e":"In assembly occupancies for 1000 or more persons, feeder wiring must be in fully sprinklered spaces, in a listed 2-hour electrical circuit protective system, in a 2-hour fire-rated assembly, or encased in 2 in. of concrete.","r":"NEC 700.10(D)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"What may be supplied by emergency lighting circuits?","o":["Break room appliances","Any convenience receptacle","Decorative luminaires","Only lamps and appliances specified as required for emergency use"],"a":3,"e":"No appliances and no lamps other than those specified as required for emergency use may be supplied by emergency lighting circuits.","r":"NEC 700.15","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":1,"q":"Emergency illumination must include which of the following?","o":["Only decorative lighting","All required means of egress lighting and illuminated exit signs","Only office task lighting","Only parking lot lighting"],"a":1,"e":"Emergency illumination includes all required means of egress lighting, illuminated exit signs and other luminaires needed for sufficient illumination.","r":"NEC 700.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"Failure of a single lamp in an area requiring emergency illumination must have what effect?","o":["It must not leave any space requiring emergency illumination in total darkness","It disables the whole system","It may leave the space dark","It need not be considered"],"a":0,"e":"Failure of any individual lighting element must not leave any space that requires emergency illumination in total darkness.","r":"NEC 700.16","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"Branch circuits supplying emergency lighting must provide service from which arrangement when the normal lighting supply is interrupted?","o":["A manual switch operated by staff","A single circuit with no backup","An independent emergency lighting supply with automatic transfer, or two or more separate and complete systems","A portable flashlight"],"a":2,"e":"Emergency lighting circuits require an independent supply with automatic transfer, or separate complete systems with independent supplies.","r":"NEC 700.17","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":1,"q":"For branch circuits supplying equipment classed as emergency, the load must be transferred to the emergency source how?","o":["By a timer after one hour","Manually by the occupant","Automatically upon failure of the normal supply","Only on request of the utility"],"a":2,"e":"There must be an emergency supply source to which the load is transferred automatically upon failure of the normal supply.","r":"NEC 700.18","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":1,"q":"Who must have access to the feeder and branch-circuit overcurrent devices of an emergency system?","o":["Authorized persons only","Only utility workers","The general public","Any occupant"],"a":0,"e":"Feeder- and branch-circuit overcurrent devices must be accessible to authorized persons only.","r":"NEC 700.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"Emergency system overcurrent devices must be selectively coordinated with what?","o":["Only the generator","Nothing","All supply-side overcurrent protective devices","Only the downstream branch devices"],"a":2,"e":"Emergency system overcurrent devices must be selectively coordinated with all supply side overcurrent protective devices.","r":"NEC 700.32","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"If an emergency system relies on a single alternate source that will be disabled for maintenance, what must be provided?","o":["Permanent switching means to connect a portable or temporary alternate source, available for the duration of the work","A second normal service","A sign only","Nothing; the system may be down"],"a":0,"e":"Where a single alternate source will be disabled for maintenance or repair, permanent switching means to connect a temporary alternate source must be provided.","r":"NEC 700.3(F)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"What record is required for emergency system tests and maintenance?","o":["A report only if a failure occurs","A verbal report only","A written record of the tests and maintenance","No record is required"],"a":2,"e":"A written record must be kept of required tests and maintenance.","r":"NEC 700.3(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"Means must be provided to test all emergency lighting and power systems during what condition?","o":["Night only","Utility outage only","No-load conditions only","Maximum anticipated load conditions"],"a":3,"e":"Means for testing emergency lighting and power systems during maximum anticipated load conditions must be provided.","r":"NEC 700.3(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"When one alternate source supplies emergency, legally required standby and optional standby loads with automatic load pickup and shedding, what is the order of priority?","o":["All equal","Legally required standby, then emergency, then optional standby","Optional standby, then legally required standby, then emergency","Emergency, then legally required standby, then optional standby"],"a":3,"e":"Load pickup and shedding must ensure adequate power to emergency circuits first, then legally required standby, then optional standby.","r":"NEC 700.4(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"Legally required standby systems are intended to automatically supply power to which loads?","o":["Only egress lighting","Selected loads, other than those classed as emergency systems, on failure of the normal source","Only loads on the utility side of the service","Any load the owner chooses, as an option"],"a":1,"e":"Legally required standby systems are required by governmental codes and automatically supply selected loads (other than emergency loads) when the normal source fails. Loads the owner chooses are optional standby (Art. 702).","r":"NEC 701.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"Article 701 applies to which systems?","o":["Only residential generators","Systems installed for the owner's convenience","Only fire pumps","Legally required standby systems for required facilities when the normal supply is interrupted"],"a":3,"e":"Article 701 covers legally required standby systems consisting of circuits and equipment supplying required facilities when the normal supply is interrupted.","r":"NEC 701.1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"On failure of the normal supply, legally required standby power must be available within what maximum time?","o":["10 seconds","5 minutes","15 minutes","60 seconds"],"a":3,"e":"Standby power must be available within the time required for the application but not to exceed 60 seconds. The 10-second limit is for emergency systems.","r":"NEC 701.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"A storage battery used as the legally required standby source must supply the total load for at least how long?","o":["2 hours","30 minutes","1-1/2 hours","1 hour"],"a":2,"e":"The battery must supply and maintain the total load for at least 1-1/2 hours.","r":"NEC 701.12(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"During that period, the battery must maintain the voltage to the load at not less than what percent of nominal?","o":["75 percent","90 percent","80 percent","87-1/2 percent"],"a":3,"e":"The voltage applied to the load must not fall below 87-1/2 percent of normal.","r":"NEC 701.12(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"An internal combustion engine-driven generator serves as the legally required standby source. What on-premises fuel supply is required?","o":["Sufficient for 24 hours","No on-site fuel is needed","Sufficient for not less than 2 hours of full-demand operation","Sufficient for 15 minutes"],"a":2,"e":"On-site fuel sufficient for not less than 2 hours of full-demand operation is required.","r":"NEC 701.12(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"Where a fuel transfer pump is needed to deliver fuel to a generator day tank for a legally required standby system, how must the pump be supplied?","o":["From the legally required standby system","From a separate battery only","From the normal power only","It needs no supply"],"a":0,"e":"A fuel transfer pump needed to deliver fuel to the day tank must be connected to the standby system.","r":"NEC 701.12(B)","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"A separate second service is proposed as the legally required standby source. What conditions apply?","o":["It must be approved by the AHJ and be sufficiently remote electrically and physically to minimize simultaneous interruption","It must be on the same transformer","It may be the same service as normal","It is never permitted"],"a":0,"e":"A separate service is permitted where approved by the AHJ and sufficiently remote from the normal service.","r":"NEC 701.12(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":3,"q":"The standby source is connected ahead of, but not within, the service disconnecting means. When is this permitted?","o":["Only inside the service enclosure","Never","Where acceptable to the AHJ and with sufficient separation to prevent simultaneous interruption of supply","Whenever the owner wishes"],"a":2,"e":"Connections ahead of but not within the main service disconnecting means may be used where acceptable to the AHJ with separation sufficient to prevent simultaneous interruption.","r":"NEC 701.12(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"Legally required standby system wiring is subject to which rule on sharing with other wiring?","o":["It must be in rigid conduit with no other circuits","It must be in separate raceways from all other wiring","It must be in red conduit only","It may occupy the same raceways, cables, boxes and cabinets with other general wiring"],"a":3,"e":"Unlike emergency systems, legally required standby wiring may share raceways, cables, boxes and cabinets with other general wiring.","r":"NEC 701.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"An electrician wants to run a smoke-control fan circuit (legally required standby) in the same raceway as a normal lighting circuit. Is that permitted?","o":["Only if both are 277 volts","Only with a barrier","No, never","Yes, standby wiring may share raceways with general wiring"],"a":3,"e":"Section 701.10 permits legally required standby wiring to occupy the same raceways as other general wiring.","r":"NEC 701.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"A legally required standby system must have adequate capacity and rating for what?","o":["The lighting load only","Half of the connected load","All equipment intended to be operated at one time","Only the largest single load"],"a":2,"e":"The system must have adequate capacity and rating for the supply of all equipment intended to be operated at one time.","r":"NEC 701.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":3,"q":"Under what condition may the legally required standby source also supply optional standby loads?","o":["Never","Only with a manual transfer","Only if the optional loads are first","Where it has adequate capacity for all connected loads, or automatic selective load pickup and load shedding ensures power to the standby circuits"],"a":3,"e":"The source may supply both if capacity covers all connected loads, or if automatic selective load pickup and shedding ensure adequate power to the legally required standby circuits.","r":"NEC 701.4","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"Transfer equipment for legally required standby systems must be designed to prevent what?","o":["Any transfer at all","Use of fuses","Inadvertent interconnection of the normal and alternate sources","Use of a generator"],"a":2,"e":"Transfer equipment must prevent inadvertent interconnection of normal and alternate sources of supply.","r":"NEC 701.5","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"Automatic transfer switches in legally required standby systems must be what?","o":["Mechanically operated and electrically held","Electrically operated and mechanically held","Operated by hand crank only","Manually operated"],"a":1,"e":"Automatic transfer switches must be electrically operated and mechanically held.","r":"NEC 701.5","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"Transfer equipment for a legally required standby system must be which of the following?","o":["Fused disconnect only","Automatic, identified for the application, and approved by the AHJ","Any switch","Manual and unlisted"],"a":1,"e":"Transfer equipment must be automatic, identified for the intended use, and approved by the AHJ.","r":"NEC 701.5(A)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"Where practicable, signals are required for a legally required standby system to indicate which condition?","o":["Room is occupied","Door is open","Normal lights are on","Derangement of the standby source"],"a":3,"e":"Audible and visual signals must indicate derangement of the standby source, that it is carrying load, and that the battery charger is not functioning.","r":"NEC 701.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"The standby source is a storage battery. A signal is required to indicate what?","o":["The charger is operating normally","The battery is 50 percent full","The battery is installed","The battery charger is not functioning"],"a":3,"e":"A signal must indicate that the battery charger is not functioning.","r":"NEC 701.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"A sign at the service-entrance equipment of a building with an on-site legally required standby source must indicate what?","o":["The next test date","The fire alarm panel location","The building owner","The type and location of the on-site standby power source"],"a":3,"e":"A sign must be placed at the service entrance indicating the type and location of the on-site legally required standby power sources, unless the source is within sight of the service.","r":"NEC 701.7(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"What must be installed in or on all legally required standby systems switchboards and panelboards?","o":["A transformer","A GFCI","A capacitor","A listed surge protective device"],"a":3,"e":"A listed SPD must be installed in or on all legally required standby system switchboards and panelboards.","r":"NEC 701.35","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"Legally required standby system overcurrent devices must be selectively coordinated with what?","o":["Nothing","Only downstream devices","All supply-side overcurrent protective devices","Only the generator breaker"],"a":2,"e":"Overcurrent devices must be selectively coordinated with all supply-side overcurrent protective devices.","r":"NEC 701.32","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":3,"q":"Two overcurrent devices are in series in a legally required standby feeder, with no loads connected in parallel with the downstream device. Is selective coordination between them required?","o":["Yes, if either is a fuse","Yes, always","No, it is not required in that case","Yes, if over 600 volts"],"a":2,"e":"Selective coordination is not required between two overcurrent devices located in series if no loads are connected in parallel with the downstream device.","r":"NEC 701.32","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"Upon installation, a legally required standby system must be tested how?","o":["It is never tested","Only after a failure","Only by the manufacturer privately","The AHJ conducts or witnesses a test of the complete system, and it is tested periodically afterward"],"a":3,"e":"The authority having jurisdiction must conduct or witness a test of the complete system upon installation, with periodic testing afterward.","r":"NEC 701.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"What record must be kept for legally required standby system tests and maintenance?","o":["None","A written record","Verbal confirmation","Only the last test"],"a":1,"e":"A written record of tests and maintenance must be kept.","r":"NEC 701.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"Optional standby systems are intended to protect public or private facilities or property in which situation?","o":["Where life safety does not depend on the performance of the system","Where life safety depends on the system","Only for exit signs","Only in hospitals"],"a":0,"e":"Optional standby systems protect facilities or property where life safety does not depend on system performance; they may supply selected loads automatically or manually.","r":"NEC 702.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"Which load is a typical use for an optional standby system?","o":["Exit signs in a hospital","Required egress lighting in a theater","Heating or refrigeration systems whose loss would cause discomfort or product damage","Fire alarm required by code"],"a":2,"e":"Optional standby systems typically serve loads such as heating, refrigeration, data processing and industrial processes where an outage causes discomfort or damage, not life-safety loads.","r":"NEC 702.1","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"Optional standby system equipment must be suitable for what?","o":["The utility transformer size only","The maximum available short-circuit current at its terminals","The total connected load only","The generator kW rating only"],"a":1,"e":"Equipment must be suitable for the maximum available short-circuit current at its terminals.","r":"NEC 702.4(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"For an optional standby system with manual transfer equipment, how may the calculated load on the standby source be determined?","o":["By the number of receptacles","It is never calculated","In accordance with Article 220 or by another approved method","Only by measuring the utility demand"],"a":2,"e":"For manual transfer equipment, the calculated load must be in accordance with Article 220 or another approved method.","r":"NEC 702.4(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"An optional standby system with automatic transfer equipment and no load management must have a source capacity for what?","o":["The largest single load","No sizing is required","The full load that could be connected","Half of the connected load"],"a":2,"e":"For automatic transfer equipment, the standby source must be sized for full load unless a load management system is used.","r":"NEC 702.4(B)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":3,"q":"An optional standby system uses a load management system with automatic transfer equipment. What capacity must the standby source have?","o":["Only 50 percent of nameplate","The same as the service rating","Sufficient for the maximum load that will be connected by the load management system","Full connected load of the building regardless"],"a":2,"e":"With load management, the standby source must have capacity sufficient for the maximum load the load management system will connect.","r":"NEC 702.4(B)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"Transfer equipment for optional standby systems must be designed and installed to prevent what?","o":["Manual operation","Use of a generator","Inadvertent interconnection of normal and alternate sources","Use of a neutral"],"a":2,"e":"Transfer equipment must be suitable for the intended use and prevent inadvertent interconnection of the normal and alternate sources.","r":"NEC 702.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":3,"q":"Transfer equipment on the load side of branch circuit protection contains supplemental overcurrent protection. What interrupting rating is required?","o":["5 kA maximum","Equal to the utility fault current only","Sufficient for the available fault current that the generator can deliver","15 kA minimum in all cases"],"a":2,"e":"The supplementary overcurrent devices must be part of listed transfer equipment and have an interrupting rating sufficient for the available fault current the generator can deliver.","r":"NEC 702.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"A homeowner proposes to connect a portable generator to a panel through a power inlet and a double-ended cord without any transfer equipment. Is it compliant?","o":["Yes, if the main breaker is turned off by hand","No, transfer equipment is required to prevent backfeed and interconnection","Yes, with a sign","Yes, if the generator is small"],"a":1,"e":"Optional standby systems require suitable transfer equipment to prevent inadvertent interconnection of the normal and alternate sources.","r":"NEC 702.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"Which type of transfer equipment is permitted for optional standby systems?","o":["Automatic only","Manual only","Either manual or automatic transfer equipment suitable for the intended use","None"],"a":2,"e":"Optional standby systems may use manual or automatic transfer equipment, as long as it is suitable for the intended use and prevents interconnection.","r":"NEC 702.4; 702.5","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"A sign is required at the service-entrance equipment of a building with an on-site optional standby source. What must it indicate?","o":["The utility meter number","The type and location of the on-site optional standby power source","The names of loads","The date of last service"],"a":1,"e":"A sign must be placed at the service-entrance equipment indicating the type and location of the on-site optional standby power source.","r":"NEC 702.7(A)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":3,"q":"A power inlet is installed for a portable generator that is a non-separately derived (floating neutral) system. What must the sign near the inlet say?","o":["No sign is needed","Caution: high voltage","Warning: for connection of any generator","Warning: for connection of a non-separately derived (floating neutral) system only"],"a":3,"e":"A warning sign near the power inlet must identify the type of derived system the inlet is intended for, such as non-separately derived (floating neutral) or separately derived (bonded neutral).","r":"NEC 702.7","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"Optional standby system wiring may occupy which raceways?","o":["Only red raceways","Only rigid conduit","The same raceways, cables, boxes and cabinets with other general wiring","Only dedicated raceways"],"a":2,"e":"Optional standby system wiring is permitted to occupy the same raceways, cables, boxes and cabinets with other general wiring.","r":"NEC 702.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"A contractor routes optional standby circuits in the same conduit as normal branch circuits of the same system voltage. Is this allowed?","o":["Only for dc","Yes","No, never","Only with a barrier"],"a":1,"e":"Optional standby wiring is not required to be kept independent of other wiring.","r":"NEC 702.10","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":3,"q":"An outdoor generator set has a readily accessible disconnecting means within sight of the building it supplies. Ungrounded conductors serve the building. Is an additional disconnect required at the building?","o":["Yes, if over 150 volts","No","Yes, always","Yes, if over 30 kW"],"a":1,"e":"Where an outdoor generator has a readily accessible disconnect within sight of the building, no additional disconnect is required where ungrounded conductors serve or pass through the building.","r":"NEC 702.11; 702.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":3,"q":"A portable optional standby generator is used as a non-separately derived system. To what is the equipment grounding conductor bonded?","o":["Nothing","The system grounding electrode","The grounded conductor at the generator only","A separate ground rod only at the generator"],"a":1,"e":"For a non-separately derived portable source, the equipment grounding conductor is bonded to the system grounding electrode.","r":"NEC 702.11; 702.12","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":3,"q":"A portable optional standby generator is used as a separately derived system. To what is the equipment grounding conductor bonded?","o":["The service neutral only","Not bonded","The load side of branch circuits","The grounding electrode conductor"],"a":3,"e":"For a separately derived portable source, the equipment grounding conductor is bonded to the grounding electrode conductor.","r":"NEC 702.11; 702.12","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"Is selective coordination of overcurrent devices required by Article 702?","o":["No, Article 702 has no selective coordination requirement","Yes, for 480-volt only","Yes, for all generators","Yes, always"],"a":0,"e":"Selective coordination is required by Articles 700 and 701, but not Article 702.","r":"NEC 702 (no selective coordination requirement)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"Does Article 702 impose a 10-second or 60-second maximum transfer time on an optional standby system?","o":["No, no specific transfer time is specified","Yes, 60 seconds","Yes, 10 seconds","Yes, 5 minutes"],"a":0,"e":"The 10-second and 60-second limits apply to emergency and legally required standby systems, respectively.","r":"NEC 700.12; 701.12; Art. 702","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"A homeowner installs a generator to keep a sump pump and refrigerator running during outages by choice. Which article applies?","o":["Article 701","Article 700","Article 708","Article 702"],"a":3,"e":"Loads backed up by the owner's choice, where life safety does not depend on it, fall under optional standby systems.","r":"NEC 702.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":3,"q":"A permanently installed optional standby generator connects through a 3-pole transfer switch that does not switch the neutral. How is the neutral treated at the generator?","o":["Not bonded at the generator; the system is not separately derived","Bonded at the generator","Disconnected from the service","Grounded at a separate electrode only"],"a":0,"e":"When the transfer switch does not switch the grounded conductor, the system is not separately derived and the neutral must not be bonded again at the generator.","r":"NEC 250.35(A)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":3,"q":"A permanently installed optional standby generator connects through a 4-pole transfer switch that switches the neutral. How is the system treated?","o":["As an ungrounded system","As a non-separately derived system","As a separately derived system, with neutral bonded at the generator per 250.30","As a service"],"a":2,"e":"When the grounded conductor is switched, the generator is a separately derived system and bonding and grounding follow 250.30.","r":"NEC 250.35(B); 250.30","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"Which set of components must emergency unit equipment include?","o":["A generator and fuel tank","A capacitor and a timer only","A rechargeable battery, battery charging means, provisions for one or more lamps, and a relaying device that energizes the lamps automatically on supply failure","A non-rechargeable battery and a manual switch only"],"a":2,"e":"Unit equipment must include a rechargeable battery, a charging means, provisions for lamps or terminals for remote lamps, and a relaying device that automatically energizes the lamps on failure of supply.","r":"NEC 700.12(F)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":1,"q":"Where practicable, an emergency system must give an audible and visual signal when the emergency source is doing what?","o":["Idle and fully charged","Carrying the load","Being tested at no load only","Out of fuel only"],"a":1,"e":"Signals are required to indicate derangement of the emergency source and that the source is carrying load, among other conditions.","r":"NEC 700.6(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":1,"q":"In Article 480, what is a cell?","o":["A type of disconnect","The basic electrochemical unit, with an anode and cathode, used to receive, store and deliver electrical energy","A conductor between batteries","A complete battery room"],"a":1,"e":"A cell is the basic electrochemical unit characterized by an anode and a cathode, used to receive, store and deliver electrical energy.","r":"NEC 480.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 490 Equipment Over 1000 Volts","d":2,"q":"When is an indoor electrical installation over 1000 volts considered accessible to qualified persons only?","o":["When located in any corridor","When it is simply mounted above 6 ft","When in a vault, room, closet or area surrounded by a wall, screen or fence with access controlled by lock and key or other approved means","When it is labeled only"],"a":2,"e":"Installations in a locked vault, room, closet or fenced area with controlled access are considered accessible to qualified persons only.","r":"NEC 110.31","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"Which battery type is not permitted as a legally required standby source?","o":["Alkali battery designed for standby service","Nickel-cadmium battery designed for standby service","Automotive-type battery","Lead-acid battery designed for standby service"],"a":2,"e":"Batteries must be designed and constructed for the service, and automotive-type batteries are not to be used.","r":"NEC 701.12(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":1,"q":"How may an optional standby system supply selected loads?","o":["Manually only","Only through the utility meter","Either automatically or manually","Automatically only"],"a":2,"e":"Optional standby systems are intended to supply on-site generation to selected loads, either automatically or manually.","r":"NEC 702.2","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"Which type of hazardous material defines a Class I location?","o":["Combustible dusts","Flammable gases or vapors","Corrosive liquids","Ignitible fibers or flyings"],"a":1,"e":"Class I locations contain flammable gases, flammable liquid-produced vapors, or combustible liquid-produced vapors. Dusts are Class II and fibers/flyings are Class III.","r":"NEC 500.5(A), 500.5(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"Which type of hazardous material defines a Class II location?","o":["Combustible dust","Ignitible fibers or flyings","Flammable vapors","Explosive gases in pipelines only"],"a":0,"e":"Class II locations are hazardous because of the presence of combustible dust.","r":"NEC 500.5(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"Which type of hazardous material defines a Class III location?","o":["Flammable gases","Easily ignitible fibers or flyings","Carbon black","Combustible metal dust"],"a":1,"e":"Class III locations are hazardous because of easily ignitible fibers or flyings, but the fibers are not likely to be suspended in the air in quantities sufficient to produce ignitible mixtures.","r":"NEC 500.5(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"In a Class I, Division 1 location, ignitible concentrations of flammable gases or vapors:","o":["Exist under normal operating conditions","Exist only in the event of a pipe rupture","Are never present","Are prevented only by positive-pressure ventilation"],"a":0,"e":"Division 1 includes locations where ignitible concentrations exist under normal operating conditions, frequently because of repair, maintenance or leakage, or because of equipment breakdown that could also cause the electrical equipment to become an ignition source.","r":"NEC 500.5(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"Acetylene is classified in which Class I group?","o":["Group A","Group C","Group D","Group B"],"a":0,"e":"Acetylene is the only atmosphere in Group A.","r":"NEC 500.6(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"Hydrogen is classified in which Class I group?","o":["Group C","Group D","Group A","Group B"],"a":3,"e":"Hydrogen, along with butadiene, ethylene oxide, propylene oxide, and acrolein, is in Group B.","r":"NEC 500.6(A)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"Gasoline vapors are classified in which Class I group?","o":["Group D","Group B","Group E","Group C"],"a":0,"e":"Gasoline, acetone, benzene, butane, hexane, methanol, natural gas, and propane are Group D atmospheres. Group E is a Class II dust group.","r":"NEC 500.6(A)(4)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"Ethylene is classified in which Class I group?","o":["Group C","Group D","Group B","Group A"],"a":0,"e":"Ethylene is a Group C atmosphere.","r":"NEC 500.6(A)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"Combustible metal dusts such as aluminum and magnesium belong to which Class II group?","o":["Group F","Group G","Group D","Group E"],"a":3,"e":"Group E consists of combustible metal dusts including aluminum, magnesium, and their commercial alloys.","r":"NEC 500.6(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":2,"q":"Coal dust, coke dust and carbon black are in which Class II group?","o":["Group F","Group G","Group C","Group E"],"a":0,"e":"Group F is carbonaceous dust such as carbon black, charcoal, coal, or coke dusts. Group G covers flour, starch, grain, wood and plastic dusts.","r":"NEC 500.6(B)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"Grain dust and wood dust are in which Class II group?","o":["Group F","Group E","Group G","Group D"],"a":2,"e":"Group G covers combustible dusts not in Group E or F, including flour, starch, grain, wood, plastic, and chemicals.","r":"NEC 500.6(B)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"How many material groups are assigned to Class III locations?","o":["None","Two","Three","One"],"a":0,"e":"Class III fibers and flyings have no group designations.","r":"NEC 500.5(D), 500.6","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum surface temperature represented by temperature class T4?","o":["300 degrees C","100 degrees C","135 degrees C","200 degrees C"],"a":2,"e":"T4 corresponds to 135 degrees C (275 degrees F).","r":"NEC 500.8(C), Table 500.8(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"Which temperature identification number represents the lowest maximum surface temperature, 85 degrees C?","o":["T6","T5","T1","T3"],"a":0,"e":"T6 is 85 degrees C; T5 is 100 degrees C.","r":"NEC Table 500.8(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"Temperature class T3 corresponds to a maximum surface temperature of:","o":["450 degrees C","300 degrees C","200 degrees C","135 degrees C"],"a":2,"e":"T1 is 450, T2 is 300, T3 is 200, T4 is 135 degrees C.","r":"NEC Table 500.8(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"Temperature class T1 corresponds to a maximum surface temperature of:","o":["450 degrees C","300 degrees C","100 degrees C","200 degrees C"],"a":0,"e":"T1 is 450 degrees C (842 degrees F).","r":"NEC Table 500.8(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":2,"q":"In a Class I location, the maximum surface temperature of equipment must not exceed:","o":["The ignition temperature of the specific gas or vapor","The flash point of the liquid","85 degrees C in all cases","The ambient temperature plus 40 degrees C"],"a":0,"e":"Equipment must not operate at a temperature above the autoignition (ignition) temperature of the specific gas or vapor involved.","r":"NEC 500.8(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":2,"q":"Equipment marked Class I, Division 1, Group D is suitable for which atmosphere, ignoring temperature class?","o":["Acetylene","Hydrogen","Gasoline vapor","Aluminum dust"],"a":2,"e":"Group D equipment is suitable for Group D atmospheres such as gasoline. It is not marked for Group A or B (acetylene, hydrogen) or for Class II dust.","r":"NEC 500.8(C), 500.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":2,"q":"Equipment approved for a Division 1 location may be used in a Division 2 location of the same:","o":["Class only, regardless of group","Enclosure color","Class, group, and temperature class","Voltage only"],"a":2,"e":"Division 1 equipment is permitted in Division 2 for the same class, group, and temperature class.","r":"NEC 500.8","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":2,"q":"When rigid metal conduit is threaded into a fitting in a hazardous (classified) location, a minimum of how many threads must be fully engaged?","o":["Seven","Four","Three","Five"],"a":3,"e":"Threaded joints in hazardous locations require at least five threads fully engaged (for example 501.10 and 500.8(E)).","r":"NEC 500.8(E)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"Locations classified as hazardous must be:","o":["Reported to the utility","Marked by a sign at the entrance only","Documented only in Division 1","Properly documented and the documentation made available to those authorized to design, install, inspect, maintain, or operate the equipment"],"a":3,"e":"Areas designated as hazardous (classified) locations must be properly documented, with documentation available to authorized persons.","r":"NEC 500.4(A)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":2,"q":"Which describes a Class I, Division 2 location?","o":["No flammable materials are present at all","Combustible metal dusts are present","Ignitible concentrations exist continuously","Flammable liquids or gases are handled in closed containers or closed systems from which they escape only by accidental rupture, breakdown, or abnormal operation"],"a":3,"e":"Division 2 locations handle flammable materials in closed containers/systems, or prevent concentrations by positive mechanical ventilation, or are adjacent to Division 1.","r":"NEC 500.5(B)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":2,"q":"A Class II, Division 1 location is one where combustible dust is:","o":["Present in the air under normal operating conditions in quantities sufficient to produce explosive or ignitible mixtures","Never in the air","Only present on the floor","Stored in closed bags"],"a":0,"e":"Class II Division 1 includes locations where combustible dust is in the air under normal operating conditions in sufficient quantity.","r":"NEC 500.5(C)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":2,"q":"A Class II, Division 2 location is one where combustible dust:","o":["Is normally in suspension in the air","Is metal dust only","Is not normally in suspension and not likely to be thrown into suspension, but accumulations may interfere with heat dissipation or be ignited by abnormal operation","Is absent"],"a":2,"e":"In Division 2, dust is not ordinarily in suspension, but accumulations could cause problems or ignite on abnormal operation.","r":"NEC 500.5(C)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":2,"q":"A Class III, Division 2 location is one where easily ignitible fibers are:","o":["Manufactured or processed","Used in a manufacturing process","Present as gases","Stored or handled other than in the process of manufacture"],"a":3,"e":"Class III Division 1 is where fibers are handled, manufactured, or used; Division 2 is where they are stored or handled other than in manufacturing.","r":"NEC 500.5(D)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":2,"q":"Which protection technique limits electrical and thermal energy so that it cannot ignite a specific hazardous atmosphere?","o":["Explosionproof enclosure","Purging","Intrinsic safety","Oil immersion"],"a":2,"e":"Intrinsic safety limits energy in circuits to a level below that needed to ignite the atmosphere.","r":"NEC 500.7(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":2,"q":"Purged and pressurized enclosures prevent ignition by:","o":["Limiting circuit energy","Immersing contacts in oil","Containing an internal explosion","Supplying a protective gas at positive pressure to keep the flammable atmosphere out"],"a":3,"e":"Purging and pressurization supplies clean air or inert gas at a pressure that prevents entry of the hazardous atmosphere.","r":"NEC 500.7(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":2,"q":"An explosionproof enclosure is designed to:","o":["Withstand an internal explosion and prevent ignition of the surrounding atmosphere","Prevent any internal sparks","Keep out all gas","Operate at positive pressure"],"a":0,"e":"Explosionproof equipment withstands an internal explosion and prevents its ignition of the surrounding flammable atmosphere.","r":"NEC 500.7(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":2,"q":"Dust-ignitionproof equipment is enclosed so that it:","o":["Limits circuit energy","Is filled with oil","Excludes dust and does not permit arcs, sparks, or heat inside to ignite exterior dust accumulations or suspensions","Contains explosions"],"a":2,"e":"Dust-ignitionproof equipment excludes dust and its internal sparks or heat cannot ignite exterior dust.","r":"NEC 500.7(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":3,"q":"A paint-mixing room has gasoline-like Group D vapors normally present in ignitible concentrations during operation. What classification applies?","o":["Unclassified","Class I, Division 2, Group D","Class I, Division 1, Group D","Class II, Division 1, Group G"],"a":2,"e":"Ignitible Group D vapors under normal operating conditions make the location Class I, Division 1.","r":"NEC 500.5(B)(1), 500.6(A)(4)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":3,"q":"A flammable-liquid storage room keeps liquid only in sealed containers that could leak only through accidental rupture. The classification is most likely:","o":["Class I, Division 1","Class I, Division 2","Class II, Division 1","Class III, Division 1"],"a":1,"e":"Flammable liquids confined in closed containers that release only on accidental rupture are Division 2.","r":"NEC 500.5(B)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":3,"q":"A grain elevator has flour and grain dust suspended in air during normal operation. The classification is:","o":["Class III, Division 1","Class II, Division 1, Group G","Class II, Division 2, Group E","Class I, Division 1, Group D"],"a":1,"e":"Grain dust is Group G, and dust in the air under normal operation is Division 1.","r":"NEC 500.5(C)(1), 500.6(B)(3)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":3,"q":"A facility handles aluminum powder, with dust suspended during normal operation. Which classification applies?","o":["Class II, Division 1, Group F","Class II, Division 1, Group E","Class II, Division 2, Group G","Class I, Division 1, Group B"],"a":1,"e":"Metal dust such as aluminum is Group E; suspended in normal operation means Division 1.","r":"NEC 500.5(C)(1), 500.6(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":3,"q":"A room has positive mechanical ventilation that normally prevents ignitible Class I concentrations, but failure of ventilation could make it hazardous. Which classification fits?","o":["Division 2","Unclassified","Class III","Division 1"],"a":0,"e":"Where ventilation normally prevents ignitible concentrations but failure could allow them, the location is Division 2.","r":"NEC 500.5(B)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":2,"q":"Ammonia and propane are both classified in which Class I group?","o":["Group B","Group A","Group D","Group C"],"a":2,"e":"Acetone, ammonia, benzene, butane, ethanol, gasoline, hexane, methanol, natural gas, and propane are Group D.","r":"NEC 500.6(A)(4)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":1,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted in a Class I, Division 1 location?","o":["Service-entrance cable","Nonmetallic-sheathed cable","Electrical metallic tubing","Threaded rigid metal conduit"],"a":3,"e":"Threaded rigid metal conduit or threaded steel IMC is permitted in Division 1; EMT, NM, and SE cable are not.","r":"NEC 501.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":1,"q":"Type MI cable in a Class I, Division 1 location must be installed with:","o":["Wire nuts inside the cable sheath only","Termination fittings listed for the location","Any standard connector","No fittings at all"],"a":1,"e":"Type MI cable is permitted in Division 1 when terminated with fittings listed for the location.","r":"NEC 501.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Type MC-HL cable may be used in Class I, Division 1 only if it has a gas/vaportight continuous corrugated metallic sheath, a suitable polymeric overall jacket, and a separate equipment grounding conductor, and it is installed:","o":["In places of assembly","In any dwelling","In industrial establishments with restricted public access where only qualified persons service the installation","In residential garages only"],"a":2,"e":"Division 1 use of MC-HL is limited to industrial establishments with restricted public access and qualified maintenance and supervision.","r":"NEC 501.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":1,"q":"Which of these wiring methods is NOT permitted for fixed wiring in a Class I, Division 2 location?","o":["Electrical metallic tubing","Threaded rigid metal conduit","Threaded steel intermediate metal conduit","Type MI cable with listed fittings"],"a":0,"e":"EMT is not among the Class I Division 2 wiring methods in 501.10(B), although it is permitted in Class II Division 2 and Class III.","r":"NEC 501.10(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":1,"q":"Which cable type is permitted in a Class I, Division 2 location, including in cable tray, when listed for the location with suitable termination fittings?","o":["Type NM cable","Type UF cable","Type MC cable","Type AC cable"],"a":2,"e":"Type MC cable with listed termination fittings is permitted in Division 2; NM, UF and AC are not.","r":"NEC 501.10(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Where flexibility is necessary in a Class I, Division 1 location, which of these is permitted?","o":["Standard flexible metal conduit with ordinary connectors","Explosionproof flexible fittings or extra-hard-usage flexible cord with a grounding conductor and listed fittings","Liquidtight flexible nonmetallic conduit with standard fittings","Type NM cable"],"a":1,"e":"Division 1 flexibility is by listed explosionproof flexible fittings or extra-hard-usage flexible cord terminated with listed fittings.","r":"NEC 501.10(A)(2), 501.140","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"In a Class I, Division 2 location where flexibility is needed, which is permitted?","o":["Non-listed rubber hose","Liquidtight flexible metal conduit with listed fittings","Flexible metal conduit with set-screw connectors only","Armored cable (Type AC)"],"a":1,"e":"Division 2 permits flexible metal conduit, LFMC or LFNC with listed fittings, or extra-hard-usage cord.","r":"NEC 501.10(B)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":1,"q":"In a Class I, Division 1 location, where an electrical conduit enters an explosionproof enclosure containing a switch that may produce arcs, a conduit seal must be installed within what distance of the enclosure?","o":["450 mm (18 in.)","900 mm (3 ft)","150 mm (6 in.)","1.2 m (4 ft)"],"a":0,"e":"The seal must be within 450 mm (18 in.) of the enclosure, with no union, coupling, box, or fitting between the seal and the enclosure except a listed explosionproof reducer.","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"In a Class I, Division 1 location, a conduit enters an explosionproof junction box containing only splices and terminals. A seal is required at the entry when the conduit trade size is at least:","o":["1 in.","2 in. (metric designator 53)","3/4 in.","1/2 in."],"a":1,"e":"Enclosures with only splices, taps, or terminals require a seal at entries of trade size 2 in. or larger.","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"In a Class I, Division 1 location, an explosionproof enclosure with arcing devices has a 3/4 in. conduit entry. Is a seal required?","o":["Only for Group D","Only if the conduit is longer than 3 m","Yes, regardless of conduit size","No, only 2 in. and larger require seals"],"a":2,"e":"Seals are required at every conduit entry into an enclosure containing arcing or high-temperature devices, regardless of size.","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"The sealing compound in a Class I conduit seal must have a melting point of not less than:","o":["93 degrees C (200 degrees F)","40 degrees C (104 degrees F)","60 degrees C (140 degrees F)","75 degrees C (167 degrees F)"],"a":0,"e":"Sealing compound must not be affected by the atmosphere or liquids and must have a melting point not less than 93 degrees C (200 degrees F).","r":"NEC 501.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"The thickness of the sealing compound in a Class I seal must be not less than the trade size of the sealing fitting and in no case less than:","o":["25 mm (1 in.)","6 mm (1/4 in.)","16 mm (5/8 in.)","10 mm (3/8 in.)"],"a":2,"e":"Minimum compound thickness is the trade size of the fitting and never less than 16 mm (5/8 in.).","r":"NEC 501.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Conductor splices or taps in a fitting intended only for sealing with compound are:","o":["Permitted if taped","Permitted for 120 V only","Permitted for Group D only","Not permitted"],"a":3,"e":"Splices and taps are not permitted in fittings intended only for sealing with compound.","r":"NEC 501.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":3,"q":"A rigid metal conduit run passes from a Class I, Division 1 area into an unclassified area. Where must the seal be placed?","o":["No seal is required","At the boundary, with no coupling, union, or fitting between the seal and the boundary except a listed explosionproof reducer","At the unclassified end only after a junction box","Anywhere within 3 m"],"a":1,"e":"Conduit leaving a Division 1 location must be sealed at the boundary, with the seal on either side.","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(4)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"A threaded rigid metal conduit passes completely through a Class I, Division 2 location with no unions, couplings, boxes or fittings in the Division 2 area, and has no fittings within 300 mm (12 in.) beyond each boundary, ending in unclassified locations. Is a seal required at the boundary?","o":["No, it is exempt","Yes, at both boundaries","Only if over 30 m","Yes, at one end"],"a":0,"e":"The exception exempts unbroken metal conduit passing through a Division 2 location with no fittings less than 300 mm beyond each boundary.","r":"NEC 501.15(B)(2) Exception","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":3,"q":"A 1 in. conduit enters a Class I, Division 2 explosionproof enclosure that contains a circuit breaker interrupting current in normal operation. A seal is:","o":["Required only for Group A","Required","Optional if fewer than 3 conductors","Not required because trade size is under 2 in."],"a":1,"e":"Enclosures required to be explosionproof because of arcing devices require seals at conduit entries.","r":"NEC 501.15(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":1,"q":"In Class I locations, locknut-bushing and double-locknut types of contacts:","o":["Are acceptable for bonding","Are required for bonding","Are permitted only in Division 2","Must not be depended on for bonding"],"a":3,"e":"Bonding must be done by methods such as bonding jumpers with proper fittings; locknut-bushing and double-locknut types are not depended on.","r":"NEC 501.30(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Where liquidtight flexible metal conduit is used in a Class I location as permitted by 501.10(B), the grounding means must include:","o":["No bonding if under 1.8 m","The conduit alone","A locknut only","A wire-type equipment bonding jumper"],"a":3,"e":"Flexible metal conduit and LFMC in Class I locations require an equipment bonding jumper of the wire type in parallel, complying with 250.102.","r":"NEC 501.30(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"In a Class I, Division 2 location, an induction motor without brushes, switching mechanisms, or integral resistance devices may be:","o":["Of the open or nonexplosionproof type","Required to be explosionproof","Prohibited","Allowed only if totally enclosed"],"a":0,"e":"Plain induction motors without sliding contacts, switching or resistance devices are permitted to be general-purpose in Division 2.","r":"NEC 501.125(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"A Class I, Division 2 motor has an integral overload switching device that can arc. What is required?","o":["Any totally enclosed fan-cooled motor","Standard open motor","No special requirement","Identified for Class I, Division 2 unless the device is in an enclosure identified for Division 1"],"a":3,"e":"Motors with sliding contacts, switching mechanisms, or integral resistance devices must be identified for Class I Division 2 unless enclosed to Division 1 standards.","r":"NEC 501.125(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"In a Class I, Division 2 location, a general-purpose enclosure is acceptable for a switch only if the current interruption occurs:","o":["Within a chamber hermetically sealed against gases and vapors, or oil immersed","In a NEMA 1 enclosure","In a lighting circuit under 20 A","In a weatherproof enclosure"],"a":0,"e":"Devices that interrupt current in normal operation need Division 1 enclosures unless hermetically sealed or oil-immersed.","r":"NEC 501.115(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Pendant luminaire stems in a Class I location longer than 300 mm (12 in.) require:","o":["No special support","A chain at the lower end only","Set screws to be omitted","Permanent and effective bracing against lateral displacement within 300 mm of the lower end, or a flexible connection with a listed fitting"],"a":3,"e":"Stems over 300 mm must be braced against lateral displacement or flexibly connected using a listed fitting within 300 mm of the supporting box.","r":"NEC 501.130(A)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":1,"q":"What flexible cord type is required in a Class I, Division 1 location?","o":["Any SPT cord","Lamp cord","Extra-hard usage, listed, with an equipment grounding conductor","Hard-usage cord without ground"],"a":2,"e":"Flexible cords in Class I locations must be listed for extra-hard usage and include a grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 501.140(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Flexible cord terminations in Class I locations must be made with:","o":["Listed fittings and supported to prevent tension on terminals","Any strain relief","Tape","Wire nuts"],"a":0,"e":"Cord must be terminated with listed fittings and supported so there is no tension on the terminal connections.","r":"NEC 501.140(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Receptacles and attachment plugs in Class I locations must:","o":["Be nonlocking only","Have no grounding","Provide connection for the equipment grounding conductor of the flexible cord and be identified for the location","Be ordinary 2-wire devices"],"a":2,"e":"They must provide for grounding and be identified for the Class I location.","r":"NEC 501.145","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":3,"q":"A transformer containing flammable oil is to be installed in a Class I, Division 1 location. It must be installed:","o":["In a vault complying with Article 450 Part III and with specific ventilation provisions","In a general-purpose enclosure","On a pole inside","In an unventilated closet"],"a":0,"e":"Transformers containing liquid that will burn in Division 1 must be in approved vaults meeting Part III of Article 450.","r":"NEC 501.100(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":1,"q":"Article 501 applies to locations where fire or explosion hazards may exist due to:","o":["Easily ignitible fibers","Wet conditions","Flammable gases, flammable liquid-produced vapors, or combustible liquid-produced vapors","Combustible dust"],"a":2,"e":"Article 501 covers Class I Division 1 and 2 locations containing flammable gases or vapors.","r":"NEC 501.1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":3,"q":"A contractor wants to run Type MC cable (not MC-HL) in a Class I, Division 1 chemical plant area. Is this permitted?","o":["Yes, any MC cable","Yes, if under 1.8 m","No, Division 1 requires threaded RMC/IMC, MI, or listed MC-HL type cable","Yes, if painted"],"a":2,"e":"Only MC-HL with its gas/vaportight sheath and jacket is permitted in Division 1.","r":"NEC 501.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":3,"q":"A Class I, Division 2 area needs wiring. A bid proposes EMT with compression fittings. What is the Code result?","o":["Not permitted; use a method such as threaded RMC/IMC or listed MC cable","Permitted","Permitted with sealing","Permitted for lighting only"],"a":0,"e":"EMT is not a recognized fixed wiring method for Class I Division 2.","r":"NEC 501.10(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"The cross-sectional area of the conductors permitted in a Class I conduit seal must not exceed what percentage of the area of a rigid metal conduit of the same trade size, unless the seal is identified for a higher fill?","o":["53 percent","40 percent","25 percent","60 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Conductor fill in a seal is limited to 25 percent of the area of rigid metal conduit of the same trade size unless specifically identified for greater fill.","r":"NEC 501.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":3,"q":"In a Class I, Division 1 area, a 1-1/2 in. conduit enters an explosionproof junction box that contains only splices and terminals (no arcing devices). Does 501.15(A) require a seal at that entry?","o":["Yes, only for Group C","Yes, always","No, because trade size is smaller than 2 in. and there are no arcing devices","Yes, within 900 mm"],"a":2,"e":"For enclosures with only splices, taps, or terminals, a seal is required only at entries of trade size 2 in. (53) or larger.","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":3,"q":"A 2-1/2 in. conduit enters a Class I, Division 1 explosionproof box containing only splices. A seal is:","o":["Required only above 4 in.","Required at the entry","Not required since no arcing devices are present","Required only for hydrogen"],"a":1,"e":"Entries of trade size 2 in. or larger into an enclosure with terminals, splices, or taps need a seal.","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":3,"q":"An installer proposes placing a conduit seal 24 in. from an explosionproof enclosure containing a circuit breaker in a Class I, Division 1 location. What is the issue?","o":["The seal must be within 450 mm (18 in.) of the enclosure","The seal must be at least 36 in. away","24 in. is acceptable","Seals are never needed there"],"a":0,"e":"The required seal must be placed within 450 mm (18 in.) of the enclosure.","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":3,"q":"May a union be installed between a Class I, Division 1 explosionproof enclosure containing arcing devices and its required conduit seal?","o":["No, except a listed explosionproof reducer","Yes, if threaded","Yes, if the union is taped","Yes, any union"],"a":0,"e":"No union, coupling, box, or fitting is allowed between the seal and enclosure except listed explosionproof reducers.","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Which of these is permitted in a Class I, Division 2 location?","o":["Open wiring on insulators","Enclosed gasketed busways","Open busways","Open ladder cable tray with unlisted cable"],"a":1,"e":"Enclosed gasketed busways and wireways are listed Division 2 wiring methods.","r":"NEC 501.10(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Boxes and fittings in a Class I, Division 2 location are required to be explosionproof:","o":["Never","Only above 600 V","Only where arcing devices or other required conditions call for it","Always"],"a":2,"e":"Division 2 boxes and fittings need not be explosionproof except where they enclose arcing devices as required in 501.105(B)(1), 501.115(B)(1), and 501.150(B)(1).","r":"NEC 501.10(B)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Threaded connections of rigid metal conduit to explosionproof fittings in Class I locations require at least how many fully engaged threads?","o":["Three","Eight","Five","Two"],"a":2,"e":"At least five threads must be fully engaged for NPT threaded entries.","r":"NEC 500.8(E)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":1,"q":"Which conduit type is permitted for Class I, Division 1 locations along with threaded rigid metal conduit?","o":["Rigid PVC above grade","Flexible metal conduit with standard fittings","Threaded steel intermediate metal conduit","Electrical metallic tubing"],"a":2,"e":"Threaded steel IMC is permitted along with threaded RMC.","r":"NEC 501.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Type PLTC cable may be used in a Class I, Division 2 location when:","o":["It is used in any manner","It is run unprotected through floors","It is buried bare","It is installed in accordance with 501.10(B) and the cable is listed for the application"],"a":3,"e":"Division 2 permits PLTC and ITC cables under the conditions of 501.10(B).","r":"NEC 501.10(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":3,"q":"A Class I, Division 2 refinery wants a trade-size 3/4 conduit with a flexible section to a motor. Which flexible method is acceptable?","o":["Flexible metal conduit as grounding path with no jumper","Liquidtight flexible metal conduit with listed fittings and a wire-type bonding jumper","Type NM cable","Unlisted hose"],"a":1,"e":"LFMC with listed fittings is permitted in Division 2, with an equipment bonding jumper of the wire type.","r":"NEC 501.10(B)(2), 501.30(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"In a Class I, Division 1 location, switches, circuit breakers and motor controllers that interrupt current must be:","o":["General-purpose NEMA 1","Weatherproof only","Provided with enclosures identified for Class I, Division 1","Dusttight only"],"a":2,"e":"Division 1 switching devices require enclosures identified for the Class I Division 1 location.","r":"NEC 501.115(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Portable lighting equipment used in Class I, Division 1 locations must be:","o":["Identified for Class I, Division 1 and marked for the group","Any flashlight","Any LED lamp","Any extension-cord lamp"],"a":0,"e":"Portable lighting must be listed for Division 1 and marked with its approved class and group.","r":"NEC 501.130(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":1,"q":"A motor installed in a Class I, Division 1 location must be:","o":["A general-purpose open motor","Identified for Class I, Division 1 (or a permitted alternative such as totally enclosed pipe-ventilated)","Any drip-proof motor","Any TEFC motor"],"a":1,"e":"Division 1 motors must be identified for the location or be one of the permitted special types.","r":"NEC 501.125(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Dry-type transformers used in a Class I, Division 1 location must be:","o":["Mounted on any ceiling","In ordinary ventilated cabinets","Open type","In explosionproof enclosures or identified for Division 1"],"a":3,"e":"Transformers without flammable liquid in Division 1 need explosionproof enclosures or identification for Division 1.","r":"NEC 501.100(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"In Class I, Division 2 locations, extra-hard-usage flexible cord may be used for:","o":["Unsupported hanging runs of any length","Connection of portable lighting or utilization equipment to the fixed supply","Permanent wiring in lieu of conduit","Service conductors"],"a":1,"e":"Flexible cord is limited to listed uses such as portable equipment connections and where flexibility is required.","r":"NEC 501.140(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Which is true about bonding for Class I locations?","o":["Locknuts alone are sufficient","Bonding is not required below 120 V","Metal conduit never needs bonding","Bonding must use bonding jumpers with proper fittings, not locknuts or bushings alone"],"a":3,"e":"Locknut-bushing and double-locknut types are not depended on for bonding.","r":"NEC 501.30(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"At the boundary between a Class I, Division 2 location and an unclassified location, conduit not meeting the exception must have a seal located:","o":["Only on the unclassified side with a union","Nowhere","At least 10 ft inside the Division 2 area","On either side of the boundary with no fitting between the seal and the boundary except permitted ones"],"a":3,"e":"Seals are placed on either side of the Division 2 boundary, with nothing but approved fittings between.","r":"NEC 501.15(B)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":3,"q":"An engineer plans a trade-size 1 in. EMT run in a Class I, Division 1 pump room. Which is correct?","o":["EMT is permitted if sealed","EMT is permitted if painted red","EMT is permitted with compression fittings","EMT is not permitted; threaded RMC or IMC is required"],"a":3,"e":"EMT is not a Division 1 wiring method.","r":"NEC 501.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":3,"q":"A Division 1 installation uses Type MI cable. Which statement is correct?","o":["The cable must be terminated with fittings listed for the location","MI cable is prohibited in Division 1","Standard locknut connectors are fine","It requires no fittings"],"a":0,"e":"MI cable is permitted in Division 1 with listed termination fittings.","r":"NEC 501.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Equipment in a Class I, Division 2 location that may produce arcs or sparks in normal operation is required to be:","o":["Any NEMA 3R enclosure","Weatherproof only","Identified for Division 2 or in a Division 1 type enclosure","General-purpose"],"a":2,"e":"Arcing equipment in Division 2 needs identified or explosionproof protection.","r":"NEC 501.115(B), 501.105(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":3,"q":"Lighting luminaires in a Class I, Division 1 pump room are replaced with standard fluorescent fixtures. What is the problem?","o":["None","They need a guard only","They need a larger wire","Fixed luminaires must be identified for Class I, Division 1"],"a":3,"e":"Fixed luminaires in Division 1 must be identified for the location.","r":"NEC 501.130(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":1,"q":"Conduit seals in Class I locations are installed to:","o":["Provide a wire pulling point","Minimize passage of gases or vapors and prevent flames from traveling through the conduit","Support the conduit","Provide drainage"],"a":1,"e":"Seals limit the passage of gases and vapors and prevent propagation of explosions through the raceway system.","r":"NEC 501.15","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"A Type MC-HL cable in Division 1 must also include which feature?","o":["Wire mesh only","An unsealed jacket","A separate equipment grounding conductor","No jacket"],"a":2,"e":"MC-HL for Division 1 must have a gas/vaportight corrugated metallic sheath, a polymeric jacket, and separate grounding conductors per 250.122.","r":"NEC 501.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Sealing fittings used in Class I locations must be:","o":["Any standard conduit body","Unlisted explosionproof-style fittings","Listed for the location and installed with compatible sealing compound","Plastic bodies filled with tape"],"a":2,"e":"Seals must be identified for the Class I location and used with the compound they are listed for.","r":"NEC 501.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"In a Class I location where condensed vapors or liquids may come in contact with conductor insulation, the insulation must be:","o":["Of a type identified for the vapors or liquids, or protected by a lead sheath or other approved means","Standard THHN only","Uninsulated","Covered with tape"],"a":0,"e":"Conductor insulation exposed to condensed vapors or liquids must be suitable or protected.","r":"NEC 501.20","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Which motor is acceptable in a Class I, Division 1 location besides motors identified for Class I, Division 1?","o":["Totally enclosed motors supplied with positive-pressure ventilation from a clean air source with safeguards","Open drip-proof motors","Open motors with filters","Any TEFC motor"],"a":0,"e":"Division 1 allows identified motors and specifically permitted totally enclosed pressurized or pipe-ventilated types.","r":"NEC 501.125(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"In a Class I, Division 2 location, meters, instruments and relays with make-and-break or sliding contacts must be:","o":["In NEMA 1 enclosures","In general-purpose enclosures","In enclosures identified for Division 1, or purged, hermetically sealed, or oil-immersed","Left open"],"a":2,"e":"Arcing meters and relays in Division 2 need Division 1 enclosures or equivalent protection.","r":"NEC 501.105(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":2,"q":"Pendant luminaire stems longer than 300 mm (12 in.) in a Class I, Division 2 location must be:","o":["Left unbraced","Braced against lateral displacement or flexibly connected near the support","Non-metallic","Longer than 1.2 m"],"a":1,"e":"The bracing or flexible connection rule applies in Division 2 as well.","r":"NEC 501.130(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":3,"q":"A Class I, Division 1 pump area has a conduit box with a threaded hub but a gasketed cover that is not explosionproof. Is it acceptable?","o":["Yes if gasketed","No, boxes and fittings in Division 1 must be identified for the location","Yes if painted","Yes"],"a":1,"e":"Division 1 boxes and fittings require explosionproof (identified) construction.","r":"NEC 501.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 501 Class I Locations","d":3,"q":"A listed explosionproof reducer is permitted between a Division 1 enclosure and its required seal. Is a standard EMT coupling permitted there?","o":["Yes","No, only listed explosionproof reducers are allowed","Yes if gasketed","Yes if threaded"],"a":1,"e":"Only listed explosionproof reducers are exempt from the no-fitting rule.","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":1,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted in a Class II, Division 1 location?","o":["Nonmetallic-sheathed cable","Threaded rigid metal conduit","Electrical metallic tubing","Open wiring"],"a":1,"e":"Threaded rigid metal conduit or threaded steel IMC is permitted in Class II Division 1; EMT is permitted only in Division 2.","r":"NEC 502.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":1,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted in Class II, Division 2 but not in Division 1?","o":["Type MI cable with listed fittings","Electrical metallic tubing","Threaded steel IMC","Threaded rigid metal conduit"],"a":1,"e":"EMT is permitted in Class II Division 2 locations, whereas Division 1 requires threaded RMC/IMC or MI cable.","r":"NEC 502.10(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Type MI cable in Class II, Division 1 locations requires:","o":["Standard connectors","No fittings","Termination fittings listed for the location","Wire nuts"],"a":2,"e":"MI cable is permitted in Division 1 when terminated with listed fittings.","r":"NEC 502.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Boxes and fittings containing taps, joints, or terminations in a Class II, Division 1 location must be:","o":["Of non-metallic material only","Provided with threaded bosses for conduit, dusttight, and without openings through which dust can enter or sparks escape","Of any general-purpose type","Open at the bottom for drainage"],"a":1,"e":"Class II Division 1 boxes must have threaded bosses, be dusttight, and have no holes allowing dust entry or spark escape.","r":"NEC 502.10(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Which flexible connection is permitted in Class II, Division 1 locations?","o":["Dusttight flexible connectors, LFMC with listed fittings, or extra-hard-usage cord","Flexible metal conduit with set-screw connectors","Standard rubber hose","Armored cable (AC)"],"a":0,"e":"Division 1 flexibility is provided by dusttight flexible connectors, liquidtight conduits with listed fittings, or extra-hard-usage cord.","r":"NEC 502.10(A)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Wireways in a Class II, Division 2 location must be:","o":["Dusttight","Ladder type without covers","Ventilated","Open"],"a":0,"e":"Dusttight wireways are permitted in Class II Division 2.","r":"NEC 502.10(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":1,"q":"Where a raceway connects a dust-ignitionproof enclosure to an enclosure that is not dust-ignitionproof, a horizontal raceway used as an alternative to a seal must be not less than:","o":["1.5 m (5 ft)","6 m (20 ft)","0.9 m (3 ft)","3.05 m (10 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"A horizontal raceway of at least 3.05 m (10 ft) is one permitted means, along with a permanent seal or a vertical raceway of at least 1.5 m (5 ft) extending downward.","r":"NEC 502.15(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"A vertical raceway extending downward from a dust-ignitionproof enclosure can be used in lieu of a seal if it is not less than:","o":["0.9 m (3 ft)","1.5 m (5 ft)","3.05 m (10 ft)","0.3 m (1 ft)"],"a":1,"e":"The vertical raceway must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft) and extend downward from the dust-ignitionproof enclosure.","r":"NEC 502.15(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":3,"q":"A 1.2 m (4 ft) vertical conduit runs down from a dust-ignitionproof enclosure to a general-purpose enclosure. Is this an acceptable alternative to a seal?","o":["Yes, if the conduit is 1 in.","Yes","Yes, for Group G only","No, it must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft) unless a seal is used"],"a":3,"e":"A vertical raceway must be 1.5 m (5 ft) minimum to substitute for a seal.","r":"NEC 502.15","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":3,"q":"A horizontal 2.4 m (8 ft) conduit connects a dust-ignitionproof enclosure to a non-dust-ignitionproof enclosure. What must be done?","o":["Add a locknut","Use a union","Install a permanent and effective seal or lengthen to at least 3.05 m (10 ft)","Nothing"],"a":2,"e":"The horizontal alternative requires at least 3.05 m (10 ft); otherwise a seal is needed.","r":"NEC 502.15","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Sealing fittings used to keep dust out of a dust-ignitionproof enclosure:","o":["Must be non-metallic","Must be explosionproof","Need not be explosionproof but must minimize dust entry and have no unprotected openings","Must be filled with compound"],"a":2,"e":"Class II sealing fittings need not be explosionproof; they must be identified to minimize dust entrance.","r":"NEC 502.15","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":1,"q":"In Class II locations, bonding using only locknut-bushing or double-locknut contacts is:","o":["Acceptable","Permitted for Division 2 only","Required","Not to be depended on"],"a":3,"e":"Bonding jumpers with proper fittings must be used; locknuts and bushings are not relied upon.","r":"NEC 502.30(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Flexible metal conduit or LFMC used in a Class II location must include:","o":["A metal strap only","A second locknut","Nothing, the conduit is enough","A wire-type equipment bonding jumper"],"a":3,"e":"A wire-type bonding jumper complying with 250.102 is required.","r":"NEC 502.30(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"In a Class II, Division 2 location, fuses, switches, circuit breakers, and motor controllers must be in:","o":["Weatherproof enclosures","Dusttight enclosures (with Group E exceptions requiring more)","Open frames","General-purpose enclosures"],"a":1,"e":"Division 2 enclosures for these devices must be dusttight.","r":"NEC 502.115(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Switches in a Class II, Division 1 location must be provided with:","o":["Open gaskets","No enclosure","NEMA 1","Enclosures identified for Class II locations (dust-ignitionproof)"],"a":3,"e":"Division 1 switching devices need enclosures identified for Class II locations.","r":"NEC 502.115(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":3,"q":"A dry-type transformer rated 480 V is installed in a Class II, Division 2 location. Which is acceptable?","o":["A ventilated dripproof enclosure","An enclosure with louvers","Windings and terminal connections enclosed within tight metal housings without ventilating openings","An open core"],"a":2,"e":"Dry-type Division 2 transformers must have tight metal housings without openings and be rated no more than 600 V.","r":"NEC 502.100(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":3,"q":"A transformer containing a liquid that will burn is required in a Class II, Division 1 location. It must be:","o":["Installed in a vault complying with 450.41 through 450.48 with additional dust restrictions","On the roof","In an ordinary room","In a metal closet"],"a":0,"e":"Such transformers must be in vaults meeting Part III of Article 450.","r":"NEC 502.100(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Receptacles in Class II, Division 2 locations must be designed so that:","o":["They have no ground","Connection to the supply cannot be made or broken while live parts are exposed, and a grounding means is provided","They are weatherproof only","Live parts are exposed"],"a":1,"e":"They must avoid exposed live parts when making or breaking connection and provide an equipment grounding connection.","r":"NEC 502.145(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Flexible cords in Class II locations must be:","o":["Lamp cord","Listed for extra-hard usage, include a grounding conductor, and terminate with dusttight fittings","Any SJ cord","Without a ground"],"a":1,"e":"Cords must be extra-hard usage with a grounding conductor and dusttight fittings.","r":"NEC 502.140","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Fixed luminaires in a Class II, Division 1 location must be:","o":["Any commercial luminaire","Weatherproof only","Incandescent only","Identified for Class II, Division 1 and marked with the maximum wattage"],"a":3,"e":"Division 1 luminaires must be identified for the location and marked with maximum lamp wattage.","r":"NEC 502.130(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Stem-mounted pendant luminaires longer than 300 mm in a Class II location need:","o":["Nothing","A chain only","Two locknuts only","Bracing or flexible connection within 300 mm of the supporting box or lower end"],"a":3,"e":"Stems longer than 300 mm must be braced against lateral displacement or flexibly connected near the box.","r":"NEC 502.130(A)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Which motor is acceptable in a Class II, Division 2 location?","o":["Open weather-protected","Open drip-proof","Totally enclosed nonventilated","Splash-proof"],"a":2,"e":"Division 2 motors must be totally enclosed or dust-ignitionproof types.","r":"NEC 502.125(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":3,"q":"A flour mill (Group G dust suspended in normal operation) plans open drip-proof motors. Result?","o":["Acceptable if under 5 hp","Acceptable","Not acceptable; use motors identified for Class II, Division 1","Acceptable with filters"],"a":2,"e":"Division 1 motors must be identified for the location (or special permitted types).","r":"NEC 502.125(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"The maximum surface temperature of Class II equipment must be less than:","o":["The ignition temperature of the dust","40 degrees C","The room temperature","The flash point of oil"],"a":0,"e":"Equipment temperature must be less than the ignition temperature of the dust involved.","r":"NEC 500.8(D), 502.5","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":3,"q":"A grain-handling area is Class II, Division 2. A contractor proposes EMT with dusttight fittings. Is this acceptable?","o":["No, only MI cable","No, never allowed","No, only RMC","Yes, EMT is permitted in Division 2"],"a":3,"e":"EMT is allowed in Class II Division 2.","r":"NEC 502.10(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":3,"q":"A coal-dust (Group F) area where dust is suspended in normal operation needs wiring. Which is acceptable?","o":["NM cable","EMT","AC cable","Threaded RMC with dusttight, threaded fittings"],"a":3,"e":"Division 1 requires threaded RMC/IMC or other listed methods.","r":"NEC 502.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":1,"q":"Article 502 applies to locations where fire or explosion hazards may exist due to:","o":["Flammable gas","Corrosive atmosphere","Ignitible fibers","Combustible dust"],"a":3,"e":"Article 502 covers Class II locations (combustible dust).","r":"NEC 502.1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Which of these is permitted as a flexible connection in Class II, Division 2?","o":["Open wiring","Nonmetallic-sheathed cable","Dusttight flexible connectors","Armored cable"],"a":2,"e":"Division 2 allows dusttight flexible connectors, listed LFMC/LFNC, or extra-hard-usage cord.","r":"NEC 502.10(B)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Receptacles and attachment plugs in Class II, Division 1 must:","o":["Be ordinary","Provide a connection to the equipment grounding conductor and be identified for Class II locations","Be non-grounding","Be weatherproof"],"a":1,"e":"Receptacles and plugs must be grounded types identified for the location.","r":"NEC 502.145(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"The dusttight requirement in Division 2 means equipment is constructed so that:","o":["It is explosionproof","Dust will not enter the enclosure under specified test conditions","It is gasketed with oil","It is watertight"],"a":1,"e":"Dusttight is defined in Article 100 as keeping dust out under specified test conditions.","r":"NEC Art. 100, 502.10(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":3,"q":"A Division 1 junction box has an unused knockout left open. Is it compliant?","o":["Yes","No, openings through which dust can enter or sparks escape are not permitted","Yes if on top","Yes if small"],"a":1,"e":"Boxes must be free of holes (including knockouts) after installation.","r":"NEC 502.10(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":3,"q":"A contractor wants Type NM cable in a Class II, Division 2 flour warehouse. What is the result?","o":["Permitted","Not permitted","Permitted in conduit-less runs","Permitted with sealing"],"a":1,"e":"NM cable is not a recognized wiring method for Class II locations.","r":"NEC 502.10(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Under Class II rules, when a raceway enters a dust-ignitionproof enclosure from a non-dust-ignitionproof enclosure, protection against dust entry through the raceway is required because:","o":["Dust could travel through the raceway into the enclosure","The conduit may overheat","Water might enter","Gas could burn"],"a":0,"e":"The raceway could otherwise allow dust to enter the dust-ignitionproof enclosure.","r":"NEC 502.15","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":3,"q":"A coal-dust (Group F) Class II, Division 2 area needs a motor starter. The enclosure must be:","o":["Open-type","Drip-proof","Dusttight","Ventilated"],"a":2,"e":"Division 2 motor controllers require dusttight enclosures.","r":"NEC 502.115(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 502 Class II Locations","d":2,"q":"Which of these is NOT a permitted wiring method in Class II, Division 1?","o":["Electrical metallic tubing","Threaded steel IMC","Threaded rigid metal conduit","Type MI cable with listed fittings"],"a":0,"e":"EMT is limited to Division 2 in Class II.","r":"NEC 502.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":1,"q":"Class III locations are hazardous because of the presence of:","o":["Flammable gases","Easily ignitible fibers or flyings that are not likely to be suspended in the air in ignitible quantities","Flammable liquids","Combustible metal dust"],"a":1,"e":"Class III covers easily ignitible fibers and flyings handled, manufactured, or used where they are not likely to be airborne in ignitible quantities.","r":"NEC 503.1, 500.5(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":1,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted in Class III, Division 1 locations?","o":["Electrical metallic tubing","Nonmetallic-sheathed cable","Type SE cable","Open wiring on insulators"],"a":0,"e":"Class III Division 1 permits RMC, IMC, EMT, dusttight wireways, and listed MC or MI cable with approved fittings.","r":"NEC 503.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":1,"q":"Which wiring method is NOT permitted in Class III, Division 1 locations?","o":["Dusttight wireways","Nonmetallic-sheathed cable","Intermediate metal conduit","Rigid metal conduit"],"a":1,"e":"NM cable is not a recognized Class III wiring method.","r":"NEC 503.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":2,"q":"Boxes and fittings in Class III, Division 1 locations must be:","o":["Dusttight with no openings through which sparks or burning material might escape","Explosionproof","Watertight only","Vented"],"a":0,"e":"Boxes and fittings must be dusttight, with covers preventing escape of sparks or burning material and no unused openings.","r":"NEC 503.10(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":2,"q":"Which flexible connection is permitted in Class III, Division 1?","o":["Dusttight flexible connectors","Armored cable","Open wire","NM cable"],"a":0,"e":"Dusttight flexible connectors, LFMC/LFNC with listed fittings, or extra-hard-usage flexible cord with grounding conductor are permitted.","r":"NEC 503.10(A)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":2,"q":"Wiring methods permitted in Class III, Division 2 locations are generally:","o":["The same as in Class III, Division 1","Only MI cable","Any method in Chapter 3","Only explosionproof"],"a":0,"e":"Division 2 wiring follows the Division 1 methods in 503.10(A).","r":"NEC 503.10(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":2,"q":"The maximum surface temperature for Class III equipment that is NOT subject to overloading is:","o":["135 degrees C","165 degrees C (329 degrees F)","200 degrees C","85 degrees C"],"a":1,"e":"To avoid dehydration or carbonization of deposits, surface temperature must not exceed 165 degrees C for non-overloaded equipment.","r":"NEC 503.5","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":2,"q":"The maximum surface temperature for Class III equipment that may be overloaded, such as motors or power transformers, is:","o":["85 degrees C","200 degrees C","120 degrees C (248 degrees F)","165 degrees C"],"a":2,"e":"Overloadable equipment is limited to 120 degrees C (248 degrees F).","r":"NEC 503.5","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":1,"q":"In Class III locations, locknut-bushing and double-locknut types for bonding are:","o":["Not to be depended on","Required","Acceptable","Permitted in Division 2 only"],"a":0,"e":"Bonding must use proper fittings or jumpers, not locknuts alone.","r":"NEC 503.30(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":2,"q":"Where flexible metal conduit or LFMC is used in Class III as permitted by 503.10, it must include:","o":["Nothing additional","A metal strap","A locknut","A wire-type equipment bonding jumper"],"a":3,"e":"A wire-type bonding jumper in accordance with 250.102 is required.","r":"NEC 503.30(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":2,"q":"Fuses, switches, circuit breakers, and motor controllers in Class III locations must be provided with:","o":["NEMA 1 only","Open frames","Explosionproof enclosures","Dusttight enclosures"],"a":3,"e":"They require dusttight enclosures.","r":"NEC 503.115","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":2,"q":"Dry-type transformers in Class III locations must be installed in:","o":["Louvered cabinets","Ventilated enclosures","Open racks","Tight metal housings without ventilating openings, or in vaults"],"a":3,"e":"Transformers without flammable liquid must be in vaults or tight metal housings without ventilating openings.","r":"NEC 503.100","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":2,"q":"Luminaires in Class III locations must:","o":["Be open type","Provide enclosures for lamps and lampholders that minimize entrance of fibers and prevent escape of sparks or hot metal, and be marked with maximum wattage","Be explosionproof","Use exposed lampholders"],"a":1,"e":"Luminaires must be enclosed to keep fibers away from lamps and to prevent escape of sparks or hot material.","r":"NEC 503.130","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":2,"q":"A luminaire in a Class III location that is exposed to physical damage must be:","o":["Left unguarded","Protected by a suitable guard","Weatherproof","Painted"],"a":1,"e":"Luminaires subject to physical damage must be guarded.","r":"NEC 503.130","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":2,"q":"Flexible cord in Class III locations must be:","o":["Without ground","SPT cord","Listed for extra-hard usage and include a grounding conductor","Lamp cord"],"a":2,"e":"Cords must be extra-hard-usage types with a conductor for grounding.","r":"NEC 503.140","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":2,"q":"Receptacles and attachment plugs in Class III locations must be:","o":["Any type","Open type","Non-grounding","Of the grounding type, designed so connection cannot be made or broken while live parts are exposed"],"a":3,"e":"Grounding-type devices designed to prevent exposed live parts are required.","r":"NEC 503.145","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":3,"q":"The power supply to contact conductors of electric cranes and hoists in a Class III location must be:","o":["Unprotected","Grounded solidly","Supplied from a grounded system without detection","Electrically isolated from all other systems and ungrounded, with a ground detector"],"a":3,"e":"Crane contact conductors must be ungrounded, isolated, and equipped with ground detectors providing an alarm or automatic de-energization.","r":"NEC 503.155(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":2,"q":"Current collectors for cranes and hoists in Class III locations must be:","o":["Of any type","Arranged to confine normal sparking and prevent escape of sparks, or enclosed","Unconfined","Open"],"a":1,"e":"Collectors must confine normal sparking.","r":"NEC 503.155(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":2,"q":"Storage-battery charging equipment in a Class III location must be located in:","o":["A closet with fibers","Any location","The same open area","A separate room of substantially noncombustible construction that is well ventilated and prevents entrance of fibers"],"a":3,"e":"Battery charging equipment must be in a separate, well-ventilated room constructed to exclude fibers or flyings.","r":"NEC 503.160","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":3,"q":"A textile mill area is classified Class III, Division 1. An electrician proposes Type MC cable with listed terminations. Is it acceptable?","o":["Yes, listed MC cable with termination fittings is a permitted wiring method","No, only EMT","No, only RMC","No, only MI"],"a":0,"e":"Type MC cable is a permitted Class III wiring method with listed fittings.","r":"NEC 503.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":3,"q":"A cotton baling room is Class III. A box contains a 1/2 in. unused knockout open to the room. Is this acceptable?","o":["Yes","Yes, if at the bottom","No, boxes must not have openings through which sparks or burning material might escape","Yes, if small"],"a":2,"e":"Unused openings must be closed.","r":"NEC 503.10(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":3,"q":"A squirrel-cage motor in a Class III, Division 1 woodworking-fibers plant should be selected as:","o":["Open drip-proof","Totally enclosed type (nonventilated, pipe-ventilated, or fan-cooled)","Splash-proof","Open ventilated"],"a":1,"e":"Class III Division 1 motors must be totally enclosed types to exclude fibers.","r":"NEC 503.125","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":3,"q":"A contractor proposes open wiring on insulators in a Class III, Division 1 facility. The result is:","o":["Permitted","Not permitted; use a Chapter 3 method recognized by 503.10","Permitted if cleaned","Permitted if in plastic"],"a":1,"e":"Open wiring is not a Division 1 wiring method.","r":"NEC 503.10(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":2,"q":"Equipment grounding for Class III equipment connected with extra-hard-usage cord requires:","o":["A grounding conductor in the cord","No ground","A neutral only","A water pipe"],"a":0,"e":"Cord must contain a grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 503.140, 503.30","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 503 Class III Locations","d":1,"q":"The temperature limits for Class III equipment are intended to prevent:","o":["Corrosion","Dehydration or carbonization of fibre deposits that could spontaneously ignite","Condensation","Overvoltage"],"a":1,"e":"Carbonized deposits are susceptible to spontaneous ignition.","r":"NEC 503.5","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":1,"q":"A Class I, Zone 0 location is one in which ignitible concentrations of flammable gases or vapors are:","o":["Never present","Not likely in normal operation","Present continuously or for long periods of time","Likely under normal operation only"],"a":2,"e":"Zone 0 means ignitible concentrations are present continuously or for long periods.","r":"NEC 505.5(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":1,"q":"A Class I, Zone 1 location is one in which ignitible concentrations are likely to exist:","o":["Under normal operating conditions","Only in rupture","Never","Continuously"],"a":0,"e":"Zone 1 includes locations where ignitible concentrations are likely under normal operation, or frequently due to repair, maintenance, or leakage.","r":"NEC 505.5(B)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":2,"q":"A Class I, Zone 2 location is one in which ignitible concentrations are:","o":["Present for long periods","Likely under normal operation","Continuously present","Not likely to occur in normal operation and, if they occur, exist only for a short period"],"a":3,"e":"Zone 2 is where ignitible concentrations are not likely under normal operation and would exist only briefly.","r":"NEC 505.5(B)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":1,"q":"Acetylene and hydrogen are in which Class I, Zone gas group?","o":["Group IIB","Group I","Group IIA","Group IIC"],"a":3,"e":"IIC atmospheres contain acetylene, hydrogen, or gases of equivalent hazard.","r":"NEC 505.6(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":1,"q":"Ethylene is typically classified in which zone gas group?","o":["Group IIB","Group IIC","Group IIA","Group I"],"a":0,"e":"Ethylene is a Group IIB atmosphere.","r":"NEC 505.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":1,"q":"Propane and gasoline vapors are in which zone gas group?","o":["Group IIB","Group III","Group IIC","Group IIA"],"a":3,"e":"Propane, gasoline, butane, and similar gases are Group IIA.","r":"NEC 505.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":2,"q":"Equipment marked for Group IIC may also be used in which atmospheres?","o":["IIB and IIA","Only IIC","None","Dust atmospheres"],"a":0,"e":"IIC is the most severe group, so IIC equipment is suitable for IIB and IIA atmospheres.","r":"NEC 505.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":3,"q":"A hydrogen process room (Zone 1) plans to install equipment marked Group IIA. What is the result?","o":["Acceptable","Acceptable with a seal","Not acceptable; equipment must be marked IIC","Acceptable if T6"],"a":2,"e":"Hydrogen is Group IIC; IIA equipment is suitable only for IIA atmospheres.","r":"NEC 505.6, 505.9","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":1,"q":"In the equipment marking 'Class I, Zone 1, AEx d IIB T4', what does 'AEx' indicate?","o":["Alternating current explosionproof","Atmosphere exclusion","Equipment built to US standards for the zone system","Acetylene exclusion"],"a":2,"e":"AEx identifies equipment meeting US zone-system standards.","r":"NEC 505.9(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":2,"q":"In the marking 'Class I, Zone 1, AEx d IIB T4', the letter d stands for:","o":["Dust protection","Flameproof enclosure protection","Double insulation","Detection"],"a":1,"e":"d is flameproof protection, which withstands an internal explosion and prevents flame propagation.","r":"NEC 505.8(A), 505.9(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":2,"q":"In the same marking, T4 means a maximum surface temperature of:","o":["200 degrees C","135 degrees C","450 degrees C","85 degrees C"],"a":1,"e":"T4 equals 135 degrees C.","r":"NEC 505.9(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":2,"q":"The protection technique designated 'p' is:","o":["Primary","Piezo","Purged and pressurized","Powder-filled"],"a":2,"e":"p stands for purged/pressurized enclosures that exclude the flammable atmosphere with protective gas.","r":"NEC 505.8(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":2,"q":"The protection technique designated 'e' is:","o":["Increased safety","Electrostatic","Encapsulation","Explosionproof"],"a":0,"e":"e is increased safety, which reduces the likelihood of arcs, sparks, or hot surfaces.","r":"NEC 505.8(F)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":2,"q":"Which protection technique is acceptable for Class I, Zone 0?","o":["Increased safety 'e'","Intrinsic safety 'ia'","Pressurization 'p'","Flameproof 'd'"],"a":1,"e":"Only intrinsically safe 'ia' (and other types listed for Zone 0) is acceptable for Zone 0.","r":"NEC 505.20(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":2,"q":"Protection technique 'nA' is generally acceptable in which zone?","o":["Zone 1","All zones","Zone 2 only","Zone 0"],"a":2,"e":"Type nA (nonsparking) is for Zone 2.","r":"NEC 505.20(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":3,"q":"Equipment marked for Zone 0 is installed in a Zone 2 location. Is this acceptable?","o":["Yes, Zone 0 equipment is suitable for Zones 1 and 2","Only with a seal","Only in IIA","No"],"a":0,"e":"Equipment suitable for a more hazardous zone may be used in less hazardous zones when group and temperature match.","r":"NEC 505.20","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":3,"q":"A contractor wants to install Zone 2 equipment marked only for Zone 2 in a Zone 1 location. The result is:","o":["Not acceptable","Acceptable if gasketed","Acceptable if T6","Acceptable"],"a":0,"e":"Zone 2 equipment is not suitable for Zone 1.","r":"NEC 505.20(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":2,"q":"A conduit run leaving a Class I, Zone 0 location requires:","o":["No seal","A seal located on either side of the boundary","An expansion fitting","A union at the boundary"],"a":1,"e":"A seal must be provided in each conduit run leaving a Zone 0 location.","r":"NEC 505.16(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":2,"q":"In a Zone 1 location, a conduit seal for an entry into a flameproof enclosure containing arcing devices must be located within:","o":["6 m","3 m","450 mm (18 in.) of the enclosure","1.5 m"],"a":2,"e":"Seals are placed within 450 mm (18 in.) of the enclosure.","r":"NEC 505.16(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":1,"q":"Locations classified as zone-system hazardous locations must be:","o":["Documented only for Zone 0","Properly documented and available to those authorized to design, install, inspect, or maintain","Noted by signs only","Left undocumented"],"a":1,"e":"Classified areas must be documented.","r":"NEC 505.4(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":2,"q":"In Class I, Zone 0, 1 and 2 locations, bonding by locknut-bushing or double locknut alone is:","o":["Acceptable in Zone 2","Required","Not to be depended on","Sufficient"],"a":2,"e":"Bonding jumpers with proper fittings are required.","r":"NEC 505.30","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":2,"q":"Intrinsically safe 'ib' equipment is acceptable in:","o":["Zone 1 (and Zone 2) but not Zone 0","Zone 0","Only dust zones","Zone 0 and 1"],"a":0,"e":"ib is for Zone 1 and 2; ia is needed for Zone 0.","r":"NEC 505.20","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":2,"q":"Group I equipment in the zone system is intended for:","o":["Gasoline","Mining applications (methane)","Hydrogen","Grain dust"],"a":1,"e":"Group I is mining; surface industry equipment is Group II (IIA, IIB, IIC).","r":"NEC 505.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":3,"q":"A gas has an autoignition temperature of 150 degrees C. Which temperature class is NOT acceptable?","o":["T4 (135 degrees C)","T3 (200 degrees C)","T6 (85 degrees C)","T5 (100 degrees C)"],"a":1,"e":"Surface temperature must remain below the gas ignition temperature, so T3 at 200 degrees C is not acceptable.","r":"NEC 505.9(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":3,"q":"The headspace above flammable liquid in a closed vented tank continuously contains ignitible vapor. Which zone applies?","o":["Unclassified","Zone 0","Zone 1","Zone 2"],"a":1,"e":"Continuous presence defines Zone 0.","r":"NEC 505.5(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":3,"q":"Equipment marked 'Class I, Zone 0, AEx ia IIC T6' has a maximum surface temperature of:","o":["135 degrees C","85 degrees C","200 degrees C","100 degrees C"],"a":1,"e":"T6 equals 85 degrees C.","r":"NEC 505.9(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":2,"q":"An area adjacent to a Zone 0 location into which ignitible concentrations might be communicated is generally classified as:","o":["Unclassified","Zone 1, unless prevented by positive-pressure ventilation","Zone 2 always","Zone 20"],"a":1,"e":"Zone 1 includes locations adjacent to Zone 0 where ignitible concentrations might be communicated.","r":"NEC 505.5(B)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 505 Class I Zone 0, 1 and 2 Locations","d":2,"q":"In the marking 'AEx ia IIC T6', the letters 'ia' indicate:","o":["Increased air flow","Insulated armor","Intrinsic safety suitable for Zone 0","Impact absorbing"],"a":2,"e":"ia is intrinsic safety at the highest level, usable in Zone 0.","r":"NEC 505.8(C), 505.9(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":1,"q":"A Zone 20 location is one in which ignitible concentrations of combustible dust or fibers are present:","o":["Continuously or for long periods of time","Occasionally in normal operation","Only for short periods","Never"],"a":0,"e":"Zone 20 means continuous presence or presence for long periods.","r":"NEC 506.5(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":1,"q":"A Zone 21 location is one in which ignitible concentrations of combustible dust are:","o":["Continuously present","Only present during fires","Likely to exist occasionally under normal operating conditions","Never present"],"a":2,"e":"Zone 21 includes locations where ignitible dust is likely to exist occasionally during normal operation.","r":"NEC 506.5(B)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":2,"q":"A Zone 22 location is one in which ignitible concentrations of combustible dust are:","o":["Not likely in normal operation and, if present, exist only for a short period","Occasional in normal operation","Continuously present","Always present in layers"],"a":0,"e":"Zone 22 is where concentrations are not likely in normal operation or, if they occur, persist only briefly (or dust is confined in closed containers).","r":"NEC 506.5(B)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":1,"q":"Group IIIA in the dust zone system refers to:","o":["Flammable gas","Combustible flyings","Nonconductive dust","Conductive dust"],"a":1,"e":"IIIA is combustible flyings, IIIB nonconductive dust, IIIC conductive dust.","r":"NEC 506.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":1,"q":"Group IIIB in the dust zone system refers to:","o":["Conductive dust","Combustible flyings","Hydrogen","Nonconductive dust"],"a":3,"e":"IIIB is combustible nonconductive dust.","r":"NEC 506.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":1,"q":"Group IIIC in the dust zone system refers to:","o":["Conductive dust","Combustible flyings","Acetylene","Nonconductive dust"],"a":0,"e":"IIIC is combustible conductive dust.","r":"NEC 506.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":2,"q":"Aluminum powder is a conductive metal dust. It is classified in which group?","o":["Group IIIA","Group IIC","Group IIIC","Group IIIB"],"a":2,"e":"Conductive dusts such as metal powders are Group IIIC.","r":"NEC 506.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":2,"q":"Cotton lint (flyings) is classified in which dust zone group?","o":["Group IIIB","Group IIA","Group IIIC","Group IIIA"],"a":3,"e":"Combustible flyings are Group IIIA.","r":"NEC 506.6","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":3,"q":"A facility handling conductive carbon dust plans to install enclosures marked Group IIIB. What is the result?","o":["Acceptable with a seal","Not acceptable; IIIC equipment is required for conductive dust","Acceptable for Zone 22 only","Acceptable"],"a":1,"e":"Conductive dust requires Group IIIC equipment.","r":"NEC 506.6, 506.9","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":2,"q":"In the equipment marking, the protection level 'Da' is associated with:","o":["Zone 21","Zone 0 gas","Zone 20","Zone 22"],"a":2,"e":"Da is used for Zone 20, Db for Zone 21, and Dc for Zone 22.","r":"NEC 506.9(C)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":2,"q":"The dust protection technique designated 'tb' is intended for:","o":["Zone 22 only","Gas locations","Zone 20","Zone 21"],"a":3,"e":"ta is for Zone 20, tb for Zone 21, tc for Zone 22.","r":"NEC 506.8, 506.9","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":2,"q":"The dust protection technique designated 'tc' is intended for:","o":["Zone 22","Zone 0","Zone 20","Zone 21"],"a":0,"e":"tc applies to Zone 22.","r":"NEC 506.8","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":2,"q":"The dust protection technique designated 'ta' is intended for:","o":["Zone 20","Zone 22","Zone 21","Zone 2 gas"],"a":0,"e":"ta provides protection by enclosure for Zone 20.","r":"NEC 506.8","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":1,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted in Zone 20 locations?","o":["Open wiring","Nonmetallic-sheathed cable","Type AC cable","Threaded rigid metal conduit"],"a":3,"e":"Threaded RMC or threaded steel IMC is permitted in Zone 20.","r":"NEC 506.15(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":2,"q":"Boxes and fittings in Zone 20 containing splices or terminations must be:","o":["Dusttight with threaded bosses","General-purpose","Ventilated","Open type"],"a":0,"e":"They must be dusttight and provided with threaded bosses for connection.","r":"NEC 506.15(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":2,"q":"Flexible connections in Zone 20 are permitted using:","o":["Rope","Type NM cable","Flexible metal conduit with set screws","Dusttight flexible connectors, listed LFMC/LFNC, or extra-hard-usage cord with fittings"],"a":3,"e":"Dusttight fittings and listed liquidtight conduit or extra-hard cord are permitted.","r":"NEC 506.15(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":2,"q":"A raceway runs from a dust-ignitionproof enclosure in a Zone 21 area to a non-dust-ignitionproof enclosure. A horizontal raceway used in lieu of a seal must be not less than:","o":["1.5 m (5 ft)","0.9 m (3 ft)","6 m (20 ft)","3.05 m (10 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"The horizontal alternative requires at least 3.05 m (10 ft).","r":"NEC 506.16","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":2,"q":"A vertical raceway extending downward from a dust-ignitionproof enclosure may substitute for a seal if it is not less than:","o":["3.05 m (10 ft)","0.3 m (1 ft)","6 m (20 ft)","1.5 m (5 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"The vertical raceway must be at least 1.5 m (5 ft).","r":"NEC 506.16","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":1,"q":"For zone-system dust locations, bonding by locknut-bushing or double-locknut contacts is:","o":["Required","Not to be depended on","Only for Zone 22","Sufficient"],"a":1,"e":"Proper bonding jumpers and fittings are required.","r":"NEC 506.30","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":2,"q":"Where LFMC is used as permitted in Zone 21, the bonding must be provided by:","o":["Tape","The conduit alone","Locknuts","A wire-type equipment bonding jumper"],"a":3,"e":"Wire-type bonding jumpers per 250.102 are required.","r":"NEC 506.30","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":3,"q":"The inside of a dust collector where dust cloud is continuously present is classified as:","o":["Unclassified","Zone 22","Zone 20","Zone 21"],"a":2,"e":"Continuous presence is Zone 20.","r":"NEC 506.5(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":1,"q":"Locations classified under Article 506 must be:","o":["Left undocumented","Reported to NFPA","Documented only at the entrance","Properly documented and available to authorized persons"],"a":3,"e":"Documentation of classified areas is required.","r":"NEC 506.4(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":3,"q":"A contractor proposes Type NM cable in a Zone 22 flour mill area. The result is:","o":["Permitted with a seal","Permitted if under 6 ft","Not permitted","Permitted"],"a":2,"e":"NM cable is not an Article 506 wiring method.","r":"NEC 506.15","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 506 Zone 20, 21 and 22 Locations","d":2,"q":"A location adjacent to Zone 20 into which ignitible dust concentrations might be communicated is generally classified as:","o":["Zone 22 always","Unclassified","Zone 0","Zone 21 (unless prevented)"],"a":3,"e":"Zone 21 includes locations adjacent to Zone 20 where ignitible concentrations might be communicated.","r":"NEC 506.5(B)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":1,"q":"A multi-level garage is used only for parking and storage of vehicles, and no repair work is done in it. How does the NEC treat the area with regard to Class I hazardous (classified) locations?","o":["The entire floor area up to 18 in. above the floor is Class I, Division 2","Classification is not required; the garage is permitted to be unclassified","The area within 5 ft of each vehicle fuel tank is Class I, Division 2","The entire floor area up to 18 in. above the floor is Class I, Division 1"],"a":1,"e":"Parking garages used for parking or storage, with no repair work, are permitted to be unclassified. Classification applies to repair garage areas where volatile flammable liquids or gases are handled.","r":"NEC 511.3","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"In a repair garage that has no mechanical ventilation, what is the extent of the Class I, Division 2 (or Zone 2) location above the floor, for the floor area that has no pits or depressions?","o":["Up to 6 in. above the floor","Up to 3 ft above the floor","Up to 5 ft above the floor","Up to 18 in. above the floor"],"a":3,"e":"Where there is no ventilation, the entire floor area up to a level of 18 in. above the floor is classified Class I, Division 2 or Zone 2.","r":"NEC 511.3","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"A stock room and restroom adjoin a classified service area of a repair garage. Flammable vapors are not likely to be released in them. Which condition allows the adjacent room to be unclassified?","o":["It has a gasketed door that is kept open during business hours","It is mechanically ventilated at four or more air changes per hour","It is located at least 3 ft from the nearest vehicle","It is mechanically ventilated at one air change per hour"],"a":1,"e":"Adjacent areas where vapors are not likely to be released are unclassified if mechanically ventilated at four or more air changes per hour, designed with positive air pressure, or effectively cut off by walls or partitions.","r":"NEC 511.3","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"In a repair garage, fixed equipment such as lampholders, switches, and motors that may produce arcs, sparks, or hot metal particles is installed in the space above a Class I location. What is required if the equipment is less than 12 ft above the floor?","o":["It must be protected by a GFCI device","It must be totally enclosed or constructed to prevent escape of sparks or hot metal particles","It must be installed in a Class I, Division 1 rated enclosure with a seal","No special construction is required if it is more than 18 in. above the floor"],"a":1,"e":"Arc-producing fixed equipment less than 3.7 m (12 ft) above the floor level must be of the totally enclosed type or constructed to prevent escape of sparks or hot metal particles.","r":"NEC 511.7(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"Which feature is required for a portable hand lamp used in a commercial repair garage?","o":["A metal-shell, paper-lined lampholder","A lampholder with a ground-fault indicator light only","A switched lampholder with a convenience receptacle on the handle","An unswitched lampholder with a handle, hook, and substantial guard"],"a":3,"e":"Portable lamps must have a handle, lampholder, hook and substantial guard, an unswitched lampholder with no provision for receptacles, and exterior surfaces of nonconducting material or insulated. Metal-shell, paper-lined lampholders are not allowed.","r":"NEC 511.7(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":1,"q":"Where may battery chargers and their control equipment be located in a commercial repair garage?","o":["Only in the Class I location nearest the vehicle being charged","Within a Class I location if a seal is installed in the supply conduit","Anywhere inside the Class I, Division 2 area if mounted 18 in. above the floor","They shall not be located within the Class I locations"],"a":3,"e":"Battery chargers and their control equipment must not be located within the Class I locations of a garage.","r":"NEC 511.10(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"In a commercial repair garage, which receptacles require GFCI protection for personnel?","o":["Only receptacles in bathrooms and outdoors","Only 250-volt receptacles","Only receptacles within 6 ft of a sink","All 125-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles in areas where electrical diagnostic equipment, hand tools, or portable lighting will be used"],"a":3,"e":"GFCI protection is required for those 125-volt, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles in areas where diagnostic equipment, electrical hand tools, or portable lighting equipment are used.","r":"NEC 511.12","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"A circuit in a repair garage supplies a pendant or portable electrical equipment through a flexible cord. How must equipment grounding be provided?","o":["Grounding is not required for equipment rated 120 volts","The grounded conductor may serve as the equipment grounding means","The metal raceway ahead of the cord is sufficient","The cord must contain a separate equipment grounding conductor"],"a":3,"e":"Where a circuit supplies portable equipment or a pendant, the flexible cord must include a separate equipment grounding conductor. Non-current-carrying metal parts are grounded regardless of voltage.","r":"NEC 511.16","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"Underground wiring in a repair garage Class I location is run in PVC conduit. What minimum cover is required, and what is required where the conduit emerges from the ground?","o":["18 in. cover; PVC all the way to the equipment","24 in. cover; threaded rigid metal conduit or threaded steel IMC for the last 2 ft before emerging","12 in. cover; EMT at the point of emergence","24 in. cover; no change of wiring method is needed"],"a":1,"e":"Underground wiring of nonmetallic conduit must be buried under not less than 600 mm (2 ft) of cover, and threaded RMC or threaded steel IMC must be used for the last 2 ft of the run to the equipment or point of emergence.","r":"NEC 511.8","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":1,"q":"Which describes a Class I, Division 1 location?","o":["Combustible dust is present but not in quantities likely to interfere with equipment","Ignitable fibers are stored but not manufactured","Flammable liquids are handled only in closed containers that can leak only by accidental rupture","Ignitable concentrations of flammable gases or vapors can exist under normal operating conditions"],"a":3,"e":"Division 1 includes locations where ignitable concentrations can exist under normal operating conditions, frequently due to repair, maintenance, or leakage, or where a breakdown could release them and also cause electrical failure.","r":"NEC 500.5(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":1,"q":"A location where volatile flammable liquids are handled only in closed containers or closed systems, from which they can escape only through accidental rupture or abnormal operation, is classified as:","o":["Class I, Division 1","Class I, Division 2","Class II, Division 2","Unclassified"],"a":1,"e":"Locations where flammable liquids or gases are confined in closed systems and escape only by accident or abnormal operation are Class I, Division 2.","r":"NEC 500.5(B)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted in a Class I, Division 1 location of a repair garage?","o":["Electrical metallic tubing with compression fittings","Intermediate metal conduit with set-screw couplings","Schedule 40 PVC conduit above grade","Threaded rigid metal conduit"],"a":3,"e":"Class I, Division 1 allows threaded rigid metal conduit, threaded steel IMC, Type MI cable with listed fittings, and Type MC-HL cable with listed fittings. EMT and PVC are not permitted there.","r":"NEC 501.10(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"In a Class I, Division 1 location, a conduit enters an enclosure housing a switch that may produce arcs or sparks during normal operation. Where must the seal be located?","o":["Within 10 ft of the enclosure","Within 18 in. of the enclosure","Within 5 ft of the enclosure","At the first coupling past the enclosure, regardless of distance"],"a":1,"e":"A seal is required in each conduit run entering an enclosure for arcing or sparking devices, and it must be within 450 mm (18 in.) of the enclosure.","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum thickness of sealing compound in a conduit seal fitting?","o":["Not less than 1/4 in.","Not less than 1 in. regardless of trade size","Not less than half the trade size, but never more than 1/2 in.","Not less than 5/8 in., and not less than the trade size of the fitting"],"a":3,"e":"The thickness of the compound must not be less than the trade size of the sealing fitting and in no case less than 16 mm (5/8 in.).","r":"NEC 501.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":3,"q":"Conductors are installed in a 1-in. rigid metal conduit that passes through a seal fitting in a Class I location. Assuming the seal is not identified for a higher percentage, what is the maximum permitted cross-sectional area of conductors in the seal?","o":["60 percent of the cross-sectional area of the seal fitting body","25 percent of the cross-sectional area of a rigid metal conduit of the same trade size","40 percent of the cross-sectional area of the conduit","53 percent of the cross-sectional area of the conduit"],"a":1,"e":"The conductor cross-sectional area in a seal must not exceed 25 percent of the area of rigid metal conduit of the same trade size unless the seal is identified for a higher percentage.","r":"NEC 501.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"Which statement about splices and taps in conduit seal fittings is correct?","o":["Taps are allowed if the conductors are 10 AWG or smaller","Splices are allowed if the compound covers them fully","Splices and taps are not permitted in fittings intended only for sealing compound","Splices are allowed in a seal only in Class I, Division 2 locations"],"a":2,"e":"Splices and taps must not be made in fittings intended only for sealing with compound; a separate listed enclosure is required.","r":"NEC 501.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":3,"q":"A 3-in. rigid metal conduit enters, from a Class I, Division 1 location, an enclosure that contains only splices and terminals and no arcing devices. What is required?","o":["No seal is required because there are no arcing devices","A drain fitting only","A seal within 18 in. of the enclosure, because the conduit is 2 in. trade size or larger","A seal at least 10 ft from the enclosure"],"a":2,"e":"Conduit of 2 in. trade size or larger entering an enclosure with terminals, splices, or taps in Division 1 needs a seal within 18 in. of the enclosure.","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":1,"q":"A listed explosionproof enclosure is marked with Class, Division, and Group. What additional marking is required on equipment in Class I locations?","o":["Maximum operating temperature or temperature class","The name of the electrical contractor","The short-circuit current rating of the grounded conductor","The date of installation"],"a":0,"e":"Equipment marking must include the class, division or zone, group, and the operating temperature or temperature range (T-code) based on a 40 degree C ambient.","r":"NEC 500.8(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum surface temperature for equipment marked with temperature class T3?","o":["135 degrees C","200 degrees C","100 degrees C","300 degrees C"],"a":1,"e":"T1 is 450 C, T2 300 C, T3 200 C, T4 135 C, T5 100 C, T6 85 C.","r":"NEC 500.8(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"When making up a threaded conduit joint into explosionproof equipment in a Class I location, what minimum thread engagement is required?","o":["Eight threads fully engaged","Five threads fully engaged","Three threads fully engaged","Four threads fully engaged"],"a":1,"e":"Threaded joints in these locations must be made up with at least five threads fully engaged.","r":"NEC 500.8(E)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"For a rigid metal conduit run in a Class I location, how is bonding of the raceway and fittings accomplished?","o":["Double locknuts are acceptable for bonding","Bonding is not required for metal raceways in Division 2","Locknut and bushing types may be relied on","Bonding jumpers with proper fittings or other approved means must be used; locknut-bushing and double-locknut contacts cannot be relied on"],"a":3,"e":"Locknut-bushing and double-locknut contacts must not be depended on for bonding; bonding jumpers or other approved means are required for all intervening raceways, fittings, boxes, and enclosures.","r":"NEC 501.30(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"What type of flexible cord is required for portable equipment used in a Class I, Division 1 location?","o":["Cord listed for extra-hard usage with an equipment grounding conductor","Any hard-usage cord without a grounding conductor","Type NM cable","Lamp cord with a polarized plug"],"a":0,"e":"Flexible cords in Class I, Division 1 must be listed for extra-hard usage, contain an equipment grounding conductor, and be terminated with listed fittings.","r":"NEC 501.140(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"Which describes a Class I, Zone 1 location?","o":["Ignitable concentrations are present continuously or for long periods","Combustible dust is present continuously","Ignitable concentrations are not likely to occur in normal operation and, if they do, exist only for a short time","Ignitable concentrations are likely to exist under normal operating conditions"],"a":3,"e":"Zone 0 is continuous or long periods, Zone 1 is likely under normal operation, and Zone 2 is not likely and short-lived if it occurs.","r":"NEC 505.5(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":1,"q":"Which Class I zone corresponds to ignitable concentrations that are present continuously or for long periods?","o":["Zone 0","Zone 1","Zone 20","Zone 2"],"a":0,"e":"Zone 0 is for continuous or long-period presence of ignitable gas or vapor; Zone 20 is the dust equivalent under Article 506.","r":"NEC 505.5(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"An enclosure marked explosionproof for Class I locations is best described as one that:","o":["Keeps equipment surface temperature below 40 degrees C","Is gasketed to keep all vapors out","Is made only from cast aluminum","Withstands an internal explosion of the specified gas and prevents ignition of the surrounding atmosphere"],"a":3,"e":"Explosionproof apparatus can withstand an explosion of a specified gas or vapor inside and prevent ignition outside by sparks, flashes, or explosion within it.","r":"NEC 500.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":2,"q":"Hydrogen and atmospheres of equivalent hazard fall into which Class I group?","o":["Group C","Group D","Group B","Group A"],"a":2,"e":"Group B includes hydrogen and manufactured gases with more than 30 percent hydrogen by volume; Group A is acetylene.","r":"NEC 500.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":3,"q":"A technician in a repair bay plugs a diagnostic scan tool into a 125-volt, 20-ampere, single-phase receptacle on the bay wall. Which statement is correct?","o":["GFCI is not needed because the garage is a Class I location","GFCI is required only for 30-ampere and larger receptacles","The receptacle needs GFCI protection for personnel","The receptacle needs GFCI only if it is within 6 ft of a floor drain"],"a":2,"e":"Receptacles of 125-volt, single-phase, 15 and 20 amperes in areas where electrical diagnostic equipment, hand tools, or portable lighting are used require GFCI protection for personnel.","r":"NEC 511.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":3,"q":"A fluorescent luminaire in a repair garage is mounted 10 ft above the floor in a ventilated bay that is not classified above 18 in. Its ballast and lampholder can produce arcs. Which is required of the luminaire?","o":["It must be suspended on a hook and cord","No special requirements apply at any height above the floor","It must be totally enclosed or constructed to prevent escape of sparks or hot metal particles","It must be explosionproof for Class I, Division 1"],"a":2,"e":"Equipment less than 12 ft above the floor that may produce arcs, sparks, or hot particles must be totally enclosed or constructed to prevent their escape.","r":"NEC 511.7(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":3,"q":"A body shop has both Class I vapor areas and sanding dust areas. For a Class II, Division 2 location, which wiring method is permitted?","o":["Electrical metallic tubing","Open wiring on insulators","Type NM cable","Flexible nonmetallic tubing"],"a":0,"e":"Class II, Division 2 permits rigid metal conduit, IMC, EMT, dusttight wireways, and certain cables, in addition to what Division 1 allows. Open wiring and NM cable are not permitted.","r":"NEC 502.10(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":1,"q":"Any pit or depression below the level of the floor in an aircraft hangar where Class I liquids are present is classified as:","o":["Class I, Division 1 or Zone 1, extending up to the hangar floor level","Class I, Division 2, up to the floor level","Class I, Division 2 to a height of 18 in. above the pit","Unclassified if ventilated"],"a":0,"e":"Any pit or depression below the hangar floor is Class I, Division 1 or Zone 1 extending up to the floor level.","r":"NEC 513.3(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"In an aircraft hangar, the entire hangar floor area that is not cut off or ventilated from the hangar, up to what height above the floor, is classified Class I, Division 2 or Zone 2?","o":["18 in.","5 ft","3 ft","6 in."],"a":0,"e":"The entire area of the hangar, including adjacent communicating areas not suitably cut off, is Class I, Division 2 or Zone 2 up to a level 450 mm (18 in.) above the floor.","r":"NEC 513.3(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"What is the extent of the area in the vicinity of an aircraft (power plant or fuel tank) that is classified Class I, Division 2 or Zone 2 in a hangar?","o":["Within 3 ft of the aircraft fuel tank vents only","Within 18 in. of the aircraft skin only","Within 10 ft horizontally of the aircraft, extending to the ceiling","Within 5 ft horizontally of aircraft power plants or fuel tanks, extending upward to 5 ft above the upper surface of wings and engine enclosures"],"a":3,"e":"The area within 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally of aircraft power plants or fuel tanks, extending upward from the floor to 1.5 m (5 ft) above the upper surfaces of wings and engine enclosures, is Division 2 or Zone 2.","r":"NEC 513.3(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":3,"q":"A hangar floor is otherwise unclassified above 18 in. An aircraft is parked with a fuel tank in the wing. An electrician wants to mount a receptacle on a column 4 ft horizontally from the fuel tank, 3 ft above the floor. How is that receptacle location classified?","o":["Class I, Division 2 or Zone 2, because it is within 5 ft horizontally of the fuel tank","Unclassified, because it is more than 18 in. above the floor","Unclassified, because the column is separate from the aircraft","Class I, Division 1, because it is within 5 ft of the tank"],"a":0,"e":"The area within 5 ft horizontally of aircraft power plants or fuel tanks is Division 2 or Zone 2, extending up from the floor. The location 4 ft from the tank is inside that area regardless of the 18 in. floor rule.","r":"NEC 513.3(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":1,"q":"Wiring and equipment installed within a Class I location of an aircraft hangar must comply with which Articles?","o":["Article 410 only","Article 725 only","Article 501 or 505, as applicable","Article 511 only"],"a":2,"e":"Within Class I locations in hangars, wiring and equipment must comply with the applicable requirements of Article 501 (Division) or Article 505 (Zone).","r":"NEC 513.4","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"In an aircraft hangar, fixed lighting, switches, and other arc-producing equipment located less than 10 ft above the upper surface of wings and engine enclosures must be:","o":["Supplied only by 24-volt circuits","Totally enclosed type or constructed to prevent escape of sparks or hot metal particles","Installed with an arc-fault circuit interrupter","Explosionproof Class I, Division 1 equipment"],"a":1,"e":"Equipment less than 3.0 m (10 ft) above wings and engine enclosures that may produce arcs, sparks, or hot particles must be totally enclosed or constructed to prevent their escape.","r":"NEC 513.7(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"Fixed wiring in an aircraft hangar that is not in a Class I location must be installed in:","o":["Metal raceways or certain cables such as Type MC, MI, or TC, depending on location","Open wiring on insulators","Any wiring method acceptable in a dwelling","Type NM cable"],"a":0,"e":"Fixed wiring above or outside Class I locations must be in metal raceways, or Type MC, MI, or TC cable (with listed fittings where required), or other methods listed in 513.7.","r":"NEC 513.7(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"Underground wiring in a hangar using rigid nonmetallic conduit buried with 2 ft of cover requires what at the point where the conduit emerges from the ground?","o":["No change in wiring method","Flexible nonmetallic conduit","Threaded rigid metal conduit or threaded steel IMC for the last 2 ft of the run","EMT with a compression fitting"],"a":2,"e":"Where rigid nonmetallic conduit is used underground, threaded RMC or threaded steel IMC is required for the last 600 mm (2 ft) to the equipment or point of emergence.","r":"NEC 513.8","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"What does the NEC require of aircraft electrical systems while the aircraft is in a hangar and undergoing maintenance, repair, or storage?","o":["They must be bonded to a ground rod only","They shall be de-energized where possible","They must remain energized to prevent corrosion","They must be energized from a 24-volt source only"],"a":1,"e":"Aircraft electrical systems should be de-energized while the aircraft is in the hangar for maintenance or repairs, when practicable.","r":"NEC 513.10(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"Aircraft batteries that are installed in an aircraft:","o":["May be charged anywhere in the hangar","May be charged only with a 12-volt charger","May be charged only within 5 ft of the engine","Shall not be charged where installed in an aircraft located inside or partially inside a hangar"],"a":3,"e":"Aircraft batteries must not be charged while installed in an aircraft that is inside or partially inside a hangar.","r":"NEC 513.10(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":3,"q":"A ground power unit (aircraft energizer) is to be mounted in a hangar. Which requirement applies to its electrical equipment and fixed wiring?","o":["It must be mounted at least 18 in. above the floor and not operated within the Class I location near the aircraft","It may be operated within 3 ft of the fuel tanks","It must be mounted at floor level","It must be explosionproof for Class I, Group B"],"a":0,"e":"Aircraft energizers must be designed and mounted so that all electrical equipment and fixed wiring is at least 450 mm (18 in.) above the floor, and they must not be operated in the vicinity-of-aircraft Class I location.","r":"NEC 513.10(D)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"In an aircraft hangar, which receptacles require GFCI protection for personnel (other than those in 513.7)?","o":["Only receptacles above 20 amperes","Only receptacles outdoors","All 125-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles where diagnostic equipment, hand tools, or portable lighting are used","Only receptacles in restrooms"],"a":2,"e":"125-volt, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles in areas where electrical diagnostic equipment, hand tools, or portable lighting equipment are used need GFCI protection for personnel.","r":"NEC 513.12","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"Which parts must be grounded in an aircraft hangar, regardless of voltage?","o":["Only equipment above 150 volts to ground","All metal raceways, metal cable armor, and non-current-carrying metal parts of fixed and portable equipment","Only metal raceways","Only portable equipment"],"a":1,"e":"All metal raceways, armor or sheath of cables, and non-current-carrying metal parts of fixed and portable equipment must be grounded regardless of voltage.","r":"NEC 513.16(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"A circuit supplies portable equipment in an aircraft hangar through a flexible cord. How is the equipment grounding provided?","o":["By the grounded conductor of the cord","By a ground clamp on the aircraft only","Grounding is not required for portable tools","By a separate equipment grounding conductor within the cord"],"a":3,"e":"Flexible cords supplying portable equipment or pendants must include a separate equipment grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 513.16(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":1,"q":"Where an aircraft hangar is used for painting aircraft, which Article additionally applies to the spray area?","o":["Article 550","Article 516","Article 511","Article 520"],"a":1,"e":"Spray painting of aircraft in hangars is covered by Article 516, Spray Application, Dipping, Coating, and Printing Processes.","r":"NEC 513.3(D), 516","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":1,"q":"Aviation gasoline vapors fall into which Class I group?","o":["Group B","Group A","Group D","Group C"],"a":2,"e":"Gasoline is a Group D atmosphere.","r":"NEC 500.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"In a hangar Class I, Division 1 pit, a conduit enters an enclosure with a snap switch in a Class I location. Which seal rule applies?","o":["No seal if the conduit is metal","A seal within 18 in. of the enclosure","A seal only if the conduit is 3 in. or larger","A seal within 5 ft of the enclosure"],"a":1,"e":"Division 1 requires a seal within 18 in. of an enclosure that may produce arcs or sparks, such as one housing a switch.","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"Portable lighting in a Class I, Division 1 pit of a hangar uses a flexible cord. The cord must be:","o":["Listed for extra-hard usage and include an equipment grounding conductor","Type NM-B cable","Zip cord with a 2-prong plug","Extension cord for household use"],"a":0,"e":"Class I, Division 1 flexible cords must be extra-hard usage listed with a grounding conductor and listed terminations.","r":"NEC 501.140(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"Which condition makes a location Class I, Division 2?","o":["Easily ignitable fibers are manufactured there","Flammable liquid is handled only in closed containers and could escape only by accident","Metal dust is always present","Ignitable concentrations exist under normal operating conditions"],"a":1,"e":"Division 2 includes locations where volatile flammable liquids or vapors are in closed containers or systems from which they could escape only by accidental rupture or abnormal operation.","r":"NEC 500.5(B)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"In a Class I location, equipment marked T4 has a maximum surface temperature of:","o":["100 degrees C","135 degrees C","85 degrees C","200 degrees C"],"a":1,"e":"T4 equals 135 degrees C; T5 is 100 C; T6 is 85 C; T3 is 200 C.","r":"NEC 500.8(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":3,"q":"A pendant luminaire is to be hung from a hangar ceiling, with its lampholder 8 ft above the upper surface of a wing, in the unclassified area above the Division 2 envelope. Which statement is correct?","o":["It must be hung from rigid conduit only","It need not be enclosed because the area is unclassified","Because it is under 10 ft above the wing, it must be totally enclosed or constructed to prevent sparks or hot particles","It must be explosionproof"],"a":2,"e":"The totally enclosed or spark-preventing rule applies to arc-producing equipment less than 10 ft above wings and engine enclosures, even outside the classified space.","r":"NEC 513.7(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":3,"q":"Pendant cords used in an aircraft hangar unclassified area must be:","o":["Lamp cord","Suitable for hard or extra-hard usage and with an equipment grounding conductor","Type NM","Flat parallel cord rated 300 volts"],"a":1,"e":"Pendants require flexible cord suitable for the type of service and approved for hard or extra-hard usage, including an equipment grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 513.7(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"In a hangar, what does a Class I, Zone 2 location describe?","o":["Ignitable concentrations are present continuously","Dust clouds are present continuously","Fibers are present in suspension","Ignitable concentrations are not likely to occur in normal operation, and if they do, exist only a short time"],"a":3,"e":"Zone 2 means not likely under normal operation, and if it occurs, only for a short period.","r":"NEC 505.5(B)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":3,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted in a Class I, Division 2 location of an aircraft hangar?","o":["Open wiring on insulators","Threaded rigid metal conduit","Knob-and-tube","Type NM cable"],"a":1,"e":"Division 2 permits all Division 1 wiring methods, including threaded RMC, plus others listed in 501.10(B).","r":"NEC 501.10(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":1,"q":"For a gasoline dispenser at an outdoor motor fuel dispensing facility, how is the space inside the dispenser enclosure classified?","o":["Unclassified","Class I, Division 2","Class I, Division 1","Class II, Division 1"],"a":2,"e":"The space inside the dispenser enclosure, and all electrical equipment in the dispenser pit or sump, is Class I, Division 1.","r":"NEC Table 514.3(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"At an outdoor gasoline dispenser, up to what height above grade, and how far horizontally, does the Class I, Division 2 area extend beyond the area within 18 in. of the dispenser enclosure?","o":["Up to 5 ft above grade, 5 ft horizontally","Up to 3 ft above grade, 10 ft horizontally","Up to 18 in. above grade, 20 ft horizontally","Up to 18 in. above grade, 5 ft horizontally"],"a":2,"e":"Table 514.3(B)(1) lists Division 2 up to 450 mm (18 in.) above grade extending 6 m (20 ft) horizontally in all directions from the inner Division 2 area at a dispenser.","r":"NEC Table 514.3(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":3,"q":"An outdoor gasoline dispenser is surrounded by pavement. A receptacle is to be installed 24 in. above grade on a wall 30 ft horizontally from the dispenser enclosure. Under the table for a typical outdoor dispenser, how is that receptacle location classified?","o":["Class I, Division 1","Class I, Zone 0","Unclassified by the dispenser rules, because it is more than 18 in. above grade and more than 20 ft away","Class I, Division 2"],"a":2,"e":"Classified areas at an outdoor dispenser are limited to the enclosure, the 18 in. envelope, and the area up to 18 in. above grade within 20 ft. A point 24 in. above grade and 30 ft away is outside all of them.","r":"NEC Table 514.3(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"At an underground gasoline tank vent outdoors, what is the extent of the Class I, Division 1 area measured from the open end of the vent?","o":["Within 5 ft in all directions","Within 18 in. in all directions","Within 3 ft in all directions","Within 10 ft in all directions"],"a":2,"e":"Division 1 extends within 900 mm (3 ft) of the open end of the vent, in all directions.","r":"NEC Table 514.3(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"The Class I, Division 2 area around the open end of an underground gasoline tank vent extends between which distances?","o":["Between 5 ft and 10 ft","Between 18 in. and 3 ft","Between 3 ft and 5 ft in all directions from the open end","Between 3 ft and 20 ft"],"a":2,"e":"The Division 2 area is between 900 mm (3 ft) and 1.5 m (5 ft) from the open end of the vent in all directions.","r":"NEC Table 514.3(B)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"Where must the listed seal be placed in a conduit that enters a gasoline dispenser?","o":["No seal is needed if the conduit is steel","The sealing fitting must be the first fitting after the conduit emerges from the earth or concrete","Inside the dispenser only","At least 10 ft from the dispenser"],"a":1,"e":"Each conduit run entering or leaving a dispenser, or cavities in direct communication, requires a listed seal as the first fitting after the conduit emerges from earth or concrete.","r":"NEC 514.9(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":3,"q":"Conduit rises from below a concrete island to an enclosure in a gasoline dispenser. The installer plans a junction box, then an elbow, then the seal. What is wrong?","o":["A seal is not allowed for gasoline dispensers","Nothing; seals may be anywhere in the run","The seal must be located 25 ft from the dispenser","The seal must be the first fitting after the conduit emerges from the earth or concrete"],"a":3,"e":"The sealing fitting must be the first fitting after the conduit emerges from the earth or concrete, before any junction box or elbow.","r":"NEC 514.9(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"A conduit run passes from a Class I location at a fuel dispensing facility into an unclassified area. What is required?","o":["Nothing, because the run is metal","A GFCI breaker at the panel","A pull box within 6 in. of the boundary","A seal in accordance with 501.15 at the boundary"],"a":3,"e":"Sealing is required where a conduit passes from a Class I location into an unclassified location, with the seal at the boundary as provided in 501.15.","r":"NEC 514.9(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"The disconnecting means for circuits leading to or through dispensing equipment must disconnect which conductors?","o":["All conductors of the circuit, including the grounded conductor, simultaneously","Only the conductor serving the pump motor","Only the equipment grounding conductor","Ungrounded conductors only"],"a":0,"e":"Each circuit leading to or through dispensing equipment needs a clearly identified, readily accessible switch or other means, remote from the dispensing devices, that simultaneously disconnects all conductors including the grounded conductor.","r":"NEC 514.11(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"An emergency shutoff device for an attended self-service gasoline station must be located at what distance from the dispensers it serves?","o":["Not less than 5 ft nor more than 10 ft","Within 5 ft","Not less than 20 ft nor more than 100 ft","More than 200 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Emergency controls must be located more than 6 m (20 ft) but less than 30 m (100 ft) from the dispensers they serve.","r":"NEC 514.11(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":1,"q":"Where must the circuit disconnect for dispensing equipment be located relative to the dispensing devices?","o":["Inside the dispenser enclosure","Remote from the dispensing devices and readily accessible","On the dispenser island within 3 ft","Underground"],"a":1,"e":"The switch or other disconnecting means must be clearly identified, readily accessible, and located remote from the dispensing devices.","r":"NEC 514.11(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"For maintenance and servicing, each dispensing device must be provided with a means to:","o":["Disconnect only the ungrounded conductors","Isolate it completely from all external sources of potential, including the grounded conductor if any","Disconnect the pump motor only","Lock out the dispenser display only"],"a":1,"e":"Each dispensing device needs one or more means to isolate it completely from all external sources of potential, including the grounded conductor if any.","r":"NEC 514.13","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"Rigid nonmetallic conduit is used for underground wiring at a motor fuel dispensing facility. What minimum burial depth is required, and what applies to the last portion before emerging?","o":["18 in. cover; EMT at the end","30 in. cover; no change","12 in. cover; PVC all the way","24 in. cover; threaded RMC or threaded IMC for the last 2 ft before emerging"],"a":3,"e":"Nonmetallic conduit must be under not less than 600 mm (2 ft) of cover, with threaded RMC or threaded steel IMC for the last 2 ft to the equipment or emergence from the ground.","r":"NEC 514.8","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":1,"q":"Electrical wiring and equipment installed within the Class I locations at a motor fuel dispensing facility must comply with:","o":["Article 410 only","The applicable requirements of Article 501 or 505","Article 692","Article 680"],"a":1,"e":"Wiring and equipment in the classified locations of Table 514.3(B)(1) must meet Article 501 (Division) or Article 505 (Zone) requirements.","r":"NEC 514.4","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"Wiring and equipment installed above Class I locations at a motor fuel dispensing facility must comply with:","o":["511.7","Article 590","Article 625 only","The rules for patient care spaces"],"a":0,"e":"Wiring and equipment above Class I locations at these facilities must comply with 511.7, as for commercial garages.","r":"NEC 514.7","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"Metal parts of dispensing equipment, remote pumps, and non-current-carrying parts that could become energized must be:","o":["Left isolated from ground","Connected only to a driven rod","Grounded and bonded as required by Article 250","Bonded to the gas piping only"],"a":2,"e":"Grounding and bonding for dispensing equipment must comply with Article 250, with additional requirements in Article 501.","r":"NEC 514.16","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":1,"q":"Gasoline vapor at a dispenser is a hazardous atmosphere belonging to:","o":["Class III","Class I, Group A","Class II, Group E","Class I, Group D"],"a":3,"e":"Gasoline is a Class I, Group D material.","r":"NEC 500.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted inside the Class I, Division 1 space of a dispenser?","o":["PVC Schedule 40 above grade","Flexible metal conduit with set-screw fittings","Electrical metallic tubing","Threaded rigid metal conduit"],"a":3,"e":"Class I, Division 1 allows threaded RMC, threaded steel IMC, Type MI and Type MC-HL cables with listed fittings; EMT, PVC and standard FMC fittings are not permitted.","r":"NEC 501.10(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":3,"q":"A conduit enters a dispenser enclosure that contains a relay that may produce arcs. How far from the enclosure can the first seal be placed, apart from the first-fitting rule at the earth?","o":["Within 10 ft","Within 5 ft","Within 18 in. of the enclosure","Within 3 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Seals for arcing equipment in Division 1 are within 18 in. of the enclosure.","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":3,"q":"A threaded RMC run leaves a classified area at a fuel dispenser. An installer uses double locknuts at each box entry and relies on them for bonding. Is this acceptable?","o":["No; bonding jumpers with proper fittings or other approved means must be used","Yes, if sealing compound is used","Yes, double locknuts are acceptable bonding","Yes, if the conduit is galvanized"],"a":0,"e":"Locknut-bushing and double-locknut contacts must not be depended on for bonding in Class I locations.","r":"NEC 501.30(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"Threaded conduit entries into explosionproof enclosures require what minimum thread engagement?","o":["Three threads","Two threads","Seven threads","Five fully engaged threads"],"a":3,"e":"Threaded joints must be made up with at least five threads fully engaged.","r":"NEC 500.8(E)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"Sealing compound in a seal fitting in a Class I location must have a thickness of at least:","o":["Half the trade size","The trade size of the fitting, and never less than 5/8 in.","1/4 in.","1 in."],"a":1,"e":"The minimum thickness is the trade size of the sealing fitting, and never less than 16 mm (5/8 in.).","r":"NEC 501.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"A location where ignitable gas concentrations are likely to exist under normal operation is classified as:","o":["Zone 1","Zone 22","Zone 2","Zone 0"],"a":0,"e":"Zone 1 is likely under normal operating conditions; Zone 0 is continuous; Zone 2 is not likely.","r":"NEC 505.5(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"Which temperature class denotes a maximum surface temperature of 135 degrees C?","o":["T3","T4","T5","T6"],"a":1,"e":"T4 is 135 degrees C. T3 is 200, T5 is 100, T6 is 85.","r":"NEC 500.8(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"May splices be made inside a fitting that is intended only for sealing compound?","o":["Yes, if insulated with tape","Yes, in Division 2 only","Yes, if 10 AWG or smaller","No, splices and taps are not permitted in such fittings"],"a":3,"e":"Splices and taps must not be made in fittings intended only for sealing compound.","r":"NEC 501.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":3,"q":"A portable work light is used in a Class I, Division 2 location at a station's canopy lube bay. What flexible cord type is required?","o":["Lamp cord","Any flat cord","Listed for extra-hard usage with an equipment grounding conductor","Type NM"],"a":2,"e":"Flexible cords in Class I locations (Division 1, and Division 2 where flexible cord is permitted) must be extra-hard usage listed with an equipment grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 501.140","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":3,"q":"A 120/240-volt, single-phase circuit with a grounded neutral feeds a gasoline dispenser. The shutoff device opens only the two ungrounded conductors. Is this compliant?","o":["Yes, but only for 120-volt dispensers","No; the means must disconnect all conductors, including the grounded conductor, simultaneously","Yes, if a tag is attached","Yes; grounded conductors are never opened"],"a":1,"e":"The disconnecting means must simultaneously disconnect all conductors of the circuit, including the grounded conductor.","r":"NEC 514.11(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"Acetylene belongs to which Class I group?","o":["Group D","Group A","Group B","Group C"],"a":1,"e":"Acetylene is Group A; hydrogen is B; ethylene C; gasoline D.","r":"NEC 500.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities","d":2,"q":"Which describes a Class I, Zone 2 location, such as the outer ring around a gasoline dispenser?","o":["Combustible dust is present continuously","Ignitable concentrations are likely to exist under normal operating conditions","Ignitable concentrations are not likely to occur in normal operation and, if they do, will exist only for a short time","Ignitable concentrations are present continuously"],"a":2,"e":"Zone 2 means not likely to occur in normal operation and, if it does, only for a short period.","r":"NEC 505.5(B)(3)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":2,"q":"At a bulk plant, what is the extent of the Class I, Division 1 area around the open end of an aboveground tank vent?","o":["Within 10 ft of the open end","Within 18 in. of the open end","Within 25 ft of the open end","Within 3 ft of the open end, in all directions"],"a":3,"e":"Table 515.3 classifies the area within 900 mm (3 ft) of the open end of the vent in all directions as Division 1.","r":"NEC Table 515.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":2,"q":"The Class I, Division 2 area around the open end of a bulk plant tank vent extends:","o":["Between 3 ft and 5 ft from the open end in all directions","Between 5 ft and 15 ft","Between 18 in. and 3 ft","Between 10 ft and 25 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Division 2 is the area between 900 mm (3 ft) and 1.5 m (5 ft) from the open end of the vent.","r":"NEC Table 515.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":2,"q":"For an outdoor aboveground Class I liquid storage tank at a bulk plant, the area within what distance of the shell, ends, or roof of the tank is classified Class I, Division 2?","o":["18 in.","15 m (50 ft)","3 m (10 ft)","1.5 m (5 ft)"],"a":2,"e":"Table 515.3 lists Division 2 within 3 m (10 ft) from the shell, ends, or roof of the tank.","r":"NEC Table 515.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":2,"q":"A tank vehicle is loaded through an open dome at a bulk plant. The area within what distance of the open dome is Class I, Division 1?","o":["900 mm (3 ft) in all directions","18 in.","15 ft","5 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Loading through an open dome has Division 1 within 900 mm (3 ft) of the open dome in all directions, with Division 2 beyond it.","r":"NEC Table 515.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":1,"q":"Gasoline dispensers installed on a bulk plant premises must comply with which Article?","o":["Article 511 only","Article 520","Article 514","Article 516"],"a":2,"e":"Gasoline dispensers at a bulk plant must comply with Article 514 requirements.","r":"NEC 515.10","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":1,"q":"Electrical wiring and equipment located within the Class I locations of a bulk plant must comply with:","o":["Article 501 or 505, as applicable","Article 680","Article 700 only","Article 410 only"],"a":0,"e":"Within classified locations, wiring and equipment must comply with Article 501 (Division) or 505 (Zone).","r":"NEC 515.4","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":2,"q":"Rigid nonmetallic conduit is used for underground wiring in a bulk plant Class I location. What cover and end treatment are required?","o":["6 in. cover with a PVC elbow","Not less than 2 ft of cover, with threaded RMC or IMC for the last 2 ft before emerging","3 ft cover and no change in wiring method","12 in. cover and EMT elbows"],"a":1,"e":"Buried nonmetallic conduit needs not less than 600 mm (2 ft) of cover and threaded RMC or threaded steel IMC for the last 600 mm (2 ft) to the equipment or emergence point.","r":"NEC 515.8","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":2,"q":"At a bulk plant, which non-current-carrying metal parts of electrical equipment must be grounded?","o":["Only parts in Division 1 locations","All metal raceways, cable armor, and non-current-carrying metal parts of fixed and portable equipment, regardless of voltage","Only portable equipment","Only parts operating at over 150 volts to ground"],"a":1,"e":"Metal raceways, cable armor, and non-current-carrying metal parts of fixed and portable electrical equipment are grounded regardless of voltage.","r":"NEC 515.16","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":3,"q":"A conduit leaves a Class I, Division 2 location in a bulk plant and enters an unclassified area. Where may the seal be installed, and what limits fittings between the seal and the boundary?","o":["No seal is required for Division 2 boundaries","On either side of the boundary, with no union, coupling, box, or fitting (except listed explosionproof reducers at the seal) between the seal and the boundary","Only on the unclassified side, with any fittings allowed","Only inside the classified area, with a pull box between the seal and boundary"],"a":1,"e":"For Division 2 boundary seals, the seal may be on either side and the conduit between the seal and the point where it leaves the Division 2 location must contain no union, coupling, box, or fitting other than listed explosionproof reducers at the seal.","r":"NEC 501.15(B)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":1,"q":"Metal dust such as aluminum or magnesium is in which Class II group?","o":["Group G","Group E","Group F","Group D"],"a":1,"e":"Group E: metal dusts such as aluminum and magnesium. Group F: carbonaceous dusts like coal, carbon black. Group G: flour, starch, grain, plastics.","r":"NEC 500.6(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":1,"q":"Coal dust and carbon black are classified in which Class II group?","o":["Group C","Group G","Group F","Group E"],"a":2,"e":"Group F covers carbonaceous dusts with more than 8 percent volatile material, such as carbon black, coal, and coke dust.","r":"NEC 500.6(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":1,"q":"Flour, starch, and grain dusts are classified as:","o":["Class III, Group F","Class II, Group G","Class II, Group E","Class I, Group D"],"a":1,"e":"Flour, starch, and grain dust are Class II, Group G materials.","r":"NEC 500.6(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":1,"q":"A Class III, Division 1 location is one where:","o":["Easily ignitable fibers or flyings are handled, manufactured, or used","Easily ignitable fibers are only stored","Metal dust is in suspension","Flammable gas is present continuously"],"a":0,"e":"Class III, Division 1 locations handle, manufacture, or use easily ignitable fibers or materials producing combustible flyings; Division 2 locations merely store or handle them (other than manufacturing).","r":"NEC 500.5(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":2,"q":"An intrinsically safe apparatus is one that is:","o":["Incapable of releasing enough electrical or thermal energy under normal or abnormal conditions to ignite a specific hazardous atmosphere","Purged with inert gas","Enclosed to withstand an internal explosion","Immersed in oil"],"a":0,"e":"Intrinsic safety limits energy so ignition cannot occur, in contrast to explosionproof (contain) or purging.","r":"NEC 500.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":2,"q":"Where liquid or condensed vapor may be trapped in enclosures or a raceway system in a Class I location, what must be provided?","o":["A GFCI breaker","Approved means to prevent accumulation or to permit periodic draining","A larger conduit size only","A union at every fitting"],"a":1,"e":"Where liquid or condensed vapor may be trapped, means to prevent accumulation or to allow periodic draining must be provided.","r":"NEC 501.15(F)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":2,"q":"A temperature class T5 equipment has a maximum surface temperature of:","o":["200 degrees C","85 degrees C","100 degrees C","135 degrees C"],"a":2,"e":"T5 is 100 C; T6 is 85 C; T4 is 135 C.","r":"NEC 500.8(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":2,"q":"Which cable is permitted in a Class I, Division 2 location when installed with listed termination fittings?","o":["Type AC cable","Type MC cable","Type NM cable","Type UF cable"],"a":1,"e":"Type MC cable with listed termination fittings is permitted in Division 2; NM, UF and AC are not.","r":"NEC 501.10(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":2,"q":"A Zone 20 location is one where:","o":["Dust is present only during cleaning","Flammable gas is likely under normal operation","Dust is not likely and exists only briefly","Ignitable concentrations of combustible dust or flyings are present continuously or for long periods"],"a":3,"e":"Zone 20 means ignitable dust concentrations are continuously or frequently present; Zone 21 is likely in normal operation; Zone 22 is not likely and brief.","r":"NEC 506.5(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":3,"q":"Zone-rated equipment is marked AEx d IIB T4. Which Class I atmosphere group does IIB correspond to?","o":["Ethylene (Group C)","Gasoline (Group D)","Hydrogen only","Acetylene (Group A)"],"a":0,"e":"Group IIC corresponds to acetylene and hydrogen, IIB to ethylene, and IIA to propane/gasoline type atmospheres.","r":"NEC 505.9","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":3,"q":"A 1-1/2 in. rigid conduit in a Class I, Division 1 area enters an enclosure holding only terminals and splices (no arcing devices). Is a seal required at that enclosure?","o":["Yes, only if the conduit is nonmetallic","No, because the conduit is smaller than 2 in. trade size and the enclosure has no arcing devices","Yes, within 18 in. regardless of conduit size","Yes, within 10 ft"],"a":1,"e":"For terminals, splices, taps, a seal within 18 in. is required only for conduit of 2 in. trade size or larger. The 1-1/2 in. conduit with no arcing devices needs no seal at that enclosure (other boundary seals may still apply).","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":2,"q":"How is bonding achieved across intervening raceways, fittings, and boxes in a Class I location?","o":["With double locknuts","With a bushing alone","With bonding jumpers and proper fittings, not with locknuts or bushings","Bonding is not required"],"a":2,"e":"Locknut-bushing and double-locknut types of contacts must not be depended on.","r":"NEC 501.30(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":2,"q":"Portable lighting in a Class I, Division 1 area at a loading rack uses a flexible cord. Which is required?","o":["Type NM","Cord with ungrounded plug","Extra-hard usage listed cord with an equipment grounding conductor","Light-duty cord"],"a":2,"e":"Extra-hard usage cord with a grounding conductor is required in Class I, Division 1.","r":"NEC 501.140(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":2,"q":"Fill in a seal fitting: how much of a rigid conduit of the same trade size's cross-sectional area may the conductors occupy, unless the seal is identified for a higher percentage?","o":["53 percent","60 percent","25 percent","40 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Conductor cross-sectional area in a seal must not exceed 25 percent of that of RMC of the same trade size.","r":"NEC 501.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":1,"q":"Ignitable concentrations of flammable gas or vapor that are present continuously or for long periods are classified as:","o":["Division 2","Zone 0","Zone 1","Zone 2"],"a":1,"e":"Zone 0 is continuous or long-period presence; Zone 1 is likely under normal conditions.","r":"NEC 505.5(B)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","d":2,"q":"Equipment marked T6 has a maximum surface temperature of:","o":["135 degrees C","100 degrees C","300 degrees C","85 degrees C"],"a":3,"e":"T6 is 85 degrees C, the lowest standard class.","r":"NEC 500.8(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":1,"q":"The interior of a spray booth in which Class I flammable finishes are applied is classified as:","o":["Unclassified","Class I, Division 2","Class III, Division 2","Class I, Division 1 (or Zone 1 or 0 as applicable)"],"a":3,"e":"The interior of a spray booth or spray room where flammable vapors are produced during spraying is a Division 1 location.","r":"NEC 516.3","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":2,"q":"In fixed electrostatic spraying equipment, where must the power packs, transformers, and control apparatus be located (other than the charging electrodes and their connections)?","o":["Inside the spray booth, in an explosionproof enclosure only","Outside the Class I or II location, or otherwise be identified for the location","Directly beneath the booth floor","Anywhere in the booth"],"a":1,"e":"Transformers, power packs, and control apparatus must be located outside the classified location or be identified for it; only the electrodes and their connections may be inside.","r":"NEC 516.10(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":2,"q":"What must happen to the high-voltage supply of electrostatic spraying equipment if the ventilating fans stop?","o":["It must be rapidly de-energized automatically","It continues until the batch ends","It switches to a lower voltage","Nothing; ventilation is not interlocked"],"a":0,"e":"Electrostatic equipment must have automatic means to rapidly de-energize the high-voltage supply on ventilation failure, conveyor stoppage, ground, or loss of required clearance.","r":"NEC 516.10(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":2,"q":"Objects being coated by electrostatic spraying must be maintained in electrical contact with conveyors or other supports. What is the maximum resistance between the object and ground?","o":["100 ohms","10 megohms","25 ohms","1 megohm"],"a":3,"e":"The resistance between the object being coated and ground must not exceed 1 megohm.","r":"NEC 516.10(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":2,"q":"In a spray application facility, which equipment must be grounded?","o":["All metal raceways, cable armor, and non-current-carrying metal parts of fixed and portable equipment, regardless of voltage","Only equipment above 150 volts","Only the booth fan","Only portable equipment"],"a":0,"e":"Metal raceways, cable armor, and non-current-carrying metal parts are grounded regardless of voltage per Article 250.","r":"NEC 516.16","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":1,"q":"Lacquer solvent vapors fall into which Class I group?","o":["Group B","Group C","Group A","Group D"],"a":3,"e":"Lacquer solvent vapors, acetone, alcohol, benzol, gasoline, naphtha are Group D.","r":"NEC 500.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":1,"q":"A Class II, Division 1 location is one where:","o":["Dust is never present","Combustible dust in air is present under normal operating conditions in quantities sufficient to produce explosive or ignitable mixtures","Only fibers are stored","Flammable gas is present"],"a":1,"e":"Class II, Division 1 includes locations where combustible dust is in suspension under normal operating conditions in sufficient quantity, where mechanical failure could create such mixtures, or where Group E dust is present.","r":"NEC 500.5(C)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":3,"q":"A raceway in a Class II location connects a dust-ignitionproof enclosure to one that is not. Which arrangement satisfies the dust seal rule?","o":["A permanent and effective seal, a horizontal run of at least 10 ft, or a vertical run of at least 5 ft extending downward from the dust-ignitionproof enclosure","A union within 6 in. of each enclosure","No sealing is required","A run of at least 18 in. in any direction"],"a":0,"e":"Dust seal alternatives: permanent and effective seal, horizontal raceway not less than 3.05 m (10 ft), or vertical raceway not less than 1.5 m (5 ft) extending downward from the dust-ignitionproof enclosure.","r":"NEC 502.15","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":2,"q":"Which location is Class I, Division 2?","o":["Where vapors exist during normal operation","Where fibers are manufactured","Where ignitable vapors are normally prevented by positive-pressure ventilation and could become hazardous only upon ventilation failure","Where metal dust is suspended"],"a":2,"e":"Division 2 includes locations where ignitable concentrations are normally prevented by positive pressure ventilation and might occur only upon failure of the ventilation.","r":"NEC 500.5(B)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":2,"q":"What minimum thread engagement is required for threaded conduit joints in hazardous (classified) locations?","o":["Three threads","One full thread","Eight threads","Five threads"],"a":3,"e":"At least five threads must be fully engaged.","r":"NEC 500.8(E)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":2,"q":"Where a seal is required for an enclosure containing an arcing device in a Class I, Division 1 location, it must be within:","o":["6 ft","25 ft","18 in. of the enclosure","10 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Seals must be within 450 mm (18 in.) of the enclosure.","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum thickness of the sealing compound in a seal fitting?","o":["1 in. always","Trade size of the fitting, not less than 5/8 in.","1/8 in.","3/8 in."],"a":1,"e":"Compound thickness is not less than the trade size and never less than 5/8 in.","r":"NEC 501.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":2,"q":"A portable lamp in a Class I, Division 1 spray area is supplied by flexible cord. Which cord is required?","o":["Type NM","Zip cord","Any 2-wire cord","Extra-hard usage listed with an equipment grounding conductor"],"a":3,"e":"Division 1 flexible cords are extra-hard usage with a grounding conductor and listed fittings.","r":"NEC 501.140(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":2,"q":"Bonding of metal raceways in a Class I location may not rely on:","o":["Locknut-bushing and double-locknut contacts","Listed bonding fittings","Threaded joints","Bonding jumpers"],"a":0,"e":"Locknut-bushing and double-locknut contacts must not be depended on for bonding.","r":"NEC 501.30(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":2,"q":"Aluminum and magnesium dust are classified as:","o":["Class II, Group E","Class I, Group B","Class II, Group G","Class II, Group F"],"a":0,"e":"Metal dusts such as aluminum and magnesium are Group E.","r":"NEC 500.6(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":2,"q":"Hazardous location equipment marking must include which of the following?","o":["Only the ampere rating","Only the voltage","Class, division or zone, group, and temperature class","Only the manufacturer name"],"a":2,"e":"Equipment marking includes class, division or zone, group, and operating temperature or temperature class.","r":"NEC 500.8(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":3,"q":"A seal fitting is installed in a Class I location. The installer plans a splice inside the fitting before filling it. Is that acceptable?","o":["Yes, if 14 AWG","Yes, if tape is used","No, splices and taps must not be made in fittings intended only for sealing compound","Yes, if conduit is metal"],"a":2,"e":"Splices and taps are not permitted in fittings intended only for sealing.","r":"NEC 501.15(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":2,"q":"A Class I, Zone 1 location is one where ignitable concentrations:","o":["Are present continuously","Are not likely and brief","Are likely to exist under normal operating conditions","Are dust clouds"],"a":2,"e":"Zone 1 is likely under normal operating conditions.","r":"NEC 505.5(B)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":2,"q":"Dust-ignitionproof equipment is designed to:","o":["Be oil-immersed","Be purged with air","Withstand a gas explosion","Exclude dust and not permit arcs, sparks, or heat inside to ignite external suspended dust"],"a":3,"e":"Dust-ignitionproof enclosures exclude dust and do not permit arcs, sparks, or heat generated inside to cause ignition of exterior accumulations or atmospheric suspensions.","r":"NEC 500.2","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":1,"q":"Spray painting of aircraft inside an aircraft hangar is covered by which Article for the spray area?","o":["Article 516","Article 604","Article 590","Article 525"],"a":0,"e":"Painting of aircraft in hangars is covered by Article 516.","r":"NEC 516.1","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":2,"q":"Combustible dust in air is present continuously or for long periods in a location classified by the Zone system. Which zone applies?","o":["Zone 20","Zone 2","Zone 21","Zone 22"],"a":0,"e":"Zone 20 is continuous or long-period presence of ignitable dust; Zone 21 is likely in normal operation; Zone 22 is not likely and brief.","r":"NEC 506.5(B)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":1,"q":"A Class III, Division 2 location is one where easily ignitable fibers or flyings are:","o":["Present as metal dust","Manufactured or processed","Stored or handled other than in the process of manufacture","Present as vapor"],"a":2,"e":"Class III, Division 2 locations store or handle easily ignitable fibers (other than manufacturing).","r":"NEC 500.5(D)(2)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping and Coating","d":1,"q":"Ethylene belongs to which Class I group?","o":["Group B","Group C","Group D","Group A"],"a":1,"e":"Group C includes ethylene, ethyl ether, cyclopropane.","r":"NEC 500.6(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":1,"q":"In a health care facility, a space in which failure of equipment or a system is likely to cause major injury or death of patients, staff, or visitors is a:","o":["Category 3 space","Category 2 space","Category 4 space","Category 1 space"],"a":3,"e":"Category 1 (critical care) spaces are those where failure is likely to cause major injury or death.","r":"NEC 517.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":1,"q":"A space where failure of equipment or a system is likely to cause minor injury to patients, staff, or visitors is:","o":["Category 4 (support)","Category 1","Category 3 (basic care)","Category 2 (general care)"],"a":3,"e":"Category 2 is general care: failure likely to cause minor injury.","r":"NEC 517.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Category 3 (basic care) spaces are those in which failure of equipment or a system is:","o":["Likely to cause major injury or death","Likely to cause minor injury","Not likely to cause injury but may cause patient discomfort","Of no consequence to patients"],"a":2,"e":"Category 3: failure is not likely to cause injury but can cause patient discomfort. Category 4 spaces: failure is not likely to have an impact on patient care.","r":"NEC 517.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"The patient care vicinity, within which receptacles and equipment of a patient bed location must meet special requirements, extends how far from the bed perimeter and how high above the floor?","o":["6 ft beyond the bed perimeter, and at least 7 ft 6 in. above the floor","6 ft beyond the bed, and 10 ft above the floor","10 ft beyond the bed, and to the ceiling","3 ft beyond the bed, and 6 ft above the floor"],"a":0,"e":"The patient vicinity is 1.8 m (6 ft) beyond the perimeter of the bed in its nominal location and extends vertically not less than 2.3 m (7 ft 6 in.) above the floor.","r":"NEC 517.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Which area is a wet procedure location?","o":["Any patient room with a sink","A room where routine housekeeping mopping occurs","A corridor with a drinking fountain","A space where a procedure is normally subject to wet conditions with patients present, such as standing fluids on the floor or drenching of the work area"],"a":3,"e":"Wet procedure locations have standing fluids or drenching associated with the procedure; routine housekeeping and incidental spillage do not make one.","r":"NEC 517.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"In Article 517, the reference grounding point of a patient care space is:","o":["The ground bus of the panelboard or isolated power system panel supplying the patient care space","The nearest water pipe","The receptacle in the patient room","The service grounding electrode"],"a":0,"e":"The reference grounding point is the ground bus of the panelboard (or isolated power panel) that supplies the patient care space.","r":"NEC 517.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is NOT a patient care space under the definition in Article 517?","o":["A patient bedroom","A business office","A treatment room","An examination room"],"a":1,"e":"Business offices, corridors, lounges, day rooms, nurses' stations, and similar areas not intended for examination or treatment are not patient care spaces.","r":"NEC 517.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"How must branch circuits serving patient care spaces be wired to provide an effective ground-fault current path?","o":["In an open wiring system with a bare ground wire","In nonmetallic conduit only","With Type NM cable","In a metal raceway system or a cable having a metallic armor or sheath that qualifies as an equipment grounding conductor under 250.118"],"a":3,"e":"Branch circuits serving patient care spaces need an effective ground-fault current path via a metal raceway or metal-armored/sheathed cable that qualifies as an equipment grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 517.13(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"In addition to the metal raceway or metal cable armor, what grounding conductor is required to serve the grounding terminals of receptacles in patient care spaces?","o":["No additional conductor","An insulated copper equipment grounding conductor sized per 250.122","The grounded (neutral) conductor","A bare aluminum conductor"],"a":1,"e":"Receptacle grounding terminals and fixed equipment likely to become energized must be connected to an insulated copper equipment grounding conductor, sized per 250.122, installed with the branch circuit conductors in the metal raceway.","r":"NEC 517.13(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A branch circuit supplies receptacles in a hospital patient room and is installed in EMT. The EMT is continuous and qualifies as an equipment grounding path. An installer proposes to omit the equipment grounding conductor. Is this acceptable?","o":["Yes; EMT is sufficient","No; an insulated copper equipment grounding conductor is also required in the raceway","Yes, if the circuit is 15 amperes","Yes, if a bonding jumper is used at the panel"],"a":1,"e":"Patient care space wiring needs both the metal raceway (or armored cable) and an insulated copper equipment grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 517.13(A) and (B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Equipment grounding terminal buses of the normal and essential branch-circuit panelboards serving the same patient vicinity must be connected together with an insulated continuous copper conductor not smaller than:","o":["10 AWG","6 AWG","12 AWG","14 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"The panelboard grounding terminal buses serving the same patient vicinity must be bonded with an insulated continuous copper conductor not smaller than 10 AWG.","r":"NEC 517.14","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"Two panelboards serve the same patient vicinity and are fed from separate transfer switches of the emergency system. What is required of their equipment grounding terminal buses?","o":["They must be separated","They must be connected with an uninsulated aluminum conductor","Nothing, because they are on different transfer switches","They must be connected together with an insulated continuous copper conductor not smaller than 10 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Where panelboards serving the same patient vicinity are served from separate transfer switches, their grounding buses must still be bonded with insulated copper not smaller than 10 AWG.","r":"NEC 517.14","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":1,"q":"Each patient bed location in a general care space (Category 2) must be provided with a minimum of how many receptacles?","o":["Fourteen","Two","Four","Six"],"a":2,"e":"General care patient bed locations require a minimum of four receptacles, single or duplex, all listed hospital grade and so identified.","r":"NEC 517.18(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"In general care spaces, receptacles at patient bed locations must be listed as:","o":["Tamper resistant only","Hospital grade and so identified","Isolated ground","Weather resistant"],"a":1,"e":"All receptacles at general care patient bed locations must be listed hospital grade and identified as such.","r":"NEC 517.18(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"A general care patient bed location must be supplied by at least two branch circuits. From which sources?","o":["Both from the emergency system","One from the service and one from a UPS","Both from the normal system","At least one from the emergency system and at least one from the normal system"],"a":3,"e":"Bed locations are supplied by at least two branch circuits: one or more from the emergency system and one or more from the normal system.","r":"NEC 517.18(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Branch circuits supplying general care patient bed locations from the normal system must originate in:","o":["Different panelboards on each floor","Any panelboard in the building","A subpanel in the corridor only","The same panelboard"],"a":3,"e":"All branch circuits from the normal system serving a bed location must originate in the same panelboard.","r":"NEC 517.18(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A general care patient room bed location has two duplex receptacles on the bed headwall, hospital grade, and no other receptacles. Does it meet the minimum receptacle count?","o":["No, a minimum of 14 is required","Yes, because two duplex receptacles equal four receptacles","Yes, but only if one is tamper resistant","No, a minimum of six is required"],"a":1,"e":"The minimum of four receptacles may be single or duplex; two duplex receptacles count as four.","r":"NEC 517.18(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Receptacles in pediatric rooms, bathrooms, playrooms, and activity rooms of a pediatric ward must be:","o":["Isolated ground type","Only GFCI protected","Listed tamper-resistant or employ a listed tamper-resistant cover","Located at least 8 ft above the floor"],"a":2,"e":"Pediatric locations require listed tamper-resistant receptacles or listed tamper-resistant covers.","r":"NEC 517.18(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"Can a multiwire branch circuit supply receptacles at a patient bed location in a general care space?","o":["No, branch circuits serving patient bed locations shall not be part of a multiwire branch circuit","Yes, if the breaker has a handle tie","Yes, if the neutral is common","Yes, if the circuit is 20 amperes"],"a":0,"e":"Branch circuits serving patient bed locations must not be part of a multiwire branch circuit.","r":"NEC 517.18(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Each patient bed location in a critical care space (Category 1) must be supplied by at least two branch circuits. What is required of the emergency system circuit?","o":["All circuits at the bed must be from the normal system","Emergency circuits may be shared among up to five bed locations","At least one branch circuit from the emergency system must supply an outlet or outlets only at that bed location","Emergency circuits are not required"],"a":2,"e":"At least one emergency system branch circuit supplies outlets only at that bed location; all normal system branch circuits at the bed come from a single panelboard.","r":"NEC 517.19(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":1,"q":"What minimum number of receptacles is required at each patient bed location in a critical care space (Category 1)?","o":["14","6","4","8"],"a":0,"e":"Category 1 bed locations require a minimum of 14 receptacles.","r":"NEC 517.19(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A Category 1 bed location is equipped with seven duplex receptacles on the headwall. Does the receptacle count meet the minimum for the critical care bed location?","o":["Yes, seven duplex receptacles provide 14 receptacles","No, 16 are required","No, 14 duplex receptacles are required","No, only 4 are needed so it exceeds"],"a":0,"e":"The minimum of 14 receptacles may be provided with single or duplex receptacles; seven duplexes equal 14.","r":"NEC 517.19(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Emergency system receptacles at a critical care bed location must be identified in what way?","o":["Marked with a red dot only","Not required to be identified","Painted blue","Identified and marked with the panelboard and branch circuit number supplying them"],"a":3,"e":"Emergency system receptacles must be identified and indicate the panelboard and branch-circuit number supplying them.","r":"NEC 517.19(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"All normal system branch circuits serving a critical care bed location must originate from:","o":["A single panelboard","The emergency panel","Two panelboards on different floors","The service equipment only"],"a":0,"e":"All branch circuits from the normal system serving the bed must originate from a single panelboard.","r":"NEC 517.19(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"The grounding terminal of each receptacle at a Category 1 bed location must be connected to the reference grounding point by:","o":["An insulated copper conductor sized per 250.122","An uninsulated aluminum wire","Nothing; a metal raceway is sufficient","The metal faceplate only"],"a":0,"e":"Each receptacle's grounding terminal is connected to the reference grounding point by an insulated copper conductor.","r":"NEC 517.19(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Which facilities must provide the additional level of ground-fault protection of equipment (feeder GFPE) under 517.17?","o":["Any building over 10,000 sq ft","Dental offices only","Hospitals and other buildings with critical care spaces or electrical life support equipment","Single-family dwellings"],"a":2,"e":"The rule applies to hospitals and other buildings (including multiple-occupancy) with critical care spaces or life support equipment, plus buildings providing essential utilities to them.","r":"NEC 517.17(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Where service or feeder ground-fault protection is required under 230.95 or 215.10 in a hospital, what must be added?","o":["A GFCI at every receptacle","A neutral-to-ground bond at each panel","An additional step of ground-fault protection in the next level of feeder disconnecting means downstream toward the load","A second service"],"a":2,"e":"An additional step of ground-fault protection is required in all next-level feeder disconnecting means downstream.","r":"NEC 517.17(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"What minimum separation must be provided between the service and feeder ground-fault tripping bands?","o":["Two cycles","One second","Twelve cycles","Six cycles"],"a":3,"e":"A separation of at least six cycles between service and feeder ground-fault tripping bands is required to achieve 100 percent selectivity.","r":"NEC 517.17(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"Where is the additional level of GFPE under 517.17 not required to be installed?","o":["On feeders to critical care spaces","On the first feeder below the service","On the line side of the service","On the load side of an essential electrical system transfer switch"],"a":3,"e":"Additional GFPE levels are not installed on the load side of an essential electrical system transfer switch, between the on-site generator and transfer switch, or on systems not solidly grounded wye with more than 150 volts to ground and not over 1000 volts phase-to-phase.","r":"NEC 517.17(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"When ground-fault protection equipment under 517.17 is first installed, what must be done?","o":["Each level must be performance tested to ensure compliance with the selectivity requirement","It must be tested annually only","Nothing; testing is optional","It must be replaced after one year"],"a":0,"e":"When first installed, each level must be performance tested to verify selectivity.","r":"NEC 517.17(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":1,"q":"The essential electrical system of a hospital consists of:","o":["A life safety system and a UPS","A normal system and a legally required standby system","A critical branch and a service","An emergency system and an equipment system"],"a":3,"e":"Hospital essential electrical systems comprise the emergency system and the equipment system.","r":"NEC 517.30(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"The emergency system of a hospital is made up of which two branches?","o":["The normal branch and the standby branch","The life safety branch and the critical branch","The equipment branch and the delayed branch","The egress branch and the data branch"],"a":1,"e":"The emergency system is limited to circuits essential to life safety and critical patient care, the life safety branch and critical branch.","r":"NEC 517.30(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"One transfer switch may serve one or more branches or systems of an essential electrical system in a facility whose maximum demand on the essential system is:","o":["50 kVA or less","1000 kVA or less","500 kVA or less","150 kVA or less"],"a":3,"e":"A single transfer switch is permitted to serve one or more branches or systems in a facility with a maximum essential system demand of 150 kVA.","r":"NEC 517.30(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A hospital addition has a maximum essential electrical system demand of 400 kVA. How many transfer switches are needed?","o":["Four, one per 100 kVA","None, because the generator output is protected by its own breaker","One is always sufficient","More than one; a single switch is permitted only up to 150 kVA demand"],"a":3,"e":"Above 150 kVA, a single transfer switch may not serve all branches; the number is based on reliability, design, and load, so more than one is required.","r":"NEC 517.30(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"The life safety and critical branches of the emergency system must be:","o":["Run in the same raceway as normal circuits","Run on the same panelboard as normal loads","Kept entirely independent of all other wiring and equipment, not occupying the same raceway, box, or cabinet with other wiring","Combined into one cable"],"a":2,"e":"These branches must be kept independent and may not enter the same raceway, box, or cabinet with other wiring, except in specific listed cases such as transfer switches.","r":"NEC 517.30(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted for emergency system wiring in a hospital without concrete encasement, for mechanical protection?","o":["Schedule 80 rigid PVC conduit","Flexible nonmetallic conduit exposed","Schedule 40 rigid PVC conduit","Type NM cable"],"a":0,"e":"Nonflexible metal raceways, Type MI cable, or Schedule 80 rigid PVC are permitted. Schedule 40 PVC needs 2 in. of concrete encasement.","r":"NEC 517.30(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"After interruption of the normal source, the emergency system of a hospital must be restored to operation within:","o":["5 minutes","60 seconds","30 seconds","10 seconds"],"a":3,"e":"The emergency system must be available within 10 seconds of interruption.","r":"NEC 517.31","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Which loads are served by the equipment system of a hospital?","o":["Exit signs only","Major electrical equipment necessary for patient care and basic hospital operation, arranged for delayed automatic or manual connection","Egress lighting only","Fire alarm only"],"a":1,"e":"The equipment system supplies major equipment necessary for patient care and basic operation, with delayed automatic or manual connection.","r":"NEC 517.34","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"The life safety branch of the essential electrical system in a hospital must also meet the requirements of:","o":["Article 701","Article 702","Article 708","Article 700, except as amended by Article 517"],"a":3,"e":"The life safety branch must meet Article 700 requirements except as amended by Article 517.","r":"NEC 517.26","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":1,"q":"In a hospital, exit signs and exit directional signs are supplied from which part of the essential electrical system?","o":["Normal system only","Critical branch","Equipment system","Life safety branch"],"a":3,"e":"Illumination of means of egress and exit signs are on the life safety branch.","r":"NEC 517.32","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"The hospital fire alarm system is connected to which part of the essential electrical system?","o":["Normal system only","Equipment system, delayed automatic connection","Critical branch","Life safety branch"],"a":3,"e":"Alarm and alerting systems, including fire alarms, are on the life safety branch.","r":"NEC 517.32","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":1,"q":"Illumination of the means of egress in a hospital is supplied from which branch?","o":["Normal system only","Equipment system","Life safety branch","Critical branch"],"a":2,"e":"Illumination of means of egress is a life safety branch load.","r":"NEC 517.32(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Elevator cab lighting, control, communications, and signal systems in a hospital are connected to:","o":["Normal system only","Life safety branch","Critical branch","Equipment system, manual connection only"],"a":1,"e":"Elevator cab lighting, control, communications, and signal systems are life safety branch loads.","r":"NEC 517.32(F)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Automatically operated doors used for building egress in a hospital are supplied from the:","o":["Life safety branch","Normal system only","Critical branch","Equipment system"],"a":0,"e":"Automatically operated doors used for building egress are on the life safety branch.","r":"NEC 517.32(G)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"At the generator set and essential transfer switch locations in a hospital, which of the following is on the life safety branch?","o":["Task illumination, battery charger for battery-powered lighting units, and selected receptacles","The cafeteria lighting","The kitchen refrigeration","The entire plant HVAC system"],"a":0,"e":"Task illumination, battery charger for battery-powered lighting units, and select receptacles at the generator and transfer switch locations are life safety branch loads.","r":"NEC 517.32(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Alarms required for nonflammable medical gas systems are served from the:","o":["Critical branch","Equipment system","Life safety branch","Normal system"],"a":2,"e":"Alarm and alerting systems, including those required for nonflammable medical gas systems, are on the life safety branch.","r":"NEC 517.32(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":1,"q":"A hospital nurse call system is supplied from which part of the essential electrical system?","o":["Life safety branch","Not part of the essential system","Equipment system","Critical branch"],"a":3,"e":"Nurse call systems are critical branch loads.","r":"NEC 517.33(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Task illumination and selected receptacles in infant nurseries are supplied from the:","o":["Normal system only","Equipment system, delayed","Life safety branch","Critical branch"],"a":3,"e":"Infant nurseries, medication preparation areas, and pharmacy dispensing areas are on the critical branch.","r":"NEC 517.33(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Blood, bone, and tissue banks in a hospital are connected to:","o":["None of the essential system","Critical branch","Equipment system","Life safety branch"],"a":1,"e":"Blood, bone, and tissue banks are critical branch loads.","r":"NEC 517.33(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Telephone equipment rooms and closets in a hospital are connected to which branch?","o":["Life safety branch","Critical branch","Normal system only","Equipment system"],"a":1,"e":"Telephone equipment rooms and closets are critical branch loads.","r":"NEC 517.33(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Task illumination, selected receptacles, and power circuits for intensive care units, cardiac catheterization labs, and hemodialysis rooms are on the:","o":["Equipment system","Life safety branch","Critical branch","Normal system only"],"a":2,"e":"These patient care areas are listed under the critical branch.","r":"NEC 517.33(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"In a psychiatric bed area of a hospital, the critical branch provides task illumination and selected receptacles. What is the rule about receptacles?","o":["Receptacles must be at 7 ft","Receptacles are 20-amp tamper-resistant only","Four receptacles are required","Receptacles are omitted in psychiatric bed areas"],"a":3,"e":"The critical branch list includes psychiatric bed areas with receptacles omitted.","r":"NEC 517.33(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"For general care beds, the critical branch must supply at least how many receptacles per patient bedroom?","o":["Fourteen duplex receptacles","At least one duplex receptacle","Four duplex receptacles","None"],"a":1,"e":"The critical branch supplies task illumination and selected receptacles, including at least one duplex receptacle per patient bedroom for general care beds.","r":"NEC 517.33(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Central suction systems serving medical and surgical functions in a hospital are connected to:","o":["Critical branch, 10 seconds","Normal system only","Equipment system, delayed automatic connection","Life safety branch"],"a":2,"e":"Central suction systems serving medical and surgical functions are equipment system loads with delayed automatic connection.","r":"NEC 517.34(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Smoke control and stair pressurization systems in a hospital are on which part of the essential system?","o":["Equipment system, delayed automatic connection","Not required to be connected","Life safety branch","Critical branch"],"a":0,"e":"Smoke control and stair pressurization systems are equipment system loads arranged for delayed automatic connection.","r":"NEC 517.34(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Kitchen hood supply or exhaust systems required to operate during a fire in or under the hood are connected to:","o":["Equipment system, delayed automatic connection","Life safety branch","Normal system","Critical branch"],"a":0,"e":"These kitchen hood systems are in the equipment system's delayed automatic group.","r":"NEC 517.34(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Compressed air systems serving medical and surgical functions in a hospital are connected to:","o":["Critical branch","Life safety branch","Equipment system, delayed automatic connection","Not on the essential system"],"a":2,"e":"Compressed air systems serving medical and surgical functions are equipment system loads.","r":"NEC 517.34(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"Heating equipment for patient rooms in a hospital, where required, may be connected to the equipment system using:","o":["Delayed automatic or manual connection","Connection within 10 seconds only","No connection","The life safety branch only"],"a":0,"e":"Heating for operating, delivery, labor, recovery, intensive care, nurseries, isolation rooms, emergency treatment spaces and general patient rooms is in the delayed automatic or manual connection group.","r":"NEC 517.34(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"Elevator service (motors) in a hospital that would otherwise strand passengers between floors is connected to:","o":["Not required to be connected","Equipment system, delayed automatic or manual connection","Life safety branch, 10 seconds","Critical branch"],"a":1,"e":"Elevator service so that elevators can be operated for the release of passengers is in the equipment system's delayed automatic or manual group.","r":"NEC 517.34(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"Which of these must be restored within 10 seconds of loss of normal power in a hospital?","o":["Kitchen hood exhaust","All normal loads","Equipment system loads only","Life safety branch and critical branch loads"],"a":3,"e":"The emergency system (life safety and critical branches) must be available within 10 seconds; equipment system loads are delayed.","r":"NEC 517.31","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"In a wet procedure location, receptacles and fixed equipment must have GFCI protection if power interruption on first ground fault can be tolerated. If it cannot be tolerated, what is required?","o":["An AFCI breaker","A larger breaker","They must be served from an isolated power system","A bonding jumper"],"a":2,"e":"Where interruption cannot be tolerated, an isolated power system is required instead of GFCI.","r":"NEC 517.20(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"At what total hazard current must a line isolation monitor in an isolated power system alarm?","o":["50 mA","30 mA","0.5 mA","5 mA"],"a":3,"e":"The LIM energizes the red lamp and audible alarm when total hazard current reaches 5 mA under nominal line voltage conditions.","r":"NEC 517.160(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A line isolation monitor in a hospital isolated power system must not alarm for a fault hazard current of less than:","o":["5 mA","3.7 mA","1 mA","0.5 mA"],"a":1,"e":"The LIM must not alarm for a fault hazard current of less than 3.7 mA, and must alarm at a total hazard current of 5 mA.","r":"NEC 517.160(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"On a line isolation monitor, what indicates that the system is adequately isolated from ground?","o":["A green signal lamp, conspicuously visible, remains lighted","A blue lamp","A flashing red lamp","An audible tone"],"a":0,"e":"A green lamp remains lit when adequately isolated; an adjacent red lamp and audible signal energize at the threshold.","r":"NEC 517.160(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"In a single-phase isolated power system, isolated circuit conductor No. 1 and No. 2 are identified by which colors?","o":["Black and red","Green and yellow","White and gray","Orange with a distinctive stripe, and brown with a distinctive stripe"],"a":3,"e":"Isolated conductor No. 1 is orange with a stripe other than white, green, or gray; No. 2 is brown with a stripe.","r":"NEC 517.160(A)(5)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"For a three-phase isolated power system, the third conductor must be:","o":["Blue","Black","Red","Yellow with a distinctive colored stripe other than white, green, or gray"],"a":3,"e":"The third conductor of a three-phase isolated system is yellow with a distinctive stripe.","r":"NEC 517.160(A)(5)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"The nominal voltage between conductors of each isolated power system circuit must not exceed:","o":["1000 volts","250 volts","120 volts","600 volts"],"a":3,"e":"Isolated circuits are limited to 600 volts between conductors.","r":"NEC 517.160(A)(1)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"The disconnecting means for a diagnostic x-ray unit must have a capacity of not less than:","o":["25 percent of the momentary rating","10 percent of the long-time rating","100 percent of the momentary rating only","50 percent of the momentary rating or 100 percent of the long-time rating, whichever is greater"],"a":3,"e":"The disconnect must be rated at least 50 percent of the input for the momentary rating or 100 percent for the long-time rating, whichever is greater.","r":"NEC 517.72(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"The ampacity of the supply branch-circuit conductors and the OCPD rating for diagnostic x-ray equipment must be not less than:","o":["25 percent of the momentary rating","100 percent of the momentary rating","50 percent of the momentary rating or 100 percent of the long-time rating, whichever is greater","200 percent of the long-time rating"],"a":2,"e":"For diagnostic x-ray equipment, conductors and OCPD are sized at 50 percent of the momentary rating or 100 percent of the long-time rating, whichever is greater.","r":"NEC 517.73(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A diagnostic x-ray unit has a momentary rating of 100 amperes and a long-time rating of 30 amperes. What is the minimum ampacity of its supply conductors?","o":["100 amperes","50 amperes","30 amperes","25 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"50 percent of the momentary rating (50 A) exceeds 100 percent of the long-time rating (30 A), so 50 A governs.","r":"NEC 517.73(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A diagnostic x-ray unit has a momentary rating of 40 amperes and a long-time rating of 25 amperes. What is the minimum disconnecting means capacity?","o":["40 amperes","25 amperes","12.5 amperes","20 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"50 percent of momentary is 20 A; 100 percent of long-time is 25 A. The greater, 25 A, governs.","r":"NEC 517.72(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"For therapeutic x-ray equipment, supply conductors and OCPDs must be sized at not less than:","o":["250 percent of the rating","50 percent of the rating","100 percent of the current rating of the equipment","125 percent of the rating"],"a":2,"e":"Therapeutic equipment's supply conductors and OCPD are rated at not less than 100 percent of the equipment's current rating.","r":"NEC 517.73(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"The disconnecting means for an x-ray unit must be located:","o":["In the attic","At the service entrance only","Outside the building","Operable from a location readily accessible from the x-ray control"],"a":3,"e":"The disconnecting means is operable from a location readily accessible from the x-ray control.","r":"NEC 517.72(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A portable x-ray unit is connected to a 120-volt, 30-ampere branch circuit. Which may serve as the disconnecting means?","o":["A snap switch","A wire connector","A grounding-type attachment plug and receptacle of proper rating","A fuse puller"],"a":2,"e":"For equipment on a 120-volt branch circuit of 30 amperes or less, a grounding-type attachment plug and receptacle of proper rating may serve as the disconnecting means.","r":"NEC 517.72(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Transformers and capacitors that are part of x-ray equipment:","o":["Must be installed in a vault","Must meet Article 450 vault requirements","Need not comply with Articles 450 and 460","Must be in an isolated power system"],"a":2,"e":"Transformers and capacitors that are part of x-ray equipment are not required to comply with Articles 450 and 460.","r":"NEC 517.76","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Non-current-carrying metal parts of x-ray equipment, such as control stands, tables, and tube supports, must be:","o":["Left floating","Bonded only to the gas piping","Grounded only if over 600 volts","Connected to an equipment grounding conductor per Part VI of Article 250"],"a":3,"e":"Non-current-carrying metal parts of x-ray and associated equipment are connected to an equipment grounding conductor per Part VI of Article 250.","r":"NEC 517.78","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"Emergency system wiring is installed in Schedule 40 PVC conduit in a hospital. What additional protection is required?","o":["Encasement in not less than 2 in. of concrete","A 12 in. steel sleeve","Encasement in 6 in. of sand","None"],"a":0,"e":"Schedule 40 PVC, flexible nonmetallic or jacketed metallic raceways, or Type MC cable may be used where encased in not less than 50 mm (2 in.) of concrete.","r":"NEC 517.30(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Communications systems used to issue instructions during emergency conditions in a hospital are on the:","o":["Equipment system","Life safety branch","Critical branch","Normal system"],"a":1,"e":"Communications systems used to issue instructions during emergencies are life safety branch loads.","r":"NEC 517.32(D)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"In an interventional procedure room that is a wet procedure location, interruption of power on the first ground fault can be tolerated. What protection is required for receptacles and fixed equipment?","o":["AFCI protection","An isolated power system only","No special protection","GFCI protection"],"a":3,"e":"If interruption can be tolerated, GFCI protection is used in wet procedure locations; if not, an isolated power system.","r":"NEC 517.20(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"Routine mopping of a patient room floor and incidental spills occur regularly. Does this make the room a wet procedure location?","o":["No, routine housekeeping and incidental spillage do not define a wet procedure location","Yes, if mopping is daily","Yes, if a sink is present","Yes, any wet floor qualifies"],"a":0,"e":"Routine housekeeping procedures and incidental spillage do not define a wet procedure location.","r":"NEC 517.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A nurses' station is not adequately lit by corridor luminaires. Under the critical branch list, what is supplied from the critical branch?","o":["Nothing; nurses' stations are unclassified","Task illumination and selected receptacles","Only the call buttons","Only the exit sign"],"a":1,"e":"Task illumination and selected receptacles in nurses' stations are critical branch loads unless adequately lighted by corridor luminaires.","r":"NEC 517.33(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"An electrician proposes to run a normal-system lighting circuit in the same raceway as life safety branch conductors to save conduit. Is this acceptable?","o":["Yes, if both are 120 volts","Yes, if tagged","Yes, if sized properly","No, the life safety and critical branches must be kept independent of all other wiring, not entering the same raceway, box, or cabinet"],"a":3,"e":"Life safety and critical branch wiring must be kept entirely independent of other wiring, with limited listed exceptions.","r":"NEC 517.30(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A general care bed location is fed by two normal-system branch circuits from two different panelboards and one emergency circuit. What is wrong?","o":["All normal system branch circuits serving the bed location must originate in the same panelboard","There must be three emergency circuits","The emergency circuit must be 30 amperes","Nothing is wrong"],"a":0,"e":"All normal-system branch circuits serving the bed must come from the same panelboard.","r":"NEC 517.18(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A hospital designates its intensive care unit as a Category 1 space. How many receptacles must each ICU patient bed location have at minimum?","o":["6","4","10","14"],"a":3,"e":"Category 1 bed locations require a minimum of 14 receptacles.","r":"NEC 517.19(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"After a utility outage, exit signs are lit in 8 seconds and a kitchen hood exhaust fan starts after a delay of 30 seconds. Which statement is correct?","o":["Both are acceptable; the life safety branch must be within 10 seconds, and the equipment system may be delayed","The exit signs violate the delayed rule","Both violate the code","The fan violates the 10-second rule"],"a":0,"e":"The emergency system (life safety and critical branches) must restore within 10 seconds; equipment system loads connect on delayed automatic or manual connection.","r":"NEC 517.31, 517.34","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A line isolation monitor in an operating room indicates a total hazard current of 5 mA. What occurs?","o":["Only the green lamp lights","The circuit trips","Nothing happens until 10 mA","The red lamp and audible alarm are energized"],"a":3,"e":"At 5 mA total hazard current, the red lamp and audible warning signal energize.","r":"NEC 517.160(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":1,"q":"The essential electrical system in a hospital is intended to supply:","o":["Only the generator room","Power to designated areas and functions during interruption of the normal source","Only the elevators","Only the lighting of the lobby"],"a":1,"e":"Essential electrical systems provide designated areas and functions with alternate power when the normal source is interrupted.","r":"NEC 517.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":1,"q":"A Category 4 (support) space is one in which failure of equipment or a system is:","o":["Not likely to have an impact on patient care","Likely to cause major injury","Likely to cause death","Likely to cause minor injury"],"a":0,"e":"Category 4 support spaces are those where failure is not likely to have an impact on patient care.","r":"NEC 517.2","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"An isolated power system, as used in Article 517, is a system comprising:","o":["An isolating transformer or equivalent, a line isolation monitor, and its ungrounded circuit conductors","A UPS and a battery","A generator and a transfer switch","A grounded wye service and a GFCI"],"a":0,"e":"An isolated power system consists of an isolating transformer (or equivalent), a line isolation monitor, and ungrounded circuit conductors.","r":"NEC 517.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"A patient bed location is defined as the location of:","o":["The nurses' station","A corridor gurney parking area","A patient sleeping bed, or the bed or procedure table of a Category 1 space","Any chair in a waiting room"],"a":2,"e":"Patient bed location means the location of a patient sleeping bed or the bed or procedure table of a Category 1 space.","r":"NEC 517.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A fixed electrical equipment frame operating at over 100 volts in a patient care area is likely to become energized and is subject to personal contact. What grounding provision is required?","o":["No grounding because the equipment is fixed","Connection to the metal raceway only","Connection to a ground rod only","Connection to an insulated copper equipment grounding conductor installed in the metal raceway with the branch-circuit conductors"],"a":3,"e":"Non-current-carrying conductive surfaces of fixed equipment over 100 volts subject to contact must be connected to an insulated copper equipment grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 517.13(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"MC cable is used for a branch circuit serving a patient room. Which condition must its armor or sheath meet?","o":["No condition applies","It must qualify as an equipment grounding conductor under 250.118","It must be aluminum only","It must be nonmetallic"],"a":1,"e":"A metallic armor or sheath cable used in patient care spaces must itself qualify as an EGC per 250.118, in addition to the insulated copper EGC.","r":"NEC 517.13(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Selected receptacles and task illumination in pharmacy dispensing areas are on the:","o":["Life safety branch","Critical branch","Equipment system","Normal system only"],"a":1,"e":"Pharmacy dispensing areas are listed under critical branch task illumination and selected receptacles.","r":"NEC 517.33(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Angiographic laboratories are supplied from which branch for task illumination, selected receptacles, and power circuits?","o":["Not supplied by the essential system","Equipment system","Life safety branch","Critical branch"],"a":3,"e":"Angiographic laboratories are in the critical branch list.","r":"NEC 517.33(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Ward treatment rooms in a hospital have task illumination and selected receptacles on the:","o":["Equipment system, delayed","Life safety branch","Normal system only","Critical branch"],"a":3,"e":"Ward treatment rooms are listed critical branch locations.","r":"NEC 517.33(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Postoperative recovery rooms have selected task illumination, receptacles, and power circuits on the:","o":["Equipment system","Normal system only","Life safety branch","Critical branch"],"a":3,"e":"Postoperative recovery rooms (selected) are on the critical branch.","r":"NEC 517.33(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Sump pumps and other equipment required to operate for the safety of major apparatus in a hospital are connected to the:","o":["Equipment system, delayed automatic connection","None of the essential systems","Life safety branch","Critical branch"],"a":0,"e":"Sump pumps and equipment required for safety of major apparatus are equipment system loads.","r":"NEC 517.34(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"Which of the following is correctly assigned to the equipment system of a hospital rather than the life safety branch?","o":["Exit signs","Elevator cab lighting","Fire alarm","Kitchen hood exhaust required to operate during a fire"],"a":3,"e":"Exit signs, fire alarm, and elevator cab lighting are life safety branch loads; kitchen hood exhaust is an equipment system load.","r":"NEC 517.32, 517.34","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"Which of the following is correctly assigned to the critical branch rather than the equipment system?","o":["Smoke control system","Central suction system","Sump pump","Nurse call system"],"a":3,"e":"Nurse call is a critical branch load; central suction, smoke control, and sump pumps are equipment system loads.","r":"NEC 517.33, 517.34","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A Category 1 bed location is supplied by one normal-system circuit and one emergency-system circuit that supplies outlets at that bed and also at the adjacent bed. What is the issue?","o":["At least one emergency circuit must supply outlets only at that bed location","The normal circuit must supply both beds","No issue","The emergency circuit must be 15 amperes"],"a":0,"e":"At least one emergency branch circuit must supply an outlet or outlets only at that bed location.","r":"NEC 517.19(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A pediatric ward playroom receptacle is a standard non-tamper-resistant receptacle with no cover. Is this compliant?","o":["Yes, if 20-amp","Yes, if hospital grade","Yes, if GFCI protected","No, it must be listed tamper-resistant or have a listed tamper-resistant cover"],"a":3,"e":"Pediatric locations including playrooms and activity rooms require listed tamper-resistant receptacles or covers.","r":"NEC 517.18(C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A hospital has a 4000-ampere, 480Y/277-volt service with ground-fault protection. The feeder to the patient wing is the next downstream level. What is required of that feeder disconnect?","o":["A GFCI receptacle","A neutral disconnect","No ground-fault protection","An additional step of ground-fault protection, selectively coordinated with at least six cycles of separation"],"a":3,"e":"The next-level feeder disconnecting means needs additional ground-fault protection, fully selective with at least a six-cycle separation.","r":"NEC 517.17(B) and (C)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A hospital's maximum demand on its essential electrical system is 120 kVA. How many transfer switches are required?","o":["Five","One transfer switch may serve all branches and systems","Two","Three, one for each branch"],"a":1,"e":"One transfer switch may serve one or more branches or systems in a facility with a maximum essential system demand of 150 kVA.","r":"NEC 517.30(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"Which statement about ventilation for airborne infectious isolation rooms in a hospital is correct?","o":["They must restore within 10 seconds","They are not part of the essential system","They are life safety branch loads","Supply, return, and exhaust ventilating systems for these rooms are equipment system loads with delayed automatic connection"],"a":3,"e":"Ventilating systems for airborne infectious/isolation rooms are in the equipment system delayed automatic group.","r":"NEC 517.34(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"The first ground fault occurs on an isolated power system supplying an operating room. Compared with a grounded system, what is the result?","o":["The breaker trips immediately","The system continues to operate, and the LIM signals an alarm when hazard current reaches the threshold","The transformer opens","The system loses power"],"a":1,"e":"Isolated power systems do not trip on a first ground fault; the line isolation monitor warns of reduced isolation when hazard current reaches 5 mA.","r":"NEC 517.160","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","d":2,"q":"Where may battery chargers and their control equipment be located in an aircraft hangar?","o":["Within the pit below the hangar floor","Anywhere if mounted 18 in. above the floor","Within the 5 ft vicinity of the aircraft engines","Not within any Class I location defined for the hangar"],"a":3,"e":"Battery chargers and their control equipment shall not be located or operated within any Class I location of the hangar, and are preferably in a separate building or an unclassified area.","r":"NEC 513.10(C)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Selected task illumination, receptacles, and power circuits in emergency room treatment areas are supplied from which part of the essential electrical system?","o":["Critical branch","Normal system only","Equipment system","Life safety branch"],"a":0,"e":"Emergency room treatment areas (selected) are on the critical branch.","r":"NEC 517.33(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Coronary care units have task illumination, selected receptacles, and power circuits supplied from the:","o":["Equipment system, delayed automatic","Life safety branch","Normal system only","Critical branch"],"a":3,"e":"Coronary care units are listed under the critical branch.","r":"NEC 517.33(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Medication preparation areas in a hospital have task illumination and selected receptacles on the:","o":["Normal system only","Equipment system","Critical branch","Life safety branch"],"a":2,"e":"Medication preparation areas are critical branch loads.","r":"NEC 517.33(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Human physiology laboratories are supplied from which part of the essential electrical system for task illumination, selected receptacles, and power circuits?","o":["Equipment system","Normal system only","Critical branch","Life safety branch"],"a":2,"e":"Human physiology labs are listed under the critical branch.","r":"NEC 517.33(E)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":2,"q":"Which statement defines a Category 2 space?","o":["A space where failure of equipment or a system is likely to cause minor injury to patients, staff, or visitors","A space with no patients","A space where failure causes no impact on patient care","A space where failure is likely to cause major injury or death"],"a":0,"e":"Category 2 is general care, with minor injury likely upon failure; Category 1 is major injury or death.","r":"NEC 517.2","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":1,"q":"Flexible cords and cables that supply portable stage equipment must generally be which type under the general rule for conductors for portables?","o":["Type NM cable","Listed extra-hard usage cords or cables","Listed hard usage (junior hard service) cords or cables","Any listed flexible cord with ampacity at least equal to the load"],"a":1,"e":"The general rule in 520.68(A)(1) is listed extra-hard usage cords or cables. Hard usage cord is allowed only in the specific protected applications and limited uses listed in 520.68(A)(2) through (A)(4).","r":"NEC 520.68(A)(1)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":2,"q":"Under what conditions may listed hard usage (junior hard service) cord be used for portable stage lighting equipment?","o":["When it is on a circuit protected at not more than 30 amperes and does not exceed 100 ft, regardless of physical protection","When it is on a circuit protected at not more than 20 amperes and does not exceed 200 ft, with no physical protection required","Whenever the circuit is GFCI protected, with no limit on length or ampere rating","When it is protected from physical damage by attachment over its entire length to a support such as a pipe or truss, is on a circuit protected at not more than 20 amperes, and does not exceed 30 m (100 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"520.68(A)(2) permits hard usage cord in protected applications only if it is protected from physical damage over its entire length, is on a circuit of 20 amperes or less, and is not over 30 m (100 ft).","r":"NEC 520.68(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":3,"q":"A 20-ampere stage circuit supplies a luminaire that has a listed hard usage supply cord attached to it. The cord is 8 ft long. Is this cord length permitted under the portable-equipment rules of Article 520?","o":["Yes, any length is permitted on a 20-ampere circuit","Yes, up to 3 m (10 ft) is permitted","No, the luminaire supply cord of hard usage type may not be longer than 2.0 m (6.6 ft)","No, hard usage cord is never permitted for a luminaire supply cord"],"a":2,"e":"520.68(A)(4) permits a listed hard usage luminaire supply cord only if it is not longer than 2.0 m (6.6 ft) and is protected by an overcurrent device of not more than 20 amperes. An 8 ft cord exceeds the limit.","r":"NEC 520.68(A)(4)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":2,"q":"A special-purpose multicircuit cable system (a trunk cable carrying several branch circuits) is used on a stage. What is the maximum rating of each branch circuit it supplies?","o":["20 amperes and not more than 150 volts to ground","30 amperes and not more than 150 volts to ground","20 amperes and not more than 300 volts to ground","15 amperes and not more than 120 volts line to line"],"a":0,"e":"520.68(D) limits branch circuits in special-purpose multicircuit cable systems to not more than 20 amperes and not more than 150 volts to ground, and requires installation by qualified persons.","r":"NEC 520.68(D)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":1,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, portable power distribution units used for stage and studio purposes must be what?","o":["Approved only by the manufacturer of the connected luminaires","Listed","Rated for outdoor use only","Built on site by any qualified person with no listing required"],"a":1,"e":"The 2023 NEC requires portable power distribution units to be listed (520.62).","r":"NEC 520.62","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":2,"q":"A portable stage switchboard is supplied from a 3-phase, 4-wire system. The largest ungrounded supply terminal has an ampacity of 400 amperes. What is the minimum ampacity required for the neutral supply terminal?","o":["600 amperes","800 amperes","200 amperes","400 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"520.53 requires the neutral terminal of a 3-phase, 4-wire portable stage switchboard to have an ampacity at least twice that of the largest ungrounded supply terminal, so 2 x 400 = 800 amperes.","r":"NEC 520.53","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":2,"q":"A stage dimmer is rated 40 amperes and is supplied through its ungrounded conductor by an overcurrent device. What is the maximum rating of that overcurrent device?","o":["60 amperes","50 amperes","80 amperes","40 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"520.25(A) requires overcurrent protection in the ungrounded conductor of not greater than 125 percent of the dimmer rating: 40 x 1.25 = 50 amperes.","r":"NEC 520.25(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":1,"q":"Which statement about a fixed stage switchboard is correct under Article 520?","o":["It must supply stage equipment only and may never supply non-stage loads","It must be listed and is permitted to supply both stage equipment and non-stage equipment","It is not required to be listed if it is dead front","It must be installed on the stage floor in view of the audience"],"a":1,"e":"520.21 requires fixed stage switchboards to be listed and permits them to supply both stage and non-stage equipment.","r":"NEC 520.21","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":1,"q":"Which of the following may NOT be installed in a theater dressing room?","o":["A wall-mounted luminaire beside the mirror","A switch-controlled receptacle at the makeup counter","A lamp with a suitable guard where it is exposed","Pendant lampholders"],"a":3,"e":"520.71 prohibits pendant lampholders in dressing rooms. The other items are not prohibited by that section.","r":"NEC 520.71","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":2,"q":"All lights and receptacles in a theater dressing room are controlled by a wall switch in the room. Where is the required pilot light for the switch controlling the receptacles located?","o":["In a conspicuous location outside the dressing room","At the main stage switchboard only","No pilot light is required for dressing rooms","Inside the dressing room, on the mirror"],"a":0,"e":"520.73 requires wall switches in the dressing room to control the lights and receptacles, with a pilot light in a conspicuous location outside the room to show when the receptacles are energized.","r":"NEC 520.73","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":2,"q":"What is the general maximum rating of a branch circuit supplying footlights, border lights, or proscenium sidelights?","o":["20 amperes (circuits rated over 20 amperes only where they supply heavy-duty lampholders, per Article 210)","15 amperes with no exceptions","30 amperes for any lampholder","50 amperes for any stage lighting"],"a":0,"e":"520.41 limits these branch circuits to 20 amperes, with circuits over 20 amperes permitted only for heavy-duty lampholders as allowed by Article 210.","r":"NEC 520.41","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":2,"q":"A primary stage switchboard is supplied by separate feeders from the building. What identification is required on the switchboard?","o":["No label, because the feeders are separate","A label showing only the voltage of the feeders","A label showing the manufacturer name of each feeder cable","A permanent, obvious label showing the number and location of the disconnects"],"a":3,"e":"520.27(A)(3) requires the switchboard to be permanently and obviously labeled with the number and location of the disconnects when separate feeders supply one primary stage switchboard.","r":"NEC 520.27(A)(3)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 520 Theaters and similar locations","d":3,"q":"A 20-ampere circuit supplies stage luminaires using listed hard usage cord. The cord is attached over its entire length to a truss and is 120 ft long. Is the installation permitted?","o":["Yes, because the cord is attached to the truss over its entire length","No, because hard usage cord is never allowed on a truss","No, because hard usage cord in protected applications is limited to 30 m (100 ft)","Yes, because the circuit is 20 amperes or less"],"a":2,"e":"Even when protected by attachment over its entire length and on a circuit of 20 amperes or less, hard usage cord under 520.68(A)(2) may not exceed 30 m (100 ft).","r":"NEC 520.68(A)(2)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 530 Motion Picture and Television Studios","d":1,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, Article 530 applies to motion picture and television studios in facilities and locations that are staffed by whom?","o":["Any crew member regardless of training","Qualified persons","The general public","Licensed fire protection engineers only"],"a":1,"e":"The revised scope of Article 530 applies to studios and locations staffed by qualified persons. Audience areas fall under the stricter rules of Article 520.","r":"NEC 530.1","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 530 Motion Picture and Television Studios","d":1,"q":"The 2023 NEC defines a remote location in connection with Article 530 as what?","o":["A location more than 1 mile from the utility service","A studio sound stage with no permanent wiring","A temporary carnival midway used for filming","A location, other than a motion picture or television studio, where a production is filmed or recorded"],"a":3,"e":"The Article 100 definition of Remote Location is a location other than a motion picture or television studio where a production is filmed or recorded.","r":"NEC Article 100 (Remote Location)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 530 Motion Picture and Television Studios","d":1,"q":"Which description matches the Article 100 definition of support areas used in Article 530?","o":["Fixed production offices with permanent wiring","Areas, other than fixed production offices, intended to support production and where image capture will not take place","Audience seating areas of a television studio","Areas where portable equipment is used to capture images"],"a":1,"e":"Support areas are areas, other than fixed production offices, that support production and where image capture does not take place. Production areas are where image capture occurs.","r":"NEC Article 100 (Support Areas)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 530 Motion Picture and Television Studios","d":1,"q":"Which description matches the Article 100 definition of production areas used in Article 530?","o":["Areas where portable electrical equipment covered by Article 530 is used to implement the capture of images","Areas used only for catering and crew parking","Fixed production offices","Areas where only parked production vehicles are stored"],"a":0,"e":"Production areas are where portable electrical equipment covered by Article 530 is used to capture images. Support areas are where image capture will not take place.","r":"NEC Article 100 (Production Areas)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 530 Motion Picture and Television Studios","d":1,"q":"The 2023 NEC added a new Part III to Article 530. What does it cover?","o":["Portable equipment in support areas","Audience areas of television studios","Electric signs at studio entrances","Fixed wiring in permanent theaters"],"a":0,"e":"The 2023 revision of Article 530 added Part III covering portable equipment in support areas, including items such as portable generators and production vehicles.","r":"NEC 530 Part III","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 530 Motion Picture and Television Studios","d":2,"q":"A special-purpose multicircuit cable system is used on a studio set. What limits apply to its branch circuits?","o":["Not more than 15 amperes and 50 volts to ground","Not more than 30 amperes and 300 volts to ground","Not more than 20 amperes and not more than 150 volts to ground, as required by 520.68(D)","No limit if installed by a qualified person"],"a":2,"e":"Article 530 sends special-purpose multicircuit cable systems to 520.68(D), which limits branch circuits to 20 amperes and 150 volts to ground and requires qualified installers.","r":"NEC 530.22, 520.68(D)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":2,"q":"Flexible cords and cables at a fair are accessible to the general public. Which statement is correct?","o":["They may lie loose on the walking surface because they are listed for extra-hard usage","They may be buried only at the depths in Table 300.5","They must be run overhead at least 18 ft above the ground in every case","They must be arranged to minimize the tripping hazard and may be covered with nonconductive matting secured to the walking surface or buried, and the burial depth rules of 300.5 do not apply"],"a":3,"e":"525.20 requires cords accessible to the public to be arranged to minimize tripping hazards; they may be covered with secured nonconductive matting or buried, and the depth requirements of 300.5 do not apply.","r":"NEC 525.20","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":2,"q":"At a county fair, which circuit must NOT be protected by a ground-fault circuit interrupter?","o":["A receptacle used for disassembly and reassembly of a ride","A 125-volt, 20-ampere nonlocking receptacle accessible to the public","Equipment accessible to the public supplied by a 125-volt, 15-ampere branch circuit","A circuit supplying egress lighting"],"a":3,"e":"525.23 requires GFCI protection for the listed receptacles and public-accessible equipment, but egress lighting must not be GFCI protected so that a ground-fault trip cannot leave an exit path dark.","r":"NEC 525.23","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":1,"q":"Which of the following must be bonded to the equipment grounding conductor at a carnival?","o":["Nothing, because portable equipment is not required to be bonded","Only the service neutral at the generator","Only the metal parts of rides that are painted","Metal frames and metal parts of rides, concessions, trailers, trucks and other equipment that contain or support electrical equipment"],"a":3,"e":"525.30 requires bonding of metal raceways, metal-sheathed cables, metal enclosures of electrical equipment, and metal frames and parts of rides, concessions, trailers and trucks that contain or support electrical equipment.","r":"NEC 525.30","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":2,"q":"Flexible cords and cables used outdoors to supply a carnival ride must be listed for what?","o":["Any usage rating if the cord is run overhead","Hard usage only","Extra-hard usage, and for wet locations and sunlight resistance","Hard usage and sunlight resistance only"],"a":2,"e":"525.20(A) requires listed extra-hard usage flexible cords and cables; for outdoor use they must also be listed for wet locations and be sunlight resistant.","r":"NEC 525.20(A)","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs and Similar Events","d":1,"q":"Which describes the equipment grounding conductor for portable cord-connected equipment at a carnival?","o":["It may be a separate bare wire taped to the outside of the cord","It must be a type recognized in 250.118 and installed with the circuit conductors","It is not required where a ground rod is driven at the ride","It may be the grounded (neutral) conductor of the cord"],"a":1,"e":"525.31 requires equipment grounding conductors of a type identified in 250.118, run with the circuit conductors. The grounded conductor cannot serve as the equipment grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 525.31","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 547 Agricultural Buildings","d":2,"q":"A farm building has an office and lunchroom attached to and physically adjoining a livestock confinement area. Under the 2023 NEC, which wiring practice is prohibited in the office and lunchroom?","o":["Installing GFCI receptacles in the office","Using any wiring method in exposed locations","Concealing nonmetallic cable within the walls and above the ceilings","Installing a metal raceway in the walls"],"a":2,"e":"547.26 does not permit nonmetallic cables to be concealed in walls or above ceilings of buildings contiguous with or physically adjoined to livestock confinement areas, because rodents damage them.","r":"NEC 547.26","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 547 Agricultural Buildings","d":2,"q":"A concrete-floored poultry house has accessible metallic equipment that may become energized. Under the 2023 NEC, is an equipotential plane required for the poultry area?","o":["Yes, in every confinement area with a concrete floor","Yes, but only outdoors","No, because livestock does not include poultry for the purposes of the equipotential plane requirement","Yes, if the house has more than 1000 birds"],"a":2,"e":"547.44 requires equipotential planes in livestock confinement areas, but for this section livestock excludes poultry.","r":"NEC 547.44(A)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 547 Agricultural Buildings","d":2,"q":"A luminaire in an agricultural building is exposed to water from condensation and from washdown. What is required?","o":["It must be explosion-proof","It must be listed for wet locations","It may be a standard dry-location luminaire if it has a lamp guard","It must be mounted at least 12 ft above the floor"],"a":1,"e":"Luminaires in agricultural buildings exposed to condensation or washing water must be listed for wet locations (547.30).","r":"NEC 547.30","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 547 Agricultural Buildings","d":2,"q":"An agricultural building is cleaned by washing down with water. What is required of the enclosures, boxes and fittings in that area?","o":["They may be ordinary indoor enclosures","They must be weatherproof","They need only be painted with corrosion-resistant paint","They must be listed as intrinsically safe"],"a":1,"e":"Where water is used for cleaning in an agricultural building, enclosures, boxes, conduit bodies and fittings must be weatherproof (547.22 through 547.24).","r":"NEC 547.22 to 547.24","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":2,"q":"What is the permitted overall length of a mobile home power-supply cord, measured from the cord entry point to the face of the attachment plug cap?","o":["Not less than 21 ft and not more than 36.5 ft","Not less than 36.5 ft and not more than 50 ft","Not less than 10 ft and not more than 25 ft","Not less than 6 ft and not more than 12 ft"],"a":0,"e":"550.10 requires the power-supply cord to be at least 6.4 m (21 ft) and not more than 11 m (36.5 ft) overall.","r":"NEC 550.10","b":7},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":2,"q":"In a multi-section manufactured home, what is used to bond the chassis of the sections together under the 2023 NEC?","o":["No bonding is needed between sections","A copper conductor sized at 14 AWG","A stranded aluminum conductor not smaller than 12 AWG","A solid copper conductor not smaller than 8 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"550.16(C)(1) requires the chassis of multisection homes to be bonded with a solid 8 AWG copper conductor, with connections made per 250.8 after removing nonconductive coatings.","r":"NEC 550.16(C)(1)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":1,"q":"In a mobile home park, what is the minimum calculated load per mobile home lot used for the park service and feeder calculation?","o":["24,000 VA","8,000 VA","12,000 VA","16,000 VA"],"a":3,"e":"550.31 requires each mobile home lot to be calculated at not less than 16,000 volt-amperes.","r":"NEC 550.31","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":2,"q":"A mobile home park has 25 lots. What demand factor from Table 550.31 applies to the total calculated load?","o":["33 percent","24 percent","55 percent","28 percent"],"a":1,"e":"Table 550.31 gives 24 percent for 22 through 40 mobile homes.","r":"NEC Table 550.31","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":3,"q":"A mobile home park has 40 lots, each calculated at the minimum of 16,000 VA. Using Table 550.31 (24 percent for 22 to 40 homes), what is the demand load of the park?","o":["211,200 VA","83,200 VA","640,000 VA","153,600 VA"],"a":3,"e":"40 x 16,000 VA = 640,000 VA; 640,000 x 0.24 = 153,600 VA, which is 640 amperes at 240 volts.","r":"NEC 550.31, Table 550.31","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":3,"q":"A mobile home park has 5 lots, each calculated at 16,000 VA. Table 550.31 allows a 33 percent demand factor for 5 homes. What is the demand load?","o":["80,000 VA","52,800 VA","26,400 VA","16,000 VA"],"a":2,"e":"5 x 16,000 VA = 80,000 VA; 80,000 x 0.33 = 26,400 VA.","r":"NEC 550.31, Table 550.31","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":1,"q":"What is the nominal secondary distribution system to mobile home lots in a park?","o":["Three-phase, 208Y/120 volts","Single-phase, 120/240 volts","Three-phase, 480 volts","Single-phase, 277 volts"],"a":1,"e":"550.30 specifies that secondary distribution to mobile home lots is single-phase, 120/240 volts, nominal.","r":"NEC 550.30","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":1,"q":"How many conductors does the feeder to a mobile home contain, and how is the equipment grounding conductor identified?","o":["Three insulated conductors, with the neutral serving as the equipment ground","Two conductors plus the raceway as the ground","Four conductors, with a bare equipment grounding conductor","Four insulated conductors, with the equipment grounding conductor green or green with yellow stripes"],"a":3,"e":"550.33 requires a feeder of four continuous, insulated, color-coded conductors, one of which is the green (or green with yellow stripes) equipment grounding conductor.","r":"NEC 550.33","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, where may the feeder disconnect for a manufactured home be installed?","o":["Only inside the bathroom","Nowhere on the home; it must always be remote","Only at the utility pole","In or on the manufactured home, as permitted by 550.33(B)"],"a":3,"e":"The 2023 NEC states that neither service equipment nor the feeder assembly may be mounted in or on a mobile home, but 550.33(B) allows a manufactured home feeder disconnect in or on the home.","r":"NEC 550.32(A), 550.33","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":2,"q":"Which branch circuit in a manufactured home must be arc-fault circuit-interrupter protected under 550.25?","o":["A 120-volt, 15-ampere circuit supplying outlets in a bedroom","A 240-volt electric range circuit","A circuit that supplies only the water heater","No branch circuits are required to have AFCI protection in manufactured homes"],"a":0,"e":"550.25 requires AFCI protection (per 210.12) for 120-volt, 15- and 20-ampere branch circuits supplying outlets or devices in living areas, including bedrooms.","r":"NEC 550.25","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes and Parks","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum number of 20-ampere small-appliance branch circuits required for the kitchen, pantry, and dining receptacle outlets of a manufactured home?","o":["Three","One","Two","Four"],"a":2,"e":"550.12 requires receptacle outlets in the kitchen, pantry, dining and similar areas to be supplied by at least two 20-ampere small-appliance branch circuits.","r":"NEC 550.12","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":2,"q":"An RV park is located beside a tidal body of water. Where is the electrical datum plane for the park?","o":["At the average tide level","2 ft above the highest high tide level that occurs under normal conditions","30 in. above the water level at the pier","12 in. above the highest high tide level"],"a":1,"e":"The 2023 NEC added 551.3, which sets the electrical datum plane in tidal areas 2 ft (606 mm) above the highest high tide level under normal conditions.","r":"NEC 551.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":1,"q":"An RV park is on a lake that is not subject to tidal fluctuation. Where is the electrical datum plane?","o":["At the highest normal water level","6 ft above the lake bed","2 ft above the highest normal water level","12 in. above the highest normal water level"],"a":2,"e":"551.3 places the datum plane 2 ft above the highest normal water level in areas not subject to tidal fluctuation.","r":"NEC 551.3","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":1,"q":"Each RV site with electrical supply must have at least one receptacle of what type?","o":["A 15-ampere, 250-volt receptacle","A 30-ampere, 250-volt receptacle","A 50-ampere, 125/250-volt receptacle","A 20-ampere, 125-volt weather-resistant receptacle"],"a":3,"e":"551.71(A) requires every RV site with electrical supply to have at least one 20-ampere, 125-volt receptacle, which the 2023 NEC requires to be weather resistant.","r":"NEC 551.71(A)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":3,"q":"An RV park has 60 sites with electrical supply. At least how many sites must be equipped with a 30-ampere, 125-volt receptacle?","o":["60","30","42","12"],"a":2,"e":"551.71(B) requires at least 70 percent of RV sites with electrical supply to have a 30-ampere, 125-volt receptacle: 0.70 x 60 = 42.","r":"NEC 551.71(B)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":2,"q":"Under 551.71(F) of the 2023 NEC, ground-fault circuit-interrupter protection for RV site equipment is required for which receptacles?","o":["All receptacles of any rating","Only 50-ampere, 125/250-volt receptacles","Only 30-ampere receptacles","125-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles"],"a":3,"e":"551.71(F)(2) states that GFCI protection is required only for 125-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles in RV site supply equipment.","r":"NEC 551.71(F)(2)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":2,"q":"An RV park distribution system serves 50-ampere, 125/250-volt site receptacles. How must each 50-ampere receptacle be supplied?","o":["From a branch circuit of the same voltage class and rating as the receptacle","From a 30-ampere branch circuit shared with other sites","From a 20-ampere branch circuit with a reducing adapter","From a 120-volt branch circuit using a neutral only"],"a":0,"e":"551.72(C) requires each 50-ampere receptacle to be supplied from a branch circuit of the same voltage class and rating as the receptacle.","r":"NEC 551.72(C)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":2,"q":"In an RV park load calculation, what calculated load is used for each site that has only a 20-ampere supply facility?","o":["600 VA","9,600 VA","2,400 VA","3,600 VA"],"a":2,"e":"551.73(A) uses 2,400 VA for each site with only 20-ampere supply facilities and 3,600 VA for sites with both 20-ampere and 30-ampere supply facilities.","r":"NEC 551.73(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":2,"q":"In an RV park load calculation, what calculated load is used for each dedicated tent site with a 20-ampere supply?","o":["1,200 VA","3,600 VA","2,400 VA","600 VA"],"a":3,"e":"551.73(A) assigns 600 VA to each tent site with a 20-ampere supply.","r":"NEC 551.73(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Parks","d":3,"q":"A small RV park has 6 sites, each with both 20-ampere and 30-ampere supply facilities. The Table 551.73(A) demand factor for 6 sites is 60 percent. What is the calculated demand load for the sites?","o":["12,960 VA","9,720 VA","14,400 VA","21,600 VA"],"a":0,"e":"Each site with both 20- and 30-ampere supply is 3,600 VA: 6 x 3,600 = 21,600 VA; x 0.60 = 12,960 VA.","r":"NEC 551.73(A), Table 551.73(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"For a floating pier, how high above the water level is the electrical datum plane, and what is the additional deck requirement?","o":["2 ft above the highest tide and 12 in. above the deck","12 in. above the water level and 30 in. above the deck","At the waterline","30 in. above the water level and at least 12 in. above the deck of the pier"],"a":3,"e":"For floating piers and boat landing stages, the datum plane is 30 in. above the water level and at least 12 in. above the deck (555.3).","r":"NEC 555.3(A)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":1,"q":"At a marina on a lake not subject to tidal fluctuation, where is the electrical datum plane for land areas?","o":["12 in. above the highest normal water level","2 ft above the highest normal water level","At the highest normal water level","30 in. above the highest normal water level"],"a":1,"e":"For land areas not subject to tidal fluctuation, the datum plane is 2 ft above the highest normal water level (555.3).","r":"NEC 555.3(C)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"Service equipment for a floating marina dock must be located on land at what minimum distance from the floating structure and what minimum height above the datum plane?","o":["At least 5 ft horizontally from the structure and at least 12 in. above the datum plane","At least 1 ft horizontally and at the datum plane","At least 10 ft horizontally and 6 ft above the datum plane","At least 5 ft horizontally and 5 ft above the datum plane"],"a":0,"e":"555.4 requires the service equipment to be on land, not less than 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally from the structure served, and at least 300 mm (12 in.) above the electrical datum plane.","r":"NEC 555.4","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"A marina has 20 shore power receptacles. What demand factor from Table 555.6 applies to the shore power receptacle load?","o":["50 percent","70 percent","100 percent","90 percent"],"a":1,"e":"Table 555.6 gives 100 percent for 1 to 4 receptacles, 90 percent for 5 to 8, 80 percent for 9 to 14, 70 percent for 15 to 30, and lower values above that.","r":"NEC 555.6, Table 555.6","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":1,"q":"A marina has 6 shore power receptacles. What demand factor from Table 555.6 applies?","o":["70 percent","80 percent","100 percent","90 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Table 555.6 gives 90 percent for 5 to 8 shore power receptacles.","r":"NEC 555.6, Table 555.6","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":1,"q":"Wiring and equipment at a marina motor fuel dispensing location must comply with which article?","o":["Article 501","Article 514","Article 680","Article 511"],"a":1,"e":"555.11 sends motor fuel dispensing facilities at marinas to Article 514. Repair facilities handling flammable liquids fall under Article 511.","r":"NEC 555.11","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":1,"q":"A boatyard repair facility handles flammable and combustible liquids. Wiring and equipment there must comply with which article?","o":["Article 516","Article 511","Article 513","Article 514"],"a":1,"e":"555.12 requires marine craft repair facilities that handle flammable or combustible liquids or gases to comply with Article 511 for commercial garages and repair.","r":"NEC 555.12","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, what is the minimum size and type of bonding conductor used for an equipotential plane at a marina?","o":["Solid copper, insulated, covered, or bare, not smaller than 8 AWG","Copper-clad steel, not smaller than 4 AWG","Solid copper, not smaller than 14 AWG","Stranded aluminum, not smaller than 6 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"New 555.14 requires the equipotential plane to be bonded with a solid copper conductor, insulated, covered or bare, not smaller than 8 AWG.","r":"NEC 555.14","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"A damaged existing piece of electrical equipment on a marina pier is repaired rather than replaced. What does 555.15 require of the repair?","o":["It must be repaired to meet, at a minimum, the Code edition under which it was originally installed","It must be repaired to the next older edition of the Code","The entire circuit must be rebuilt to the current Code","No requirements apply to repairs"],"a":0,"e":"555.15 (new in 2023) requires existing equipment to be repaired to at least the Code edition under which it was installed, without requiring existing circuits to be rebuilt to the current Code.","r":"NEC 555.15","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"Shore power receptacles at a marina are rated 100 amperes. What type of receptacle is required?","o":["Any type listed for dry locations","Pin-and-sleeve type","Standard locking type","Straight-blade type"],"a":1,"e":"555.33 requires shore power receptacles rated 60 amperes or higher to be of the pin-and-sleeve type.","r":"NEC 555.33(A)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"Which cable is permitted as permanent wiring on the underside of fixed or floating piers at a marina?","o":["Sunlight-resistant extra-hard usage portable power cable listed for the environment","Type NMC cable","Type NM cable","Hard usage cord listed for dry locations"],"a":0,"e":"555.34(A)(2) permits sunlight-resistant extra-hard usage portable power cable listed for the environment as permanent wiring on the underside of piers and where flexibility is needed.","r":"NEC 555.34(A)(2)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":1,"q":"Feeder conductors on a marina docking facility require ground-fault protection of equipment set to trip at what level?","o":["100 mA or less","300 mA or less","5 mA or less","30 mA or less"],"a":0,"e":"555.35(A) requires feeder conductors on docking facilities to have GFPE set to trip at 100 mA or less.","r":"NEC 555.35(A)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":3,"q":"A separately derived transformer secondary supplies a dock panel through 8 ft of raceway. Is GFPE at 100 mA required on those secondary conductors under 555.35(A)?","o":["No, but only if the raceway is nonmetallic","No, secondary conductors of a separately derived system 10 ft or less in a raceway, with the equipment they supply, are exempt","Yes, there are no exceptions","Yes, unless the transformer is over 50 kVA"],"a":1,"e":"The exception to 555.35(A) exempts the secondary conductors of a separately derived system that are 10 ft or less and installed in a raceway, along with the equipment they supply.","r":"NEC 555.35(A) Exception","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"Each shore power receptacle at a marina must be provided with individual ground-fault protection of equipment set to trip at what level?","o":["300 mA or less","30 mA or less","6 mA","100 mA or less"],"a":1,"e":"555.35(B)(1) requires individual GFPE set to trip at 30 mA or less for each shore power receptacle.","r":"NEC 555.35(B)(1)","b":6},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"A marina has five receptacles that supply shore power to boats. What additional equipment must be available?","o":["A battery-backed emergency generator","A tide gauge","A leakage current measurement device to test each boat","A second service for the dock"],"a":2,"e":"Where more than three receptacles supply shore power to boats, a leakage current measurement device must be available and used to test each boat (555.35). It is not needed if the equipment has a leakage indicator and alarm.","r":"NEC 555.35","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":3,"q":"A boat hoist at a marina is supplied by a 480-volt, three-phase circuit. Does 555.35(C) require GFCI protection of the hoist outlet?","o":["No, boat hoists are never covered by Article 555","Yes, all boat hoist outlets require GFCI protection regardless of voltage","Yes, but only at 6 mA","No, because the GFCI requirement for boat hoists applies where the circuit voltage does not exceed 240 volts"],"a":3,"e":"555.35(C) requires GFCI protection for boat hoist outlets only where the circuit voltage does not exceed 240 volts.","r":"NEC 555.35(C)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum distance between a marina shore power receptacle and the circuit breaker or switch that serves as its disconnecting means?","o":["12 in.","30 ft","30 in.","10 ft"],"a":2,"e":"555.36 requires each shore power receptacle to have a readily accessible disconnecting means, identified for that receptacle, within 30 in. of it.","r":"NEC 555.36","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":3,"q":"Which feature is required of the emergency shutoff for a marina power outlet that supplies shore power?","o":["It must be marked Emergency Shutoff, be in sight of the outlet, externally operable, manually resettable, and not rely on a circuit breaker handle","It must be automatically resettable after one minute","It may be a circuit breaker handle located inside the pedestal","It must be located at the marina office, out of sight of the outlet"],"a":0,"e":"555.36(C), new in 2023, requires a listed emergency shutoff marked Emergency Shutoff that is in sight, readily accessible, externally operable and manually resettable; a circuit breaker handle cannot be used.","r":"NEC 555.36(C)","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"A 15-ampere branch circuit supplies a receptacle on a marina pier. What is the smallest insulated equipment grounding conductor permitted?","o":["8 AWG","10 AWG","14 AWG","12 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"555.37 requires an insulated equipment grounding conductor sized per 250.122 but not smaller than 12 AWG.","r":"NEC 555.37","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"A luminaire installed below the highest high tide level at a marina could be submerged. Which is a permitted way to supply it?","o":["Use a standard wet-location luminaire on a 120-volt branch circuit","Use a dry-location luminaire in an enclosure with a gasket","Supply it directly from a 277-volt circuit with GFCI","Use a submersible luminaire at or below the low-voltage contact limit, supplied by an isolating transformer or power supply per 680.23(A)(2)"],"a":3,"e":"555.38(B) requires luminaires that may be submerged to be submersible, operate at or below the low-voltage contact limit, and be supplied by a transformer or power supply per 680.23(A)(2).","r":"NEC 555.38","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards and Docking Facilities","d":2,"q":"Which of these is covered by the NEC rather than excluded from it?","o":["An aircraft","A floating building permanently moored in one location, such as a restaurant on a dock","A pleasure sailboat underway","A commercial cargo ship"],"a":1,"e":"The NEC covers floating buildings (90.2(A)(1) and Article 555) but does not cover ships and watercraft other than floating buildings (90.2(B)(1)).","r":"NEC 90.2(A)(1), 90.2(B)(1), 555.1","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":2,"q":"A temporary multiwire branch circuit is installed on a construction site. What is required for its disconnecting means?","o":["A switch that opens only the neutral","A means that simultaneously disconnects all ungrounded conductors, such as handle-tied breakers or a common-trip breaker","No disconnecting means is required for temporary wiring","Individual single-pole breakers with no handle tie"],"a":1,"e":"590.4(E) requires suitable switches or plug connectors to disconnect all ungrounded conductors of each temporary circuit, and a multiwire branch circuit must disconnect simultaneously.","r":"NEC 590.4(E)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":2,"q":"A temporary lamp on a construction site uses a metal guarded socket. What does the 2023 NEC require of the metal guard?","o":["It must be bonded only if the lamp exceeds 100 watts","It must be connected to the grounded (neutral) conductor","It must be connected to the circuit equipment grounding conductor","It must be insulated and left unconnected"],"a":2,"e":"The 2023 revision of 590.4(F) requires the metal guard on a guarded lampholder to be connected to the equipment grounding conductor of the circuit.","r":"NEC 590.4(F)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":2,"q":"A temporary feeder splice on a construction site is made using two lengths of nonmetallic multiconductor cord with continuous equipment grounding. Is a box, conduit body, or enclosure with cover required at the splice?","o":["Yes, every splice always requires a box with a cover","No, because splices are not allowed at all on construction sites","Yes, unless the cord is more than 100 ft long","No, 590.4(G) exempts splices of nonmetallic multiconductor cord or cable on construction sites where grounding continuity is maintained"],"a":3,"e":"590.4(G) requires splices to be in a box, conduit body or enclosure with cover, but exceptions for construction sites cover nonmetallic multiconductor cord or cable with continuous equipment grounding.","r":"NEC 590.4(G)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":1,"q":"Flexible cords and cables entering an enclosure that contains devices requiring termination, such as a temporary power box, must be secured how?","o":["By knotting the cord inside the box","With electrical tape wrapped around the cord","No securing is needed on temporary wiring","With fittings listed for the purpose"],"a":3,"e":"590.4(I) requires flexible cords and cables entering enclosures with devices to be secured to the box with fittings listed for the purpose.","r":"NEC 590.4(I)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":2,"q":"On a construction site, an outlet other than those requiring GFCI protection supplies temporary power used by personnel. Which is an acceptable alternative to GFCI or SPGFCI protection?","o":["A sign at the outlet asking workers to inspect the cord","A written assured equipment grounding conductor program that is continuously enforced","Nothing; protection is optional","Verbal approval by the site supervisor"],"a":1,"e":"590.6(B) permits an assured equipment grounding conductor program, written and continuously enforced on site, as an alternative to GFCI or SPGFCI protection for other temporary receptacles.","r":"NEC 590.6(B)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":2,"q":"A contractor wants to use a previously used circuit breaker in a temporary distribution panel. What does 590.8(A) require?","o":["Nothing, because used breakers are always acceptable","It may be used only if it is rated under 15 amperes","It must be recalibrated by the AHJ","The breaker must be examined for proper installation and maintenance and for evidence of impending failure"],"a":3,"e":"590.8(A) requires previously used overcurrent protective devices to be examined per 110.3 for proper installation and maintenance and for evidence of impending failure.","r":"NEC 590.8(A)","b":14},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":1,"q":"Because of the transient nature of temporary wiring, who may require additional measures to ensure safety?","o":["No one; Article 590 is fixed","The property owner's insurer only","Only the equipment manufacturer","The authority having jurisdiction"],"a":3,"e":"590.2(B) lets the AHJ require additional measures for temporary wiring to ensure safety to personnel.","r":"NEC 590.2(B)","b":4},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":1,"q":"Which of the following was added by the 2023 NEC as a listed example of an assembly occupancy?","o":["Hotel guest rooms","Warehouses","Single-family dwellings","Casinos and gaming facilities"],"a":3,"e":"518.2(A) now lists casinos and gaming facilities among examples of assembly occupancies.","r":"NEC 518.2(A)","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is listed as an example of an assembly occupancy in 518.2(A)?","o":["A single-family dwelling","A hotel guest room","A mortuary chapel","A parking garage with no occupants"],"a":2,"e":"518.2(A) lists mortuary chapels, along with places such as auditoriums, gymnasiums, museums, and restaurants, as assembly occupancies.","r":"NEC 518.2(A)","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":2,"q":"Portable switchboards and portable power distribution equipment used in an assembly occupancy must be supplied from what?","o":["An extension cord plugged into a lighting circuit","Any ordinary receptacle on the wall","Listed power outlets of sufficient voltage and ampere rating","A nonlisted outlet if protected by an overcurrent device"],"a":2,"e":"518.5 requires portable switchboards and portable power distribution equipment to be supplied only from listed power outlets of sufficient voltage and ampere rating.","r":"NEC 518.5","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":1,"q":"The informational note to the Article 518 scope says that, in the absence of a local building code, which document can be used for methods of determining the occupant load?","o":["NFPA 72","NFPA 110","NFPA 13","NFPA 101, Life Safety Code"],"a":3,"e":"The informational note to 518.1 directs users to the local building code or, in its absence, NFPA 101 for determining occupant load.","r":"NEC 518.1 Informational Note","b":15},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":2,"q":"A single 120-volt stand-alone supply is connected to a 120/240-volt, 3-wire panelboard. What must be installed at the panelboard?","o":["No marking is needed","A label showing the grid utility account number","A plaque stating that all circuits are 240 volts","A permanent plaque warning that the supply is a single 120-volt supply and that multiwire branch circuits must not be connected"],"a":3,"e":"710.15(C) permits a single 120-volt supply to feed 120/240-volt equipment only with no multiwire branch circuits and no 240-volt outlets, and requires a warning plaque at the equipment.","r":"NEC 710.15(C)","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":3,"q":"A single 120-volt stand-alone inverter supplies a 120/240-volt, 3-wire panelboard. Which condition must be met under 710.15(C)?","o":["The panelboard must have a 100-ampere main breaker","There must be no multiwire branch circuits and no 240-volt outlets supplied from the panelboard","Multiwire branch circuits are acceptable if the neutral is oversized","240-volt outlets are acceptable if GFCI protected"],"a":1,"e":"710.15(C) allows a single 120-volt stand-alone supply to feed 120/240-volt equipment only where there are no multiwire branch circuits and no 240-volt outlets.","r":"NEC 710.15(C)","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":2,"q":"Circuit conductors between several stand-alone sources and the building disconnecting means are sized on what basis?","o":["The sum of the output ratings of the stand-alone sources","The largest single source output only","The average of the source outputs","The sum of the loads only, ignoring the sources"],"a":0,"e":"710.15(B) sizes the conductors between the stand-alone sources and the building disconnecting means on the sum of the output ratings of the sources.","r":"NEC 710.15(B)","b":12},{"k":"code","c":"Art. 710 and 712 Stand-Alone Systems and DC Microgrids","d":2,"q":"What must the disconnecting means for each dc source output in a dc microgrid be capable of?","o":["Being operated only by remote radio signal","Being locked in the open position","Opening only when the load is zero","Being bypassed during maintenance"],"a":1,"e":"Article 712 requires the disconnecting means for each dc microgrid source to be lockable in the open position.","r":"NEC 712.34","b":4},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 90 Introduction","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which installation is specifically listed in 90.2(A) as covered by the Code?","o":["Installations underground in mines","Installations of railway power and signaling","Installations in ships and watercraft other than floating buildings","Installations supplying shore power to watercraft in marinas and boatyards, including leakage current monitoring"],"a":3,"e":"2020 90.2(A)(5) is new. The other choices are the kind of installations that 90.2(B) says the Code does NOT cover (ships and watercraft other than floating buildings, underground mines, railway power and signaling). Find it in the book at 90.2(A), the list of covered installations.","r":"NEC 90.2(A)(5) (2020); 90.2(A) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 90 Introduction","d":2,"q":"Which new item was added to the scope of the NEC at 90.2(A)(6) in the 2020 edition?","o":["Installations used to export power from electric vehicles to premises wiring or that allow bidirectional current flow","Installations of wireless charging pads only","Installations of public utility EV fast-charging stations on utility property","Electrical equipment inside the electric vehicle itself"],"a":0,"e":"2020 added 90.2(A)(6) so that vehicle-to-home and other bidirectional EV power transfer is within the Code. The wiring and equipment inside the vehicle is not covered. Look at 90.2(A) in the 2020 book.","r":"NEC 90.2(A)(6) (2020); 90.2(A) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, in which Article 100 part are definitions applying to hazardous (classified) locations located?","o":["They remain only in Article 500","Part I, 1000 Volts, Nominal, or Less only","Part III, Hazardous (Classified) Locations","Part II, Over 1000 Volts, Nominal only"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 edition created Article 100 Part III so the hazardous location terms are in one place, with no technical change intended. In the 2017 edition they were spread through the Chapter 5 .2 sections.","r":"NEC Article 100 Scope, Part III (2020); Article 100 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":3,"q":"Which section of the 2020 NEC makes direct use of the new Article 100 term Habitable Room?","o":["210.52(A), general wall receptacle spacing","250.104(A), bonding of metal water piping","230.67, surge protection for services","314.27(C), outlet boxes at ceiling-suspended (paddle) fan locations in dwellings"],"a":3,"e":"The new term supports 314.27(C), which applies to ceiling outlet boxes in habitable rooms of dwelling units. The other sections do not use the term. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Habitable Room; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC Art. 100 Habitable Room (2020); 314.27(C) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, which informational note terms are listed with the new Article 100 definition of Reconditioned?","o":["Rebuilt, refurbished, and remanufactured","Listed, labeled, and identified","Qualified, approved, and accepted","Field labeled, field evaluated, and field marked"],"a":0,"e":"2020 added the Reconditioned definition because new rules (210.15, 240.88, 408.8, 406.3, 410.7 and others) limit reconditioned equipment. The other groups are unrelated Article 100 terms.","r":"NEC Art. 100 Reconditioned (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"A new Article 100 definition in the 2020 NEC, Island Mode, relates most closely to which situation?","o":["A microgrid or power system operating isolated from the primary source","A panelboard fed by two main breakers","A building supplied by two separate utility services","An ungrounded delta system with a ground detector"],"a":0,"e":"Island Mode was added in 2020 and describes isolated or standalone microgrid operation. It is used in Chapter 7 for microgrids and stand-alone systems. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Island Mode; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC Art. 100 Island Mode (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a Dormitory Unit as defined in Article 100 provides group sleeping accommodations for more than how many unrelated persons?","o":["16","8","24","4"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 definition ties to NFPA 101 style occupancies with more than 16 unrelated sleepers. The term is used in rules such as 210.12 and 406.12. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Dormitory Unit; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC Art. 100 Dormitory Unit (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"Which equipment is part of the Article 100 definition of Laundry Area in the 2020 NEC?","o":["Only a clothes dryer","Only a clothes washer and its receptacle","A water heater and a furnace","A laundry tray, clothes washer, and/or clothes dryer"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC defined Laundry Area because 210.8(A) and 210.8(B) rely on the term for GFCI locations. Look it up in Article 100. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Laundry Area; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC Art. 100 Laundry Area (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, where is the definition of Bonding Jumper, Supply-Side found?","o":["250.102(C)","Article 100","230.2","250.2 only"],"a":1,"e":"In 2017 the term was defined in 250.2. The 2020 edition moved it to Article 100 because it is used in several articles. This is a relocation, not a new rule. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Bonding Jumper, Supply-Side; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC Art. 100 (2020); 250.2 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which section now contains the general workmanship requirements for exposed cables and conductors that previously appeared in the individual Chapter 7 and 8 articles?","o":["300.4(A)","110.12(C)","300.11(A)","110.3(B)"],"a":1,"e":"The 2017 edition repeated the language in each xxx.24 section of the low-voltage and communications articles. 2020 consolidated it in 110.12(C). Check 110.12 in the 2020 book.","r":"NEC 110.12(C) (2020); 725.24, 760.24, 770.24, 800.24 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 110.14(D), where a torque value is specified for a terminal, how must the required torque be achieved?","o":["By tightening until snug and then a quarter turn","Only with a calibrated torque screwdriver","By an approved means, such as a torque tool or a torque-indicating fastener","Torque values are advisory and need not be verified"],"a":2,"e":"The 2017 text named a calibrated torque tool; 2020 broadened it to an approved means and added informational notes. Do not confuse with the older practice of tightening by feel.","r":"NEC 110.14(D) (2020); 110.14(D) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, what must be done to the original listing mark on equipment that has been reconditioned?","o":["It may remain if the equipment passes a test","It must be moved to the back of the enclosure","It must be left in place and covered with a label","The original listing mark must be removed"],"a":3,"e":"110.21(A)(2) in 2020 pairs the 'reconditioned' marking with removal of the original listing mark so the product is not mistaken for new listed equipment. This ties to the new Reconditioned definition.","r":"NEC 110.21(A)(2) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a disconnecting means in a commercial building must be marked to show its purpose and also to identify what?","o":["The date the disconnect was installed","The available fault current at the load","The circuit source that supplies the disconnecting means","The name of the licensed electrician"],"a":2,"e":"The added source-of-supply marking in 110.22(A) is for other than one- and two-family dwellings. Available fault current marking is a different rule (110.24 and 408.6). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 110.22(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 110.22(A) (2020); 110.22(A) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":2,"q":"The 2020 NEC revised 200.3 so that the rule on connecting to a supply system's grounded conductor applies to what?","o":["Only services over 600 volts","All premises wiring systems, not only interior wiring","Only separately derived systems","Only interior wiring systems"],"a":1,"e":"The 2017 text referred to interior wiring; 2020 uses premises wiring so outdoor installations are included. Look at 200.3 in the 2020 book. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 200.3; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 200.3 (2020); 200.3 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":1,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a terminal to which a grounded conductor is connected on a polarized device may be identified by which color?","o":["Only blue","Only yellow","Only green","Substantially white or silver"],"a":3,"e":"2020 clarified that the requirement is for polarized connections and recognized silver. Green is for equipment grounding terminals (250.126). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 200.9; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 200.9 (2020); 200.9 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which receptacle in a dwelling unit garage requires GFCI protection under 210.8(A)?","o":["A 240-volt, single-phase receptacle on a 150-volts-or-less-to-ground circuit, such as one for a welder","Only 125-volt receptacles within 6 ft of a sink","None; garage GFCI was removed for 250-volt equipment","Only 125-volt, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles"],"a":0,"e":"The 2017 NEC limited 210.8(A) to 125-volt 15/20-ampere receptacles. The 2020 NEC extended it to 125 through 250 volts, single-phase, 150 volts or less to ground. A 120/240-volt single-phase system qualifies.","r":"NEC 210.8(A) (2020); 210.8(A) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which dwelling unit basement receptacle requires GFCI protection?","o":["Only receptacles in unfinished storage areas of the basement","Only receptacles within 6 ft of a basement sink","A 125-volt receptacle in a finished basement family room","None if the basement has a bedroom"],"a":2,"e":"The 2017 text used 'unfinished' basement areas. 2020 simply says basements, removing the judgment about finished space. The alarm-only receptacle is the exception. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.8(A)(5); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(5) (2020); 210.8(A)(5) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Which new item was added to the list of dwelling unit locations in 210.8(A) in the 2020 NEC?","o":["Indoor damp and wet locations","Bedroom closets","Living room walls above 6 ft","Attic spaces over 10 ft high"],"a":0,"e":"210.8(A)(11) is new for 2020 and reaches places like mud rooms. Bedrooms, living rooms and attics are not GFCI locations under 210.8(A). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.8(A)(11); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(11) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 210.8(B), which receptacle in a listed non-dwelling location requires GFCI protection?","o":["A three-phase receptacle rated 100 amperes or less on a circuit of 150 volts or less to ground","Only receptacles rated 15 amperes","Only single-phase 125-volt, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles","Three-phase receptacles are exempt from GFCI in all cases"],"a":0,"e":"2020 broadened 210.8(B) to higher ratings: single-phase 50 A or less and three-phase 100 A or less, 150 V to ground or less. In 2017 it was limited to 125 V, 15 and 20 A.","r":"NEC 210.8(B) (2020); 210.8(B) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"Which location was added to 210.8(B) (other than dwelling units) in the 2020 NEC?","o":["Offices","Corridors","Conference rooms","Laundry areas"],"a":3,"e":"2020 added laundry areas to 210.8(B) (item 11) alongside areas within 6 ft of a bathtub or shower (item 12). Offices and corridors are not listed. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.8(B)(11); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 210.8(B)(11) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a receptacle in a non-dwelling occupancy that is within what distance of the edge of a bathtub or shower stall requires GFCI protection under 210.8(B)(12)?","o":["3 ft","6 ft","10 ft","20 ft"],"a":1,"e":"210.8(B)(12) is new in 2020 and uses the same 6 ft measure as other GFCI sink and tub provisions. Pool receptacles use 20 ft in 680.22. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.8(B)(12); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 210.8(B)(12) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, where is the GFCI requirement for dishwashers found?","o":["680.5","422.16(B)(2) only","210.8(A)(7) only","422.5(A)(7), referenced by 210.8(D)"],"a":3,"e":"2020 moved the dishwasher rule to Article 422 and expanded it beyond dwelling units. 210.8(D) now just cross-references 422.5. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.8(D); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 210.8(D), 422.5(A)(7) (2020); 210.8(D) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which receptacle requires GFCI protection under 210.8(E)?","o":["Only receptacles in dwelling bathrooms","Receptacles in the main electrical room only","The receptacle for a dedicated range circuit","The 125-volt receptacle required by 210.63 for servicing HVAC equipment on a rooftop"],"a":3,"e":"210.8(E) is new in 2020 and ties GFCI protection to the service receptacle required by 210.63. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.8(E); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 210.8(E), 210.63 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a 240-volt, 30-ampere hardwired air conditioner condensing unit outdoors at a one-family dwelling must be supplied by what?","o":["A standard circuit breaker without GFCI, since it is not a receptacle","A GFCI-protected branch circuit","An AFCI-protected circuit only","A circuit of at least 60 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"210.8(F) was added in 2020 for outdoor outlets (not only receptacles). The circuit must be single-phase, 150 V or less to ground, 50 A or less. Outdoor lighting outlets are excepted.","r":"NEC 210.8(F) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, AFCI protection is newly required for 15- and 20-ampere, 120-volt branch circuits in which location?","o":["Warehouse offices","Hospital operating rooms","Parking structures","Patient sleeping rooms of nursing homes and limited-care facilities"],"a":3,"e":"2020 added 210.12(C). Operating rooms, warehouses and parking structures are not on the list. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.12(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 210.12(C) (2020); 210.12 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, the branch circuit extension rule of 210.12(D) now also applies to which space?","o":["Garages of commercial buildings","Guest rooms and guest suites","Outdoor receptacles","Hospital patient rooms only"],"a":1,"e":"2020 added guest rooms and guest suites to the extension and modification rule. The 6 ft exception measures only conductors outside enclosures. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.12(D); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 210.12(D) (2020); 210.12(C) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":1,"q":"Which statement about reconditioning is correct under the 2020 NEC 210.15?","o":["Only AFCI devices may not be reconditioned","Reconditioned GFCIs are allowed in dwelling units only","GFCI, AFCI and ground-fault protection of equipment devices may not be reconditioned","GFCI devices may be reconditioned if retested"],"a":2,"e":"210.15 is new in 2020. It bars reconditioning of protective devices where failure could cause shock or fire. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.15; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 210.15 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 220 Branch-Circuit","d":2,"q":"How did the 2020 NEC change the general lighting unit loads in Table 220.12 for most non-dwelling occupancies compared with 2017?","o":["They were reduced for most occupancies","They were removed in favor of the energy code only","They were increased for every occupancy","They were left unchanged"],"a":0,"e":"2020 lowered most values based on lighting energy data while raising a few. The 2017 values were the long-standing numbers. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 220.12 / Table 220.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC Table 220.12 (2020); Table 220.12 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, power distribution blocks used to tap service conductors on the line side of the service disconnect must be marked how?","o":["No marking is required","Listed for load-side use only","Suitable for use on the line side of the service equipment","Rated for use only with copper"],"a":2,"e":"2020 expanded 230.46 to require listed devices marked for line-side use. Load-side-only blocks are not permitted for this use. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.46; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 230.46 (2020); 230.46 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, where is the barrier requirement for uninsulated energized service busbars and terminals found?","o":["408.3(A)(2)","250.92(A)","110.27(A)","230.62(C)"],"a":3,"e":"The 2017 rule sat in Article 408 and applied to panelboards, switchboards and switchgear. The 2020 rule is in Article 230 and covers all service equipment. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.62(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 230.62(C) (2020); 408.3(A)(2) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, all services supplying dwelling units must be provided with what?","o":["A ground-fault protection device","A Type 1 or Type 2 surge protective device","An AFCI main breaker","A Type 3 SPD at each receptacle"],"a":1,"e":"230.67 is new for 2020. The SPD is Type 1 or Type 2, installed integral to or adjacent to the service equipment. Type 3 point-of-use SPDs do not satisfy it. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.67; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 230.67 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, when an existing dwelling unit's service equipment is replaced, what applies?","o":["No SPD is needed unless a new service is installed","A Type 1 or Type 2 SPD must be installed per 230.67","An SPD is needed only for services over 200 amperes","A Type 4 component assembly is required"],"a":1,"e":"230.67 applies to replacement of service equipment as well as new services. The exception permits the SPD downstream at the next distribution equipment. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.67; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 230.67 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which arrangement does NOT comply with 230.71 for a service with six disconnects?","o":["Six disconnects in separate compartments of switchgear","Six disconnects in separate vertical sections of a switchboard","Six switches in six separate enclosures","All six circuit breakers mounted in a single panelboard enclosure"],"a":3,"e":"2020 stopped permitting up to six service disconnects in one panelboard enclosure; they must be separated per 230.71(B). Also 408.36 Exception for six mains was deleted. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.71; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 230.71(A), (B) (2020); 230.71 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 230.71, which is NOT counted toward the number of service disconnects?","o":["A switch in a meter-main combination serving as a service disconnect","A circuit breaker serving as a service disconnect","A surge protective device","A fused switch serving as a service disconnect"],"a":2,"e":"230.71(A) in 2020 excludes power monitoring, SPD, GFP control circuits and power-operable disconnect functions. Actual service disconnecting devices are counted. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.71; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 230.71(A) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":1,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a one-family dwelling's service must have an emergency disconnect located where?","o":["Inside the garage next to the panel","At a readily accessible location outside the building","In the basement near the meter","At the utility pole only"],"a":1,"e":"230.85 requires an outdoor, readily accessible emergency disconnect for 1- and 2-family dwellings to let first responders de-energize the structure. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.85; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 230.85 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, an outdoor emergency disconnect that is not the service disconnect must be marked how?","o":["EMERGENCY DISCONNECT, NOT SERVICE EQUIPMENT","FIRE DEPARTMENT CONNECTION","EMERGENCY SHUTDOWN, SERVICE DISCONNECT","MAIN DISCONNECT, SERVICE EQUIPMENT"],"a":0,"e":"230.85 distinguishes between a service-disconnect emergency disconnect and a non-service one by the marking text. This rule did not exist in 2017. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.85; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 230.85 (2020); 110.21(B)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 240.6(C), which is an accepted method of restricting access to adjustable trip settings of a circuit breaker?","o":["Using a keyed receptacle on the load side","Password protection","Posting a warning label near the breaker","Mounting the breaker above 6 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Password protection was added to 240.6(C) in 2020 along with removable and sealable covers, bolted enclosure doors and locked doors. A label or mounting height does not restrict access.","r":"NEC 240.6(C) (2020); 240.6(C) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 240.67(C), arc energy reduction for 1200 A or higher fuses requires what?","o":["A coordination study of all downstream fuses","Performance testing of the method when first installed","Only a label stating the maintenance mode","Nothing beyond the 2017 requirements"],"a":1,"e":"Performance testing was added in 2020 for fuses in 240.67(C) and for breakers in 240.87(C). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 240.67(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 240.67(A), (C) (2020); 240.67 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 240.87, which is NOT an acceptable arc energy reduction method?","o":["Differential relaying","Zone-selective interlocking","Temporarily adjusting the instantaneous trip setting","An energy-reducing maintenance switch"],"a":2,"e":"2020 clarified that temporary instantaneous adjustment is not a permitted method. ZSI, differential relaying and an energy-reducing maintenance switch are permitted methods.","r":"NEC 240.87(B), (C) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 240.88, which circuit breaker may NOT be reconditioned?","o":["A high-voltage circuit breaker","A molded-case circuit breaker","A low-voltage power circuit breaker","A medium-voltage circuit breaker"],"a":1,"e":"240.88 is new in 2020 and prohibits reconditioning molded-case breakers. Larger power breakers may be reconditioned when listed as reconditioned. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 240.88; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 240.88 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 242 Overvoltage Protection","d":1,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, surge-protective devices rated 1000 volts or less are covered in which article?","o":["Article 242","Article 245","Article 285","Article 280"],"a":0,"e":"The 2017 NEC used Article 285 for SPDs and Article 280 for surge arresters. 2020 combined them into Article 242. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Art. 242; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC Article 242 (2020); Articles 280, 285 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which is prohibited?","o":["Using a concrete-encased electrode as a grounding electrode","Using a ground rod electrode","Using rebar as the conductor to interconnect grounding electrodes","Extending rebar to a connection point if tied or continuous and protected from earth contact"],"a":2,"e":"250.53 and 250.68(C)(3) in 2020 say rebar may not interconnect electrodes. A concrete-encased electrode is still allowed. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 250.53; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 250.53 (2020); 250.68(C)(3) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, what type of PVC is specified to protect a grounding electrode conductor from physical damage?","o":["Schedule 20 PVC","Schedule 40 PVC","Any thin-wall PVC","Schedule 80 PVC"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC calls for Schedule 80 PVC in 250.64(B) where PVC protection is used. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 250.64(B); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 250.64(B) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 250.68(C)(3), a rebar extension from a concrete-encased electrode may serve as a connection point when it is protected from what?","o":["Contact with copper conductors","Exposure to sunlight","Contact with the earth","Contact with concrete"],"a":2,"e":"The rebar extension must be protected from contact with earth (corrosion protection) under 250.68(C)(3)(a)-(c). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 250.68(C)(3); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 250.68(C)(3) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 250.104(A)(1), a bonding jumper to metal water piping is never required to be larger than what?","o":["500 kcmil copper","3/0 AWG copper or 250 kcmil aluminum or copper-clad aluminum","4/0 AWG copper","1/0 AWG copper"],"a":1,"e":"2020 capped the water-pipe bonding jumper at 3/0 copper (250 kcmil aluminum). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 250.104(A)(1); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 250.104(A)(1) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, when phase conductors are increased in size solely because of adjustment factors for more than three current-carrying conductors, the EGC must be:","o":["Increased to the same size as the phase conductors","Increased proportionately","Sized per Table 250.122 without increasing it proportionately","Replaced by a bonding jumper"],"a":2,"e":"250.122(B) in 2020 excludes increases made for adjustment or correction factors. The 2017 rule applied to any upsizing. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 250.122(B); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 250.122(B) (2020); 250.122(B) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":1,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 250.12, nonconductive coatings such as paint must be removed from surfaces for which connections?","o":["Bonding connections only","Both grounding and bonding connections","Grounding connections only","Neither, if listed fittings are used"],"a":1,"e":"2020 expanded 250.12 to say bonding too. The 2017 text focused on grounding. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 250.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 250.12 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 300.7(A), a raceway sealant used where there is a temperature difference must be:","o":["Any approved caulk","Of silicone only","Rated for explosionproof seal use","Identified for use with the conductors and components"],"a":3,"e":"2020 replaced approved with identified, so the sealant must be suitable for the conductor insulation. Explosionproof seals are not required for this purpose. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 300.7(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 300.7(A) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 300.25, what wiring is permitted in an exit enclosure such as a stair tower?","o":["Only Type NM cable","No electrical wiring of any kind","Any building branch circuit wiring","Only wiring serving equipment permitted by the authority having jurisdiction in the enclosure"],"a":3,"e":"300.25 is new for 2020 and limits exit enclosure wiring to equipment permitted there; wiring for other areas goes outside. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 300.25; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 300.25 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 300.45, signs at access to conductors over 1000 volts must read:","o":["NOTICE - ENERGIZED","DANGER - HIGH VOLTAGE - KEEP OUT","WARNING - HIGH VOLTAGE - KEEP OUT","CAUTION - HIGH VOLTAGE"],"a":1,"e":"The signal word for over-1000 V signs changed from Warning to Danger in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 300.45; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 300.45 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":1,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, the table of ampacities of insulated conductors rated up to 2000 volts, 60 through 90 degrees C (not more than three current-carrying conductors) is numbered:","o":["Table 310.15(B)(16)","Table 310.16","Table 310.12","Table 310.15(C)(1)"],"a":1,"e":"2017 used Table 310.15(B)(16). 2020 renumbered it Table 310.16. Many exam questions reference the table number. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Table 310.16; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC Table 310.16 (2020); Table 310.15(B)(16) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":3,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, the adjustment factors for more than three current-carrying conductors in a raceway appear in:","o":["Table 310.15(C)(1)","Table 310.15(B)(3)(a)","Table 310.16","Table 310.15(B)(1)"],"a":0,"e":"2017 placed adjustment factors in Table 310.15(B)(3)(a). In 2020 they moved to 310.15(C)(1), and the ambient correction table is 310.15(B)(1). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 310.15(B)(1) / 310.15(C)(1); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC Table 310.15(B)(1), Table 310.15(C)(1) (2020); Tables 310.15(B)(2)(a), 310.15(B)(3)(a) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, which section contains the reduced conductor sizing for single-phase dwelling services and feeders?","o":["310.15(B)(7)","310.14","310.16","310.12"],"a":3,"e":"310.15(B)(7) in 2017 became 310.12 in 2020, with a table. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 310.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 310.12, Table 310.12 (2020); 310.15(B)(7) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 311 Medium Voltage Conductors and Cable","d":1,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, medium voltage cable (Type MV) requirements are located in which article?","o":["Article 310 Part IV","Article 311","Article 328","Article 330"],"a":1,"e":"Article 328 was deleted. MV requirements are now in Article 311 and 310 is for 2000 V or less. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Art. 311; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC Article 311, 310.1 (2020); Article 328 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 337 Type P Cable","d":2,"q":"Which new cable article was added to Chapter 3 in the 2020 NEC?","o":["Article 346, RMC","Article 400, Flexible Cords","Article 337, Type P Cable","Article 321, Messenger-Supported Wiring"],"a":2,"e":"Type P (marine shipboard) cable was added in 2020 as Article 337. The others are older articles. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Art. 337; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC Article 337 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 312.8(B), which equipment is permitted in switch and overcurrent device enclosures?","o":["Auxiliary gutters feeding other loads","Splices and taps of any feeder","Any cable-connected appliance","Power monitoring and control (energy management) equipment"],"a":3,"e":"312.8(B) now explicitly permits power monitoring and energy management equipment. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 312.8(B); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 312.8(B) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a ceiling outlet box in a dwelling bedroom must be:","o":["A box rated for 100 lb of load","A pancake box only","Listed for fan support, or a box with accessible structural framing capable of supporting a ceiling fan","A standard box of any type"],"a":2,"e":"2020 expanded fan support to all habitable room ceiling outlets in dwelling units. The 2017 rule was triggered by a spare switched conductor. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 314.27(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 314.27(C) (2020); 314.27(C) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 392 Cable Trays","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 392.30(B), cable ties used to secure cables in cable tray must be:","o":["Listed and identified for the purpose","Any nylon tie","Orange in color","Metallic only"],"a":0,"e":"2020 added a listing requirement for cable ties in tray. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 392.30(B); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 392.30(B) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 404 Switches","d":2,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, 404.9 provisions on faceplates and grounding apply to snap switches and which other devices?","o":["Dimmers and control switches","Only lampholders","Only circuit breakers","Only receptacles"],"a":0,"e":"404.9 was expanded to dimmers and control switches. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 404.9; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 404.9 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 406.4(D)(7), an automatically controlled receptacle must be replaced with:","o":["An equivalently controlled receptacle","A standard receptacle","A GFCI receptacle with no marking","A tamper-resistant receptacle only"],"a":0,"e":"406.4(D)(7) was added in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 406.4(D)(7); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 406.4(D)(7) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 406.5(G)(2), receptacles in the area below a sink:","o":["Shall not be installed in a face-up position","May be installed face-up with a cover","Must be GFCI only","Are prohibited entirely"],"a":0,"e":"406.5(G)(2) is new in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 406.5(G)(2); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 406.5(G)(2) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a receptacle may NOT be installed within what zone of a bathtub rim?","o":["1 ft horizontally and 4 ft vertically","Only within or directly over the tub","6 ft horizontally and 8 ft vertically","3 ft horizontally and 8 ft vertically"],"a":3,"e":"2017 only barred receptacles within or directly over the tub. 2020 added a 3 ft x 8 ft zone with an exception for small bathrooms. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 406.9(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 406.9(C) (2020); 406.9(C) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, tamper-resistant receptacles are required in which location?","o":["Only living rooms","A detached garage of a one-family dwelling","Only bedrooms","Not in garages"],"a":1,"e":"2020 406.12(1) names garages and accessory buildings. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 406.12(1); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 406.12(1) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":2,"q":"Which facility was added as new item (8) of 406.12 in the 2020 NEC?","o":["Car dealerships","Assisted living facilities","Parking garages","Warehouses"],"a":1,"e":"406.12(8) is new. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 406.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 406.12 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":1,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, reconditioned attachment plugs are:","o":["Allowed with a label","Allowed if tested","Allowed in industrial only","Prohibited"],"a":3,"e":"2020 added the prohibition. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 406.3 / 406.7; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 406.3, 406.7 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":3,"q":"Which new organization type is defined in Article 100 of the 2020 NEC and referenced for field evaluation of custom or one-of-a-kind equipment such as generators?","o":["Authority Having Jurisdiction Evaluation Board","Qualified Person Committee","Field Evaluation Body (FEB)","Nationally Recognized Testing Laboratory Council"],"a":2,"e":"The FEB definition was added in 2020 so equipment like one-of-a-kind generators (445.6) and field-assembled service equipment (230.66) can be field evaluated and labeled.","r":"NEC Art. 100 Field Evaluation Body (2020); 445.6 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":2,"q":"The 2020 NEC revised the voltage reference in 215.2 from 600 volts to:","o":["1000 volts","2000 volts","480 volts","250 volts"],"a":0,"e":"2020 aligns feeder rules with the 1000 V upper limit used for low-voltage systems. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 215.2; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 215.2 (2020); 215.2 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear, and Panelboards","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 408.8, which equipment may NOT be reconditioned?","o":["Low-voltage power circuit breakers","Panelboards","Switchboards","Switchgear"],"a":1,"e":"408.8 is new in 2020. Panelboards cannot be reconditioned; switchboards and switchgear can under conditions. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 408.8; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 408.8 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear, and Panelboards","d":1,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 408.43, panelboards:","o":["Must be face-down","May be installed face-up in damp locations","Must be mounted with a vertical orientation only","Shall not be installed in the face-up position"],"a":3,"e":"408.43 is new in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 408.43; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 408.43 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders, and Lamps","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 410.16, the luminaire rules for closets apply to what?","o":["Clothes closets","Pantries","All storage closets","Linen closets only"],"a":0,"e":"2020 added the word clothes to the definition and rule. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 410.2 / 410.16; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 410.2, 410.16 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders, and Lamps","d":2,"q":"Which new part was added to Article 410 in the 2020 NEC?","o":["Part XVI, Solar Luminaires","Part XVI, Electric Vehicle Charging","Part XVI, Emergency Lighting","Part XVI, Horticultural Lighting Equipment"],"a":3,"e":"Part XVI is new in 2020 for grow lights. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Part XVI (410.170-); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC Article 410 Part XVI (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 422 Appliances","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 422.5(A), appliances require what type of GFCI protection?","o":["Class A GFCI protection for personnel","Any GFCI","Class B","AFCI"],"a":0,"e":"2020 specified Class A and 150 V to ground. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 422.5(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 422.5(A) (2020); 422.5(A) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits, and Controllers","d":2,"q":"Which motor protection term was newly defined in 430.2 of the 2020 NEC?","o":["Fuse Protected (as applied to motors)","Thermally Mounted (as applied to motors)","Electronically Protected (as applied to motors)","Magnetically Protected (as applied to motors)"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 NEC added the Electronically Protected definition in 430.2 (a motor with an integral electronic control that protects against overheating, failure to start, and similar) and extended 430.32 to cover it. Thermally Protected was already defined; the other choices are not NEC terms.","r":"NEC 430.2, 430.32(A)(2), (B)(2) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 445.6, stationary generators rated 600 volts or less must be:","o":["Limited to 15 kW or less","Listed (or field labeled by a Field Evaluation Body if custom)","Installed outdoors only","Approved by the owner only"],"a":1,"e":"445.6 is new in 2020 and is why the Field Evaluation Body definition was added. Nothing in 445.6 limits kW size or requires outdoor installation. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 445.6; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 445.6 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 445 Generators","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a permanently installed standby generator at a one-family dwelling must have an emergency shutdown device located:","o":["At the generator only, inside its enclosure","In the electrical panel only","Inside the garage next to the transfer switch","Outside the dwelling at a readily accessible location"],"a":3,"e":"445.18(D) is new for 2020 and parallels the outdoor emergency disconnect of 230.85. It does not apply to cord-and-plug portable generators. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 445.18(D); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 445.18(D) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","d":3,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, rules for interconnecting battery cells, units, or modules were relocated from Article 706 to:","o":["Article 480 Part III","Article 690","Article 480 (480.12)","Article 710"],"a":2,"e":"Relocation only. The 2017 NEC kept battery interconnection in 706.32. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 480.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 480.12 (2020); 706.32 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":1,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, section 500.4 is titled:","o":["General","Classification of Locations","Equipment","Documentation"],"a":3,"e":"2017 titled it General; 2020 changed it to Documentation. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 500.4; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 500.4 (2020); 500.4 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, GFCI protection in commercial garages (511.12) is expanded by reference to which section?","o":["680.22","422.5","210.8(A)","210.8(B)"],"a":3,"e":"2020 pointed 511.12 to 210.8(B), which now covers garages, service bays and similar areas. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 511.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 511.12 (2020); 210.8(B)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","d":2,"q":"A new section 518.6 in the 2020 NEC requires illumination of:","o":["Outdoor working space about service equipment serving an assembly occupancy","Exit signs inside the assembly space","Parking lot poles only","Stage rigging"],"a":0,"e":"518.6 is new in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 518.6; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 518.6 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards, Floating Buildings","d":3,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, ground-fault protection of equipment for marina feeders and branch circuits is located in:","o":["555.19","555.9","555.3","555.35"],"a":3,"e":"Number moved: 555.3 became 555.35, and the new 555.3 deals with electrical datum plane distances. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 555.35; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 555.35 (2020); 555.3 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 590.8, an overcurrent protective device reused for temporary power must be:","o":["Replaced with a new device every time","Rated for 600 volts","Examined for proper installation, maintenance, and impending failure","Used only for lighting"],"a":2,"e":"590.8 is new for 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 590.8; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 590.8 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":2,"q":"Which new term was defined in 625.2 of the 2020 NEC?","o":["Vehicle Charge Controller","Electric Vehicle Charging Station Operator","Mobile Energy Storage Unit","Electric Vehicle Power Export Equipment (EVPE)"],"a":3,"e":"2020 added EVPE and bidirectional current flow to Article 625, consistent with 90.2(A)(6). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 625.1 / 625.2; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 625.1, 625.2 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"A new section 625.60 in the 2020 NEC addresses what?","o":["AC receptacle outlets in electric vehicles used for connecting off-board equipment","DC fast charging station signage","Wireless power transfer pads","Overhead charging gantries"],"a":0,"e":"625.60 is new in 2020 and relates to vehicle power export. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 625.60; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 625.60 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 680.4, the authority having jurisdiction may:","o":["Require periodic inspection and testing after installation","Inspect only at the time of permit","Waive GFCI requirements after one year","Not require any follow-up inspections"],"a":0,"e":"680.4 was added in 2020 as Inspections After Installation. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 680.4; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 680.4 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 680.21(C), a 240-volt, single-phase, 50-ampere pool pump motor circuit:","o":["Requires GFCI protection","Is exempt because it exceeds 20 amperes","Is exempt if hard-wired","Requires AFCI protection instead"],"a":0,"e":"2020 extended pool motor GFCI to 150 V-to-ground circuits up to 60 A, single- or three-phase. The 2017 limit was 15 or 20 A. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 680.21(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 680.21(C) (2020); 680.21(C) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, when an existing pool pump motor is replaced, the circuit:","o":["Needs AFCI protection","Must be upgraded to 60 amperes","Need not be changed","Must be provided with GFCI protection per 680.21(C)"],"a":3,"e":"680.21(D) is new in 2020 and brings existing pool pumps up to GFCI protection when the motor is replaced. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 680.21(D); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 680.21(D) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 690.12(B)(2)(1), the listing-based compliance path inside the array boundary is a:","o":["Field-labeled rapid shutdown PV array","Listed PV hazard control system","Manual disconnect switch","Fusible disconnect at the inverter"],"a":1,"e":"2020 replaced the listed or field labeled rapid shutdown PV array with a listed PV hazard control system. The 30 second time limit existed in 2017 too (the 10 second limit was the 2014 NEC).","r":"NEC 690.12(B)(2)(1) (2020); 690.12(B)(2)(1) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 690.45, must a PV circuit EGC be increased in size because the ungrounded conductors were upsized for voltage drop?","o":["Yes, in the same proportion","Yes, by one AWG size","Yes, only for circuits over 600 volts","No, voltage drop upsizing does not require a larger EGC"],"a":3,"e":"2020 removed the requirement that the EGC be increased for voltage drop. Table 250.122 sizing with the overcurrent device applies. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 690.45; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 690.45 (2020); 690.45 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a meter-mounted transfer switch is permitted for which system?","o":["An emergency system","A fire pump power supply","A legally required standby system","An optional standby system"],"a":3,"e":"700.5 and 701.5 prohibit meter-mounted transfer switches; 702.5(B) allows them for optional standby systems. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 700.5; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 700.5, 701.5, 702.5(B) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 701.5, transfer equipment for legally required standby systems must be:","o":["Meter-mounted for easy access","Reconditioned if tested","Manually operated only","Listed, and not meter-mounted or reconditioned"],"a":3,"e":"701.5 in 2020 matches 700.5 on meter-mounted switches and adds a ban on reconditioned equipment. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 701.5; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 701.5 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 702.5(C), outside dwelling units, transfer equipment for optional standby systems must be field marked with its:","o":["Short-circuit current rating","Date of manufacture","Fuel type of the generator","Transfer time in seconds"],"a":0,"e":"702.5 was split into four subsections in 2020: (A) General, (B) meter-mounted transfer switches, (C) Documentation, (D) inadvertent interconnection. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 702.5(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 702.5(C) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 702.5(D), a circuit breaker interlock kit used to prevent backfeed from a portable generator must be:","o":["Field fabricated by the installer","Listed as transfer equipment","Labeled with a wiring diagram only","Rated for 100 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"2020 added the listing requirement for interlock kits. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 702.5(D); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 702.5(D) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 702.7(A), at a one-family dwelling the sign for an on-site standby power source is placed at:","o":["No sign is required","Inside the garage","Only the generator","The exterior emergency disconnect required by 230.85"],"a":3,"e":"702.7(A) was revised to coordinate with 230.85. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 702.7(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 702.7(A) (2020); 230.85","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":2,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, connection of an interactive power source to the supply side of the service disconnect is covered by:","o":["705.13","705.12","705.11","705.10"],"a":2,"e":"The 2017 NEC covered both supply-side and load-side connections in 705.12. In 2020 supply side is 705.11 and load side is 705.12. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 705.11; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 705.11, 705.12 (2020); 705.12(A) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":3,"q":"A new 2020 section 705.13 permits the use of:","o":["An AFCI to detect arcing in sources","A lightning rod system","A surge arrester on the DC side","A power control system to limit current on a busbar or conductor"],"a":3,"e":"705.13 is new in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 705.13; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 705.13 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":1,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, 705.10 is titled:","o":["Point of Connection","Equipment Approval","Identification of Power Sources","Directory"],"a":2,"e":"2020 retitled the section; 710.10 and 712.10 also use Identification of Power Sources or Directory language. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 705.10; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 705.10 (2020); 705.10 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 710 Stand-Alone Systems","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 710.10, a building supplied by a stand-alone system requires:","o":["A neutral-to-ground bond at every panel","A surge protective device","A utility meter","A permanent plaque or directory identifying the power sources"],"a":3,"e":"710.10 is new in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 710.10; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 710.10 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 710 Stand-Alone Systems","d":3,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, stand-alone inverter input circuit current is addressed in:","o":["710.12","705.28","690.8(A)(4)","706.30"],"a":0,"e":"Relocation only. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 710.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 710.12 (2020); 690.8(A)(4) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 800 General Requirements for Communications Systems","d":2,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, which article covers communications circuits specifically (telephone and similar)?","o":["Article 805","Article 800","Article 840","Article 820"],"a":0,"e":"In 2017 Article 800 was Communications Circuits. In 2020 Article 800 is the general article, and 805 holds the specific communications circuits rules. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Art. 800 / 805; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC Art. 800, 805 (2020); Art. 800 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 800 General Requirements for Communications Systems","d":3,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, the general rule on applications of listed communications wires, cables and raceways is located in:","o":["840.154","800.154","820.154","805.154"],"a":1,"e":"2020 consolidated permitted applications in 800.154. Article 805, 820 etc. keep the substitution tables. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 800.154; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 800.154 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","d":3,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, the general requirements for overhead (aerial) communications and CATV cables are located in:","o":["800.44","820.44 in full","830.44","840.44"],"a":0,"e":"The general Chapter 8 requirements were consolidated in Article 800 in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 820.44; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 800.44, 820.44 (2020); 820.44 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which arrangement is NOT permitted as a service disconnecting means?","o":["A circuit breaker in a meter-main combination","A two-pole molded-case circuit breaker","Two single-pole circuit breakers with a handle tie serving as the 240-volt service disconnect","A fusible safety switch rated for the service"],"a":2,"e":"Holt notes the 2020 NEC deleted the handle-tie single-pole provisions in 230.71. A listed two-pole breaker or fusible switch is a normal service disconnect. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.71(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 230.71(A) (2020); 230.71 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 230.85, the dwelling emergency disconnect must have a short-circuit current rating:","o":["At least equal to the available fault current","No short-circuit rating requirement applies","Of 22,000 amperes only","Of 10,000 amperes regardless of the fault current"],"a":0,"e":"The emergency disconnect is service equipment on the line side of other devices in many installations, so it must be rated for the available fault current (110.9, 230.65).","r":"NEC 230.85 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 210.12(D) exception, the 6 ft extension limit is measured how?","o":["Including all conductors inside boxes","As the total length of cable removed","There is no length limit in 2020","Excluding conductors inside enclosures, cabinets or junction boxes"],"a":3,"e":"2020 clarified the measurement and treated splicing devices as devices. Check the exception to 210.12(D). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.12(D) Exception; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 210.12(D) Exception (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, tamper-resistant receptacles are required in which type of facility under 406.12(4)?","o":["No educational facilities","Elementary schools only","Colleges only","Preschool and all educational facilities, not just elementary schools"],"a":3,"e":"The term elementary was removed in 2020 so the rule covers all educational facilities. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 406.12(4); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 406.12(4) (2020); 406.12(4) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 220 Branch-Circuit","d":2,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, which section contains the floor area calculation rule for general lighting loads?","o":["220.14(J)","220.12","220.42","220.11"],"a":3,"e":"The floor area measurement moved from 220.12 to its own section, 220.11, in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 220.11; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 220.11 (2020); 220.12 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC, Table 310.12 for single-phase dwelling services and feeders applies when the conductors supply:","o":["Only 3-phase loads","A single sub-panel","The entire load of the dwelling unit","Only lighting loads"],"a":2,"e":"310.12 reduced sizes (83 percent of rating) are for single-phase 120/240 V conductors carrying the whole dwelling load. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 310.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 310.12, Table 310.12 (2020); 310.15(B)(7) (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":3,"q":"Which insulation types were added to Article 310 in the 2020 NEC?","o":["MI-2 and MTW-2","RHW-3, RHH-3","THHN-3, THWN-4","XHHN, XHWN and XHWN-2"],"a":3,"e":"ez-pdh notes the 2020 Article 310 additions of XHHN, XHWN and XHWN-2. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 310.10 / Tables; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 310.10 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 311 Medium Voltage Conductors and Cable","d":3,"q":"The new ampacity tables in the 2020 NEC Article 311 cover conductors rated:","o":["601 to 1000 volts","1001 to 2000 volts","Over 100,000 volts","2001 to 35,000 volts"],"a":3,"e":"Article 311 gathers MV cable rules; Article 310 is limited to 2000 volts and below. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Art. 311 Tables; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC Article 311 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards, Floating Buildings","d":3,"q":"The new 2020 section 555.3 gives:","o":["Boat hoist wiring methods","Electrical datum plane distances for floating piers and tidal and non-tidal areas","Maximum voltage for shore power","Ground-fault protection requirements"],"a":1,"e":"555.3 is new; the ground-fault protection rule from 2017 555.3 is now 555.35. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 555.3; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 555.3 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":3,"q":"A new 2020 section in Article 705 that specifies permitted wiring methods is:","o":["705.13","705.25","705.20","705.40"],"a":1,"e":"705.25 is new; 705.13 is power control systems. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 705.25; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 705.25 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 230.66, service equipment may be field evaluated by:","o":["The utility","The manufacturer only","A Field Evaluation Body","The owner"],"a":2,"e":"FEB was added in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.66; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 230.66 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 240.62, reconditioning of low-voltage fuseholders and nonrenewable fuses is:","o":["Allowed for 600 V only","Allowed with a label","Allowed","Prohibited"],"a":3,"e":"240.62 was added in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 240.62; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 240.62 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 338 Service-Entrance Cable","d":3,"q":"Which new term was defined in 338.2 of the 2020 NEC?","o":["Service Drop Cable","Entrance Raceway Assembly","Service-Entrance Conductor Assembly","Meter Base Assembly"],"a":2,"e":"Added in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 338.2; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 338.2 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"A new 2020 list item 517.10(B)(3) excludes additional areas from:","o":["The 210.8 GFCI rules","Article 700","Chapter 3 wiring methods","The Part II wiring and protection requirements for patient care"],"a":3,"e":"Added in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 517.10(B)(3); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 517.10(B)(3) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"The 2020 NEC definition of EVSE was revised to include:","o":["The wireless pad only","The utility meter","The personnel protection system","The vehicle battery"],"a":2,"e":"Revised in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 625.2 EVSE; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 625.2 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 210.70, the required control of lighting outlets is by a:","o":["Wall switch only; occupancy sensors are not allowed","Pull chain on the luminaire","Listed wall-mounted control device","Plug-in timer"],"a":2,"e":"2020 recognized listed wall-mounted control devices (such as smart controls) instead of only wall switches. Find it in 210.70(A) through (C). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.70; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 210.70 (2020); 210.70 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 230.82, which equipment was added to the list of equipment permitted on the supply side of the service disconnect?","o":["Lighting contactors","Ground-fault circuit interrupter receptacles","Emergency disconnects and meter-mounted transfer switches","Branch-circuit panelboards"],"a":2,"e":"230.82 was updated to correlate with the new 230.85 and 702.5(B). Look at 230.82 in the 2020 book. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.82; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 230.82 (2020); 230.82 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 312.6, the wire-bending and deflection rules apply to which additional enclosures?","o":["Surface raceways","Cable tray","Meter socket enclosures","Pull boxes over 1000 volts only"],"a":2,"e":"2020 added meter socket enclosures to 312.6. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 312.6; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 312.6 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 320 Armored Cable","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 320.80(A), when more than two Type AC cables are installed in contact with thermal insulation without spacing, ampacity must be:","o":["Ignored because AC cable is armored","Adjusted per 310.15(C)(1)","Increased 20 percent","Taken from Table 310.15(B)(1) only"],"a":1,"e":"The same change was made to Type MC cable in 330.80(C). Look at 320.80(A) in the 2020 book. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 320.80(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 320.80(A) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 330 Metal-Clad Cable","d":3,"q":"The 2020 NEC added what ampacity requirement for Type MC cables in thermal insulation in 330.80(C)?","o":["Apply only the temperature correction in 310.15(B)(1)","Apply the adjustment factors of 310.15(C)(1) when more than two cables are installed together without spacing","Reduce ampacity by 50 percent","No adjustment is ever needed"],"a":1,"e":"Parallels the 320.80(A) change for Type AC cable. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 330.80(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 330.80(C) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 450 Transformers and Transformer Vaults","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 450.14, a remote disconnecting means for a transformer must be:","o":["Interlocked with the primary fuse","Marked only","Lockable in the open position","Lockable in the closed position"],"a":2,"e":"2020 aligned several disconnect sections (422.31, 424.19, 450.14, 620.51) with the lockable-open rule of 110.25. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 450.14; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 450.14 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators, and Moving Walks","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 620.51(A), an elevator main disconnecting means must be lockable:","o":["In either position","It need not be lockable","In the closed position only","In the open position only"],"a":3,"e":"2020 aligned elevator disconnects with the lockable-in-open-position rule. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 620.51(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 620.51(A) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 680.5, GFCI protection required by Article 680 is:","o":["Limited to 125-volt receptacles","In addition to the GFCI requirements of 210.8","In place of the requirements of 210.8","Optional where 210.8 applies"],"a":1,"e":"Check 680.5 in the 2020 book. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 680.5; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 680.5 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 690.12, AC inverter output circuits that originate in the PV array:","o":["Must be grounded","Must have a 10-second delay","Must be shut down by the rapid shutdown function","Are exempt from rapid shutdown"],"a":2,"e":"2020 clarified that circuits originating in the array are controlled conductors for rapid shutdown. Look at 690.12. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 690.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 690.12 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 702.2, an optional standby system supplies power from on-site generated power or:","o":["Only a second utility service","Only the utility","Stored power","Fuel cell only"],"a":2,"e":"2020 broadened the definition. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 702.2; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 702.2 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":3,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, the requirements for unbalanced interconnections are located in:","o":["705.100","705.40","705.45","705.12"],"a":2,"e":"Article 705 was restructured in 2020 and Parts II and III deleted. Find 705.45. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 705.45; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 705.45 (2020); 705.100 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 800 General Requirements for Communications Systems","d":3,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, general requirements for plenum, riser, general-purpose and limited-use communications cables are in:","o":["820.179 only","805.179 only","725.179","800.179"],"a":3,"e":"Consolidated in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 800.179; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 800.179 (2020); 805.179, 820.179, 830.179 (2017)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 800 General Requirements for Communications Systems","d":3,"q":"In the 2020 NEC, the bonding and grounding of communications cables and primary protectors for all Chapter 8 systems (except Article 810) is covered in:","o":["820.100","800.100","810.21","805.100"],"a":1,"e":"Consolidated in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 800.49 / 800.100; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 800.49, 800.100 (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","d":2,"q":"Under the 2020 NEC 702.5(A), reconditioned transfer switches are:","o":["Permitted for optional standby only","Not permitted","Permitted for generators over 20 kW","Permitted with a label"],"a":1,"e":"Added in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 702.5(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","r":"NEC 702.5(A) (2020)","b":2,"t":2,"s":"2017->2020"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, a special purpose GFCI (SPGFCI) is the protection device used for which circuits?","o":["Circuits with more than 150 volts to ground where ground-fault protection is required, such as certain pool equipment circuits","Only circuits of 50 volts or less","Only AFCI-protected bedroom circuits","Only 120-volt receptacle circuits in dwelling units"],"a":0,"e":"The 2023 NEC added the SPGFCI definition and uses it in 680.5(C) for circuits rated over 150 V to ground (not exceeding 480 V). Class A GFCIs (210.8, 680.5(B)) remain the device for 150 V or less to ground. Look up the term in Article 100 and then read 680.5.","r":"NEC 100, 680.5(C) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 100 Definitions","d":3,"q":"A weight-supporting ceiling receptacle (WSCR) under the 2023 NEC is a device intended to do what?","o":["Act as the disconnecting means for a ceiling fan","Provide GFCI protection for ceiling outlets","Serve as the required lighting outlet in a closet","Support the weight of ceiling-suspended equipment such as a luminaire or paddle fan, within its listed limits"],"a":3,"e":"The WSCR and WSAF terms were added so that boxes, fans and luminaires can be matched to listed support ratings. They are about mechanical support, not ground-fault protection or disconnecting. See Article 100, 314.27(E) and 422.18.","r":"NEC 100, 314.27(E), 422.18 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Which of these was newly added to the 110.3(A) list of equipment examination considerations in the 2023 NEC?","o":["Color of the equipment enclosure","Equipment weight in pounds","Cybersecurity of network-connected life safety equipment","Manufacturer's country of origin"],"a":2,"e":"The 2023 NEC added 110.3(A)(8) so that approval of network-connected life safety equipment considers cybersecurity. It is a new item, not in the 2020 list. Find it in 110.3(A).","r":"NEC 110.3(A)(8) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, an informational note to 110.3(B) recognizes that installation and use instructions for equipment may be provided in which of these ways?","o":["Only as printed material attached to the equipment","Only as a verbal instruction from the inspector","Printed material, a QR code, or an internet address","Only on the equipment nameplate"],"a":2,"e":"The 2023 informational note acknowledges electronic delivery of instructions (QR code or web address). The enforceable rule is still that listed, labeled, or identified equipment be installed and used per its instructions. See 110.3(B).","r":"NEC 110.3(B) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, in other than dwelling units, arc-flash warning labels are required on service equipment and feeder-supplied equipment rated at least:","o":["600 amperes","1200 amperes","2000 amperes","1000 amperes"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC applied 110.16(B) only to service equipment of 1200 A or more (the 1200 A figure is the tempting old answer). The 2023 NEC lowered the threshold to 1000 A and added feeder-supplied equipment. Look in 110.16(B).","r":"NEC 110.16(B) (2023); 110.16(B) (2020)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":3,"q":"Under 2023 NEC 110.20, equipment that is required to be listed and has been reconditioned must be:","o":["Listed or field-labeled as reconditioned","Exempt from any further listing requirements","Approved verbally by the supplier","Labeled with only the original listing mark"],"a":0,"e":"110.20(A) requires reconditioned equipment that must be listed to be listed or field-labeled as reconditioned. 110.20(B) covers equipment not required to be listed, and (C) allows AHJ approval when (A) or (B) cannot be met. Many articles also prohibit reconditioning certain products outright.","r":"NEC 110.20 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, which of these may NOT be reconditioned?","o":["A low-voltage power circuit breaker","An electromechanical protective relay with current transformers","A switchboard section, if the article permits it and it is relabeled","A GFCI device"],"a":3,"e":"2023 article-level reconditioning sections (210.2, 240.2 and others) prohibit reconditioning GFCI, AFCI and GFP equipment, among others. 240.2 specifically permits reconditioning of low-voltage power circuit breakers and electromechanical protective relays with current transformers. Check the .2 reconditioned-equipment section of the article involved.","r":"NEC 210.2, 240.2, 110.20 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, equipment doors that open into the working space must not reduce the egress path below what minimum width?","o":["36 inches","30 inches","24 inches (with a 6-1/2 ft height)","12 inches"],"a":2,"e":"The 2023 NEC added the egress consideration in 110.26(A)(4): 24 in. wide by 6-1/2 ft high. The 30-inch figure is the minimum working-space width in 110.26(A)(2) and the 36-inch figure is a common depth, so they are tempting but wrong. Look in 110.26(A)(4).","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(4) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"In the 2023 NEC, the floor or grade within the required working space must be:","o":["As level and flat as practicable for the full depth and width of the space","Covered by a rubber mat listed for electrical use","Sloped slightly away from the equipment for drainage","Concrete at least 4 inches thick"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC gave working space dimensions but did not mention the condition of the floor. The 2023 NEC added that the floor, grade or platform be level and flat as practicable for the full depth and width. It does not require concrete, a slope for drainage or rubber mats. Look in 110.26(A)(6).","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(6) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"A 'Class A' GFCI, as referenced in the 2023 NEC 210.8, trips when the ground-fault current reaches about:","o":["100 milliamperes","30 milliamperes","5 milliamperes","500 milliamperes"],"a":2,"e":"The 2023 NEC 210.8 uses 'listed Class A GFCI' for personnel protection. Class A devices trip at a nominal 5 mA (range 4 to 6 mA). 30 mA is typical equipment-protection range and 100 to 500 mA is far above personnel protection. Look in 210.8 and the Article 100 definition.","r":"NEC 210.8 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, which receptacles in a dwelling unit kitchen require GFCI protection?","o":["Only those within 6 feet of the sink","All 125 V through 250 V receptacles in the kitchen (single-phase, 150 V or less to ground)","Only those serving the countertop surfaces","Only the 15-ampere receptacles above the counter"],"a":1,"e":"The 2020 NEC limited kitchen GFCI to receptacles serving countertops, which is the tempting old answer. The 2023 NEC removed that limitation, so the refrigerator, microwave and range-hood receptacles are covered too. Look in 210.8(A)(6).","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(6) (2023); 210.8(A)(6) (2020)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, which dwelling unit location gets GFCI protection under the new 210.8(A)(7)?","o":["Only the main living room","An area with a sink and permanent provisions for food preparation, beverage preparation, or cooking, such as a wet bar","Only a bedroom with a bathroom attached","Only the kitchen"],"a":1,"e":"The 2023 NEC added a separate list item for sink areas with permanent food, beverage or cooking provisions. The kitchen itself is item (A)(6). Look in 210.8(A)(6) and (A)(7).","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(7) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Which of these locations was added to the non-dwelling GFCI list in 210.8(B) in the 2023 NEC?","o":["A storage closet with no sink","A buffet or beverage serving area with permanent provisions for food or beverage preparation","An interior hallway","A mechanical room with no water source"],"a":1,"e":"The 2023 NEC broadened non-dwelling GFCI beyond classic kitchens to cover places such as break rooms, coffee kiosks and buffet areas. Dry spaces with no sink or food provisions are not added. See 210.8(B).","r":"NEC 210.8(B) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 210.11(C)(4) clarification, additional branch circuits in a dwelling garage beyond the required 20-ampere garage receptacle circuit:","o":["Must be GFCI-only 15-ampere circuits","Must be supplied by the laundry circuit","Are prohibited in any garage","Are permitted to serve other receptacles and loads in the garage"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 text required at least one 20 A garage circuit with no other outlets, so some read it as prohibiting any other garage load. The 2023 clarification confirms that additional branch circuits may be installed and that in a one-vehicle-bay garage the required circuit may serve other equipment within limits. Look in 210.11(C)(4).","r":"NEC 210.11(C)(4) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, if no receptacle is installed at a dwelling kitchen island countertop, what is required?","o":["Provisions for a receptacle to be added in the future","A GFCI receptacle on the island's side face","A dedicated 30-ampere circuit","Nothing at all"],"a":0,"e":"The 2023 NEC removed the mandatory island/peninsula receptacle but requires a future-use provision, not nothing. The 2020 rule required at least one receptacle. Look in 210.52(C)(2).","r":"NEC 210.52(C)(2) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, where may a required kitchen countertop receptacle NOT be installed?","o":["On a backsplash wall within reach","Below the countertop or work surface","Within 20 inches above the countertop","In a listed pop-up assembly in the countertop"],"a":1,"e":"The 2020 NEC allowed receptacles up to 12 in. below the countertop in certain situations; the 2023 NEC removed that, requiring receptacles to be on or above the surface (not over 20 in. above) or in a listed assembly. Receptacles within 20 inches above and listed pop-up assemblies remain permitted. Look in 210.52(C)(3).","r":"NEC 210.52(C)(3) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, a wall-mounted control for a required lighting outlet may rely only on batteries if:","o":["It is installed in a garage","The control is not accessible to children","The battery is a lithium type","The lighting outlet automatically energizes upon battery failure"],"a":3,"e":"In 2020 a required lighting control could be a battery-dependent device with no fail-safe. The 2023 NEC says switches or wall controls for the 210.70(A), (B) and (C) lighting outlets cannot rely exclusively on a battery unless the lighting outlet automatically turns on if the battery fails. The battery type, location, or child access are not the test. Look in 210.70.","r":"NEC 210.70 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":3,"q":"Under new 2023 NEC 215.15, barriers are required in equipment supplied by feeder taps or transformer secondary conductors to prevent:","o":["Overheating of the disconnect handle","Interference from nearby radio equipment","Corrosion of terminals","Inadvertent contact with energized line-side busbars or terminals when servicing the load terminations"],"a":3,"e":"215.15 mirrors the service rule in 230.62(C), applied to feeders. It protects workers from line-side parts that stay energized even with the disconnect open. See 215.15.","r":"NEC 215.15 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 215 Feeders","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 215.18, an SPD on a feeder supplying dwelling units must have a minimum nominal discharge current (In) rating of:","o":["10 kA","3 kA","5 kA","20 kA"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC required service SPDs for dwelling units only (230.67); feeder SPDs did not exist. The 2023 NEC added 215.18 for feeders and set the same minimum 10 kA nominal discharge current (In), Type 1 or Type 2, installed in or adjacent to the distribution equipment on the load side of the feeder overcurrent device. 3 kA and 5 kA are lower values that do not meet the rule, and 20 kA is not the minimum. Look in 215.18.","r":"NEC 215.18 (2023); 230.67 (2020)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 220 Branch-Circuit","d":2,"q":"Under 2023 NEC 220.57, the load for an EVSE in a service or feeder calculation is the larger of the nameplate rating or:","o":["5000 watts","3600 watts","7200 watts","9600 watts"],"a":2,"e":"In the 2020 NEC there was no 220.57; EVSE loads were treated as continuous loads under Article 625 and were often added at nameplate. The 2023 NEC added 220.57 so the EVSE load is the larger of 7200 W or the nameplate rating, which prevents under-counting low-rated chargers. 3600 W and 5000 W are lower than the minimum and 9600 W is not the floor. Look in 220.57.","r":"NEC 220.57 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 220 Branch-Circuit","d":3,"q":"Under the new 2023 NEC 220.70, when an energy management system limits the maximum current on a service or feeder, the calculated load is based on:","o":["50 percent of the service rating","The EMS maximum setpoint, treated as a continuous load","The lowest nameplate rating of any load","The sum of all connected loads regardless of the EMS"],"a":1,"e":"The 2020 NEC created Article 750 for energy management systems, but Article 220 did not tell you how to count the EMS in a load calculation. The 2023 NEC added 220.70: when an EMS limits the maximum current, the calculation uses the EMS maximum setpoint as a continuous load. Summing all connected loads ignores the EMS, 50 percent of the service is arbitrary, and the lowest nameplate is not the method. Look in 220.70 and 750.30.","r":"NEC 220.70, 750.30 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":3,"q":"The 2023 NEC added 225.41. What does it require?","o":["An outdoor emergency disconnect for feeders supplying one- and two-family dwellings","An outdoor GFCI receptacle on every feeder","An arc-flash label on every panelboard","A second grounding electrode at each structure"],"a":0,"e":"230.85 (2020) covered services. 225.41 (2023) brings a similar outdoor emergency disconnect requirement to feeders supplying one- and two-family dwellings or other structures. A GFCI receptacle, arc-flash label or second electrode is not what the section requires. Look in 225.41.","r":"NEC 225.41 (2023); 230.85 (2020)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":3,"q":"A new 2023 NEC rule, 225.42, requires what for outside feeders supplying dwelling units?","o":["Arc-flash labeling","Ground-fault protection of equipment","A neutral disconnect link","Surge protection"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC required SPDs only at dwelling unit services (230.67). The 2023 NEC added 215.18 for feeders and 225.42 for outside feeders, covering dwelling units, dormitory units, hotel and motel guest rooms and suites, and nursing home and limited-care patient sleeping rooms. Ground-fault protection (230.95), neutral disconnect links (250.30) and arc-flash labels (110.16) are different topics. Look in 225.42.","r":"NEC 225.42 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, service SPD protection is required for services supplying dwelling units and also those supplying:","o":["Agricultural barns","Parking garages","Temporary construction trailers","Guest rooms and suites of hotels and motels"],"a":3,"e":"The 2023 NEC expanded the 2020 dwelling-only requirement to dormitory units, hotel and motel guest rooms/suites, and nursing home patient sleeping rooms. Look in 230.67(A).","r":"NEC 230.67 (2023); 230.67 (2020)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, a service SPD installed under 230.67 must have a minimum nominal discharge current (In) rating of:","o":["10 kA","20 kA","5 kA","No minimum is specified"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC required a Type 1 or Type 2 SPD at dwelling unit services but gave no minimum discharge rating, which is why 'no minimum' is the tempting old answer. The 2023 NEC set a minimum nominal discharge current (In) of 10 kA. 5 kA and 20 kA are not the code values. Look in 230.67.","r":"NEC 230.67 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 230.85, when service equipment is replaced at a one- or two-family dwelling:","o":["The emergency disconnect requirements apply to the replacement","Only the grounding changes","They do not apply to replacement work","Only a label must be added"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC introduced 230.85 emergency disconnects for services to one- and two-family dwellings but did not include a replacement trigger. The 2023 NEC restructured 230.85 to list acceptable disconnect types and to make the requirements apply when service equipment is replaced, plus identification (plaque or directory) of nearby isolation equipment for other energy sources. A replacement is not exempt, and it is more than a grounding change or a label. Look in 230.85.","r":"NEC 230.85 (2023); 230.85 (2020)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Under 2023 NEC 230.62(C), barriers in service equipment protect against contact with energized line-side parts when:","o":["The panel is outdoors only","The service is over 1000 volts","The cover is installed","Servicing load terminations with the service disconnect in the open position"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 text of 230.62(C) required barriers so energized parts are not exposed while servicing load terminations but did not say under what condition. The 2023 NEC clarifies the condition: with the service disconnect in the open position. Covers on, outdoor-only and over-1000-volt limits are not part of the rule. Look in 230.62(C), and compare the new feeder rule in 215.15.","r":"NEC 230.62(C) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Which wiring method was added to 230.43 for service-entrance conductors in the 2023 NEC?","o":["Flat conductor cable","Type NM cable","Open knob-and-tube wiring","Flexible bus systems"],"a":3,"e":"Article 371 (flexible bus systems) is new in 2023, and 230.43 now lists flexible bus systems as a permitted service-entrance wiring method for 1000 V or less. Knob-and-tube and flat conductor cable are not service wiring methods, and Type NM cable is never permitted for service entrance. Look in 230.43.","r":"NEC 230.43, Article 371 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, 14 AWG copper-clad aluminum conductors may be protected at not more than:","o":["15 amperes","30 amperes","20 amperes","10 amperes"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no rule for 14 AWG copper-clad aluminum conductors in 240.4(D); 15 A is the limit for 14 AWG copper, which is the tempting wrong answer. The 2023 NEC added 240.4(D)(3) with a 10 A overcurrent limit for 14 AWG CCA, correlating with the new 10 A branch circuit in 210.23 and the 10 A standard rating in 240.6(A). 20 A and 30 A exceed what 14 AWG may carry. Look in 240.4(D)(3).","r":"NEC 240.4(D)(3) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, the standard ampere ratings table 240.6(A) lists which new rating for both fuses and circuit breakers?","o":["10 amperes","12 amperes","18 amperes","5 amperes"],"a":0,"e":"In the 2020 NEC the standard ratings in 240.6(A) for circuit breakers started at 15 A, while fuses also had additional standard ratings of 1, 3, 6, 10 and 601 A. The 2023 NEC lists 10 A as a standard rating for both, supporting the new 10 A branch circuit in 210.23. 5 A, 12 A and 18 A are not standard ratings. Look in 240.6(A).","r":"NEC 240.6(A) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"A new 2023 NEC section 240.7 requires which devices to be listed?","o":["Only transformers","Branch-circuit overcurrent devices and GFCI devices","Only fuses","Only enclosures"],"a":1,"e":"The 2020 NEC did not have a single section requiring listing of these protective devices. The 2023 NEC added 240.7: branch-circuit overcurrent protective devices, ground-fault protection of equipment (GFPE) relays and breakers, and GFCI devices must be listed. It is not limited to fuses, transformers or enclosures. Look in 240.7.","r":"NEC 240.7 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, overcurrent protective devices (other than supplementary) may not be installed in:","o":["Any bathroom, shower area, or locker room with showering facilities","Only hotel bathrooms","Only bathrooms of dwelling units","Only outdoor shower areas"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC prohibited overcurrent devices (other than supplementary) in the bathrooms of dwelling units, dormitories, and guest rooms or suites. The 2023 NEC broadens it to any bathroom, shower facility, or locker room with showering facilities, so it is not limited to dwellings, hotels, or outdoor showers. Look in 240.24(E).","r":"NEC 240.24(E) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 242 Overvoltage Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 242.9, an SPD must provide:","o":["An audible alarm only","An indication that it is functioning properly","A GFCI function","A built-in disconnect"],"a":1,"e":"Article 242 itself was created in the 2020 NEC, but it had no indicating requirement. The 2023 NEC added 242.9: permanently connected surge-protective devices of 1000 V or less must provide a means to indicate they are functioning properly (such as an indicator light). A built-in disconnect, audible alarm only, or GFCI function is not what the rule requires. Look in 242.9.","r":"NEC 242.9 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under 2023 NEC 250.64(G), a grounding electrode conductor shall not:","o":["Be bare","Be installed in PVC conduit","Be solid copper","Pass through ventilation openings of an enclosure"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC did not address grounding electrode conductors passing through enclosure ventilation openings. The 2023 NEC added 250.64(G), which prohibits a GEC from passing through a ventilation opening so airflow is not obstructed and the conductor is not exposed to heating. The conductor may still be solid copper, bare, or in PVC conduit as 250.64 allows. Look in 250.64(G).","r":"NEC 250.64(G) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Which items were specifically added to 250.109 in the 2023 NEC for maintaining the equipment grounding path?","o":["Wire nuts","Cable staples","Plaster rings and extension rings","Conduit straps only"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 text of 250.109 said metal enclosures could be connected together by wiring methods. The 2023 NEC explicitly names metal plaster rings and extension rings (and covers and fittings), which must be connected to the box to maintain an effective ground-fault path. Wire nuts, cable staples and conduit straps are not named. Look in 250.109.","r":"NEC 250.109 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 300.2(A), Chapter 3 wiring methods are for systems of 1000 V ac or what maximum dc voltage, nominal?","o":["1500 V dc","2000 V dc","600 V dc","1000 V dc"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC stated a single 1000 V limit, so 1000 V dc is the tempting old answer. The 2023 NEC recognizes 1500 V dc to match PV and storage systems. 2000 V dc and 600 V dc are not limits in this section. Look in 300.2(A).","r":"NEC 300.2(A) (2023); 300.2(A) (2020)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"In the 2023 NEC, where is the 83 percent sizing permission for single-phase dwelling unit service and feeder conductors found?","o":["310.14(A)(3)","310.15(B)(7)","310.12","220.82"],"a":2,"e":"310.15(B)(7) is the 2020 location and is the tempting old answer. In the 2023 NEC the rule is in 310.12 with Table 310.12. 220.82 is the optional dwelling load calculation, a different topic. Look in 310.12 and 240.4(H).","r":"NEC 310.12 (2023); 310.15(B)(7) (2020)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 314 Boxes, Conduit Bodies, and Handhole Enclosures","d":3,"q":"What hazard is addressed by the new 2023 NEC 314.5?","o":["Corroded box knockouts","Unsupported ceiling fan boxes","Overfilled boxes","Field-installed screws or fasteners that extend into the box wiring space and could damage conductors"],"a":3,"e":"The new section is about fasteners (such as long cover or mounting screws) projecting into the wiring space. Box fill is 314.16 and fan support is 314.27. Look in 314.5 for boxes and 312.10 for cabinets.","r":"NEC 314.5 (2023); 312.10 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 334.10, NM cable may be used in multifamily dwellings and their detached garages of which construction types?","o":["Types I and II","Types III, IV, and V","Type I only","Any construction type"],"a":1,"e":"Type NM cable is limited to buildings of Types III, IV and V construction (combustible). Types I and II are noncombustible. The 2020 NEC did not allow NM in detached garages of multifamily dwellings; the 2023 NEC does. Look in 334.10(2).","r":"NEC 334.10(2) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 352 Rigid Polyvinyl Chloride Conduit","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 352.44(B), expansion fittings for rigid PVC conduit are also required where conduit is subject to:","o":["Earth movement, such as settling or frost heave","Vibration from motors","Lightning currents","Direct sunlight only"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC addressed only thermal expansion and contraction in 352.44. The 2023 NEC divided it into (A) thermal expansion and contraction and (B) earth movement, requiring expansion fittings where PVC conduit emerges from the ground and could be affected by settling or frost heave. Vibration, sunlight and lightning are not the listed conditions. Look in 352.44(B).","r":"NEC 352.44(B) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 369 Insulated Bus Pipe","d":3,"q":"Which new 2023 NEC article covers insulated bus pipe and tubular covered conductors?","o":["Article 366","Article 369","Article 371","Article 376"],"a":1,"e":"Article 366 is auxiliary gutters, Article 371 is flexible bus systems and Article 376 is metal wireways. The 2020 NEC had no article for insulated bus pipe (IBP) or tubular covered conductors (TCC); the 2023 NEC added Article 369 for those listed products.","r":"NEC Article 369 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 371 Flexible Bus Systems","d":3,"q":"Flexible bus systems are covered by which new article in the 2023 NEC?","o":["Article 372","Article 369","Article 368","Article 371"],"a":3,"e":"Article 368 is busways and Article 369 is insulated bus pipe. The 2020 NEC had no article for flexible bus systems; the 2023 NEC added Article 371, and 230.43 recognizes flexible bus systems as a service-entrance wiring method.","r":"NEC Article 371 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 406.9(C), a receptacle within 3 ft of a tub but separated from it by a fixed wall is:","o":["Outside the restricted zone","Permitted only if GFCI-protected and 20 A","Permitted only if tamper-resistant and above 8 ft","Still prohibited"],"a":0,"e":"The 2023 change keeps the zone from reaching through walls and doors. A receptacle on the other side of a fixed barrier is outside the zone (other rules such as GFCI in 210.8 still apply). Look in 406.9(C).","r":"NEC 406.9(C) (2023); 406.9(C) (2020)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 406 Receptacles","d":3,"q":"Which appliance may be supplied by a single receptacle inside the tub/shower receptacle zone under the 2023 NEC exception?","o":["A portable heater","A clothes iron","An electronic bidet or toilet seat","A hair dryer"],"a":2,"e":"The exception is limited to electronic toilet seats, bidets and personal hygiene devices. It does not cover general bathroom appliances. Look in 406.9(C) Exception No. 4.","r":"NEC 406.9(C) Exception No. 4 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear, and Panelboards","d":3,"q":"A new 2023 NEC section, 408.9, covers which topic?","o":["Panelboard working clearances","Replacement panelboards (interiors) installed in existing enclosures","Switchboard bus bracing","Panelboard grounding bars"],"a":1,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no rule for replacing the interior of a panelboard in an existing enclosure. The 2023 NEC added 408.9 for replacement panelboard interiors. Working clearance is in 110.26, grounding is in 408.40, and bus bracing is a switchboard topic, so those are wrong choices. Look in 408.9.","r":"NEC 408.9 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","d":3,"q":"Under 2023 NEC 440.11, an AC disconnect enclosure door that exposes live parts and is readily accessible to unqualified persons must be:","o":["Painted yellow","Marked with the voltage only","Lockable or require a tool to open","Removable without tools"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no requirement about the door of an AC disconnect that exposes live parts. The 2023 NEC added that if a disconnect door or cover can expose live parts in a place readily accessible to unqualified persons, it must be lockable or require a tool to open. Paint color, being removable, or being vented does not address the hazard. Look in 440.11.","r":"NEC 440.11 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 512 Cannabis Oil Equipment","d":3,"q":"A new 2023 NEC article, 512, covers:","o":["Cannabis oil equipment and systems using flammable materials","Aircraft hangars","Bulk storage plants","Spray application"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no cannabis oil article. The 2023 NEC added Article 512 for cannabis oil equipment and systems using flammable materials in commercial and industrial facilities. Aircraft hangars are Article 513, bulk storage plants 515 and spray application 516. Look in Article 512.","r":"NEC Article 512 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","d":3,"q":"According to the new 2023 NEC 517.6, reconditioned-equipment provisions do NOT apply to:","o":["Patient care-related electrical equipment","Luminaires in corridors","Panelboards in the facility","Receptacles in business offices"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC reconditioned-equipment rules applied broadly. The 2023 NEC added 517.6 stating that those provisions do not apply to patient care-related electrical equipment, which is regulated by other standards. Panelboards, luminaires and business-office receptacles are still covered by the NEC reconditioning rules. Look in 517.6.","r":"NEC 517.6 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards, and Docking Facilities","d":3,"q":"A new 2023 NEC section, 555.15, addresses:","o":["Dock lighting color","Replacement of equipment at docking facilities","Boat lift motor sizing","Shore power cord length"],"a":1,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no rule about replacing equipment at docking facilities. The 2023 NEC added 555.15: modifications or replacements must comply with the NEC, the circuit must be inspected, and damaged existing equipment must be identified and repaired by a qualified person. It does not address boat lift motor sizing, dock light color, or cord length. Look in 555.15.","r":"NEC 555.15 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 300.25, exterior egress lighting serving the exterior door of an exit stairwell enclosure may be:","o":["Supplied from the stairwell lighting circuit inside the exit enclosure","Supplied only from a circuit in a separate building","Omitted if the stairs are enclosed","Powered only by a battery"],"a":0,"e":"The new exception keeps the egress lighting on the same circuit as the interior stairwell lighting, so a loss of one does not leave the exit dark while the other is on. Check 300.25 and its exceptions.","r":"NEC 300.25 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 625.40, more than one EVSE may be supplied by a single branch circuit when:","o":["An energy management system controls the EVSE loads","All the EVSE are installed in the same garage","Each EVSE is rated 120 V","A GFCI breaker protects the circuit"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 rule was strictly one individual branch circuit per EVSE. The 2023 NEC allows sharing under an EMS (625.42, 750.30). Same garage, voltage and GFCI do not by themselves allow sharing. Look in 625.40.","r":"NEC 625.40 (2023); 625.40 (2020)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","d":3,"q":"Which new 2023 NEC section permits bidirectional EVSE and EV power export equipment to be part of a system operating in island mode?","o":["625.101","625.52","625.17","625.49"],"a":3,"e":"625.48 covers interactive systems connected to the utility. The island-mode permission is new 625.49. 625.52 covers ventilation and 625.101 is wireless power transfer grounding. Look in 625.49.","r":"NEC 625.49 (2023); 625.48 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 630 Electric Arc Welders","d":3,"q":"A new 2023 NEC section, 630.8, requires GFCI protection for:","o":["The welder's output terminals","All welder disconnects","125 V, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles that supply hand tools or portable lighting","Only 480 V welders"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no GFCI requirement in Article 630. The 2023 NEC added 630.8: GFCI protection for personnel on 125 V, 15 and 20 A receptacles that supply hand tools or portable lighting in welding areas. The welder output and welder types are not what the rule covers. Look in 630.8.","r":"NEC 630.8 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 680.5, special purpose GFCI (SPGFCI) protection is used for pool equipment circuits rated:","o":["More than 50 volts to ground","More than 120 volts line to line","More than 150 volts to ground","Less than 50 volts"],"a":2,"e":"Class A GFCIs cover circuits at 150 V or less to ground. SPGFCIs cover higher-voltage circuits (up to 480 V) such as 240 V or 480 V pump motors. Look in 680.5(B) and (C).","r":"NEC 680.5 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC definitions, a storable pool is one that is:","o":["Any pool under 1000 gallons","Less than 42 inches deep","Installed entirely on or above the ground and designed for storage and relocation","Any pool with a vinyl liner"],"a":2,"e":"Depth was part of the 2020 definition, which is the tempting wrong answer. In 2023 location (on or above the ground) and designed relocation define it. Look in Article 100.","r":"NEC Article 100 (2023); 680.2 (2020)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 680.10, pool heat pumps and chillers must be:","o":["Sized at 125 percent of the heater load as for resistance heaters","Installed within 5 feet of the pool wall","Listed for pool use, with circuit sizing based on the equipment nameplate","Supplied by 15-ampere circuits only"],"a":2,"e":"Resistance heaters keep the 125 percent rule. Heat pumps and chillers follow nameplate minimum circuit ampacity and maximum overcurrent protection. Look in 680.10.","r":"NEC 680.10 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"A pool equipment room must have at least one receptacle that is:","o":["Located inside the pool bonded grid","AFCI-protected","GFCI-protected, 125 V, 15 or 20 A","A 30-ampere twist-lock type"],"a":2,"e":"The receptacle requirement is for GFCI-protected 15 or 20 A receptacles. It was in 680.22 in 2020 and is now in 680.12 with drainage requirements. Look in 680.12.","r":"NEC 680.12 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 680.54(B), a conductor used for equipotential bonding of a fountain must be at least:","o":["12 AWG stranded copper","6 AWG aluminum","4 AWG insulated copper","8 AWG solid copper"],"a":3,"e":"The 2023 NEC states a minimum size for a conductor used for equipotential bonding of a fountain: 8 AWG solid copper. Stranded 12 AWG, aluminum and larger insulated conductors are not what the rule specifies. Look in 680.54(B).","r":"NEC 680.54(B) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"A new 2023 NEC provision in 690.4 addresses:","o":["PV rooftop vent clearances","PV systems on aircraft","PV used only for pools","PV equipment floating on bodies of water"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no specific rules for floating PV. The 2023 NEC added floating PV system provisions to 690.4: equipment must be listed or identified for the environment (water and wave action) and wiring must be suitable for movement, including flexible or floating-rated wiring. PV on aircraft and pool-only PV are not what the section covers. Look in 690.4.","r":"NEC 690.4(G) (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 690.12, rapid shutdown is not required for PV arrays on:","o":["Roofs with metal decking only","All residential roofs","All buildings over 2 stories","Detached nonenclosed structures such as carports or canopies"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC applied rapid shutdown to PV on or in buildings, including carports and canopies that are part of or attached to a building. The 2023 NEC added Exception No. 2 for detached, nonenclosed structures such as carports and canopies, but rooftop PV on enclosed buildings still needs rapid shutdown. Look in 690.12.","r":"NEC 690.12 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 691 Large-Scale Photovoltaic Electric Supply Stations","d":3,"q":"In the 2023 NEC, 691.10 for large-scale PV stations is titled:","o":["Ground-Fault Detection","Arc-Fault Mitigation","Fire Mitigation","Rapid Shutdown"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 NEC titled 691.10 'Arc-Fault Mitigation.' The 2023 NEC retitled it 'Fire Mitigation' and added an informational note, broadening it beyond arc faults. Rapid shutdown (690.12) and ground-fault detection (690.41) are in other sections. Look in 691.10.","r":"NEC 691.10 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":3,"q":"In addition to testing and maintenance, the 2023 NEC 700.3 requires emergency systems to be:","o":["Operated only from utility power","Commissioned","Disconnected annually","Replaced every 10 years"],"a":1,"e":"The 2020 NEC required emergency systems to be tested and maintained. The 2023 NEC added commissioning in 700.3 (and 701.3 for legally required standby). Replacement every 10 years, annual disconnection, or utility-only operation are not requirements. Look in 700.3.","r":"NEC 700.3 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":3,"q":"A new 2023 NEC 700.8 requires emergency system switchboards and panelboards to have:","o":["Arc-flash relays","A second main breaker","A surge-protective device","GFCI protection"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no surge protection rule for emergency system distribution equipment. The 2023 NEC added 700.8: a surge-protective device must be installed in or on emergency system switchboards and panelboards (switchgear). Arc-flash relays, a second main breaker or GFCI protection are not required. Look in 700.8.","r":"NEC 700.8 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 700.32, when an overcurrent device in an emergency system is replaced:","o":["The device must be oversized","No evaluation is needed","Selective coordination must be re-evaluated","Only a label is needed"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 NEC required overcurrent devices in emergency systems to be selectively coordinated (700.32) but did not say what happens after changes. The 2023 NEC requires the coordination to be selected by a qualified person and documented, and to be re-evaluated when an overcurrent device is replaced or the system is modified; 701.32 and 708.54 are parallel. No evaluation, a label only, or oversizing the new device is wrong. Look in 700.32(B) and (C).","r":"NEC 700.32 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","d":3,"q":"A new Part III added to Article 705 in the 2023 NEC covers:","o":["Interconnected systems operating in island mode","Fuel cells only","Wind turbines only","Utility metering"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC Article 705 had Part I (General) and Part II (Microgrid Systems) only. The 2023 NEC added Part III for interconnected systems operating in island mode (705.80 power source capacity, 705.81 voltage and frequency control, 705.82 single 120 V supply). Utility metering, fuel cells only and wind turbines only are not the topics. Look in 705.80 through 705.82.","r":"NEC 705.80, 705.81, 705.82 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 706.7, commissioning of an energy storage system is NOT required at:","o":["One- and two-family dwellings","Hospitals","Factories","Schools"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no commissioning requirement for energy storage systems. The 2023 NEC divided 706.7 into (A) commissioning and (B) maintenance; commissioning is required before the ESS is placed in service except in one- and two-family dwellings. Hospitals, factories and schools are not exempt. Look in 706.7.","r":"NEC 706.7 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 710 Stand-Alone Systems","d":3,"q":"In the 2023 NEC, where are the requirements for stand-alone PV systems found?","o":["706.10","705.10","690.10","710.15"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC put the stand-alone PV system requirements in 690.10. The 2023 NEC removed 690.10 and placed the stand-alone rules in 710.15. 705.10 is the system directory and 706.10 is ESS scope. Look in 710.15.","r":"NEC 710.15 (2023); 690.10 (2020)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 722 Cables for Power-Limited Circuits","d":3,"q":"A new 2023 NEC article, 722, covers:","o":["Elevators","Fire pumps","Optical fiber only","Cables for power-limited and fault-managed power circuits"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC spread power-limited and fire alarm cable requirements across Articles 725, 760 and 770. The 2023 NEC added Article 722 for cables for power-limited circuits (Class 2 and 3), power-limited fire alarm circuits and the new Class 4 fault-managed power circuits. Optical fiber is Article 770, fire pumps are 695 and elevators are 620. Look in Article 722.","r":"NEC Article 722 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 724 Class 1 Circuits","d":3,"q":"In the 2023 NEC, Class 1 circuits are covered in:","o":["Article 726","Article 725","Article 724","Article 727"],"a":2,"e":"Article 725 covered Class 1 circuits in the 2020 NEC. In the 2023 NEC Class 1 circuits (limited to 30 V and 1000 VA when power-limited) are in new Article 724. Article 726 is Class 4 fault-managed power, and Article 727 is instrumentation tray cable. Look in Article 724.","r":"NEC Article 724, 725 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 726 Class 4 Fault-Managed Power Systems","d":3,"q":"Class 4 fault-managed power systems are covered by:","o":["Article 726","Article 722","Article 724","Article 725"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no Class 4 article. The 2023 NEC added Article 726 for Class 4 fault-managed power systems, which detect faults and limit energy transfer. Article 725 is Class 2/3, 724 is Class 1 and 722 is the cable article. Look in Article 726.","r":"NEC Article 726 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 750 Energy Management Systems","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 750.6, an energy management system must be:","o":["Installed by a utility","Rated 600 volts","Installed outdoors","Listed"],"a":3,"e":"Article 750 (energy management systems) was new in the 2020 NEC but had no general listing rule. The 2023 NEC added 750.6, which requires energy management systems to be listed. Installation outdoors, a 600 volt rating, or utility installation are not requirements. Look in 750.6.","r":"NEC 750.6 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 530 Motion Picture and Television Studios","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, which term was added to the Article 100 definitions to support the rewritten Article 530?","o":["Location, meaning a place outside a studio where production is filmed or recorded","Dimmer, meaning a switch that controls only incandescent lamps","Stage, meaning a studio floor under 1000 square feet","Portable, meaning anything under 20 pounds"],"a":0,"e":"The 2023 NEC rewrote Article 530 for motion picture and television production. The new definition of Location covers temporary production sites outside the studio. The other options are invented definitions. Look in Article 100 and Article 530.","r":"NEC Article 530, Article 100 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","d":3,"q":"Which circuits does Article 725 cover in the 2023 NEC?","o":["Class 1, Class 2 and Class 3 circuits","Only fire alarm circuits","Class 2 and Class 3 remote-control, signaling, and power-limited circuits","Class 4 fault-managed power circuits only"],"a":2,"e":"In 2020 Article 725 also covered Class 1, which is the tempting old answer. In 2023 Class 1 is in Article 724 and Class 4 is in Article 726. Fire alarm circuits remain in Article 760.","r":"NEC Article 725 (2023); Article 724 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC 240.11, if one feeder overcurrent device must be selectively coordinated with the service overcurrent device, then:","o":["Only feeders over 400 A need coordination","All feeder overcurrent devices supplied directly by the service overcurrent device must also be selectively coordinated with it","No other feeder devices need coordination","All branch circuit devices must be current-limiting"],"a":1,"e":"The new section prevents a non-coordinated feeder from tripping the service device. It applies when another section (such as 700.32 or 701.32) already requires coordination for one feeder. Look in 240.11.","r":"NEC 240.11 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2023 NEC, the 6 ft distance used for sink-area GFCI is measured along:","o":["The wall surface only","The shortest path the power-supply cord would follow without piercing a floor, wall, ceiling, or fixed barrier","A straight line through walls","The distance from the receptacle to the panelboard"],"a":1,"e":"The measurement is a cord path, not a straight line, and it cannot go through fixed barriers. Look in 210.8(A)(7) and 210.8(B) for the measurement language.","r":"NEC 210.8 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Under 2023 NEC 110.9, equipment intended to interrupt current at fault levels must have an interrupting rating at least equal to:","o":["125 percent of the continuous load","The ampere rating of the conductors","The available fault current at the line terminals","The full-load current of the load"],"a":2,"e":"The 2023 wording is a clarification to 'available fault current'. The technical requirement did not change. Look in 110.9.","r":"NEC 110.9 (2023)","b":4,"t":4,"s":"2020->2023"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit","d":1,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, in which article are branch-circuit, feeder and service load calculations found?","o":["Article 210","Article 130","Article 220","Article 120"],"a":3,"e":"In the 2023 NEC these rules were Article 220. The 2026 NEC moved them to Article 120 (Article 220 is no longer the load calculation article). Do not confuse it with Article 130, which is now energy management/power control systems. Per Texas municipal 2026 NEC significant-changes summaries, IAEI Magazine, EC&M and Electrical Contractor magazine. In the book, use the Article 120 table of contents and the Annex D examples.","r":"NEC 120.1 (2026); Art. 220 (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 130 Energy Management Systems","d":1,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, energy management systems are covered in which article?","o":["Article 130","Article 750","Article 706","Article 120"],"a":0,"e":"Article 750 (2023) was relocated to Article 130 in 2026, per the Texas 2026 NEC significant-changes summary, IAEI Magazine, EC&M and Minnesota DLI's change summary. Article 750 still exists in 2026 but now deals with grounding and bonding of limited-energy systems (IAEI). Find it in the 2026 Chapter 1 table of contents.","r":"NEC Art. 130 (2026); Art. 750 (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 90 / Chapter 8","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, how does Chapter 8 relate to Chapters 1 through 4?","o":["Chapter 8 was deleted entirely","Chapter 8 is still independent of Chapters 1-7 unless specifically referenced","Chapter 8 is no longer independent; the general rules in Chapters 1-4 apply to it","Chapter 8 now overrides Chapters 1-4"],"a":2,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's 2026 change summary, IAEI Magazine, Belden and Electrical Contractor magazine, the independence of Chapter 8 was removed (described as among the biggest structural changes in 50+ years). Limited-energy content is consolidated in Chapter 7 (Arts. 720-760).","r":"NEC 90.3 (2026); 90.3 (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Global terminology","d":1,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, the term 'overcurrent protective device' is replaced throughout by:","o":["OPD","OCPD","OCD","BCOPD"],"a":1,"e":"Texas municipal summaries (over 700 uses) and EC&M's top-25 article both note the global OCPD change. EC&M also notes the older term 'overcurrent device' was updated.","r":"NEC global changes (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Chapter 7 Limited-energy articles 720-723, 742, 750","d":3,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, which article covers limited-energy cables?","o":["Article 722","Article 721","Article 723","Article 720"],"a":0,"e":"EC&M, IAEI Magazine and Belden's article list the new limited-energy articles (Belden's list is pre-publication/provisional). Article 722 is limited-energy cable, 720 is installation/wiring methods, 721 is power sources and 723 is raceways, cable routing assemblies and trays.","r":"NEC Arts. 720-723, 742, 750 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 624 Electric Self-Propelled Vehicle Power Transfer","d":1,"q":"Which 2026 NEC article covers power transfer for electric self-propelled vehicles such as forklifts and golf carts that are not 'electric vehicles' under Article 100?","o":["Article 625","Article 624","Article 691","Article 626"],"a":1,"e":"EC&M's list, IAEI Magazine's EV article, Minnesota DLI and the Mike Holt 2026 guide all identify new Article 624. Article 625 stays for on-road EV charging; Article 626 is electrified truck parking spaces.","r":"NEC Art. 624 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 100 Definitions - limited energy","d":3,"q":"The new Article 100 term 'Cable, Limited-Energy' in the 2026 NEC is intended to:","o":["Replace the definition of power-limited circuit cable only","Define medium-voltage cable","Cover all limited-energy cable types in one defined term, including Class 4 fault-managed power","Define only communications cable"],"a":2,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary, Belden's coverage and Electrical Contractor magazine (NECA article), the term was created largely because 'low-voltage' and 'power-limited' no longer describe Class 4 fault-managed power systems.","r":"NEC Art. 100 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 100 Definitions - PCS / EMS","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which system is defined to monitor and control power so as to prevent overload conditions (for example limiting loads to protect a backup source)?","o":["Interactive inverter","Transfer equipment","Power control system (PCS)","Energy management system (EMS) in the 2023 sense"],"a":2,"e":"Per EC&M's 'Key Revisions to Chapter 1' and Electrical Contractor magazine (NECA article), PCS is a new definition (EMS wording also changed), and 120.7 and Article 130 Part II were built around it. Solar/ESS articles note PCS listing to UL 3141.","r":"NEC Art. 100, 120.7, 130.2 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 100 Definitions - GFCI classes","d":2,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, a traditional 4-6 mA personnel-protection GFCI is also called a:","o":["Class C SPGFCI","Class E SPGFCI","Class D SPGFCI","Class A GFCI"],"a":3,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary, EC&M ('Key Revisions to Chapter 2'), IAEI Magazine and Interstates, the 2026 Article 100 adds Class A as an alternate term and defines Class C, D and E SPGFCIs. Class C is the one cited for HVAC under 210.8(F).","r":"NEC Art. 100 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 100 Definitions - EV vs ESV","d":2,"q":"A battery-electric golf cart or forklift is treated under the 2026 NEC as:","o":["An electric self-propelled vehicle covered by Article 624","An electric vehicle covered by Article 625","Covered by Article 480 only","Outside the scope of the NEC"],"a":0,"e":"IAEI Magazine's EV infrastructure article (EV vs ESV definitions) and Minnesota DLI's change summary (Article 624 is for vehicles not meeting the Article 100 EV definition) both support this. EC&M adds that Article 624 includes forklifts, golf carts and electric boats.","r":"NEC Art. 100, 624, 625 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 110 General Requirements","d":2,"q":"Under 2026 110.3(B), if manufacturer installation instructions would allow something the NEC prohibits:","o":["The AHJ must approve the instructions case by case","Both are optional for listed equipment","The instructions govern because the product is listed","The NEC still governs; instructions cannot make an installation less stringent than the Code"],"a":3,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, Minnesota DLI (which gives GFCI as an example), the Texas summary and Electrical Contractor magazine, a sentence was added requiring instructions to yield NEC-compliant installation and use.","r":"NEC 110.3(B) (2026); 110.3(B) (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 110 General Requirements","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, equipment doors are checked for obstructing the working space egress path with the doors open:","o":["120 degrees","45 degrees","90 degrees","180 degrees"],"a":2,"e":"Per EC&M's 'Key Revisions to Chapter 1', Electrical Contractor magazine (NECA article), Interstates and the Texas summary, doors are measured at 90 degrees. Minnesota DLI describes it as assessing door obstruction regardless of position, removability or swing angle. Egress dimensions 24 in. wide and 6.5 ft high come from the NECA article.","r":"NEC 110.26 (2026); 110.26 (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 110 General Requirements","d":3,"q":"The 2026 110.26(C)(2) large-equipment entrance and egress rule now also applies to:","o":["Only service disconnects, as before","Dwelling unit panelboards","Feeder disconnecting means (see 225.33(A)) in addition to service disconnects","Only generator disconnects"],"a":2,"e":"Per Electrical Contractor magazine (NECA article), Minnesota DLI's change summary, Interstates and the Texas summary. The NECA article states the 1200 A / 6 ft thresholds for the prior rule.","r":"NEC 110.26(C)(2) (2026); 110.26(C)(2) (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 120 Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, 3 VA per square foot is retained in 120.13 for:","o":["Determining the minimum number of general lighting and receptacle branch circuits in a dwelling","Sizing the service conductors","The optional method feeder calculation","Sizing the grounding electrode conductor"],"a":0,"e":"Per EC&M (dwelling unit calculations article), Kopperfield, Electrical Contractor magazine (NECA article) and the Mike Holt study guide, 120.13 prevents the circuit count from falling when the service load value dropped to 2 VA. Example: 5000 sq ft x 3 VA = 15,000 VA, 125 A at 120 V, so 9 15-A or 7 20-A circuits.","r":"NEC 120.13 (2026); 220.14/220.41 (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 120 Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"In the 2026 optional method for dwellings, EVSE load is counted at:","o":["50 percent of nameplate","100 percent of EVSE nameplate rating, no demand factor","125 percent of nameplate","80 percent of the branch-circuit rating"],"a":1,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, LightNow's article and the Mike Holt study guide (EVSE at 100 percent of nameplate). Do not confuse with the 80 percent figure used for EVSE added to existing-dwelling calculations in 120.83.","r":"NEC 120.82(D) (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 130 Power Control Systems","d":2,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, Article 130 Part II covers:","o":["Fire pump controllers","Power control systems (PCS)","Optional standby systems","Overvoltage protection"],"a":1,"e":"EC&M's top-25 article, Minnesota DLI's change summary and Electrical Contractor magazine (NECA article) describe Article 130 Part II and note EMS/PCS must be listed (informational notes reference UL 916 and UL 3141).","r":"NEC Art. 130, 130.2, 130.50 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 6 AWG or smaller grounded conductor may be identified by:","o":["A single non-green stripe, in addition to the previously recognized methods","Only a green stripe","A tag at both ends only","A red stripe only"],"a":0,"e":"Per EC&M ('Key Revisions to Chapter 2'), Minnesota DLI's change summary and a 2026 training quiz (stripe or other marking for 6 AWG and smaller). Check 200.7 in the 2026 book for exact placement.","r":"NEC 200.7(A)(9) (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, an outdoor listed HVAC unit at a dwelling may be protected by:","o":["No ground-fault protection is ever allowed","Any AFCI breaker","A Class C SPGFCI, with a warning marking at the disconnect","Only a Class A GFCI"],"a":2,"e":"Per EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2' (Exception 3 new), Minnesota DLI's FAQ and change summary (marking text), IAEI Magazine and EC&M's top-25 article (Sept. 1, 2026 date on the older exception). Timing of the old exception differs among sources, so read the exception text in your adopted edition.","r":"NEC 210.8(F) Ex. 3 (2026); 210.8(F) Ex. (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, a GFCI marked 'HF' is intended to address:","o":["Weather resistance","Arc faults","High-frequency ground-fault current from inverter-driven equipment","Higher voltage ratings"],"a":2,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary, IAEI Magazine, EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2' and EC&M's Chapter 2 coverage (two new informational notes about GFCI device changes).","r":"NEC 210.8 Info. Note (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, GFCI protection for accessory buildings (storage/work use) at a dwelling applies:","o":["Only where the floor is at or below grade","Only if the building has a bathroom","Regardless of whether the floor is at or below grade","Only for 20 A receptacles above 6 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's FAQ and a 2026 NEC course outline listing 210.8(A)(3) for accessory buildings. Minnesota explains elevation does not remove the shock hazard. Habitable areas in or above a garage are treated the same as accessory building habitable areas.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(2), (A)(3) (2026); 210.8(A)(2) (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"New 210.52(C)(4) in the 2026 NEC addresses:","o":["Spacing of island receptacles","Where to add a GFCI","AFCI exceptions","Locations where countertop receptacle outlets are prohibited, including within 24 in. of the countertop"],"a":3,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article (item 11), Minnesota DLI's change summary (new subdivision) and its FAQ (adjacent walls extending from base cabinets, measured from the nearest surface edge).","r":"NEC 210.52(C)(4) (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under 2026 210.70(A)(1) Exception No. 3, the lighting for a closet-type laundry area may be provided by:","o":["A lighting outlet located outside the laundry area","A receptacle-controlled lamp only","No lighting at all","A battery lantern"],"a":0,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary and a 2026 training quiz (closet laundry light/control changes) and the 2026 NEC course outlines listing 210.70(A)(1).","r":"NEC 210.70(A)(1) Ex. 3 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, dormitory guest rooms/suites with permanent provisions for cooking are:","o":["Exempt from AFCI and GFCI requirements","Required to use 15 A circuits only","Covered only by Article 517","Covered by the same branch-circuit requirements as hotel and motel guest rooms with cooking"],"a":3,"e":"Per EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2' (dormitories added) and a 2026 training quiz (rules now apply to dormitories and break rooms with permanent cooking). Detail on exact lists should be read in 210.17.","r":"NEC 210.17 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 215 / 230 Surge protection","d":2,"q":"Which occupancy is newly added to the 2026 surge-protection requirements for sleeping quarters?","o":["Parking garages","Sleeping quarters in fire, police, ambulance, rescue and ranger stations","Warehouses","Retail stores"],"a":1,"e":"Per EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2' (230.67(A) item 5 new), RectorSeal's 2026 SPD article, the Texas summary (emergency responder sleeping areas) and Minnesota DLI's FAQ (10 kA In, Type 1 or 2).","r":"NEC 215.18, 230.67(A) (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":3,"q":"For an outside feeder disconnect serving a dwelling in the 2026 NEC, the disconnect must be readily accessible and:","o":["On or within sight of the building served","At the utility pole only","Inside the first floor only","Anywhere on the property"],"a":0,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's FAQ and change summary, Electrical Contractor magazine (Chapter 2 coverage; 225.31 'within sight' approach) and an ecmag dwelling emergency disconnect article (225.41, 110.29). Within sight is the Article 100 definition (50 ft).","r":"NEC 225.31(A), (B), 225.41 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, the service disconnecting means for a one- or two-family dwelling must be located:","o":["Nearest the point of entrance inside the dwelling","At the meter only if the utility requires","In a readily accessible outdoor location on or within sight of the dwelling","Inside the garage only"],"a":2,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2', Minnesota DLI, IAEI Magazine and Electrical Contractor magazine. This effectively folds the 230.85 emergency disconnect into the service disconnect rule.","r":"NEC 230.70(A)(1) (2026); 230.70, 230.85 (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, the emergency disconnect requirements for one- and two-family dwellings are found in:","o":["225.42","230.95","230.85 unchanged","230.70 (230.85 was deleted)"],"a":3,"e":"EC&M's top-25 article, EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2', Minnesota DLI and Electrical Contractor magazine note 230.85 was removed and merged into 230.70. A utility disconnect no longer counts as the emergency disconnect unless it complies (Minnesota DLI).","r":"NEC 230.70 (2026); 230.85 (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, the enclosure marking for a one- and two-family dwelling emergency disconnect must be:","o":["Any legible handwritten label","Black text on yellow, 1/4 in.","White text on a red background, letters at least 1/2 in. high","Red text on white, 1 in."],"a":2,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article and EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2' (both give red background, white text, 1/2 in.), citing 230.70(B). Both attribute the content to the relocated 230.85.","r":"NEC 230.70(B) (2026); 230.85 (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, a plaque or directory at the service disconnect is required to identify:","o":["The utility meter number","The ground resistance of the electrode","The location of disconnects for other energy sources not at the service disconnect","The date of the load calculation"],"a":2,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary, EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2' (applied only to dwelling emergency disconnects in 2023), EC&M's top-25 article, the Texas summary and Interstates.","r":"NEC 230.70(D) (2026); 230.85 (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Under 2026 230.70(F), a remote-control device that trips the service disconnect:","o":["Is prohibited entirely","Is required for all dwellings","Qualifies as the service disconnecting means","May operate the disconnect but does not itself qualify as the service disconnecting means"],"a":3,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article and EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2'.","r":"NEC 230.70(F) (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"The new 2026 exception in 240.24(E) allows:","o":["Adding OCPDs to an existing panelboard in a bathroom installed under prior editions","New panelboards in any bathroom","Subpanels in tub areas","Panelboards in showers"],"a":0,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary and a 2026 NEC course topic list (where OCPDs are permitted, including in bathrooms). The general bathroom prohibition remains.","r":"NEC 240.24(E) (2026); 240.24(E) (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, a grounding electrode conductor may be spliced using:","o":["Listed grounding and bonding equipment at an accessible location (in addition to exothermic welding or irreversible compression)","Any approved tape","Wire nuts","Twist-on splice caps"],"a":0,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI (FAQ and summary), the Texas summary and a Mike Holt course topic list (permitted splicing methods for GEC). Exothermic welding and irreversible compression connectors stay permitted.","r":"NEC 250.64(C) (2026); 250.64(C) (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 300 Wiring Methods","d":1,"q":"Under 2026 300.4(C), conductors and wiring methods damaged by fire or water exposure must be:","o":["Painted","Replaced","Cleaned and reused","Re-tested with a megohmmeter and reused"],"a":1,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 3', Minnesota DLI and the Texas summary. NEMA GD 1-2019 is for water damage and GD 2-2021 for fire/heat.","r":"NEC 300.4(C) (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 300 Wiring Methods","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 300.13(E), cable ties used to secure cables must be:","o":["Only metal ties","Listed and identified for the purpose","Any plastic tie rated for the temperature","Approved by the AHJ only"],"a":1,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, Minnesota DLI's change summary (listed and identified for securement and support) and EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 3'.","r":"NEC 300.13(E) (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets and Cutout Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, service conductors may NOT pass through:","o":["A CT cabinet","Service equipment enclosures","A listed meter socket enclosure","An enclosure containing switches or overcurrent devices that is not service equipment"],"a":3,"e":"Per EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 3' (312.11) and the Mike Holt 2026 course topic list (312.8, 312.11).","r":"NEC 312.11 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 314 Outlet Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under 2026 314.27(B), fan-rated ceiling outlet boxes are required in dwelling:","o":["Living and sleeping areas at typical or designated fan locations","Garages only","Every room including kitchen pendant locations","Bathrooms only"],"a":0,"e":"Per EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 3' and Minnesota DLI's FAQ. (EC&M describes the 2023 rule as covering nearly all habitable spaces.) Verify the section location in the 2026 book.","r":"NEC 314.27(B) (2026); 314.27 (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 320/330/334 Cable securing","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, supports such as staples and straps used for NM cable must be:","o":["Approved by the manufacturer only","Any metal strap","Listed","Unlisted if painted"],"a":2,"e":"Per EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 3' (334.2), NFPA's first-revision record (320.30 listed ties, staples, straps, hangers) and a 2026 training quiz (320.2, 332.3, 334.2 listed supports; MI staples removed).","r":"NEC 334.2, 334.30, 320.2, 330.2 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 334 NM Cable","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 334.10 exception, NM cable need not have the 15-minute thermal barrier in:","o":["Non-habitable, grade-level storage buildings under 1500 sq ft","Any dwelling over 1500 sq ft","Multifamily buildings","Habitable accessory dwelling units"],"a":0,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary and FAQ (area counts the whole building, not individual storage units).","r":"NEC 334.10 Ex. to (3) (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 408 Switchboards and Panelboards","d":3,"q":"Under 2026 408.6, after a modification that changes available fault current, a non-dwelling installation must:","o":["Replace the panelboard","Do nothing","Recalculate the available fault current and update the field marking","Mark only the voltage"],"a":2,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article (408.6 details), a 2026 NEC course outline and the Mike Holt course list (408.6, 110.10). Details are from one detailed source; confirm exact wording at 408.6.","r":"NEC 408.6 (2026); 408.6 (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 430 Motors","d":3,"q":"Under 2026 430.98(A), an MCC supplied by a feeder must be marked with:","o":["The location of the means to disconnect all power to the MCC","The motor horsepower of the largest load","The date of manufacture","The arc-flash PPE category only"],"a":0,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary and Interstates' 430.98 coverage.","r":"NEC 430.98(A) (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 440 HVAC","d":3,"q":"New 440.15 in the 2026 NEC requires, for a split-system outdoor disconnect in other than one- and two-family dwellings:","o":["Arc-flash labeling only","A GFCI receptacle","Marking that identifies the indoor units supplied","A red disconnect cover"],"a":2,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary, a 2026 training quiz (outdoor disconnect marking for split systems in non-dwellings) and the Mike Holt course list (440.15).","r":"NEC 440.15 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 480 Stationary Batteries","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, Article 480 applies to:","o":["All stationary battery installations (not only standby), with overcharge prevention required","Only lead-acid batteries","Only batteries over 1000 V","Only standby batteries"],"a":0,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary (480.1 note, 480.14 overcharge) and a 2026 training quiz (word 'standby' removed; stationary batteries need overcharge control).","r":"NEC 480.1, 480.14 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","d":3,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, bonding requirements for hazardous (classified) locations are consolidated in:","o":["505.25","250.100","500.30","501.30 only"],"a":2,"e":"Per Interstates, IAEI Magazine's proposed-changes article (new 500.30 for Arts. 501-503) and a 2026 training quiz (500.30).","r":"NEC 500.30 (2026); 501.30, 502.30, 503.30 (2023)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 501-506 Hazardous Locations wiring methods","d":3,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, permitted wiring methods for Class I locations are presented in:","o":["Chapter 9 Table 1","Annex C","Narrative text only","Tables, such as Table 501.10(A)(1)"],"a":3,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary and IAEI Magazine's proposal coverage (new tables in 501, 502, 503, 505, 506).","r":"NEC Tables 501.10(A)(1), (B)(1) (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 547 Agricultural Buildings","d":3,"q":"Which is a recognized equipotential plane construction method in the 2026 547.44(C)?","o":["Aluminum foil","A single 6 AWG loop buried 6 in. deep","Isolated ground rods","Structural reinforcing steel, unencapsulated welded wire or a copper grid"],"a":3,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's FAQ and change summary and the Mike Holt course list (547.44, 555.14).","r":"NEC 547.44(C) (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 555 Marinas","d":3,"q":"Under 2026 555.9, the AHJ may request:","o":["Documentation of an engineered design for the pier distribution system","A GFCI test report only","A manufacturer warranty","A soil resistivity report"],"a":0,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article and Minnesota DLI's change summary.","r":"NEC 555.9 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 555 Marinas","d":3,"q":"Under 2026 555.35(F), GFPE for docking facility feeders must be:","o":["Set to trip at 30 mA","Coordinated and performance tested, with written records available to the AHJ","Installed without testing","Tested only by the manufacturer"],"a":1,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article and Minnesota DLI's change summary (555.35(F)).","r":"NEC 555.35(F) (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 625 EV Power Transfer","d":2,"q":"Under the new 2026 625.4, permanently installed EV power transfer equipment must be installed by:","o":["Qualified persons","Only utility employees","The vehicle manufacturer only","Anyone with a homeowner permit"],"a":0,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, LightNow, IAEI Magazine's EV article and Minnesota DLI.","r":"NEC 625.4 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 625 EV Power Transfer","d":3,"q":"Under 2026 625.5, EVSE enclosures must show for each supply circuit:","o":["Voltage, phases, frequency, full-load current, and short-circuit current rating","The charger brand","Only the voltage","The installer's license number"],"a":0,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, LightNow and IAEI Magazine.","r":"NEC 625.5 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 625 EV Power Transfer","d":3,"q":"The 2026 EVSE emergency shutoff for non-dwelling installations must be located:","o":["Within sight and at least 20 ft but not more than 100 ft from the equipment","Within 5 ft of the charger","Not more than 10 ft","Inside the building only"],"a":0,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, LightNow and IAEI Magazine.","r":"NEC 625.43(D) (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 625 EV Power Transfer","d":3,"q":"2026 625.54 extends ground-fault protection requirements to:","o":["Only receptacle outlets","Permanently wired EV charging equipment","Only residential garages","Only DC chargers"],"a":1,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary, IAEI Magazine and the Mike Holt course list. IAEI notes pending TIA actions on details, so verify current text.","r":"NEC 625.54 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 690 Solar PV","d":3,"q":"Under new 690.4(G), PV calculated currents and voltages may be rounded by:","o":["Truncating to the nearest 10","Rounding up always","Dropping fractions below 0.5 after applying correction factors","No rounding allowed"],"a":2,"e":"Per Solar Builder (Mayfield), Greenlancer and Portlandia Electric Supply.","r":"NEC 690.4(G) (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"chg","c":"Art. 702 Optional Standby","d":3,"q":"The new third option in 2026 702.4 applies to:","o":["Marinas","One- and two-family dwellings using a listed power control system","All commercial buildings","Hospitals only"],"a":1,"e":"Per Solar Builder, Greenlancer, Minnesota DLI and Portlandia Electric Supply.","r":"NEC 702.4 (2026)","b":8,"t":8,"s":"2023->2026"},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, a plaque or directory at the service disconnect is required to identify:","o":["The utility meter number","The ground resistance of the electrode","The location of disconnects for other energy sources not at the service disconnect","The date of the load calculation"],"a":2,"e":"Correct answer: The location of disconnects for other energy sources not at the service disconnect. [NEC 230.70(D) (2026); 230.85 (2023)]","r":"NEC 230 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, a GFCI marked 'HF' is intended to address:","o":["Weather resistance","Arc faults","High-frequency ground-fault current from inverter-driven equipment","Higher voltage ratings"],"a":2,"e":"Correct answer: High-frequency ground-fault current from inverter-driven equipment. [NEC 210.8 Info. Note (2026)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, 3 VA per square foot is retained in 120.13 for:","o":["Determining the minimum number of general lighting and receptacle branch circuits in a dwelling","Sizing the service conductors","The optional method feeder calculation","Sizing the grounding electrode conductor"],"a":0,"e":"Example: 5000 sq ft x 3 VA = 15,000 VA, 125 A at 120 V, so 9 15-A or 7 20-A circuits. [NEC 120.13 (2026); 220.14/220.41 (2023)]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":2,"q":"In the 2026 NEC, the emergency disconnect requirements for one- and two-family dwellings are found in:","o":["225.42","230.95","230.85 unchanged","230.70 (230.85 was deleted)"],"a":3,"e":"Correct answer: 230.70 (230.85 was deleted). [NEC 230.70 (2026); 230.85 (2023)]","r":"NEC 230 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","d":2,"q":"Under 2026 NEC 225.31(A), a single disconnect for an outside feeder to a dwelling can satisfy:","o":["Both 225.31 and the emergency disconnect rule of 225.41","Only the 225.31 requirement","Only the service disconnect requirement","The grounding electrode requirements of Article 250"],"a":0,"e":"2026: one disconnect can meet both 225.31 and 225.41. [NEC 225.31(A) (2026)]","r":"NEC 225 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, supports such as staples and straps used for NM cable must be:","o":["Approved by the manufacturer only","Any metal strap","Listed","Unlisted if painted"],"a":2,"e":"Correct answer: Listed. [NEC 334.2, 334.30, 320.2, 330.2 (2026)]","r":"NEC 334 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 334.10 exception, NM cable need not have the 15-minute thermal barrier in:","o":["Non-habitable, grade-level storage buildings under 1500 sq ft","Any dwelling over 1500 sq ft","Multifamily buildings","Habitable accessory dwelling units"],"a":0,"e":"Correct answer: Non-habitable, grade-level storage buildings under 1500 sq ft. [NEC 334.10 Ex. to (3) (2026)]","r":"NEC 334 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods and Materials","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 300.13(E), cable ties used to secure cables must be:","o":["Only metal ties","Listed and identified for the purpose","Any plastic tie rated for the temperature","Approved by the AHJ only"],"a":1,"e":"Correct answer: Listed and identified for the purpose. [NEC 300.13(E) (2026)]","r":"NEC 300 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods and Materials","d":2,"q":"Under 2026 300.4(C), conductors and wiring methods damaged by fire or water exposure must be:","o":["Painted","Replaced","Cleaned and reused","Re-tested with a megohmmeter and reused"],"a":1,"e":"NEMA GD 1-2019 is for water damage and GD 2-2021 for fire/heat. [NEC 300.4(C) (2026)]","r":"NEC 300 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods and Materials","d":2,"q":"Under 2026 NEC 300.7(D)(3), a warning ribbon is required over:","o":["Direct-buried service conductors only","Underground service raceways as well as direct-buried service conductors","Feeders inside buildings","Branch circuits in slab floors"],"a":1,"e":"2023: the ribbon requirement applied to direct-buried service conductors. 2026: it also applies to underground service raceways. [NEC 300.7(D)(3) (2026)]","r":"NEC 300 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","d":2,"q":"For 10 A branch circuits covered in Article 210 Part II, the 2026 NEC (310.5) allows a minimum copper conductor size of:","o":["18 AWG","16 AWG","14 AWG","12 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"2023: no conductors smaller than 14 AWG copper or 12 AWG copper-clad aluminum. 2026: 16 AWG copper or 14 AWG copper-clad aluminum are allowed on 10 A circuits. [NEC 310.5 (2026)]","r":"NEC 310 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under 2026 NEC 210.12(E), a listed outlet branch-circuit AFCI used to protect an existing branch circuit may be located at:","o":["The service equipment only","Any junction box on the circuit","The last receptacle on the circuit","The first receptacle or switch of the existing branch circuit"],"a":3,"e":"2026: 210.12(E) allows a listed OBCAFCI to be located at the first receptacle or switch of the existing branch circuit. [NEC 210.12(E) (2026)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 312 Cabinets, Cutout Boxes, and Meter Socket Enclosures","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, service conductors may NOT pass through:","o":["A CT cabinet","Service equipment enclosures","A listed meter socket enclosure","An enclosure containing switches or overcurrent devices that is not service equipment"],"a":3,"e":"Correct answer: An enclosure containing switches or overcurrent devices that is not service equipment. [NEC 312.11 (2026)]","r":"NEC 312 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods and Materials","d":2,"q":"Under 2026 NEC 300.11(B), raceways installed in indoor wet locations must include:","o":["A listed grounding bushing","A red warning marking","Provisions for drainage","Expansion fittings at each box"],"a":2,"e":"2026: 300.11(B) requires raceways in indoor wet locations to include drainage. [NEC 300.11(B) (2026)]","r":"NEC 300 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"In the 2026 optional method for dwellings, EVSE load is counted at:","o":["50 percent of nameplate","100 percent of EVSE nameplate rating, no demand factor","125 percent of nameplate","80 percent of the branch-circuit rating"],"a":1,"e":"Do not confuse with the 80 percent figure used for EVSE added to existing-dwelling calculations in 120.83. [NEC 120.82(D) (2026)]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under 2026 NEC 250.53(A)(4), a rod or pipe grounding electrode is initially driven vertically to a depth of:","o":["4 feet","8 feet","6 feet","10 feet"],"a":1,"e":"2026: 250.53(A)(4) says the rod or pipe electrode is initially driven vertically to a depth of 8 ft. [NEC 250.53(A)(4) (2026)]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under 2026 210.70(A)(1) Exception No. 3, the lighting for a closet-type laundry area may be provided by:","o":["A lighting outlet located outside the laundry area","A receptacle-controlled lamp only","No lighting at all","A battery lantern"],"a":0,"e":"Correct answer: A lighting outlet located outside the laundry area. [NEC 210.70(A)(1) Ex. 3 (2026)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a standard metal device box contains no internal clamps or fittings. Two 12 AWG copper 12/2 NM cables with ground enter the box, the four insulated conductors terminate on one single-pole switch on one yoke, and the two equipment grounding conductors are spliced together. What is the minimum required box volume?","o":["11.25 in³","15.75 in³","13.50 in³","18.00 in³"],"a":1,"e":"Count 4 insulated conductors, 1 allowance for the equipment grounding conductors (up to four), and 2 for the yoke: 4 + 1 + 2 = 7 allowances. At 2.25 in.³ each for 12 AWG, 7 x 2.25 = 15.75 in.³. [NEC 314.16(B)(1), (B)(4), (B)(5); Table 314.16(B)(4)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a metal box contains internal cable clamps and one duplex receptacle on one yoke. Three 14 AWG copper 14/2 NM cables with ground enter the box and the six insulated conductors terminate in the box or on the receptacle. The equipment grounding conductors are spliced together. What is the minimum required box volume?","o":["16.00 in³","24.00 in³","18.00 in³","20.00 in³"],"a":3,"e":"Allowances: 6 insulated conductors + 1 for the grounds + 1 for all clamps (one allowance regardless of number) + 2 for the yoke = 10. At 2.00 in.³ for 14 AWG, 10 x 2.00 = 20.0 in.³. [NEC 314.16(B)(2), (B)(4), (B)(5); Table 314.16(B)(4)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a metal box contains five insulated 12 AWG conductors that terminate or are spliced in the box, two 12 AWG equipment grounding conductors, one set of internal cable clamps, and one receptacle on one yoke. Using Table 314.16(B)(4), what is the minimum box volume?","o":["332.10 cm³","369.00 cm³","405.90 cm³","295.20 cm³"],"a":0,"e":"5 conductors + 1 (both grounds) + 1 (clamps) + 2 (yoke) = 9 allowances. Table 314.16(B)(4) gives 36.9 cm³ for 12 AWG, so 9 x 36.9 = 332.1 cm³. [NEC 314.16(B); Table 314.16(B)(4)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a box contains four insulated 14 AWG conductors, two insulated 12 AWG conductors, one 12 AWG equipment grounding conductor, internal cable clamps, and one device on one yoke. Every conductor connected to the device is 14 AWG. What is the minimum required box volume?","o":["22.50 in³","20.50 in³","21.00 in³","21.50 in³"],"a":2,"e":"Conductors: 4 x 2.00 + 2 x 2.25 = 12.5. The ground and clamp allowances use the largest conductor in the box (12 AWG): 2.25 + 2.25 = 4.5. The yoke is double the largest conductor connected to the device (14 AWG): 2 x 2.00 = 4.0. Total = 12.5 + 4.5 + 4.0 = 21.0 in.³. [NEC 314.16(B)(2), (B)(4), (B)(5); Table 314.16(B)(4)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, an unmarked standard 100 × 38 mm (4 × 1½ in.) square metal box is listed in Table 314.16(A) at 21.0 in.³. It contains one device on one yoke, one set of internal cable clamps, and one group of 12 AWG equipment grounding conductors. All conductors are 12 AWG. What is the maximum number of insulated 12 AWG conductors that can be added?","o":["6","4","5","7"],"a":2,"e":"21.0 / 2.25 = 9.33, so 9 allowances fit. Subtract 2 for the yoke, 1 for the clamps, and 1 for the grounds: 9 - 4 = 5 insulated conductors. [NEC 314.16(A), (B); Tables 314.16(A) and 314.16(B)(4)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a box requires 25.0 in.³ of volume for its 10 AWG fill (6 insulated conductors, a ground allowance, a clamp allowance, and one yoke). Which is the smallest-volume unmarked standard square metal box in Table 314.16(A) that is permitted?","o":["100 × 38 mm (4 × 1½ in.), 21.0 in.³","120 × 38 mm (4 11⁄16 × 1½ in.), 29.5 in.³","100 × 54 mm (4 × 2⅛ in.), 30.3 in.³","120 × 32 mm (4 11⁄16 × 1¼ in.), 25.5 in.³"],"a":3,"e":"Table 314.16(A) lists the square boxes at 18.0, 21.0, 25.5, 29.5, 30.3, and 42.0 in.³. The 4 x 1½ in. box is too small; the 4 11/16 x 1¼ in. box at 25.5 in.³ is the first that is at least 25.0 in.³. [NEC 314.16(A)(1); Table 314.16(A)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, six 12 AWG equipment grounding conductors enter a metal box, and 12 AWG is the largest equipment grounding conductor. What total volume allowance is required for the equipment grounding conductors only?","o":["5.625 in³","3.375 in³","13.50 in³","2.25 in³"],"a":1,"e":"Up to four grounds get one allowance; each additional one gets 1/4 allowance. Six grounds = 1 + 2 x 0.25 = 1.5 allowances, and 1.5 x 2.25 = 3.375 in.³. [NEC 314.16(B)(5); Table 314.16(B)(4)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a metal box contains one set of internal clamps and a receptacle on one yoke. Two 12/2 NM cables with ground enter and all four circuit conductors are spliced to two 12 AWG insulated pigtails that are entirely inside the box and terminate on the receptacle. A 12 AWG ground pigtail also lies entirely in the box. What is the minimum required volume?","o":["18.00 in³","15.75 in³","20.25 in³","22.50 in³"],"a":0,"e":"Pigtails with no part leaving the box are not counted. Count 4 conductors + 1 grounds + 1 clamps + 2 yoke = 8 allowances x 2.25 = 18.0 in.³. [NEC 314.16(B)(1), (B)(2), (B)(4), (B)(5)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a box has three 12 AWG conductors that originate outside and are spliced in the box, plus one unbroken loop of 12 AWG conductor that is not less than twice the minimum free-conductor length required by 300.16. There are no clamps, devices, fittings, or grounds. What is the minimum required box volume?","o":["13.50 in³","6.75 in³","11.25 in³","9.00 in³"],"a":2,"e":"Such a loop is counted twice. The count is 3 + 2 = 5 conductors, and 5 x 2.25 = 11.25 in.³. [NEC 314.16(B)(1); Table 314.16(B)(4)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a ceiling box has two 14/2 NM cables with ground (four insulated 14 AWG conductors, two grounds), internal clamps, and one luminaire stud. A domed luminaire canopy sends two 16 AWG fixture wires into the box, where they terminate. What is the minimum required box volume?","o":["12.00 in³","14.00 in³","16.00 in³","17.50 in³"],"a":1,"e":"Not over four fixture wires smaller than 14 AWG entering from a domed luminaire may be omitted. Count 4 conductors + 1 grounds + 1 clamps + 1 stud = 7 x 2.00 = 14.0 in.³. [NEC 314.16(B)(1) Exception; Table 314.16(B)(4)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a multi-gang box has internal clamps and one device on a single yoke that is wide enough to require two gangs for mounting. Four insulated 12 AWG conductors and one group of 12 AWG grounds are present. What is the minimum required box volume?","o":["18.00 in³","20.25 in³","24.75 in³","22.50 in³"],"a":3,"e":"Utilization equipment wider than a single 2 in. device box gets a double allowance for each gang required: 2 gangs x 2 = 4. Total = 4 + 1 + 1 + 4 = 10 allowances x 2.25 = 22.5 in.³. [NEC 314.16(B)(4); Table 314.16(B)(4)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a two-gang metal box holds a switch on one yoke and a receptacle on a second yoke. Two 10/2 NM cables with ground enter; four insulated 10 AWG conductors terminate in the box and the grounds are spliced. There are no clamps. Using Table 314.16(B)(4), what is the minimum box volume?","o":["410.00 cm³","287.00 cm³","328.00 cm³","369.00 cm³"],"a":3,"e":"Count 4 conductors + 1 grounds + 2 yokes x 2 = 4, so 9 allowances. For 10 AWG, 41.0 cm³ each: 9 x 41.0 = 369.0 cm³. [NEC 314.16(B)(1), (B)(4), (B)(5); Table 314.16(B)(4)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 75 × 50 × 65 mm (3 × 2 × 2½ in.) device box is listed in Table 314.16(A) at 12.5 in.³. It has one device on one yoke, internal clamps, and one group of 14 AWG grounds. How many insulated 14 AWG conductors can be added?","o":["4","2","1","3"],"a":1,"e":"12.5 / 2.00 = 6.25, so 6 allowances fit. Subtract 2 (yoke), 1 (clamps), and 1 (grounds): 6 - 4 = 2 insulated conductors. [NEC 314.16(A), (B); Tables 314.16(A), 314.16(B)(4)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a unmarked FD multiple-cover/gang box (min. 60.3 mm depth) is listed in Table 314.16(A) at 24.0 in.³. It holds two devices on two yokes and one group of 10 AWG grounds, with no clamps. How many insulated 10 AWG conductors can be added?","o":["5","6","4","3"],"a":2,"e":"24.0 / 2.50 = 9.6, so 9 allowances fit. Subtract 4 for two yokes and 1 for the grounds: 9 - 5 = 4 insulated conductors. [NEC 314.16(A), (B)(4), (B)(5); Tables 314.16(A), 314.16(B)(4)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a straight pull uses a pull box for 4 AWG conductors. The largest raceway entering is trade size 3. What is the minimum length of the box?","o":["24 in.","30 in.","21 in.","18 in."],"a":0,"e":"For straight pulls the box length shall be not less than eight times the trade size of the largest raceway: 8 x 3 = 24 in. [NEC 314.28(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a straight pull is made in a conduit body for 2 AWG conductors, and dimensions are expressed in millimeters. The largest raceway has metric designator 63. What is the minimum length of the conduit body?","o":["378 mm","630 mm","504 mm","400 mm"],"a":2,"e":"The diameter is taken as the metric designator in the units employed, and straight pulls need 8 times it: 8 x 63 = 504 mm. [NEC 314.28(A), (A)(1)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, an angle pull for 1/0 AWG conductors has a trade size 3 raceway and a trade size 2 raceway entering the same wall in a single row. What is the minimum distance from the raceway entries to the opposite wall?","o":["20 in.","24 in.","18 in.","23 in."],"a":0,"e":"Six times the largest raceway in the row plus the other entries on that wall: 6 x 3 + 2 = 20 in. [NEC 314.28(A)(2)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a splice box for 500 kcmil conductors has two rows of raceways entering one wall. Row 1 has two trade size 3 raceways; row 2 has one trade size 4 and one trade size 2 raceway. What is the minimum distance to the opposite wall?","o":["24 in.","32 in.","21 in.","26 in."],"a":3,"e":"Calculate each row separately and use the largest. Row 1: 6 x 3 + 3 = 21 in. Row 2: 6 x 4 + 2 = 26 in. The maximum is 26 in. [NEC 314.28(A)(2)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, in a U pull box for 4/0 AWG conductors, a trade size 2½ raceway and a trade size 2 raceway enclose the same conductors. What is the minimum distance between these two raceway entries?","o":["27 in.","15 in.","12 in.","20 in."],"a":1,"e":"The distance between raceway entries enclosing the same conductor is at least six times the trade size of the larger raceway: 6 x 2.5 = 15 in. [NEC 314.28(A)(2)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, what is the volume of an unmarked standard 100 × 54 mm (4 × 2⅛ in.) round/octagonal metal box?","o":["497 cm³ (30.3 in.³)","254 cm³ (15.5 in.³)","205 cm³ (12.5 in.³)","353 cm³ (21.5 in.³)"],"a":3,"e":"Standard boxes not marked with volume use Table 314.16(A). The 4 x 2⅛ in. round/octagonal box is 353 cm³ (21.5 in.³). [NEC 314.16(A)(1); Table 314.16(A)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a nonmetallic device box is to be used. How is its volume for box fill determined?","o":["By measuring only if it exceeds 1650 cm³","By using the same-size metal box in Table 314.16(A)","It must be durably and legibly marked by the manufacturer","By using the smallest value in Table 314.16(A)"],"a":2,"e":"Nonmetallic boxes, and metal boxes of 1650 cm³ or less not described in Table 314.16(A), must carry a manufacturer's durable, legible volume marking. [NEC 314.16(A)(2)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a cable connector has its clamping mechanism outside the box. What clamp-fill allowance is required for it?","o":["None","Two allowances based on the largest conductor","One allowance based on the largest conductor","One allowance per cable connector"],"a":0,"e":"A clamp allowance applies to internal cable clamps. No allowance is required for a cable connector with its clamping mechanism outside the box. [NEC 314.16(B)(2)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a box is installed in a gypsum wall. How far may the front edge of the box be set back from the finished surface?","o":["Not more than 3 mm (⅛ in.)","Not more than 6 mm (¼ in.)","Not more than 10 mm (⅜ in.)","Not more than 13 mm (½ in.)"],"a":1,"e":"In or behind noncombustible materials such as gypsum, the front edge of boxes, rings, or extenders must not be set back more than 6 mm (1/4 in.). [NEC 314.20(A)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a box is installed in a wood-framed wall with a combustible finish surface. How must the front edge of the box relate to the finished surface?","o":["Flush with it or projecting from it","Set back up to 6 mm (¼ in.)","Set back up to 13 mm (½ in.)","Set back up to 3 mm (⅛ in.)"],"a":0,"e":"In combustible surfaces, boxes, plaster rings, extension rings, or listed extenders must extend to the finished surface or project from it. [NEC 314.20(B)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, an outlet box is used exclusively for lighting at a ceiling outlet. What is the minimum luminaire weight the box must be designed or installed to support?","o":["3 kg (6 lb)","16 kg (35 lb)","32 kg (70 lb)","23 kg (50 lb)"],"a":3,"e":"At every ceiling outlet used exclusively for lighting, the box must be able to support a luminaire weighing at least 23 kg (50 lb). [NEC 314.27(A)(2)(a)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 2 kg (4 lb) luminaire on a vertical surface is supported on a plaster ring secured to a box. How must the luminaire or its yoke be secured to the box?","o":["With one No. 8 or larger screw","With no fewer than two No. 6 or larger screws","With four No. 6 or larger screws","With no fewer than two No. 4 screws"],"a":1,"e":"Luminaires weighing 3 kg (6 lb) or less may be supported on boxes or plaster rings secured to boxes if secured with no fewer than two No. 6 or larger screws. [NEC 314.27(A)(1)(a)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, what is the maximum weight a listed outlet box or outlet box system may support as the sole support of a ceiling-suspended (paddle) fan?","o":["16 kg (35 lb)","45 kg (100 lb)","32 kg (70 lb)","23 kg (50 lb)"],"a":2,"e":"Boxes for sole support of paddle fans must be listed, marked for the purpose, and support no more than 32 kg (70 lb). [NEC 314.27(B)(1)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, at what size of a pull or junction box dimension must all conductors be secured in an approved manner?","o":["Any dimension over 1.8 m (6 ft)","Any dimension over 3 m (10 ft)","Any dimension over 1.2 m (4 ft)","Any dimension over 0.9 m (3 ft)"],"a":0,"e":"In pull or junction boxes with any dimension over 1.8 m (6 ft), all conductors must be secured in an approved manner. [NEC 314.28(B)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a conduit body encloses only 6 AWG conductors, with no splices, and is not a short-radius type. What minimum cross-sectional area applies?","o":["Three times the largest attached conduit or tubing","One and one-half times the largest conduit","Equal to the largest attached conduit or tubing","Twice that of the largest attached conduit or tubing"],"a":3,"e":"Conduit bodies enclosing 6 AWG or smaller must have a cross-sectional area not less than twice that of the largest conduit or tubing attached. [NEC 314.16(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 314 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which of the following is a standard ampere rating for fuses and inverse time circuit breakers listed in Table 240.6(A)?","o":["95 A","75 A","65 A","90 A"],"a":3,"e":"Table 240.6(A) lists 70, 80, 90, 100, and 110 in sequence, so 90 A is a standard rating. The values 65, 75, and 95 do not appear in the table.","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, in addition to the ratings in Table 240.6(A), which additional standard ampere ratings does 240.6(A) recognize for fuses?","o":["1, 3, 6, and 801 A","1, 3, 6, and 601 A","2, 4, 6, and 601 A","1, 3, 6, and 1000 A"],"a":1,"e":"Section 240.6(A) states that additional standard ampere ratings for fuses shall be 1, 3, 6, and 601. These are fuse-only ratings and are not part of the circuit breaker list.","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a branch circuit uses 6 AWG copper THWN conductors with an ampacity of 65 A from the 75°C column of Table 310.16. The circuit does not supply multiple receptacles for portable loads, and all 240.4(B) conditions are met. What is the maximum standard OCPD rating permitted?","o":["60 A","70 A","65 A","80 A"],"a":1,"e":"65 A is not a standard rating in Table 240.6(A) (60 A and 70 A are adjacent), so 240.4(B) permits the next higher standard rating above the ampacity, 70 A, which is below the 800 A limit. [Calc: next standard above 65 = 70.]","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a feeder uses 3 AWG copper THWN conductors with an ampacity of 100 A from the 75°C column of Table 310.16. The feeder is not a branch circuit for multiple receptacles. What is the maximum OCPD rating permitted for these conductors?","o":["100 A","110 A","125 A","90 A"],"a":0,"e":"The ampacity of 100 A corresponds to a standard fuse or fixed breaker rating, so 240.4(B)(2) does not allow rounding up to the next size. The OCPD may not exceed 100 A. [Calc: 100 A is itself standard, so no round-up.]","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, 4 AWG copper THHN conductors (95 A in the 90°C column) are installed with enough current-carrying conductors that their adjusted ampacity is 76 A. All 240.4(B) conditions are met. What is the maximum standard OCPD rating permitted?","o":["70 A","90 A","80 A","100 A"],"a":2,"e":"Overcurrent protection is based on the adjusted ampacity of 76 A, which is not a standard rating. The next higher standard rating in Table 240.6(A) is 80 A, and 240.4(B) permits it. [Calc: 76 A rounds up to 80 A; 95 A unadjusted would wrongly give 100 A.]","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, three parallel sets of 500 kcmil copper THWN conductors are used, with 380 A per conductor from the 75°C column of Table 310.16, for 1140 A total ampacity. What is the largest standard OCPD rating that may protect the parallel conductors?","o":["800 A","1200 A","1600 A","1000 A"],"a":3,"e":"For an OCPD rated over 800 A, 240.4(C) requires conductor ampacity equal to or greater than the OCPD rating, so rounding up is not allowed. [Calc: 3 x 380 = 1140 A; the largest standard rating not exceeding 1140 A is 1000 A, as 1200 A is greater than 1140 A.]","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 12 AWG aluminum THWN conductor has an ampacity of 20 A in the 75°C column of Table 310.16. No provision in 240.4(E) or 240.4(G) applies. What is the maximum overcurrent protection permitted for this conductor?","o":["15 A","20 A","25 A","30 A"],"a":0,"e":"Small conductor limits in 240.4(D) apply: 12 AWG aluminum and copper-clad aluminum is limited to 15 amperes, even though Table 310.16 shows 20 A. [Calc: Table ampacity 20 A is capped at 15 A by 240.4(D)(5).]","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, 16 AWG copper conductors are protected by a 10 A OCPD marked for use with 16 AWG copper under 240.4(D)(2). What is the maximum continuous load permitted on these conductors?","o":["5.6 A","7 A","10 A","8 A"],"a":3,"e":"Section 240.4(D)(2) allows 16 AWG copper to be protected at 10 amperes only if continuous loads do not exceed 8 amperes. The 5.6 A limit belongs to 18 AWG copper protected at 7 A.","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a feeder is protected by a 400 A OCPD. A 7.5 m (25 ft) feeder tap meeting all of 240.21(B)(2) other than conductor size ends in a single circuit breaker limiting the load. Using the 75°C column of Table 310.16 for copper, what is the smallest tap conductor size permitted?","o":["2 AWG (115 A)","1/0 AWG (150 A)","1 AWG (130 A)","2/0 AWG (175 A)"],"a":1,"e":"The tap ampacity must be not less than one-third of the feeder OCPD rating: 400/3 = 133.3 A. 1 AWG at 130 A falls short, so 1/0 AWG at 150 A is the smallest. [Calc: 400 / 3 = 133.3 A.]","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 3 m (10 ft) feeder tap is made in a field installation from a feeder protected by an 800 A OCPD, and the tap conductors leave the enclosure in which the tap is made. Applying only the minimum-ampacity rule tied to the feeder OCPD rating in 240.21(B)(1), what minimum ampacity must the tap conductors have?","o":["80 A","133 A","267 A","400 A"],"a":0,"e":"For field installations where tap conductors leave the enclosure or vault in which the tap is made, ampacity must be not less than one-tenth of the feeder OCPD rating. [Calc: 800 x 0.10 = 80 A.]","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 7.5 m (25 ft) feeder tap meeting 240.21(B)(2) from a 250 A feeder OCPD uses 4 AWG copper with an ampacity of 85 A (75°C column), terminating in a single circuit breaker that limits the load to the conductor ampacity. What is the largest standard breaker rating permitted at the tap termination?","o":["70 A","90 A","80 A","100 A"],"a":2,"e":"The tap ampacity of 85 A meets the minimum of 250/3 = 83.3 A. Section 240.21(B) states that 240.4(B) shall not be permitted for tap conductors, so the breaker may not exceed the ampacity; the largest standard rating at or below 85 A is 80 A. [Calc: 250/3 = 83.3 A <= 85 A; next-size-up to 90 A is prohibited.]","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 300 A feeder supplies a transformer through a tap meeting 240.21(B)(3). The transformer is 480 V primary to 208 V secondary. What is the minimum ampacity of the secondary conductors, using the primary-to-secondary voltage ratio?","o":["231 A","100 A","43 A","300 A"],"a":0,"e":"The secondary ampacity must be not less than the primary-to-secondary voltage ratio multiplied by one-third of the feeder OCPD rating. [Calc: (480 / 208) x (300 / 3) = 2.3077 x 100 = 230.8, or about 231 A.]","r":"NEC 230 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, transformer secondary conductors not over 7.5 m (25 ft) long are installed under 240.21(C)(6). The primary OCPD is 125 A, and the transformer is 480 V primary to 240 V secondary. What is the minimum secondary conductor ampacity?","o":["42 A","83 A","63 A","125 A"],"a":1,"e":"Section 240.21(C)(6)(1) requires ampacity not less than the primary-to-secondary voltage ratio multiplied by one-third of the primary OCPD rating. [Calc: (480 / 240) x (125 / 3) = 2 x 41.67 = 83.3 A.]","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, transformer secondary conductors not over 3 m (10 ft) long are installed in a field installation under 240.21(C)(2) and leave the enclosure in which the supply connection is made. The primary OCPD is 200 A, and the transformer is 480 V primary to 208 V secondary. What minimum ampacity does the one-tenth rule require?","o":["20 A","67 A","46 A","154 A"],"a":2,"e":"The secondary ampacity must be not less than the primary-to-secondary voltage ratio multiplied by one-tenth of the primary OCPD rating. [Calc: (480 / 208) x (200 / 10) = 2.3077 x 20 = 46.2 A.]","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a single-phase transformer has a 2-wire (single-voltage) secondary and is rated 480 V primary, 240 V secondary. Its secondary conductors have an ampacity of 60 A, and the primary protection complies with 450.5. What is the maximum primary OCPD value that can protect the secondary conductors under 240.21(C)(1)?","o":["30 A","15 A","60 A","120 A"],"a":0,"e":"Primary protection may not exceed the secondary conductor ampacity multiplied by the secondary-to-primary voltage ratio. [Calc: 60 A x (240 / 480) = 30 A.]","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, an adjustable-trip circuit breaker has external means of adjusting its long-time pickup from 400 A to 1000 A and is set at 600 A. It has no restricted access under 240.6(C) and is not remotely adjustable under 240.6(D). What ampere rating must be used for this breaker?","o":["400 A","600 A","1000 A","800 A"],"a":2,"e":"Under 240.6(B), the rating of an adjustable-trip breaker with external adjustment is the maximum setting possible unless 240.6(C) or 240.6(D) applies. [Calc: maximum setting = 1000 A, so the rating is 1000 A, not the 600 A present setting.]","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 242 Overvoltage Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, what is the smallest copper conductor size permitted for SPD line conductors and conductors to ground under Article 242?","o":["16 AWG","12 AWG","10 AWG","14 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Section 242.28 states that SPD line conductors and conductors to ground shall not be smaller than 14 AWG copper or 12 AWG aluminum.","r":"NEC 242 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a lower-rated circuit breaker with a 10,000 A interrupting rating is part of a series-rated combination. Two motors connected between the higher-rated OCPD and that breaker have full-load currents of 52 A and 68 A. May the series rating be used?","o":["No; the 120 A total exceeds 1 percent (100 A) of the 10,000 A rating","Yes; the 120 A total is under 10 percent of the 10,000 A rating","Yes; motor currents matter only above 25 percent of the rating","No; series ratings are never allowed with any motor loads"],"a":0,"e":"Under 240.86(C), series ratings shall not be used where motor circuits are connected between the two devices and the sum of their full-load currents exceeds 1 percent of the lower-rated breaker's interrupting rating. [Calc: 52 + 68 = 120 A > 0.01 x 10,000 = 100 A.]","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, under 240.4(B), the next higher standard OCPD rating is NOT permitted where the conductors being protected are part of which circuit?","o":["A branch circuit supplying one fixed water heater only","A branch circuit supplying a single receptacle for a window air conditioner","A feeder supplying a panelboard for fixed lighting loads","A branch circuit with several receptacles for cord- and plug-connected portable loads"],"a":3,"e":"Condition 240.4(B)(1) excludes conductors that are part of a branch circuit supplying more than one receptacle for cord- and plug-connected portable loads. The other circuits are not excluded by this condition.","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, the center of the grip of the operating handle of a circuit breaker or fused switch, when in its highest position, may be no more than what height above grade, floor, or working platform, unless an exception applies?","o":["1.7 m (5 ft 7 in.)","1.8 m (6 ft)","2.1 m (6 ft 10 in.)","2.0 m (6 ft 7 in.)"],"a":3,"e":"Section 240.24(A) requires that the center of the grip of the handle in its highest position be not more than 2.0 m (6 ft 7 in.) above grade, floor, or working platform, subject to listed exceptions.","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which OCPD may be installed in a bathroom of a new dwelling unit, with no existing-panelboard exception involved?","o":["A 20 A branch-circuit breaker in a new subpanel","A supplementary OCPD built into a bathroom exhaust fan","The main breaker of a new dwelling panelboard","A 15 A fuse cutout for a bathroom lighting circuit"],"a":1,"e":"Section 240.24(E) prohibits OCPDs in bathrooms, showering facilities, and locker rooms with showering facilities, other than supplementary overcurrent protection. The existing-panelboard exception does not apply here.","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, arc energy reduction in 240.87 applies where the highest continuous current trip setting for which the actual OCPD installed in a circuit breaker is rated or can be adjusted is at least what value?","o":["800 A","1000 A","1200 A","1600 A"],"a":2,"e":"Section 240.87 applies where the highest continuous current trip setting is 1200 amperes or higher, then requiring documentation, a clearing-time reduction method, and performance testing.","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which statement about using an instantaneous trip setting as the 240.87(B) arc energy reduction method is correct?","o":["It is permitted, and staff may lower it temporarily","It is not a listed method; only interlocking and relaying count","It is permitted, but temporarily adjusting the setting is not permitted","It is permitted only when paired with zone-selective interlocking"],"a":2,"e":"Section 240.87(B)(5) lists an instantaneous trip setting as a method but states that temporary adjustment of it to achieve arc energy reduction shall not be permitted. Temporary changes are the role of an energy-reducing maintenance switch under (B)(3).","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, where fuses rated 1200 A or higher are installed, 240.67(B) requires the fuse to have a clearing time of not more than what value at the available arcing current, unless another listed means is provided?","o":["0.07 seconds","0.01 seconds","0.10 seconds","0.50 seconds"],"a":0,"e":"Section 240.67(B) states that a fuse shall have a clearing time of 0.07 seconds or less at the available arcing current, or one of the listed alternative means shall be provided and set to operate below the available arcing current.","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, what minimum interrupting rating must OCPDs specified in 240.7 have under 240.16?","o":["2500 A","5000 A","10,000 A","22,000 A"],"a":1,"e":"Section 240.16 states that OCPDs specified in 240.7 shall have an interrupting rating no less than 5000 amperes. Higher ratings are required where the available fault current is greater (see 110.9).","r":"NEC 110 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which of the following reconditioned equipment is permitted by 240.3(A)?","o":["Molded-case circuit breakers","Low-voltage power circuit breakers","Ground-fault circuit interrupters","Low-voltage nonrenewable fuses"],"a":1,"e":"Section 240.3(A) permits reconditioned low-voltage power circuit breakers and electromechanical protective relays and current transformers. Section 240.3(B) prohibits reconditioned molded-case breakers, GFCIs, and nonrenewable fuses.","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which statement matches the Article 100 definition of overload?","o":["Excess load that can cause overheating; faults are not overloads","Any current above ampacity, including short circuits and faults","A ground fault that persists long enough to cause damage","Operation below rated current for more than 3 hours"],"a":0,"e":"Article 100 defines overload as operation of equipment above full-load rating, or of a conductor above its ampacity, that would cause damage if it persists, and states a short circuit or ground fault is not an overload. The broader term overcurrent includes overload, short circuit, and ground fault.","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which describes a tap conductor as defined in Article 100?","o":["A service conductor with its OCPD installed at the load end","A non-service conductor whose OCPD ahead of its supply point exceeds the 240.4 value","Any conductor spliced to a feeder, regardless of how it is protected","A conductor protected at a value below its ampacity at the point of supply"],"a":1,"e":"Article 100 defines a tap conductor as a conductor, other than a service conductor, that has overcurrent protection ahead of its point of supply that exceeds the value permitted for similar conductors protected as described elsewhere in 240.4.","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which of the following is listed in 240.7 as an OCPD for protecting branch-circuit, feeder, and service conductors and equipment?","o":["Thermal overload relays","Ground-fault protection relays alone","Motor starter overload heaters","Solid-state or solid-state hybrid circuit breakers"],"a":3,"e":"Section 240.7(3) lists solid-state or solid-state hybrid circuit breakers alongside molded-case and low-voltage power circuit breakers and the listed fuse classes. Thermal devices that cannot open short circuits are limited by 240.9.","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a high bay manufacturing building has walls over 11 m (35 ft) high, and a tap longer than 7.5 m (25 ft) is made under 240.21(B)(4) at least 9 m (30 ft) above the floor. What is the smallest copper conductor size permitted for the tap conductors under that section?","o":["10 AWG","8 AWG","6 AWG","4 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Section 240.21(B)(4)(7) requires tap conductors to be 6 AWG copper or 4 AWG aluminum or larger. The tap must also have ampacity of at least one-third of the feeder OCPD rating and may not exceed 30 m (100 ft) total length.","r":"NEC 240 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 120/240-volt single-phase service uses one set of 3/0 AWG copper ungrounded service-entrance conductors. The grounding electrode system consists of a metal underground water pipe and a supplemental ground rod. What is the minimum size copper grounding electrode conductor per Table 250.66?","o":["6 AWG copper","2 AWG copper","4 AWG copper","8 AWG copper"],"a":2,"e":"Table 250.66 places 3/0 AWG copper in the '2/0 or 3/0' row, which requires a 4 AWG copper grounding electrode conductor. The 6 AWG limit in 250.66(A) does not apply because the conductor also serves a water pipe electrode, not just rods. [NEC 250.66; Table 250.66]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a service has one set of 500 kcmil copper ungrounded conductors. The grounding electrode system includes a metal underground water pipe and footing rebar. What is the minimum copper grounding electrode conductor size per Table 250.66?","o":["1/0 AWG copper","2/0 AWG copper","2 AWG copper","3/0 AWG copper"],"a":0,"e":"500 kcmil copper falls in the 'Over 350 through 600' row of Table 250.66, which requires 1/0 AWG copper. The 2 AWG size belongs to the row for over 3/0 through 350 kcmil, and 2/0 is for over 600 through 1100 kcmil. [NEC 250.66; Table 250.66]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a service has one set of 250 kcmil aluminum ungrounded conductors. Using Table 250.66, what is the minimum size of an aluminum grounding electrode conductor (water pipe and rebar electrodes are both present)?","o":["4 AWG aluminum","1/0 AWG aluminum","1 AWG aluminum","2 AWG aluminum"],"a":3,"e":"In Table 250.66 the aluminum conductor column reads '4/0 or 250' in the same row as 2/0 or 3/0 copper. That row lists 4 AWG copper and 2 AWG aluminum for the grounding electrode conductor, so 2 AWG aluminum is the minimum. [NEC 250.66; Table 250.66]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, each phase of a service uses three parallel 400 kcmil copper conductors. What is the minimum copper grounding electrode conductor per Table 250.66 for a grounding electrode system that includes a water pipe and footing rebar?","o":["1/0 AWG copper","3/0 AWG copper","2/0 AWG copper","4/0 AWG copper"],"a":1,"e":"Three 400 kcmil conductors equal 3 x 400 = 1,200 kcmil of equivalent area, which is 'Over 1100' in Table 250.66. That row requires 3/0 AWG copper. The 2/0 AWG result comes from wrongly using the 'Over 600 through 1100' row. [NEC 250.66; Table 250.66]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a service has 600 kcmil copper ungrounded conductors. The grounding electrode conductor connects only to two driven ground rods spaced 6 ft apart and does not extend to any other electrode. What is the largest size copper conductor that can be required?","o":["1/0 AWG copper","4 AWG copper","3 AWG copper","6 AWG copper"],"a":3,"e":"Table 250.66 alone would call for 1/0 AWG copper for 600 kcmil. For a conductor connected only to rod, pipe, or plate electrodes, 250.66(A) states it is not required to be larger than 6 AWG copper. [NEC 250.66(A); Table 250.66]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a service has 400 kcmil copper ungrounded conductors. The grounding electrode conductor connects only to a concrete-encased electrode (footing rebar) and does not extend to other electrodes. What is the maximum size of copper conductor required?","o":["4 AWG copper","6 AWG copper","2 AWG copper","1/0 AWG copper"],"a":0,"e":"Table 250.66 would give 1/0 AWG copper for 400 kcmil. 250.66(B) says a conductor connected only to concrete-encased electrodes is not required to be larger than 4 AWG copper. [NEC 250.66(B); Table 250.66]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a copper equipment grounding conductor is installed in a raceway for a circuit protected by a 50-ampere circuit breaker. What is the minimum size per Table 250.122?","o":["12 AWG copper","10 AWG copper","8 AWG copper","6 AWG copper"],"a":1,"e":"Table 250.122 is read by the OCPD rating 'not exceeding' the listed value. A 50-ampere device falls in the 60-ampere row, which requires 10 AWG copper. The 12 AWG size applies only to devices up to 20 amperes. [NEC 250.122; Table 250.122]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a feeder is protected by a 200-ampere circuit breaker, and an aluminum wire-type equipment grounding conductor is permitted in the installation. What is the minimum aluminum EGC size per Table 250.122?","o":["6 AWG aluminum","2 AWG aluminum","4 AWG aluminum","1 AWG aluminum"],"a":2,"e":"The 200-ampere row of Table 250.122 lists 6 AWG copper and 4 AWG aluminum or copper-clad aluminum. The 6 AWG answer is the copper value taken from the wrong column. [NEC 250.122; Table 250.122]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 1200-ampere feeder uses parallel copper conductors in three separate metal raceways, with a wire-type EGC in each raceway. Each EGC is smaller than the largest ungrounded conductor in its raceway. What is the minimum copper EGC size in each raceway?","o":["3/0 AWG copper","3 AWG copper","1/0 AWG copper","4/0 AWG copper"],"a":0,"e":"250.122(H)(1)(b) requires the EGC in each raceway to be sized by Table 250.122 using the rating of the feeder OCPD, not the share of current in each raceway. The 1200-ampere row requires 3/0 AWG copper in each raceway. [NEC 250.122(H)(1)(b); Table 250.122]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, an installer increases the size of the ungrounded conductors of a circuit to reduce voltage drop. A wire-type EGC is installed. What does 250.122(D) require of the EGC (no qualified-person design is performed)?","o":["It may remain at the minimum Table 250.122 size for the OCPD rating","It must be increased to equal the ungrounded conductor size exactly","It must be doubled in circular mil area regardless of the upsizing","It must be increased in proportion to the circular mil area increase"],"a":3,"e":"250.122(D) applies when ungrounded conductors are increased for reasons other than 310.15(B) or (C), such as voltage drop. The wire-type EGC must be increased proportionately in circular mil area, unless a qualified person sizes it under the exception. [NEC 250.122(D)]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a service has four parallel sets of 400 kcmil copper ungrounded conductors (1600 kcmil total). Per Table 250.102(C)(1) Note 1, what is the minimum circular mil area of the main bonding jumper?","o":["160 kcmil","200 kcmil","400 kcmil","800 kcmil"],"a":1,"e":"Note 1 to Table 250.102(C)(1) applies above 1100 kcmil copper and requires an area of at least 12-1/2 percent of the equivalent area. 4 x 400 = 1,600 kcmil, and 1,600 x 0.125 = 200 kcmil. [NEC 250.28(D)(1); Table 250.102(C)(1), Note 1]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a wire-type aluminum supply-side bonding jumper is installed with a single raceway of 500 kcmil aluminum ungrounded service conductors. What is the minimum size per Table 250.102(C)(1)?","o":["2 AWG aluminum","3/0 AWG aluminum","1/0 AWG aluminum","1 AWG aluminum"],"a":2,"e":"Table 250.102(C)(1) places 500 kcmil aluminum in the 'Over 250 through 500' row, which pairs with 2 AWG copper and 1/0 AWG aluminum. The 2 AWG answer is the copper value taken from the wrong column. [NEC 250.102(C)(1); Table 250.102(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a building's service has 750 kcmil copper ungrounded conductors. What is the minimum copper bonding jumper size for the metal water piping system under 250.104(A)(1)?","o":["2/0 AWG copper","1/0 AWG copper","3/0 AWG copper","4/0 AWG copper"],"a":0,"e":"250.104(A)(1) sizes the water piping bonding jumper from Table 250.102(C)(1), capped at 3/0 copper. 750 kcmil is in the 'Over 600 through 1100' row, which requires 2/0 AWG copper, below the cap. [NEC 250.104(A)(1); Table 250.102(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a separately derived system has 4/0 AWG copper derived ungrounded conductors, and metal water piping is in the area served. What is the minimum copper bonding jumper from the grounded conductor to the water piping under 250.104(D)(1)?","o":["4 AWG copper","1/0 AWG copper","6 AWG copper","2 AWG copper"],"a":3,"e":"The jumper is sized by Table 250.102(C)(1) from the largest ungrounded conductor of the system. 4/0 AWG is above 3/0 and within 'Over 3/0 through 350', so 2 AWG copper is required. [NEC 250.104(D)(1); Table 250.102(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a gas piping system in a building is likely to become energized by a 20-ampere appliance branch circuit. What is the minimum copper size of the bonding conductor for that piping under 250.104(B)?","o":["14 AWG copper","12 AWG copper","10 AWG copper","8 AWG copper"],"a":1,"e":"250.104(B) sizes bonding for other metal piping, including gas piping, by Table 250.122 using the rating of the circuit likely to energize the piping. The 20-ampere row of Table 250.122 requires 12 AWG copper. [NEC 250.104(B); Table 250.122]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a separately derived system has 1/0 AWG copper derived ungrounded conductors and its grounding electrode conductor connects to the building water pipe and structural steel. What is the minimum copper grounding electrode conductor?","o":["8 AWG copper","4 AWG copper","6 AWG copper","2 AWG copper"],"a":2,"e":"250.30(A)(5) sizes the conductor under 250.66 from the derived ungrounded conductors. 1/0 AWG copper is in the '1 or 1/0' row of Table 250.66, which requires 6 AWG copper. [NEC 250.30(A)(5); Table 250.66]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a common grounding electrode conductor serves several separately derived systems, and the grounding electrode system includes building steel and a water pipe. If the common conductor is a wire type, what is the minimum size?","o":["3/0 AWG copper or 250 kcmil aluminum","1/0 AWG copper or 3/0 AWG aluminum","4/0 AWG copper or 350 kcmil aluminum","2/0 AWG copper or 4/0 AWG aluminum"],"a":0,"e":"250.30(A)(6)(a)(1) lets the common grounding electrode conductor be a wire not smaller than 3/0 AWG copper or 250 kcmil aluminum. Taps from each system are then sized by 250.66 from that system's derived ungrounded conductors. [NEC 250.30(A)(6)(a), (b)]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 500-VA transformer supplies a Class 2 circuit and has 18 AWG copper derived ungrounded conductors. What is the minimum copper system bonding jumper allowed by 250.30(A)(1) Exception No. 3?","o":["18 AWG copper","12 AWG copper","10 AWG copper","14 AWG copper"],"a":3,"e":"Exception No. 3 applies to a Class 1, 2, or 3 system derived from a transformer of 1000 VA or less. The jumper must not be smaller than the derived ungrounded conductors and not smaller than 14 AWG copper, so 14 AWG governs. [NEC 250.30(A)(1), Exception No. 3]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which statement about the earth in the ground-fault current path is correct?","o":["The earth is acceptable if the electrode resistance is 25 ohms or less","The earth is not considered an effective ground-fault current path","The earth may serve as the path if a ground rod is within 6 ft","The earth is acceptable on ungrounded systems only"],"a":1,"e":"250.4(A)(5)(c) states the earth shall not be considered an effective ground-fault current path, and 250.4(B)(4) repeats this for ungrounded systems. Fault current must return through a low-impedance metallic path so the OCPD opens. [NEC 250.4(A)(5)(c); 250.4(B)(4)]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which rebar installation meets the minimum requirements for a concrete-encased electrode in 250.52(A)(3)?","o":["20 ft of 1/2-in. rebar with at least 2 in. of concrete in contact with earth","10 ft of 5/8-in. rebar with at least 3 in. of concrete in contact with earth","20 ft of 3/8-in. rebar with at least 2 in. of concrete in contact with earth","20 ft of 1/2-in. rebar with only 1 in. of concrete in contact with earth"],"a":0,"e":"250.52(A)(3) requires at least 6.0 m (20 ft) of rebar not less than 13 mm (1/2 in.) in diameter. Metal components must be encased by at least 50 mm (2 in.) of concrete in a footing or foundation in direct contact with the earth. [NEC 250.52(A)(3)]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a single 8-ft ground rod is the only electrode at a building and it has a measured resistance to earth of 20 ohms. Is a supplemental electrode required?","o":["Yes, a single rod always needs a second electrode","Yes, unless resistance is 5 ohms or less","No, but only if the rod is stainless steel","No, because resistance is 25 ohms or less"],"a":3,"e":"250.53(A)(2) requires a supplemental electrode for a single rod, pipe, or plate. Its exception removes the requirement when the single electrode has a resistance to earth of 25 ohms or less, and 20 ohms satisfies that. [NEC 250.53(A)(2), Exception]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, what is the minimum diameter of an unlisted zinc-coated steel rod electrode under 250.52(A)(5)?","o":["1/2 in.","5/8 in.","3/4 in.","3/8 in."],"a":1,"e":"250.52(A)(5) requires rod-type electrodes of stainless steel and copper or zinc-coated steel to be at least 15.87 mm (5/8 in.) in diameter unless listed. The 1/2 in. size applies to rebar in a concrete-encased electrode. [NEC 250.52(A)(5)(2)]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, rock bottom is encountered at an angle of up to 45 degrees when installing a rod electrode. If the rod is buried in a trench instead, how deep must the trench be at minimum?","o":["30 in. (750 mm)","18 in. (450 mm)","24 in. (600 mm)","12 in. (300 mm)"],"a":0,"e":"250.53(A)(4)(b), Exception No. 2, permits the electrode to be buried in a trench at least 750 mm (30 in.) deep when rock bottom is met at an angle up to 45 degrees. The 18 in. figure belongs to other clearance rules. [NEC 250.53(A)(4)(b), Exception No. 2]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, multiple rod electrodes are installed to meet the requirements of 250.53(A). What is the minimum spacing required between the rods?","o":["8 ft","12 ft","6 ft","16 ft"],"a":2,"e":"250.53(A)(3) says multiple rod, pipe, or plate electrodes shall not be less than 1.8 m (6 ft) apart. The informational note says spacing at twice the rod length improves paralleling efficiency, but that is not the Code minimum. [NEC 250.53(A)(3)]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which of the following shall not be used as a grounding electrode?","o":["A ground ring of 20 ft of bare 2 AWG copper","A plate electrode exposing 2 ft2 of surface to soil","A metal in-ground support structure in contact with earth for 10 ft","A metal underground gas piping system"],"a":3,"e":"250.52(B) lists metal underground gas piping systems, aluminum, and the structures and rebar described in 680.26(B)(1) and (B)(2) as not permitted. The ground ring, plate, and in-ground support structure are all permitted electrodes under 250.52(A). [NEC 250.52(B)(1)]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a subpanel in the same building is fed from the service by a 4-wire feeder (not a separately derived system). How must its grounded conductor be handled?","o":["Bonded to the subpanel enclosure through a new main bonding jumper","Isolated from the enclosure and EGCs, with no reconnection to ground","Bonded to the enclosure only where the subpanel is rated over 100 A","Connected to a new local ground rod and to the subpanel enclosure"],"a":1,"e":"250.24(B) bars connecting a grounded conductor to equipment grounding conductors, to normally non-current-carrying metal parts, or to ground on the load side of the service disconnecting means, except as permitted elsewhere. A feeder subpanel is not one of the exceptions. [NEC 250.24(B); 250.142(B)]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which method may NOT be the only means of bonding service raceways to a service enclosure?","o":["A standard locknut and bushing","A listed bonding-type locknut","A threaded hub made up wrenchtight","A bushing with a bonding jumper"],"a":0,"e":"250.92(B) states that standard locknuts or bushings shall not be the only means of bonding at the service, though they may be used for the mechanical connection. Bonding-type locknuts, wrenchtight threaded hubs, and bushings with jumpers are listed methods. [NEC 250.92(B)]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, an intersystem bonding termination (IBT) device at the service must provide what minimum capacity?","o":["Connection of at least one intersystem bonding conductor","Connection of at least two intersystem bonding conductors","Connection of not less than three intersystem bonding conductors","Connection of at least six intersystem bonding conductors"],"a":2,"e":"250.94(A)(2) requires an IBT to consist of a set of terminals with the capacity for connecting not less than three intersystem bonding conductors. It must also be accessible and listed as grounding and bonding equipment. [NEC 250.94(A)(2)]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, in a dwelling, an installer wants to use interior metal water piping that is 12 ft (measured along the pipe) from where it enters the building to connect a footing rebar electrode to the grounding electrode system. Is this permitted?","o":["Yes, if the piping is electrically continuous with the underground pipe","No, piping beyond 5 ft from the entrance can't interconnect electrodes","Yes, provided bonding jumpers are installed around the water meter","No, interior water piping is never allowed in the grounding system"],"a":1,"e":"250.68(C)(1) allows interior water piping within 1.52 m (5 ft) of the building entrance to extend the connection to electrodes. Beyond 5 ft it shall not be used to interconnect electrodes, except under the qualified-persons exception for industrial, commercial, and institutional buildings. [NEC 250.68(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 250 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, how many small-appliance branch circuits are required for the kitchen, pantry, breakfast room, and dining room receptacle outlets of a dwelling unit, and what is their rating?","o":["One 20-ampere circuit shared with the dining room lighting","At least two 20-ampere circuits, with no other outlets","At least two 15-ampere circuits with no other outlets","At least three 20-ampere circuits, one per room"],"a":1,"e":"210.11(C)(1) requires two or more 20-ampere small-appliance branch circuits for the receptacle outlets specified by 210.52(B). 210.52(B)(2) says these circuits have no other outlets, subject to the clock and gas range exceptions. [NEC 210.11(C)(1); 210.52(B)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which describes the branch circuit required for the laundry area receptacle outlet(s) in a dwelling unit?","o":["A 120-volt, 20-ampere circuit shared with the basement lighting","A 120-volt, 15-ampere circuit shared with hallway receptacles","A 240-volt, 30-ampere circuit shared with the dryer","A 120-volt, 20-ampere circuit with no other outlets"],"a":3,"e":"210.11(C)(2) requires at least one additional 120-volt, nominal, 20-ampere branch circuit for the laundry area receptacle outlet(s) required by 210.52(F). The circuit must have no other outlets. [NEC 210.11(C)(2); 210.52(F)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 20-ampere bathroom receptacle circuit serves only one bathroom in a dwelling. May it also supply the exhaust fan and lighting in that same bathroom?","o":["Yes, within that same bathroom if 210.23(B)(1) and (B)(2) are met","No, bathroom receptacle circuits can never have other outlets","Yes, and it may also feed bedroom receptacles next door","Yes, but only if the circuit is reduced to 15 amperes"],"a":0,"e":"210.11(C)(3) requires the bathroom circuit to have no other outlets. Its exception allows a circuit serving a single bathroom to also supply other equipment within that bathroom, as long as 210.23(B)(1) and (B)(2) are met. [NEC 210.11(C)(3), Exception]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, an attached garage has the required 20-ampere, 120-volt garage receptacle branch circuit. May that same circuit also supply outdoor receptacle outlets?","o":["No, the circuit may supply garage receptacles only","Yes, but only if the circuit is increased to 30 amperes","Yes, 210.11(C)(4) Exception No. 1 permits outdoor receptacles","Yes, but only if it also supplies the garage lighting"],"a":2,"e":"210.11(C)(4) requires at least one 20-ampere circuit for the garage receptacle outlets with no other outlets. Exception No. 1 allows that circuit to supply outdoor receptacle outlets. [NEC 210.11(C)(4), Exception No. 1]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 125-volt, 15-ampere receptacle is installed in a finished basement of a one-family dwelling on a single-phase 120-volt branch circuit. Is GFCI protection required?","o":["Yes, 210.8(A) lists basements without exempting finished ones","No, only unfinished areas of basements require GFCI protection","No, GFCI applies only within 6 ft of a sink in basements","Yes, but only if the receptacle is rated 20 amperes or more"],"a":0,"e":"210.8(A) requires GFCI protection for 125- to 250-volt receptacles on single-phase circuits rated 150 volts or less to ground in basements. Dwelling units are covered with no finished or unfinished exclusion. Only 210.8(B), for other than dwellings, says 'unfinished areas of basements.' [NEC 210.8(A)(6)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which of the following must have GFCI protection for the branch circuit or outlet supplying it under 210.8(D), assuming 120/240 volts, single phase, and 30 amperes?","o":["A central air-conditioner compressor","An electric tankless water heater","An electric clothes dryer","A ceiling-mounted paddle fan"],"a":2,"e":"210.8(D) lists specific appliances rated 150 volts or less to ground and 60 amperes or less, and it includes clothes dryers, electric ranges, dishwashers, microwave ovens, and sump pumps. Water heaters, fans, and compressors are not in that list. [NEC 210.8(D)(11)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a commercial kitchen has a 125-volt-to-250-volt receptacle supplied by a 3-phase, 208Y/120-volt, 60-ampere branch circuit. Is GFCI protection required?","o":["No, GFCI is required only on single-phase circuits of 50 A or less","No, only receptacles on circuits of 20 amperes or less are covered","Yes, but only where the circuit is rated above 150 volts to ground","Yes, 3-phase circuits up to 100 A and 150 V to ground are covered"],"a":3,"e":"210.8(B) covers single-phase circuits of 150 volts or less to ground and 50 amperes or less, and 3-phase circuits of 150 volts or less to ground and 100 amperes or less. A 208Y/120 circuit is 120 volts to ground, 60 A is under 100 A, and kitchens are a listed location. [NEC 210.8(B)(2)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 120-volt lighting outlet is installed in the crawl space of a dwelling. What GFCI requirement applies?","o":["No, GFCI protection is required only for receptacle outlets there","Yes, 120-volt lighting outlets in crawl spaces need GFCI protection","Yes, but only if the lighting outlet is controlled by a switch","Yes, but only if the lighting exceeds 150 volts to ground"],"a":1,"e":"210.8(C) requires GFCI protection for lighting outlets not exceeding 120 volts, nominal, installed in crawl spaces. Exception No. 1 to 210.8(F) confirms lighting outlets are not covered except as in 210.8(C). [NEC 210.8(C); 210.8(F), Exception No. 1]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, listed HVAC equipment is protected by a Class C SPGFCI instead of a GFCI. What marking is required at the disconnect serving the HVAC equipment?","o":["Warning: Class A GFCI Protection Provided for HVAC Unit","Caution: Ground-Fault Protection Not Provided for HVAC Unit","Notice: Arc-Fault Protection Provided for HVAC Unit","Warning: Class C SPGFCI Protection Provided for HVAC Unit"],"a":3,"e":"210.8(F), Exception No. 3, permits a listed Class C SPGFCI for listed HVAC equipment. If one is provided, the disconnect serving the equipment must be marked with the warning wording given in that exception. [NEC 210.8(F), Exception No. 3]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which dwelling-unit location listed in 210.12(B) requires AFCI protection for 120-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere branch circuits?","o":["Laundry areas","Bathrooms","Attached garages","Outdoor receptacle outlets"],"a":0,"e":"210.12(B) lists kitchens, family rooms, dining rooms, living rooms, parlors, libraries, dens, bedrooms, sunrooms, recreation rooms, closets, hallways, laundry areas, and similar areas. Bathrooms, garages, and outdoors are not on the dwelling-unit list. [NEC 210.12(B)(13)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which location is listed for AFCI protection in dormitory units under 210.12(C) but is not on the dwelling-unit list in 210.12(B)?","o":["Closets","Hallways","Bathrooms","Bedrooms"],"a":2,"e":"210.12(C) for dormitories lists bedrooms, living rooms, hallways, closets, bathrooms, and similar rooms. Closets, hallways, and bedrooms are also on the 210.12(B) dwelling list, but bathrooms are only on the dormitory list. [NEC 210.12(B); 210.12(C)(5)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a listed outlet branch-circuit-type AFCI at the first outlet is combined with a listed branch-circuit OCPD per 210.12(A)(4). What is the maximum length of 12 AWG wiring from the OCPD to the first outlet?","o":["50 ft (15.2 m)","70 ft (21.3 m)","100 ft (30 m)","35 ft (10.7 m)"],"a":1,"e":"210.12(A)(4)b limits the wiring from the branch-circuit OCPD to the first outlet to 15.2 m (50 ft) for 14 AWG and 21.3 m (70 ft) for 12 AWG. The 50 ft limit is the 14 AWG value. [NEC 210.12(A)(4)b]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, the conductors of an existing bedroom branch circuit are extended by 5 ft to move a receptacle location, with no additional outlets or devices other than splicing devices. Is AFCI protection required for the extension?","o":["No, extensions of 6 ft or less without added outlets are exempt","Yes, a listed combination-type AFCI is always required","Yes, a listed outlet branch-circuit-type AFCI is required at the first outlet","No, but only if the extension is 3 ft or less"],"a":0,"e":"210.12(E) requires AFCI protection when wiring in the 210.12(B) areas is modified, replaced, or extended. Its exception waives it when the extension is not more than 1.8 m (6 ft) and adds no outlets or devices other than splicing devices. [NEC 210.12(E), Exception]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a living room has one unbroken wall space 25 ft long along the floor line with no doors or openings. What is the minimum number of receptacle outlets required on that wall under the 6-ft spacing rule in 210.52(A)(1)?","o":["2","4","3","5"],"a":2,"e":"No point along the floor line may be more than 6 ft from a receptacle, so each receptacle covers at most 12 ft of wall. 25 ft / 12 ft = 2.08, which rounds up to 3 outlets. Two outlets would cover only 24 ft. [NEC 210.52(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a kitchen has a 14-ft (168 in.) unbroken straight countertop wall with no range, sink, or other break. What is the minimum number of countertop receptacle outlets needed under 210.52(C)(1)?","o":["3","5","2","4"],"a":3,"e":"No point along the wall line may be more than 24 in. from a receptacle, so each outlet serves at most 48 in. of countertop. 168 in. / 48 in. = 3.5, which rounds up to 4 outlets. Three outlets would cover only 144 in. [NEC 210.52(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a building has both a 208Y/120-volt and a 480Y/277-volt system supplying branch circuits. How must the branch-circuit ungrounded conductors be identified under 210.5(C)(2)?","o":["By phase only, at the panelboard terminations and branch circuit breakers","By phase and voltage system at every termination, connection, and splice","By voltage system only, on a handwritten label at the panelboard","By phase and system only for ungrounded conductors 4 AWG and larger"],"a":1,"e":"Where branch circuits come from more than one nominal voltage system, 210.5(C)(2) requires each ungrounded conductor to be identified by phase or line and by voltage system at all termination, connection, and splice points. The method must be documented or permanently posted, and the label cannot be handwritten. [NEC 210.5(C)(2)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, what is the required location of the bathroom receptacle outlet relative to the sink?","o":["Within 3 ft of the edge of each sink and no more than 12 in. below its top","Within 6 ft of the sink and not more than 12 in. below the countertop top","Within 3 ft of the edge of each sink and no more than 18 in. below its top","Within 3 ft of the bathtub edge and at least 12 in. above the floor"],"a":0,"e":"210.52(D)(1) requires at least one receptacle within 900 mm (3 ft) of the outside edge of each sink. 210.52(D)(2) says receptacles shall not be located more than 300 mm (12 in.) below the top of the sink or sink countertop. [NEC 210.52(D)(1), (D)(2)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, what is the minimum hallway length, measured along the centerline without passing through a doorway, at which a dwelling unit hallway must have at least one receptacle outlet?","o":["6 ft","8 ft","12 ft","10 ft"],"a":3,"e":"210.52(H) requires at least one receptacle outlet in dwelling unit hallways of 3.0 m (10 ft) or more in length. The length is measured along the centerline without passing through a doorway. [NEC 210.52(H)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 10-ampere multioutlet branch circuit is limited to supplying which of the following?","o":["General-use 15-ampere receptacles","Lighting outlets","Small-appliance receptacles in the kitchen","A window air-conditioner receptacle"],"a":1,"e":"210.23(A) limits 10-ampere branch circuits to lighting outlets or dwelling unit exhaust fans on bathroom and laundry room lighting circuits. 210.18 Exception No. 2 and 210.22 prohibit 10-ampere circuits from supplying receptacle outlets. [NEC 210.23(A); 210.18, Exception No. 2]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a branch circuit supplies 18 A of continuous load and 3 A of noncontinuous load. The circuit uses 75 degrees C-rated THWN copper conductors, with no more than three current-carrying conductors and normal ambient. What is the smallest copper conductor that satisfies 210.19(A)(1) using Table 310.16?","o":["12 AWG","14 AWG","10 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Minimum ampacity = 3 A + (1.25 x 18 A) = 3 + 22.5 = 25.5 A. In the 75 degrees C copper column of Table 310.16, 12 AWG is 25 A, which is less than 25.5 A, so 10 AWG (35 A) is required. [NEC 210.19(A)(1); Table 310.16]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a circuit supplies 24 A of continuous load and 5 A of noncontinuous load through an OCPD that is not listed for 100 percent operation. What is the minimum OCPD rating required by 210.20(A)?","o":["30 A","40 A","45 A","35 A"],"a":3,"e":"Minimum rating = 5 A + (1.25 x 24 A) = 5 + 30 = 35 A, which is a standard size in Table 240.6(A). Counting all loads at 100 percent gives only 29 A, which would lead to the incorrect 30 A choice. [NEC 210.20(A); Table 240.6(A)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 12-kW household electric range is supplied by a 120/240-volt branch circuit. The demand is taken from Column C of Table 120.55 (8 kW). What is the minimum branch-circuit rating?","o":["40 A","30 A","35 A","60 A"],"a":0,"e":"Column C gives 8 kW for one range, and 8,000 W / 240 V = 33.3 A. However, 210.19(C) says for ranges of 8-3/4 kW or more the minimum branch-circuit rating is 40 A, and a 12-kW range exceeds 8-3/4 kW. [NEC 210.19(C); Table 120.55]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 40-ampere, 3-wire branch circuit supplies a household range whose demand was calculated from Column C of Table 120.55. Using Table 310.16 (75 degrees C copper), what is the smallest copper neutral conductor permitted by 210.19(C) Exception No. 2?","o":["12 AWG","10 AWG","8 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"The neutral must have ampacity of at least 70 percent of the branch-circuit rating: 0.70 x 40 A = 28 A. It also cannot be smaller than 10 AWG. 12 AWG (25 A) is too small, and 10 AWG (35 A) satisfies both limits. [NEC 210.19(C), Exception No. 2; Table 310.16]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 20-ampere multioutlet branch circuit in a shop also supplies other loads. What is the maximum rating of a single cord-and-plug-connected utilization equipment item not fastened in place on this circuit?","o":["10 A","15 A","16 A","20 A"],"a":2,"e":"210.23(B)(1) limits any one cord-and-plug-connected item not fastened in place to 80 percent of the branch-circuit rating. 0.80 x 20 A = 16 A. [NEC 210.23(B)(1)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 20-ampere multioutlet branch circuit supplies luminaires and cord-and-plug-connected equipment not fastened in place. What is the maximum total rating of utilization equipment fastened in place, other than luminaires, on this circuit?","o":["10 A","16 A","12 A","20 A"],"a":0,"e":"210.23(B)(2) limits fastened-in-place equipment other than luminaires to 50 percent of the branch-circuit rating where lighting units or plug-connected equipment are also supplied. 0.50 x 20 A = 10 A. [NEC 210.23(B)(2)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 15-ampere receptacle is installed on a 20-ampere branch circuit that supplies two or more receptacles. Per Table 210.21(B)(2), what is the maximum total cord-and-plug-connected load that this receptacle may supply?","o":["10 A","15 A","16 A","12 A"],"a":3,"e":"Table 210.21(B)(2) lists a 15-ampere receptacle on a 15- or 20-ampere circuit with a maximum load of 12 amperes. The 16 A value belongs to a 20-ampere receptacle on a 20-ampere circuit. [NEC 210.21(B)(2); Table 210.21(B)(2)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a single receptacle is installed on an individual 30-ampere branch circuit for a cord-and-plug-connected appliance. What ampere rating must the receptacle have?","o":["Not less than 24 amperes","Not less than 30 amperes","Not less than 20 amperes","Not less than 15 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"210.21(B)(1) requires a single receptacle on an individual branch circuit to have an ampere rating not less than that of the branch circuit. The 24 A figure incorrectly applies an 80 percent factor, which does not apply to the receptacle rating. [NEC 210.21(B)(1)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, the 210.19 informational note recommends sizing branch-circuit conductors to limit voltage drop at the farthest outlet to 3 percent. What is that maximum drop, in volts, on a 240-volt branch circuit?","o":["12 V","4.8 V","7.2 V","3.6 V"],"a":2,"e":"The informational note recommends no more than 3 percent drop at the farthest outlet. 0.03 x 240 V = 7.2 V. The 12 V result uses the 5 percent total for feeder plus branch circuit, and 3.6 V uses 120 volts. The note is a recommendation, not a mandatory rule. [NEC 210.19, Informational Note]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, what disconnecting means must a multiwire branch circuit have at the point where it originates?","o":["A means that simultaneously disconnects all ungrounded conductors","Independent single-pole devices with no common trip or handle tie","A disconnect for the grounded conductor only","A switch ahead of the first outlet only"],"a":0,"e":"210.4(B) requires each multiwire branch circuit to have a means that simultaneously disconnects all ungrounded conductors where the circuit originates. This is to protect people servicing the shared neutral. [NEC 210.4(B)]","r":"NEC 210 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, the white conductor of a 14/2 nonmetallic-sheathed cable is used as the ungrounded supply to a single-pole switch in a switch loop. What must be done with the white conductor?","o":["Nothing; white may be used for any switch-loop conductor, including the return","Reidentify it with green tape at the terminations and use it as a return","Reidentify it with a color other than white, gray, or green; use for supply only","Reidentify it with white tape and use it as the switch return conductor"],"a":2,"e":"200.8(C)(1) lets cable-assembly conductors be reidentified as ungrounded at termination and each visible, accessible location with an encircling color other than white, gray, or green. In a switch loop, the reidentified conductor may be used only for the supply to the switch, not as the return. [NEC 200.8(C)(1)(a), (b)]","r":"NEC 200 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a dwelling has a 1,800 sq ft first floor and a 1,200 sq ft second floor (outside dimensions), plus a 240 sq ft open porch and a 400 sq ft detached garage. Using the standard method, what is the minimum general lighting and general-use receptacle load?","o":["6,000 VA","7,280 VA","9,000 VA","10,920 VA"],"a":0,"e":"Floor area comes from outside dimensions and excludes detached garages and open porches: 1,800 + 1,200 = 3,000 sq ft. The unit load is 2 VA/sq ft: 3,000 x 2 = 6,000 VA. [NEC 120.5(C); 120.41]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a dwelling service load calculation includes two EVSE. EVSE No. 1 has a nameplate rating of 9,600 VA. EVSE No. 2 has no nameplate rating available. What total EVSE load is included?","o":["14,400 VA","16,800 VA","9,600 VA","19,200 VA"],"a":1,"e":"Each EVSE is calculated at its nameplate rating if available, or 7,200 VA if not: 9,600 + 7,200 = 16,800 VA. [NEC 120.57]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 2,500 sq ft dwelling has two 2-wire small-appliance branch circuits and one laundry branch circuit. Using the standard method with the small-appliance and laundry loads included with the general lighting load, what is the demand load after Table 120.45?","o":["9,500 VA","6,150 VA","5,275 VA","3,325 VA"],"a":2,"e":"General lighting/receptacles 2,500 x 2 = 5,000 VA; small-appliance 2 x 1,500 = 3,000 VA; laundry 1,500 VA; total 9,500 VA. Table 120.45 dwelling: first 3,000 at 100% plus remainder at 35%: 3,000 + 0.35 x 6,500 = 3,000 + 2,275 = 5,275 VA. [NEC 120.41; 120.52; Table 120.45]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 4,000 VA lighting load in a non-dwelling building operates continuously for more than 3 hours. For the purposes of the Article 120 feeder load calculation, what value is included for that load?","o":["5,000 VA","4,000 VA","4,800 VA","3,200 VA"],"a":1,"e":"Article 120 load calculations do not require continuous loads to be calculated at 125%, so the load is counted at 4,000 VA. Any 125% multiplier for conductor and overcurrent device sizing is applied for other reasons, not in the load calculation. [NEC 120.5(E)]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a dwelling clothes dryer has a nameplate rating of 4,000 W. What load is used for that dryer in the load calculation?","o":["5,000 VA","4,000 VA","6,250 VA","3,200 VA"],"a":0,"e":"The dryer load is the nameplate rating or 5,000 W (VA), whichever is larger. 4,000 W is less than 5,000, so 5,000 VA is used. [NEC 120.54; 120.14(B)]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a feeder supplies four household electric clothes dryers, each with a 5,800 W nameplate rating. Applying Table 120.54, what is the dryer demand load?","o":["23,200 VA","17,400 VA","18,560 VA","15,080 VA"],"a":2,"e":"Four dryers falls in the 3-5 range at 80%. Connected load 4 x 5,800 = 23,200 VA; 23,200 x 0.80 = 18,560 VA. [Table 120.54]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a multifamily service supplies 13 household electric dryers rated 5,000 W each. Using Table 120.54, what is the dryer demand load?","o":["29,250 VA","30,550 VA","29,900 VA","32,500 VA"],"a":0,"e":"For 12-23 dryers the factor is 47% minus 1% for each dryer exceeding 11. 13 - 11 = 2, so 45%. Connected load 13 x 5,000 = 65,000 VA; 65,000 x 0.45 = 29,250 VA. [Table 120.54]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a dwelling has one household electric range rated 11 kW. Using Table 120.55, Column C, what is the range demand?","o":["8,800 VA","11,000 VA","8,000 VA","6,600 VA"],"a":2,"e":"One range not over 12 kW rating uses Column C maximum demand of 8 kW = 8,000 VA. The 8,800 VA figure (80%) is the Column A/B factor, not Column C. [Table 120.55]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 120-volt branch circuit supplies fastened-in-place motor-operated equipment with a motor larger than 1/8 hp (16 A) in combination with other loads totaling 6 A. What is the minimum calculated circuit load?","o":["22 A","27.5 A","20 A","26 A"],"a":3,"e":"The calculated load must be not less than 125% of the largest motor load plus the other loads: 1.25 x 16 = 20 A; 20 + 6 = 26 A. Applying 125% to everything gives 27.5 A, which is not required. [NEC 120.11(A)]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a dwelling has one household electric range rated 14 kW. Using Table 120.55 Column C with Note 1, what is the maximum demand?","o":["8,800 VA","8,000 VA","8,400 VA","11,200 VA"],"a":0,"e":"Column C for one range is 8 kW. The range exceeds 12 kW by 2 kW, and Note 1 adds 5% per additional kW: 2 x 5% = 10%. 8,000 x 1.10 = 8,800 VA. [Table 120.55 Note 1]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a feeder supplies two household electric ranges, each rated 6 kW. Using the Note 3 alternative to Column C with the Column B factor for 2 appliances, what is the demand load?","o":["11,000 VA","9,000 VA","7,800 VA","12,000 VA"],"a":2,"e":"Ranges rated 3.5 kW through 8.75 kW use Column B. For 2 appliances the factor is 65%: 12,000 x 0.65 = 7,800 VA. Column C would give 11,000 VA. [Table 120.55 Note 3]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a multifamily building service supplies 28 household electric ranges, each rated 12 kW. Using Table 120.55 Column C, what is the maximum demand?","o":["40,000 VA","36,000 VA","46,000 VA","43,000 VA"],"a":3,"e":"For 26-30 ranges Column C is 15 kW plus 1 kW for each range: 15 + 28 = 43 kW = 43,000 VA. [Table 120.55]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 120/208-volt, 3-phase, 4-wire feeder supplies seven single-phase 12 kW household ranges connected AB = 3, BC = 2, CA = 2. Using Table 120.55 Column C, what is the maximum demand?","o":["22,000 VA","14,000 VA","20,000 VA","21,000 VA"],"a":3,"e":"The load is based on twice the maximum number connected between any two phases: 3 x 2 = 6 ranges. Column C for 6 ranges is 21 kW = 21,000 VA. Using all 7 would give 22 kW. [NEC 120.55; Table 120.55]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a one-family dwelling service supplies four fastened-in-place appliances (not cooking, dryer, heating, air-conditioning, or EVSE): a 4,500 W water heater, 1,200 W dishwasher, 900 W disposer, and 1,500 W pool pump. What is the appliance demand?","o":["8,100 VA","4,050 VA","6,075 VA","7,290 VA"],"a":2,"e":"Four or more fastened-in-place appliances permit a 75% demand factor. Total 4,500 + 1,200 + 900 + 1,500 = 8,100 VA; 8,100 x 0.75 = 6,075 VA. [NEC 120.53]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which of these loads is excluded from the 75 percent demand factor for four or more fastened-in-place appliances in a one-family dwelling?","o":["Fixed dishwasher rated 1,200 watts","Water heater rated 4,500 watts","Garbage disposer rated 1/3 hp","Electric vehicle supply equipment (EVSE)"],"a":3,"e":"The 75% factor does not apply to household cooking equipment, clothes dryers, space heating, air conditioning, or EVSE. Water heaters, dishwashers, and disposers are not excluded. [NEC 120.53(1)-(5)]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a feeder under the standard method supplies five fixed electric baseboard heaters rated 1,500, 2,000, 2,000, 2,500 and 1,500 W. What load is used for space heating?","o":["9,500 VA","6,175 VA","3,800 VA","7,125 VA"],"a":0,"e":"Fixed electric space-heating loads are calculated at 100% of connected load: 1,500 + 2,000 + 2,000 + 2,500 + 1,500 = 9,500 VA. The 65% and 40% factors belong only to the optional method. [NEC 120.51]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":2,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a dwelling has a 5,400 VA air conditioner and a 9,600 VA electric furnace that are prevented from operating at the same time by listed interlocking equipment. What load is used for the two?","o":["15,000 VA","9,600 VA","5,400 VA","12,000 VA"],"a":1,"e":"Noncoincident loads allow the single largest load to be used. The 9,600 VA furnace is larger than the 5,400 VA air conditioner, so 9,600 VA is included. Adding both would give 15,000 VA. [NEC 120.6(2)]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 2,000 sq ft dwelling on a 120/240-volt, single-phase service has two small-appliance circuits and one laundry circuit; lighting, small-appliance, and laundry load is on 120 volts (4,925 VA after Table 120.45). A 12 kW range (8,000 VA demand) and a 5,000 VA dryer are also supplied. Assume 120-volt loads are balanced. What is the service neutral load, using permitted reductions?","o":["58 A","75 A","52 A","21 A"],"a":0,"e":"The 70% demand factor may be applied only to the range and dryer portion: 0.70 x (8,000 + 5,000) = 9,100 VA. Neutral = (4,925 + 9,100) / 240 = 58.4, rounded to 58 A. [NEC 120.61(A); 120.61(B)(1); 120.5(B)]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 120/240-volt, single-phase feeder has a maximum unbalanced load of 260 amperes, none of it nonlinear. What neutral load is permitted?","o":["260 A","182 A","200 A","242 A"],"a":3,"e":"A 70% demand factor may be applied to the portion of the unbalanced load over 200 A: 260 - 200 = 60 A; 60 x 0.70 = 42 A; 200 + 42 = 242 A. [NEC 120.61(B)(2)]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a mobile home calculation under 120.140(B) has motor loads (after omitting the smaller of heating and cooling) of an 18 A air-conditioner and a 2 A exhaust fan. What is the motor total including the largest-motor addition?","o":["25 A","20.5 A","24.5 A","20 A"],"a":2,"e":"Motors total 18 + 2 = 20 A. Add 25% of the current of the largest motor (18 A): 0.25 x 18 = 4.5 A. Total = 24.5 A. [NEC 120.140(B)(2); 120.140(B)(3)]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, using the optional dwelling method, a 2,400 sq ft dwelling has two small-appliance circuits and one laundry circuit, a 12,000 VA range, 1,200 VA dishwasher, 4,500 VA water heater, and a 5,000 VA dryer not on the laundry circuit. What is the general calculated load of 120.82(B)?","o":["32,000 VA","17,600 VA","12,800 VA","13,150 VA"],"a":1,"e":"Loads: 4,800 + 4,500 + 12,000 + 1,200 + 4,500 + 5,000 = 32,000 VA. Take 100% of the first 8 kVA plus 40% of the remainder: 8,000 + 0.40 x 24,000 = 17,600 VA. [NEC 120.82(B)]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, under the optional dwelling method, a home has a 5,000 VA air conditioner and five separately controlled electric space-heating units totaling 16,000 VA. What heating and air-conditioning load is included under 120.82(C)?","o":["5,000 VA","10,400 VA","21,000 VA","6,400 VA"],"a":3,"e":"The largest of the selections is used. Cooling at 100% is 5,000 VA; heating with four or more separately controlled units is 40%: 0.40 x 16,000 = 6,400 VA. The larger, 6,400 VA, is included. [NEC 120.82(C)(1), (5)]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, under the optional dwelling method, a central heat pump system has a 3,600 VA compressor and 10,000 VA of supplemental electric heat that can operate with the compressor. Using 120.82(C)(3), what is the load?","o":["10,100 VA","13,600 VA","8,840 VA","7,600 VA"],"a":0,"e":"Selection (3) takes 100% of the compressor and 65% of the supplemental heat: 3,600 + 0.65 x 10,000 = 3,600 + 6,500 = 10,100 VA. [NEC 120.82(C)(3)]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, using the optional method, a 2,200 sq ft dwelling (120/240 V single-phase) has two small-appliance and one laundry circuit, an 11,000 VA range, 1,200 VA dishwasher, 4,500 VA water heater, 5,000 VA dryer (not on the laundry circuit), a 5,500 VA air conditioner (gas heat), and a 9,600 VA EVSE nameplate. What is the minimum standard service disconnect rating?","o":["125 A","150 A","175 A","200 A"],"a":1,"e":"General loads: 4,400 + 4,500 + 11,000 + 1,200 + 4,500 + 5,000 = 30,600; 8,000 + 0.4 x 22,600 = 17,040 VA. Add A/C 5,500 and EVSE 9,600 at 100%: total 32,140 VA / 240 = 133.9 A, so 134 A. The disconnect must be not less than the calculated load; the next standard rating is 150 A. [NEC 120.82(B)-(D); 230.79; Table 240.6(A)]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, using the standard method, a 2,000 sq ft dwelling has two small-appliance and one laundry circuit, dishwasher 1,200 VA, disposer 900 VA, water heater 4,500 VA, attic fan 600 VA (all fastened in place), a 5,000 VA dryer, a 12 kW range, and a 5,500 VA air conditioner interlocked with a 7,500 VA electric furnace. What is the total calculated service load?","o":["36,325 VA","30,825 VA","32,625 VA","25,825 VA"],"a":1,"e":"Lighting/small-appliance/laundry 4,000 + 3,000 + 1,500 = 8,500: 3,000 + 0.35 x 5,500 = 4,925. Appliances 7,200 x 0.75 = 5,400. Dryer 5,000; range 8,000 (Column C); heat 7,500 (noncoincident with A/C). Total = 30,825 VA. [NEC 120.40; 120.41; 120.52-120.55; 120.6]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 230 Services","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, using the standard method, a 1,200 sq ft one-family dwelling has two small-appliance and one laundry circuit, one 10 kW range, a 4,000 VA water heater and a 5,000 VA dryer, with no other loads. What is the minimum service disconnecting means rating?","o":["90 A","110 A","125 A","100 A"],"a":3,"e":"General: 2,400 + 3,000 + 1,500 = 6,900; 3,000 + 0.35 x 3,900 = 4,365 VA. Add range 8,000 + water heater 4,000 + dryer 5,000: 21,365 VA / 240 = 89.0, about 89 A. A one-family dwelling disconnect must still be not less than 100 A. [NEC 230.79(C); Table 120.55]","r":"NEC 230 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, to check an existing 120/240-volt dwelling (1,800 sq ft) for adding a 9,600 VA EVSE, 120.83 is used. Existing loads: two small-appliance and one laundry circuit, 12,000 VA range, 5,000 VA dryer, 4,500 VA water heater, 4,000 VA A/C and 6,000 VA electric heat. What is the resulting service current?","o":["119 A","111 A","99 A","148 A"],"a":1,"e":"Existing: 3,600 + 4,500 + 12,000 + 5,000 + 4,500 + 6,000 (larger of heat or A/C) = 35,600 VA. First 8 kVA at 100% plus 40% of the remainder: 19,040 VA. New EVSE at 80%: 0.80 x 9,600 = 7,680 VA. Total 26,720 / 240 = 111.3, or 111 A. [NEC 120.83; Table 120.83]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"new","c":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Load Calculations","d":3,"q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a feeder supplies 12 dwelling units of a multifamily dwelling that meet the 120.84(A)(1) conditions. Each unit's calculated load under 120.84(C) is 21,500 VA. What is the feeder demand load for the dwelling units?","o":["103,200 VA","108,360 VA","105,780 VA","113,520 VA"],"a":2,"e":"Connected 12 x 21,500 = 258,000 VA. Table 120.84(C) gives 41% for 12-13 dwelling units: 258,000 x 0.41 = 105,780 VA. House loads are added separately. [NEC 120.84(A), (C); Table 120.84(C)]","r":"NEC 120 (2026 text)","b":8},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Dwelling and multi-family service (Art. 220)","d":3,"q":"A single-family dwelling has 2,800 sq ft of living area, a 120/240-V single-phase service, two 20-A small-appliance circuits, one laundry circuit, a 12-kW range, a 5-kW dryer, a 1.2-kW dishwasher and a 4.5-kW water heater (all fixed appliances 240 V or as listed). Using the standard method of Part III of Article 220 (no optional method), what is the calculated service load?","o":["121.5 A","131.7 A","104.9 A","96.5 A"],"a":2,"e":"General load = 2,800x3 + 3,000 + 1,500 = 12900 VA; first 3,000 at 100% + remainder at 35% = 6465 VA. Range 12 kW = 8,000 VA (Table 220.55 Col. C), dryer 5,000 VA, fixed appliances 5700 VA at 100% (fewer than four). Total 25165 VA / 240 = 104.9 A.","r":"NEC 2023 220.12, 220.42, 220.52, 220.53, 220.54, 220.55","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Dwelling and multi-family service (Art. 220)","d":3,"q":"A 12-unit apartment building has eight of its units each with a 11-kW household electric range (the other four units are gas). The feeder is sized with Table 220.55. What is the demand load for the eight ranges?","o":["23 kVA","26 kVA","88 kVA","21 kVA"],"a":0,"e":"Each range is rated not over 12 kW, so Table 220.55 Column C applies. For eight ranges the Column C maximum demand is 23 kW (8 ranges x 11 kW = 88 kW connected). Using 26 kW is the 11-range row, 21 kW the 6-range row.","r":"NEC 2023 220.55, Table 220.55 Col. C","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Dwelling and multi-family service (Art. 220)","d":3,"q":"What is the feeder demand for one 16-kW household electric range in a dwelling unit?","o":["8.0 kW","11.2 kW","12.0 kW","9.6 kW"],"a":3,"e":"One range not over 12 kW would be 8 kW (Col. C). Note 1 adds 5% for each kW the range exceeds 12 kW: (16 - 12) = 4 kW x 5% = 20%, so 8 x 1.20 = 9.6 kW. 11.2 kW wrongly counts excess over 8 kW.","r":"NEC 2023 Table 220.55 Note 1","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Dwelling and multi-family service (Art. 220)","d":3,"q":"Two household electric ranges rated 14 kW and 16 kW are on the same feeder in a duplex. What is the demand load for the two ranges using Table 220.55 and its notes?","o":["11.00 kW","12.65 kW","11.55 kW","13.20 kW"],"a":1,"e":"Table 220.55 Col. C gives 11 kW for two ranges. Note 1: use the average rating (14+16)/2 = 15 kW, which exceeds 12 kW by 3 kW, so add 3 x 5% = 15%: 11 x 1.15 = 12.65 kW. 13.20 kW wrongly uses the larger (16 kW) rating.","r":"NEC 2023 Table 220.55 Note 1","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Dwelling and multi-family service (Art. 220)","d":3,"q":"A multi-family building has seven dryers each rated 5.5 kW (one per unit, all on one feeder). What is the feeder demand for the dryers under Table 220.54?","o":["38.5 kVA","19.25 kVA","32.725 kVA","25.025 kVA"],"a":3,"e":"Total connected = 7 x 5.5 = 38.5 kVA. Table 220.54 gives 65% for seven dryers, so demand = 38.5 x 0.65 = 25.025 kVA. 85% is the factor for five dryers, 50% for ten.","r":"NEC 2023 220.54, Table 220.54","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Dwelling and multi-family service (Art. 220)","d":3,"q":"A 24-unit apartment building is fed by one 208Y/120-V, 3-phase, 4-wire feeder, each unit supplied by only one feeder. Each unit is 1,100 sq ft with a 10-kW range (nameplate), 1.2-kW dishwasher, 4.5-kW water heater, 5-kW dryer, 5-kW electric space heat (larger than the A/C), two small-appliance circuits and a laundry circuit. Using the optional method for multi-family dwellings, what is the calculated feeder current (house loads excluded)?","o":["825.7 A","781.1 A","2231.7 A","664.5 A"],"a":1,"e":"Per unit: lighting 3,300 + SA/laundry 4,500 + range 10,000 + dishwasher 1,200 + water heater 4,500 + dryer 5,000 + heat 5,000 = 33500 VA. 24 units x 33500 = 804000 VA; Table 220.84 factor for 24 units is 35% = 281400 VA. I = 281400/(1.732x208) = 781.1 A.","r":"NEC 2023 220.84, Table 220.84","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Dwelling and multi-family service (Art. 220)","d":3,"q":"Using the optional method for a single-family dwelling, determine the calculated load for a 2,400 sq ft house with a 120/240-V, single-phase service: two small-appliance circuits and one laundry circuit, an 11-kW range, 5.5-kW dryer, 1.2-kW dishwasher, 4.5-kW water heater, and a 5.5-kW air-conditioner (no electric heat).","o":["104.4 A","164.2 A","90.7 A","81.5 A"],"a":0,"e":"General and appliance loads = 7,200 + 4,500 + 11,000 + 5,500 + 1,200 + 4,500 = 33900 VA. First 10 kVA at 100% plus remainder at 40% = 19560 VA. A/C at 100% adds 5,500 VA: 25060 VA / 240 = 104.4 A.","r":"NEC 2023 220.82(B), 220.82(C)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Dwelling and multi-family service (Art. 220)","d":3,"q":"A 1,800 sq ft dwelling (120/240-V, single-phase) with a 12-kW range, 5-kW dryer, 1.2-kW dishwasher, 4.5-kW water heater, two small-appliance circuits and a laundry circuit gets a 600 sq ft addition with no new air-conditioning or heating. Using the existing-dwelling-unit addition method, what is the calculated load for the whole dwelling?","o":["143.3 A","74.3 A","77.3 A","82.3 A"],"a":2,"e":"Existing loads = 5,400 + 4,500 + 12,000 + 5,000 + 1,200 + 4,500 = 32600 VA; the addition adds 600 x 3 = 1,800 VA, total 34400 VA. 220.83(A): first 8 kVA at 100%, remainder at 40% = 18560 VA; 18560/240 = 77.3 A. Using 10 kVA as the first block is the 220.82 optional-method figure.","r":"NEC 2023 220.83(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Dwelling and multi-family service (Art. 220)","d":3,"q":"A dwelling addition includes a new single-unit 9-kW electric furnace (the only new heating or cooling). The remaining general and appliance loads, existing plus new, total 27,000 VA. Using the existing-dwelling-unit method, what is the calculated load on the 120/240-V single-phase service?","o":["102.5 A","89.4 A","80.0 A","136.9 A"],"a":1,"e":"220.83(B): other loads = 8,000 + 0.40 x (27,000 - 8,000) = 15,600 VA. One separately controlled electric heating unit (fewer than four) is taken at 65%: 9,000 x 0.65 = 5850 VA. Total 21450 VA / 240 = 89.4 A.","r":"NEC 2023 220.83(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Dwelling and multi-family service (Art. 220)","d":3,"q":"What is the minimum size copper conductor permitted for the ungrounded service conductors of a single-family dwelling with a 150-A, 120/240-V single-phase service (75 degree C terminations, 83% rule applied)?","o":["1/0 AWG","2 AWG","2/0 AWG","1 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"310.12 lets service conductors for a one-family dwelling be 83% of the service rating: 150 x 0.83 = 124.5 A. Table 310.16 (75 degree C Cu): 2 AWG = 115 A (too small), 1 AWG = 130 A (enough). 1/0 AWG is what the full 150 A would require.","r":"NEC 2023 310.12(A), (C), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Dwelling and multi-family service (Art. 220)","d":3,"q":"A 3,200 sq ft dwelling (120/240-V, single-phase, standard method) has two small-appliance circuits, a laundry circuit, a 14-kW range, a 5.5-kW dryer, four fixed appliances (1.2-kW dishwasher, 0.9-kW disposer, 4.5-kW water heater, 1.0-kW compactor), 10-kW of electric heat in a single unit and a 4-kW air-conditioner that cannot run with the heat. What is the calculated service current?","o":["153.7 A","161.6 A","170.4 A","150.4 A"],"a":0,"e":"General 14100 VA -> 6885 VA after 220.42. Range 14 kW: 8 kW + 2x5% = 8800 VA; dryer 5,500; four appliances 7600 x 75% = 5700; heat 10,000 (A/C omitted per 220.60). Total 36885 VA / 240 = 153.7 A.","r":"NEC 2023 220.42, 220.51, 220.53, 220.55, 220.60","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Dwelling and multi-family service (Art. 220)","d":3,"q":"A 10-unit apartment house is fed by a single 208Y/120-V, 3-phase feeder using the standard method. Each unit is 900 sq ft with two small-appliance circuits, a laundry circuit, an 8-kW range, a 5-kW dryer, and four fixed appliances totaling 7.6 kW. What is the calculated feeder current (no common-area or HVAC loads)?","o":["377.9 A","425.1 A","372.4 A","441.8 A"],"a":2,"e":"Lighting/SA/laundry = 72000 VA -> 27150 VA (3,000 at 100%, rest at 35%). Ten ranges = 25,000 VA (Col. C); ten dryers 5,000 each x 50% = 25000; appliances 76,000 x 75% = 57000. Total 134150 VA / (1.732x208) = 372.4 A.","r":"NEC 2023 220.42, 220.53, 220.54, 220.55","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Dwelling and multi-family service (Art. 220)","d":3,"q":"Which of the following is a requirement for using the optional calculation for multifamily dwellings in 220.84?","o":["Each dwelling unit may be supplied by two feeders as long as each is under 100 A.","Dwelling units must have no electric cooking so that the table factor is conservative.","A minimum of two dwelling units is sufficient for the table to apply.","Each dwelling unit is equipped with electric cooking and with electric space heating, air conditioning, or both."],"a":3,"e":"220.84(A) requires: no dwelling unit supplied by more than one feeder, every unit equipped with electric cooking, and every unit equipped with electric space heating, air conditioning, or both. Table 220.84 begins at three dwelling units.","r":"NEC 2023 220.84(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Dwelling and multi-family service (Art. 220)","d":3,"q":"An 18-unit apartment building (each unit on only one feeder) has 208Y/120-V, 3-phase service. Each unit has 1,000 sq ft, two small-appliance circuits, a laundry circuit, a 9-kW range, 1.2-kW dishwasher, 4.5-kW water heater, 5-kW dryer, and a 4-kW air-conditioner (no electric heat). House loads (corridor lighting, pumps, elevator) total 22,000 VA after their own demand factors. Using the optional method, what is the calculated service current?","o":["615.6 A","653.4 A","592.4 A","684.6 A"],"a":1,"e":"Per unit = 31200 VA; 18 units = 561600 VA. Table 220.84 for 18 units = 38%: 213408 VA. House loads are added at 100% (not reduced): total 235408 VA / (1.732 x 208) = 653.4 A.","r":"NEC 2023 220.84(B), (C), Table 220.84","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Dwelling and multi-family service (Art. 220)","d":3,"q":"A 400-A, 120/240-V service uses two parallel sets of 3/0 AWG copper (167,800 cmil each) as ungrounded service conductors. What is the minimum copper grounding electrode conductor size from Table 250.66 for a connection to a metal water pipe electrode?","o":["2 AWG","4 AWG","1/0 AWG","1 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"For parallel conductors Table 250.66 uses the combined area: 2 x 167,800 = 335,600 cmil. This is over 3/0 AWG up to 350 kcmil, so the copper GEC is 2 AWG. 4 AWG would be right only for a single 2/0 or 3/0 set.","r":"NEC 2023 250.66, Table 250.66","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Commercial and industrial service and feeder loads","d":3,"q":"A retail store (12,000 sq ft at 3 VA/sq ft) and an attached restaurant (4,000 sq ft at 2 VA/sq ft) are supplied by a 480Y/277-V, 3-phase feeder. The general lighting is the only load and is continuous. What is the minimum standard feeder overcurrent device (240.6(A))?","o":["60 A","70 A","80 A","175 A"],"a":1,"e":"Lighting = 36,000 + 8,000 = 44000 VA; continuous so x1.25 = 55000 VA. I = 55000/(1.732x480) = 66.2 A, so the next standard size is 70 A. 60 A skips the 125% (52.9 A); 175 A divides by 208 V.","r":"NEC 2023 220.12, Table 220.12, 215.3, 240.6(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Commercial and industrial service and feeder loads","d":3,"q":"A storage warehouse has 80,000 sq ft of floor area. Using Table 220.12 for the general lighting load (0.25 VA/sq ft) and the lighting demand factors of Table 220.42, what is the lighting demand load?","o":["20,000 VA","10,000 VA","20,312 VA","16,250 VA"],"a":3,"e":"Connected = 80,000 x 0.25 = 20000 VA. Table 220.42 for warehouses: first 12,500 VA at 100% and the remainder at 50%: 12,500 + 0.5 x 7500 = 16250 VA. The 125% continuous factor is applied later, not in the demand table.","r":"NEC 2023 Table 220.12, Table 220.42","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Commercial and industrial service and feeder loads","d":3,"q":"An office has 100 general-use duplex receptacles (each on a single yoke) and 40 ft of multioutlet assembly where appliances are likely to be used simultaneously. Using 180 VA per receptacle and 180 VA per foot where simultaneous use is likely, what is the receptacle demand load under Table 220.44?","o":["17,600 VA","25,200 VA","12,600 VA","14,720 VA"],"a":0,"e":"Connected = 100 x 180 + 40 x 180 = 25200 VA. Table 220.44: first 10 kVA at 100%, remainder at 50%: 10,000 + 0.5 x 15200 = 17600 VA. 14720 VA wrongly counts 180 VA per 5 ft.","r":"NEC 2023 220.14(H), 220.14(I), 220.44","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Commercial and industrial service and feeder loads","d":3,"q":"A restaurant has seven commercial electric cooking units rated 12, 9, 8, 6, 5, 4 and 3 kW on a 208Y/120-V, 3-phase feeder. Using Table 220.56, what is the calculated feeder current for the kitchen equipment?","o":["130.5 A","91.3 A","84.8 A","58.3 A"],"a":2,"e":"Total = 47 kW. Table 220.56: six or more units are 65%, so demand = 30.55 kW, which exceeds the two largest units (21 kW). I = 30550/(1.732x208) = 84.8 A.","r":"NEC 2023 220.56, Table 220.56","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Commercial and industrial service and feeder loads","d":3,"q":"A commercial kitchen feeder serves four commercial electric cooking units rated 30 kW, 25 kW, 1 kW and 1 kW. What is the minimum kitchen equipment demand permitted under 220.56?","o":["55 kW","45.6 kW","57 kW","51.3 kW"],"a":0,"e":"Four units at 80% gives 57 x 0.8 = 45.6 kW. 220.56 states the demand cannot be less than the sum of the two largest units: 30 + 25 = 55 kW, which governs.","r":"NEC 2023 220.56 (last sentence), Table 220.56","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Commercial and industrial service and feeder loads","d":3,"q":"An existing 480Y/277-V, 3-phase service has a recorded maximum demand of 142 kVA for the past 12 months (15-minute demand, continuous recording). A noncontinuous 30-kVA load is to be added. What is the calculated load current for the service with the addition?","o":["258.6 A","206.9 A","249.6 A","213.5 A"],"a":2,"e":"220.87: existing load = 125% of the maximum demand = 142 x 1.25 = 177.5 kVA; add the new noncontinuous load at 100%: 207.5 kVA. I = 207500/(1.732x480) = 249.6 A.","r":"NEC 2023 220.87","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Commercial and industrial service and feeder loads","d":3,"q":"A 480-V, 3-phase feeder supplies 40-hp, 30-hp and 20-hp squirrel-cage motors (Table 430.250 at 460 V: 52, 40 and 27 A) plus a 16-A continuous lighting load. What is the minimum copper feeder conductor size (THHN, 75 degree C terminations)?","o":["1/0 AWG","2/0 AWG","1 AWG","3/0 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"430.24: 125% of the largest motor + the other motors + other loads = 1.25x52 + 40 + 27 + 1.25x16 = 152 A. Table 310.16 (75 degree C): 1/0 = 150 A (too small), 2/0 = 175 A (enough).","r":"NEC 2023 430.24, 215.2(A)(1), Table 310.16, Table 430.250","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Commercial and industrial service and feeder loads","d":3,"q":"A 208Y/120-V, 3-phase, 4-wire commercial service has a calculated 90 kVA of continuous load and 58 kVA of noncontinuous load. What is the minimum standard service overcurrent device (240.6(A)) permitted?","o":["400 A","450 A","500 A","600 A"],"a":2,"e":"125% x 90 + 58 = 170.5 kVA. I = 170500/(1.732x208) = 473.3 A, so the next standard size above is 500 A (230.90/240.4(B)). 450 A comes from omitting the 125% (411 A).","r":"NEC 2023 215.3, 230.90, 240.6(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Commercial and industrial service and feeder loads","d":3,"q":"A hospital area of 60,000 sq ft (2 VA/sq ft per Table 220.12) is calculated under Table 220.42, assuming the entire area could be lit simultaneously. What is the general lighting demand?","o":["34,000 VA","120,000 VA","48,000 VA","24,000 VA"],"a":0,"e":"Connected = 60,000 x 2 = 120000 VA. Hospitals: first 50,000 VA at 40% = 20,000, remainder 70000 VA at 20% = 14000. Demand = 34000 VA.","r":"NEC 2023 Table 220.12, Table 220.42","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Commercial and industrial service and feeder loads","d":3,"q":"An existing 800-A, 208Y/120-V, 3-phase service shows a 12-month maximum demand of 215 kVA (15-minute recorded demand). How many additional noncontinuous kVA can be added without exceeding the service rating?","o":["73.2 kVA","19.5 kVA","15.6 kVA","91.5 kVA"],"a":1,"e":"Service capacity = 800 x 1.732 x 208 = 288.2 kVA. 220.87 requires 125% of recorded demand: 215 x 1.25 = 268.75 kVA. Available = 288.2 - 268.75 = 19.5 kVA.","r":"NEC 2023 220.87","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Commercial and industrial service and feeder loads","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase feeder carries 160 A in eight current-carrying THHN copper conductors in one conduit (30 degree C ambient, terminations rated 75 degree C). What is the minimum conductor size?","o":["4/0 AWG","2/0 AWG","3/0 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"Seven to nine current-carrying conductors require a 70% adjustment. 160/0.70 = 228.6 A is needed in the 90 degree C column: 3/0 = 225 A is short; 4/0 = 260 A works (and 4/0 at 75 degree C = 230 A covers 160 A). 2/0 ignores the adjustment.","r":"NEC 2023 310.14, Table 310.16, 310.15(C)(1)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Commercial and industrial service and feeder loads","d":3,"q":"A 112.5-kVA, 480-V delta to 208Y/120-V transformer has primary-only overcurrent protection and no secondary protection. What is the maximum primary overcurrent device size permitted?","o":["150 A","200 A","175 A","225 A"],"a":2,"e":"Primary FLC = 112,500/(1.732x480) = 135.3 A. Table 450.3(B) for primary only, 9 A or more: 125% = 169.1 A; Note 1 allows the next higher standard size, 175 A (240.6(A)).","r":"NEC 2023 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B), 240.6(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Neutral loads, harmonics and unbalanced loads","d":3,"q":"A 208Y/120-V, 3-phase, 4-wire panelboard carries only linear 120-V line-to-neutral loads. The phase currents are 120 A, 90 A and 60 A, 120 degrees apart. What is the actual neutral current?","o":["60.0 A","90.0 A","120.0 A","52.0 A"],"a":3,"e":"Neutral current = sqrt(A^2+B^2+C^2 - AB - BC - CA) = sqrt(14,400+8,100+3,600-10,800-5,400-7,200) = sqrt(2,700) = 52.0 A. 60 A (120-60) ignores the 120-degree phase relationship.","r":"NEC 2023 220.61(A) (physical neutral current)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Neutral loads, harmonics and unbalanced loads","d":3,"q":"A 208Y/120-V, 3-phase, 4-wire feeder serves only linear (resistive and incandescent) line-to-neutral loads. The maximum unbalanced load is 380 A. What is the neutral load for sizing the feeder neutral under 220.61?","o":["380 A","326 A","266 A","320 A"],"a":1,"e":"220.61(B) allows a 70% demand on the portion of the unbalanced load over 200 A: 200 + 0.70 x (380 - 200) = 326 A. 266 A applies 70% to the whole load; 320 A reduces only the first 200 A.","r":"NEC 2023 220.61(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Neutral loads, harmonics and unbalanced loads","d":3,"q":"A 208Y/120-V, 3-phase, 4-wire feeder supplies electronic loads and carries 140 A per phase. Four THHN copper conductors are in one conduit (30 degree C ambient; 75 degree C terminations). What is the minimum conductor size for the phases and neutral?","o":["2/0 AWG","1/0 AWG","1 AWG","3/0 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"With the major load nonlinear, the neutral is a current-carrying conductor, so four conductors count and the 80% factor applies: 140/0.80 = 175 A in the 90 degree C column. 1/0 = 170 A is short; 2/0 = 195 A works, and 2/0 at 75 degree C is 175 A, covering 140 A. 1/0 results from counting only three conductors.","r":"NEC 2023 310.15(C)(1), 310.15(E), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Neutral loads, harmonics and unbalanced loads","d":3,"q":"A balanced 3-phase, 4-wire wye system supplies single-phase electronic power supplies. Each phase carries 100 A at the fundamental plus 60 A of third-harmonic current. What is the approximate neutral current?","o":["0 A","180 A","60 A","100 A"],"a":1,"e":"Fundamental currents cancel in the neutral in a balanced system, but triplen harmonics are in phase and add: 3 x 60 = 180 A. The neutral can carry more current than the phases.","r":"NEC 2023 220.61, 310.15(E)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Neutral loads, harmonics and unbalanced loads","d":3,"q":"A true-RMS meter reads 120 A on each phase of a balanced 3-phase, 4-wire system with a third-harmonic content equal to 55% of the fundamental (other harmonics negligible). What is the neutral current?","o":["120.0 A","66.0 A","360.0 A","173.5 A"],"a":3,"e":"I_rms^2 = I1^2 + I3^2 with I3 = 0.55 I1, so I1 = 120/sqrt(1+0.55^2) = 105.1 A and I3 = 57.8 A. Neutral = 3 x I3 = 173.5 A. 360 A wrongly adds three full RMS readings.","r":"NEC 2023 220.61, 310.15(E)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Neutral loads, harmonics and unbalanced loads","d":3,"q":"A 208Y/120-V, 3-phase, 4-wire feeder carries 150 A of fundamental current per phase with third-harmonic current equal to 70% of the fundamental. Using 75 degree C copper (Table 310.16), what is the minimum neutral conductor size to carry the neutral current only?","o":["400 kcmil","350 kcmil","300 kcmil","500 kcmil"],"a":0,"e":"Neutral = 3 x 0.70 x 150 = 315 A. Table 310.16 (75 degree C Cu): 300 kcmil = 285 A, 350 kcmil = 310 A (short), 400 kcmil = 335 A.","r":"NEC 2023 220.61, Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Neutral loads, harmonics and unbalanced loads","d":3,"q":"Three 3-phase, 4-wire branch circuits supplying LED drivers and electronic loads (a majority nonlinear) share one conduit with 10 AWG THHN copper. What is the adjusted ampacity of each conductor, using the 90 degree C column (40 A for 10 AWG)?","o":["28 A","32 A","20 A","35 A"],"a":2,"e":"The neutral of each circuit carries harmonic current and counts, so 3 x 4 = 12 current-carrying conductors. For 10-20 conductors the factor is 50%: 40 x 0.50 = 20 A. 28 A (70%) counts only nine conductors, ignoring the neutrals.","r":"NEC 2023 310.15(C)(1), 310.15(E)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Neutral loads, harmonics and unbalanced loads","d":3,"q":"A 120/240-V, single-phase, 3-wire feeder has 45 A of 120-V loads on line 1, 28 A of 120-V loads on line 2, and a 50-A 240-V load. What is the neutral current?","o":["73 A","50 A","28 A","17 A"],"a":3,"e":"The 240-V load does not use the neutral. The neutral carries the difference between the line-to-neutral loads: 45 - 28 = 17 A. 73 A adds them as though on a 3-phase circuit.","r":"NEC 2023 220.61(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Neutral loads, harmonics and unbalanced loads","d":3,"q":"For a dwelling's 120/240-V service, the demand loads are 8,000 VA of general lighting and receptacles (120 V, divided evenly), a 9,600-VA range demand and a 5,500-VA dryer. Using the 70% rule for the range and dryer neutral load and VA/240, what is the service neutral load?","o":["96.3 A","77.4 A","61.3 A","110.7 A"],"a":1,"e":"General: 8,000/240 = 33.3 A. Range neutral: 9,600 x 0.70/240 = 28.0 A. Dryer neutral: 5,500 x 0.70/240 = 16.0 A. Total = 77.4 A. Using 100% for the range and dryer gives 96.3 A.","r":"NEC 2023 220.61(B), 220.55, 220.54","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Neutral loads, harmonics and unbalanced loads","d":3,"q":"A 480Y/277-V, 3-phase, 4-wire feeder supplies 277-V LED and electronic-ballast lighting. Which statement is correct?","o":["The neutral counts as a current-carrying conductor for adjustment factors, and the 70% neutral demand over 200 A may not be applied to that load.","The neutral never counts as current-carrying since the phases are balanced, and a 70% reduction over 200 A is allowed.","The neutral counts, but it may be reduced to 50% size under 220.61 because the 3rd harmonic cancels.","The neutral is not counted, but no demand reduction is permitted."],"a":0,"e":"For a 4-wire wye circuit whose major load is nonlinear, harmonic currents are present in the neutral, so it is a current-carrying conductor, and 220.61(C) prohibits the 70% reduction for that nonlinear portion. Triplen harmonics add rather than cancel.","r":"NEC 2023 310.15(E), 220.61(C)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Neutral loads, harmonics and unbalanced loads","d":3,"q":"A multiwire branch circuit uses two ungrounded conductors from different phases of a 208Y/120-V system and one shared neutral. The loads are 16 A and 12 A (linear). What is the neutral current?","o":["4.0 A","28.0 A","24.3 A","20.0 A"],"a":2,"e":"The phases are 120 degrees apart, so neutral = sqrt(16^2 + 12^2 + 16x12) = sqrt(592) = 24.3 A. The 4-A answer applies only to the opposite-polarity legs of a 120/240-V single-phase system.","r":"NEC 2023 210.4(A), 310.15(E)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Neutral loads, harmonics and unbalanced loads","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 4-wire service uses 400 kcmil copper ungrounded conductors. The maximum unbalanced load is 90 A. What is the minimum copper grounded (neutral) service conductor size?","o":["3 AWG","1/0 AWG","2 AWG","3/0 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"The service neutral must carry the 90 A unbalance (3 AWG = 100 A at 75 degree C) but must also not be smaller than Table 250.102(C)(1) for 400 kcmil (over 350 to 600 kcmil), which is 1/0 AWG copper. The larger requirement governs.","r":"NEC 2023 250.24(C)(1), Table 250.102(C)(1), 220.61","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Neutral loads, harmonics and unbalanced loads","d":3,"q":"A 208Y/120-V, 3-phase, 4-wire feeder has line-to-neutral loads of 85 A on phase A, 60 A on phase B and 40 A on phase C, plus 120 A of line-to-line 208-V loads. What is the maximum unbalanced load under 220.61(A)?","o":["85 A","185 A","45 A","205 A"],"a":0,"e":"The maximum unbalanced load is the largest net computed load between the neutral and any one ungrounded conductor: 85 A. Line-to-line loads do not use the neutral, so 205 A and 185 A are wrong.","r":"NEC 2023 220.61(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor branch circuits and overloads (Art. 430)","d":3,"q":"A 15-hp, 460-V motor has a nameplate current of 19.6 A and a service factor of 1.15. Table 430.250 FLC is 21 A. What is the maximum overload trip current permitted by 430.32(A)(1) before applying 430.32(C)?","o":["22.5 A","26.3 A","24.5 A","27.4 A"],"a":2,"e":"Overload sizing uses the nameplate current, not the table FLC, per 430.6(A)(2). A motor with service factor 1.15 or greater gets 125%: 19.6 x 1.25 = 24.5 A. 115% (22.5 A) applies to motors without those markings, 26.3 A wrongly uses the table FLC, and 140% is only available under 430.32(C).","r":"NEC 2023 430.6(A)(2), 430.32(A)(1), 430.32(C)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor branch circuits and overloads (Art. 430)","d":3,"q":"A 10-hp, 460-V motor is nameplated 13.6 A, service factor 1.0, temperature rise 55 C. Table 430.250 FLC is 14 A. What is the maximum overload device trip current under 430.32(A)(1), before 430.32(C)?","o":["17.00 A","16.10 A","17.50 A","15.64 A"],"a":3,"e":"The motor has service factor below 1.15 and a temperature rise above 40 C, so 115% of the nameplate applies: 13.6 x 1.15 = 15.64 A. 125% is for SF 1.15+ or 40 C rise; the other two wrongly start from the 14-A table FLC, which is not used for overloads.","r":"NEC 2023 430.6(A)(2), 430.32(A)(1)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor branch circuits and overloads (Art. 430)","d":3,"q":"A 75-hp, 460-V, 3-phase induction motor is fed with copper THWN conductors in conduit, 75 C terminations, 30 C ambient, no derating. What is the minimum conductor size for the branch circuit?","o":["2 AWG","1 AWG","1/0 AWG","3 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Table 430.250 FLC = 96 A. Per 430.22 the conductor ampacity is 96 x 1.25 = 120 A. Table 310.16 at 75 C: 2 AWG is 115 A (short), 1 AWG is 130 A (OK). 2 AWG results from using 115%, 3 AWG from using 100% (96 A), and 1/0 from the 60 C column.","r":"NEC 2023 430.22, 430.250, Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor branch circuits and overloads (Art. 430)","d":3,"q":"A 5-hp, 230-V, single-phase motor (Table 430.248 FLC 28 A) trips its inverse-time breaker on starting even at the 430.52 Table maximum. What is the maximum inverse-time breaker permitted under 430.52(C)(1) Exception No. 2?","o":["70 A","125 A","110 A","90 A"],"a":2,"e":"FLC from Table 430.248 is 28 A. For FLC of 100 A or less, the breaker may be raised but not above 400%: 28 x 4 = 112 A, so the largest standard size not exceeding that is 110 A. 70 A is the 250% value, 125 A would exceed 400%, and 90 A is 300%.","r":"NEC 2023 430.248, 430.52(C)(1) Exception 2, Table 430.52","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor branch circuits and overloads (Art. 430)","d":3,"q":"A 20-hp, 460-V Design B motor (Table 430.250 FLC 27 A) is protected by an instantaneous-trip circuit breaker (motor circuit protector) that is part of a listed combination motor controller. What is the maximum instantaneous setting under Table 430.52 (no energy-efficient exception)?","o":["297 A","135 A","81 A","216 A"],"a":3,"e":"Table 430.52 allows an instantaneous-trip breaker up to 800% of FLC: 27 x 8 = 216 A. 297 A is 1100%, which is only for the 430.52(C)(3) Exception when 800% will not allow starting; 135 A and 81 A are 500% and 300%.","r":"NEC 2023 430.52(C)(3), Table 430.52, 430.250","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor branch circuits and overloads (Art. 430)","d":3,"q":"A 50-hp, 230-V, 3-phase motor (Table 430.250 FLC 130 A) is served through an ampere-rated motor disconnect. What is the minimum ampere rating required by 430.110(A)?","o":["130.0 A","162.5 A","149.5 A","195.0 A"],"a":2,"e":"430.110(A) requires an ampere rating of at least 115% of the motor FLC from the Table: 130 x 1.15 = 149.5 A. 130 A ignores the 115%, 162.5 A applies 125% (conductor rule), and 195 A applies 150%.","r":"NEC 2023 430.110(A), 430.250","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor branch circuits and overloads (Art. 430)","d":3,"q":"A 10-hp, 460-V, 3-phase motor is nameplated 12.8 A; Table 430.250 FLC is 14 A. What is the minimum branch-circuit conductor ampacity (before any correction) required by 430.22?","o":["16.0 A","14.7 A","14.0 A","17.5 A"],"a":3,"e":"Branch-circuit conductors are sized on the Table 430.250 FLC, not the nameplate: 14 x 1.25 = 17.5 A. 16.0 A uses the nameplate, 14.7 A uses nameplate and 115%, and 14.0 A skips the 125%.","r":"NEC 2023 430.6(A)(1), 430.22, 430.250","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor branch circuits and overloads (Art. 430)","d":3,"q":"A 3-hp, 460-V, 3-phase Design B motor has Table 430.250 FLC of 4.8 A. What is the maximum standard inverse-time breaker rating under 430.52?","o":["15 A","10 A","20 A","25 A"],"a":0,"e":"250% of 4.8 A = 12.0 A. 12 A is not a standard size, so 430.52(C)(1) Exception No. 1 permits the next higher, 15 A (240.6(A)). 10 A rounds down; 20 A and 25 A are 400% (19.2 A) and 500% values.","r":"NEC 2023 430.250, Table 430.52, 430.52(C)(1) Exception 1, 240.6(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor branch circuits and overloads (Art. 430)","d":3,"q":"A 30-hp, 460-V motor is nameplated 40.0 A with a service factor of 1.15. The overload relay selected at 125% of nameplate trips on starting. What is the maximum trip setting permitted by 430.32(C)?","o":["50.0 A","56.0 A","52.0 A","46.0 A"],"a":1,"e":"If the 125% selection is not sufficient to start the motor, 430.32(C) permits up to 140% for a motor with SF 1.15 or greater: 40.0 x 1.40 = 56.0 A. 130% is the limit for motors rated with SF below 1.15 or temperature rise above 40 C; 125% is the original selection and 115% is lower.","r":"NEC 2023 430.32(A)(1), 430.32(C)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor branch circuits and overloads (Art. 430)","d":3,"q":"A 25-hp, 208-V, 3-phase Design B motor (Table 430.250 FLC 74.8 A) is protected by dual-element fuses. What is the maximum standard fuse rating under Table 430.52 and 430.52(C)(1) Exception No. 1?","o":["125 A","200 A","225 A","150 A"],"a":3,"e":"74.8 x 1.75 = 130.9 A, not a standard size, so the next higher size, 150 A, is permitted. 125 A rounds down; 200 A is 250% and 225 A is 300%.","r":"NEC 2023 430.250, Table 430.52, 430.52(C)(1) Exception 1","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor branch circuits and overloads (Art. 430)","d":3,"q":"A 40-hp, 460-V motor (Table 430.250 FLC 52 A) is controlled by a variable frequency drive whose nameplate rated input current is 46 A. What is the minimum ampacity of the conductors supplying the drive?","o":["57.5 A","65.0 A","46.0 A","52.9 A"],"a":0,"e":"Conductors supplying power conversion equipment are sized at 125% of the rated input current on the drive nameplate: 46 x 1.25 = 57.5 A (430.122(A)). 65 A would be 125% of the motor FLC, 46 A skips the 125%, and 52.9 A uses 115%.","r":"NEC 2023 430.122(A), 430.22","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor branch circuits and overloads (Art. 430)","d":3,"q":"A 7.5-hp, 460-V motor (Table 430.250 FLC 11 A) is protected by an integral thermal protector as its running overload protection. What is the maximum protector trip current permitted by 430.32(A)(2)?","o":["15.40 A","17.16 A","13.75 A","18.70 A"],"a":1,"e":"For integral thermal protectors, 430.32(A)(2) limits trip current by FLC: 9 A or less 170%, 9.1 to 20 A 156%, over 20 A 140%. At 11 A the 156% row applies: 11 x 1.56 = 17.16 A. The others are the 140%, 125% and 170% rows.","r":"NEC 2023 430.32(A)(2), 430.250","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor feeders, multiple motors and starting","d":3,"q":"A 460-V, 3-phase feeder supplies a 50-hp, a 25-hp and a 10-hp squirrel-cage motor (Table 430.250 FLC 65, 34, 14 A). Copper conductors, 75 C terminations, no derating. What is the minimum feeder conductor size?","o":["1 AWG","2 AWG","1/0 AWG","2/0 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"430.24(A): 125% of the largest motor plus the others = 65 x 1.25 + 34 + 14 = 129.25 A, so 1 AWG (130 A). Without any 125% (113 A) you get 2 AWG; 125% of all (141.25 A) gives 1/0; 2/0 would result from the 60 C column.","r":"NEC 2023 430.24(A), Table 430.250, Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor feeders, multiple motors and starting","d":3,"q":"For the 50-, 25- and 10-hp, 460-V motor feeder (FLC 65, 34, 14 A), each branch circuit uses the maximum inverse-time breaker permitted by Table 430.52. What is the maximum standard breaker for the feeder per 430.62(A)?","o":["225 A","200 A","175 A","250 A"],"a":1,"e":"Largest branch device: 65 x 2.5 = 162.5 A, rounded up to 175 A. Add the other FLCs (34+14): 175+48 = 223 A, and the feeder device may not exceed that, so the next lower standard size is 200 A. 225 A exceeds the limit; 175 A ignores the other motors; 250 A has no basis.","r":"NEC 2023 430.62(A), 430.52, 240.6(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor feeders, multiple motors and starting","d":3,"q":"A 230-V single-phase feeder supplies three motors with Table 430.248 FLC of 40 A, 28 A and 17 A. Copper conductors, 75 C terminations, no derating. What is the minimum feeder conductor size?","o":["4 AWG","2 AWG","3 AWG","1 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"40 x 1.25 + 28 + 17 = 95 A, which needs 3 AWG (100 A). 4 AWG results from omitting the 125% (85 A), 2 AWG from applying 125% to all (106.25 A), and 1 AWG is oversized.","r":"NEC 2023 430.24(A), Table 430.248, Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor feeders, multiple motors and starting","d":3,"q":"A 208-V, 3-phase feeder supplies a 15-hp motor (46.2 A), a 10-hp motor (30.8 A) and 14 kVA of continuous lighting load. Copper, 75 C terminations, no derating. What is the minimum feeder conductor size?","o":["1 AWG","1/0 AWG","3 AWG","2/0 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Lighting: 14000/(1.732 x 208) = 38.9 A, x125% (continuous) = 48.6 A. Motors: 46.2 x 1.25 + 30.8 = 88.55 A. Total = 137.1 A, so 1/0 AWG (150 A). 1 AWG omits the 125% on lighting (127.4 A), 3 AWG omits the lighting entirely, and 2/0 uses the single-phase kVA/V conversion for the lighting.","r":"NEC 2023 430.24(A), 430.24(C), 215.2(A)(1), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor feeders, multiple motors and starting","d":3,"q":"A 460-V feeder serves 40-, 20- and 10-hp motors (Table 430.250 FLC 52, 27, 14 A). Each branch circuit is protected by the maximum dual-element fuses permitted by Table 430.52. What is the maximum standard feeder fuse per 430.62(A)?","o":["150 A","100 A","125 A","175 A"],"a":2,"e":"Largest branch fuse: 52 x 1.75 = 91 A, which is 100 A. Feeder limit = 100 + 27 + 14 = 141 A; the largest standard size not exceeding it is 125 A. 150 A is next higher (not permitted), 100 A omits the other motors, and 175 A is unsupported.","r":"NEC 2023 430.62(A), Table 430.52, 240.6(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor feeders, multiple motors and starting","d":3,"q":"A 15-hp, 460-V Design B motor (Table 430.250 FLC 21 A) on dual-element fuses will not start on the 430.52 Table maximum (175%, next size up). What is the maximum fuse permitted under 430.52(C)(1) Exception No. 2?","o":["50 A","40 A","60 A","45 A"],"a":3,"e":"Exception 2(b) allows dual-element fuses to be increased, but not above 225% of FLC: 21 x 2.25 = 47.25 A, so the largest standard size not exceeding it is 45 A. 50 A exceeds 225%, 40 A is the normal 175% result, and 60 A is 300%.","r":"NEC 2023 430.52(C)(1) Exception 2, Table 430.52","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor feeders, multiple motors and starting","d":3,"q":"A 460-V feeder supplies two 30-hp motors and one 10-hp motor (Table 430.250 FLC 40, 40, 14 A). Copper, 75 C terminations, no derating. What is the minimum feeder conductor size?","o":["2 AWG","3 AWG","1 AWG","4 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"Only one of the equal largest motors gets 125%: 40 x 1.25 + 40 + 14 = 104 A, requiring 2 AWG (115 A). 3 AWG results from no 125% (94 A); 1 AWG from 125% of all (117.5 A); 4 AWG is undersized.","r":"NEC 2023 430.24(A), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor feeders, multiple motors and starting","d":3,"q":"A 230-V feeder supplies 40-, 30- and 7.5-hp motors (Table 430.250 FLC 104, 80, 22 A). Each branch circuit uses the maximum nontime-delay fuses permitted by Table 430.52. What is the maximum standard feeder fuse under 430.62(A)?","o":["500 A","350 A","450 A","400 A"],"a":2,"e":"Largest branch fuse: 104 x 3.00 = 312 A, next higher standard 350 A. Feeder limit = 350 + 80 + 22 = 452 A; the largest standard size not exceeding it is 450 A. 500 A is above the limit, 350 A ignores the other motors, 400 A has no basis.","r":"NEC 2023 430.62(A), Table 430.52, 240.6(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor feeders, multiple motors and starting","d":3,"q":"A 208-V, 3-phase feeder serves 25-, 15- and 7.5-hp motors (Table 430.250 FLC 74.8, 46.2, 24.2 A). Copper, 75 C terminations, no derating. What is the minimum feeder conductor size?","o":["1/0 AWG","3/0 AWG","4/0 AWG","2/0 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"74.8 x 1.25 + 46.2 + 24.2 = 163.9 A, so 2/0 AWG (175 A). 1/0 AWG results from no 125% (145.2 A), 3/0 from 125% of all (181.5 A), and 4/0 is oversized.","r":"NEC 2023 430.24(A), Table 430.250, Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor feeders, multiple motors and starting","d":3,"q":"A 208-V feeder supplies 30-, 20- and 10-hp motors (Table 430.250 FLC 88, 59.4, 30.8 A). Each branch circuit uses the maximum inverse-time breaker per Table 430.52. What is the maximum standard feeder breaker under 430.62(A)?","o":["300 A","350 A","225 A","400 A"],"a":0,"e":"Largest branch breaker: 88 x 2.5 = 220 A, which is the standard 225 A. Feeder limit = 225 + 59.4 + 30.8 = 315.2 A; the largest standard size not exceeding it is 300 A. 350 A exceeds the limit; 225 A ignores the other motors.","r":"NEC 2023 430.62(A), Table 430.52","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor feeders, multiple motors and starting","d":3,"q":"A 50-hp, 460-V motor (Table 430.250 FLC 65 A) is fed by 90 C THHN copper conductors, 3 in conduit, in a 40 C ambient. Terminations are rated 75 C. Use 310.15(B)(1) correction of 0.91 for 90 C and 0.88 for 75 C at 40 C. What is the minimum conductor size?","o":["3 AWG","4 AWG","2 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"430.22 requires 65 x 1.25 = 81.25 A. 4 AWG THHN: 95 A x 0.91 = 86.5 A, which is at least 81.25 A, and the 75 C termination limit of 85 A is also at least 81.25 A, so 4 AWG qualifies. 3 AWG results from applying 0.88 to the 75 C column; 2 AWG from over-derating; 6 AWG fails because its 75 C termination limit of 65 A is below 81.25 A.","r":"NEC 2023 430.22, 310.14(A)(3), 310.15(B)(1), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor feeders, multiple motors and starting","d":3,"q":"A 25-hp, 460-V, 3-phase motor is marked with code letter G; use 5.9 kVA per hp locked rotor. What is the approximate locked-rotor current?","o":["321 A","107 A","204 A","185 A"],"a":3,"e":"Locked-rotor kVA = 25 x 5.9 = 147.5 kVA. I = kVA x 1000 / (1.732 x 460) = 185.1 A. 321 A omits the 1.732 (single-phase formula); 107 A divides by 1.732 twice; 204 A is 6 x the 34-A FLC, which is a different assumption.","r":"NEC 2023 430.7(B), Table 430.7(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor feeders, multiple motors and starting","d":3,"q":"A 40-hp, 460-V motor (Table 430.250 FLC 52 A) has a locked-rotor current of 6 times FLC on full voltage. It is started with an autotransformer reduced-voltage starter on the 65% tap. Neglecting magnetizing current, what is the approximate line current at start?","o":["132 A","203 A","312 A","86 A"],"a":0,"e":"Full-voltage locked rotor = 6 x 52 = 312 A. Motor current at the 65% tap = 203 A, but autotransformer action reduces the line current by the tap ratio again: 312 x 0.65^2 = 131.8 A. 203 A forgets the second reduction; 312 A is full voltage; 86 A over-applies it.","r":"NEC 2023 430.7, 430.83","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Motor feeders, multiple motors and starting","d":3,"q":"A 60-hp, 460-V Design B motor (Table 430.250 FLC 77 A) on an inverse-time breaker will not start with the normal Table 430.52 breaker. What is the maximum inverse-time breaker permitted under 430.52(C)(1) Exception No. 2?","o":["200 A","300 A","225 A","350 A"],"a":1,"e":"For FLC of 100 A or less, the limit is 400%: 77 x 4 = 308 A, so the largest standard size not exceeding it is 300 A. 200 A is the normal 250% result, 225 A is 300%, and 350 A would exceed 400%.","r":"NEC 2023 430.52(C)(1) Exception 2(c), Table 430.52","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Transformers and secondary protection (Art. 450)","d":3,"q":"A 75-kVA, 3-phase, 480-V to 208Y/120-V transformer has primary protection only, with no secondary protection. What is the maximum standard primary overcurrent device rating under Table 450.3(B)?","o":["125 A","110 A","175 A","225 A"],"a":0,"e":"Primary FLA = 75,000/(1.732 x 480) = 90.2 A, which is 9 A or more, so 125%: 112.8 A. That is not standard, and Note 1 permits the next higher size, 125 A. 110 A rounds down; 175 A uses 167%; 225 A uses 250%.","r":"NEC 2023 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B), 240.6(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Transformers and secondary protection (Art. 450)","d":3,"q":"The 75-kVA, 480-V to 208Y/120-V transformer also has secondary overcurrent protection (primary protection permitted up to 250%). What is the maximum standard secondary overcurrent device rating under Table 450.3(B)?","o":["250 A","300 A","350 A","225 A"],"a":1,"e":"Secondary FLA = 75,000/(1.732 x 208) = 208.2 A. Secondary of 9 A or more: 125% = 260.2 A, next higher standard 300 A. 250 A rounds down; 350 A is 167%; 225 A is 100%.","r":"NEC 2023 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Transformers and secondary protection (Art. 450)","d":3,"q":"A 15-kVA, 3-phase, 480-V dry-type transformer has primary-only protection. What is the maximum standard primary overcurrent device under Table 450.3(B)?","o":["20 A","35 A","25 A","45 A"],"a":2,"e":"FLA = 15,000/(1.732 x 480) = 18.04 A (9 A or more), so 125% = 22.6 A, next higher standard 25 A. 20 A rounds down; 35 A is 167%; 45 A is 250%.","r":"NEC 2023 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Transformers and secondary protection (Art. 450)","d":3,"q":"A 150-kVA, 480-V to 208Y/120-V transformer has both primary and secondary protection. What is the maximum standard secondary overcurrent device rating per Table 450.3(B)?","o":["500 A","600 A","700 A","450 A"],"a":1,"e":"Secondary FLA = 150,000/(1.732 x 208) = 416.4 A. 125% = 520.4 A, next higher standard 600 A. 500 A rounds down; 700 A is 167%; 450 A is 100%.","r":"NEC 2023 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Transformers and secondary protection (Art. 450)","d":3,"q":"A 208Y/120-V, 3-phase panel has a calculated load of 190 A (noncontinuous and continuous already included). The standard transformer sizes are 45, 75, 112.5, 150 and 225 kVA. What is the smallest standard 3-phase transformer that will serve the load?","o":["45 kVA","112.5 kVA","75 kVA","150 kVA"],"a":2,"e":"kVA = 1.732 x 208 x 190/1000 = 68.5 kVA, so 75 kVA is the first standard size at or above. 45 kVA results from the single-phase formula (39.5 kVA); 112.5 kVA from an extra 125% (85.6 kVA); 150 kVA from multiplying by 3 instead of 1.732.","r":"NEC 2023 450.3(B), 215.2, Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Transformers and secondary protection (Art. 450)","d":3,"q":"A 112.5-kVA, 480-V to 208Y/120-V transformer is protected with a 350-A secondary breaker. Secondary conductors are copper, 75 C, and must carry the transformer secondary rated current. What is the minimum conductor size per phase (240.4(B) allowed, one conductor per phase)?","o":["350 kcmil","500 kcmil","300 kcmil","400 kcmil"],"a":3,"e":"Secondary FLA = 112,500/(1.732 x 208) = 312.3 A. 350 kcmil is 310 A, which is below 312.3 A, so 240.4(B) cannot be used (ampacity must be at least the load). 400 kcmil (335 A) is the minimum and is protected by the next-standard 350 A device. 500 kcmil is oversized; 300 kcmil is short.","r":"NEC 2023 240.4(B), 450.3(B), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Transformers and secondary protection (Art. 450)","d":3,"q":"A 45-kVA, 3-phase, 480-V transformer has primary-only protection per 450.3(B), at the maximum permitted standard device. Primary conductors are copper, 75 C, one set. Using 240.4(B) where permitted, what is the minimum primary conductor size?","o":["6 AWG","8 AWG","4 AWG","3 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"Primary FLA = 45,000/(1.732 x 480) = 54.1 A. 125% = 67.7 A so the device is 70 A. 6 AWG (65 A) exceeds the 54.1 A load and the next standard above 65 A is 70 A, so 240.4(B) permits 6 AWG. 8 AWG (50 A) is below the load; 4 AWG and 3 AWG are sized to the device or more.","r":"NEC 2023 450.3(B), 240.4(B), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Transformers and secondary protection (Art. 450)","d":3,"q":"A 50-kVA, single-phase, 480-V to 240/120-V transformer has primary-only protection. What is the maximum standard primary overcurrent device under Table 450.3(B)?","o":["125 A","175 A","150 A","250 A"],"a":2,"e":"Primary current = 50,000/480 = 104.2 A; 125% = 130.2 A, next higher standard 150 A. 125 A rounds down; 175 A is 167%; 250 A is 250%.","r":"NEC 2023 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Transformers and secondary protection (Art. 450)","d":3,"q":"A 112.5-kVA, 480-V to 208Y/120-V transformer has 3.5% impedance. Assuming an infinite primary source, what is the approximate available 3-phase fault current at the secondary terminals?","o":["15,453 A","3,866 A","1,093 A","8,922 A"],"a":3,"e":"Secondary FLA = 112,500/(1.732 x 208) = 312.3 A. I(sc) = FLA / %Z = 312.3 / 0.035 = 8,922 A. 15,453 A multiplies by 1.732; 3,866 A uses the primary current; 1,093 A multiplies by 3.5.","r":"NEC 2023 110.9, 110.10, 450.3","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Transformers and secondary protection (Art. 450)","d":3,"q":"A 75-kVA, 480-V to 208Y/120-V separately derived system has a secondary of one 250 kcmil copper conductor per phase. The grounding electrode conductor is connected to building structural steel. What is the minimum copper GEC size per 250.30(A)(5) and Table 250.66?","o":["2 AWG","4 AWG","1/0 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"250.30(A)(5) sizes the GEC from Table 250.66 based on the derived phase conductors. 250 kcmil falls in the 'over 3/0 through 350 kcmil' row, which is 2 AWG copper. 4 AWG is the 2/0-3/0 row, 1/0 is the 350-600 kcmil row, and 6 AWG is the 1 or 1/0 row.","r":"NEC 2023 250.30(A)(5), Table 250.66","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Transformers and secondary protection (Art. 450)","d":3,"q":"A 45-kVA, 480-V to 208Y/120-V transformer has both primary and secondary protection. What is the maximum standard secondary overcurrent device under Table 450.3(B)?","o":["150 A","175 A","350 A","125 A"],"a":1,"e":"Secondary FLA = 45,000/(1.732 x 208) = 124.9 A. 125% = 156.1 A, next higher standard 175 A. 150 A rounds down; 350 A uses 250%; 125 A is 100%.","r":"NEC 2023 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Transformers and secondary protection (Art. 450)","d":3,"q":"A 75-kVA, 3-phase, 240-V delta transformer secondary supplies a load. What is the rated secondary line current?","o":["312.5 A","104.2 A","541.3 A","180.4 A"],"a":3,"e":"I = kVA x 1000 / (1.732 x V) = 75,000 / (1.732 x 240) = 180.4 A. 312.5 A uses the single-phase formula, 104.2 A divides by 1.732 a second time, and 541.3 A multiplies by 3.","r":"NEC 2023 450.3(B), 220.5","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Transformers and secondary protection (Art. 450)","d":3,"q":"A 15-kVA, single-phase, 480-V primary transformer has primary-only protection. What is the maximum standard primary overcurrent device under Table 450.3(B)?","o":["35 A","40 A","60 A","80 A"],"a":1,"e":"Primary current = 15,000/480 = 31.25 A (9 A or more). 125% = 39.06 A, next higher standard 40 A. 35 A rounds down; 60 A is 167%; 80 A is 250%.","r":"NEC 2023 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: HVAC, generators and standby sizing (Art. 440, 445, 700-702)","d":3,"q":"A hermetic refrigerant motor-compressor has a rated-load current of 24.0 A. Using the 175% rule of 440.22(A), what is the largest standard branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault device size not exceeding the limit?","o":["40 A","45 A","50 A","30 A"],"a":0,"e":"175% x 24.0 = 42.0 A, so the largest standard size not exceeding it is 40 A. 45 A exceeds 175%; 50 A uses the 225% starting exception; 30 A is the conductor multiplier.","r":"NEC 2023 440.22(A), 240.6(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: HVAC, generators and standby sizing (Art. 440, 445, 700-702)","d":3,"q":"An air conditioner nameplate shows MCA 28.4 A and MOCP 45 A. Copper conductors, 75 C terminations, no derating. What is the minimum conductor size, and what is the maximum standard breaker?","o":["8 AWG with a 45-A breaker","10 AWG with a 45-A breaker","12 AWG with a 45-A breaker","10 AWG with a 50-A breaker"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors are selected from the MCA: 28.4 A needs 10 AWG (35 A at 75 C). The breaker may not exceed the MOCP of 45 A. 8 AWG sizes the wire to the MOCP, 12 AWG (25 A) is under the MCA, and a 50-A breaker exceeds the marked MOCP.","r":"NEC 2023 440.4(B), 440.22, 440.32, Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: HVAC, generators and standby sizing (Art. 440, 445, 700-702)","d":3,"q":"A condensing unit has a 18.6-A compressor RLC and a 1.8-A condenser fan motor. What is the minimum circuit ampacity (MCA) per 440.33?","o":["20.4 A","25.5 A","25.1 A","23.2 A"],"a":2,"e":"440.33: 125% of the largest motor plus the others = 18.6 x 1.25 + 1.8 = 25.05 A (25.1 A). 20.4 A adds without 125%; 25.5 A applies 125% to all; 23.2 A omits the fan.","r":"NEC 2023 440.33, 440.4(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: HVAC, generators and standby sizing (Art. 440, 445, 700-702)","d":3,"q":"A hermetic compressor has an RLC of 31.0 A and no branch-circuit selection current. What is the minimum ampere rating of the disconnecting means under 440.12(A)(1)?","o":["38.8 A","35.7 A","31.0 A","54.2 A"],"a":1,"e":"440.12(A)(1): at least 115% of the nameplate RLC: 31.0 x 1.15 = 35.65 A, or 35.7 A. 38.8 A applies 125%, 31.0 A has no margin, and 54.2 A is the protective-device multiplier.","r":"NEC 2023 440.12(A)(1)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: HVAC, generators and standby sizing (Art. 440, 445, 700-702)","d":3,"q":"A 150-kW, 0.8 power factor, 480-V, 3-phase generator supplies a transfer switch through a single set of copper conductors, 75 C terminations. What is the minimum conductor size per 445.13(A)?","o":["250 kcmil","350 kcmil","300 kcmil","4/0 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"kVA = 150/0.8 = 187.5 kVA, I = 187,500/(1.732 x 480) = 225.5 A. 445.13(A): 115% of nameplate = 259.4 A, which needs 300 kcmil (285 A). 250 kcmil results from 100% (225.5 A); 4/0 and 350 kcmil are not supported.","r":"NEC 2023 445.13(A), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: HVAC, generators and standby sizing (Art. 440, 445, 700-702)","d":3,"q":"A standby generator will carry 90 kVA of running load when a 50-hp, 460-V motor (Table 430.250 FLC 65 A, locked-rotor current 6 x FLC, across the line) starts. The generator can momentarily supply starting kVA up to 250% of its rating while carrying the other load. What is the smallest standard generator (100, 125, 150, 175, 200, 225, 250, 300 kVA)?","o":["150 kVA","125 kVA","225 kVA","175 kVA"],"a":3,"e":"Locked-rotor kVA = 1.732 x 460 x 390 / 1000 = 310.7 kVA. Starting check: (90 + 310.7) / 2.5 = 160.3 kVA. Running check: 90 + 51.8 (motor) = 141.8 kVA. The larger requirement is 160.3 kVA, so 175 kVA. 150 kVA sizes only on running load, 125 kVA on the motor start alone, and 225 kVA over-sizes using a 2.0 starting factor.","r":"NEC 2023 430.7, 445.13, 702.4(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: HVAC, generators and standby sizing (Art. 440, 445, 700-702)","d":3,"q":"A 480-V, 3-phase standby generator must carry three motors (Table 430.250 FLC 52, 34 and 21 A) and a 54-kW resistance heating load (power factor 1.0). Size the generator on 125% of the largest motor plus the other motors and 100% of the heater. Which standard size (100, 125, 150, 175, 200, 225, 250 kVA) is the smallest that works?","o":["175 kVA","150 kVA","200 kVA","125 kVA"],"a":0,"e":"Motor current = 52 x 1.25 + 34 + 21 = 120 A, which is 1.732 x 480 x 120 / 1000 = 99.8 kVA. Adding the 54-kW heater gives 153.8 kVA, so 175 kVA. Without the 125% (143.0 kVA) you would choose 150 kVA; 200 kVA is oversized and 125 kVA is below the requirement.","r":"NEC 2023 430.24, 445.13, 702.4(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: HVAC, generators and standby sizing (Art. 440, 445, 700-702)","d":3,"q":"A legally required standby system under Article 701 serves smoke-control fans through an automatic transfer switch. Within what maximum time must power be available after normal-source failure?","o":["10 seconds","30 seconds","60 seconds","5 minutes"],"a":2,"e":"Article 701.12 requires legally required standby power to be available within the time required for the application, not to exceed 60 seconds. The 10-second limit is for Article 700 emergency systems, and optional standby (Article 702) has no time limit. 30 seconds and 5 minutes are not NEC limits.","r":"NEC 2023 701.12, 700.12, 702.4","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: HVAC, generators and standby sizing (Art. 440, 445, 700-702)","d":3,"q":"Under Article 700, emergency circuit wiring must be kept entirely independent of other wiring. Which of the following is an allowed exception for sharing an enclosure with normal-source conductors?","o":["In a common raceway with normal lighting if all conductors are 600-V rated","In any junction box if emergency conductors are identified with orange tape","In a common feeder raceway to a panel if the raceway is derated by 20%","Within transfer equipment enclosures"],"a":3,"e":"700.10(B) lists the limited exceptions: transfer equipment enclosures, luminaires or junction boxes fed from two sources, and emergency circuits from the same source sharing a raceway. Insulation voltage rating, identification, or derating do not permit mixing emergency and normal circuits in a raceway.","r":"NEC 2023 700.10(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: HVAC, generators and standby sizing (Art. 440, 445, 700-702)","d":3,"q":"A 240-V rooftop unit has two hermetic compressors (RLC 18 A and 12 A) and a 2.4-A fan motor. Copper, 75 C terminations, no derating. What is the minimum branch-circuit conductor size per 440.33?","o":["8 AWG","10 AWG","6 AWG","12 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"125% of the largest + the others = 18 x 1.25 + 12 + 2.4 = 36.9 A, which needs 8 AWG (50 A). 10 AWG results from no 125% (32.4 A), 6 AWG would be oversized, and 12 AWG (25 A) is far too small.","r":"NEC 2023 440.33, Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: HVAC, generators and standby sizing (Art. 440, 445, 700-702)","d":3,"q":"For the same rooftop unit (compressors 18 A and 12 A RLC, fan 2.4 A), what is the largest standard branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault device under 440.22(B), without using the 225% exception?","o":["50 A","45 A","40 A","60 A"],"a":1,"e":"18 x 1.75 + 12 + 2.4 = 45.9 A, so the largest standard size not exceeding it is 45 A. 50 A is above the limit; 40 A is 225% of the largest compressor alone (the other loads ignored); 60 A applies 175% to all loads (56.7 A) and rounds up.","r":"NEC 2023 440.22(A), 440.22(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: HVAC, generators and standby sizing (Art. 440, 445, 700-702)","d":3,"q":"A 20-kW, 240-V, single-phase generator (power factor 1.0) feeds a transfer switch with copper conductors, 75 C terminations. What is the minimum conductor size per 445.13(A)?","o":["4 AWG","2 AWG","1 AWG","3 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Nameplate current = 20,000/240 = 83.3 A. 445.13(A) requires 115%: 95.8 A, so 3 AWG (100 A). 4 AWG (85 A) results from 100% of nameplate; 2 AWG from 125% (104.2 A); 1 AWG is oversized.","r":"NEC 2023 445.13(A), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: HVAC, generators and standby sizing (Art. 440, 445, 700-702)","d":3,"q":"A 250-kVA, 0.8 pf, 480-V, 3-phase optional standby generator serves a building with a calculated connected load of 380 A. Automatic load management under 702.4(B) is used. How many amperes of load must be shed or limited to keep the generator at its rated output current?","o":["79.3 A","139.4 A","118.5 A","None; the generator output exceeds the load"],"a":0,"e":"Rated output = 250,000/(1.732 x 480) = 300.7 A. Shed = 380 - 300.7 = 79.3 A. 139.4 A wrongly converts to kW first; 118.5 A wrongly adds a 115% allowance; the last answer applies the single-phase formula (520.8 A) and wrongly concludes no shedding is needed.","r":"NEC 2023 702.4(B)(2), 445.13","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: HVAC, generators and standby sizing (Art. 440, 445, 700-702)","d":3,"q":"An emergency panel on a 208-V, 3-phase system has a calculated load of 90 kW at 0.9 power factor. Automatic transfer switches are available in 100, 150, 260, 400 and 600 A ratings. What is the smallest ATS rating that carries the load?","o":["260 A","400 A","600 A","150 A"],"a":1,"e":"kVA = 90/0.9 = 100 kVA, I = 100,000/(1.732 x 208) = 277.6 A. The 260-A switch is below 277.6 A, so 400 A is the smallest adequate rating. 260 A results from using kW instead of kVA (249.8 A); 600 A and 150 A are oversized and undersized.","r":"NEC 2023 700.4, 700.5, 230.82","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conductor ampacity, derating and parallel runs","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 4-wire feeder load of 95 A (noncontinuous) is run in a single raceway with 8 current-carrying copper THHN conductors in total (ampacity adjustment applies). The ambient temperature is 43 C (109 F) and all terminations are rated 75 C. Using the 90 C column of Table 310.16 for ampacity adjustment and correction, what is the smallest copper conductor?","o":["2/0 AWG","1 AWG","1/0 AWG","3 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"8 conductors give a 70% adjustment (310.15(C)(1)) and 43 C ambient gives 0.87 for 90 C insulation (310.15(B)(1)). Required 90 C table ampacity = 95 / (0.87 x 0.70) = 156.0 A, so 1/0 AWG (170 A) is chosen; adjusted ampacity = 103.5 A. The 75 C termination limit (150 A) also exceeds 95 A (110.14(C)).","r":"NEC 2023 310.15(B)(1), 310.15(C)(1), Table 310.16, 110.14(C)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conductor ampacity, derating and parallel runs","d":3,"q":"A copper feeder (THHN, 75 C terminations, 30 C ambient, 3 current-carrying conductors per raceway) must carry 240 A of continuous load plus 160 A of noncontinuous load. The feeder will be installed as parallel sets in separate raceways. Which is the smallest parallel installation that complies?","o":["2 sets of 4/0 AWG","2 sets of 3/0 AWG","2 sets of 250 kcmil","3 sets of 1/0 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"Minimum ampacity = 1.25 x 240 + 160 = 460 A (215.2(A)(1)); per set of two = 230 A. 4/0 AWG is rated 230 A at 75 C, giving 2 x 230 = 460 A. Using 400 A without 125% selects 3/0 AWG; 125% on everything selects 250 kcmil; 3 sets of 1/0 give only 3 x 150 = 450 A, and parallel conductors must be 1/0 or larger (310.10(H)).","r":"NEC 2023 215.2(A)(1), 310.10(H), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conductor ampacity, derating and parallel runs","d":3,"q":"A feeder supplies three 3-phase, 460 V induction motors with full-load currents (Table 430.250) of 52 A, 27 A and 14 A. The copper THHN conductors are 3 current-carrying conductors in conduit in a 52 C (126 F) ambient. Terminations are rated 75 C. What is the smallest copper conductor size?","o":["2 AWG","1 AWG","1/0 AWG","2/0 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Feeder ampacity (430.24) = 1.25 x 52 + 27 + 14 = 106 A. Ambient 52 C gives 0.76 for 90 C insulation, so 90 C table ampacity needed = 106/0.76 = 139.5 A; 1 AWG (145 A) works and its 75 C rating (130 A) covers 106 A. Skipping ambient correction gives 2 AWG; 125% on all motors gives 1/0 AWG; using the 75 C factor (0.67) on the 75 C column gives 2/0 AWG.","r":"NEC 2023 430.24, 310.15(B)(1), Table 310.16, 110.14(C)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conductor ampacity, derating and parallel runs","d":3,"q":"A 90 A noncontinuous load is served by copper THHN conductors in a raceway with 6 current-carrying conductors at 30 C ambient. The equipment terminals are marked 60 C (not listed for higher temperature). The conductors may be selected from the 90 C column for adjustment. What is the smallest copper conductor?","o":["3 AWG","2 AWG","4 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Adjustment (80%) on the 90 C column: 90/0.80 = 112.5 A gives 3 AWG (115 A). But the 60 C terminals limit the conductor to its 60 C ampacity (110.14(C)(1)), and 3 AWG is only 85 A at 60 C, below 90 A; 2 AWG is 95 A at 60 C. Adjusting the 60 C column instead gives 1/0 AWG; ignoring adjustment gives 4 AWG.","r":"NEC 2023 110.14(C)(1), 310.15(C)(1), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conductor ampacity, derating and parallel runs","d":3,"q":"A 600 A feeder overcurrent device protects a feeder. A 25 ft (horizontal) tap is run from the feeder to a 175 A fused disconnect that is the only overcurrent device on the tap, and the tap conductors are protected from physical damage in a raceway. The load on the tap is 160 A noncontinuous. Using the 75 C column, what is the smallest copper tap conductor under the 25 ft feeder tap rule?","o":["2/0 AWG","1/0 AWG","250 kcmil","3/0 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"240.21(B)(2) requires tap ampacity of at least one-third of the feeder device rating: 600/3 = 200 A, which exceeds the 175 A device and 160 A load. 3/0 AWG is 200 A at 75 C. Sizing only for the 175 A device gives 2/0 AWG, using one-fourth gives 1/0 AWG, and adding 125% gives 250 kcmil.","r":"NEC 2023 240.21(B)(2), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conductor ampacity, derating and parallel runs","d":3,"q":"Copper 3/0 AWG THHN conductors are in a conduit with 6 current-carrying conductors in a 36 C (97 F) ambient. All terminations are 75 C. The circuit is a feeder to a panelboard (not a multioutlet receptacle branch circuit for cord-and-plug loads), and the load is 150 A. What is the maximum standard overcurrent device rating permitted to protect the 3/0 conductors?","o":["175 A","150 A","200 A","225 A"],"a":0,"e":"Corrected and adjusted ampacity = 225 x 0.91 x 0.80 = 163.8 A (90 C column; termination limit is 200 A). That is not a standard device rating, so 240.4(B) allows the next higher standard size, 175 A, when 800 A or less. Using the 75 C ampacity (200 A) or the unadjusted 90 C value (225 A) overstates protection; 150 A is the next lower size and is permitted but is not the maximum.","r":"NEC 2023 240.4(B), 310.15(B)(1), 310.15(C)(1), Table 310.16, 240.6(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conductor ampacity, derating and parallel runs","d":3,"q":"Three multiwire branch circuits (3-phase, 4-wire, 277/480 V) serving electric-discharge lighting share one conduit with copper THHN, giving 12 current-carrying conductors (the three neutrals count). Each phase conductor carries 17 A of continuous load. Terminations are 75 C and ambient is 30 C. What is the smallest copper conductor size?","o":["10 AWG","12 AWG","8 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Continuous load requires 17 x 1.25 = 21.25 A. With 12 current-carrying conductors the adjustment is 50% (10-20 conductors), so 90 C ampacity needed = 21.25/0.50 = 42.5 A, giving 8 AWG (55 A). Not counting the neutrals (9 conductors, 70%) gives 10 AWG, no adjustment gives 12 AWG (the practical minimum under 240.4(D)), and adjusting the 60 C column gives 6 AWG.","r":"NEC 2023 310.15(C)(1), 310.15(E)(3), 210.19(A)(1), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conductor ampacity, derating and parallel runs","d":3,"q":"A copper feeder with an 800 A overcurrent device carries a 750 A noncontinuous load. It is installed as 3 parallel sets, each in its own conduit with 3 current-carrying conductors. Ambient is 47 C (117 F), conductors are THHN, and terminations are 75 C. What is the smallest conductor in each set?","o":["250 kcmil","400 kcmil","600 kcmil","300 kcmil"],"a":3,"e":"Current per set = 750/3 = 250 A. At 47 C the 90 C factor is 0.82, so 90 C ampacity needed = 250/0.82 = 304.9 A; 300 kcmil (320 A, 262.4 A corrected) and its 75 C rating is 285 A. Ignoring ambient gives 250 kcmil; applying the 75 C factor (0.75) to the 75 C column gives 400 kcmil; bundling all 9 conductors (70%) gives 600 kcmil.","r":"NEC 2023 310.10(H), 310.15(B)(1), 310.15(C)(1), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conductor ampacity, derating and parallel runs","d":3,"q":"A feeder with 3 current-carrying copper THHN conductors serves 100 A of continuous load and 40 A of noncontinuous load. The conductors run through a mechanical room with an ambient temperature of 118 F, and terminations are rated 75 C. What is the smallest copper conductor?","o":["2/0 AWG","3/0 AWG","4/0 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Minimum ampacity = 1.25 x 100 + 40 = 165 A. 118 F = 47.8 C, in the 46-50 C row, so the 90 C factor is 0.82: 165/0.82 = 201.2 A, which selects 3/0 AWG (225 A); its 75 C rating (200 A) covers 165 A. No ambient correction gives 2/0 AWG; using the 75 C factor of 0.75 on the 75 C column gives 4/0 AWG; multiplying instead of dividing gives 1/0 AWG.","r":"NEC 2023 310.15(B)(1), Table 310.16, 215.2(A)(1)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conductor ampacity, derating and parallel runs","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 4-wire, 208Y/120 V feeder has a maximum neutral (unbalanced) load of 340 A, of which 80 A is electric-discharge lighting. Using 220.61 and the 75 C column, what is the smallest copper neutral?","o":["350 kcmil","250 kcmil","400 kcmil","500 kcmil"],"a":2,"e":"The discharge lighting portion gets no demand reduction (220.61(C)). Remaining 260 A: first 200 A at 100% plus 60 A x 70% = 242 A; adding 80 A gives 322 A. 400 kcmil is 335 A at 75 C. Reducing the full 340 A gives 298 A (350 kcmil); taking 70% of everything gives 238 A (250 kcmil); no reduction gives 500 kcmil.","r":"NEC 2023 220.61(B), 220.61(C), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conductor ampacity, derating and parallel runs","d":3,"q":"Copper 2 AWG THHN conductors (6 current-carrying conductors in total) are installed in a conduit in a 52 C ambient. Terminals are rated 75 C. What is the maximum continuous load current the circuit may carry?","o":["63.2 A","79.0 A","83.2 A","49.3 A"],"a":0,"e":"Adjusted ampacity = 130 x 0.76 (52 C, 90 C column) x 0.80 (6 conductors) = 79.0 A, below the 75 C terminal rating of 115 A. A continuous load may not exceed 80% of that: 79.0/1.25 = 63.2 A. Quoting 79.0 A ignores the 125% rule; omitting the ambient correction gives 83.2 A; the 75 C column with 0.67 gives 49.3 A.","r":"NEC 2023 310.15(B)(1), 310.15(C)(1), 210.19(A)(1), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conductor ampacity, derating and parallel runs","d":3,"q":"Which of the following 3-phase, 4-wire, copper feeder installations using 2 parallel sets complies with 310.10(H) and 300.3(B)?","o":["Two sets of 500 kcmil, each 120 ft long with identical insulation and terminations, each set in its own steel conduit containing one conductor of each phase, the neutral and an equipment grounding conductor","Two sets of 500 kcmil, with one set 120 ft long and the other 126 ft long to ease routing, each in its own conduit","Three sets of 4 AWG in three conduits for a 200 A feeder to reduce voltage drop","Two sets of 500 kcmil with both phase A conductors in conduit 1 and both phase B conductors in conduit 2, the phase C conductors and neutrals in conduit 3"],"a":0,"e":"310.10(H) requires parallel conductors of 1/0 AWG or larger, with the same length, material, circular-mil area, insulation type and termination method. 300.3(B) requires each parallel set to be in its own raceway with all phase conductors, the neutral and the EGC grouped together so induced currents balance. The other choices violate length equality, the 1/0 minimum, or the grouping requirement.","r":"NEC 2023 310.10(H), 300.3(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conductor ampacity, derating and parallel runs","d":3,"q":"A single 75 hp, 460 V, 3-phase induction motor has a Table 430.250 full-load current of 96 A. The branch-circuit conductors are 3 current-carrying copper THHN conductors in conduit in a 48 C (118 F) ambient with 75 C terminations. What is the smallest copper conductor?","o":["1 AWG","2/0 AWG","1/0 AWG","2 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"430.22 requires 125% x 96 = 120 A. At 48 C the 90 C factor is 0.82, so 120/0.82 = 146.3 A and 1/0 AWG (170 A). Ignoring ambient gives 1 AWG; using 0.75 on the 75 C column gives 2/0 AWG; omitting the 125% gives 2 AWG.","r":"NEC 2023 430.22, 310.15(B)(1), Table 430.250, Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conductor ampacity, derating and parallel runs","d":3,"q":"Twelve current-carrying copper 12 AWG THHN conductors (six 2-wire 20 A noncontinuous circuits) run through an 18 in. conduit nipple between two enclosures at 30 C ambient. Each circuit is protected at 20 A and carries 20 A. What is the smallest conductor permitted?","o":["14 AWG","12 AWG","10 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"The ampacity adjustment of 310.15(C)(1) does not apply to conductors in a nipple 24 in. or less in length, so no derating is required. 12 AWG (25 A at 75 C, 30 A at 90 C) carries 20 A and is permitted with 20 A protection under 240.4(D). 14 AWG is limited to 15 A protection. 10 AWG and 8 AWG come from wrongly applying a 50% adjustment for 12 conductors to a nipple.","r":"NEC 2023 310.15(C)(1) Exception, 240.4(D), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Voltage drop and conductor selection","d":3,"q":"A 240 V, single-phase, 2-wire load draws 80 A (noncontinuous) at a point 250 ft from the source. The maximum permitted voltage drop is 3% and copper conductors are used with K = 12.9. The 75 C ampacity column applies. What is the smallest copper conductor that meets the voltage-drop limit?","o":["4 AWG","1 AWG","2 AWG","2/0 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Allowed drop = 0.03 x 240 = 7.2 V. CM = 2 K I L / VD = 2 x 12.9 x 80 x 250 / 7.2 = 71,667 CM. Ch. 9 Table 8 gives 1 AWG = 83,690 CM (the next smaller 2 AWG = 66,360 CM is too small). The ampacity-only choice is 4 AWG; a 2% limit would select 2/0 AWG.","r":"NEC 2023 210.19(A) Informational Note 4, Chapter 9 Table 8","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Voltage drop and conductor selection","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 480 V, 3-wire feeder supplies a 120 A continuous load located 400 ft away. Design for a maximum 2% voltage drop with copper conductors (K = 12.9) and 75 C terminations. What is the smallest copper conductor meeting both ampacity and the voltage-drop limit?","o":["1/0 AWG","1 AWG","3/0 AWG","2/0 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Ampacity: 1.25 x 120 = 150 A, satisfied by 1/0 AWG (150 A). Voltage drop: allowed = 0.02 x 480 = 9.6 V; CM = 1.732 x 12.9 x 120 x 400 / 9.6 = 111,714 CM, which needs 2/0 AWG (133,100 CM). A 3% limit gives 1 AWG; applying 125% to the voltage-drop current gives 3/0 AWG.","r":"NEC 2023 215.2(A)(1), 215.2(A)(2) Informational Note, Chapter 9 Table 8","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Voltage drop and conductor selection","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 208 V feeder uses 4/0 AWG copper (211,600 CM, K = 12.9) to carry 180 A for 300 ft one way. What is the approximate voltage drop at full load?","o":["5.70 V (2.74%)","6.58 V (3.17%)","5.70 V (4.75%)","2.85 V (1.37%)"],"a":0,"e":"For 3-phase, VD = 1.732 K I L / CM = 1.732 x 12.9 x 180 x 300 / 211,600 = 5.70 V, and 5.70/208 = 2.74%. Using the single-phase factor of 2 gives 6.58 V; dividing the line-to-line drop by 120 V is a base error; halving the result forgets the full run length.","r":"Chapter 9 Table 8, 215.2(A)(2) Informational Note, 210.19(A) Informational Note 4","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Voltage drop and conductor selection","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 208Y/120 V feeder of 3/0 AWG copper (167,800 CM) carries 160 A for 120 ft to a panelboard. From that panelboard, a 120 V, 2-wire branch circuit of 12 AWG copper (6,530 CM) carries 12 A for 90 ft. Using K = 12.9 and full-load currents, which statement is correct regarding the recommended limits of 3% for the feeder or branch circuit and 5% total?","o":["The total drop is 4.78%; both the 3% and 5% recommendations are met","The total drop is 3.28%; both the 3% and 5% recommendations are met","The total drop is 4.78%; the branch circuit exceeds 3% but the total is within 5%","The total drop is 4.78%; because the branch exceeds 3%, the total recommendation of 5% is also not met"],"a":2,"e":"Feeder VD = 1.732 x 12.9 x 160 x 120 / 167,800 = 2.56 V = 1.23% of 208 V. Branch VD = 2 x 12.9 x 12 x 90 / 6,530 = 4.27 V = 3.56% of 120 V. The total is 1.23 + 3.56 = 4.78%, within 5%, but the branch exceeds the 3% recommendation. Adding volts and dividing by 208 V gives 3.28%, which misstates the branch percentage.","r":"NEC 2023 210.19(A) Informational Note 4, 215.2(A)(2) Informational Note","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Voltage drop and conductor selection","d":3,"q":"A 240 V, single-phase, 2-wire load of 60 A is 180 ft from the panel using 4 AWG copper. Using the Chapter 9 Table 8 direct-current resistance of 0.308 ohms per 1000 ft for 4 AWG uncoated copper at 75 C, what is the percent voltage drop?","o":["1.39%","5.54%","2.40%","2.77%"],"a":3,"e":"VD = 2 x R x I x L / 1000 = 2 x 0.308 x 60 x 180 / 1000 = 6.65 V; 6.65/240 = 2.77%. Using one conductor length only halves the answer (1.39%); dividing by 120 V gives 5.54%; using the 3-phase multiplier (1.732) instead of 2 gives 2.40%.","r":"Chapter 9 Table 8, 210.19(A) Informational Note 4","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Voltage drop and conductor selection","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 208 V, 3-wire feeder supplies a 140 A continuous load 120 ft away. Voltage drop must not exceed 3%. Use copper, K = 12.9, and 75 C terminations. What is the smallest copper conductor?","o":["2 AWG","2/0 AWG","1/0 AWG","1 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Ampacity: 1.25 x 140 = 175 A needs 2/0 AWG (175 A). Voltage drop: allowed 0.03 x 208 = 6.24 V, CM = 1.732 x 12.9 x 140 x 120 / 6.24 = 60,154 CM, which only needs 2 AWG (66,360 CM). Ampacity governs, so 2/0 AWG. Choosing by voltage drop alone gives 2 AWG, by 100% of the load gives 1/0 AWG, and applying 125% to the voltage-drop current gives 1 AWG.","r":"NEC 2023 215.2(A)(1), 210.19(A) Informational Note 4, Chapter 9 Table 8","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Voltage drop and conductor selection","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 480 V, 3-wire feeder carries 500 A (noncontinuous) over 600 ft with copper conductors (K = 12.9) in 2 parallel sets. Voltage drop is limited to 2% and ampacity uses the 75 C column. Which is the smallest compliant installation?","o":["2 sets of 250 kcmil","2 sets of 300 kcmil","2 sets of 350 kcmil","2 sets of 500 kcmil"],"a":2,"e":"Allowed drop = 0.02 x 480 = 9.6 V. Total CM = 1.732 x 12.9 x 500 x 600 / 9.6 = 698,212 CM, or 349,106 CM per set, which requires 350 kcmil (350,000 CM). Ampacity alone (250 A per set) would permit 250 kcmil, and 300 kcmil (300,000 CM) is below the CM requirement. 500 kcmil is larger than needed.","r":"Chapter 9 Table 8, 310.10(H), 215.2(A)(2) Informational Note","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Voltage drop and conductor selection","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 208 V feeder of 4/0 AWG copper in steel conduit supplies 120 A at 0.85 lagging power factor for 350 ft. Assume R = 0.063 ohm and X = 0.048 ohm per 1000 ft for this conductor and raceway. Using the effective impedance Ze = R cos(theta) + X sin(theta), what is the percent voltage drop?","o":["2.76%","2.20%","3.88%","2.13%"],"a":0,"e":"sin(theta) = sqrt(1 - 0.85^2) = 0.5268. Ze = 0.063 x 0.85 + 0.048 x 0.5268 = 0.07884 ohm/1000 ft. VD = 1.732 x 120 x 0.07884 x 350/1000 = 5.73 V = 2.76% of 208 V. Using resistance only gives 2.20%, adding R and X arithmetically gives 3.88%, and the K-factor (unity-PF DC) method gives 2.13%.","r":"Chapter 9 Table 9 and its Notes, 215.2(A)(2) Informational Note","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Voltage drop and conductor selection","d":3,"q":"A 240 V, single-phase, 2-wire load of 100 A is 200 ft from the panel. The conductors are aluminum (K = 21.2) and voltage drop is limited to 3%. Using Chapter 9 Table 8 circular mils, what is the smallest aluminum conductor that meets the voltage-drop limit? (Ampacity is satisfied by any size considered.)","o":["2/0 AWG aluminum","1 AWG aluminum","1/0 AWG aluminum","3/0 AWG aluminum"],"a":0,"e":"Allowed drop = 0.03 x 240 = 7.2 V. CM = 2 x 21.2 x 100 x 200 / 7.2 = 117,778 CM, so 2/0 AWG (133,100 CM). 1 AWG (83,690 CM) is what the copper K of 12.9 would produce; 1/0 (105,600 CM) is too small; using 208 V as the base overstates the need.","r":"Chapter 9 Table 8, 210.19(A) Informational Note 4","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Voltage drop and conductor selection","d":3,"q":"A 120 V, single-phase, 2-wire branch circuit on a 20 A breaker serves a 16 A load 130 ft from the panel. Using copper (K = 12.9) and a 3% voltage-drop limit, what is the smallest copper conductor?","o":["12 AWG","10 AWG","8 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Allowed drop = 0.03 x 120 = 3.6 V. CM = 2 x 12.9 x 16 x 130 / 3.6 = 14,907 CM. 10 AWG is 10,380 CM (too small); 8 AWG is 16,510 CM. 12 AWG satisfies ampacity for a 20 A breaker only, and a 2% limit would need 6 AWG.","r":"NEC 2023 210.19(A) Informational Note 4, Chapter 9 Table 8","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Voltage drop and conductor selection","d":3,"q":"A 120 V, single-phase, 2-wire circuit uses 12 AWG copper (6,530 CM, K = 12.9) to carry a 16 A load. What is the maximum one-way length that keeps the voltage drop at or below 3%?","o":["113 ft","56 ft","37 ft","75 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Allowed drop = 0.03 x 120 = 3.6 V. L = VD x CM / (2 K I) = 3.6 x 6,530 / (2 x 12.9 x 16) = 56.9 ft, so 56 ft. 113 ft forgets the return conductor; 37 ft corresponds to a 2% limit; 75 ft corresponds to 4%.","r":"Chapter 9 Table 8, 210.19(A) Informational Note 4","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Voltage drop and conductor selection","d":3,"q":"A 75 hp, 460 V, 3-phase motor (Table 430.250 FLC 96 A) is 350 ft from its starter. Use copper (K = 12.9), 75 C terminations, and limit voltage drop at full-load current to 3%. What is the smallest copper conductor for the branch circuit?","o":["3 AWG","2 AWG","1/0 AWG","1 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Ampacity: 1.25 x 96 = 120 A needs 1 AWG (130 A). Voltage drop: allowed 0.03 x 460 = 13.8 V, CM = 1.732 x 12.9 x 96 x 350 / 13.8 = 54,400 CM, which needs 2 AWG (66,360 CM). The larger requirement governs: 1 AWG. 3 AWG fails the CM test, and 2 AWG fails the 125% ampacity test.","r":"NEC 2023 430.22, Table 430.250, Chapter 9 Table 8, 210.19(A) Informational Note 4","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Voltage drop and conductor selection","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 480 V feeder of 2/0 AWG copper (133,100 CM, K = 12.9) carries 150 A. What is the maximum one-way length for a 3% voltage drop?","o":["495 ft","330 ft","571 ft","857 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Allowed drop = 0.03 x 480 = 14.4 V. L = VD x CM / (1.732 x K x I) = 14.4 x 133,100 / (1.732 x 12.9 x 150) = 571.9 ft, so 571 ft. Using the single-phase factor of 2 gives 495 ft; using 277 V line-to-neutral as the base gives 330 ft; 857 ft is a 4.5% limit.","r":"Chapter 9 Table 8, 215.2(A)(2) Informational Note","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Voltage drop and conductor selection","d":3,"q":"A 240 V, single-phase load draws 60 A through 4 AWG copper (41,740 CM, K = 12.9) located 150 ft from a source that holds a steady 240 V. What is the approximate voltage at the load?","o":["234.4 V","237.2 V","235.2 V","228.9 V"],"a":0,"e":"VD = 2 K I L / CM = 2 x 12.9 x 60 x 150 / 41,740 = 5.56 V, so the load voltage is 240 - 5.56 = 234.4 V. A one-way length (half the drop) gives 237.2 V, the 3-phase factor gives 235.2 V, and doubling the drop gives 228.9 V.","r":"Chapter 9 Table 8, 210.19(A) Informational Note 4","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Voltage drop and conductor selection","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 4-wire, 208Y/120 V feeder serves 160 A of continuous load and 40 A of noncontinuous load, 260 ft from the source. Limit the voltage drop to 2% at the 200 A total load. Use copper (K = 12.9) and 75 C terminations. What is the smallest copper conductor?","o":["250 kcmil","350 kcmil","4/0 AWG","300 kcmil"],"a":3,"e":"Ampacity: 1.25 x 160 + 40 = 240 A, so 250 kcmil (255 A). Voltage drop: allowed 0.02 x 208 = 4.16 V, CM = 1.732 x 12.9 x 200 x 260 / 4.16 = 279,285 CM, requiring 300 kcmil. Voltage drop governs: 300 kcmil. Using 240 A as the voltage-drop current gives 350 kcmil; a 3% limit gives 4/0 AWG.","r":"NEC 2023 215.2(A)(1), 215.2(A)(2) Informational Note, Chapter 9 Table 8","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conduit, wireway and box fill (Ch. 9, Art. 314, 376)","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 4-wire feeder is installed in EMT using copper THHN: three 3/0 AWG phase conductors, one 250 kcmil neutral and one 1 AWG equipment grounding conductor. Using Chapter 9 Table 5 conductor areas and Table 4 for EMT, what is the smallest trade size of EMT?","o":["2 in. EMT","1-1/2 in. EMT","3 in. EMT","2-1/2 in. EMT"],"a":3,"e":"Table 5 areas: 3 x 0.2679 + 0.397 + 0.1562 = 1.3569 sq in. With more than 2 wires the fill limit is 40% (Table 1), and the EGC is counted. EMT 2-1/2 in. has 40% area 2.343 sq in. and the next smaller size does not hold 1.3569. Leaving out the EGC (1.2007 sq in.) gives 2 in.; counting only the three phases (0.8037 sq in.) gives 1-1/2 in.; 3 in. complies but is not the smallest.","r":"Chapter 9 Table 1, Table 4, Table 5, Note 3 to Chapter 9 tables","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conduit, wireway and box fill (Ch. 9, Art. 314, 376)","d":3,"q":"A 3/4 in. EMT already contains six 10 AWG THHN conductors. Using Chapter 9 Table 1, Table 4 and Table 5, what is the maximum number of 12 AWG THHN conductors that can be added?","o":["7","6","10","11"],"a":1,"e":"3/4 in. EMT at 40% fill = 0.213 sq in. Six 10 AWG THHN use 6 x 0.0211 = 0.1266 sq in., leaving 0.0864 sq in. Each 12 AWG THHN is 0.0133 sq in., so 0.0864/0.0133 = 6.50, which rounds down to 6 conductors. Rounding up gives 7; subtracting six from the all-12 AWG count (16) gives 10; using 53% fill gives 11.","r":"Chapter 9 Table 1, Table 4, Table 5","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conduit, wireway and box fill (Ch. 9, Art. 314, 376)","d":3,"q":"A 24 in. long EMT nipple connects two enclosures and contains six 350 kcmil copper THHN conductors. Using Chapter 9 Table 4 (total internal area), Table 5 and Note 4 to Chapter 9 tables, what is the smallest trade size of EMT nipple permitted?","o":["3 in. EMT","2 in. EMT","2-1/2 in. EMT","3-1/2 in. EMT"],"a":2,"e":"Six 350 kcmil THHN = 6 x 0.5242 = 3.1452 sq in. Note 4 permits 60% fill in nipples not exceeding 24 in.; 60% of the total internal area is 3.515 sq in. for 2-1/2 in. EMT, which holds 3.1452. The next smaller size (2 in., 60% = 2.014 sq in.) is too small. Using the 40% column gives 3 in. EMT, which is larger than necessary.","r":"Chapter 9 Note 4, Table 4, Table 5","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conduit, wireway and box fill (Ch. 9, Art. 314, 376)","d":3,"q":"A single EMT run contains two 10 AWG, two 12 AWG and three 8 AWG THHN circuit conductors plus one 8 AWG THHN equipment grounding conductor. Using Chapter 9 Table 5 and Table 4, what is the smallest EMT trade size?","o":["1 in. EMT","3/4 in. EMT","1-1/4 in. EMT","1/2 in. EMT"],"a":0,"e":"Areas: 2 x 0.0211 + 2 x 0.0133 + 4 x 0.0366 (the EGC counts) = 0.2152 sq in. 3/4 in. EMT at 40% allows only 0.213 sq in., so it fails; 1 in. allows 0.346 sq in. Leaving the EGC out gives 0.1786 sq in., which would fit 3/4 in. 1-1/4 in. is oversized and 1/2 in. (0.122 sq in.) is far too small.","r":"Chapter 9 Table 1, Table 4, Table 5, Note 3","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conduit, wireway and box fill (Ch. 9, Art. 314, 376)","d":3,"q":"A metal outlet box contains: six 12 AWG and two 10 AWG insulated circuit conductors, all originating or ending in the box; two equipment grounding conductors (one 10 AWG, one 12 AWG); internal cable clamps; and one duplex receptacle wired with 12 AWG conductors. Using Table 314.16(B) volume allowances (12 AWG = 2.25 cu in., 10 AWG = 2.50 cu in.), what is the minimum required box volume?","o":["23.00 cu in.","25.75 cu in.","28.00 cu in.","30.25 cu in."],"a":2,"e":"Conductors: 6 x 2.25 + 2 x 2.50 = 18.50. All EGCs together: one allowance of the largest, 2.50. Internal clamps: one allowance of the largest conductor present, 2.50. Receptacle: two allowances of the largest conductor connected to it, 2 x 2.25 = 4.50. Total = 28.00 cu in. Omitting EGCs and clamps gives 23.00; counting the device once gives 25.75; counting both EGCs separately gives 30.25.","r":"NEC 2023 314.16(B), Table 314.16(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conduit, wireway and box fill (Ch. 9, Art. 314, 376)","d":3,"q":"A 3 x 2 x 3-1/2 in. device box (24.0 cu in., Table 314.16(A)) contains two 12-2 w/ground NM cables with internal clamps (4 insulated conductors and 2 EGCs) and one duplex receptacle wired with 12 AWG. How many additional 12 AWG insulated conductors, each requiring a 2.25 cu in. allowance, can be added without exceeding the box volume?","o":["0","2","3","6"],"a":1,"e":"Fill = 4 conductors + 1 (all EGCs, largest) + 1 (clamps) + 2 (receptacle yoke) = 8 allowances x 2.25 = 18.00 cu in. Remaining = 24.0 - 18.00 = 6.00 cu in., which is 2.67 allowances, so 2 more conductors. Counting each EGC and clamp separately leaves 0; rounding up gives 3; leaving out the clamp, EGC and device allowances gives 6.","r":"NEC 2023 314.16(A), 314.16(B), Table 314.16(A), Table 314.16(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conduit, wireway and box fill (Ch. 9, Art. 314, 376)","d":3,"q":"A box contains five 14 AWG and six 12 AWG insulated conductors, three equipment grounding conductors (two 14 AWG, one 12 AWG), internal clamps, and two duplex receptacles each wired with 14 AWG conductors. Table 314.16(B) allowances are 2.00 cu in. for 14 AWG and 2.25 cu in. for 12 AWG. What is the minimum required box volume?","o":["33.75 cu in.","31.50 cu in.","38.00 cu in.","36.00 cu in."],"a":3,"e":"14 AWG conductors: 5 x 2.00 = 10.00. 12 AWG conductors: 6 x 2.25 = 13.50. EGCs: one allowance for the largest (12 AWG) = 2.25. Clamps: one allowance for the largest conductor in the box = 2.25. Each receptacle counts twice the largest conductor connected (14 AWG): 2 x 2 x 2.00 = 8.00. Total = 36.00 cu in. Omitting the clamps gives 33.75; omitting both clamps and EGCs gives 31.50; counting each EGC size separately adds 2.00 (38.00).","r":"NEC 2023 314.16(B)(1)-(5), Table 314.16(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conduit, wireway and box fill (Ch. 9, Art. 314, 376)","d":3,"q":"A metal wireway must contain nine 3/0 AWG and six 500 kcmil copper THHN conductors (power conductors, no ampacity adjustment issue). Using Chapter 9 Table 5 and the Article 376 fill limit for conductors at any cross section, what is the smallest standard wireway (inside dimensions, inches)?","o":["4 x 6 in. wireway","4 x 4 in. wireway","6 x 6 in. wireway","8 x 8 in. wireway"],"a":2,"e":"Conductor area = 9 x 0.2679 + 6 x 0.7073 = 6.6549 sq in. 376.22(A) limits fill to 20%. 4 x 6 allows 0.20 x 24 = 4.8, 6 x 6 allows 0.20 x 36 = 7.2 sq in., which holds 6.655. The 4 x 6 size would require about 28% fill and the 4 x 4 about 42%, both above the 20% limit; 8 x 8 is larger than necessary.","r":"NEC 2023 376.22(A), Chapter 9 Table 5","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conduit, wireway and box fill (Ch. 9, Art. 314, 376)","d":3,"q":"A pull box is used for a straight pull of conductors 4 AWG and larger. Conduits entering one side are 4 in., 3 in. and 2 in. (trade size). Conductors leave through conduits in the opposite wall. What is the minimum length of the box (distance between entry and the opposite wall)?","o":["24 in.","29 in.","48 in.","32 in."],"a":3,"e":"314.28(A)(1) requires the length of a box in straight pulls to be not less than 8 times the trade diameter of the largest raceway: 8 x 4 = 32 in. The 6 x 4 = 24 in. value is the angle/U-pull rule, 6 x 4 + 3 + 2 = 29 in. adds the other raceways as an angle pull would, and 48 in. is 6 times the sum of all raceways.","r":"NEC 2023 314.28(A)(1)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conduit, wireway and box fill (Ch. 9, Art. 314, 376)","d":3,"q":"A junction box has an angle pull. Three conduits (4 in., 3 in. and 2 in.) enter one wall in a row, and a single 4 in. conduit leaves through an adjacent wall (conductors 4 AWG and larger). What is the minimum distance from the row of three entries to the opposite wall?","o":["24 in.","29 in.","32 in.","54 in."],"a":1,"e":"314.28(A)(2): the distance from each raceway entry to the opposite wall is at least 6 times the largest raceway plus the sum of the other raceways in the same row on the same wall: 6 x 4 + 3 + 2 = 29 in. The 24 in. value would apply to the single 4 in. exit wall only; 32 in. is the straight-pull rule (8 x 4); 54 in. is 6 times the sum of all three entering raceways.","r":"NEC 2023 314.28(A)(2)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conduit, wireway and box fill (Ch. 9, Art. 314, 376)","d":3,"q":"Two 350 kcmil copper THHN conductors are to be installed in EMT, with no other conductors. Using Chapter 9 Table 1, Table 4 and Table 5, what is the smallest trade size of EMT?","o":["2 in. EMT","3 in. EMT","1-1/2 in. EMT","2-1/2 in. EMT"],"a":3,"e":"Two 350 kcmil THHN = 2 x 0.5242 = 1.0484 sq in. With two wires the limit is 31% (Table 1). 2 in. EMT allows 0.31 x 3.356 = 1.040 sq in., which is just below 1.0484; 2-1/2 in. allows 1.816 sq in. Using the 40% column permits 2 in. (1.342 sq in.), which overfills the conduit; 3 in. is larger than needed; 1-1/2 in. (0.631 sq in.) is far too small.","r":"Chapter 9 Table 1, Table 4, Table 5","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Conduit, wireway and box fill (Ch. 9, Art. 314, 376)","d":3,"q":"A 8 in. x 8 in. metal wireway contains splices and taps. What is the maximum combined cross-sectional area of the conductors, splices and taps at any splice point, compared with the 20% limit that applies elsewhere in the wireway?","o":["48.0 sq in. at the splice point (75%); 12.8 sq in. elsewhere (20%)","25.6 sq in. at the splice point (40%); 12.8 sq in. elsewhere (20%)","38.4 sq in. at the splice point (60%); 12.8 sq in. elsewhere (20%)","48.0 sq in. at the splice point (75%); 25.6 sq in. elsewhere (40%)"],"a":0,"e":"The interior cross-section of an 8 x 8 in. wireway is 64 sq in. 376.22(A) limits conductors at any cross section to 20% (12.8 sq in.), while 376.56(A) permits splices, taps and conductors at a splice point to fill up to 75% (48.0 sq in.). The 40% and 60% values are conduit or other limits that do not apply to wireways.","r":"NEC 2023 376.22(A), 376.56(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Short-circuit current, grounding and bonding calculations","d":3,"q":"A 500 kVA, 3-phase, 480 V transformer has a nameplate impedance of 5.75%. Assuming an infinite primary source, what is the available fault current at the transformer secondary terminals?","o":["10,460 A","18,120 A","3,460 A","601 A"],"a":0,"e":"Secondary full-load current = 500,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 601.4 A. Isc = FLA / %Z = 601.4 / 0.0575 = 10,460 A. Omitting 1.732 gives 18,116 A; multiplying by %Z gives 3,458 A; the full-load current alone is 601 A.","r":"NEC 2023 110.9, 110.24","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Short-circuit current, grounding and bonding calculations","d":3,"q":"A 750 kVA, 480 V, 3-phase transformer with 5.75% impedance (infinite primary) feeds a switchboard through 2 parallel sets of 500 kcmil copper per phase in steel conduit, 150 ft long. Use the point-to-point method with C = 22,185 for 500 kcmil copper in steel conduit. What is the approximate available fault current at the switchboard?","o":["15,690 A","11,350 A","13,170 A","12,690 A"],"a":2,"e":"FLA = 750,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 902.1 A; Isc at the transformer = 902.1 / 0.0575 = 15,689 A. f = 1.732 x L x I / (C x n x V) = 1.732 x 150 x 15,689 / (22,185 x 2 x 480) = 0.1914. M = 1/(1+f) = 0.8394. Isc = 15,689 x 0.8394 = 13,169 A. Using the transformer value ignores conductor impedance; using n = 1 gives 11,346 A; subtracting f (M = 1 - f) gives 12,687 A.","r":"NEC 2023 110.9, 110.24, 240.87","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Short-circuit current, grounding and bonding calculations","d":3,"q":"A 75 kVA, 240 V single-phase transformer with 2.0% impedance (infinite primary) feeds a panel through 60 ft of 3/0 AWG copper in steel conduit (one set). Use the single-phase point-to-point method, f = 2 L I / (C n V), with C = 12,844. What is the approximate available fault current at the panel (line-to-line)?","o":["15,620 A","9,720 A","10,230 A","9,020 A"],"a":1,"e":"Secondary FLA = 75,000 / 240 = 312.5 A; Isc at the terminals = 312.5/0.02 = 15,625 A. f = 2 x 60 x 15,625 / (12,844 x 1 x 240) = 0.6083; M = 0.6218; Isc = 9,715 A. Using the 3-phase multiplier 1.732 gives 10,234 A; the terminal value ignores the cable; using 3-phase FLA for a single-phase unit gives 9,021 A.","r":"NEC 2023 110.9, 110.24","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Short-circuit current, grounding and bonding calculations","d":3,"q":"The utility transformer supplies 22,000 A of available fault current at a 480 V switchboard. The switchboard also feeds motors with Table 430.250 full-load currents of 124 A (100 hp), 65 A (50 hp) and 34 A (25 hp) at 460 V. Assume each running motor contributes 4 times its full-load current to a fault at the switchboard bus. What total fault current should be used to check equipment interrupting ratings?","o":["22,000 A","892 A","22,223 A","22,892 A"],"a":3,"e":"Motor contribution = 4 x (124 + 65 + 34) = 4 x 223 = 892 A. Total = 22,000 + 892 = 22,892 A. Using only the utility value ignores motor contribution; using only the motor contribution is not the bus fault current; adding 1 x FLC instead of 4 x gives 22,223 A. Interrupting ratings must exceed the total available (110.9).","r":"NEC 2023 110.9, 110.10, Table 430.250","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Short-circuit current, grounding and bonding calculations","d":3,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the available fault current marking requirements for service equipment in a commercial building under 110.24?","o":["Marking is required only on the utility transformer nameplate, not on the service equipment","The marking is required on service equipment in all occupancies, including one- and two-family dwellings, but no date is required","Service equipment must be field-marked with the maximum available fault current and the date the calculation was performed, and the marking must be updated when modifications change the available fault current","Marking must be updated only when the service equipment is replaced; load or feeder modifications do not require a new calculation"],"a":2,"e":"110.24(A) requires service equipment in other than dwelling units to be legibly field-marked with the maximum available fault current and the date the calculation was performed, using durable marking. 110.24(B) requires the available fault current to be verified or recalculated and the marking updated when modifications to the electrical installation occur that affect the maximum available fault current. Dwelling units are excluded.","r":"NEC 2023 110.24(A), 110.24(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Short-circuit current, grounding and bonding calculations","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 4-wire service uses 2 parallel sets of 500 kcmil copper per phase. What is the minimum size copper grounding electrode conductor to a building steel and metal water pipe electrode (not limited to a rod, ring or concrete-encased electrode)?","o":["2/0 AWG copper","1/0 AWG copper","3/0 AWG copper","2 AWG copper"],"a":0,"e":"For parallel sets, Table 250.66 is used with the total circular-mil area of the corresponding conductors of each set: 2 x 500 = 1000 kcmil. 1000 kcmil falls in the over 600 through 1100 kcmil row, so 2/0 AWG copper. Using one set (500 kcmil) gives 1/0; a row for over 1100 kcmil gives 3/0; 2 AWG corresponds to a 4/0 to 350 kcmil conductor.","r":"NEC 2023 250.66, Table 250.66, 250.24(D)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Short-circuit current, grounding and bonding calculations","d":3,"q":"A service has 3 parallel sets of 400 kcmil copper per phase. Separate grounding electrode conductors are run from the service to (1) the metal water pipe, (2) a concrete-encased electrode as the only connection to that electrode, and (3) a ground rod as the only connection to that rod. Which set of minimum copper conductor sizes complies with 250.66?","o":["(1) 3/0 AWG, (2) 3/0 AWG, (3) 3/0 AWG","(1) 1/0 AWG, (2) 4 AWG, (3) 6 AWG","(1) 3/0 AWG, (2) 6 AWG, (3) 4 AWG","(1) 3/0 AWG, (2) 4 AWG, (3) 6 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Total area = 3 x 400 = 1200 kcmil, over 1100 kcmil, so 3/0 AWG copper to the water pipe (250.66, Table 250.66). The conductor to a concrete-encased electrode that is the sole connection need not be larger than 4 AWG (250.66(B)), and to a ground rod that is the sole connection need not be larger than 6 AWG (250.66(A)). 1/0 uses a single set (400 kcmil) in error; swapping 6 and 4 or using 3/0 for all ignores the limitations.","r":"NEC 2023 250.66(A), 250.66(B), Table 250.66","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Short-circuit current, grounding and bonding calculations","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 480 V delta to 208Y/120 V separately derived system has one set of 250 kcmil copper secondary phase conductors. The primary conductors are 3 AWG. What is the minimum size copper grounding electrode conductor for the separately derived system connecting to the nearest effective building steel?","o":["6 AWG copper","2 AWG copper","1/0 AWG copper","4 AWG copper"],"a":1,"e":"250.30(A)(3) requires the grounding electrode conductor to be sized from Table 250.66 using the derived (secondary) ungrounded conductors, not the primary. 250 kcmil falls in the over 3/0 through 350 kcmil row, giving 2 AWG copper. 6 AWG corresponds to a 2 AWG or smaller conductor row, 4 AWG would be for 2/0-3/0, and 1/0 is for conductors over 350 kcmil.","r":"NEC 2023 250.30(A)(3), Table 250.66","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Short-circuit current, grounding and bonding calculations","d":3,"q":"An 800 A feeder is installed as 2 parallel sets of copper conductors in two separate steel conduits. What is the minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor required in each conduit?","o":["3 AWG copper","1 AWG copper","2/0 AWG copper","1/0 AWG copper"],"a":3,"e":"250.122(F) requires a full-size equipment grounding conductor in each raceway, each sized by Table 250.122 for the rating of the overcurrent device ahead of the feeder: 800 A requires 1/0 AWG copper. Sizing each for half the load (400 A) gives 3 AWG, 1 AWG corresponds to 600 A, and 2/0 corresponds to 1000 A.","r":"NEC 2023 250.122(A), 250.122(F), Table 250.122","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Short-circuit current, grounding and bonding calculations","d":3,"q":"A copper feeder protected at 100 A would normally use 3 AWG ungrounded conductors (75 C). To limit voltage drop the ungrounded conductors are increased to 1/0 AWG. What is the minimum copper equipment grounding conductor under 250.122(B)?","o":["4 AWG copper","8 AWG copper","6 AWG copper","1/0 AWG copper"],"a":0,"e":"Table 250.122 gives 8 AWG (16,510 CM) for a 100 A device. 250.122(B) increases the EGC proportionally to the increase in ungrounded circular mils: 105,600/52,620 = 2.007. Required = 16,510 x 2.007 = 33,133 CM, so 4 AWG (41,740 CM). 8 AWG ignores the increase, 6 AWG (26,240 CM) is too small, and 1/0 AWG is larger than required.","r":"NEC 2023 250.122(B), Table 250.122, Chapter 9 Table 8","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Short-circuit current, grounding and bonding calculations","d":3,"q":"A 200 A feeder normally requires 3/0 AWG copper ungrounded conductors (75 C). The conductors are increased to 300 kcmil to limit voltage drop. What is the minimum copper equipment grounding conductor under 250.122(B)?","o":["6 AWG copper","4 AWG copper","3 AWG copper","2 AWG copper"],"a":2,"e":"Table 250.122 gives 6 AWG (26,240 CM) for a 200 A device. Proportional increase = 300,000/167,800 = 1.7878, so the EGC must be at least 26,240 x 1.7878 = 46,913 CM. 4 AWG is 41,740 CM (too small); 3 AWG is 52,620 CM. 6 AWG ignores 250.122(B), and 2 AWG is larger than required.","r":"NEC 2023 250.122(B), Table 250.122, Chapter 9 Table 8","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Short-circuit current, grounding and bonding calculations","d":3,"q":"A service uses 4 parallel sets of 600 kcmil copper per phase (the sets are in separate raceways but the main bonding jumper is a single conductor at the service equipment). What is the minimum copper main bonding jumper under Table 250.102(C)(1)?","o":["3/0 AWG copper","250 kcmil copper","600 kcmil copper","300 kcmil copper"],"a":3,"e":"The equivalent ungrounded area is 4 x 600 = 2400 kcmil, which is over 1100 kcmil, so the bonding jumper must be at least 12.5% of that area: 0.125 x 2400 = 300 kcmil copper. 3/0 AWG is the Table 250.66 grounding electrode conductor size for over 1100 kcmil; 250 kcmil is only about 10.4%; 600 kcmil is the size of one phase conductor and larger than required.","r":"NEC 2023 250.28(D), 250.102(C)(1), Table 250.102(C)(1)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Short-circuit current, grounding and bonding calculations","d":3,"q":"Two separately derived systems (SDS A with 4/0 AWG copper secondary phase conductors, SDS B with 500 kcmil copper secondary phase conductors) are connected to a common grounding electrode conductor under 250.30(A)(6). Which set of minimum copper sizes is correct?","o":["Common conductor: 2 AWG; tap for SDS A: 2 AWG; tap for SDS B: 1/0 AWG","Common conductor: 3/0 AWG; tap for SDS A: 2 AWG; tap for SDS B: 1/0 AWG","Common conductor: 3/0 AWG; tap for SDS A: 1/0 AWG; tap for SDS B: 1/0 AWG","Common conductor: 3/0 AWG; tap for SDS A: 4 AWG; tap for SDS B: 2 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"250.30(A)(6) permits a common grounding electrode conductor not smaller than 3/0 AWG copper. Each tap is sized from Table 250.66 for its own derived conductors: 4/0 AWG (over 3/0 through 350 kcmil) needs 2 AWG, and 500 kcmil (over 350 through 600) needs 1/0 AWG. A 2 AWG common conductor is below the 3/0 minimum; SDS A does not need 1/0; and 4 AWG and 2 AWG taps are sized one row too small.","r":"NEC 2023 250.30(A)(6), Table 250.66","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Short-circuit current, grounding and bonding calculations","d":3,"q":"A 1000 kVA, 480 V, 3-phase transformer with 5.75% impedance (infinite primary) feeds a switchboard through 2 parallel sets of 600 kcmil copper per phase in steel conduit, 80 ft (C = 22,965). A 3/0 AWG copper feeder in steel conduit, 120 ft (C = 12,844, one set) then runs from the switchboard to a panelboard. What is the approximate available fault current at the panelboard (point-to-point, 3-phase)?","o":["18,490 A","20,920 A","11,390 A","12,270 A"],"a":2,"e":"FLA = 1,000,000/(1.732 x 480) = 1202.8 A; transformer Isc = 20,919 A. Switchboard: f = 1.732 x 80 x 20,919/(22,965 x 2 x 480) = 0.1315, M = 0.8838, Isc = 18,488 A. Panelboard: f = 1.732 x 120 x 18,488/(12,844 x 480) = 0.6233, M = 0.6160, Isc = 11,389 A. Stopping at the switchboard gives 18,488 A, using the transformer value gives 20,919 A, and applying the second run to the transformer value gives 12,268 A.","r":"NEC 2023 110.9, 110.24, 110.10","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Hazardous locations and special occupancies","d":3,"q":"In a Class I, Division 1 location, a 3-in. threaded rigid metal conduit enters an explosionproof junction box that contains only splices and taps (no arcing or sparking devices). Which statement is correct?","o":["No seal is required because the box contains no arcing or sparking devices.","A seal is required only if the conduit is trade size 4 or larger.","A seal is required, installed within 900 mm (36 in.) of the box.","A conduit seal is required, installed within 450 mm (18 in.) of the box."],"a":3,"e":"501.15(A)(1) requires a seal in each conduit entry to an explosionproof enclosure that contains arcing devices, OR where the entry is trade size 2 or larger and the enclosure contains terminals, splices or taps. A 3-in. entry with splices meets the second condition. The seal must be within 450 mm (18 in.) of the enclosure.","r":"NEC 2023 501.15(A)(1), 501.15(A)(2)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Hazardous locations and special occupancies","d":3,"q":"A seal fitting with a trade size 1/2 conduit connection is installed in a Class I, Division 1 raceway. What is the minimum thickness of the sealing compound?","o":["16 mm (5/8 in.)","13 mm (1/2 in.), equal to the trade size","19 mm (3/4 in.)","25 mm (1 in.)"],"a":0,"e":"The compound thickness may not be less than the trade size of the sealing fitting and in no case less than 16 mm (5/8 in.). For a 1/2 trade size fitting the trade-size minimum (1/2 in.) is smaller than the absolute minimum, so 5/8 in. governs.","r":"NEC 2023 501.15(C)(3)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Hazardous locations and special occupancies","d":3,"q":"A process pump seal in a refinery area releases flammable vapor intermittently during normal operation, so ignitable concentrations are likely to exist under normal operating conditions. The area is to be classified under the Zone system. Which classification applies?","o":["Zone 0","Zone 2","Zone 1","Zone 21"],"a":2,"e":"Zone 0 is continuous or long-period presence, Zone 1 is likely under normal operation, and Zone 2 is not likely under normal operation and then only briefly. Intermittent release in normal operation is Zone 1. Zone 21 is a dust classification.","r":"NEC 2023 505.5(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Hazardous locations and special occupancies","d":3,"q":"A battery-charging room is classified Class I, Division 1 because of hydrogen. The designer proposes a fixture marked 'Class I, Division 1, Group D'. What is the correct judgment?","o":["Acceptable; Group D is the most severe group and covers all other Class I gases.","Acceptable if conduit seals are added within 450 mm of the fixture.","Not acceptable; hydrogen is Group A, so Group A equipment is required.","Not acceptable; hydrogen is a Group B atmosphere, so the equipment must be marked for Group B (or specifically evaluated for hydrogen)."],"a":3,"e":"Class I groups are A (acetylene), B (hydrogen and similar), C (ethylene) and D (propane, gasoline). Group D equipment is not tested for hydrogen's lower gap and ignition energy, and seals do not change the group requirement.","r":"NEC 2023 500.6(A), 500.8(C)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Hazardous locations and special occupancies","d":3,"q":"A grain-handling facility has a Class II, Division 1, Group G location. Which wiring method is permitted for a fixed branch circuit to a bucket-elevator motor?","o":["EMT with compression fittings","Type NM cable in a protective sleeve","Liquidtight flexible nonmetallic conduit throughout","Threaded rigid metal conduit with dusttight (threaded) fittings"],"a":3,"e":"Class II, Division 1 wiring is limited to threaded RMC or IMC, MC-HL, MI and similar methods with dusttight fittings. EMT, NM cable and nonmetallic flexible raceways are not in the Division 1 list. Flexible connections are permitted only where flexibility is needed and use dusttight flexible connectors.","r":"NEC 2023 502.10(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Hazardous locations and special occupancies","d":3,"q":"At a motor fuel dispensing facility, where must the emergency electrical shutoff (disconnect) for the dispensers be located?","o":["Within 3 m (10 ft) of the dispensers so the attendant can reach it quickly.","Readily accessible, at least 6 m (20 ft) but not more than 30 m (100 ft) from the dispensers it serves.","Anywhere inside the cashier building regardless of distance.","Between 30 m and 60 m (100 and 200 ft) from the dispensers."],"a":1,"e":"514.11 requires emergency controls that shut off power to all dispensers, readily accessible to the attendant and located away from the dispensers, at least 6 m (20 ft) but not more than 30 m (100 ft). Distance is a requirement, not an option.","r":"NEC 2023 514.11(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Hazardous locations and special occupancies","d":3,"q":"A 20-A, 480-V, three-phase motor circuit in a Class I, Division 2 location is wired in rigid conduit with a 4-ft length of listed liquidtight flexible metal conduit (LFMC) at the motor. The circuit is not a signaling or control circuit. What bonding is required across the flexible section?","o":["An internal or external bonding jumper, sized per 250.122 (12 AWG copper for a 20-A circuit), because LFMC alone is not accepted as the bond.","None; LFMC 1.8 m (6 ft) or shorter is acceptable as the equipment grounding conductor on circuits up to 20 A.","A 10 AWG copper bonding jumper, sized for the motor FLC.","A 14 AWG copper bonding jumper, sized for the control circuit."],"a":0,"e":"In Class I locations flexible metal conduit needs a bonding jumper; the 250.118 short-length allowance is available only where the circuit protection is 10 A or less and the load is not a power utilization load. The jumper is sized from the 20-A OCPD using 250.122, giving 12 AWG Cu.","r":"NEC 2023 501.30(B), 250.102, 250.122","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Hazardous locations and special occupancies","d":3,"q":"A control enclosure with general-purpose equipment is installed in a Class I, Division 1 area and is protected by a Type X purge system per NFPA 496. What effect does this have on the enclosure interior?","o":["The interior is reduced to Division 2 only.","The interior is treated as unclassified, so general-purpose equipment is permitted inside while the purge system operates.","The interior is reduced to Division 2 and requires Division 2 listed equipment.","There is no effect; the interior remains Division 1."],"a":1,"e":"Type X purging reduces the classification inside the enclosure from Division 1 to unclassified; Type Y reduces Division 1 to Division 2; Type Z reduces Division 2 to unclassified. Purge failure must be monitored as required by NFPA 496.","r":"NEC 2023 500.7(E), NFPA 496","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Hazardous locations and special occupancies","d":3,"q":"Equipment for a Class I location is marked with a T-code. The flammable vapor has an autoignition temperature of 230 degrees C. Which marked luminaire is suitable?","o":["T3 (maximum surface temperature 200 degrees C)","T2B (260 degrees C)","T2 (300 degrees C)","T1 (450 degrees C)"],"a":0,"e":"The equipment's maximum surface temperature must be below the autoignition temperature of the gas. 200 degrees C (T3) is below 230 degrees C; T2B, T2 and T1 all exceed it.","r":"NEC 2023 500.8(D), Table 500.8(D)(1)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Hazardous locations and special occupancies","d":3,"q":"A hangar stores aircraft with fuel in the tanks. A standard 20-A GFCI receptacle is proposed at 36 in. above the floor, 3 ft horizontally from an engine nacelle. Is the location acceptable?","o":["Yes; only the area up to 450 mm (18 in.) above the floor is classified.","Yes; GFCI protection removes the ignition hazard from the receptacle.","No; within 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally of aircraft power plants or fuel tanks the area is Class I, Division 2 up to 1.5 m above wings and engine enclosures, so a Division 2 suitable receptacle is required.","No; receptacles are prohibited anywhere in a hangar."],"a":2,"e":"513.3 classifies the area within 5 ft horizontally of power plants and fuel tanks as Class I, Division 2, extending vertically from the floor to 5 ft above the upper surface of wings and engine enclosures. The 36-in. receptacle is within that zone. GFCI protection does not substitute for hazardous location suitability.","r":"NEC 2023 513.3(B), 513.7","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Hazardous locations and special occupancies","d":3,"q":"A textile mill is classified Class III, Division 1 (easily ignitable fibers). Which wiring method is permitted?","o":["Type NM cable","Open wiring on insulators","EMT with dusttight fittings","Concealed knob-and-tube wiring"],"a":2,"e":"Class III, Division 1 permits RMC, IMC, EMT, dusttight wireways, MC and MI cable with dusttight fittings. NM cable, open wiring and knob-and-tube are not recognized methods for the location.","r":"NEC 2023 503.10(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Hazardous locations and special occupancies","d":3,"q":"An enclosed room is classified Class I, Division 1 only because of a flammable-liquid transfer process. Which condition would allow the room to be reclassified as Division 2?","o":["Natural ventilation through wall louvers","Exhaust-only fans running continuously without an airflow interlock","Positive-pressure mechanical ventilation from a clean-air source, with effective safeguards against ventilation failure","Installation of explosionproof lighting fixtures"],"a":2,"e":"500.5(B)(2) permits Division 1 to be treated as Division 2 where ignitable concentrations are normally prevented by positive-pressure ventilation from a clean source, with safeguards (such as interlocks and alarms) against failure. Natural or exhaust-only ventilation and equipment type do not change the classification.","r":"NEC 2023 500.5(B)(2)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Hazardous locations and special occupancies","d":3,"q":"Which agricultural building situation requires an equipotential plane under Article 547?","o":["A dry hay storage barn with a dirt floor and no metallic equipment","A livestock confinement area with a concrete floor where metallic equipment that could become energized is accessible to the animals","A machinery shed used only for storing tractors","A commercial grain bin with no livestock access"],"a":1,"e":"547.10 requires equipotential planes in indoor concrete confinement areas with metallic equipment that may become energized and is accessible to livestock, bonded to the building grounding system to reduce stray-voltage gradients. The other situations do not meet that description.","r":"NEC 2023 547.10","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Health care, pools, EV and solar design (Art. 517, 680, 625, 690, 705)","d":3,"q":"A PV source circuit has 14 modules in series, Voc = 45.8 V at 25 C, temperature coefficient of Voc = -0.27%/C, and the lowest expected ambient temperature is -22 C. What is the maximum system voltage per 690.7(A)?","o":["641 V","723 V","560 V","679 V"],"a":1,"e":"Correction factor = 1 + (-0.0027) x (-22 - 25) = 1.1269. Voltage = 14 x 45.8 x 1.1269 = 722.6 V. Using 25 C Voc gives 641.2 V, subtracting the correction gives 560 V, and measuring from 0 C gives 679 V.","r":"NEC 2023 690.7(A)(2)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Health care, pools, EV and solar design (Art. 517, 680, 625, 690, 705)","d":3,"q":"A single-phase 7.68-kW, 240-V PV inverter has a maximum continuous output of 32 A. Using 75 C terminations and copper, what is the minimum inverter output circuit conductor and OCPD?","o":["10 AWG Cu with a 40 A breaker","10 AWG Cu with a 35 A breaker","8 AWG Cu with a 40 A breaker","8 AWG Cu with a 35 A breaker"],"a":2,"e":"Output circuit current is 32 A, and 690.8 requires 125%: 32 x 1.25 = 40 A, so the OCPD is 40 A and the conductor needs at least 40 A. 10 AWG is only 35 A at 75 C terminations (the 90 C value of 40 A cannot be used), so 8 AWG (50 A at 75 C) is required.","r":"NEC 2023 690.8(A)(3), 690.9, 110.14(C)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Health care, pools, EV and solar design (Art. 517, 680, 625, 690, 705)","d":3,"q":"A 225-A busbar panelboard is protected by a 200-A main. Using the 120% busbar method (125% of the inverter output current plus the main OCPD rating may not exceed 120% of the busbar ampacity), what is the maximum inverter continuous output current?","o":["70 A","25 A","56 A","45 A"],"a":2,"e":"120% of 225 A = 270 A. Subtract the 200-A main: 270 - 200 = 70 A available for 125% of the inverter current, so the inverter current is 70 / 1.25 = 56 A (a 70-A breaker). Using 70 A as the output current ignores the 125%, and 100% of the busbar leaves only 25 A.","r":"NEC 2023 705.12(B)(3)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Health care, pools, EV and solar design (Art. 517, 680, 625, 690, 705)","d":3,"q":"A rooftop PV system under 690.12 receives a rapid shutdown initiation. After 30 seconds, what voltage limits apply to controlled conductors more than 305 mm (1 ft) from the array and to those within the array boundary?","o":["80 V maximum outside the array boundary, and 30 V maximum within it","30 V maximum both outside and inside the array boundary","15 V maximum outside and 50 V maximum inside the array boundary","30 V maximum outside the array boundary, and 80 V maximum within the array boundary"],"a":3,"e":"Rapid shutdown limits controlled conductors outside the array boundary to 30 V and conductors within the array boundary to 80 V, within 30 seconds of initiation. The array boundary is 305 mm (1 ft) from the array in all directions. The tighter limit applies where firefighters work outside the array.","r":"NEC 2023 690.12(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Health care, pools, EV and solar design (Art. 517, 680, 625, 690, 705)","d":3,"q":"A hardwired 240-V, single-phase EVSE has a nameplate output of 48 A continuous. Using 75 C terminations and copper, what are the minimum branch-circuit conductor and OCPD?","o":["6 AWG Cu with a 60 A breaker","8 AWG Cu with a 50 A breaker","8 AWG Cu with a 60 A breaker","6 AWG Cu with a 50 A breaker"],"a":0,"e":"EVSE is a continuous load, so the branch circuit is rated at 125%: 48 x 1.25 = 60 A. The OCPD is 60 A and the conductor needs at least 60 A: 6 AWG at 75 C is 65 A, while 8 AWG is only 50 A. Using 48 A directly leads to the 50 A options.","r":"NEC 2023 625.41, 210.19(A), 210.20(A), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Health care, pools, EV and solar design (Art. 517, 680, 625, 690, 705)","d":3,"q":"A feeder calculation includes four 240-V EVSEs with nameplate ratings of 48 A, 32 A, 24 A and 24 A. Using 220.57 (7200 VA or the nameplate rating, whichever is larger, per EVSE), no demand factor and no EVSE energy management system, what is the load added before any other multiplier?","o":["30,720 VA","33,600 VA","42,000 VA","28,800 VA"],"a":1,"e":"Per EVSE: 48 A x 240 = 11,520 VA; 32 A x 240 = 7,680 VA; 24 A x 240 = 5,760 VA, which is below 7,200 so 7,200 VA is used for each of the two. Total = 11,520 + 7,680 + 7,200 + 7,200 = 33,600 VA. Using nameplates only gives 30,720 VA and using 7,200 for all gives 28,800 VA.","r":"NEC 2023 220.57, 625.42","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Health care, pools, EV and solar design (Art. 517, 680, 625, 690, 705)","d":3,"q":"Which EVSE installation requires a lockable disconnecting means under 625.43?","o":["A hardwired 30-A EVSE supplied from a 480Y/277-V system (277 V to ground)","A hardwired 48-A EVSE on a 240-V single-phase circuit (120 V to ground)","A hardwired 50-A EVSE on a 208Y/120-V three-phase circuit (120 V to ground)","A 40-A EVSE on a 240-V single-phase circuit in an attached garage"],"a":0,"e":"625.43 requires a readily accessible disconnecting means, lockable in the open position, for EVSE rated more than 60 A or more than 150 V to ground. The 277-V-to-ground installation exceeds 150 V; the other three are 60 A or less and 120 V to ground.","r":"NEC 2023 625.43","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Health care, pools, EV and solar design (Art. 517, 680, 625, 690, 705)","d":3,"q":"Under 680.26, bonding of the perimeter surface around a permanently installed pool must extend how far horizontally beyond the inside walls of the pool?","o":["1.5 m (5 ft)","1 m (3 ft)","3 m (10 ft)","300 mm (1 ft)"],"a":1,"e":"The equipotential bonding requirements apply to the perimeter surface extending 1 m (3 ft) horizontally beyond the inside walls of the pool, using reinforcing steel, a copper grid (such as 8 AWG solid in a 12-in. pattern) or other listed means.","r":"NEC 2023 680.26(B)(2), 680.26(C)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Health care, pools, EV and solar design (Art. 517, 680, 625, 690, 705)","d":3,"q":"A 30-A, 120-V GFCI-protected branch circuit supplies several wet-niche pool luminaires through a junction box. What is the minimum insulated copper equipment grounding conductor size for the circuit?","o":["10 AWG","12 AWG","8 AWG","14 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"The EGC for a pool luminaire circuit is sized per 250.122 and may not be smaller than 12 AWG. A 30-A OCPD calls for 10 AWG in Table 250.122, which is larger than the 12 AWG floor. 12 AWG would apply only to a 20-A circuit.","r":"NEC 2023 680.23(B)(2), Table 250.122","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Health care, pools, EV and solar design (Art. 517, 680, 625, 690, 705)","d":3,"q":"An isolated power system serves a critical-care operating room. At what total hazard current must the line isolation monitor alarm?","o":["When it exceeds 2 mA","When the total hazard current exceeds 5 mA","When it exceeds 10 mA","When it exceeds 30 mA"],"a":1,"e":"The LIM is designed to indicate when the total hazard current (resistive plus capacitive leakage) reaches 5 mA, and it may not alarm for a hazard current of 3.7 mA or less. 2 mA would cause nuisance alarms, while 10 mA and 30 mA exceed the limit.","r":"NEC 2023 517.160(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Health care, pools, EV and solar design (Art. 517, 680, 625, 690, 705)","d":3,"q":"Which branch-circuit wiring method for a 20-A, 120-V circuit in a general-care patient room satisfies the redundant grounding requirement of 517.13?","o":["EMT containing only the circuit conductors, using the EMT as the grounding path","Rigid PVC conduit with an insulated copper equipment grounding conductor","EMT containing the circuit conductors plus a separate insulated copper equipment grounding conductor","Type NM cable with its bare equipment grounding conductor"],"a":2,"e":"517.13 requires both a metal raceway or cable armor that qualifies as an equipment grounding conductor and an insulated copper equipment grounding conductor. The EMT only option lacks the insulated EGC, and PVC and NM cable lack the metal path.","r":"NEC 2023 517.13(A), 517.13(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Health care, pools, EV and solar design (Art. 517, 680, 625, 690, 705)","d":3,"q":"In a wet procedure location where loss of power on a first ground fault could endanger the patient, which protection is correct?","o":["GFCI-protected receptacles only","A standard 20-A circuit breaker","An isolated power system with a line isolation monitor","AFCI protection on the branch circuits"],"a":2,"e":"517.20 requires GFCI protection or an isolated power system for receptacles and fixed equipment in wet procedure locations, and an isolated system is used where interruption on the first fault would be unacceptable. A GFCI trips on the first fault.","r":"NEC 2023 517.20(A), 517.160","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Overcurrent protection, selective coordination and fault protection","d":3,"q":"A 40-hp, 460-V, three-phase induction motor (Table 430.250 FLC = 52 A) starts without nuisance tripping on an inverse-time breaker. What is the maximum standard breaker size permitted for branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault protection?","o":["125 A","150 A","175 A","225 A"],"a":1,"e":"Table 430.52 allows 250% for an inverse-time breaker: 52 x 2.5 = 130 A, which is not a standard size. 430.52(C)(1) Exception 1 permits the next higher standard size, 150 A. Rounding down gives 125 A, 300% of FLC rounded up gives 175 A, and 400% gives 225 A.","r":"NEC 2023 430.52(C)(1), Table 430.52, 240.6(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Overcurrent protection, selective coordination and fault protection","d":3,"q":"A feeder supplies three 460-V motors with FLCs from Table 430.250 of 34 A, 21 A and 14 A. Each branch circuit uses an inverse-time breaker at the maximum 250% rating (next higher standard size). Using 430.62(A), what is the maximum feeder OCPD?","o":["125 A","110 A","150 A","175 A"],"a":0,"e":"The largest branch-circuit device is for the 34-A motor: 34 x 2.5 = 85 A, next higher standard 90 A. Add the other FLCs: 90 + 21 + 14 = 125 A, which is a standard size. Using 85 + 35 = 120 A rounds down to 110 A, 90 + all three FLCs = 159 A rounds to 150 A, and 250% of the 69-A sum is 172.5 A, or 175 A.","r":"NEC 2023 430.62(A), 430.52, 240.6(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Overcurrent protection, selective coordination and fault protection","d":3,"q":"A 500-kVA, 480-V, three-phase transformer with 5.75% impedance feeds a panelboard (assume an infinite primary source and negligible conductor impedance). Standard breaker interrupting ratings are 10, 14, 18, 22 and 25 kA. What is the minimum rating that satisfies 110.9?","o":["14 kA","10 kA","18 kA","22 kA"],"a":0,"e":"Secondary FLA = 500,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 601.4 A. Available fault current = 601.4 / 0.0575 = 10,459 A. That exceeds 10 kA, so the next standard rating, 14 kA, is the minimum; 18 and 22 kA are adequate but not minimum.","r":"NEC 2023 110.9, 110.24","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Overcurrent protection, selective coordination and fault protection","d":3,"q":"A 75-kVA, 480-V three-phase to 208Y/120-V transformer is protected on the primary only, with no secondary overcurrent device. Using Table 450.3(B) (125% for primary current of 9 A or more, with the next higher standard rating permitted), what is the maximum primary OCPD rating?","o":["110 A","125 A","100 A","225 A"],"a":1,"e":"Primary current = 75,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 90.2 A. 125% gives 112.8 A, which is not a standard size, so Note 1 permits the next higher standard rating, 125 A. 110 A is the next lower size, 100 A is 110% rounded, and 250% of the primary current is 226 A, or 225 A.","r":"NEC 2023 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B), 240.6(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Overcurrent protection, selective coordination and fault protection","d":3,"q":"A feeder supplies 120 A of noncontinuous load and 90 A of continuous load. The OCPD is not listed for operation at 100% of its rating. Using 75 C copper, what is the minimum conductor and OCPD?","o":["4/0 AWG Cu with a 250 A OCPD","3/0 AWG Cu with a 225 A OCPD","250 kcmil Cu with a 250 A OCPD","250 kcmil Cu with a 225 A OCPD"],"a":2,"e":"Design load = 120 + 1.25 x 90 = 232.5 A. The conductor must carry at least 232.5 A: 4/0 AWG is 230 A (too small), 250 kcmil is 255 A. The OCPD must be at least 232.5 A, so the next standard size is 250 A. 225 A is below the required value.","r":"NEC 2023 215.2(A)(1), 215.3, Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Overcurrent protection, selective coordination and fault protection","d":3,"q":"A 1600-A frame circuit breaker has an adjustable long-time pickup that is currently set at 1000 A, though it can be adjusted up to 1600 A. Does the arc-energy reduction requirement of 240.87 apply?","o":["No; it applies only if the present setting is 1200 A or higher.","No; it applies only to breakers rated 2000 A or more.","Yes, but only if the equipment is in a dwelling unit.","Yes; the requirement applies because the highest continuous current trip setting to which the device can be adjusted is 1200 A or higher."],"a":3,"e":"240.87 applies where the highest continuous current trip setting for which the actual installed device is rated or can be adjusted is 1200 A or higher, even if it is currently set lower. A method such as zone-selective interlocking, differential relaying or a maintenance switch must then be provided and documented.","r":"NEC 2023 240.87(A), 240.87(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Overcurrent protection, selective coordination and fault protection","d":3,"q":"A 2000-A, 480Y/277-V, three-phase, solidly grounded wye service requires ground-fault protection of equipment. Which setting meets the maximum limits?","o":["Pickup at 1600 A with a 0.5 second delay","Pickup at 1200 A with a 2 second delay at 3000 A","Pickup at 1200 A with a maximum time delay of 1 second for ground-fault currents of 3000 A or more","Pickup at 2000 A with no intentional delay"],"a":2,"e":"230.95 limits the maximum ground-fault pickup setting to 1200 A and the maximum time delay to 1 second for ground-fault currents of 3000 A or more. A pickup above 1200 A or a delay beyond 1 second does not comply.","r":"NEC 2023 230.95(A)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Overcurrent protection, selective coordination and fault protection","d":3,"q":"Which of the following is NOT required by the NEC to have its overcurrent protective devices selectively coordinated?","o":["An optional standby system serving a data room","An emergency system serving exit lighting and egress loads","A legally required standby system serving fire alarm and smoke control","Elevator feeders serving multiple elevators on one feeder"],"a":0,"e":"Selective coordination is required for emergency systems (700.32), legally required standby systems (701.32), critical operations power systems (708.54) and multiple-elevator supply circuits (620.62). Optional standby systems under Article 702 do not carry this requirement.","r":"NEC 2023 700.32, 701.32, 620.62, 702","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Overcurrent protection, selective coordination and fault protection","d":3,"q":"A series-rated combination (lower-rated 10 kA breaker protected by an upstream device) is proposed. Motors with FLCs of 40 A, 40 A and 34 A are connected between the line side of the lower-rated breaker and its load side. Is the series rating permitted under 240.86(C)?","o":["Yes; 114 A is less than 10% of the 10 kA rating.","Yes; motor FLC is not considered in a series rating.","No; the series rating is never allowed where motors are connected.","No; the combined motor FLC of 114 A exceeds 1% of the lower breaker's 10 kA interrupting rating (100 A)."],"a":3,"e":"240.86(C) prohibits a series rating where motors are connected between the two devices and the sum of their FLCs exceeds 1% of the interrupting rating of the lower-rated breaker. 1% of 10,000 A = 100 A, and 40 + 40 + 34 = 114 A is larger. A 10% criterion is not in the Code.","r":"NEC 2023 240.86(C)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Overcurrent protection, selective coordination and fault protection","d":3,"q":"A 400-A feeder is upsized for voltage drop from 600 kcmil to 900 kcmil copper ungrounded conductors. Table 250.122 gives 3 AWG copper for a 400-A OCPD. What is the minimum copper equipment grounding conductor under 250.122(B)?","o":["3 AWG","2 AWG","1/0 AWG","1 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"250.122(B) requires the EGC to be increased in proportion to the circular mil area of the ungrounded conductors. 900 / 600 = 1.5, so 52,620 cmil x 1.5 = 78,930 cmil. 2 AWG (66,360) is too small; 1 AWG (83,690) is the first size that meets it.","r":"NEC 2023 250.122(B), Table 250.122, Ch. 9 Table 8","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Overcurrent protection, selective coordination and fault protection","d":3,"q":"A 25-ft feeder tap rule conductor is installed from a 400-A feeder to a single 100-A load center OCPD supplying a 110-A calculated load (terminations rated 75 C, copper). What is the minimum tap conductor size?","o":["1/0 AWG","3 AWG","2 AWG","1 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"The 25-ft tap conductor must have an ampacity of at least 1/3 of the feeder OCPD rating: 400 / 3 = 133.3 A. 1 AWG at 75 C is 130 A, which is too small; 1/0 AWG is 150 A and also covers the 110-A load. 2 AWG and 3 AWG fail both tests.","r":"NEC 2023 240.21(B)(2), Table 310.16","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Overcurrent protection, selective coordination and fault protection","d":3,"q":"An industrial control panel has a marked short-circuit current rating (SCCR) of 10 kA. The available fault current at its supply terminals is 18 kA. Which action is acceptable?","o":["Install the panel because the main breaker is rated 65 kA","Install the panel; the SCCR only needs to exceed the largest motor FLC","Obtain a panel (or listed modification) with an SCCR of at least 18 kA before installation","Add a label stating 18 kA available and leave the panel as is"],"a":2,"e":"409.22 requires the SCCR to be not less than the available fault current at the supply terminals. The panel's SCCR is limited by the weakest component or its tested combination, not by the rating of an upstream device unless tested together. Labeling does not cure the deficiency.","r":"NEC 2023 409.22, 409.110, 110.10","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Overcurrent protection, selective coordination and fault protection","d":3,"q":"A new 800-A, 480Y/277-V commercial switchboard is being energized. Which statement about field marking of fault current under 110.24 is correct?","o":["Marking is required only for dwelling units.","The maximum available fault current and the date it was calculated must be marked, and the marking must be updated when modifications change the available fault current.","Marking is required only if the available fault current exceeds 65 kA.","A one-time marking is enough even if the utility transformer is later replaced."],"a":1,"e":"110.24(A) requires service equipment in other than dwelling units to be field marked with the maximum available fault current and the date of the calculation. 110.24(B) requires the marking to be updated when modifications change the available fault current.","r":"NEC 2023 110.24(A), 110.24(B)","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Wisconsin master electrician practice (supervision, permits, inspection, plan review)","d":3,"q":"After plans are approved, the owner increases the commercial service from 400 A to 800 A. What must the master electrician do?","o":["Install the 800-A service, then note the change on the as-built drawings.","Install it if the inspector verbally agrees at the final inspection.","No action is needed because the service size is chosen in the field.","Submit revised plans and obtain approval of the change before installing the larger service."],"a":3,"e":"Changes to approved plans that affect service size, load calculations, fault current or protection require revised plans and approval before the changed work proceeds. As-built notes after installation do not satisfy that requirement.","r":"Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316, SPS 361","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Wisconsin master electrician practice (supervision, permits, inspection, plan review)","d":3,"q":"A master electrician learns that drywall has been installed over rough-in wiring before the rough-in inspection was requested. What is the most likely outcome?","o":["The inspection is waived because the wiring is concealed.","The inspector must accept a verbal description from the master.","The inspector can require the work to be exposed so it can be inspected before it is concealed.","The final inspection alone is sufficient."],"a":2,"e":"Rough-in work is to be inspected before concealment. Concealing it without inspection can lead to a requirement to open the walls, causing cost and delay.","r":"Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Wisconsin master electrician practice (supervision, permits, inspection, plan review)","d":3,"q":"The owner pressures a contractor to energize a permanent service before the final inspection because the tenant opens soon. What is the correct response?","o":["Do not energize until the inspection authority approves or authorizes the release; use properly permitted temporary power in the meantime if needed.","Energize it and complete the inspection afterwards.","Energize it if the master electrician signs a statement that it is safe.","Energize it if the owner signs a waiver."],"a":0,"e":"Energizing is not left to owner or contractor discretion; the inspection authority must approve or authorize the connection. Waivers and signed statements do not replace inspection.","r":"Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Wisconsin master electrician practice (supervision, permits, inspection, plan review)","d":3,"q":"A journeyman electrician wants to pull an electrical permit in his own name to wire a customer's garage after hours, with no contractor involved. What is the correct answer?","o":["Permitted, because a journeyman license authorizes independent contracting.","Not permitted; electrical work for hire must be performed through a registered electrical contractor that has a master electrician qualifying it, and the permit is obtained through that contractor.","Permitted if the customer signs the permit application.","Permitted if the work is under 20 A."],"a":1,"e":"A journeyman license does not authorize independent contracting; the contractor registration with a qualifying master electrician is required, and permits for work for hire are obtained through that contractor.","r":"Wis. Admin. Code SPS 305, SPS 316","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Wisconsin master electrician practice (supervision, permits, inspection, plan review)","d":3,"q":"Which plan submittal best demonstrates NEC compliance for a new 800-A, 480Y/277-V commercial service?","o":["Equipment cut sheets only","A written description of the equipment and loads","A permit application without drawings","A single-line diagram, service load calculation, available fault current and equipment interrupting ratings, panel schedules, and grounding details"],"a":3,"e":"A plan reviewer needs the single-line, load calculation, short-circuit data, panel schedules and grounding and protection details to check Article 220, 110.9, 250 and others. Cut sheets or descriptions alone are inadequate.","r":"Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316, SPS 361; NEC 2023 110.9, 220","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Wisconsin master electrician practice (supervision, permits, inspection, plan review)","d":3,"q":"A journeyman installs an incorrectly sized breaker on a job supervised by a master electrician. Who bears responsibility?","o":["Only the journeyman who installed it.","The master electrician remains accountable for the compliance of work done under his or her supervision, in addition to the installer.","The inspector, because a permit was issued.","No one, if the equipment is listed."],"a":1,"e":"The master electrician is responsible for ensuring work under his or her supervision complies with the code, so supervision includes verifying the installation. Issuance of a permit does not shift responsibility.","r":"Wis. Admin. Code SPS 305, SPS 316","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Wisconsin master electrician practice (supervision, permits, inspection, plan review)","d":3,"q":"A construction site needs a temporary 120/240-V service for power tools. How is it treated?","o":["It is exempt from permits and inspections because it is temporary.","It needs inspection only if it remains longer than 90 days.","As electrical wiring subject to the same permit and inspection requirements, and installed per Article 590 (including GFCI protection)","It may be installed by any worker without a license."],"a":2,"e":"Temporary wiring is electrical wiring: Article 590 provides installation rules, and permit and inspection requirements still apply. Short-term use does not exempt it.","r":"NEC 2023 590.6; Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Wisconsin master electrician practice (supervision, permits, inspection, plan review)","d":3,"q":"A master electrician disagrees with an inspector's interpretation and believes an alternate method provides equivalent safety. What is the proper course?","o":["Install the alternate method and argue about it later.","Ask the engineer to stamp the drawings to override the inspector.","Seek resolution through the appeal or petition-for-variance process and install per the inspector's order until a variance is granted.","Ignore the order since interpretations are not binding."],"a":2,"e":"The petition-for-variance and appeal procedures let a contractor demonstrate equivalent safety. Installing a disputed method on one's own authority risks correction orders and enforcement.","r":"Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Wisconsin master electrician practice (supervision, permits, inspection, plan review)","d":3,"q":"Approved stamped plans for an electrical installation should be located where during construction?","o":["At the contractor's office only","At the job site, available to the installer and inspector","With the owner","Destroyed after approval"],"a":1,"e":"Installers must work from the approved plans and inspectors verify against them, so they should be available on site. Work from an unapproved set may not match the approved design.","r":"Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316, SPS 361","b":4},{"k":"mast","c":"Master: Wisconsin master electrician practice (supervision, permits, inspection, plan review)","d":3,"q":"A homeowner is hiring an electrician for a service upgrade in a one-family dwelling. Which statement reflects Wisconsin practice?","o":["The work should be performed by a licensed contractor/electrician, with a permit obtained and the work inspected before it is concealed or energized.","No permit is needed for a like-for-like service upgrade.","Inspection is required only if the work fails.","Only the utility inspects service work."],"a":0,"e":"Service work requires an electrical permit, a licensed installer (apart from limited owner exemptions) and inspection. The utility depends on the inspection release before connecting.","r":"Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316, SPS 320-325","b":4}];